window.ATLAS_DICT = [{"term":"Abduction","def":"Reasoning to the best explanation: from some evidence, you infer the hypothesis that would explain it best.","more":"Peirce introduced abduction as a third kind of inference beside deduction and induction. Gilbert Harman later called it 'inference to the best explanation'. Detectives, doctors and scientists reason this way all the time, but philosophers debate what makes one explanation 'best': simplicity, scope, fit with other beliefs? Critics such as Bas van Fraassen doubt that the best explanation we have is likely to be true.","etym":"Latin abductio, 'a leading away'.","example":"You hear scratching in the wall at night and conclude there are mice, because that explains the noise better than anything else.","notConfused":"Not the same as deduction: abduction can lead from true evidence to a false conclusion, because a better explanation may exist that you have not thought of.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Deduction","Induction","Occam’s razor"],"thinkers":["peirce","harman"],"tags":["inference to the best explanation","abductive reasoning","best explanation","hypothesis","peirce"]},{"term":"Abstract object","def":"A thing that is not located in space or time and does not cause anything, such as a number or a set.","more":"Numbers, sets, propositions and perhaps Platonic Forms are standard candidates. Platonists say such objects really exist, since mathematics seems to talk about them truly. Nominalists, such as Hartry Field, deny it and try to explain mathematics without them. A key puzzle is how we could ever know about things we cannot interact with.","etym":"Latin abstractus, 'drawn away'.","example":"The number 7 is not anywhere: you cannot trip over it, yet '7 is prime' seems true.","notConfused":"Not the same as an abstract idea in your head: a Platonist thinks the number 7 exists whether or not anyone thinks of it.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Platonism","Nominalism","Universals"],"thinkers":["frege","field"],"tags":["abstract entity","abstracta","numbers","platonism","non-physical object"]},{"term":"Absurd","def":"Camus’ name for the clash between our hunger for meaning and a universe that gives no answer.","example":"Like Sisyphus pushing his boulder uphill forever: Camus says we must imagine him happy.","related":["Existentialism","Nihilism"],"thinkers":["camus"],"compass":"q20","more":"Camus developed the idea in 'The Myth of Sisyphus' (1942). He rejected two escapes from the absurd: suicide, and 'philosophical suicide', meaning a leap into religious or other comforting faith. Instead he urged revolt: living fully and honestly while keeping the absurd in view. Camus refused the existentialist label, though he is often grouped with Sartre.","etym":"Latin absurdus, 'out of tune, senseless'.","notConfused":"Not the same as nihilism: Camus thought life can still be lived with passion and value in the face of the absurd.","field":"Ethics","tags":["absurdity","absurdism","meaninglessness","camus","sisyphus","meaning of life"]},{"term":"Accident (property)","def":"A feature a thing happens to have but could lose and still be the same thing.","more":"Aristotle contrasted accidents with essence: being rational was, for him, essential to a human, while being pale or sitting down was accidental. The distinction matters for questions of identity and change. Medieval philosophers and theologians used it heavily, for example in discussing how bread could change in substance while keeping its appearance.","etym":"Latin accidens, 'happening', from accidere, 'to befall'.","example":"Your hair colour is an accidental property: dye it blue and you are still you.","notConfused":"Not the same as an accident in everyday speech (a mishap): here it means a non-essential feature.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Essence","Substance"],"thinkers":["aristotle","aquinas"],"tags":["accidental property","non-essential property","contingent property","attribute","essence"]},{"term":"Acts and omissions","def":"The question of whether doing harm is morally worse than allowing a harm you could have prevented.","more":"Many people feel that killing is worse than letting die, and many legal systems reflect this. James Rachels argued in 1975 that the difference does not matter morally in itself, using paired cases where the only difference is acting or not acting. Others, including Philippa Foot, defend the distinction as part of the difference between negative duties (not to harm) and positive duties (to help).","etym":null,"example":"Pushing someone into a river seems worse than failing to throw a rope to someone already drowning, even if both die.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Doctrine of double effect","Trolley problem","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["rachels","foot"],"tags":["doing and allowing","killing and letting die","omission","inaction","doctrine of doing and allowing"]},{"term":"Ad hominem","def":"Attacking the person making an argument instead of the argument itself.","example":"‘You can’t trust her climate data: she drives a car.’","related":["Fallacy","Straw man"],"more":"The fallacy works by shifting attention from what is said to who says it. Not every remark about a person is fallacious: pointing out that a witness has been bribed is relevant to whether we should trust their testimony. The error is treating a personal fact as if it refuted the argument itself. A common form is the 'tu quoque' ('you too'), which dismisses a claim because the speaker does not live up to it.","etym":"Latin, 'to the person'.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","thinkers":[],"tags":["personal attack","attacking the person","argumentum ad hominem","name-calling","fallacy"]},{"term":"Advaita Vedanta","def":"A school of Hindu philosophy teaching that only Brahman is fully real and that the true self (atman) is identical with it.","more":"Its most famous teacher is Shankara, traditionally dated to the 8th century CE. Advaita holds that the world of many separate things is maya, an appearance that hides the one reality. Liberation (moksha) comes from knowledge that dissolves this ignorance. Other Vedanta schools, led by Ramanuja and Madhva, reject strict non-dualism.","etym":"Sanskrit advaita, 'not two', and vedānta, 'end of the Vedas'.","example":"A rope in dim light mistaken for a snake: the snake was never there, only the rope. Advaita uses this image for the world and Brahman.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Brahman","Atman","Dharma"],"thinkers":["shankara","vivekananda"],"tags":["non-dualism","advaita","shankara","nondual vedanta","brahman","atman"]},{"term":"Aesthetics","def":"The study of beauty, art and taste. It asks whether beauty is in the object or in the eye of the beholder.","example":"Arguing over whether a pop song can be as great as a symphony is an aesthetics debate.","related":["Relativism","Moral realism"],"thinkers":["hume","kant"],"compass":"q5","more":"Aesthetics covers questions such as what makes something art, whether judgements of taste can be right or wrong, and why we enjoy tragedy or sad music. Hume argued that a 'standard of taste' can be found in the shared verdicts of experienced, unbiased critics. Kant held that a judgement of beauty claims everyone's agreement even though it rests on a feeling, not on a concept or rule.","etym":"Greek aisthētikos, 'to do with perception by the senses'; the modern sense comes from Alexander Baumgarten in the 1700s.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'an aesthetic' in everyday talk (a look or style): aesthetics is the philosophical study of beauty and art in general.","field":"Beauty and art","tags":["philosophy of art","beauty","art","taste","aesthetic experience"]},{"term":"Affirming the consequent","def":"Reasoning ‘If A then B; B is true; so A is true.’ It is invalid, because something else could have caused B.","example":"‘If it rained, the grass is wet. The grass is wet. So it rained.’ (A sprinkler could have done it.)","related":["Fallacy","Valid"],"more":"In 'If A then B', A is called the antecedent and B the consequent. Affirming the consequent wrongly treats the if-then claim as if it ran both ways. It is the invalid look-alike of a valid form called modus ponens ('If A then B; A; so B'). It often appears when a theory predicts something and the prediction comes true: that supports the theory but does not prove it.","etym":"From 'consequent', Latin consequens, 'following', the second part of an if-then statement.","notConfused":"Not the same as modus ponens: 'If A then B; A; so B' is valid, while 'If A then B; B; so A' is not.","field":"Logic and argument","thinkers":[],"tags":["converse error","fallacy of the converse","formal fallacy","invalid inference"]},{"term":"Agnosticism","def":"The view that we do not, or cannot, know whether God exists.","more":"The word was coined by T. H. Huxley in 1869. Some agnostics say the question is unanswerable in principle; others say only that the evidence is not yet decisive. Philosophers note that agnosticism is about knowledge, so a person could be an agnostic who believes in God, or one who does not.","etym":"Greek a-, 'not', and gnōstos, 'known'.","example":"Someone who says, 'I cannot see how anyone could settle whether there is a God, so I suspend judgement.'","notConfused":"Not the same as atheism: atheism denies or lacks belief in God, while agnosticism is a claim about what can be known.","field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Skepticism","Deism"],"thinkers":["huxley"],"tags":["agnostic","unknowability of god","religious doubt","suspension of belief","huxley"]},{"term":"Ahimsa","def":"Non-violence: the principle of not harming living beings, central to Jainism and important in Hinduism and Buddhism.","more":"Jain ethics takes ahimsa further than any other tradition: some Jain monks sweep the path before them to avoid crushing insects. In Hinduism it appears among the basic ethical restraints in the Yoga Sutras. In the 20th century Gandhi made ahimsa the basis of non-violent political resistance.","etym":"Sanskrit a-, 'not', and hiṃsā, 'injury'.","example":"Choosing a vegetarian diet so that no animal is killed for your meal.","notConfused":"Not the same as passivity: ahimsa can involve active, even courageous, resistance without violence.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Karma","Dharma","Anekantavada"],"thinkers":["mahavira","patanjali"],"tags":["non-violence","nonviolence","non-harming","harmlessness","gandhi","jainism"]},{"term":"Akrasia","def":"Weakness of will: knowingly acting against what you judge to be best.","more":"In Plato's 'Protagoras', Socrates argues that akrasia is impossible: if you truly knew what was best, you would do it, so apparent cases are really ignorance. Aristotle replied that the knowledge can be present but not active, for example clouded by desire. Modern philosophers such as Donald Davidson asked how an action can be intentional yet against one's own best judgement.","etym":"Greek akrasia, 'lack of control' (a- 'not' + kratos 'power').","example":"You decide to go to bed early before an exam, then watch three more episodes anyway.","notConfused":"Not the same as changing your mind: in akrasia you still think the other choice is better while you act.","field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue","Free will","Arete"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato","aristotle"],"tags":["weakness of will","incontinence","acrasia","lack of self-control","akrasia problem"]},{"term":"Alienation","def":"Being cut off or estranged from something that is properly yours. For Marx, workers become alienated from their work, its products, and each other.","example":"A factory worker who spends all day on one tiny task and never sees the finished product.","related":["Dialectic"],"thinkers":["marx","hegel"],"more":"Hegel used the idea for the way the mind can find its own products standing over against it as something foreign. Marx, in his early manuscripts of 1844, applied it to wage labour: workers do not control what they make or how they make it. The concept has since been used in sociology, psychology and existentialism for many kinds of estrangement.","etym":"Latin alienus, 'belonging to another'; renders the German Entfremdung.","notConfused":"Not the same as simple loneliness: alienation means being separated from something that should be part of your own life or activity.","field":"Politics and society","tags":["estrangement","entfremdung","marx","alienated labour","estranged labor"]},{"term":"Allegory of the cave","def":"Plato's story of prisoners who see only shadows on a cave wall and take them for reality, until one escapes into the sunlight.","more":"It appears in Book VII of the 'Republic'. The shadows stand for the world of the senses, and the sunlit world for the Forms, with the sun representing the Form of the Good. The freed prisoner who returns is mocked, which many readers link to the fate of Socrates. The story is also about education as turning the whole soul towards the light.","etym":"'Allegory' is from Greek allēgoria, 'speaking otherwise'.","example":"Someone who has only seen a city through social media posts, then visits and realises the posts were a thin shadow of the real place.","notConfused":null,"field":"History of philosophy","related":["Forms","Platonism","Anamnesis"],"thinkers":["plato"],"tags":["plato's cave","cave allegory","myth of the cave","republic","shadows on the wall"]},{"term":"Altruism","def":"Acting for the good of others, not for your own benefit.","more":"The word was coined by Auguste Comte in the 1800s as the opposite of egoism. Psychological egoists claim that true altruism never occurs, since helping others always serves some hidden self-interest; most philosophers think this claim is either false or made true only by stretching the meaning of 'self-interest'. Evolutionary biologists ask how altruistic behaviour could have evolved.","etym":"French altruisme, from Latin alter, 'other'.","example":"Giving blood anonymously to a stranger you will never meet.","notConfused":"Not the same as self-sacrifice: altruism need not cost you anything, it only needs to aim at others' good.","field":"Ethics","related":["Egoism","Utilitarianism","Care ethics"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["selflessness","unselfishness","benevolence","concern for others","helping others"]},{"term":"Analytic philosophy","def":"A style of philosophy, dominant in English-speaking universities, that stresses clear argument, logic and the careful analysis of concepts and language.","more":"It is usually traced to Frege, Russell, Moore and the early Wittgenstein around 1900. Early analytic philosophers hoped logic and language analysis would dissolve old problems. Today it is less a doctrine than a style, and its topics range across all of philosophy. The contrast with 'continental philosophy' is useful but rough, and many philosophers work across both.","etym":null,"example":"Asking 'what exactly do we mean by knowledge?' and testing each proposed definition against tricky cases.","notConfused":"Not the same as the analytic/synthetic distinction: that is a distinction between kinds of statement, not a school of philosophy.","field":"Method","related":["Continental philosophy","Conceptual analysis","Logic"],"thinkers":["frege","russell","moore"],"tags":["analytical philosophy","anglo-american philosophy","linguistic philosophy","logical analysis","russell","frege"]},{"term":"Analytic/synthetic","def":"An analytic statement is true just because of what its words mean; a synthetic one says something about the world beyond the words.","example":"‘All bachelors are unmarried’ is analytic. ‘Some bachelors own cats’ is synthetic.","related":["A priori","Necessary/contingent"],"thinkers":["kant","quine"],"more":"Kant made the distinction famous and asked a key question: can there be synthetic truths known a priori? He thought arithmetic and the principle that every event has a cause were examples. The logical positivists later said all a priori truths are analytic. In 'Two Dogmas of Empiricism' (1951) Quine argued that no clear line can be drawn between the two kinds, a challenge that is still debated.","etym":"Greek analysis, 'a loosening up', and synthesis, 'a putting together'.","notConfused":"Not the same as a priori/a posteriori: analytic/synthetic is about what makes a statement true, a priori/a posteriori is about how we know it.","field":"Language","tags":["analytic-synthetic distinction","analytic truth","synthetic truth","true by definition","quine","two dogmas"]},{"term":"Anamnesis","def":"Plato's theory that learning is really recollection: the soul knew the truth before birth and is reminded of it.","more":"In the 'Meno', Socrates questions an enslaved boy with no training in geometry and leads him to work out how to double a square, which Socrates takes as recollection. In the 'Phaedo' the theory supports the soul's immortality. It is an early form of the idea that some knowledge is not learned from the senses.","etym":"Greek anamnēsis, 'remembering'.","example":"Struggling with a maths puzzle until the answer suddenly feels as if you had always known it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["A priori","Rationalism","Forms"],"thinkers":["plato","socrates"],"tags":["recollection","theory of recollection","plato","innate knowledge","meno"]},{"term":"Anarchism","def":"The view that the state is unjustified and that society should be organised by voluntary cooperation instead of coercive government.","more":"Proudhon was the first to call himself an anarchist, in 1840. Social anarchists such as Kropotkin stressed mutual aid, while individualist and market anarchists stress personal liberty and property. Robert Paul Wolff argued that respecting your own moral autonomy is incompatible with accepting any state's authority. Critics doubt that order and rights could be secured without a state.","etym":"Greek anarchia, 'without a ruler'.","example":"A neighbourhood that runs its own food co-op and settles disputes through community meetings, with no outside authority.","notConfused":"Not the same as chaos: most anarchists want a highly organised society, only without a state.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Legitimacy","Social contract","Autonomy"],"thinkers":["proudhon","kropotkin","rp_wolff"],"tags":["anarchy","anarchist","statelessness","anti-statism","proudhon","bakunin"]},{"term":"Anatta (no-self)","def":"The Buddhist teaching that there is no permanent, unchanging self: a person is a changing stream of body, feelings, perceptions and thoughts.","example":"Like a river: the name stays the same, but the water is always new.","related":["Dukkha","Personal identity","Atman"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna","hume"],"compass":"q7","more":"Anatta is one of the three marks of existence in Buddhism, alongside impermanence and dukkha. Buddhist thinkers argue that when we look for a self among our body, feelings, perceptions, mental formations and consciousness, we find only changing processes. Some Western philosophers note a likeness to Hume's 'bundle' view of the self and to Parfit's view of personal identity.","etym":"Pali anattā (Sanskrit anātman), 'not-self'.","notConfused":"Not the same as saying you do not exist at all: Buddhism accepts persons conventionally but denies an unchanging inner core.","field":"Asian traditions","tags":["anatman","anatta","no-self","non-self","selflessness","no soul"]},{"term":"Anekantavada","def":"The Jain doctrine of 'many-sidedness': reality is complex, so any single viewpoint captures only part of the truth.","more":"Jains illustrate it with the parable of blind men touching different parts of an elephant, each sure it is like a rope, a wall or a pillar. A related method, syadvada, qualifies every claim with 'in some respect'. The doctrine encourages intellectual humility and tolerance and is linked to ahimsa in thought.","etym":"Sanskrit an-, 'not', eka, 'one', anta, 'end, aspect': 'not one-sided'.","example":"A cup is 'full' from one point of view (of air) and 'empty' from another (of water).","notConfused":"Not the same as relativism: Jains hold there is one complex reality, which partial views each describe in part.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Ahimsa","Relativism","Dialectic"],"thinkers":["mahavira","umasvati"],"tags":["many-sidedness","non-absolutism","jain pluralism","syadvada","perspectivism"]},{"term":"Angst","def":"In existentialism, a deep anxiety that comes from facing your own freedom and the lack of fixed meaning, not from any particular threat.","more":"Kierkegaard described anxiety as 'the dizziness of freedom', felt when we see that we could choose anything. Heidegger thought it reveals our situation as beings who will die. Sartre linked it to the realisation that we alone are responsible for what we make of ourselves.","etym":"German Angst, 'fear, anxiety'; Danish angest in Kierkegaard.","example":"Standing at a cliff edge and feeling not only fear of falling but unease that nothing stops you from jumping.","notConfused":"Not the same as fear: fear has an object (a dog, an exam), while angst is about your existence and freedom as a whole.","field":"Ethics","related":["Existentialism","Authenticity","Absurd"],"thinkers":["kierkegaard","heidegger","sartre"],"tags":["anxiety","dread","existential anxiety","anguish","kierkegaard","heidegger"]},{"term":"Animalism","def":"The view that each of us is a human animal, so we persist as long as that living organism does.","example":"On animalism, you were once a fetus, even though you have no memories of it.","related":["Personal identity","Psychological continuity"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen"],"compass":"q10","more":"Animalists say the question 'what am I?' has a simple answer: an organism. This lets them avoid puzzles that face memory-based theories, but leads to striking claims, for example that you would not go wherever your brain went in a brain transplant. Eric Olson and Peter van Inwagen are leading defenders. Critics answer that what matters to us in survival is our psychology, not our biology.","etym":"Latin animal, 'living being', from anima, 'breath, soul'.","notConfused":"Not the same as animal rights or animism: animalism is only a theory of what persons are.","field":"Mind","tags":["biological view of personal identity","we are animals","organism view","personal identity","olson"]},{"term":"Antinomy","def":"A pair of contradictory claims that each seem to be proved by equally good reasoning.","more":"Kant, in the 'Critique of Pure Reason' (1781), set out four antinomies, such as the claims that the world has a beginning in time and that it does not. He argued they arise when reason applies its concepts beyond any possible experience. The word is also used for paradoxes in logic, such as Russell's paradox about the set of all sets that are not members of themselves.","etym":"Greek antinomia, 'conflict of laws'.","example":"One argument shows the universe must have had a first moment; another, equally convincing, shows there could be no first moment.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Paradox","Contradiction","Dialectic"],"thinkers":["kant","russell"],"tags":["antinomies","contradiction","kant's antinomies","paradox","antinomies of pure reason"]},{"term":"Anti-realism","def":"The view that some kind of fact (often moral facts) does not exist independently of our minds, attitudes or practices.","example":"A moral anti-realist may say wrongness is something we project onto the world, like disgust.","related":["Moral realism","Expressivism","Error theory","Relativism"],"thinkers":["mackie","street"],"compass":"q4","more":"The term is used in many areas. In ethics it covers expressivism, error theory and many forms of relativism. In the philosophy of science it covers views such as instrumentalism, which treat theories as useful tools rather than literal descriptions. In logic and language, Michael Dummett used it for the view that truth cannot outrun what we could in principle verify.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as believing nothing exists: an anti-realist about morality can be a realist about tables and atoms.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["antirealism","non-realism","irrealism","dummett","realism debate"]},{"term":"Apatheia","def":"The Stoic ideal of freedom from destructive passions such as fear, anger and greed.","more":"The Stoics thought such passions rest on false judgements, for example that losing money is truly bad. The wise person still has 'good feelings', such as joy and caution, based on correct judgements. Apatheia was meant as inner freedom and steadiness, not coldness.","etym":"Greek apatheia, 'without passion' (a- 'not' + pathos 'suffering, passion').","example":"Missing your train and calmly catching the next one instead of fuming, because the delay is not truly harmful.","notConfused":"Not the same as apathy (not caring): a Stoic cares a great deal about acting well.","field":"Ethics","related":["Stoicism","Ataraxia","Virtue"],"thinkers":["zeno_citium","epictetus","marcus_aurelius"],"tags":["apathy","freedom from passion","equanimity","stoic calm","dispassion","stoicism"]},{"term":"Aporia","def":"A state of puzzlement, when careful questioning shows that you do not know what you thought you knew.","more":"Many of Plato's early dialogues end in aporia: Socrates and his partner fail to find a definition of courage or piety. Socrates treated this as progress, since recognising ignorance is the first step to learning. Aristotle began inquiries by setting out the aporiai, the puzzles a subject raises.","etym":"Greek aporia, 'no way through' (a- 'not' + poros 'passage').","example":"After an hour trying to define 'fairness', you realise every definition you suggest fails.","notConfused":null,"field":"Method","related":["Dialectic","Socratic method","Skepticism"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato","aristotle"],"tags":["puzzlement","impasse","perplexity","aporetic","socratic dialogue"]},{"term":"A posteriori","def":"Knowable only through experience or observation.","example":"You can only know that it is raining in Paris by looking or being told.","related":["A priori","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["hume","locke"],"compass":"q13","more":"Most of our knowledge, from history to chemistry, is a posteriori: it rests on observation, testimony or experiment. Empiricists such as Locke and Hume held that all substantial knowledge of the world is of this kind. A posteriori claims can always, in principle, be overturned by further experience.","etym":"Latin, 'from what comes after', that is, after experience.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'after the fact' reasoning in everyday speech: in philosophy it simply means justified by experience.","field":"Knowledge","tags":["aposteriori","empirical knowledge","knowledge from experience","posterior","a priori"]},{"term":"Appeal to authority","def":"Treating a claim as true just because someone important said it, especially when they are not an expert on the topic.","example":"‘A famous footballer says this vitamin cures colds, so it must.’","related":["Fallacy","Justification"],"more":"Relying on experts is often perfectly reasonable: most of what we know comes from trusted sources. The fallacy occurs when the authority is not an expert in the relevant area, when experts widely disagree, or when the authority's word is treated as settling the matter regardless of evidence. Good reasoning asks why the authority believes what they say.","etym":"From the Latin label argumentum ad verecundiam, 'argument to respect (or modesty)', a name used by Locke.","notConfused":"Not the same as citing experts: quoting a qualified scientist on their own field is usually good evidence, not a fallacy.","field":"Logic and argument","thinkers":[],"tags":["argument from authority","argumentum ad verecundiam","ad verecundiam","expert opinion","fallacy"]},{"term":"Appeal to ignorance","def":"Arguing that a claim is true because it has not been proved false, or false because it has not been proved true.","more":"Lack of evidence is sometimes informative: if a thorough search finds no elephant in the room, there probably is none. The fallacy lies in treating mere absence of proof as proof, especially when nobody has looked carefully. It is closely tied to questions about who carries the burden of proof.","etym":"Latin label argumentum ad ignorantiam.","example":"'No one has proved ghosts don't exist, so they must exist.'","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Burden of proof","Fallacy","Justification"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["argument from ignorance","argumentum ad ignorantiam","ad ignorantiam","absence of evidence","burden of proof"]},{"term":"Appeal to nature","def":"Arguing that something is good because it is natural, or bad because it is unnatural.","more":"'Natural' is vague: diseases and poisons are natural, while medicine and glasses are not. Mill's essay 'Nature' argued that nature cannot be a moral guide, since it does terrible things without remorse. The fallacy is related to, but not the same as, Moore's 'naturalistic fallacy'.","etym":null,"example":"'This herbal remedy is natural, so it must be safe.'","notConfused":"Not the same as the naturalistic fallacy: that is Moore's claim that goodness cannot be defined as any natural property.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Naturalistic fallacy","Fallacy","Is–ought gap"],"thinkers":["mill"],"tags":["argument from nature","natural is good","naturalistic fallacy","unnatural","fallacy"]},{"term":"A priori","def":"Knowable by thinking alone, without needing to check the world.","example":"You know that every triangle has three sides without measuring any triangles.","related":["A posteriori","Rationalism","Analytic/synthetic"],"thinkers":["kant","leibniz"],"compass":"q13","more":"Mathematics and logic are standard examples of a priori knowledge. You may need experience to learn the concepts, but once you have them, no further observation is needed to see the truth. A central debate is whether any a priori knowledge is about the world rather than just about meanings: rationalists and Kant say yes, many empiricists say no. Kripke argued that a priori and necessary are different ideas that need not coincide.","etym":"Latin, 'from what comes before', that is, prior to experience.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'innate': a priori concerns how a belief is justified, not whether you were born with it.","field":"Knowledge","tags":["apriori","prior to experience","independent of experience","rational knowledge","a posteriori"]},{"term":"Arete","def":"Excellence: the quality that makes a thing good at what it is for. For a person, it is excellence of character and mind.","more":"In ancient Greek, a knife or a horse could have arete. Plato and Aristotle used the word for human virtues such as courage, justice and wisdom. Aristotle argued that living with arete is the core of eudaimonia, a flourishing life.","etym":"Greek aretē, 'excellence, goodness'.","example":"A great teacher's arete is to make students understand; a great knife's is to cut cleanly.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'virtue' in the narrow modern sense of sexual or moral purity: arete covers any excellence.","field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue","Eudaimonia","Telos"],"thinkers":["aristotle","plato"],"tags":["areté","excellence","virtue","greek virtue","aristotle"]},{"term":"Argument","def":"A set of claims (premises) offered as reasons to accept another claim (the conclusion). In philosophy an argument is not a quarrel.","example":"‘It is raining. If it is raining, the game is cancelled. So the game is cancelled.’","related":["Premise","Valid","Sound"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"more":"Philosophers judge an argument in two ways: does the conclusion follow from the premises (validity), and are the premises actually true (soundness)? Deductive arguments aim to make the conclusion certain if the premises are true; inductive ones only make it probable. Laying an argument out step by step makes it easier to see exactly where you disagree.","etym":"Latin argumentum, 'proof, evidence', from arguere, 'to make clear'.","notConfused":"Not the same as a quarrel: a philosophical argument is a chain of reasons, and it can be calm and written by one person.","field":"Logic and argument","tags":["reasoning","premises and conclusion","inference","case","proof"]},{"term":"Argument from analogy","def":"Reasoning that because two things are alike in some known ways, they are probably alike in a further way too.","more":"Such arguments are only as strong as the similarities are relevant. Mill offered a famous one for other minds: others have bodies and behaviour like mine, so they probably have feelings like mine. Hume criticised the design argument as a weak analogy between the universe and a human-made machine.","etym":"Greek analogia, 'proportion'.","example":"This new restaurant is run by the same chef as my favourite one, so it will probably be good too.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Design argument","Induction","Abduction"],"thinkers":["mill","hume"],"tags":["analogical argument","analogy","analogical reasoning","comparison"]},{"term":"Argument from illusion","def":"An argument that, since illusions and hallucinations can look just like real perception, what we directly perceive is never the physical object itself.","more":"A straight stick in water looks bent; the bent shape is not in the stick, so, the argument says, we must be seeing a mental image. Ayer discussed it at length. Defenders of direct realism, and philosophers such as J. L. Austin, argue that the step from 'things can look different' to 'we only see images' does not follow.","etym":null,"example":"A straight straw in a glass of water looks bent, though the straw has not changed.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Direct realism","Skepticism","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["ayer","berkeley"],"tags":["illusion","perceptual illusion","sense data","bent stick","perception"]},{"term":"Artworld","def":"Arthur Danto's term for the theories, history and institutions that let something count as art.","more":"Danto (1964) asked why Andy Warhol's 'Brillo Box' was art while identical boxes in a shop were not; his answer was that seeing it as art requires a background of art theory. George Dickie built on this in his 'institutional theory': something is art if it is offered for appreciation within the artworld. Critics ask whether this makes art status depend too much on insiders.","etym":null,"example":"A urinal displayed in a gallery, as in Duchamp's 'Fountain' (1917), being treated as an artwork.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Beauty","Disinterestedness"],"thinkers":["danto","dickie"],"tags":["art world","danto","institutional theory of art","what is art"]},{"term":"Ataraxia","def":"Tranquillity: a calm mind, free from worry and disturbance.","more":"For Epicurus, ataraxia together with freedom from bodily pain was the highest pleasure, reached by simple living, friendship and removing fear of the gods and death. The Pyrrhonian sceptics claimed it follows, unexpectedly, from suspending judgement on disputed questions. In both schools, philosophy was a kind of therapy for the soul.","etym":"Greek ataraxia, 'not being troubled' (a- 'not' + tarassein 'to disturb').","example":"Feeling settled after realising a worry was about something you could neither control nor need.","notConfused":"Not the same as apatheia: ataraxia is the Epicurean and sceptical ideal of calm, apatheia the Stoic freedom from passions.","field":"Ethics","related":["Epicureanism","Apatheia","Skepticism"],"thinkers":["epicurus","pyrrho","sextus"],"tags":["tranquility","tranquillity","peace of mind","imperturbability","epicureanism","serenity"]},{"term":"Atheism","def":"The view that there is no God or gods; more loosely, lacking belief in any god.","more":"Philosophical atheism usually rests on arguments such as the problem of evil, or on the claim that there is no good evidence for God. Some writers distinguish 'strong' atheism, which asserts there is no God, from 'weak' atheism, which simply lacks belief. J. L. Mackie's 'The Miracle of Theism' (1982) is a well-known defence.","etym":"Greek atheos, 'without god'.","example":"Someone who finds the arguments for God unconvincing and the problem of evil decisive, and so concludes there is no God.","notConfused":"Not the same as agnosticism: an agnostic says we cannot know; an atheist denies (or lacks) the belief.","field":"Religion","related":["Agnosticism","Problem of evil","Deism"],"thinkers":["mackie","russell"],"tags":["atheist","disbelief in god","nonbelief","godlessness","irreligion"]},{"term":"Atman","def":"In Hindu philosophy, the true inner self. In Advaita Vedanta, atman is ultimately identical with Brahman.","example":"The witness behind all your changing thoughts and moods.","related":["Brahman","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["shankara"],"compass":"q7","more":"The Upanishads teach that knowing atman is the key to liberation. Different schools of Vedanta understand it differently: Advaita says atman and Brahman are identical, while Ramanuja and Madhva keep some distinction between the self and the divine. Buddhism's teaching of anatta was largely a denial of atman.","etym":"Sanskrit ātman, 'breath, self'.","notConfused":"Not the same as the personality or ego: atman is the pure witnessing consciousness behind the changing mind.","field":"Asian traditions","tags":["self","soul","true self","brahman","hinduism","inner self"]},{"term":"Atomism","def":"The ancient theory that everything is made of tiny, indivisible particles moving through empty space.","more":"Leucippus and Democritus proposed it in the 5th century BCE, partly to answer Parmenides' arguments against change. Epicurus and Lucretius used it to free people from fear of the gods. Modern atoms turned out to be divisible, but the idea that the world is built from basic particles remains central to physics.","etym":"Greek atomos, 'uncuttable'.","example":"Building a castle out of Lego: lots of tiny identical pieces, arranged differently, make very different things.","notConfused":"Not the same as modern atomic theory: ancient atomism was a philosophical argument, not based on experiments.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Materialism","Physicalism","Epicureanism"],"thinkers":["democritus","epicurus","lucretius"],"tags":["atoms","atomist","democritus","leucippus","indivisible particles","epicurus"]},{"term":"Authenticity","def":"Living in a way that is truly your own, owning your choices instead of hiding behind convention or what 'one' does.","more":"Heidegger contrasted authentic existence with losing oneself in 'the they' (das Man). Sartre linked authenticity with facing one's freedom instead of retreating into bad faith. Charles Taylor and others warn that the ideal can slide into self-absorption, and argue it needs shared values in the background.","etym":"Greek authentikos, 'original, genuine'.","example":"Choosing a career because you value it, not because it is what your family or friends expect.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Bad faith","Existentialism","Angst"],"thinkers":["heidegger","sartre","kierkegaard"],"tags":["authentic","being true to oneself","self-realisation","self-realization","existentialism","genuineness"]},{"term":"Autonomy","def":"Self-rule: the capacity to govern yourself by your own reasons and choices.","more":"For Kant, autonomy means giving the moral law to yourself through reason, and it is the basis of human dignity. In medical ethics, respect for autonomy grounds informed consent. Feminist philosophers have developed 'relational autonomy', stressing that we become self-governing through our relationships, not in isolation.","etym":"Greek autos, 'self', and nomos, 'law'.","example":"A patient deciding, after hearing the risks, whether to have an operation.","notConfused":"Not the same as independence or doing whatever you like: Kantian autonomy means following rules you can rationally endorse.","field":"Ethics","related":["Categorical imperative","Paternalism","Free will"],"thinkers":["kant","mill"],"tags":["self-governance","self-determination","self-rule","independence","kant","freedom"]},{"term":"Axiom","def":"A basic statement accepted without proof, used as a starting point for proving other things.","more":"Euclid's geometry is the classic example of a system built from axioms. In ancient thought axioms were seen as self-evident truths; in modern logic and mathematics they are often just chosen starting assumptions. Gödel showed in 1931 that any consistent axiom system strong enough for basic arithmetic leaves some true statements unprovable.","etym":"Greek axiōma, 'what is thought worthy'.","example":"'Between any two points you can draw a straight line' is one of Euclid's starting assumptions.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Deduction","Premise","Logic"],"thinkers":["godel","hilbert"],"tags":["postulate","first principle","self-evident truth","assumption","axiomatic"]},{"term":"Bad faith","def":"Sartre’s term for lying to yourself about your freedom, pretending you have no choice.","example":"‘I had to cheat: everyone does,’ said by someone who could have chosen otherwise.","related":["Existentialism","Existence precedes essence"],"thinkers":["sartre","beauvoir"],"compass":"q21","more":"In 'Being and Nothingness' (1943) Sartre describes a waiter who plays his role too perfectly, as if he were nothing but a waiter. Bad faith is puzzling because it seems to require both knowing and not knowing the same truth. Beauvoir applied related ideas to how women can be pressed into accepting a fixed role. The opposite ideal is often called authenticity.","etym":"Translates the French mauvaise foi.","notConfused":"Not the same as ordinary dishonesty to others: bad faith is self-deception about one's own freedom.","field":"Ethics","tags":["mauvaise foi","self-deception","sartre","inauthenticity","denial of freedom"]},{"term":"Bayesianism","def":"The view that beliefs come in degrees, and that rational people update them using the rules of probability, especially Bayes' theorem.","more":"Bayes' theorem, from Thomas Bayes in the 1700s, says how much new evidence should shift your confidence, given how likely the evidence was under different hypotheses. Bayesians use it to explain scientific confirmation and everyday learning. Critics ask where the starting degrees of belief ('priors') come from, and whether they are too subjective.","etym":"Named after Thomas Bayes (c. 1701–1761).","example":"A rare disease test comes back positive, but since the disease is very rare, the chance you actually have it may still be low.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Induction","Abduction","Justification"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["bayes","bayes' theorem","bayesian probability","credence","subjective probability","probability"]},{"term":"Beauty","def":"The quality that makes something pleasing to perceive or contemplate, in nature, art, people or ideas.","more":"Plato treated beauty as an objective Form we can climb towards, as in the 'Symposium'. Hume and Kant gave more subject-centred accounts, but both thought judgements of beauty could be more or less correct. Twentieth-century art often turned away from beauty, and philosophers asked whether art needs to be beautiful at all.","etym":"Old French biauté, from Latin bellus, 'pretty'.","example":"A mathematician calling an elegant proof 'beautiful' uses the word just as a painter might for a sunset.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Disinterestedness","Forms"],"thinkers":["plato","hume","kant"],"tags":["beautiful","aesthetic value","attractiveness","aesthetics","taste"]},{"term":"Beetle in a box","def":"Wittgenstein's thought experiment: if everyone had a box with a 'beetle' that only they could see, the word 'beetle' would get its meaning from public use, not from what is in the boxes.","more":"It appears in the 'Philosophical Investigations' (1953). The target is the idea that words for sensations, such as 'pain', get their meaning by naming a private inner object. Wittgenstein suggests the inner object 'drops out' as irrelevant to the language game. It is part of his wider 'private language argument'.","etym":null,"example":"Two people both call a wine 'fruity'; you cannot check their inner tastes, yet the word still works between them.","notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Language game","Qualia","Private language"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"tags":["beetle in the box","wittgenstein","private language","private experience","inner experience"]},{"term":"Begging the question","def":"Assuming the very thing you are trying to prove. The argument goes in a circle. (It does not mean ‘raising the question’.)","example":"‘This book is trustworthy because the book itself says so.’","related":["Fallacy","Justification"],"more":"The argument may look fine on the surface, but one of its premises already assumes the conclusion, often in different words. Such an argument can even be valid, yet it gives no new reason to someone who doubts the conclusion. Philosophers sometimes accuse whole positions of begging the question, for example an argument for trusting the senses that relies on what the senses report.","etym":"A translation of the medieval Latin petitio principii, 'assuming the starting point'.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'raising the question': in philosophy, begging the question means assuming what you set out to prove.","field":"Logic and argument","thinkers":[],"tags":["circular reasoning","petitio principii","circular argument","assuming the conclusion","circularity"]},{"term":"Behaviorism","def":"In philosophy, the view that talk about the mind is really talk about behaviour and tendencies to behave.","example":"On this view, ‘she is thirsty’ means roughly ‘she would drink water if offered it’.","related":["Functionalism","Language game"],"compass":"q6","more":"Philosophical (or logical) behaviourism, associated with Gilbert Ryle and in some readings Wittgenstein, tried to avoid treating the mind as a hidden inner thing. It faced strong objections: a perfect actor could behave as if in pain without feeling it, and beliefs and desires only produce behaviour in combination with each other. Functionalism grew partly out of these criticisms.","etym":"From 'behaviour'; the term was coined by the psychologist John B. Watson in 1913.","notConfused":"Not the same as psychological behaviourism: that is a method in psychology (study only observable behaviour), while the philosophical view is a theory of what mental words mean.","field":"Mind","thinkers":[],"tags":["behaviourism","logical behaviorism","skinner","ryle","behavior","dispositions"]},{"term":"Belief","def":"Taking something to be true. In philosophy, it is the attitude you have towards a claim when you accept it.","more":"Knowledge is traditionally analysed as justified true belief, so belief is one of its building blocks. Philosophers debate whether belief is all-or-nothing or comes in degrees, whether we can choose what to believe, and whether belief is a hidden mental state or a pattern of behaviour. Clifford argued it is always wrong to believe anything on insufficient evidence; William James disagreed.","etym":"Old English gelēafa, related to 'love' and 'leave' (to hold dear).","example":"You believe the shop is open until 8 pm, which is why you set off at 7:30.","notConfused":"Not the same as faith: a belief can rest entirely on evidence, as with believing the sun will rise.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Justification","Gettier problem","Bayesianism"],"thinkers":["clifford","james"],"tags":["believing","opinion","conviction","credence","doxa","propositional attitude"]},{"term":"Übermensch","def":"Nietzsche’s ‘overman’ or ‘superman’: an ideal of a person who creates their own values after traditional ones lose their hold.","more":"Nietzsche introduced the figure in Thus Spoke Zarathustra (1883–85) as a goal for humanity after the 'death of God'. Scholars disagree about whether it describes a real type of person or an ideal to strive towards. Nazi propaganda distorted it into a racial idea, which scholars widely regard as a misreading.","etym":"German über, 'over, beyond', and Mensch, 'human being'.","example":"Someone who does not just follow inherited rules but forges a life-affirming code of their own.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Will to power","Nihilism"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"tags":["ubermensch","overman","superman","beyond-man","nietzsche","zarathustra"]},{"term":"Bivalence","def":"The principle that every statement is either true or false, with no third option.","more":"Aristotle raised a famous worry about statements about the future, such as 'there will be a sea battle tomorrow'. Vague statements, paradoxes and some views of quantum physics also test the principle. Michael Dummett linked rejecting bivalence for some areas with anti-realism about them.","etym":"Latin bi-, 'two', and valentia, 'strength, value'.","example":"'It is raining in Rome now' is either true or false, even if you do not know which.","notConfused":"Not the same as the law of excluded middle ('A or not A'), though the two are closely related.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Vagueness","Contradiction","Anti-realism"],"thinkers":["aristotle","dummett"],"tags":["principle of bivalence","true or false","two truth values","classical logic","excluded middle"]},{"term":"Bodhisattva","def":"In Mahayana Buddhism, someone who seeks enlightenment in order to free all beings from suffering, and is often described as postponing their own final liberation to do so.","more":"The bodhisattva path stresses compassion (karuna) and wisdom (prajna) together, and involves cultivating perfections such as generosity and patience. Mahayana schools present it as the highest ideal, in contrast to an earlier ideal of the arhat, who seeks personal liberation. Figures such as Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) are widely venerated.","etym":"Sanskrit bodhi, 'awakening', and sattva, 'being'.","example":"Someone who reaches the exit of a burning building but turns back to help everyone else out first.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Buddhism","Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Dukkha"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["bodhisatta","enlightened being","compassion","mahayana","buddhahood"]},{"term":"Brahman","def":"In Vedanta, the ultimate reality underlying everything. Advaita teaches that only Brahman is fully real.","example":"Like the ocean beneath the many waves, which are just ocean in different shapes.","related":["Atman"],"thinkers":["shankara"],"more":"The Upanishads describe Brahman in terms such as truth, knowledge and the infinite, and later Vedanta summed it up as being, consciousness and bliss (sat-chit-ananda). For Shankara's Advaita, the world of many separate things is maya, an appearance of Brahman. Other Vedanta schools, such as Ramanuja's, treat Brahman as a personal God and the world as truly real, like God's body.","etym":"Sanskrit brahman, probably from a root meaning 'to grow, expand'.","notConfused":"Not the same as Brahma (the creator god) or Brahmin (the priestly class), though the words are related.","field":"Asian traditions","tags":["ultimate reality","absolute","world soul","hinduism","atman","vedanta"]},{"term":"Brain in a vat","def":"A thought experiment: how do you know you are not just a brain in a vat of nutrients, fed false experiences by a computer?","more":"It is a modern version of Descartes' evil demon and is used to motivate radical scepticism about the external world. Hilary Putnam argued in 1981 that if you were a brain in a vat, your word 'vat' could not refer to real vats, so the sentence 'I am a brain in a vat' could not be truly thought. Not everyone accepts that this answer defeats scepticism.","etym":null,"example":"The film 'The Matrix', where people live a simulated life while their bodies lie in pods.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Skepticism","Cogito","Experience machine"],"thinkers":["putnam","descartes"],"tags":["brain-in-a-vat","bivat","skepticism","matrix","evil demon","putnam"]},{"term":"Buddhism","def":"The religious and philosophical tradition founded by Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, teaching a path to freedom from suffering through ethics, meditation and wisdom.","more":"Its core teachings include the Four Noble Truths, the Eightfold Path, impermanence, dependent origination and no-self. Over centuries it divided into major branches, including Theravada and Mahayana, with philosophers such as Nagarjuna and Vasubandhu developing sophisticated theories of mind and reality. The Buddha's dates are uncertain; many scholars place his death c. 400 BCE.","etym":"From Sanskrit buddha, 'awakened one'.","example":"A meditator noticing an angry thought arise and pass without acting on it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dukkha","Anatta (no-self)","Dependent origination"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna","vasubandhu"],"tags":["buddhist","buddha","dharma","theravada","mahayana","nirvana"]},{"term":"Bundle theory","def":"The view that a thing, or a self, is nothing over and above a bundle of qualities or experiences, with no underlying substance holding them together.","more":"Hume famously said that when he looked inward he never caught a self, only particular perceptions, and called the mind a 'bundle' of them. Bundle theories of objects face a puzzle: two things with exactly the same qualities would seem to be the same thing. Buddhist no-self teaching is often compared with Hume's view.","etym":null,"example":"An apple as nothing but redness, roundness, sweetness and so on, bundled together.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Substance","Anatta (no-self)","Personal identity"],"thinkers":["hume"],"tags":["bundle theory of self","bundle of perceptions","hume","no substance","bundle of properties"]},{"term":"Burden of proof","def":"The obligation, in a dispute, to provide evidence for your claim.","more":"Usually the person making a positive or surprising claim carries the burden. But who carries it is often itself disputed, for example in debates about God, free will or other minds. Shifting the burden unfairly to your opponent is a common debating trick.","etym":"From the Latin legal phrase onus probandi.","example":"If you claim your friend owes you money, it is up to you to show it, not for them to prove they do not.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Appeal to ignorance","Justification","Argument"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["onus probandi","onus of proof","who must prove","appeal to ignorance"]},{"term":"Butterfly dream","def":"Zhuangzi's story of dreaming he was a butterfly, then waking and not knowing whether he was Zhuangzi who had dreamt of being a butterfly, or a butterfly dreaming of being Zhuangzi.","more":"The story closes the second chapter of the 'Zhuangzi'. It is often read as raising sceptical doubt about dreams and waking, like Descartes' dream argument. Many scholars stress a different moral: the transformation of things, and the freedom of not clinging to one fixed identity.","etym":null,"example":"Waking from a vivid dream and, for a moment, being unsure which world is real.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dao","Skepticism","Personal identity"],"thinkers":["zhuangzi"],"tags":["zhuangzi","chuang tzu","zhuangzi's butterfly","dream argument","butterfly"]},{"term":"Cardinal virtues","def":"The four main virtues in classical Western ethics: wisdom (prudence), courage, temperance (self-control) and justice.","more":"Plato sets them out in the 'Republic', matching them to parts of the soul and classes of the city. The Stoics also taught them. Christian thinkers, drawing on St Paul, set beside them the three 'theological' virtues of faith, hope and charity, and Aquinas gave both sets a systematic treatment.","etym":"Latin cardo, 'hinge': the virtues on which the others turn.","example":"A firefighter who keeps calm (temperance), goes in (courage), assesses risks well (wisdom) and helps whoever needs it first (justice).","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue","Virtue ethics","Arete"],"thinkers":["plato","aquinas"],"tags":["four virtues","prudence","justice","temperance","courage","fortitude"]},{"term":"Care ethics","def":"An approach to ethics that puts caring relationships and responsiveness to particular people at the centre of morality.","more":"It grew from Carol Gilligan's psychological research in the 1980s and was developed by Nel Noddings, Virginia Held and Joan Tronto. Care ethicists argue that theories based on impartial rules or general welfare overlook the moral importance of dependence, attention and relationships. Critics worry it may favour those close to us unfairly or reinforce traditional gender roles.","etym":null,"example":"A nurse who notices a patient is frightened and stays a few minutes longer, though no rule requires it.","notConfused":"Not the same as being nice: care ethics is a theory about what morality basically is, with views on politics and justice too.","field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Altruism","Justice"],"thinkers":["noddings","held","tronto"],"tags":["ethics of care","feminist ethics","caring","gilligan","noddings","relational ethics"]},{"term":"Categorical imperative","def":"Kant’s supreme moral rule. One version: act only on principles you could will everyone to follow. Another: treat people always as ends, never merely as means. Unlike a hypothetical imperative (‘If you want X, do Y’), it binds you whatever you want.","example":"Lying to get a loan fails the test: if everyone did it, nobody would believe promises to repay.","related":["Deontology"],"thinkers":["kant"],"compass":"q1","more":"Kant presents the idea in his 'Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals' (1785) and gives several formulations that he thought were equivalent. The Formula of Universal Law tests a plan by asking whether it could become a rule for everyone without contradiction. The Formula of Humanity forbids using people merely as tools. Critics argue the test can give odd results and struggles with cases such as lying to a murderer at the door.","etym":"Late Latin categoricus, from Greek katēgorikos, 'affirmative, predicative' (in logic a 'categorical' statement holds without conditions), and Latin imperare, 'to command'.","notConfused":"Not the same as the Golden Rule: the Golden Rule depends on what you would like done to you, whereas Kant's test asks whether a rule could hold for every rational agent.","field":"Ethics","tags":["kant","universal law","moral law","humanity formula","deontology","universalisability"]},{"term":"Category mistake","def":"Treating something as belonging to a kind of thing it does not belong to, so that what you say about it makes no sense.","more":"Gilbert Ryle introduced the term in 'The Concept of Mind' (1949). His example is a visitor shown all the colleges and libraries of Oxford who then asks, 'but where is the university?' Ryle argued that Descartes' view of the mind as a hidden thing inside the body is a category mistake of this kind.","etym":null,"example":"'What colour is the number seven?' or 'Is Tuesday asleep?'","notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Dualism","Behaviorism","Conceptual analysis"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["category error","ryle","ghost in the machine","conceptual confusion"]},{"term":"Catharsis","def":"Aristotle's term for the release or purification of emotions such as pity and fear that tragedy brings about in its audience.","more":"Aristotle uses the word in his definition of tragedy in the 'Poetics', but says little about what he means. Scholars disagree whether he meant purging emotions, purifying them, or clarifying them for understanding. The idea offers one answer to the puzzle of why we enjoy painful art.","etym":"Greek katharsis, 'cleansing, purging'.","example":"Feeling lighter after crying at the end of a sad film.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Beauty"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["katharsis","purgation","emotional release","purification","tragedy","aristotle"]},{"term":"Causation","def":"The relation between a cause and its effect. Philosophers ask what it is for one thing to make another happen.","example":"Hume noticed we see one billiard ball hit another and the second move, but never see the ‘making’ itself.","related":["Counterfactual","Determinism","Problem of induction"],"thinkers":["hume","lewis"],"compass":"q18","more":"Hume argued that we never observe a necessary connection between cause and effect, only one kind of event regularly following another. Later theories analyse causation in terms of regularities, counterfactuals ('if the cause had not happened, the effect would not have'), probabilities, or transfers of energy. Causation matters well beyond metaphysics, in science, law and moral blame.","etym":"Latin causa, 'reason, cause'.","notConfused":"Not the same as correlation: two things can rise and fall together because a third factor drives both.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["causality","cause and effect","cause","causal relation","hume"]},{"term":"Chinese room","def":"John Searle's thought experiment: a person following a rulebook to answer Chinese questions could pass for a Chinese speaker without understanding a word, so running a program is not enough for understanding.","more":"Searle presented it in 1980 against 'strong AI', the claim that a suitably programmed computer literally has a mind. The 'systems reply' says the whole room, not the person, understands. Searle answered that the person could memorise the rulebook and still not understand. The argument remains a central debate in philosophy of AI.","etym":null,"example":"Using a phrasebook to give perfect answers in a language you cannot actually speak.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Turing test","Consciousness"],"thinkers":["searle"],"tags":["chinese room argument","searle","strong ai","understanding","artificial intelligence","machine understanding"]},{"term":"Civil disobedience","def":"Publicly and non-violently breaking a law in order to protest against an injustice, while accepting the legal consequences.","more":"Henry David Thoreau's essay (1849), Gandhi's campaigns and Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) are key sources. Rawls discussed it in 'A Theory of Justice' as a way of appealing to a majority's own sense of justice. Philosophers debate whether it must be non-violent, public and accepting of punishment.","etym":null,"example":"Sitting in a segregated section of a restaurant and refusing to leave until arrested.","notConfused":"Not the same as ordinary crime or revolution: it aims to change a law while still accepting the legal system as a whole.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Legitimacy","Justice","Social contract"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"tags":["nonviolent resistance","lawbreaking","protest","thoreau","martin luther king","conscientious objection"]},{"term":"Cogito","def":"Descartes' starting point, 'I think, therefore I am': even if I doubt everything, the fact that I am doubting shows that I exist.","more":"Descartes wrote the phrase in French in the 'Discourse on Method' (1637) as 'je pense, donc je suis', and the Latin 'cogito, ergo sum' in the 'Principles of Philosophy' (1644). He wanted a certain foundation for knowledge that no evil demon could undermine. Critics, including Lichtenberg, objected that he was entitled only to 'thinking is happening', not to an 'I' that thinks.","etym":"Latin cogito, 'I think'.","example":"However convincing a dream or simulation is, you cannot be fooled about the fact that you are having some experience.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Rationalism","Skepticism","Dualism"],"thinkers":["descartes","avicenna","augustine"],"tags":["cogito ergo sum","i think therefore i am","descartes","je pense donc je suis","self-knowledge"]},{"term":"Coherence theory of truth","def":"The view that a belief is true if it fits consistently into a whole system of beliefs.","more":"It was defended by British idealists such as F. H. Bradley and linked to some rationalists. Its main rival is the correspondence theory. A common objection is that a well-written novel can be perfectly coherent but false, so coherence seems to need some link to the world.","etym":"Latin cohaerere, 'to stick together'.","example":"A detective accepts a story because it fits with every clue and every other fact known about the case.","notConfused":"Not the same as coherentism: that is a theory of justification (when beliefs are reasonable), not of truth.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Correspondence theory of truth","Coherentism","Idealism"],"thinkers":["bradley"],"tags":["coherentism","coherence","truth as coherence","theories of truth"]},{"term":"Coherentism","def":"The view that a belief is justified by how well it fits with your other beliefs, not by resting on a foundation of basic, self-evident beliefs.","more":"It answers the regress problem: if every belief needs another to support it, the chain must end somewhere, go round in a circle, or go on forever. Foundationalists end it with basic beliefs; coherentists accept a web of mutual support. Laurence BonJour defended coherentism and later rejected it. The main worry is that a coherent system could still float free of reality.","etym":null,"example":"Your belief that your friend is in Paris is supported by her photos, her messages and her flight booking, which all support each other.","notConfused":"Not the same as the coherence theory of truth: coherentism is about justification, not about what makes beliefs true.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Justification","Foundationalism","Coherence theory of truth"],"thinkers":["bonjour","lehrer"],"tags":["coherence theory of justification","web of belief","coherence","justification","foundationalism"]},{"term":"Communitarianism","def":"The view that people are shaped by their communities and traditions, so political theory should give weight to shared values and the common good, not just individual rights.","more":"It arose in the 1980s as a response to Rawls' liberalism. Michael Sandel argued that Rawls' picture of a self choosing behind a veil of ignorance is too thin. Alasdair MacIntyre and Michael Walzer also stressed traditions and shared meanings. Liberals reply that communities can be oppressive, so individual rights are still needed.","etym":"Latin communitas, 'fellowship'.","example":"Arguing that a town should protect its local festival because it is part of who its people are, not just because individuals enjoy it.","notConfused":"Not the same as communism: communitarians are usually not against private property or markets.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Justice","Veil of ignorance","Conservatism"],"thinkers":["sandel","macintyre","walzer"],"tags":["community","common good","communitarian","sandel","macintyre","taylor"]},{"term":"Compatibilism","def":"The view that free will is compatible with determinism: you act freely when you act from your own reasons and desires, even if those were caused.","example":"You freely ate the cake because you wanted it, even if that want had causes.","related":["Determinism","Free will"],"thinkers":["hume","hobbes","frankfurt","dennett"],"compass":"q8","more":"Compatibilists usually argue that freedom is the absence of compulsion, not the absence of causes. Frankfurt added that you act freely when you act on desires you endorse on reflection. Critics such as Peter van Inwagen reply with the Consequence Argument: if determinism is true, our acts follow from the distant past and the laws of nature, neither of which is up to us. Surveys suggest compatibilism is the most common view among professional philosophers.","etym":"From 'compatible', Latin compati, 'to suffer with, agree'.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'soft determinism' in every respect: soft determinism means compatibilism plus the claim that determinism is actually true.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["soft determinism","compatibilist","free will","determinism","frankfurt"]},{"term":"Conceptual analysis","def":"Breaking a concept down to find its necessary and sufficient conditions, that is, exactly what must be true for something to fall under it.","more":"Much 20th-century analytic philosophy worked this way, testing proposed definitions of knowledge, causation or free action against imagined cases. The Gettier problem is a famous result of the method. Critics, including Quine and later experimental philosophers, question whether concepts have neat definitions at all.","etym":null,"example":"Asking: is 'unmarried adult man' enough for 'bachelor'? What about the Pope, or a man in a long-term partnership?","notConfused":null,"field":"Method","related":["Analytic philosophy","Counterexample","Thought experiment"],"thinkers":["moore","russell"],"tags":["analysis of concepts","definition","necessary and sufficient conditions","analytic method"]},{"term":"Conclusion","def":"The claim that an argument tries to establish, supported by its premises.","more":"Words such as 'so', 'therefore' and 'hence' often signal a conclusion. Finding the conclusion first is the best way to understand any argument. A conclusion in one argument can be used as a premise in another.","etym":"Latin conclusio, 'a shutting up, ending'.","example":"In 'All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; so Socrates is mortal', the conclusion is 'Socrates is mortal'.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Argument","Premise","Valid"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["claim","thesis","inference","result","argument"]},{"term":"Conditional","def":"An 'if...then' statement, such as 'If it rains, the match is off'. The 'if' part is the antecedent; the 'then' part is the consequent.","more":"In standard logic, the 'material conditional' counts as false only when the antecedent is true and the consequent false. This gives odd results: 'If the moon is cheese, then 2+2=4' comes out true. Philosophers such as Dorothy Edgington have argued that everyday conditionals work differently.","etym":"Latin condicio, 'agreement, condition'.","example":"'If you heat ice, it melts.'","notConfused":"Not the same as a cause: 'If it is Monday, then tomorrow is Tuesday' is true, but Monday does not cause Tuesday.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Affirming the consequent","Denying the antecedent","Counterfactual"],"thinkers":["edgington"],"tags":["if-then","if then","material conditional","implication","antecedent","consequent"]},{"term":"Confucianism","def":"The tradition founded on the teachings of Confucius, stressing moral cultivation, family relationships, ritual and humane government.","more":"Its central ideas include ren (humaneness), li (ritual propriety), xiao (filial piety) and the ideal of the junzi, the exemplary person. Mencius argued that human nature is good, while Xunzi argued it needs shaping by education and ritual. Later Neo-Confucians such as Zhu Xi and Wang Yangming added metaphysics and theories of knowledge.","etym":"From Confucius, the Latinised name of Kong Fuzi, 'Master Kong'.","example":"Showing respect to elders and keeping family rituals as a way of becoming a better person.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Ren","Li (ritual propriety)","Dao","De"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius","xunzi"],"tags":["confucius","kongzi","ruism","confucian","ren","li"]},{"term":"Consciousness","def":"Being aware, or having experiences: there is something it is like to be you, to see red or to feel pain.","more":"Philosophers distinguish 'phenomenal' consciousness (what an experience feels like) from 'access' consciousness (information available for thinking and action), a distinction drawn by Ned Block. Thomas Nagel's 1974 paper 'What Is It Like to Be a Bat?' argued that physical science may not capture the subjective point of view. Chalmers called explaining it the 'hard problem'.","etym":"Latin conscius, 'knowing with'.","example":"The difference between you seeing a sunset and a camera recording it.","notConfused":"Not the same as conscience (the sense of right and wrong), though the words share a root.","field":"Mind","related":["Hard problem","Qualia","Dualism"],"thinkers":["nagel","chalmers","block"],"tags":["awareness","subjective experience","phenomenal consciousness","sentience","mind","qualia"]},{"term":"Consequentialism","def":"The view that whether an act is right depends only on its consequences, the good and bad it produces.","example":"A consequentialist would lie to protect a friend if lying led to the best outcome overall.","related":["Utilitarianism","Deontology"],"thinkers":["bentham","mill","singer"],"compass":"q1","more":"Consequentialism is a family of theories that differ over which consequences count: pleasure, preference satisfaction, wellbeing, or other goods. Its strength is a simple idea: it is hard to see why you should ever make things worse. Its critics, including Bernard Williams, object that it can require terrible acts if they produce slightly better outcomes and leaves too little room for personal projects. The term itself was introduced by Elizabeth Anscombe in 1958.","etym":"From 'consequence', Latin consequi, 'to follow'.","notConfused":"Not the same as utilitarianism: utilitarianism is one kind of consequentialism, the kind that measures outcomes by total wellbeing.","field":"Ethics","tags":["consequences","outcome-based ethics","teleological ethics","utilitarianism","results"]},{"term":"Conservatism","def":"A political outlook that values tradition, gradual change and inherited institutions, and distrusts plans to remake society from scratch.","more":"Edmund Burke's 'Reflections on the Revolution in France' (1790) is a founding text: he argued that institutions hold practical wisdom no single generation can design. Michael Oakeshott criticised 'rationalism in politics'. Critics reply that traditions can preserve injustice, and conservatives disagree among themselves about markets and the state.","etym":"Latin conservare, 'to preserve'.","example":"Preferring to reform an old institution step by step instead of abolishing it and starting again.","notConfused":"Not the same as any particular party: philosophical conservatism is an outlook, and parties with the name vary widely.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Liberty (negative/positive)","Social contract"],"thinkers":["burke","oakeshott","scruton"],"tags":["conservative","tradition","burke","traditionalism","gradual reform"]},{"term":"Continental philosophy","def":"A loose name for traditions of philosophy from mainland Europe since the 1800s, such as phenomenology, existentialism, critical theory and post-structuralism.","more":"It is often linked with history, culture and lived experience more than with formal logic. Key figures include Husserl, Heidegger, Sartre, Beauvoir and Foucault. The label was largely coined by analytic philosophers and covers very different thinkers, so many find it unhelpful.","etym":null,"example":"Studying how the experience of time shapes human life, drawing on literature and history as well as argument.","notConfused":"Not the same as philosophy done on the European continent: many analytic philosophers worked there, and many continental philosophers work in Britain and America.","field":"Method","related":["Analytic philosophy","Phenomenology","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["husserl","heidegger","sartre"],"tags":["european philosophy","phenomenology","existentialism","post-structuralism","critical theory"]},{"term":"Contractualism","def":"The view that an act is wrong if it would be forbidden by principles that no one could reasonably reject.","more":"T. M. Scanlon developed it in 'What We Owe to Each Other' (1998). It grounds morality in what we can justify to each person, so it does not simply add up benefits as utilitarianism does. Critics ask what makes a rejection 'reasonable' without already assuming moral views, and how it covers animals and future people.","etym":"From 'contract', Latin contractus, 'drawn together'.","example":"A rule letting one person be sacrificed for slightly greater total benefit could reasonably be rejected by that person.","notConfused":"Not the same as social contract theory in politics, though related: contractualism is a theory of right and wrong, not of state authority.","field":"Ethics","related":["Social contract","Deontology","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["scanlon"],"tags":["scanlon","reasonable rejection","what we owe to each other","social contract","contractarianism"]},{"term":"Contradiction","def":"A pair of claims that cannot both be true, such as 'A' and 'not A'.","more":"The law of non-contradiction, which Aristotle defended as the firmest principle of all, says no claim is both true and false at once. Finding a contradiction in a view is the strongest way to refute it, as in a reductio ad absurdum. A small group of 'dialetheists', such as Graham Priest, argue some contradictions are true.","etym":"Latin contradicere, 'to speak against'.","example":"'The shop is open' and 'the shop is not open' at the same time and in the same sense.","notConfused":"Not the same as a contrary: 'the wall is all red' and 'the wall is all blue' cannot both be true, but both can be false.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Reductio ad absurdum","Bivalence","Antinomy"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["inconsistency","contradictory","self-contradiction","law of non-contradiction","p and not p"]},{"term":"Correspondence theory of truth","def":"The view that a statement is true when it matches, or corresponds to, the way the world is.","more":"It is the most widely held theory of truth and goes back to Aristotle's statement that to say of what is that it is, is true. Russell and the early Wittgenstein developed versions where true sentences correspond to facts. Critics ask what 'correspondence' and 'facts' are, and rivals include coherence, pragmatist and deflationary theories.","etym":"Latin correspondere, 'to answer together'.","example":"'Snow is white' is true because snow is in fact white.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Coherence theory of truth","Scientific realism","Pragmatism"],"thinkers":["aristotle","russell","wittgenstein"],"tags":["correspondence","truth as correspondence","facts","agreement with reality","theories of truth"]},{"term":"Cosmological argument","def":"An argument that the existence of the universe, or of change and causes, requires a first cause or necessary being.","example":"Every domino falls because another hit it. So what started the chain?","related":["Ontological argument","Design argument","Causation"],"thinkers":["aquinas","avicenna","leibniz"],"compass":"q19","more":"Versions include Aquinas' first cause argument, the kalam argument developed by medieval Islamic thinkers and revived by William Lane Craig, and Leibniz's argument from the principle of sufficient reason. Critics, including Hume and Kant, ask why the chain of causes could not be infinite, and why a first cause must be the God of religion.","etym":"Greek kosmos, 'order, world'.","notConfused":"Not the same as the design argument: the cosmological argument starts from the bare existence of things, not from their order or purpose.","field":"Religion","tags":["first cause argument","first cause","argument from contingency","kalam","aquinas"]},{"term":"Cosmopolitanism","def":"The view that all human beings belong to a single moral community, so our duties do not stop at national borders.","more":"Diogenes the Cynic is said to have called himself a 'citizen of the world'. The Stoics developed the idea, and Kant argued for a 'cosmopolitan right' of hospitality. Modern cosmopolitans such as Martha Nussbaum and Peter Singer argue for strong duties to distant strangers. Nationalists and communitarians reply that special ties to compatriots matter morally.","etym":"Greek kosmopolitēs, 'citizen of the world'.","example":"Giving to fight malaria abroad because a child's life there matters as much as one at home.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Justice","Communitarianism","Stoicism"],"thinkers":["diogenes","kant","nussbaum"],"tags":["world citizenship","citizen of the world","global justice","cosmopolitan","kosmopolites"]},{"term":"Counterexample","def":"A single case that shows a general claim is false.","more":"If someone says 'all swans are white', one black swan refutes them. Counterexamples are the main tool for testing definitions and principles in philosophy. The Gettier cases are famous counterexamples to the definition of knowledge as justified true belief.","etym":null,"example":"Claim: 'Every bird can fly.' Counterexample: penguins.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Gettier problem","Falsifiability","Conceptual analysis"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["counter-example","refutation","exception","disproof"]},{"term":"Counterfactual","def":"A claim about what would have happened if things had been different.","example":"‘If I had left five minutes earlier, I would have caught the bus.’","related":["Causation","Possible worlds"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"compass":"q18","more":"Counterfactuals are central to how we think about causes, responsibility and regret. Their truth is puzzling, because they describe situations that never happened. Lewis and Robert Stalnaker gave an influential answer: a counterfactual is true if, in the most similar possible worlds where the 'if' part holds, the 'then' part also holds.","etym":"'Counter to fact', because the 'if' clause is usually false.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","tags":["counterfactual conditional","what if","subjunctive conditional","possible worlds","lewis"]},{"term":"Critical theory","def":"A tradition, begun by the Frankfurt School, that studies society in order to expose and challenge forms of domination, not just to describe them.","more":"Max Horkheimer set out the programme in 1937, contrasting it with 'traditional theory'. Theodor Adorno criticised mass culture as a 'culture industry', and Jürgen Habermas later focused on communication and democracy. The phrase is now also used more broadly for approaches such as critical race theory.","etym":null,"example":"Asking who benefits from advertising that makes people feel their bodies are never good enough.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Alienation","Continental philosophy","Dialectic"],"thinkers":["horkheimer","adorno","marx"],"tags":["frankfurt school","adorno","horkheimer","habermas","marcuse","critique of society"]},{"term":"Cynicism","def":"The ancient Cynics’ view that virtue means living simply and according to nature, scorning wealth, status and convention.","example":"Diogenes is said to have lived in a large storage jar and owned almost nothing.","related":["Stoicism","Virtue"],"thinkers":["diogenes"],"more":"Diogenes of Sinope, who lived in the 4th century BCE, is the most famous Cynic, known for public provocations that mocked social convention. The Cynics influenced the Stoics, who kept the stress on virtue but softened the rejection of society. Many stories about Diogenes come from much later sources and may be legends.","etym":"Greek kynikos, 'dog-like', a nickname for their shameless way of life.","notConfused":"Not the same as modern cynicism (distrust of other people's motives): ancient Cynicism was a demanding ethical way of life.","field":"History of philosophy","tags":["cynics","diogenes","antisthenes","kynikos","cynic philosophy"]},{"term":"Dao","def":"Chinese for ‘the Way’. In Daoism, the nameless source and pattern of all things; in Confucianism, the right way of living and governing.","example":"Water flowing around rocks, never forcing, is a classic Daoist image of the Way.","related":["Wu wei","Ren"],"thinkers":["laozi","zhuangzi","confucius"],"more":"The word appears across Chinese thought. The 'Daodejing', traditionally attributed to Laozi, opens by saying that the dao that can be spoken of is not the constant dao. Zhuangzi stresses the dao in the spontaneous skill of craftsmen. For Confucians, the dao is the way of the sage kings, carried on through ritual and virtue.","etym":"Chinese 道 dào, 'road, path, way'; older spelling Tao.","notConfused":"Not the same as a god: the Dao is not usually pictured as a person who creates or commands.","field":"Asian traditions","tags":["tao","the way","daoism","taoism","laozi","dao de jing"]},{"term":"Dasein","def":"Heidegger's term for the human way of being: a being for whom its own existence is an issue.","more":"In 'Being and Time' (1927) Heidegger used Dasein instead of words such as 'subject' or 'consciousness', to avoid older assumptions. Dasein is always already 'in the world', caught up in tasks and relationships, and it understands itself in the light of its death. Heidegger's aim was to use Dasein to approach the question of the meaning of being.","etym":"German, literally 'being there'; in ordinary German, 'existence'.","example":"You do not first exist and then notice a world; you find yourself already using a pen, worrying about a deadline, caring about friends.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Existentialism","Phenomenology","Authenticity"],"thinkers":["heidegger"],"tags":["being-there","being-in-the-world","heidegger","being and time","existence"]},{"term":"De","def":"In Chinese philosophy, virtue or power: the moral force or natural potency a person or thing has when it follows the Dao.","more":"Confucians spoke of the ruler's de, which wins people over by example rather than force. In the 'Daodejing', de is the power that comes from following the Dao without striving. Translators render it 'virtue', 'power' or 'charisma', since no single English word fits.","etym":"Chinese 德 dé; older spelling Te.","example":"A teacher whose quiet integrity makes students behave well without any threats.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dao","Confucianism","Wu wei"],"thinkers":["confucius","laozi"],"tags":["te","virtue","power","moral force","dao de jing","tao te ching"]},{"term":"Deconstruction","def":"Jacques Derrida's approach to reading texts, which shows how their key oppositions (such as speech/writing or nature/culture) undermine themselves.","more":"Derrida argued that Western philosophy relies on pairs of opposites in which one term is treated as primary, and that close reading reveals the dependence of each on the other. He introduced it in the late 1960s. Admirers see it as a powerful critical tool; critics, including many analytic philosophers, regard it as obscure.","etym":null,"example":"Noticing that a text praising spoken language over writing relies on images of writing to make its case.","notConfused":"Not the same as simply taking something apart or criticising it: deconstruction is a specific method of reading.","field":"Language","related":["Continental philosophy","Language game"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["derrida","deconstructionism","post-structuralism","différance","binary oppositions"]},{"term":"Deduction","def":"Reasoning in which, if the premises are true, the conclusion must be true.","more":"Deduction is the standard of validity in logic, and Aristotle's syllogisms were its first systematic study. Deduction cannot produce information not already contained in the premises, which is both its strength and its limit. The opposite form is induction, which goes beyond the premises but only makes the conclusion probable.","etym":"Latin deducere, 'to lead down'.","example":"All mammals breathe air; whales are mammals; so whales breathe air.","notConfused":"Not the same as how Sherlock Holmes uses the word: his 'deductions' are mostly abductions, inferences to the best explanation.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Induction","Abduction","Valid"],"thinkers":["aristotle","frege"],"tags":["deductive reasoning","deductive argument","deductive logic","validity","necessary inference"]},{"term":"Defeasible","def":"Able to be overturned by new information. A defeasible reason is a good reason that could still be outweighed or cancelled.","more":"Most everyday reasoning is defeasible: 'Tweety is a bird, so Tweety can fly' is reasonable until you learn Tweety is a penguin. The idea comes partly from law, via H. L. A. Hart, and is central to theories of knowledge and ethics. W. D. Ross's 'prima facie' duties are a defeasible kind of moral reason.","etym":"Old French defesable, 'able to be undone'.","example":"You trust a clock until you learn it stopped yesterday.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallibilism","Induction","Justification"],"thinkers":["hart","ross"],"tags":["defeasibility","defeasible reasoning","non-monotonic","defeater","overridable"]},{"term":"Definite description","def":"A phrase of the form 'the so-and-so' that picks out one particular thing, such as 'the first person on the Moon'.","more":"In 'On Denoting' (1905), Russell argued that 'The present King of France is bald' is not about a non-existent king. It really says: there is exactly one present King of France, and he is bald, which is simply false. His analysis became a model for how logic can clarify language, though P. F. Strawson and others challenged it.","etym":null,"example":"'The tallest person in your class' picks out one person, even if you do not know who.","notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Language game","Logic","Analytic philosophy"],"thinkers":["russell","pfstrawson"],"tags":["theory of descriptions","russell","the present king of france","descriptions"]},{"term":"Deism","def":"Belief in a God who created the world but does not intervene in it, known through reason and nature rather than revelation.","more":"Deism flourished in 17th- and 18th-century Europe and America among thinkers who rejected miracles and church authority but accepted a creator. Voltaire and Thomas Paine are well-known examples. The common image of God as a watchmaker who winds up the universe and lets it run captures the view.","etym":"Latin deus, 'god'.","example":"Someone who thinks the laws of physics show a designer, but does not believe in prayer or miracles.","notConfused":"Not the same as theism, which usually includes a God who acts in the world, answers prayer or reveals truths.","field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Agnosticism","Design argument"],"thinkers":["voltaire","paine"],"tags":["deist","clockmaker god","watchmaker god","natural religion","god"]},{"term":"Democracy","def":"Government by the people, through voting and equal political rights. Philosophers debate whether its value lies in good results, fairness, or both.","example":"A class voting on where to go for a school trip.","related":["Legitimacy","Justice"],"thinkers":["mill","dewey"],"compass":"q24","more":"Plato distrusted democracy, comparing it to letting passengers rather than a trained navigator steer a ship. Mill defended it partly for its educational effects on citizens. Today 'epistemic democrats' argue that groups often decide well, while critics such as Jason Brennan argue many voters are poorly informed.","etym":"Greek dēmokratia, from dēmos, 'people', and kratos, 'rule'.","notConfused":"Not the same as majority rule alone: most democracies also protect rights that a majority cannot vote away.","field":"Politics and society","tags":["democratic","rule by the people","popular sovereignty","self-government","voting","majority rule"]},{"term":"Denying the antecedent","def":"Reasoning 'If A then B; not A; so not B.' It is invalid, because B could still be true for another reason.","more":"It is the invalid look-alike of modus tollens ('If A then B; not B; so not A'), which is valid. Like affirming the consequent, it treats a one-way 'if' as if it ran both ways.","etym":null,"example":"'If I study, I will pass. I did not study. So I will not pass.' You might pass anyway.","notConfused":"Not the same as modus tollens: 'If A then B; not B; so not A' is valid.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Affirming the consequent","Conditional","Fallacy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["inverse error","fallacy of the inverse","formal fallacy","invalid inference"]},{"term":"Deontology","def":"The view that some acts are right or wrong in themselves, because of duties and rules, not just because of their results.","example":"A deontologist says you must not break a promise even if breaking it would make things slightly better.","related":["Categorical imperative","Consequentialism","Doctrine of double effect"],"thinkers":["kant","korsgaard"],"compass":"q1","more":"Deontologists hold that there are constraints on what you may do to people, even for good ends, and that some duties, such as keeping promises, have special weight. Kant is the classic source, but W. D. Ross offered a more flexible version with several 'prima facie' duties that can conflict. Critics ask why it can be wrong to break one rule if doing so prevents five others from being broken.","etym":"Greek deon, 'duty, what is binding', plus logos, 'study'.","notConfused":"Not the same as rule-worship: deontologists can still care about consequences, but deny they are all that matters.","field":"Ethics","tags":["deontological ethics","duty ethics","duty-based ethics","kantian ethics","rules","kant"]},{"term":"Dependent origination","def":"The Buddhist teaching that everything arises in dependence on conditions, and nothing exists independently or by itself.","more":"It explains how suffering arises through a chain of twelve linked conditions, and so how it can be ended by removing them. Nagarjuna argued that dependent origination is the same as emptiness: since things depend on other things, they lack fixed independent natures. It underpins the doctrine of no-self.","etym":"Sanskrit pratītyasamutpāda, 'arising in dependence'.","example":"A rainbow exists only when sun, rain and a viewer's position come together; take one away and it is gone.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Anatta (no-self)","Karma"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna"],"tags":["pratityasamutpada","paticcasamuppada","dependent arising","interdependence","conditioned arising"]},{"term":"Desert","def":"What a person deserves because of what they did, such as reward for effort or blame for wrongdoing.","example":"‘She deserves the prize: she worked hardest.’","related":["Retributivism","Moral responsibility"],"compass":"q9","more":"Desert claims link a person's treatment to some fact about them, such as their effort, choices or wrongdoing. They are central to debates about punishment, wages and prizes. Some philosophers, including Rawls, argued that people do not deserve their natural talents, which limits how much desert can justify inequality. Others, such as Pereboom, argue that if we lack free will, nobody basically deserves blame at all.","etym":"Old French deservir, from Latin deservire, 'to serve well'.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'dessert': the word is pronounced like it, as in 'just deserts', but it means what one deserves.","field":"Ethics","thinkers":[],"tags":["deserve","deserving","merit","just deserts","moral desert","retributivism"]},{"term":"Design argument","def":"An argument that the order and fine-tuning of nature point to an intelligent designer.","example":"Finding a watch on a beach, you would infer a watchmaker; the eye is far more intricate.","related":["Cosmological argument","Telos"],"thinkers":["aquinas","hume"],"compass":"q19","more":"The watch analogy comes from William Paley's 'Natural Theology' (1802). Hume had already criticised design arguments in his 'Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion', and Darwin's theory of natural selection later offered a non-designed explanation for complex living things. Modern versions often appeal instead to the fine-tuning of physical constants.","etym":"Also called the teleological argument, from Greek telos, 'end, purpose'.","notConfused":"Not the same as intelligent design as a modern movement: the design argument is a general philosophical argument with many historical forms.","field":"Religion","tags":["teleological argument","argument from design","intelligent design","watchmaker argument","paley","fine-tuning"]},{"term":"Determinism","def":"The view that everything that happens is fixed by earlier events plus the laws of nature.","example":"If determinism is true, what you eat tomorrow was settled before you were born.","related":["Indeterminism","Compatibilism","Free will"],"thinkers":["spinoza","hobbes"],"compass":"q8","more":"Determinism is a claim about how the world works, not a claim about what we can predict: a deterministic world could still be far too complex to forecast. Its main interest for philosophers is the threat it seems to pose to free will and moral responsibility. Whether our universe is deterministic is partly a scientific question, and some interpretations of quantum mechanics suggest it is not.","etym":"Latin determinare, 'to set limits, fix'.","notConfused":"Not the same as fatalism: fatalism says certain outcomes will happen whatever you do, while determinism says your choices are links in the causal chain that brings outcomes about.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["causal determinism","determined","predetermination","laplace's demon","free will"]},{"term":"Dharma","def":"A key term with several meanings: in Hindu thought, duty, law and the right order of things; in Buddhism, the Buddha's teaching and also the basic elements of existence.","more":"In the 'Bhagavad Gita', Arjuna is urged to do his dharma as a warrior, even when it is painful. Hindu texts link dharma to one's stage of life and social role, which has been criticised, notably by Ambedkar, for supporting caste. In Buddhism, the Dharma is one of the 'three jewels' with the Buddha and the community.","etym":"Sanskrit dharma, from a root meaning 'to hold, support'.","example":"A doctor treating patients during an epidemic despite the risk, because that is what the role requires.","notConfused":"Not the same as karma: karma is action and its consequences, dharma the right way to act or the teaching.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Karma","Buddhism","Advaita Vedanta"],"thinkers":["ambedkar"],"tags":["dhamma","duty","cosmic law","righteousness","buddha's teaching"]},{"term":"Dialectic","def":"Moving toward truth through the clash of opposing views. In Socrates it is question-and-answer; in Hegel it is a process where conflicts are overcome and preserved at a higher level.","example":"A debate where both sides change their minds and reach a better third view is dialectical.","related":["Argument","Alienation"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato","hegel","marx"],"more":"For Socrates and Plato, dialectic was disciplined conversation that tests a definition by drawing out its consequences until it fails or improves. Hegel used the word for the way ideas and historical forms develop: each stage contains tensions that push it to a richer stage. Marx kept Hegel's pattern of development but applied it to material and economic conditions. The popular 'thesis, antithesis, synthesis' formula is a simplification that Hegel himself did not use as his key terms.","etym":"Greek dialektikē (technē), 'the art of conversation', from dialegesthai, 'to converse'.","notConfused":"Not the same as a dialect (a regional form of a language), though both words come from the Greek for talking.","field":"Method","tags":["dialectics","dialectical method","thesis antithesis synthesis","hegel","socratic dialogue"]},{"term":"Difference principle","def":"Rawls’ principle that social and economic inequalities are acceptable only if they benefit the least advantaged members of society.","example":"Paying doctors more is fine if it means better healthcare for the poorest.","related":["Veil of ignorance","Justice"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"compass":"q23","more":"It is part of Rawls' second principle of justice in 'A Theory of Justice' (1971), and it ranks below the principles of equal basic liberty and fair equality of opportunity. Rawls argued that people choosing behind a veil of ignorance would accept it. Nozick objected that it ignores how holdings came about; others, such as G. A. Cohen, argued it allows too much inequality.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as strict equality: the principle allows inequalities, but only those that help the worst off.","field":"Politics and society","tags":["rawls","maximin","worst-off","justice as fairness","inequality"]},{"term":"Direct realism","def":"The view that in perception we are directly aware of physical objects themselves, not of mental images that represent them.","more":"Thomas Reid defended it against Locke's and Hume's 'way of ideas', on which we perceive only ideas in our minds. The argument from illusion is the main challenge to it. Modern versions, sometimes called 'disjunctivism', say a real perception and a matching hallucination are different kinds of state.","etym":null,"example":"When you look at a tree, it is the tree itself you see, not a picture of it in your head.","notConfused":"Not the same as naive realism in the sense of thinking everything is exactly as it looks: direct realists can accept that we sometimes misperceive.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Argument from illusion","Empiricism","Idealism"],"thinkers":["reid"],"tags":["naive realism","direct perception","perception","common-sense realism"]},{"term":"Disinterestedness","def":"Enjoying something for its own sake, without any desire to own, use or consume it.","more":"Kant, in the 'Critique of Judgement' (1790), made disinterested pleasure a mark of judging something beautiful. The idea has roots in 18th-century British writers such as Shaftesbury. Critics, including Nietzsche and later Pierre Bourdieu, argue that the ideal hides personal and social interests.","etym":null,"example":"Admiring a cake's shape and colour in a shop window without wanting to eat it.","notConfused":"Not the same as being uninterested: disinterested attention can be intense, it just has no personal stake.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Beauty","Aesthetics"],"thinkers":["kant","bourdieu"],"tags":["disinterested pleasure","aesthetic attitude","kant","impartiality","detachment"]},{"term":"Divine command theory","def":"The view that what makes an act morally right or wrong is that God commands or forbids it.","more":"It faces the Euthyphro dilemma, from Plato's 'Euthyphro': is something good because God commands it, or does God command it because it is good? If the first, morality seems arbitrary; if the second, goodness seems independent of God. Robert Merrihew Adams defended a modified version tying obligation to the commands of a loving God.","etym":null,"example":"Believing stealing is wrong simply because it is forbidden in the Ten Commandments.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Euthyphro dilemma","Moral realism","Deontology"],"thinkers":["adams_rm","ockham"],"tags":["divine command ethics","god's commands","theological voluntarism","euthyphro dilemma","morality from god"]},{"term":"Doctrine of double effect","def":"The principle that it can be permissible to cause a harm as a foreseen side effect of a good action, when it would be wrong to cause the same harm as a means.","example":"A doctor may give painkillers that might shorten life, aiming at relief, but may not give a drug intending death.","related":["Trolley problem","Deontology"],"thinkers":["aquinas","anscombe","foot"],"compass":"q3","more":"The principle is usually traced to Aquinas' discussion of killing in self-defence. It distinguishes what you intend from what you merely foresee. It is used in medical ethics and the ethics of war, for example to separate bombing a military target with foreseen civilian deaths from deliberately targeting civilians. Critics question whether intention, a fact inside the agent's head, can really change whether an act is permitted.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as the claim that good ends justify any means: the doctrine forbids using a harm as a means.","field":"Ethics","tags":["double effect","principle of double effect","intended vs foreseen","aquinas","side effects"]},{"term":"Dogmatism","def":"Holding beliefs firmly without being open to questioning or evidence against them.","more":"The ancient Greek sceptics called anyone who asserted views about hidden truths a 'dogmatist'. Kant said that Hume woke him from his 'dogmatic slumber', meaning uncritical acceptance of metaphysics. In modern epistemology 'dogmatism' also names a technical view that seeming to perceive something gives some reason to believe it.","etym":"Greek dogma, 'opinion, belief'.","example":"Refusing to look at any evidence that might show your favourite theory is wrong.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Skepticism","Fallibilism"],"thinkers":["kant","sextus"],"tags":["dogmatic","dogma","closed-mindedness","certainty","skepticism"]},{"term":"Dualism","def":"The view that the mind is not just physical: there is something non-physical about minds.","example":"Descartes held that you could exist as a thinking thing even without a body.","related":["Physicalism","Epiphenomenalism","Substance"],"thinkers":["descartes","chalmers"],"compass":"q6","more":"Substance dualism, Descartes' view, says the mind is a separate non-physical thing. Property dualism, defended by Chalmers among others, says there is one kind of stuff but it has both physical and non-physical properties, such as conscious experience. The main objection to substance dualism, raised by Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia, is how a non-physical mind could move a physical body.","etym":"Latin dualis, 'containing two'.","notConfused":"Not the same as believing in a soul in a religious sense only: dualism is a general claim about what minds are, and some dualists are not religious.","field":"Mind","tags":["mind-body dualism","cartesian dualism","substance dualism","descartes","mind and body","soul"]},{"term":"Dukkha","def":"A Buddhist term usually translated ‘suffering’ or ‘unsatisfactoriness’: the stress of a life built on craving for things that do not last.","example":"Even a great holiday carries a quiet ache because you know it will end.","related":["Anatta (no-self)","Karma"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"more":"Dukkha is the subject of the first of the Four Noble Truths: life as ordinarily lived involves dukkha. The second truth traces it to craving, the third says it can end (nirvana), and the fourth gives the path. Buddhist texts describe obvious pain, the pain of change, and a subtle unsatisfactoriness built into all conditioned things.","etym":"Pali dukkha (Sanskrit duḥkha); often linked to a word for an ill-fitting axle hole, though this explanation is traditional.","notConfused":"Not the same as saying life is all misery: the point is that nothing impermanent can bring lasting satisfaction.","field":"Asian traditions","tags":["suffering","dissatisfaction","duhkha","unsatisfactoriness","four noble truths"]},{"term":"Duty","def":"Something you are morally required to do, or not do, whether or not you want to.","more":"Kant held that a good will acts from duty, not just in line with it. W. D. Ross listed several prima facie duties, such as fidelity, gratitude and non-harm, which must be weighed when they conflict. Philosophers also distinguish 'perfect' duties, which allow no exceptions, from 'imperfect' duties, such as helping others, where you have some choice when and how.","etym":"Anglo-French dueté, 'what is owed'.","example":"Keeping a promise to help a friend move house even though something more fun has come up.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Categorical imperative","Moral responsibility"],"thinkers":["kant","ross"],"tags":["obligation","moral duty","responsibility","deontology","ought"]},{"term":"Effective altruism","def":"A movement that uses evidence and careful reasoning to work out how to do the most good with one's time and money, and then acts on it.","more":"It grew in the late 2000s, drawing on Peter Singer's argument that if we can prevent something very bad at little cost to ourselves, we ought to. Effective altruists compare charities by measurable impact, and some focus on animal welfare or risks to humanity's long-term future. Critics argue it neglects justice and political change, overrates what can be measured, and can be used to justify troubling means.","etym":null,"example":"Giving to a charity that distributes malaria nets because studies suggest it saves more lives per pound than most alternatives.","notConfused":"Not the same as altruism in general: effective altruism is a specific method and movement for choosing how to help.","field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Consequentialism","Egoism"],"thinkers":["singer"],"tags":["ea","singer","doing good better","charity effectiveness","macaskill","giving"]},{"term":"Egalitarianism","def":"The family of views holding that people are equal in some basic way and that this should shape how society treats them.","more":"Egalitarians disagree about what should be equal: welfare, resources, opportunities, capabilities or social standing. Luck egalitarians, such as G. A. Cohen, aim to remove inequalities that come from bad luck rather than choice; relational egalitarians, such as Elizabeth Anderson, focus on people living as equals without domination. Critics, including Nozick, argue that enforcing equality conflicts with liberty.","etym":"French egal, 'equal', from Latin aequalis","example":"Arguing that a child's chances in life should not depend on which family they happened to be born into.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Justice","Difference principle","Desert"],"thinkers":["rawls","ga_cohen","anderson_e"],"tags":["equality","egalitarian","equal distribution","social equality","fairness"]},{"term":"Egoism","def":"Psychological egoism says people always act for their own benefit. Ethical egoism says people ought to. Its opposite, altruism, is acting for the good of others.","more":"Hobbes is often read as a psychological egoist. Critics such as Joseph Butler argued that we must first desire things other than our own pleasure, or else satisfying our desires would give us no pleasure. Ethical egoism, defended in different forms by Max Stirner and Ayn Rand, faces the objection that it cannot give impartial advice to two people whose interests clash.","etym":"Latin ego, 'I'","example":"A psychological egoist says even your charity donation is really about feeling good.","notConfused":"Not the same as egotism: egotism is excessive self-importance, while egoism is a theory about motivation or morality.","field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["hobbes"],"compass":"q1","tags":["ethical egoism","psychological egoism","selfishness","self-interest","rand"]},{"term":"Eightfold Path","def":"The Buddha's practical path out of suffering: right view, intention, speech, action, livelihood, effort, mindfulness and concentration.","more":"The path is the fourth of the Four Noble Truths. Its eight parts are traditionally grouped into wisdom, ethical conduct and mental discipline, and they are practised together rather than one after another. 'Right' (samma) here means complete or fitting, not right as opposed to a rule-breaking wrong.","etym":"Pali ariya atthangika magga, 'noble eight-limbed path'","example":"Choosing work that does not harm others is an example of right livelihood.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Four Noble Truths","Dukkha","Karma"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["noble eightfold path","eight-fold path","right view","right action","buddhist path","middle way"]},{"term":"Eliminative materialism","def":"The view that some everyday mental concepts, such as belief and desire, belong to a false theory and will one day be dropped, as science moves on.","more":"Paul and Patricia Churchland argue that 'folk psychology' is a theory like any other, and a poor one, which neuroscience will replace rather than explain. They compare beliefs to phlogiston, a substance once thought to explain burning that turned out not to exist. Critics reply that the view undermines itself: arguing for it seems to require believing it.","etym":"Latin eliminare, 'to turn out of doors'","example":"Just as we stopped talking about 'demonic possession' once we understood epilepsy, we might stop talking about 'beliefs' once we understand the brain.","notConfused":"Not the same as ordinary materialism: most materialists think beliefs exist and are brain states; eliminativists doubt there are beliefs at all.","field":"Mind","related":["Folk psychology","Physicalism","Materialism"],"thinkers":["churchland","pchurchland"],"tags":["eliminativism","churchland","folk psychology","no beliefs","eliminativist"]},{"term":"Emergence","def":"The idea that a whole can have features that its parts do not have, and that cannot easily be predicted from them.","more":"Weak emergence says the new features are surprising but in principle follow from the parts, like traffic jams from individual drivers. Strong emergence says some features, perhaps consciousness, are genuinely new and cannot even in principle be derived from the parts. C. D. Broad defended a version in The Mind and Its Place in Nature (1925). Critics doubt that strong emergence is coherent or that it ever occurs.","etym":"Latin emergere, 'to rise out of'","example":"Wetness: a single water molecule is not wet, but many together are.","notConfused":"Not the same as dualism: emergentists usually hold that there is only one kind of stuff, which develops new properties when organised in complex ways.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Physicalism","Hard problem","Holism"],"thinkers":["broad"],"tags":["emergent properties","emergentism","whole greater than parts","emergent","complexity"]},{"term":"Emotivism","def":"An early anti-realist view: moral statements express feelings rather than describe facts. ‘Stealing is wrong!’ works like ‘Boo to stealing!’","more":"A. J. Ayer set out emotivism in Language, Truth and Logic (1936), and C. L. Stevenson developed it in more detail. It grew from the verification principle: since moral claims cannot be checked by observation, Ayer said they were not literally true or false. A major objection, the Frege-Geach problem, asks how moral words keep their meaning inside an 'if...then' sentence, where nothing is being booed.","etym":"Latin emovere, 'to move out, stir up'","example":"Saying ‘Cruelty is wrong’ is like booing at a football match.","notConfused":"Not the same as subjectivism: a subjectivist says 'X is wrong' reports your feeling and so is true or false; an emotivist says it expresses the feeling and is neither.","field":"Ethics","related":["Expressivism","Verification principle"],"thinkers":["ayer"],"compass":"q4","tags":["boo-hurrah theory","boo hurrah","ayer","stevenson","moral expressions","non-cognitivism"]},{"term":"Empiricism","def":"The view that our knowledge of the world comes mainly from the senses and experience.","more":"The classic British empiricists were Locke, Berkeley and Hume. Locke argued that the mind starts as a blank slate and that all ideas come from sensation and reflection. Empiricists usually accept that maths and logic can be known without experience, but say these tell us only about relations between ideas, not about the world. The opposing view is rationalism.","etym":"Greek empeiria, 'experience'","example":"An empiricist says you learned that fire burns by feeling heat, not by pure reasoning.","notConfused":"Not the same as being 'empirical' in science: empiricism is a philosophical theory about where knowledge comes from, not just a habit of running experiments.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Rationalism","A posteriori","Induction"],"thinkers":["locke","berkeley","hume"],"compass":"q13","tags":["empiricist","experience","locke","hume","sense experience","observation"]},{"term":"Emptiness (śūnyatā)","def":"The Madhyamaka Buddhist teaching that nothing has an independent, self-standing nature; everything exists only in dependence on other things.","more":"Nagarjuna (c. 2nd century CE) argued that everything that arises in dependence on other things is empty of svabhava, or 'own-being'. He insisted emptiness is not nothingness, and that emptiness is itself empty: it is not a hidden absolute. The idea became central to Mahayana Buddhism and to Chan and Zen in East Asia.","etym":"Sanskrit sunyata, 'emptiness', from sunya, 'empty, zero'","example":"A chair is a chair only because of its parts, its maker and how we use it: it has no chair-ness of its own.","notConfused":"Not the same as nihilism or 'nothing exists': things do exist and function, just not independently.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["nagarjuna"],"tags":["sunyata","shunyata","emptiness","nagarjuna","madhyamaka","no inherent existence"]},{"term":"Enlightenment","def":"A European intellectual movement of roughly the late 17th and 18th centuries that championed reason, science, individual rights and the criticism of tradition and authority.","more":"Key figures include Locke, Voltaire, Hume, Smith, Rousseau and Kant. In his 1784 essay What Is Enlightenment?, Kant summed it up as humanity's emergence from self-imposed immaturity, urging people to dare to use their own understanding. Later critics, from Romantics and conservatives such as Burke to Adorno and Horkheimer, argued that its faith in reason had blind spots and dangers.","etym":"A translation of German Aufklärung and French les Lumières, 'the lights'","example":"Arguing that a law should be changed because it cannot be justified by reason, not just because it is old.","notConfused":"Not the same as Buddhist enlightenment (bodhi): that is a spiritual awakening, not a historical movement.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Humanism","Empiricism","Social contract"],"thinkers":["kant","voltaire","locke"],"tags":["age of reason","age of enlightenment","aufklärung","aufklarung","kant","sapere aude"]},{"term":"Entailment","def":"The relation between statements when one must be true if the others are: the premises entail the conclusion if it is impossible for them to be true and it false.","more":"Entailment is the backbone of deductive logic: a valid argument is one whose premises entail its conclusion. Logicians distinguish it from mere probability, where premises only make the conclusion likely. Some philosophers, such as relevance logicians, argue that the standard definition is too loose, because it lets a contradiction entail anything at all.","etym":"From Middle English 'entail', to settle property on a fixed line of heirs","example":"'All cats are mammals' and 'Tom is a cat' together entail 'Tom is a mammal'.","notConfused":"Not the same as implying in conversation: 'some students passed' suggests not all did, but it does not entail it.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Valid","Inference","Logic"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["logical consequence","implication","follows from","logical implication","validity"]},{"term":"Epicureanism","def":"A Hellenistic school teaching that pleasure, understood mainly as freedom from pain and fear, is the goal of life, best reached by simple living and friendship. The goal is ataraxia: tranquillity, freedom from disturbance.","more":"Epicurus founded his school, known as the Garden, in Athens c. 307 BCE. He taught that the universe is made of atoms and void, that the gods do not interfere, and that death is nothing to fear because when it comes we are no longer there. The Roman poet Lucretius set out these views in On the Nature of Things.","etym":"From Epicurus (341–270 BCE)","example":"Choosing a quiet dinner with friends over a stressful luxury party.","notConfused":"Not the same as the modern word 'epicure', meaning a lover of fine food: Epicurus advised simple meals.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Hedonism","Stoicism"],"thinkers":["epicurus","lucretius"],"compass":"q22","tags":["epicurus","epicurean","garden","ataraxia","pleasure","lucretius"]},{"term":"Epiphenomenalism","def":"The view that conscious experiences are caused by brain processes but do not cause anything themselves.","more":"T. H. Huxley proposed the steam-whistle comparison in the 19th century. Frank Jackson defended epiphenomenal qualia in 1982 to explain how experience could be non-physical while physics stays complete. The main objection is that if experiences cause nothing, they could not even cause us to talk or think about them.","etym":"Greek epi, 'upon', + phainomenon, 'appearance': a by-product appearing on top of something","example":"Like the whistle on a steam train: produced by the engine, but not pulling the train.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Dualism","Qualia"],"thinkers":["jackson"],"compass":"q6","tags":["epiphenomenon","epiphenomena","causally inert mind","mental causation","huxley"]},{"term":"Epistemic injustice","def":"A wrong done to someone in their capacity as a knower, for example by not believing them because of who they are.","more":"The philosopher Miranda Fricker named two kinds in 2007. Testimonial injustice is giving someone's word less credibility because of prejudice. Hermeneutical injustice is when a group lacks the shared concepts to make sense of its own experience, as when there was not yet a term for 'sexual harassment'. The idea has influenced work in law, medicine and education.","etym":"Greek episteme, 'knowledge'","example":"A patient's account of their pain is dismissed because of their accent or age.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemology","Justice","Feminist philosophy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["testimonial injustice","hermeneutical injustice","fricker","credibility deficit","being believed"]},{"term":"Epistemicism","def":"The view that vague words do have sharp boundaries, but we cannot know where they are.","more":"Epistemicism keeps classical logic intact: every statement is either true or false, even 'this is a heap'. Its best-known defender is Timothy Williamson, who argues that our ignorance of the boundary is explained by the fact that meaning depends on usage in ways too complex to track. Most people find it hard to believe that a single grain could make the difference, which is the main objection.","etym":"Greek episteme, 'knowledge'","example":"There is an exact number of grains that makes a heap; we just can never find out what it is.","notConfused":"Not the same as epistemology: epistemicism is one specific theory of vagueness.","field":"Language","related":["Vagueness","Sorites paradox"],"thinkers":["williamson"],"compass":"q16","tags":["epistemic view of vagueness","williamson","sharp boundaries","vagueness","sorites"]},{"term":"Epistemology","def":"The study of knowledge: what it is, how we get it, and how far it reaches. It also asks when a belief is reasonable.","more":"Epistemologists ask what separates knowledge from lucky guesses, which sources (perception, memory, testimony, reason) can be trusted, and whether we can answer the sceptic who says we know nothing. A long-running debate is between empiricists, who rest knowledge on experience, and rationalists, who give reason a larger role. Since 1963 much work has focused on defining knowledge after the Gettier problem.","etym":"Greek episteme, 'knowledge', + logos, 'study'","example":"Wondering how you know your friend is not lying, or whether you can trust your memory, is epistemology.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Justification","Skepticism","Empiricism","Rationalism"],"thinkers":["descartes","hume"],"tags":["theory of knowledge","knowledge","justification","belief","gnoseology"]},{"term":"Epoché","def":"Suspending judgment: holding back from saying whether something is true or false.","more":"Ancient sceptics such as Sextus Empiricus recommended epoché when arguments on both sides seem equally strong, and claimed it brings peace of mind (ataraxia). Edmund Husserl later borrowed the word for his method of 'bracketing' the question of whether the world exists, so as to describe experience exactly as it appears. Phenomenologists still use it in this second sense.","etym":"Greek epoche, 'holding back, pause'","example":"When two experts give equally good arguments and you decide not to take a side for now.","notConfused":"Not the same as denial: suspending judgment means neither believing nor disbelieving.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Skepticism","Phenomenology"],"thinkers":["sextus","pyrrho","husserl"],"tags":["epoche","bracketing","suspension of judgment","suspension of judgement","husserl","phenomenological reduction"]},{"term":"Equivocation","def":"Using one word in two different senses within the same argument, so the argument only seems to work.","more":"Equivocation is dangerous because each premise can look true when read on its own, while the argument needs the word to mean the same thing throughout. Philosophers guard against it by defining key terms carefully. Words like 'free', 'natural', 'right' and 'theory' are common culprits.","etym":"Latin aequivocus, 'having equal voice', that is, one word sounding the same for different things","example":"‘A feather is light. What is light cannot be dark. So a feather cannot be dark.’","notConfused":"Not the same as vagueness: a vague word has fuzzy edges, while an equivocal argument switches between two distinct meanings.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Vagueness"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["ambiguity","double meaning","fallacy of equivocation","shifting meaning"]},{"term":"Error theory","def":"The view that moral claims try to state objective facts, but there are no such facts, so all positive moral claims are false.","more":"J. L. Mackie defended it in Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), arguing that objective values would be 'queer': unlike anything else in the universe. Error theorists disagree about what to do next: some say we should give up moral talk, others (fictionalists, such as Richard Joyce) that we should keep using it as a useful fiction.","etym":null,"example":"An error theorist says ‘murder is wrong’ is false, not because murder is fine, but because nothing is objectively wrong.","notConfused":"Not the same as nihilism in the everyday sense of 'nothing matters': error theory is a specific claim about the truth of moral statements.","field":"Ethics","related":["Anti-realism","Moral realism"],"thinkers":["mackie"],"compass":"q4","tags":["moral error theory","mackie","queerness argument","moral anti-realism","no moral facts"]},{"term":"Essence","def":"The features that make something the kind of thing it is, which it could not lose and still be that thing.","more":"Aristotle distinguished a thing's essential properties from its accidental ones, which it could lose and still be the same kind of thing. Medieval thinkers such as Aquinas distinguished a thing's essence (what it is) from its existence (that it is). In the 20th century Kripke and Putnam revived talk of essences, for example the idea that water essentially is H2O, while existentialists denied that humans have a fixed essence.","etym":"Latin essentia, from esse, 'to be'","example":"Having three sides is part of a triangle’s essence; being blue is not.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Substance","Existence precedes essence"],"thinkers":["aristotle","aquinas"],"tags":["essential property","essentialism","nature","what it is","quiddity"]},{"term":"Eternalism","def":"The view that past, present and future things all equally exist; ‘now’ is just where we happen to be, like ‘here’.","more":"Eternalism is often called the 'block universe' view, and many think it fits Einstein's relativity, which suggests there is no single universal present. Its rivals are presentism (only the present exists) and the growing block view (past and present exist, the future does not). Critics say eternalism struggles to explain why time seems to pass.","etym":"Latin aeternus, 'everlasting'","example":"On eternalism, your tenth birthday still exists, just at an earlier time.","notConfused":"Not the same as believing in eternal life: eternalism is a theory about time, not about living forever.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Presentism"],"thinkers":[],"compass":"q17","tags":["block universe","four-dimensionalism","b-theory of time","time","presentism"]},{"term":"Eternal recurrence","def":"Nietzsche’s thought experiment: imagine living your exact life again, endlessly. Could you welcome that?","more":"Nietzsche introduced the idea in The Gay Science (1882) and returned to it in Thus Spoke Zarathustra. Scholars debate whether he meant it as a claim about the universe or mainly as a test of how far you affirm your life. Either way, the ideal response is amor fati: loving your fate, including its suffering.","etym":null,"example":"If you would dread reliving this week forever, what should you change?","notConfused":null,"field":"History of philosophy","related":["Will to power","Übermensch"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"compass":"q20","tags":["eternal return","nietzsche","amor fati","endless repetition","recurrence"]},{"term":"Ethics","def":"The study of how we ought to live and act. It asks what is right, wrong, good and bad.","more":"Philosophers usually split ethics into three layers. Normative ethics asks which actions are right (for example consequentialism, deontology, virtue ethics); applied ethics tackles specific issues such as euthanasia or animal welfare; metaethics asks what moral claims mean and whether they can be true. Much of the subject goes back to Socrates' question of how one should live.","etym":"Greek ethikos, from ethos, 'character, custom'","example":"Deciding whether to tell a friend a painful truth is an ethical question.","notConfused":"Not the same as law or etiquette: something can be legal or polite and still be wrong, or illegal and still be right.","field":"Ethics","related":["Metaethics","Consequentialism","Deontology","Virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["aristotle","kant","mill"],"compass":"q1","tags":["moral philosophy","morality","morals","right and wrong","normative ethics"]},{"term":"Ethics of care","def":"A moral theory that puts caring relationships and responsiveness to particular people at the centre of ethics, rather than abstract rules or calculations.","more":"It grew in the 1980s from Carol Gilligan's psychology and Nel Noddings' philosophy, and was developed by Virginia Held, Joan Tronto and others. Care ethicists argue that traditional theories modelled the moral agent on an independent adult and ignored dependence, which is a normal part of every life. Critics worry it can be partial, favouring those close to us, and may reinforce unequal burdens of caring.","etym":null,"example":"Deciding what to do about an ageing parent by attending to their needs and your relationship, not by applying a universal rule.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Feminist philosophy","Deontology"],"thinkers":["noddings","held","tronto"],"tags":["care ethics","caring","feminist ethics","gilligan","noddings","relational ethics"]},{"term":"Eudaimonia","def":"Greek for flourishing or living well: Aristotle’s name for the highest human good.","more":"Aristotle argued that eudaimonia is the one thing we seek for its own sake, and that it consists in activity of the soul in accordance with virtue over a complete life. It is often translated 'happiness', but it means a whole life going well, not a passing mood. Stoics and Epicureans also took eudaimonia as the goal, but disagreed about what it consists in.","etym":"Greek eu, 'good', + daimon, 'spirit': having a good guardian spirit","example":"A life of meaningful work, friendship and good character, not just pleasant feelings.","notConfused":"Not the same as feeling happy: you can feel cheerful while living badly, and a flourishing life can include hard times.","field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Telos"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"compass":"q20","tags":["eudaemonia","flourishing","happiness","human flourishing","well-being","aristotle"]},{"term":"Euthyphro dilemma","def":"The question from Plato's dialogue Euthyphro: is something good because God (or the gods) commands it, or does God command it because it is good?","more":"If goodness depends on God's command, it seems arbitrary: God could have commanded cruelty. If God commands things because they are good, goodness seems independent of God. Defenders of divine command theory, such as Robert Adams, try to escape by grounding goodness in God's unchanging nature. The dilemma is widely used to argue that morality does not depend on religion.","etym":"From Euthyphro, the man Socrates questions in Plato's dialogue of that name","example":"Is lying wrong because it is forbidden, or is it forbidden because it is wrong?","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["God","Moral realism","Metaethics"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato","adams_rm"],"tags":["euthyphro problem","euthyphro","divine command theory","plato","is it good because god commands"]},{"term":"Evidentialism","def":"The view that a belief is justified only if it fits the evidence you have.","more":"W. K. Clifford put an ethical version strongly in 1877, arguing that it is always wrong to believe anything on insufficient evidence. William James replied in The Will to Believe (1896) that for some live, momentous choices, such as religious belief, we may believe without conclusive evidence. Modern epistemologists such as Earl Conee and Richard Feldman defend evidentialism as a theory of justification.","etym":"Latin evidentia, 'clearness'","example":"You should not believe a rumour about a classmate just because you would like it to be true.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Justification","Fideism","Faith"],"thinkers":["clifford","james","feldman_r"],"tags":["evidence","clifford","proportion belief to evidence","ethics of belief"]},{"term":"Evolutionary debunking argument","def":"An argument that some beliefs, often moral ones, are untrustworthy because they were shaped by evolution for survival rather than for truth.","more":"Sharon Street argues that if moral realism were true, it would be a coincidence for evolved moral feelings to match the independent moral facts. Richard Joyce uses similar reasoning to support moral scepticism. Realists reply that the same logic would debunk maths and logic, or that tracking moral truth could itself have helped survival.","etym":null,"example":"We feel strongly that we owe more to family than to strangers; but would we feel that way whether or not it was true, simply because it helped our ancestors' genes?","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral realism","Anti-realism","Error theory"],"thinkers":["street","joyce"],"tags":["evolutionary debunking","debunking argument","street","evolution and morality","darwinian dilemma"]},{"term":"Existence","def":"Being real: being part of what there is, rather than merely imagined or possible.","more":"Philosophers ask whether existence is a property like redness. Kant argued that it is not a real predicate: saying 'God exists' adds nothing to the concept of God, which he used against the ontological argument. Frege and Russell analysed existence as a feature of concepts (having at least one instance) rather than of things. Existentialists use the word more narrowly for the concrete life of a human being.","etym":"Latin exsistere, 'to stand out, appear'","example":"Unicorns can be described in detail, but they do not exist; horses do.","notConfused":"Not the same as essence: essence is what a thing is; existence is that it is.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Essence","Ontology","Ontological argument"],"thinkers":["kant","frege","russell"],"tags":["being","existing","reality","is existence a predicate","ontology"]},{"term":"Existence precedes essence","def":"Sartre’s slogan: humans first exist and only then define themselves through their choices; there is no human nature fixed in advance.","more":"Sartre stated the slogan in his lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism (1945/46). It means that for humans there is no blueprint, whether from God or from nature, that fixes what we should be. Critics point to evidence of a shared human nature from biology and psychology, and Beauvoir stressed that social situation also limits how freely we create ourselves.","etym":null,"example":"A knife is made for cutting; a human is not made ‘for’ anything until they choose.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Existentialism","Essence","Bad faith"],"thinkers":["sartre"],"compass":"q21","tags":["sartre","existentialism","self-creation","no human nature","radical freedom"]},{"term":"Existentialism","def":"A 19th–20th century movement focused on individual existence, freedom and choice in a world with no ready-made meaning.","more":"Kierkegaard and Nietzsche are usually counted as forerunners; the label became popular in France after the Second World War through Sartre, Beauvoir and, though he rejected the name, Camus. Central themes are anxiety, authenticity, freedom and responsibility. Critics charge it with individualism, and some religious existentialists, such as Kierkegaard, saw freedom as leading to faith rather than away from it.","etym":"Latin existentia, 'existence'","example":"Choosing a career not because it was expected, but because you take responsibility for who you become.","notConfused":"Not the same as nihilism: existentialists accept there is no ready-made meaning, but most insist we can and must create it.","field":"Ethics","related":["Existence precedes essence","Absurd","Bad faith"],"thinkers":["kierkegaard","sartre","beauvoir","camus"],"compass":"q21","tags":["existentialist","sartre","kierkegaard","camus","de beauvoir","freedom and meaning"]},{"term":"Experience machine","def":"Nozick’s imagined device that gives you any experiences you want while you float in a tank. If you would not plug in, pleasure may not be all that matters.","more":"Robert Nozick introduced the machine in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974). He suggested we want to actually do things, to be a certain kind of person and to be in contact with reality, not just to have experiences. Some hedonists reply that our reluctance to plug in reflects status quo bias rather than a real value beyond pleasure.","etym":null,"example":"A perfect virtual-reality life where you think you are a famous musician.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Hedonism","Thought experiment"],"thinkers":["nozick"],"tags":["nozick","pleasure machine","matrix","hedonism","simulated pleasure"]},{"term":"Expression theory of art","def":"The view that art is essentially the expression of the artist's emotions, which the work then passes on to its audience.","more":"Tolstoy, in What Is Art? (1897), held that art transmits feelings the artist has lived through. R. G. Collingwood gave a subtler version in The Principles of Art (1938): expressing an emotion is a way of discovering and clarifying it, unlike arousing feelings on purpose, which he called craft. Critics note that many good works do not seem to express any emotion, and that we rarely know what the artist actually felt.","etym":null,"example":"A song written after a break-up that helps listeners make sense of their own heartbreak.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Formalism (art)","Intentional fallacy"],"thinkers":["tolstoy","collingwood"],"tags":["expressionism","art as expression","collingwood","tolstoy","emotion in art"]},{"term":"Expressivism","def":"The view that moral claims express attitudes, such as approval or plans for action, rather than describe facts. It is a more developed descendant of emotivism.","more":"Modern expressivists such as Simon Blackburn and Allan Gibbard try to explain why moral talk behaves like factual talk (we argue, reason and call moral claims 'true') while still resting on attitudes. Blackburn calls this project quasi-realism. Hume is often seen as a forerunner, because he grounded morality in sentiment rather than reason.","etym":"Latin exprimere, 'to press out'","example":"‘You ought to apologise’ expresses your stance toward apologising, not a fact about the universe.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Emotivism","Anti-realism"],"thinkers":["hume"],"compass":"q4","tags":["non-cognitivism","noncognitivism","quasi-realism","blackburn","gibbard","emotivism"]},{"term":"Extended mind","def":"The idea that the mind can reach beyond the skull, so that tools like notebooks or phones can be literal parts of our thinking.","more":"Andy Clark and David Chalmers argued for it in 1998 with the case of Otto, who has memory loss and relies on a notebook in the way others rely on memory. If the notebook plays the same role, they say, its contents count as Otto's beliefs. Critics reply that inner memory and outside tools differ in important ways, such as how directly we access them.","etym":null,"example":"Your phone's calendar remembers your appointments for you; is it part of your mind?","notConfused":"Not the same as telepathy or the soul leaving the body: it is a claim about which physical processes count as thinking.","field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Externalism","Physicalism"],"thinkers":["chalmers"],"tags":["extended cognition","clark and chalmers","otto's notebook","external mind","embodied cognition"]},{"term":"Externalism","def":"The name for several views holding that something about the mind or knowledge depends on factors outside the person.","more":"In philosophy of mind and language, content externalism (Putnam, Burge) says what your words and thoughts mean depends partly on your environment: someone on a 'Twin Earth' with a different liquid would mean something else by 'water'. In epistemology, externalism says justification can depend on facts you cannot access, such as whether your belief came from a reliable process (Goldman's reliabilism). Both are opposed by forms of internalism.","etym":"Latin externus, 'outward'","example":"On reliabilism, a young child's belief that there is a dog in front of her can be justified because her eyesight is reliable, even though she cannot explain why.","notConfused":"Not the same as the extended mind: externalism about content concerns what thoughts are about; the extended mind concerns where thinking happens.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Internalism","Justification","Extended mind"],"thinkers":["putnam","goldman"],"tags":["epistemic externalism","reliabilism","content externalism","semantic externalism","twin earth"]},{"term":"Faith","def":"Trust or belief that goes beyond what can be proven, especially in religious matters.","more":"Thinkers disagree about how faith relates to reason. Aquinas held that some truths about God can be shown by reason and others are accepted by faith, and the two cannot conflict. Kierkegaard described faith as a passionate commitment that reason cannot secure. Evidentialists like Clifford argue that believing beyond the evidence is a moral failing, while others say faith is more like trust in a person than a belief about facts.","etym":"Latin fides, 'trust'","example":"Trusting that a friend will keep a promise, even without proof, is a non-religious kind of faith.","notConfused":"Not the same as blind belief: many religious thinkers see faith as reasonable trust, not belief against all evidence.","field":"Religion","related":["Fideism","Evidentialism","Pascal’s wager"],"thinkers":["aquinas","kierkegaard"],"tags":["religious belief","trust","fides","belief in god","fideism"]},{"term":"Fallacy","def":"A common mistake in reasoning that can make a bad argument look good.","more":"Philosophers distinguish formal fallacies, where the logical shape of the argument is broken (such as affirming the consequent), from informal fallacies, where the problem lies in the content or context (such as ad hominem or equivocation). Aristotle catalogued many in his Sophistical Refutations. Naming a fallacy is not a knock-down reply: you still need to show that the reasoning actually goes wrong in this case.","etym":"Latin fallacia, 'deceit, trick', from fallere, 'to deceive'","example":"Saying a diet works because a famous actor uses it commits a fallacy.","notConfused":"Not the same as a false belief: a fallacy is a flaw in reasoning, and a fallacious argument can still have a true conclusion.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Ad hominem","Straw man","Begging the question","Valid"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["fallacies","logical fallacy","bad argument","error in reasoning","faulty reasoning"]},{"term":"Fallibilism","def":"The view that we can know things even though we might be wrong. Knowledge does not require absolute certainty.","more":"The term was made popular by Charles Peirce, who held that inquiry can always correct itself. Fallibilism is now the majority view among epistemologists, because demanding certainty would leave us knowing almost nothing. The puzzle it faces is how to explain why saying 'I know it, but I might be wrong' sounds odd.","etym":"Latin fallibilis, 'liable to err'","example":"You know your bike is where you left it, even though it could, in theory, have been stolen.","notConfused":"Not the same as scepticism: the sceptic says we lack knowledge, while the fallibilist says we have plenty of it, just not with certainty.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Skepticism","Justification"],"thinkers":["peirce","popper"],"compass":"q14","tags":["fallibility","fallibilist","could be wrong","peirce","uncertainty"]},{"term":"False dilemma","def":"Pretending there are only two options when there are more.","more":"Also called a false dichotomy or the either-or fallacy. It works by hiding the other options, such as middle positions, combinations, or doing nothing at all. Real dilemmas exist, so the question is always whether the two options listed really are the only ones.","etym":"Greek dilemma, 'double proposition'","example":"‘Either you support every part of this plan, or you want the city to fail.’","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["false dichotomy","either-or fallacy","black-and-white thinking","false choice","bifurcation"]},{"term":"Falsifiability","def":"Popper’s test for a scientific theory: it must make claims that some possible observation could prove wrong.","more":"Karl Popper proposed falsifiability in the 1930s to separate science from non-science, which he called the demarcation problem. He thought theories cannot be proven true by any number of confirming cases, but can be shown false by one clear counterexample. Critics such as Quine and Kuhn, drawing on the earlier work of Pierre Duhem, point out that scientists can usually save a theory by adjusting a background assumption instead, so refutation is rarely so simple.","etym":"From English 'falsify', Latin falsus, 'false'","example":"‘All swans are white’ is falsifiable: one black swan would refute it. ‘Everything happens for a reason’ is not.","notConfused":"Not the same as being false: a falsifiable claim is one that could be shown false, and it may well be true.","field":"Science","related":["Problem of induction","Paradigm","Verification principle"],"thinkers":["popper"],"tags":["falsification","falsificationism","popper","refutability","testability","demarcation problem"]},{"term":"Fatalism","def":"The view that whatever will happen must happen, so our efforts make no difference to the outcome.","more":"Logical fatalism argues from truth: if it is already true that you will pass tomorrow's exam, nothing you do can change that. Aristotle discussed this with the example of a sea battle tomorrow. Most philosophers think the argument confuses 'it is true that X will happen' with 'X must happen'. Fatalism differs from determinism, which says outcomes happen because of causes, including our own efforts.","etym":"Latin fatum, 'what has been spoken', destiny","example":"Refusing to revise because 'if I'm meant to pass, I'll pass anyway'.","notConfused":"Not the same as determinism: a determinist says your revising is one of the causes of passing, so effort still matters.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Determinism","Free will","Indeterminism"],"thinkers":["aristotle","richard_taylor"],"tags":["fate","destiny","inevitability","predestination","que sera sera"]},{"term":"Feminist philosophy","def":"Philosophy that examines how gender shapes society, knowledge and ideas, and that aims to understand and end the oppression of women.","more":"Early works include Mary Wollstonecraft's A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) and Simone de Beauvoir's The Second Sex (1949), which argued that one becomes a woman through social forces. Feminist philosophers work in every field: ethics (the ethics of care), epistemology (whose knowledge counts), and political philosophy (justice within the family). Feminist thinkers disagree with each other on many questions, for example about sex, work and the role of the state.","etym":"Latin femina, 'woman'","example":"Asking why household and caring work has often been left out of theories of justice.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Ethics of care","Epistemic injustice","Justice"],"thinkers":["wollstonecraft","beauvoir"],"tags":["feminism","gender","de beauvoir","feminist theory","patriarchy","feminist ethics"]},{"term":"Fideism","def":"The view that religious belief rests on faith rather than reason, and does not need, or cannot get, rational proof.","more":"Fideists range from those who say reason is simply not the right tool for faith to those, like some readings of Kierkegaard, who embrace belief that reason finds paradoxical. Pascal is sometimes counted a fideist because he held that the heart has reasons reason cannot know. Critics say fideism leaves no way to choose between rival faiths.","etym":"Latin fides, 'faith'","example":"Saying 'I can't prove God exists, and I don't need to: I believe.'","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Faith","Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["kierkegaard","pascal"],"tags":["faith over reason","kierkegaard","pascal","faith","credo quia absurdum"]},{"term":"Filial piety","def":"The Confucian virtue of respect, care and loyalty toward one's parents and ancestors.","more":"The Analects treats filial piety (xiao) as the root of humaneness (ren), in a saying of Confucius' disciple Youzi: we first learn to care for others within the family. Confucius stresses that it is not mere feeding, but serving parents with respect, and that a child may gently remonstrate with parents who go wrong. Mohists criticised Confucians for favouring family over impartial care, and modern critics ask how far it supports obedience.","etym":"Translates Chinese xiao (孝)","example":"Visiting and looking after elderly parents, and honouring them after their death.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Ren","Li (ritual propriety)","Junzi"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius","mozi"],"tags":["xiao","hsiao","respect for parents","family duty","confucianism"]},{"term":"Five Ways","def":"Thomas Aquinas' five short arguments for the existence of God, from motion, causation, contingency, degrees of perfection and the order of nature.","more":"Aquinas set them out in his Summa Theologiae (begun c. 1265), building on Aristotle and earlier Islamic and Jewish thinkers. Each argues from a familiar feature of the world to a first cause or source, which he says everyone calls God. Critics ask why the chain of causes cannot go back forever, and whether a first cause must have the qualities of God.","etym":null,"example":"Every event you know of has a cause; Aquinas argues there must be a first cause that is not itself caused.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Cosmological argument","Design argument","Kalam"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"tags":["quinque viae","aquinas","five proofs","arguments for god","thomas aquinas"]},{"term":"Folk psychology","def":"Our everyday way of explaining and predicting behaviour in terms of beliefs, desires, hopes and fears.","more":"We use folk psychology constantly: she took an umbrella because she believed it would rain and wanted to stay dry. Philosophers debate whether it is a theory that science might prove false (as eliminative materialists claim) or a practice that will always be needed. Dennett argues it works because treating systems as having beliefs and desires is predictively useful.","etym":null,"example":"Guessing that your friend is quiet because she is worried about her exam.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Eliminative materialism","Functionalism","Intentionality"],"thinkers":["churchland","dennett"],"tags":["commonsense psychology","belief-desire psychology","theory of mind","propositional attitudes","eliminative materialism"]},{"term":"Formalism (art)","def":"The view that what matters in art is its form: its lines, colours, sounds and structure, rather than what it depicts or expresses.","more":"Clive Bell argued in Art (1914) that all real art shares 'significant form', which produces a special aesthetic emotion. Eduard Hanslick held that music's beauty lies in its moving patterns of sound, not in representing feelings. Critics point out that knowing a work's subject, history or meaning often changes what we see in it.","etym":"Latin forma, 'shape'","example":"Admiring an abstract painting for its balance of colours without asking what it is 'about'.","notConfused":"Not the same as formal logic: formalism here is a theory of art.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Expression theory of art","Institutional theory of art"],"thinkers":["bell","hanslick"],"tags":["aesthetic formalism","significant form","clive bell","form over content","formalism"]},{"term":"Forms","def":"Plato’s perfect, unchanging patterns (such as Justice itself or Beauty itself) that the things we see only imperfectly copy.","more":"Plato argued that the Forms are more real than physical things: they are eternal, unchanging and known by reason rather than the senses. His Allegory of the Cave pictures ordinary people as watching shadows, while the philosopher turns to see what casts them. Aristotle, Plato's own student, objected that forms exist only in things, not in a separate realm.","etym":"Greek eidos or idea, 'look, shape, form'","example":"No drawn circle is perfectly round, yet we understand the perfect circle they all fall short of.","notConfused":"Not the same as shape: a Platonic Form is an ideal pattern, such as Beauty itself, not the outline of an object.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Platonism","Universals"],"thinkers":["plato"],"tags":["theory of forms","platonic forms","ideas","plato","eidos","theory of ideas"]},{"term":"Foundationalism","def":"The view that justified beliefs rest on a base of basic beliefs that are justified without needing support from other beliefs.","more":"Foundationalism answers the regress problem: if every belief needs another to support it, the chain must end somewhere. Descartes sought a foundation in certain beliefs such as 'I think, therefore I am'; modern foundationalists usually accept more modest foundations, such as beliefs about how things look to us. Its main rival is coherentism, which says beliefs are justified by fitting together, like a web.","etym":"Latin fundamentum, 'foundation'","example":"Your belief that the room looks bright needs no further argument; it can support other beliefs about the room.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Justification","Infinite regress","Epistemology"],"thinkers":["descartes","bonjour"],"tags":["basic beliefs","foundations of knowledge","foundational beliefs","descartes","regress problem"]},{"term":"Four causes","def":"Aristotle's four kinds of answer to 'why?': what a thing is made of, its form, what brought it about, and what it is for.","more":"They are the material cause (the stuff), the formal cause (the structure or pattern), the efficient cause (the maker or trigger) and the final cause (the purpose or end). Aristotle thought a full explanation of anything in nature needed all four. Early modern science dropped final causes from physics, but they remain debated in biology and ethics.","etym":"Greek aitia, 'reason, explanation', usually translated 'cause'","example":"For a statue: bronze (material), the shape of a hero (formal), the sculptor (efficient), and honouring the hero (final).","notConfused":"Not the same as the modern idea of cause: Aristotle's 'causes' are kinds of explanation, not only events that make things happen.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Telos","Causation","Hylomorphism"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["aristotle's four causes","material cause","formal cause","efficient cause","final cause","aitia"]},{"term":"Four Noble Truths","def":"The Buddha's core teaching: life involves suffering, suffering has a cause in craving, it can end, and there is a path to its end.","more":"Tradition holds that the Buddha taught them in his first sermon after his awakening. The structure is often compared to a doctor's: diagnosis, cause, prognosis, cure. The fourth truth is the Eightfold Path. Scholars note the word translated 'suffering', dukkha, covers wider unsatisfactoriness, not just pain.","etym":"Pali cattari ariyasaccani, 'four truths of the noble ones'","example":"Feeling let down after getting something you badly wanted, because the craving simply moves on, illustrates the second truth.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dukkha","Eightfold Path","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["noble truths","dukkha","suffering","buddha","cessation of suffering","eightfold path"]},{"term":"Frankfurt cases","def":"Thought experiments by Harry Frankfurt meant to show that a person can be morally responsible even if they could not have done otherwise.","more":"In Frankfurt's 1969 example, a hidden controller is ready to make Jones act if Jones hesitates, but Jones acts on his own. Since Jones could not have done otherwise but acted freely, Frankfurt concluded that alternative possibilities are not needed for responsibility. Critics, such as Widerker and Kane, argue that if indeterminism is true the controller could not know in time whether to step in.","etym":"Named after Harry Frankfurt (1929–2023)","example":"A scientist secretly wires your brain to make you vote for Smith if you start to choose Jones, but you choose Smith anyway.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Free will","Moral responsibility","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["frankfurt","widerker","fischer"],"tags":["frankfurt-style cases","frankfurt examples","principle of alternate possibilities","pap","harry frankfurt"]},{"term":"Free rider problem","def":"The problem that people can benefit from a shared good without paying for it, so if everyone reasons that way the good is not provided.","more":"Public goods like clean air, national defence or street lighting are hard to exclude people from. Each person may reason that their own contribution makes little difference, so it is rational to let others pay. The problem is central to arguments about why we need states, taxes or social norms, and is a classic case where individual and group rationality come apart.","etym":null,"example":"Enjoying a group project's grade while letting the others do all the work.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Social contract","State of nature","Egoism"],"thinkers":["hume","hobbes"],"tags":["free riding","freeloader","free-rider","public goods","collective action problem"]},{"term":"Free will","def":"The kind of control over our actions needed for them to really be ours and for us to be responsible for them.","more":"The central debate is whether free will can exist if determinism is true. Compatibilists say yes; libertarians say free will needs indeterminism and that we have it; hard incompatibilists and hard determinists say we lack the kind of freedom that would make us deserve blame or praise. Frankfurt's cases, where a person could not have done otherwise but still seems responsible, changed the shape of the debate after 1969.","etym":null,"example":"Did you choose cereal this morning, or did your brain and history make that ‘choice’ for you?","notConfused":"Not the same as political freedom: you may be free to vote yet, on some views, lack free will; free will is about whether choices are truly yours.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Determinism","Compatibilism","Libertarianism (free will)","Hard incompatibilism"],"thinkers":["augustine","kant","frankfurt"],"compass":"q8","tags":["freedom of choice","free choice","choice","freedom of the will","agency","determinism"]},{"term":"Free will defense","def":"The reply to the problem of evil that a world with free creatures is better than one without, and freedom brings the possibility of evil.","more":"Augustine argued that evil comes from the misuse of free will, not from God. Alvin Plantinga gave a modern, logically careful version in the 1970s, aiming to show only that God and evil are not contradictory. Critics ask why God did not create free beings who always choose well, and note that the defence does not easily explain natural evils such as earthquakes.","etym":null,"example":"A parent lets a teenager make choices, knowing some will be bad ones.","notConfused":"Not the same as a full theodicy: a defence aims only to show that God and evil are consistent, not to explain God's actual reasons.","field":"Religion","related":["Problem of evil","Theodicy","Free will"],"thinkers":["augustine"],"compass":"q19","tags":["free will defence","plantinga","problem of evil","theodicy","augustine"]},{"term":"Functionalism","def":"The view that a mental state is defined by what it does (its causes, effects and links to other states), not by what it is made of.","more":"Functionalism became the leading theory of mind in the 1960s and 70s, partly because it allows the same mental state to exist in very different bodies or machines, an idea called multiple realisability. It underlies much of cognitive science and artificial intelligence. Critics such as Searle (with the Chinese room) and Block argue that getting the functions right may still leave out understanding or conscious experience.","etym":"Latin functio, 'performance'","example":"On functionalism, a robot could feel pain if something in it played pain’s role: caused by damage, causing avoidance.","notConfused":"Not the same as behaviourism: functionalists count inner states and their links to one another, not just outward behaviour.","field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism","Identity theory","Behaviorism"],"thinkers":["putnam","lewis","dennett"],"compass":"q6","tags":["functional role","machine functionalism","multiple realisability","putnam","mind as software"]},{"term":"Genealogy","def":"A method that studies the history of a value or idea to show where it came from, often to loosen its hold over us.","more":"Nietzsche's On the Genealogy of Morality (1887) argued that values such as humility and pity arose from the resentment of the weak against the strong. Later Michel Foucault used genealogy to trace how institutions such as prisons shaped ideas of normality. Philosophers debate whether showing an idea's history can show it is false, which risks the genetic fallacy.","etym":"Greek genea, 'family, descent', + logos, 'account'","example":"Asking how our idea of 'proper manners' grew from the habits of a particular social class.","notConfused":"Not the same as family-tree research, though Nietzsche borrowed the word from it.","field":"Method","related":["Genetic fallacy","Will to power","Ideology"],"thinkers":["nietzsche","williams"],"tags":["genealogical method","nietzsche","foucault","genealogy of morals","history of concepts"]},{"term":"General will","def":"Rousseau's idea of the shared will of the people as a whole, aimed at the common good rather than at private interests.","more":"In The Social Contract (1762), Rousseau distinguished the general will from the 'will of all', which is just the sum of private wants. Laws are legitimate when they express the general will, and citizens are free when they obey laws they give themselves. Critics such as Isaiah Berlin warned that the idea could be used to justify forcing people to be 'free' against their wishes.","etym":"French volonté générale","example":"Voting for a clean-air law because it is good for the town, even though you own a factory that pollutes.","notConfused":"Not the same as majority opinion: Rousseau thought a majority could be wrong about the general will.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Social contract","Democracy","Legitimacy"],"thinkers":["rousseau","berlin"],"tags":["volonté générale","volonte generale","rousseau","common good","social contract","popular sovereignty"]},{"term":"Genetic fallacy","def":"Judging a claim true or false because of where it came from, rather than on its merits.","more":"It is a fallacy because a good idea can have a bad source and the reverse. The line is not always sharp: a claim's origin can be relevant evidence about how reliable it is, as with a well-known liar or a careful scientist. Debunking arguments and genealogies have to show why a particular origin matters.","etym":"Greek genesis, 'origin'","example":"'That theory was first proposed by a fraudster, so it must be wrong.'","notConfused":"Not the same as ad hominem in every case: the genetic fallacy can concern any origin, such as a culture or historical period, not just a person.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Ad hominem","Genealogy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["fallacy of origins","origin fallacy","source fallacy","ad hominem"]},{"term":"Gettier problem","def":"Cases where someone has a justified true belief that still does not seem like knowledge, because they got lucky.","more":"In a three-page paper of 1963, Edmund Gettier gave cases where a person has a justified true belief that is true only by luck. The paper challenged the traditional analysis of knowledge as justified true belief and set off decades of attempts to add a fourth condition. Earlier cases of the same kind appear in Russell's stopped clock and, much earlier, in the Indian Buddhist philosopher Dharmottara (8th century).","etym":"Named after the American philosopher Edmund Gettier (1927–2021)","example":"You look at a stopped clock that happens to show the right time. Your belief is true and justified, but is it knowledge?","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Justification","Epistemology"],"thinkers":["russell"],"tags":["gettier cases","gettier","justified true belief","analysis of knowledge","epistemic luck"]},{"term":"Ghost in the machine","def":"Gilbert Ryle's mocking phrase for Descartes' view that the mind is a non-physical thing inside the body.","more":"In The Concept of Mind (1949), Ryle argued that Cartesian dualism makes a 'category mistake': treating the mind as a separate object, when talk of the mind is really talk about how a person behaves and is disposed to behave. His view pushed philosophy of mind toward behaviourism and later functionalism. Dualists reply that experience cannot be reduced to behaviour.","etym":null,"example":"Asking 'where is the university?' after being shown all its colleges and libraries is the kind of category mistake Ryle had in mind.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Dualism","Behaviorism","Functionalism"],"thinkers":["descartes"],"tags":["ryle","cartesian dualism","category mistake","mind-body dualism","descartes"]},{"term":"God","def":"In philosophy of religion, usually a being who is all-powerful, all-knowing, perfectly good, and the creator of everything else.","more":"This idea is shared by the main Western monotheisms and is called classical theism. Philosophers argue about whether such a being exists (through arguments such as the ontological, cosmological and design arguments, and the problem of evil) and about whether the attributes fit together. Other traditions conceive the divine differently, for example as an impersonal ground of being or as present in all things.","etym":"Old English god; Greek theos and Latin deus give 'theism' and 'deism'","example":"Asking whether an all-powerful God could make a stone too heavy for God to lift tests whether 'all-powerful' makes sense.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Ontological argument","Problem of evil","Euthyphro dilemma"],"thinkers":["anselm","aquinas"],"tags":["deity","supreme being","the divine","creator","allah","theism"]},{"term":"Golden mean","def":"Aristotle’s idea that each virtue lies between two vices: one of too much and one of too little.","more":"Aristotle stressed that the mean is 'relative to us': the right amount of anger or generosity depends on the person and situation, so it is not a mathematical midpoint. Practical wisdom (phronesis) is needed to find it. Confucian thought has a related ideal of the 'doctrine of the mean' (zhongyong), though the two developed separately.","etym":"Latin aurea mediocritas, 'golden middle', a phrase from the Roman poet Horace","example":"Courage sits between cowardice (too little) and recklessness (too much).","notConfused":"Not the same as always choosing a compromise: sometimes the mean is a strong reaction, such as great anger at a great injustice.","field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue","Virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["doctrine of the mean","the mean","moderation","aristotle","middle path","virtue"]},{"term":"Golden rule","def":"The principle of treating others as you would want to be treated.","more":"Versions appear in many traditions: Confucius' advice not to do to others what you would not want done to yourself, Jewish and Christian scripture, and others. Kant thought it too weak to be a basic moral law, partly because people's wants differ. Philosophers still see it as an early form of the demand that morality be impartial and reversible.","etym":null,"example":"Not spreading a rumour about a classmate because you would hate others to do it to you.","notConfused":"Not the same as Kant's categorical imperative: Kant asks whether your rule could hold for everyone, not what you personally would like.","field":"Ethics","related":["Categorical imperative","Ren","Ethics"],"thinkers":["confucius"],"tags":["do unto others","ethic of reciprocity","reciprocity","treat others as you would be treated"]},{"term":"Good will","def":"For Kant, the will to do one's duty because it is right; the only thing good without qualification.","more":"Kant opens his Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785) by arguing that intelligence, courage and even happiness can be bad when used by a bad will. A good will is good in itself, even if it achieves nothing. Critics, such as consequentialists, reply that good intentions that never help anyone are of limited worth.","etym":null,"example":"A shopkeeper who gives correct change because it is right, rather than to protect his reputation, acts from a good will.","notConfused":"Not the same as goodwill in the sense of friendliness: Kant means acting from duty.","field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Categorical imperative","Kingdom of ends"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["goodwill","kant","moral worth","acting from duty","good intentions"]},{"term":"Grue","def":"Nelson Goodman's made-up word for things that are green if examined before a certain future time and blue otherwise, used to pose the 'new riddle of induction'.","more":"All emeralds observed so far are green, but they are also all grue. Why does our evidence support 'all emeralds are green' rather than 'all emeralds are grue', which predicts future emeralds will be blue? Goodman, in Fact, Fiction, and Forecast (1955), argued that the answer lies in which words are 'entrenched' in our past predictions. The puzzle shows that induction depends on choosing the right categories.","etym":"A blend of 'green' and 'blue'","example":"Every emerald you have seen supports 'emeralds are grue' just as well as 'emeralds are green'.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Induction","Problem of induction"],"thinkers":["goodman"],"tags":["grue paradox","new riddle of induction","goodman","bleen","projectibility","induction"]},{"term":"Hard determinism","def":"The view that determinism is true and therefore we do not have free will.","more":"Hard determinists are incompatibilists: they think free will and determinism cannot both be true, and they accept determinism. Baron d'Holbach argued for it in the 18th century. Many hard determinists argue that this should change how we punish, away from retribution and towards protection and reform. Compatibilists reply that freedom means acting on your own reasons, not escaping causes.","etym":null,"example":"A hard determinist says that someone who commits a crime could not have done otherwise, given their genes and life history.","notConfused":"Not the same as fatalism: hard determinists agree that our choices cause outcomes; they deny those choices are free in the deep sense.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Determinism","Hard incompatibilism","Free will"],"thinkers":["holbach"],"tags":["determinism","incompatibilism","no free will","free will denial"]},{"term":"Hard incompatibilism","def":"The view that free will of the kind needed for deserving blame is incompatible with both determinism and indeterminism, so we probably lack it.","more":"Derk Pereboom developed this view, arguing that if our actions are determined we are not ultimately responsible, and if they are random we are not either. He argues that life without basic desert need not be bleak: we can still hold people to account for the future, protect society and value relationships. Critics say the view underestimates how central blame and gratitude are to human life.","etym":null,"example":"A hard incompatibilist would still lock up a dangerous person, to protect others, not because they deserve to suffer.","notConfused":"Not the same as hard determinism: hard determinism says determinism is true, while hard incompatibilism says free will is ruled out whether or not determinism is true.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Free will","Desert","Moral responsibility"],"thinkers":["pereboom","gstrawson"],"compass":"q8","tags":["pereboom","no free will","free will skepticism","incompatibilism","moral responsibility"]},{"term":"Hard problem","def":"David Chalmers’ name for the question of why and how physical processes give rise to conscious experience at all.","more":"Chalmers contrasted it, in 1995, with the 'easy problems' of explaining how the brain processes information, controls behaviour and reports its states. Those may be very hard in practice, but we know what an answer would look like. Some, such as Dennett, deny there is a separate hard problem; others think it shows physicalism is incomplete.","etym":null,"example":"We can explain how the eye sends signals to the brain, but why does it feel like anything to see?","notConfused":"Not the same as the mind-body problem in general: it focuses on why there is subjective experience at all.","field":"Mind","related":["Qualia","Zombie","Panpsychism"],"thinkers":["chalmers","nagel"],"compass":"q6","tags":["hard problem of consciousness","chalmers","explanatory gap","consciousness","qualia","why experience"]},{"term":"Harm principle","def":"Mill’s principle that the only good reason to restrict someone’s liberty against their will is to prevent harm to others.","more":"Mill set it out in On Liberty (1859), saying a person's own good, physical or moral, is not enough reason to interfere with them. Debate turns on what counts as harm: does offence count, and what about harms spread across society? The principle is a foundation of liberal arguments for freedom of speech and against paternalism.","etym":null,"example":"The law may ban drunk driving, but not, on this view, unhealthy eating.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'do no harm' in medicine: the harm principle is about when the state or society may restrict liberty.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Paternalism","Liberty (negative/positive)"],"thinkers":["mill"],"compass":"q26","tags":["mill","on liberty","liberty principle","harm to others","personal freedom"]},{"term":"Hasty generalisation","def":"Drawing a general conclusion from too few or untypical examples.","more":"It is a misuse of induction: the sample is too small or biased to support the conclusion. Stereotypes often rest on it. Statistics offers tools to judge when a sample is big and varied enough.","etym":null,"example":"'I've met two rude people from that town, so everyone there is rude.'","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Induction"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["hasty generalization","overgeneralisation","overgeneralization","jumping to conclusions","small sample","anecdotal evidence"]},{"term":"Hedonic calculus","def":"Jeremy Bentham's method for measuring pleasures and pains in order to choose the action that produces the most happiness.","more":"In An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), Bentham listed factors such as intensity, duration, certainty, nearness, fruitfulness, purity and extent (how many people are affected). Critics argue that pleasures cannot really be measured on one scale, and Mill held that some pleasures are higher in quality, not just quantity.","etym":"Greek hedone, 'pleasure'; Latin calculus, 'pebble used for counting'","example":"Comparing a short, intense party with a longer, calmer evening that won't leave you tired for an exam.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Hedonism","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["bentham","mill"],"tags":["felicific calculus","bentham","utility calculus","pleasure calculation","utilitarianism"]},{"term":"Hedonism","def":"The view that pleasure is the only thing good in itself and pain the only thing bad in itself.","more":"Ethical hedonism says pleasure is the only intrinsic good; psychological hedonism says we in fact always seek pleasure. Bentham treated all pleasures as comparable by amount, while Mill argued some pleasures are higher in quality. Nozick's experience machine is the best-known objection: most people would not plug in, which suggests they value more than pleasant experiences.","etym":"Greek hedone, 'pleasure'","example":"A hedonist says friendship is good only because it brings pleasure.","notConfused":"Not the same as the everyday sense of indulgence: Epicurus, a hedonist, recommended simple food and calm.","field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Experience machine","Epicureanism"],"thinkers":["epicurus","bentham"],"tags":["pleasure","hedonist","pleasure principle","epicureanism","pursuit of pleasure"]},{"term":"Hermeneutics","def":"The theory and practice of interpretation, especially of texts but also of actions, art and whole cultures.","more":"It began with methods for interpreting scripture and law. Friedrich Schleiermacher made it a general theory of understanding, and Heidegger and Hans-Georg Gadamer argued that all human understanding is interpretive. A central idea is the 'hermeneutic circle': we understand parts in the light of the whole, and the whole through its parts.","etym":"Greek hermeneuein, 'to interpret', traditionally linked with the god Hermes, messenger of the gods","example":"Reading a single line of a poem differently once you have read the whole poem.","notConfused":null,"field":"Method","related":["Phenomenology","Language game","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["schleiermacher","heidegger"],"tags":["interpretation","hermeneutic circle","gadamer","schleiermacher","textual interpretation"]},{"term":"Historical materialism","def":"Marx's theory that history is driven mainly by changes in how societies produce what they need, and by the class conflicts these create.","more":"Marx and Engels held that the economic 'base' (technology and relations of production) shapes the 'superstructure' of law, politics, religion and ideas. Societies move through stages, such as feudalism and capitalism, as new productive forces clash with old social relations. Critics argue that ideas and politics shape economics as much as the reverse, and that its predictions have often failed.","etym":"A term popularised by Engels; Marx himself did not use it. Engels also called the theory the 'materialist conception of history'.","example":"Explaining the decline of feudal lords by the growth of trade and towns, rather than by the ideas of kings.","notConfused":"Not the same as materialism in philosophy of mind: historical materialism is a theory of social change.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Ideology","Alienation","Dialectic"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["materialist conception of history","marx","base and superstructure","dialectical materialism","class struggle"]},{"term":"Holism","def":"The view that a whole must be understood as a whole, because its parts get their nature or meaning from their place in it.","more":"In philosophy of science, confirmation holism (from Duhem and Quine) says that theories face the evidence as a group: a failed prediction shows that something in the web of beliefs is wrong, but not exactly what. In philosophy of language, meaning holism says a word's meaning depends on its links to many other words. Critics worry that extreme holism makes learning, communication or testing impossible.","etym":"Greek holos, 'whole'","example":"When an experiment fails, scientists can blame the equipment, a background assumption or the theory itself.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Falsifiability","Emergence","Incommensurability"],"thinkers":["quine","duhem"],"tags":["confirmation holism","quine-duhem thesis","duhem-quine","web of belief","whole over parts"]},{"term":"Humanism","def":"An outlook that puts human beings, human reason and human welfare at the centre of thought and value.","more":"Renaissance humanism (14th–16th centuries) was a movement to revive classical Greek and Latin learning and the study of the humanities. Modern secular humanism holds that people can live ethical, meaningful lives without religion. Sartre called existentialism a humanism; later critics, such as some postmodern thinkers, questioned the idea of a fixed human nature.","etym":"Latin humanitas, 'human nature, culture'","example":"Supporting science and education because they improve human lives, without appeal to religious authority.","notConfused":"Not the same as humanitarianism: humanitarianism is the practical work of relieving suffering.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Enlightenment","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["montaigne"],"tags":["humanist","renaissance humanism","secular humanism","human dignity","erasmus"]},{"term":"Hume's fork","def":"Hume's division of all knowledge into 'relations of ideas', true by definition, and 'matters of fact', known by experience.","more":"Relations of ideas, like maths, are certain but tell us nothing about the world; matters of fact tell us about the world but are never certain. Hume ended his Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748) by saying books with neither kind of reasoning should be committed to the flames. The fork anticipates Kant's analytic/synthetic distinction and the logical positivists' verification principle.","etym":"Named after David Hume (1711–1776)","example":"'All bachelors are unmarried' is a relation of ideas; 'the café opens at eight' is a matter of fact.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Analytic/synthetic","A priori","Verification principle"],"thinkers":["hume"],"tags":["relations of ideas","matters of fact","hume","analytic/synthetic","empiricism"]},{"term":"Hylomorphism","def":"Aristotle's view that ordinary things are a combination of matter and form.","more":"The matter is what a thing is made of; the form is the structure that makes it the kind of thing it is. Applied to living things, the soul is the form of the body, which is why Aristotle doubted the soul could exist apart from it. Aquinas adapted the view for Christian theology, and it has revived in recent metaphysics as an account of how parts make up wholes.","etym":"Greek hyle, 'matter, wood', + morphe, 'form'","example":"A bronze statue: bronze is the matter, the statue's shape is the form.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Four causes","Substance","Essence"],"thinkers":["aristotle","aquinas"],"tags":["matter and form","form and matter","aristotle","hylemorphism","substance"]},{"term":"Hypothetical imperative","def":"A command that applies only if you want something: 'if you want X, do Y'.","more":"Kant contrasted hypothetical imperatives with the categorical imperative, which binds everyone whatever they want. He argued morality must be categorical, because moral duties do not disappear when we lack the relevant desire. Philippa Foot later questioned this, suggesting morality might be a system of hypothetical imperatives.","etym":"Greek hypothesis, 'supposition'; Latin imperare, 'to command'","example":"'If you want to get fit, exercise regularly.'","notConfused":"Not the same as the categorical imperative: that applies regardless of your goals.","field":"Ethics","related":["Categorical imperative","Deontology","Good will"],"thinkers":["kant","foot"],"tags":["conditional imperative","kant","if you want","instrumental reason","categorical imperative"]},{"term":"Hypothetico-deductive method","def":"The scientific method of proposing a hypothesis, deducing predictions from it, and testing those predictions by observation.","more":"If the predictions fail, the hypothesis is in trouble; if they succeed, it gains support but is not proven. Popper favoured this picture, stressing refutation. Critics note that it says little about how good hypotheses are found, and that holism makes it hard to know which assumption a failed test refutes.","etym":null,"example":"If a new drug lowers blood pressure, patients taking it should show lower readings than those on a placebo.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Falsifiability","Induction","Inference to the best explanation"],"thinkers":["popper"],"tags":["scientific method","hypothesis testing","h-d method","hypothetico deductive","falsification"]},{"term":"Idealism","def":"The view that reality is fundamentally mental: what exists is minds and their ideas.","more":"There are several kinds. Berkeley's subjective idealism says material objects are collections of ideas in minds, ultimately sustained by God. Kant's transcendental idealism says we know things only as they appear to us, shaped by the structure of our minds. Hegel's absolute idealism treats reality as the unfolding of a single rational spirit. Materialists and realists reject all three.","etym":"Greek idea, 'form, appearance'","example":"Berkeley argued that a tree is a bundle of perceptions: it exists by being perceived.","notConfused":"Not the same as having high ideals: philosophical idealism is a theory about what reality is made of.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Materialism"],"thinkers":["berkeley","hegel"],"compass":"q6","tags":["idealist","berkeley","mind-dependence","immaterialism","hegel","german idealism"]},{"term":"Identity","def":"Being one and the same thing. Philosophers distinguish numerical identity (being literally the same object) from qualitative identity (being exactly alike).","more":"Identity raises puzzles about change: if every part of a ship is replaced, is it the same ship? It also matters for personal identity, the question of what makes you the same person over time. Logicians treat identity as a relation each thing has only to itself, governed by Leibniz's law: if a and b are identical, whatever is true of one is true of the other.","etym":"Latin idem, 'the same'","example":"Identical twins are qualitatively similar but numerically two people.","notConfused":"Not the same as identity in the social sense (such as national or gender identity), which concerns how people understand themselves.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Personal identity","Ship of Theseus","Identity of indiscernibles"],"thinkers":["leibniz"],"tags":["sameness","numerical identity","qualitative identity","self-identity","personal identity"]},{"term":"Identity of indiscernibles","def":"Leibniz's principle that no two distinct things can be exactly alike in every respect.","more":"Leibniz argued for it partly from the principle of sufficient reason: God would have no reason to place one of two identical things here and the other there. Max Black challenged it in 1952 with a possible universe containing just two exactly similar iron spheres. Its converse, that identical things share all properties, is much less controversial.","etym":null,"example":"No two leaves you examine closely turn out to be exactly alike; Leibniz thought the same must hold for everything.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Identity","Possible worlds"],"thinkers":["leibniz"],"tags":["leibniz","leibniz's law","indiscernibility","pii","black's spheres"]},{"term":"Identity theory","def":"The view that each type of mental state is identical to a type of brain state.","more":"Defended in the 1950s by U. T. Place and J. J. C. Smart, the theory treats mind-brain identity like other scientific discoveries, such as water being H2O. Its main problem is multiple realisability: an octopus or an alien might feel pain with a very different nervous system, which suggests pain is not one brain type. Functionalism grew partly out of this objection.","etym":null,"example":"Pain just is a certain kind of nerve firing, the way lightning just is electrical discharge.","notConfused":"Not the same as personal identity: the identity theory is about minds and brains, not about what makes you the same person over time.","field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism","Functionalism"],"thinkers":[],"compass":"q6","tags":["mind-brain identity theory","type identity theory","type physicalism","smart","place","brain states"]},{"term":"Ideology","def":"A system of ideas and beliefs, especially one that shapes how a group sees society and serves certain interests.","more":"Marx and Engels used the word for ideas that make an unjust social order seem natural, serving the ruling class. In everyday politics it now usually means any broad political outlook, such as liberalism or socialism. Critics of the Marxist use ask how the critic escapes ideology himself.","etym":"French idéologie, 'science of ideas', coined by Destutt de Tracy in the 1790s","example":"The belief that anyone who works hard enough will become rich can make poverty seem always deserved.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Historical materialism","Alienation","Genealogy"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["ideologies","false consciousness","worldview","marx","dominant ideas"]},{"term":"Ijtihad","def":"In Islamic law, independent reasoning by a qualified scholar to reach a ruling where scripture and precedent give no clear answer.","more":"A scholar who exercises ijtihad is a mujtahid. Many historians say that from about the 10th century Sunni schools tended to rely on following established rulings (taqlid), a view sometimes called the 'closing of the gate of ijtihad', though scholars dispute how real that closing was. Modern reformers have called for renewed ijtihad to address new questions.","etym":"Arabic ijtihad, 'striving, effort'","example":"Working out whether a new medical technology is permitted when early texts could not mention it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Kalam","Justification"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["independent reasoning","islamic jurisprudence","fiqh","mujtahid","interpretation"]},{"term":"Immanence","def":"Being present within the world, rather than beyond it. A God or principle is immanent if it is found in things, not apart from them.","more":"It contrasts with transcendence, being beyond or outside the world. Spinoza's identification of God with nature is a strong version of immanence, and many forms of pantheism share it. Most monotheistic traditions say God is both transcendent and immanent. Kant used the terms differently, for what lies within or beyond possible experience.","etym":"Latin immanere, 'to remain within'","example":"A view on which the divine is present in every river and tree, rather than ruling from above.","notConfused":"Not the same as imminent, meaning about to happen.","field":"Religion","related":["God","Dao","Brahman"],"thinkers":["spinoza"],"tags":["immanent","god in the world","indwelling","transcendence","pantheism"]},{"term":"Immortality","def":"Living forever, or in philosophy, the survival of a person after the death of the body.","more":"Plato's Phaedo gives several arguments that the soul is immortal. Others imagine survival through bodily resurrection or rebirth rather than a separate soul. Epicureans and many physicalists deny any afterlife, and some philosophers, such as Bernard Williams, argued that living forever would eventually become tedious and so would not be desirable.","etym":"Latin in-, 'not', + mortalis, 'subject to death'","example":"Would you want to live forever if you had already done everything you care about?","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Dualism","Personal identity","Atman"],"thinkers":["plato","williams"],"tags":["eternal life","afterlife","life after death","survival of death","immortal soul"]},{"term":"Incommensurability","def":"The lack of a common measure: two theories or values are incommensurable if they cannot be fully compared on a single scale.","more":"In philosophy of science, Kuhn and Paul Feyerabend argued in the 1960s that theories from different paradigms can use words differently and value different things, so choosing between them is not settled by logic alone. In ethics, some values, such as friendship and money, may be incommensurable. Critics argue this risks relativism and that comparison is usually possible in practice.","etym":"Latin in-, 'not', + commensurabilis, 'measurable together'","example":"Is a career as a surgeon better than one as a poet? There may be no single scale on which to rank them.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Paradigm","Relativism","Holism"],"thinkers":["kuhn"],"tags":["incommensurable","kuhn","feyerabend","paradigm shift","no common measure"]},{"term":"Indeterminism","def":"The view that some events are not fixed by the past and the laws: more than one future is genuinely possible.","more":"Indeterminism is a claim about the world, not about people, but it matters for free will. Libertarians about free will say indeterminism is needed for genuine choice; critics reply that if an action is random it is not under the agent's control either. The ancient Epicureans proposed a random 'swerve' of atoms to make room for freedom.","etym":"Latin in-, 'not', + determinare, 'to set limits'","example":"Many physicists read quantum mechanics as saying exactly when an atom decays is not fixed in advance.","notConfused":"Not the same as free will: a random event in your brain would be undetermined without being something you chose.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Determinism","Libertarianism (free will)"],"thinkers":["epicurus","james"],"compass":"q8","tags":["chance","randomness","indeterminacy","quantum indeterminacy","free will"]},{"term":"Induction","def":"Reasoning from observed cases to general rules or to unobserved cases.","more":"Induction gives conclusions that are probable rather than guaranteed: even true premises leave room for a false conclusion. Most of science and everyday life depends on it. Francis Bacon championed careful inductive method, while Hume asked whether induction can be justified at all, which became the problem of induction.","etym":"Latin inductio, 'a leading in', translating Aristotle's Greek epagoge","example":"‘The sun has risen every morning I have known, so it will rise tomorrow.’","notConfused":"Not the same as deduction: in a valid deduction the conclusion must be true if the premises are; in induction it is only made likely.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Problem of induction","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["bacon","hume"],"tags":["inductive reasoning","inductive argument","generalisation","generalization","problem of induction"]},{"term":"Ineffable","def":"Unable to be expressed in words. Mystics and religious thinkers often describe God or ultimate reality as ineffable.","more":"William James listed ineffability as a mark of mystical experience in The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902). The Daodejing opens by saying the Dao that can be spoken is not the constant Dao. Critics point out a puzzle: calling something 'ineffable' already says something about it.","etym":"Latin in-, 'not', + effabilis, 'utterable'","example":"Struggling to describe the taste of a fruit to someone who has never tried it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Dao","Brahman","Emptiness (śūnyatā)"],"thinkers":["james","laozi"],"tags":["ineffability","inexpressible","beyond words","unspeakable","mysticism"]},{"term":"Inference","def":"The step of reasoning from some statements (premises) to another (a conclusion).","more":"Inferences can be deductive, where the conclusion follows necessarily; inductive, where it is made probable; or abductive, where it is the best explanation of the evidence. Logic studies which inferences are good. Lewis Carroll's story of Achilles and the tortoise (1895) shows that a rule of inference cannot simply be added as one more premise without an endless regress.","etym":"Latin inferre, 'to bring in'","example":"Seeing wet streets and inferring that it has rained.","notConfused":"Not the same as implication: implication is a relation between statements; inference is something a reasoner does.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Entailment","Induction","Inference to the best explanation"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["reasoning","drawing conclusions","deduction","induction","argument"]},{"term":"Inference to the best explanation","def":"Reasoning that a hypothesis is probably true because it would explain the evidence better than its rivals.","more":"Charles Peirce called a related kind of reasoning abduction, and Gilbert Harman coined the phrase 'inference to the best explanation' in 1965. Scientists, detectives and doctors use it constantly. Debate centres on what makes an explanation 'best' (simplicity, scope, fit with other knowledge) and why that should make it more likely to be true.","etym":null,"example":"The cheese is gone and there are tiny droppings: the best explanation is a mouse.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Induction","Occam’s razor","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["peirce","harman"],"tags":["abduction","ibe","best explanation","abductive reasoning","explanatory reasoning"]},{"term":"Infinite regress","def":"A series in which each step depends on another before it, without end.","more":"Some regresses are vicious: they show a theory fails, because the explanation is never completed. Others are harmless, like each number having a successor. The regress problem in epistemology asks how justification can end, and cosmological arguments try to block a regress of causes. Ancient sceptics such as Agrippa used the regress as one of their 'modes' for suspending judgment.","etym":"Latin regressus, 'going back'","example":"'Why should I believe that?' 'Because of this.' 'And why believe that?' ... and so on forever.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Foundationalism","Cosmological argument","Skepticism"],"thinkers":["sextus"],"tags":["regress","regress argument","turtles all the way down","vicious regress","endless chain"]},{"term":"Innate ideas","def":"Ideas or knowledge that the mind has from birth, rather than learning from experience.","more":"Plato argued that learning is recollecting what the soul already knew. Descartes and Leibniz held that ideas such as God, self and mathematical truths are innate. Locke attacked the view in his Essay (1689), arguing the mind starts as a blank slate. In the 20th century Noam Chomsky argued that humans have an innate capacity for language.","etym":"Latin innatus, 'inborn'","example":"Small children seem to grasp that one more than three is four without being taught the general rule.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Rationalism","Empiricism","A priori"],"thinkers":["descartes","locke","chomsky"],"tags":["innatism","nativism","inborn knowledge","descartes","rationalism","tabula rasa"]},{"term":"Institutional theory of art","def":"The view that something is a work of art if it has been presented as art within the 'artworld' of artists, galleries and critics.","more":"Arthur Danto introduced the idea of an artworld in 1964 after seeing Warhol's Brillo Boxes, which looked just like ordinary boxes. George Dickie developed a formal institutional theory in the 1970s. Critics object that it is circular and does not explain why the artworld picks out some things rather than others.","etym":null,"example":"Marcel Duchamp's Fountain (1917), a urinal submitted to an exhibition, became art through how it was presented.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Formalism (art)","Expression theory of art"],"thinkers":["danto","dickie"],"tags":["dickie","artworld","danto","what is art","art institutions"]},{"term":"Instrumentalism","def":"The view that scientific theories are tools for predicting what we observe, not literal descriptions of hidden reality.","more":"Instrumentalists point out that past theories which predicted well, such as ones involving the ether, turned out to describe nothing real. Scientific realists reply that the success of science would be a miracle unless its theories were at least roughly true. John Dewey also used the word for his wider view that ideas are tools for solving problems.","etym":"Latin instrumentum, 'tool'","example":"An instrumentalist treats a physics model like a map app: useful, but not a picture of what is ‘really’ there.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific realism"],"thinkers":[],"compass":"q11","tags":["instrumentalist","anti-realism","scientific anti-realism","theories as tools","dewey"]},{"term":"Intentional fallacy","def":"The supposed mistake of judging or interpreting a work of art by what its author intended.","more":"W. K. Wimsatt and Monroe Beardsley coined the term in 1946, arguing that the meaning of a poem is found in the text, not in the poet's private plans. Intentionalists reply that meaning cannot be separated from what an author was trying to do, and that knowing the context often changes our reading.","etym":null,"example":"A novelist says her book is about hope, but readers find it bleak: whose reading counts?","notConfused":"Not the same as intentionality in philosophy of mind, which is about the 'aboutness' of thoughts.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Hermeneutics","Expression theory of art","Aesthetics"],"thinkers":["wimsatt","beardsley"],"tags":["authorial intent","author's intention","wimsatt","beardsley","new criticism"]},{"term":"Intentionality","def":"The 'aboutness' of mental states: the way thoughts, beliefs and desires are directed at things.","more":"Franz Brentano argued in 1874 that intentionality is the mark of the mental, since nothing physical seems to be 'about' anything in itself. Husserl made it central to phenomenology. A key puzzle is that we can think about things that do not exist, such as unicorns. Philosophers such as Searle and Dennett disagree about whether computers or brains can have intentionality in their own right.","etym":"Latin intendere, 'to aim at, stretch towards'","example":"Your hope that it will be sunny tomorrow is about tomorrow's weather.","notConfused":"Not the same as intending to do something: all thoughts have intentionality, not just plans.","field":"Mind","related":["Phenomenology","Functionalism","Folk psychology"],"thinkers":["husserl","searle","dennett"],"tags":["aboutness","brentano","directedness","mental content","representation"]},{"term":"Internalism","def":"The name for several views holding that something about justification, meaning or motivation depends only on factors within the person.","more":"In epistemology, internalism says whether a belief is justified depends on things you can access by reflection, such as your evidence; Laurence BonJour is a leading defender. In ethics, motivational internalism says that sincerely judging something right necessarily gives you some motivation to do it. Each version is opposed by a form of externalism.","etym":"Latin internus, 'inward'","example":"An internalist says a brain in a vat with your exact experiences is just as justified as you are.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Externalism","Justification","Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["bonjour"],"tags":["epistemic internalism","access internalism","justification","motivational internalism","externalism"]},{"term":"Intrinsic value","def":"The value something has in itself, as an end, rather than for what it leads to.","more":"It is contrasted with instrumental value, the value of a means to something else. Money has instrumental value; many say happiness, knowledge or friendship have intrinsic value. Hedonists say only pleasure is intrinsically good. G. E. Moore proposed an 'isolation test': imagine the thing existing alone and ask if it would still be good.","etym":"Latin intrinsecus, 'on the inside'","example":"Medicine is valuable because it brings health; health may be valuable in itself.","notConfused":"Not the same as objective value: something could be valued for its own sake and still, on some views, be valuable only because people value it.","field":"Ethics","related":["Hedonism","Eudaimonia","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["moore"],"tags":["intrinsic good","value in itself","end in itself","final value","instrumental value"]},{"term":"Intuition","def":"A judgment that seems true to you on reflection, before or without an argument. Philosophers use intuitions as evidence, but argue about how much they count.","more":"Thought experiments such as the trolley problem rely on intuitions as data that theories must fit. Critics, including some experimental philosophers, have found that intuitions can vary with culture, wording or the order cases are presented in, and ask whether they can be trusted. Defenders reply that all reasoning must start somewhere, and that intuitions can be tested against one another through reflective equilibrium.","etym":"Latin intueri, 'to look at, consider'","example":"Most people feel at once that punishing an innocent person to calm a mob is wrong.","notConfused":"Not the same as a hunch or gut feeling about a fact: a philosophical intuition is a considered judgment that something seems true on reflection.","field":"Method","related":["Thought experiment","Reflective equilibrium"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["intuitions","gut feeling","intuitive judgement","intuitionism","insight"]},{"term":"Intuition pump","def":"Daniel Dennett's name for a thought experiment designed to trigger an intuition, which he warns can mislead as well as help.","more":"Dennett suggests 'turning the knobs': changing the details of the story to see if the intuition survives. He has argued that famous cases such as Searle's Chinese room and Jackson's Mary rely on misleading framing. Supporters of these cases reply that changing details does not change their core point.","etym":null,"example":"Asking whether a perfect copy of you made on Mars would be you is an intuition pump about personal identity.","notConfused":null,"field":"Method","related":["Thought experiment","Intuition"],"thinkers":["dennett"],"tags":["dennett","thought experiment","intuition"]},{"term":"Invalid","def":"Describes an argument whose conclusion does not follow from its premises: the premises could all be true and the conclusion false.","more":"Validity concerns the form of an argument, not whether its statements are true. An invalid argument can happen to have true premises and a true conclusion. Logicians show invalidity by finding a counterexample: an argument of the same form with true premises and a false conclusion.","etym":"Latin in-, 'not', + validus, 'strong'","example":"'All dogs are mammals. All cats are mammals. So all dogs are cats.' The premises are true; the conclusion is false.","notConfused":"Not the same as false: statements are true or false; arguments are valid or invalid.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Valid","Sound","Affirming the consequent"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["invalidity","invalid argument","does not follow","non sequitur","formal fallacy"]},{"term":"Inverted spectrum","def":"The thought experiment of someone who sees colours swapped, red where you see green, while behaving exactly as you do.","more":"Locke raised the possibility in his Essay (1689). If it could happen undetectably, it seems that the felt quality of experience (qualia) is not fixed by behaviour or function, which challenges functionalism. Sydney Shoemaker and others have argued that inversion is possible but can be handled within a functionalist theory; Dennett doubts the idea is coherent.","etym":null,"example":"Your friend calls ripe tomatoes 'red' just as you do, yet her experience might look like your experience of green.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Qualia","Functionalism","Hard problem"],"thinkers":["locke","shoemaker"],"tags":["spectrum inversion","inverted qualia","red and green","qualia","locke"]},{"term":"Is–ought gap","def":"Hume’s point that you cannot validly reach a conclusion about what ought to be from premises only about what is.","more":"Hume noticed, in his Treatise of Human Nature (1739–40), that writers move from statements about what is to statements about what ought to be without explaining the step. This is often called Hume's law. Some philosophers, such as John Searle and Philippa Foot, have tried to bridge the gap, for example from facts about promises or about what living things need to flourish.","etym":null,"example":"‘People are naturally selfish’ does not by itself show that people should be selfish.","notConfused":"Not the same as the naturalistic fallacy: that is G. E. Moore's separate claim that 'good' cannot be defined in natural terms.","field":"Ethics","related":["Naturalistic fallacy","Metaethics"],"thinkers":["hume"],"tags":["is-ought problem","is ought gap","hume's law","hume's guillotine","fact-value distinction","naturalistic fallacy"]},{"term":"Jainism","def":"An Indian religion and philosophy that teaches non-violence toward all living beings and the liberation of the soul from karma.","more":"Jains trace their tradition through a line of teachers, the latest being Mahavira (traditionally 6th century BCE). Central ideas include ahimsa (non-harm), non-possession, and anekantavada, the many-sidedness of reality, which holds that any single viewpoint captures only part of the truth. Umasvati's Tattvartha Sutra is a classic summary accepted by all Jain sects.","etym":"From Sanskrit jina, 'conqueror': one who has conquered inner passions","example":"Some Jain monks sweep the path before them to avoid stepping on insects.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Karma","Atman"],"thinkers":["mahavira","umasvati"],"tags":["jain","jaina","mahavira","ahimsa","anekantavada","tirthankara"]},{"term":"Junzi","def":"The Confucian ideal of the 'exemplary person': someone of noble character who acts with humaneness, propriety and integrity.","more":"Originally junzi meant a 'ruler's son', a nobleman by birth. Confucius changed it to mean nobility of character, open to anyone who cultivates virtue. The Analects often contrasts the junzi, who seeks what is right, with the 'petty person' (xiaoren), who seeks profit.","etym":"Chinese junzi (君子), literally 'son of a lord'","example":"Keeping your word to a friend even when breaking it would bring you an advantage.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Ren","Li (ritual propriety)","Virtue"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius","xunzi"],"tags":["chun tzu","chün-tzu","gentleman","exemplary person","noble person","confucius"]},{"term":"Justice","def":"Giving each person what they are due. Political philosophy asks what a just distribution of goods, rights and power looks like.","more":"Plato's Republic asks what justice is and whether a just life is better for the person who lives it. Modern debate centres on distributive justice: Rawls argues for principles that would be chosen behind a veil of ignorance, while Nozick holds that a distribution is just if it came about through fair acquisition and free exchange. Other theories focus on desert, needs, equality or capabilities.","etym":"Latin iustitia, from ius, 'right, law'","example":"Is it just that two people doing the same job earn very different pay?","notConfused":"Not the same as legality: a law can itself be unjust, as with laws enforcing slavery.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Difference principle","Legitimacy","Desert"],"thinkers":["plato","rawls","nozick"],"compass":"q23","tags":["fairness","just","distributive justice","rawls","dikaiosyne","equity"]},{"term":"Justification","def":"What makes a belief reasonable to hold: good evidence, reasons or a reliable source.","more":"Since Plato, many have said knowledge needs more than true belief: it needs a good reason. Internalists say justification depends on things you can access by reflection, such as your evidence; externalists say it can depend on facts outside your awareness, such as whether your belief came from a reliable process. A further debate is how justification is structured: foundationalism, coherentism and infinitism give different answers.","etym":"Latin iustificare, 'to make just or right'","example":"Believing it will rain because of a forecast is justified; believing it because of a coin flip is not.","notConfused":"Not the same as truth: a belief can be well justified and still false, like trusting an accurate-looking but wrong forecast.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Gettier problem","Skepticism","Epistemology"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["epistemic justification","warrant","justified belief","reasons for belief","evidence"]},{"term":"Justified true belief","def":"The traditional analysis of knowledge: you know something if you believe it, it is true, and you have good reason for believing it.","more":"The idea is often traced to Plato's Theaetetus, though Plato himself raised doubts about it. Gettier's 1963 cases showed that the three conditions may not be enough, leading many to look for a fourth condition or a new analysis. Timothy Williamson argues that knowledge cannot be analysed this way at all.","etym":null,"example":"You believe your train leaves at nine, it does, and you checked the timetable: on this analysis, you know it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Gettier problem","Justification","Epistemology"],"thinkers":["plato","williamson"],"tags":["jtb","tripartite analysis of knowledge","definition of knowledge","gettier problem","knowledge"]},{"term":"Just war theory","def":"A tradition of thought about when it is morally permissible to go to war and how war may be fought.","more":"It distinguishes jus ad bellum (justice in going to war: just cause, legitimate authority, right intention, last resort, reasonable chance of success, proportionality) from jus in bello (justice in fighting: discrimination between combatants and civilians, and proportionality). It developed from Augustine and Aquinas and was revived by Michael Walzer's Just and Unjust Wars (1977). Pacifists reject it, and revisionists such as Jeff McMahan challenge parts of it.","etym":null,"example":"Asking whether bombing a factory is acceptable if it will also kill nearby civilians.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Doctrine of double effect","Legitimacy","Justice"],"thinkers":["augustine","aquinas","mcmahan"],"tags":["just war","jus ad bellum","jus in bello","ethics of war","augustine","aquinas"]},{"term":"Kalam","def":"The tradition of Islamic theology that uses reasoned argument to defend and explain religious beliefs; also a famous argument for God from it.","more":"Mutakallimun (practitioners of kalam), such as the Ash'arites, debated God's attributes, free will and causation. Al-Ghazali argued that the universe must have had a beginning and so a cause. William Lane Craig revived this 'kalam cosmological argument' in 1979: whatever begins to exist has a cause; the universe began to exist; so it has a cause. Critics question both premises.","etym":"Arabic kalam, 'speech, discourse'","example":"Arguing that an infinite series of past days could never have been completed, so time must have had a beginning.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Cosmological argument","Five Ways","God"],"thinkers":["alghazali","ashari","craig"],"tags":["kalam cosmological argument","ilm al-kalam","craig","al-ghazali","first cause","islamic theology"]},{"term":"Kami","def":"In Shinto, the sacred powers or spirits found in nature, places, ancestors and remarkable things.","more":"Kami are not usually thought of as all-powerful gods; they include the spirits of mountains, rivers, trees and honoured ancestors. The 18th-century scholar Motoori Norinaga described kami as anything extraordinary that inspires awe. Philosophers discuss kami in comparing ideas of the sacred that are immanent in nature rather than beyond it.","etym":"Japanese kami (神), 'spirit, deity'","example":"A great old tree wrapped in a rope at a shrine is honoured as home to a kami.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Immanence","Dao"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["shinto","spirits","deities","gods","sacred"]},{"term":"Karma","def":"In Indian traditions, the principle that intentional actions have moral consequences that shape one’s future, including future lives.","more":"Hindu, Buddhist and Jain traditions share the idea but differ on its details. Buddhists stress that karma is intention (cetana): it is the will behind the action that counts. For Jains, karma is a fine kind of matter that sticks to the soul. In each tradition the goal is liberation from the cycle of rebirth that karma keeps going.","etym":"Sanskrit karman, 'action, deed'","example":"Cruelty today plants seeds of future suffering for the one who is cruel.","notConfused":"Not the same as fate or instant cosmic payback: karma works through the agent's own choices and can ripen over many lifetimes.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dukkha","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["buddha","shankara"],"tags":["kamma","action and consequence","cause and effect","rebirth","samsara","what goes around comes around"]},{"term":"Kingdom of ends","def":"Kant's ideal of a community of rational beings in which each treats every other as an end in themselves, never merely as a means.","more":"In the Groundwork (1785), Kant presents it as a version of the categorical imperative: act as if your rules were laws for such a community. Every member is both lawmaker and subject, and each has dignity, which has no price. Christine Korsgaard and others have developed the idea in modern Kantian ethics.","etym":null,"example":"A business that refuses to deceive customers, treating them as people with their own goals rather than as walking wallets.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Categorical imperative","Good will","Deontology"],"thinkers":["kant","korsgaard"],"tags":["realm of ends","kant","humanity as an end","categorical imperative","moral community"]},{"term":"Knowing how","def":"Practical knowledge: the ability to do something, such as ride a bike or speak a language.","more":"Gilbert Ryle argued in 1949 that knowing how cannot be reduced to knowing that (knowing facts): you can know all the physics of cycling and still fall off. Jason Stanley and Timothy Williamson challenged this in 2001, arguing that knowing how is a kind of knowing that. The debate matters for theories of skill, expertise and education.","etym":null,"example":"A child who rides a bike knows how, though she could not explain the physics of balance.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Knowledge by acquaintance","Epistemology"],"thinkers":["williamson"],"tags":["know-how","practical knowledge","knowing that","ryle","skill","procedural knowledge"]},{"term":"Knowledge argument","def":"Frank Jackson's argument that physical facts cannot be all the facts, because someone could know every physical fact about colour and still learn something new by seeing it.","more":"In Jackson's 1982 thought experiment, Mary is a scientist who knows everything physical about colour vision but has lived in a black-and-white room. When she first sees red, she seems to learn what it is like. Physicalists reply that she gains a new ability or a new way of knowing an old fact; Jackson himself later rejected the argument.","etym":null,"example":"However much you read about the taste of mango, tasting one seems to teach you something.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Qualia","Physicalism","Hard problem"],"thinkers":["jackson"],"tags":["mary's room","mary the colour scientist","mary the color scientist","jackson","qualia","physicalism"]},{"term":"Knowledge by acquaintance","def":"Russell's term for direct knowledge of something through being presented with it, as opposed to knowledge by description.","more":"Russell held in 1910–12 that we are acquainted with our sense-data and perhaps ourselves, while we know most things, such as distant people, only by description. The distinction remains useful: you can know a lot about Paris from books without knowing it as someone who has been there.","etym":null,"example":"Knowing your best friend versus knowing facts about a historical figure.","notConfused":"Not the same as knowing how: acquaintance is direct awareness of something, not a practical skill.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Knowing how","Epistemology","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["russell"],"tags":["acquaintance","knowledge by description","russell","direct awareness","sense data"]},{"term":"Koan","def":"In Zen Buddhism, a puzzling story, question or saying used in training to break through ordinary thinking.","more":"Famous koans include 'What is the sound of one hand?' and the case of Mu, in which a master answers 'Mu' (no, or nothing) when asked if a dog has Buddha-nature. Koans are especially associated with the Rinzai school. They are not riddles with a clever answer; their purpose is to bring about direct insight.","etym":"Japanese koan (公案), from Chinese gong'an, 'public case'","example":"Puzzling over a question until you realise the question itself reflects a habit of thought.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Kyoto School","Ineffable"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["kōan","kong'an","zen riddle","sound of one hand clapping","zen","mu"]},{"term":"Kyoto School","def":"A group of 20th-century Japanese philosophers, centred on Kyoto University, who combined Western philosophy with Buddhist ideas, especially about nothingness.","more":"Its founder is usually taken to be Nishida Kitaro, whose An Inquiry into the Good (1911) built on the idea of 'pure experience'. Members such as Tanabe Hajime and Nishitani Keiji developed the idea of 'absolute nothingness' in dialogue with Hegel, Heidegger and Christianity. Some members' wartime writings supporting Japanese nationalism remain controversial.","etym":null,"example":"Nishida asks what experience is like before it is split into a subject who sees and an object seen.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Phenomenology","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["nishida"],"tags":["nishida","nishitani","tanabe","japanese philosophy","absolute nothingness"]},{"term":"Language game","def":"Later Wittgenstein’s idea that words get their meaning from their use within a shared activity, like moves in a game.","example":"‘Checkmate’ only makes sense within chess; ‘I do’ means something special at a wedding.","related":["Verification principle","Behaviorism"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"compass":"q15","more":"In Philosophical Investigations (1953) Wittgenstein argued that there is no single essence of language, only many overlapping practices such as giving orders, telling jokes and praying. Many philosophical puzzles, he thought, come from taking words out of the games where they work. The idea influenced later work on meaning as use.","etym":"Wittgenstein's German Sprachspiel.","notConfused":"Not the same as word games or puns: a language game is any rule-governed activity involving speech.","field":"Language","tags":["language-game","wittgenstein","meaning as use","philosophical investigations","forms of life"]},{"term":"Law of excluded middle","def":"The logical principle that, for any claim P, either P or not-P is true.","more":"Classical logic, from Aristotle onward, accepts the law. Intuitionist mathematicians such as Brouwer rejected it for statements we have no way to prove or disprove, and Dummett extended the doubt to wider areas. Vague statements and claims about the future are other test cases.","etym":"Latin tertium non datur, 'no third option is given'.","example":"Either it is raining in Paris right now or it is not; there is no third option.","notConfused":"Not the same as a false dilemma: the law covers a claim and its exact denial, while a false dilemma wrongly offers two options when there are more.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Law of non-contradiction","Logic","Vagueness"],"thinkers":["aristotle","brouwer","dummett"],"tags":["excluded middle","tertium non datur","lem","p or not p","bivalence"]},{"term":"Law of non-contradiction","def":"The principle that a statement and its denial cannot both be true at the same time and in the same respect.","more":"Aristotle called it the firmest principle of all and argued that anyone who denies it cannot even say anything definite. It is a foundation of classical logic. A small group of modern logicians, called dialetheists, argue that a few statements, such as the liar sentence, are both true and false.","etym":null,"example":"A door cannot be both fully open and fully closed at the same moment.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Law of excluded middle","Liar paradox","Logic"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["principle of non-contradiction","non-contradiction","lnc","pnc","aristotle","contradiction"]},{"term":"Laws of nature","def":"The basic regularities that govern how the physical world behaves, such as the law of gravitation.","more":"Philosophers ask what a law is. Humeans, such as Lewis, say laws are just the neatest summaries of what actually happens. Others, such as Armstrong, say laws are real relations between properties that make things happen, while some deny there are laws at all, only the powers of things.","etym":null,"example":"All copper conducts electricity seems to be a law; 'all the coins in my pocket are copper' is true but only by accident.","notConfused":"Not the same as laws made by governments: laws of nature describe what does happen, not what people must do.","field":"Science","related":["Causation","Induction","Natural kind"],"thinkers":["armstrong","lewis","mumford"],"tags":["natural laws","laws of physics","scientific laws","regularity","humean laws"]},{"term":"Leap of faith","def":"Committing to a belief, especially religious belief, that goes beyond what evidence and reason can prove.","more":"The idea is usually linked to Kierkegaard, who wrote of a 'leap' into faith, though the exact phrase 'leap of faith' is not his. He held that faith is a passionate personal commitment, not the conclusion of an argument. Critics such as Clifford argued it is wrong to believe anything on insufficient evidence.","etym":null,"example":"Someone who prays seriously for the first time without being sure anyone is listening is making a kind of leap.","notConfused":"Not the same as Pascal's wager: the wager offers a calculated reason to believe, while the leap goes beyond calculation.","field":"Religion","related":["Existentialism","Pascal’s wager","Absurd"],"thinkers":["kierkegaard"],"tags":["kierkegaard","faith","fideism","religious commitment","trust"]},{"term":"Legitimacy","def":"The rightful authority of a government to make and enforce rules, not just the power to do so.","example":"A gang can force you to pay; an elected government’s tax demand at least claims to be legitimate.","related":["Social contract","Democracy"],"thinkers":["locke","rousseau"],"compass":"q23","more":"Theories of legitimacy point to consent, democratic process, fair procedures or good results as what gives a state the right to rule. Philosophical anarchists argue that no actual state is fully legitimate. Max Weber, a sociologist, separated legitimacy as people's belief that power is rightful from whether it really is.","etym":"Latin legitimus, 'lawful'.","notConfused":"Not the same as legality: a government can act legally under its own laws and still lack legitimacy.","field":"Politics and society","tags":["political legitimacy","authority","legitimate government","consent of the governed","political obligation"]},{"term":"Leibniz's law","def":"The principle that if two things are truly identical, they share every property; and, in a stronger version, that things sharing every property are identical.","more":"The first half, the indiscernibility of identicals, is almost universally accepted and is used in many arguments, for example to argue that mind and brain differ because they seem to have different properties. The second half, the identity of indiscernibles, is more controversial: critics imagine two perfectly alike spheres in an otherwise empty universe.","etym":"Named after Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz (1646–1716).","example":"If Clark Kent is Superman, then whatever is true of Superman, such as being able to fly, is true of Clark Kent.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Personal identity","Dualism","Identity theory"],"thinkers":["leibniz"],"tags":["indiscernibility of identicals","identity of indiscernibles","leibniz","substitutivity","identity"]},{"term":"Liar paradox","def":"The puzzle of the sentence 'This sentence is false': if it is true it is false, and if it is false it is true.","more":"An ancient version is traditionally credited to the Greek philosopher Eubulides. The paradox matters because it shows that simple ideas of truth lead to contradiction. Proposed solutions include banning sentences that talk about their own truth, using a hierarchy of languages, or accepting truth-value gaps or even true contradictions.","etym":null,"example":"A card that says on one side 'The sentence on the other side is true' and on the other 'The sentence on the other side is false'.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Paradox","Law of non-contradiction","Vagueness"],"thinkers":["russell"],"tags":["liar sentence","this sentence is false","epimenides paradox","self-reference","semantic paradox"]},{"term":"Liberalism","def":"A family of political views that put individual freedom, equal rights, tolerance and limits on government power at the centre.","more":"Early liberals such as Locke defended rights against kings and religious persecution. Mill argued for free speech and the harm principle, and Rawls developed a liberalism that also calls for fair shares of resources. Critics on the left say it ignores economic power; communitarians say it neglects community and shared values.","etym":"Latin liberalis, 'befitting a free person'.","example":"Freedom of religion, where the state lets people worship as they choose or not at all, is a core liberal idea.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'liberal' in current US politics, which usually means centre-left; in philosophy liberalism includes many right-leaning views too.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Liberty (negative/positive)","Harm principle","Libertarianism (politics)","Social contract"],"thinkers":["locke","mill","rawls"],"tags":["liberal","individual liberty","locke","mill","rights","classical liberalism"]},{"term":"Libertarianism (free will)","def":"The view that we have free will and that free will requires indeterminism. (Not the same as libertarianism in politics.)","example":"A libertarian says that at the moment you chose, you really could have chosen otherwise: the past did not settle it.","related":["Indeterminism","Free will","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["reid","kant"],"compass":"q8","more":"Libertarians agree with hard determinists that free will and determinism cannot both be true, but they hold that we are free, so determinism must be false. Some locate freedom in undetermined events in the brain; 'agent-causal' libertarians say the person themself starts new causal chains. Critics argue that adding chance does not add control.","etym":"Latin liber, 'free'.","notConfused":"Not the same as political libertarianism: this one is about whether choices are determined, not about the size of government.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["libertarian free will","agent causation","incompatibilism","free will","indeterminism"]},{"term":"Libertarianism (politics)","def":"The political view that individual liberty, especially freedom from coercion and the right to property, should be the highest political value, so the state should be very small or not exist at all.","more":"Nozick's Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) argued that only a minimal 'night-watchman' state, protecting against force, theft and fraud, can be justified. Anarcho-capitalists such as Rothbard reject even that. Critics argue that it lets poverty and unequal starting points limit real freedom.","etym":"Latin liber, 'free'.","example":"A libertarian might say taxing your wages to fund other people's projects is wrong, since it takes what you earned without your consent.","notConfused":"Not the same as libertarianism about free will, which is a view about whether choices are determined.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Liberalism","Natural rights","Liberty (negative/positive)"],"thinkers":["nozick","rothbard","hayek"],"tags":["libertarian","minimal state","nozick","free market","self-ownership","night-watchman state"]},{"term":"Liberty (negative/positive)","def":"Negative liberty is freedom from interference by others. Positive liberty is the power or capacity to actually run your own life. (Distinction made famous by Isaiah Berlin.)","example":"No law stops you from studying abroad (negative), but you also need the money to do it (positive).","related":["Harm principle","Natural rights"],"thinkers":["berlin","mill","hayek"],"compass":"q23","more":"Berlin's 1958 lecture Two Concepts of Liberty warned that positive liberty could be twisted to justify forcing people to be 'free' in the name of their 'true self'. Defenders of positive liberty reply that freedom from interference is empty without resources and abilities. A third view, republican liberty, defines freedom as not being under anyone's arbitrary power.","etym":"Latin libertas, 'freedom'.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","tags":["negative liberty","positive liberty","freedom","isaiah berlin","two concepts of liberty","freedom from interference"]},{"term":"Li (principle)","def":"In Neo-Confucian thought, the underlying pattern or principle that makes each thing what it is and gives the world its order.","more":"Zhu Xi taught that every thing has li, which always appears together with qi, the energy or stuff of the world. Studying things carefully lets us grasp li and become good, since our own nature is li. Wang Yangming objected that li is found in the mind itself, not by investigating outside things.","etym":"Chinese 理 (lǐ), originally 'the pattern in jade'.","example":"The grain of a piece of wood, which a skilled carpenter follows, is a common image for li.","notConfused":"Not the same as li (ritual propriety), a different Chinese word (禮) for rites and manners.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Neo-Confucianism","Dao","Li (ritual propriety)"],"thinkers":["zhuxi","wangyangming"],"tags":["pattern","principle","neo-confucianism","zhu xi","rational order"]},{"term":"Li (ritual propriety)","def":"Confucian ritual and good manners: the patterns of respectful behaviour that shape character and hold society together.","example":"Greeting elders properly, or bowing at a funeral, as a way of training respect.","related":["Ren","Virtue"],"thinkers":["confucius","xunzi"],"more":"For Confucius, li covered everything from state ceremonies to how to greet a guest. By practising it, people learn self-control and respect, and the outward form shapes the inner self. Xunzi stressed that ritual is needed to reshape a selfish human nature; Daoists criticised it as artificial.","etym":"Chinese 禮 (lǐ), originally 'sacrifice, rite'.","notConfused":"Not the same as li (principle), a different Chinese word (理) used by Neo-Confucians for the pattern or order in things.","field":"Asian traditions","tags":["ritual","propriety","rites","etiquette","confucianism","decorum"]},{"term":"Logic","def":"The study of good reasoning: which conclusions really follow from which starting points.","example":"Spotting that ‘All cats are mammals, so all mammals are cats’ does not follow is a logic skill.","related":["Argument","Valid","Sound","Fallacy"],"thinkers":["aristotle","frege","russell"],"more":"Logicians study the forms of arguments, so that they can say which patterns of reasoning are guaranteed to keep truth from premises to conclusion. Aristotle's syllogistic logic dominated for about two thousand years, until Frege and Russell built modern symbolic logic around 1900. Logic now underpins mathematics and computer science as well as philosophy.","etym":"Greek logike, from logos, 'word, reason, account'.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","tags":["formal logic","reasoning","valid inference","deductive logic","argument"]},{"term":"Logical positivism","def":"A 20th-century movement that held that a statement is meaningful only if it is true by definition or can be checked by experience.","more":"The movement grew out of the Vienna Circle in the 1920s and 1930s, and was spread in English by Ayer's Language, Truth and Logic (1936). It dismissed metaphysics, theology and most ethics as literally meaningless. It declined partly because its own verification principle seemed neither true by definition nor checkable by experience.","etym":null,"example":"A positivist would call 'The Absolute is beyond time' meaningless, since no observation could show it true or false.","notConfused":"Not the same as positivity or optimism; 'positive' here means based on what is given in experience.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Verification principle","Empiricism","Metaphysics"],"thinkers":["schlick","carnap","ayer"],"tags":["logical empiricism","vienna circle","verificationism","ayer","carnap","verification principle"]},{"term":"Logos","def":"An ancient Greek word for word, speech, reason or account; in philosophy, the rational order behind the world.","more":"Heraclitus spoke of a logos that governs all things, which most people fail to notice. The Stoics identified logos with the divine reason running through nature, and the Gospel of John uses it for the divine Word. In rhetoric, logos also means persuasion by argument, as opposed to character (ethos) or emotion (pathos).","etym":"Greek logos, from legein, 'to speak, gather'; the root of '-logy' in words like biology.","example":"A Stoic would say the regular cycle of the seasons shows the logos at work in nature.","notConfused":null,"field":"History of philosophy","related":["Stoicism","Dao","Logic"],"thinkers":["heraclitus","zeno_citium"],"tags":["reason","word","rational principle","heraclitus","divine reason"]},{"term":"Madhyamaka","def":"The 'Middle Way' school of Mahayana Buddhist philosophy, which teaches that nothing has a fixed, independent nature of its own.","more":"Founded by Nagarjuna, probably in the 2nd century CE, it uses careful arguments to show that any claim that things have an inherent essence leads to absurd results. Its key idea is emptiness, which Nagarjuna identified with dependent origination. Critics have accused it of nihilism; its defenders say it avoids both nihilism and belief in fixed essences.","etym":"Sanskrit madhyama, 'middle'.","example":"A chariot has no 'chariot-essence' hidden in its wheels or axle; it exists only in dependence on its parts and our naming.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Pratityasamutpada (dependent origination)","Middle Way"],"thinkers":["nagarjuna"],"tags":["middle way school","madhyamika","nagarjuna","emptiness","sunyata","two truths"]},{"term":"Mandate of Heaven","def":"The Chinese idea that rulers govern by the approval of Heaven, which they keep only as long as they rule justly.","more":"The idea was used by the Zhou dynasty to justify overthrowing the Shang. Mencius developed it, arguing that Heaven sees through the eyes of the people, so a cruel ruler loses his right to rule and may be removed. It became a lasting theme in Chinese political thought.","etym":"Chinese tianming 天命, 'Heaven's command'.","example":"Floods, famine and rebellion were often read as signs that a dynasty had lost the mandate.","notConfused":"Not the same as the European divine right of kings: the mandate can be lost through bad rule, while divine right was usually held to be unconditional.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Legitimacy","Ren","Neo-Confucianism"],"thinkers":["mencius","confucius"],"tags":["tianming","tian ming","divine right","heaven's mandate","legitimacy","zhou dynasty"]},{"term":"Marxism","def":"The body of ideas developed from Karl Marx (with Friedrich Engels): that history is driven by economic forces and class struggle, and that capitalism will give way to a classless society.","more":"Its core is historical materialism, the idea that how a society produces goods shapes its laws, politics and ideas. Marx argued that capitalism exploits workers and causes alienation. It inspired both scholarly traditions and political movements; critics point to failed predictions and to the record of states that called themselves Marxist.","etym":"Named after Karl Marx (1818–1883).","example":"A Marxist might explain a law protecting landlords by asking which class benefits from it.","notConfused":"Not the same as communism in general: Marxism is a theory of history and society; communism is a goal or system, and there are non-Marxist communists.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Alienation","Materialism","Dialectic"],"thinkers":["marx","hegel","ga_cohen"],"tags":["marx","communism","socialism","class struggle","historical materialism","engels"]},{"term":"Materialism","def":"The older name for the view that only matter exists. Today ‘physicalism’ is more common, since physics includes forces and fields, not just matter.","example":"Ancient atomists thought even the soul was made of tiny atoms.","related":["Physicalism","Idealism"],"thinkers":["democritus","hobbes","lucretius","marx"],"compass":"q6","more":"Materialism has roots in ancient atomism and was revived by Hobbes and by French Enlightenment thinkers. Marx's 'historical materialism' is a related but different idea about how economic life shapes history. In everyday speech 'materialism' also means caring too much about possessions.","etym":"Latin materia, 'matter, timber'.","notConfused":"Not the same as being materialistic: the philosophical view is about what exists, not about loving shopping.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["physicalism","materialist","matter","only matter exists","naturalism"]},{"term":"Maya","def":"In Hindu philosophy, especially Advaita Vedanta, the power of illusion that makes the one reality, Brahman, appear as a world of many separate things.","more":"For Shankara, the everyday world is not simply unreal, but it is not ultimately real either: it is like a dream that seems solid until we wake. Knowledge that the self (atman) is Brahman dissolves maya. Other Vedanta schools, such as Ramanuja's, reject the idea that the world is illusion.","etym":"Sanskrit maya, 'magic, illusion'.","example":"Shankara's classic image is a rope seen in dim light as a snake: the fear is real, but the snake is not.","notConfused":"Not the same as saying nothing exists: maya is a mistaken appearance of something real.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Brahman","Atman","Moksha"],"thinkers":["shankara"],"tags":["māyā","illusion","cosmic illusion","appearance","advaita vedanta"]},{"term":"Meno's paradox","def":"The puzzle of how you can search for something you do not know: if you know it, you need not search; if you do not, you will not recognise it when you find it.","more":"Meno raises it in Plato's dialogue of the same name. Socrates answers with the theory of recollection: learning is remembering what the soul knew before birth, which he tries to show by drawing geometry out of an untaught slave boy. Modern replies point out that we can know partly what we are looking for.","etym":null,"example":"How do you look up a word you cannot spell? You know enough about it to recognise it when you see it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Paradox","Forms","A priori"],"thinkers":["plato","socrates"],"tags":["paradox of inquiry","learner's paradox","meno","plato","recollection"]},{"term":"Mental causation","def":"The question of how the mind, such as a decision or a desire, can cause physical events, such as your arm moving.","more":"Elisabeth of Bohemia pressed this objection against Descartes: how can an unextended soul push an extended body? Physicalists face a version too: if every physical event has a full physical cause, mental properties seem to have no work left to do. This 'exclusion problem' is a major argument for reductive physicalism.","etym":null,"example":"You feel thirsty and decide to get water; it seems the thirst moved your legs, but the brain story seems complete without it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Mind-body problem","Epiphenomenalism","Dualism"],"thinkers":["elisabeth","descartes"],"tags":["mind-body causation","causal exclusion","exclusion problem","kim","epiphenomenalism"]},{"term":"Mereology","def":"The study of parts and wholes: how parts make up wholes and when several things compose a further thing.","more":"Its central puzzle, the special composition question, asks when some things add up to a single object. Some say always (so your nose and the Eiffel Tower make an object), some say never (there are only simples arranged in shapes), and van Inwagen said only when they make up a living thing. The debate tests how far common sense should guide metaphysics.","etym":"Greek meros, 'part', plus logos, 'study'.","example":"Is a pile of LEGO bricks one thing, or just many bricks? Does it become one thing when you click them into a castle?","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Ontology","Ship of Theseus","Substance"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen","lewis","sider"],"tags":["parts and wholes","part-whole relation","composition","mereological","wholes"]},{"term":"Metaethics","def":"The study of what morality itself is. It asks whether moral claims can be true, and where moral facts could come from.","example":"Asking ‘Is “stealing is wrong” a fact, or just a feeling?’ is a metaethics question.","related":["Moral realism","Anti-realism","Error theory","Expressivism"],"thinkers":["moore","mackie","street"],"compass":"q4","more":"Ethics in the ordinary sense asks which actions are right; metaethics steps back and asks what 'right' means and whether moral claims can be true at all. Realists say there are moral facts; anti-realists of different kinds deny it or reinterpret moral talk. Moore's Principia Ethica (1903) is often treated as the starting point of modern metaethics.","etym":"Greek meta, 'beyond, about', plus ethics: the study about ethics.","notConfused":"Not the same as normative ethics: normative ethics asks what we ought to do; metaethics asks what an 'ought' is.","field":"Ethics","tags":["meta-ethics","nature of morality","moral realism","moral semantics","moral metaphysics"]},{"term":"Metaphysics","def":"The study of what exists and what it is like at the most basic level. It asks about time, causes, minds, objects and possibility.","example":"Asking whether the past still exists, or whether a smashed vase is the same thing as its pieces, is doing metaphysics.","related":["Ontology","Epistemology","Causation"],"thinkers":["aristotle","lewis"],"more":"Metaphysics deals with questions that experiments alone cannot settle, such as whether time passes, whether there are objects beyond particles, or what makes something the same thing over time. Some empiricists, such as Hume and the logical positivists, attacked it as empty talk. It returned strongly in 20th-century analytic philosophy, especially through work on possibility and identity.","etym":"Greek ta meta ta phusika, 'the things after the Physics': the title later editors gave to Aristotle's books placed after his Physics.","notConfused":"Not the same as the mystical or the paranormal: in bookshops 'metaphysics' can mean crystals and spirits, but in philosophy it means the careful study of reality's basic structure.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["nature of reality","ontology","being","first philosophy","reality"]},{"term":"Middle Way","def":"The Buddha's path between extremes: between harsh self-denial and indulgence in pleasure, and in philosophy between eternalism and annihilationism.","more":"Tradition holds that the Buddha tried both luxury and extreme fasting before finding that neither leads to awakening. Later, the Middle Way also came to mean avoiding the views that things have permanent essences or that nothing exists. Nagarjuna's Madhyamaka school takes its name from this second sense.","etym":null,"example":"A meditator who neither starves nor overeats, but eats enough to practise well, follows the Middle Way.","notConfused":"Not the same as Aristotle's golden mean, though they are often compared: the golden mean is about virtues of character, while the Middle Way is a path to liberation.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Madhyamaka","Dukkha","Golden mean"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna"],"tags":["middle path","madhyama pratipada","moderation","buddha","madhyamaka","avoiding extremes"]},{"term":"Mimesis","def":"Imitation or representation: the idea that art copies or represents reality.","more":"In Republic Book X Plato criticised artists as imitators of imitations, twice removed from the Forms. Aristotle in the Poetics defended mimesis as natural to humans and a source of learning and pleasure. Later theories of art, such as expression theory and formalism, challenged the idea that art is mainly imitation.","etym":"Greek mimesis, 'imitation'; the root of 'mime' and 'mimic'.","example":"A painting of a bowl of fruit so lifelike you want to pick an apple is mimetic art.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Forms","Paradox of fiction"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle"],"tags":["imitation","representation","art as imitation","plato","aristotle"]},{"term":"Mind-body problem","def":"The question of how the mind (thoughts, feelings, experiences) relates to the body, and especially the brain.","more":"Descartes made the problem sharp by arguing that mind and body are different substances. Answers range from dualism to physicalism, idealism and panpsychism. The modern hard problem of consciousness is one part of the wider mind-body problem.","etym":null,"example":"When you stub your toe, how is the pain you feel related to the nerves firing in your brain?","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Dualism","Physicalism","Hard problem","Mental causation"],"thinkers":["descartes","elisabeth","chalmers"],"tags":["mind body problem","mind and body","dualism","physicalism","consciousness"]},{"term":"Miracle","def":"An event that breaks or goes beyond the ordinary laws of nature, usually thought to be caused by God.","more":"In his essay Of Miracles (1748) Hume argued that it is always more likely that a witness is mistaken or lying than that a law of nature was broken, so testimony can hardly ever justify belief in a miracle. Defenders such as Swinburne reply that Hume sets the bar unfairly high. Debate continues over how evidence and prior probability should be weighed.","etym":"Latin miraculum, 'object of wonder'.","example":"Reports that someone rose from the dead are a classic test case.","notConfused":"Not the same as a lucky coincidence: a surprising but natural event is not a miracle in the philosophical sense.","field":"Religion","related":["Laws of nature","Natural theology","Problem of induction"],"thinkers":["hume","swinburne"],"tags":["miracles","supernatural event","violation of natural law","hume on miracles","divine intervention"]},{"term":"Modal realism","def":"David Lewis's view that other possible worlds are just as real as ours: whole universes, concrete like this one, but not connected to it.","more":"Lewis defended it in On the Plurality of Worlds (1986), arguing that it gives the simplest theory of possibility, properties and meaning. Most philosophers reject it, and the common reaction is an 'incredulous stare', which Lewis admitted was hard to answer. Rivals treat possible worlds as abstract descriptions instead.","etym":"Latin modus, 'manner, mode': modal claims are about the ways things could or must be.","example":"On Lewis's view, in another world someone very like you, your counterpart, really did become an astronaut.","notConfused":"Not the same as the multiverse of physics: Lewis's worlds include every possibility, not just ones that physics might produce.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Possible worlds","Necessary/contingent","Counterfactual"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"tags":["david lewis","possible worlds","concrete possible worlds","plurality of worlds","counterpart theory"]},{"term":"Modus ponens","def":"The basic valid argument form: if P then Q; P; therefore Q.","more":"It is the simplest example of a deductive inference and is built into almost every system of logic. Lewis Carroll's dialogue What the Tortoise Said to Achilles (1895) showed that we cannot justify it simply by adding it as a further premise. Philosophers sometimes say 'one person's modus ponens is another's modus tollens': the same conditional can be used to argue in either direction.","etym":"Latin modus ponens, 'the mode that affirms'.","example":"If it is raining, the street is wet. It is raining. So the street is wet.","notConfused":"Not the same as affirming the consequent ('if P then Q; Q; so P'), which is a fallacy.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Modus tollens","Valid","Affirming the consequent"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["affirming the antecedent","mp","if p then q","rule of inference","implication elimination"]},{"term":"Modus tollens","def":"The valid argument form: if P then Q; not Q; therefore not P.","more":"It is the logical pattern behind refutation: if a theory predicts something that does not happen, the theory is false. Popper made this central to his view of science. In real science it is complicated by the fact that predictions depend on many background assumptions.","etym":"Latin modus tollens, 'the mode that removes (denies)'.","example":"If the cake were done, the skewer would come out clean. It didn't. So the cake isn't done.","notConfused":"Not the same as denying the antecedent ('if P then Q; not P; so not Q'), which is a fallacy.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Modus ponens","Falsifiability","Valid"],"thinkers":["popper"],"tags":["denying the consequent","mt","contrapositive","rule of inference","if p then q"]},{"term":"Mohism","def":"The school of Mozi in ancient China, which taught impartial care for everyone and judged actions by whether they benefit the people.","more":"Mohists argued against offensive war, wasteful funerals and costly music, and criticised Confucians for favouring family over strangers. They are often compared to consequentialists. The school was influential for a few centuries and also did early work in logic, but later faded.","etym":"From Mozi (Master Mo), c. 470–391 BCE.","example":"A Mohist would ask of an expensive state ceremony: does this feed anyone, or protect anyone?","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Consequentialism","Ren","Li (ritual propriety)"],"thinkers":["mozi"],"tags":["mozi","mo tzu","mohist","universal love","jian ai","impartial care"]},{"term":"Moksha","def":"In Indian philosophy, liberation from the cycle of birth, death and rebirth (samsara).","more":"Most Hindu schools, and Jainism, see moksha as the highest human goal. In Advaita Vedanta it comes from realising that the self (atman) is the same as Brahman; in Ramanuja's theistic Vedanta it involves loving union with God. Buddhists use the related idea of nirvana.","etym":"Sanskrit moksa, 'release'.","example":"A yogi who meditates for years to escape the cycle of rebirth is seeking moksha.","notConfused":"Not the same as heaven in many Western religions: moksha is release from the whole cycle, not a better place within it.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Karma","Atman","Nirvana","Maya"],"thinkers":["shankara","ramanuja"],"tags":["mokṣa","mukti","liberation","release","liberation from samsara","salvation"]},{"term":"Monism","def":"The view that reality is, at bottom, one thing or one kind of thing.","more":"Substance monism, as in Spinoza, says there is only one substance (God or Nature). Kind monism says there is only one kind of stuff, as in physicalism or idealism. Schaffer has recently defended priority monism: the whole universe is basic and its parts depend on it.","etym":"Greek monos, 'single, alone'.","example":"Waves on the sea look like many things, but a monist might say they are all just the one ocean.","notConfused":"Not the same as monotheism, which is belief in one God; a monist need not be religious.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Dualism","Pantheism","Brahman"],"thinkers":["parmenides","spinoza","schaffer"],"tags":["monist","oneness","one substance","spinoza","parmenides"]},{"term":"Mono no aware","def":"A Japanese aesthetic idea: a gentle, bittersweet sensitivity to the passing of things.","more":"The phrase was made central to Japanese aesthetics by the scholar Motoori Norinaga in the 18th century, in his reading of The Tale of Genji. It links beauty with impermanence, a theme also found in Buddhist thought and in Kenko's Essays in Idleness. It suggests that things are moving partly because they do not last.","etym":"Japanese, roughly 'the pathos (or poignancy) of things'.","example":"Watching cherry blossoms fall in spring, and feeling sad and moved at once.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Dukkha","Mu"],"thinkers":["kenko"],"tags":["pathos of things","sensitivity to ephemera","transience","impermanence","japanese aesthetics"]},{"term":"Moral dilemma","def":"A situation where you ought to do each of two things but cannot do both, so whatever you do seems to involve doing something wrong.","more":"Sartre described a student torn between caring for his mother and joining the Free French in the Second World War. Some philosophers deny real dilemmas exist, since 'ought implies can' and a good theory should always give one answer. Williams argued that even when we choose rightly, the leftover regret shows that something of moral value was lost.","etym":"Greek dilemma, 'double proposition'.","example":"You promised to meet a friend, but on the way you find someone injured who needs help.","notConfused":"Not the same as a hard decision: a dilemma is when moral demands conflict, not just when a choice is difficult.","field":"Ethics","related":["Ought implies can","Trolley problem","Deontology"],"thinkers":["sartre","williams"],"tags":["ethical dilemma","moral conflict","tragic choice","dilemma","dirty hands"]},{"term":"Moral luck","def":"Cases where people are praised or blamed for things that partly depend on factors outside their control.","more":"The term comes from papers by Williams and Nagel (1976). It poses a puzzle: we think people should only be judged for what they control, yet we blame the drunk driver who kills more than the one who gets home safely. Some respond that the two drivers are equally blameworthy; others accept that luck really does affect moral standing.","etym":null,"example":"Two people drive home equally drunk; a child runs into the road in front of one of them.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral responsibility","Free will","Desert"],"thinkers":["williams","nagel"],"tags":["luck","williams","nagel","resultant luck","constitutive luck"]},{"term":"Moral realism","def":"The view that there are objective moral facts that hold whatever anyone thinks or feels.","example":"A realist says ‘torturing for fun is wrong’ is true the way ‘water is H₂O’ is true.","related":["Anti-realism","Error theory","Metaethics"],"thinkers":["plato","moore","parfit"],"compass":"q4","more":"Realists argue that moral facts best explain why moral disagreement feels like disagreement about something real, and why moral progress seems possible. Opponents such as Mackie say objective values would be strange, unlike anything else we know. Realists split into naturalists, who think moral facts are natural facts, and non-naturalists such as Moore and Parfit.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as being strict or certain: a realist can be humble and unsure which moral claims are true.","field":"Ethics","tags":["moral facts","moral objectivity","objective morality","ethical realism","metaethics"]},{"term":"Moral responsibility","def":"Being the right target for praise or blame for what you did.","example":"We hold an adult who steals responsible, but not a sleepwalker who knocks over a vase.","related":["Free will","Desert"],"thinkers":["aristotle","frankfurt"],"compass":"q9","more":"Aristotle said we are responsible for actions done voluntarily and with knowledge, excusing force and ignorance. Modern debate asks whether responsibility needs the ability to do otherwise; Frankfurt argued it does not. Some philosophers, such as Pereboom, argue that nobody is ever truly deserving of blame.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as legal liability: you can be legally liable without being morally to blame, and the other way round.","field":"Ethics","tags":["responsibility","blame","praise","accountability","blameworthiness","free will"]},{"term":"Moral status","def":"The property of mattering morally for your own sake, so that others have duties to take your interests into account.","more":"Philosophers disagree about what grants moral status: being human, being rational, being a person, or simply being able to suffer. Kant linked it to rational agency; Bentham and Singer to the capacity to feel pain. The question shapes debates on animals, abortion, future generations and artificial minds.","etym":null,"example":"Kicking a rock wrongs no one, but kicking a dog wrongs the dog: the dog has moral status.","notConfused":"Not the same as legal status: a being can have moral status even if the law gives it no protection.","field":"Ethics","related":["Person","Utilitarianism","Natural rights"],"thinkers":["singer","kant","quinn_warren"],"tags":["moral considerability","moral standing","who counts","moral patient","personhood"]},{"term":"Mu","def":"A Zen word meaning 'no' or 'nothing', used to cut through ordinary yes-or-no thinking.","more":"It is best known from a koan in which the master Zhaozhou is asked whether a dog has Buddha-nature and answers 'Mu'. Students meditate on the answer, which is not meant to be solved by logic. In the 20th century Nishida built a philosophy of 'absolute nothingness' (zettai mu) as the ground of all reality.","etym":"Japanese mu, Chinese wu 無, 'not, nothing'.","example":"Answering the question 'have you stopped cheating?' with 'mu' rejects the question's assumption rather than picking yes or no.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Mushin","Wu wei"],"thinkers":["nishida","dogen"],"tags":["wu","nothingness","no","joshu's dog","zen","koan"]},{"term":"Multiple realisability","def":"The idea that the same mental state, such as pain, could be produced by very different physical systems.","more":"Putnam argued in the 1960s that octopuses, humans and perhaps aliens or robots could all feel pain despite having very different physical make-ups. If so, pain cannot simply be identical to one particular brain state. This argument helped turn many philosophers from the identity theory to functionalism.","etym":null,"example":"A clock can be made of gears, quartz or atoms; being a clock is about what it does, not what it is made of.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Identity theory","Physicalism"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"tags":["multiple realizability","multiple realization","putnam","functionalism","identity theory"]},{"term":"Mushin","def":"In Zen and Japanese martial arts, 'no-mind': a calm, open state in which you act fully and freely without self-conscious thought getting in the way.","more":"Zen teachers described it as a mind that does not stop or fix on anything, and so responds to whatever comes. The idea shaped teaching in swordsmanship, archery and the tea ceremony. It is often compared with the Daoist idea of wu wei.","etym":"Japanese mushin 無心, 'no mind'.","example":"A skilled pianist who plays without thinking about where each finger goes is close to mushin.","notConfused":"Not the same as having an empty or lazy mind: mushin is highly alert, just not cluttered.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Mu","Wu wei","Dao"],"thinkers":["dogen"],"tags":["mushin no shin","no-mind","no mind","empty mind","zen","martial arts"]},{"term":"Naturalism","def":"The view that the natural world is all there is, and that the methods of science are our best way of finding out about it.","more":"Metaphysical naturalism denies anything supernatural, such as gods, souls or ghosts. Methodological naturalism says philosophy should work alongside science rather than above it, a view strongly associated with Quine. Critics ask whether values, numbers and consciousness can find a place in a purely natural world.","etym":"Latin natura, 'nature'.","example":"A naturalist explains human kindness through evolution, psychology and culture, not through a soul.","notConfused":"Not the same as the naturalistic fallacy, and not the same as being a naturalist who studies wildlife.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Physicalism","Materialism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["quine","dewey","papineau"],"tags":["naturalist","philosophical naturalism","methodological naturalism","no supernatural","science"]},{"term":"Naturalistic fallacy","def":"G. E. Moore’s charge that it is a mistake to define ‘good’ as some natural property, such as ‘pleasant’ or ‘what we desire’.","example":"Saying ‘good just means what evolution favours’ would commit it, according to Moore.","related":["Open question argument","Is–ought gap"],"thinkers":["moore"],"more":"Moore introduced the term in Principia Ethica (1903), arguing that goodness is a simple, non-natural property. Many philosophers doubt it is really a fallacy, since 'water is H2O' was also an informative discovery about meaning and reference. The phrase is now often used loosely for the inference from 'natural' to 'good'.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as the appeal to nature ('it's natural, so it's good'): that is a related but looser error.","field":"Ethics","tags":["moore","open question argument","defining good","is-ought","appeal to nature"]},{"term":"Natural kind","def":"A grouping of things that reflects a real division in nature, rather than one people made up for their own purposes.","more":"Gold, water, electrons and tigers are standard examples; 'things in my drawer' or 'weeds' are not. Kripke and Putnam argued that natural kind words like 'water' refer to whatever shares the hidden nature of the samples we name, which we discover by science. Philosophers debate whether biological species and races are natural kinds or partly social ones.","etym":null,"example":"Whales were once grouped with fish, but the natural kind they belong to is mammals.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Essence","Laws of nature","Universals"],"thinkers":["kripke","putnam"],"tags":["natural kinds","kinds","species","water is h2o","classification"]},{"term":"Natural law","def":"The idea that there is a moral law built into human nature and knowable by reason, which human laws should follow.","more":"Aquinas held that natural law is our share in God's eternal law, and that its first rule is to do good and avoid evil. It guides us towards basic human goods such as life, knowledge and society. Modern natural law theorists such as John Finnis defend it without relying on religion; critics doubt that ethics can be read off human nature.","etym":"Latin lex naturalis.","example":"A natural law theorist might argue that a law allowing slavery is not a true law at all, since it violates human nature.","notConfused":"Not the same as laws of nature in science: natural law tells us how we ought to act, not how things in fact behave.","field":"Ethics","related":["Natural rights","Telos","Virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["aquinas","finnis","cicero"],"tags":["natural law theory","aquinas","lex naturalis","moral law","human nature"]},{"term":"Natural rights","def":"Rights people have simply as human beings, not because a government grants them.","example":"Locke named life, liberty and property.","related":["State of nature","Social contract","Liberty (negative/positive)"],"thinkers":["locke","nozick"],"more":"Natural rights theory shaped the American Declaration of Independence and the French Declaration of the Rights of Man. Bentham famously mocked natural rights as 'nonsense upon stilts', arguing rights come only from law. Today's human rights are a close descendant, though many defend them without appealing to nature.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as legal rights: legal rights are created by law, while natural rights are said to exist whether or not any law recognises them.","field":"Politics and society","tags":["inalienable rights","human rights","locke","life liberty property","rights"]},{"term":"Natural theology","def":"Trying to learn about God using only reason and observation of the world, without relying on scripture or revelation.","more":"Classic arguments for God's existence, such as the cosmological and design arguments, are part of natural theology. Aquinas held that reason can show that God exists, though some truths need revelation. Hume's Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779) is a famous criticism; Swinburne is a leading modern defender.","etym":null,"example":"Arguing from the fine-tuning of physical constants to a designer is natural theology.","notConfused":"Not the same as revealed theology, which starts from sacred texts or religious experience.","field":"Religion","related":["Design argument","Cosmological argument","Ontological argument"],"thinkers":["aquinas","swinburne","hume"],"tags":["arguments for god","proofs of god","reason and god","paley","aquinas"]},{"term":"Necessary and sufficient conditions","def":"A necessary condition is something that must be present for something else to happen or be true; a sufficient condition is something that guarantees it.","more":"Philosophers often try to define a concept by listing conditions that are each necessary and together sufficient, as in the classic analysis of knowledge as justified true belief. Counterexamples, such as Gettier cases, attack such analyses by showing the conditions are not sufficient. Wittgenstein doubted that many concepts have such definitions at all.","etym":null,"example":"Having oxygen is necessary for a fire but not sufficient; being a square is sufficient for being a rectangle but not necessary.","notConfused":"Not the same as each other: mixing them up is behind many errors, such as assuming that because studying is necessary for passing it is sufficient.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Gettier problem","Argument","Modus ponens"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["necessary condition","sufficient condition","if and only if","iff","definition"]},{"term":"Necessary/contingent","def":"A necessary truth could not have been false; a contingent truth happens to be true but could have been otherwise.","example":"‘2 + 2 = 4’ is necessary. ‘Paris is the capital of France’ is contingent.","related":["Possible worlds","Analytic/synthetic"],"thinkers":["leibniz","kripke"],"more":"Philosophers distinguish kinds of necessity: logical (denying it leads to contradiction), physical (fixed by the laws of nature) and metaphysical. Leibniz argued that God is a necessary being while the world is contingent. Kripke argued that some necessary truths, such as 'water is H2O', can only be discovered by experience.","etym":"Latin necessarius, 'unavoidable'; contingens, 'happening, touching on'.","notConfused":"Not the same as a priori/a posteriori: that distinction is about how we know something, while necessary/contingent is about whether it could have been otherwise.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["necessity","contingency","necessary truth","contingent truth","modality","possible worlds"]},{"term":"Negative theology","def":"The approach that says we can only describe God by saying what God is not, since God is beyond all human concepts.","more":"Also called the via negativa, it appears in Neoplatonism, in Christian mystics, and in Maimonides, who held that positive attributes wrongly treat God as one thing among others. Saying God is not limited, not ignorant and not material brings us closer to the truth than calling God 'wise' in our sense. Critics worry it leaves nothing to believe in.","etym":"Greek apophasis, 'denial'; Latin via negativa, 'the negative way'.","example":"Saying 'God is not in time' rather than 'God is eternal' in a way we can picture.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Brahman","Dao","Ontological argument"],"thinkers":["maimonides","plotinus"],"tags":["apophatic theology","apophaticism","via negativa","pseudo-dionysius","god beyond description"]},{"term":"Neo-Confucianism","def":"A revival and reworking of Confucian thought in China from about the 11th century, which added a full metaphysics partly in response to Buddhism and Daoism.","more":"Its great systematiser was Zhu Xi, whose commentaries on the Four Books became the basis of the imperial exams for centuries. He taught that the world is made of li (principle) and qi (vital energy). Wang Yangming founded a rival school stressing that principle is found in the mind and that knowing and acting are one.","etym":null,"example":"A student in imperial China memorising Zhu Xi's commentary on the Analects for the civil service exam.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Li (principle)","Ren","Dao"],"thinkers":["zhuxi","wangyangming"],"tags":["neoconfucianism","zhu xi","wang yangming","li and qi","song dynasty"]},{"term":"Nihilism","def":"The view that something we prize does not exist: often that life has no meaning, or that there are no moral values.","example":"‘Nothing matters, so nothing is worth doing’ is a nihilist thought.","related":["Absurd","Existentialism","Error theory"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"compass":"q20","more":"The word covers several distinct views: moral nihilism (there are no moral facts), existential nihilism (life has no meaning) and even nihilism about objects in metaphysics. Nietzsche saw nihilism as a crisis following the decline of religious belief and hoped humans could create new values. Existentialists such as Camus tried to show how to live well without rejecting meaning altogether.","etym":"Latin nihil, 'nothing'.","notConfused":"Not the same as pessimism: pessimism says things are bad, while nihilism says there are no real values at all.","field":"Ethics","tags":["nihilist","meaninglessness","nothing matters","nietzsche","moral nihilism","existential nihilism"]},{"term":"Nirvana","def":"In Buddhism, the ending of craving, ignorance and suffering, and so release from the cycle of rebirth.","more":"The Buddha taught it as the goal of the Eightfold Path. Early texts describe it mostly by what it is not, as the cessation of greed, hatred and delusion. Mahayana thinkers such as Nagarjuna argued that nirvana and the everyday world (samsara) are not ultimately different.","etym":"Sanskrit nirvana, 'blowing out', as of a flame.","example":"A flame goes out when its fuel runs out; in the same way suffering ends when craving ends.","notConfused":"Not the same as heaven, nor as simply being blissful: it is the end of the causes of suffering.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dukkha","Moksha","Anatta (no-self)","Karma"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna"],"tags":["nibbana","nirvāṇa","enlightenment","liberation","extinction","cessation of suffering"]},{"term":"Nominalism","def":"The view that only particular things exist; ‘universals’ and abstract objects like numbers are just names or useful fictions.","example":"A nominalist says there are many red things, but no extra thing called ‘redness’.","related":["Universals","Platonism"],"thinkers":["ockham"],"compass":"q12","more":"The medieval debate over universals set realists, who said shared properties like 'redness' really exist, against nominalists such as Ockham. Modern nominalists also deny abstract objects such as numbers and sets, and try to rewrite mathematics and science without them. Their challenge is to explain what all red things have in common without appealing to a shared property.","etym":"Latin nomen, 'name': universals are 'mere names'.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","tags":["nominalist","no universals","only particulars","ockham","universals"]},{"term":"No-miracles argument","def":"The argument that science's success would be a miracle unless its best theories were at least roughly true.","more":"Putnam gave the argument its classic form in the 1970s, and it is the main support for scientific realism. Anti-realists reply with the history of science: many successful past theories turned out false, which is the 'pessimistic meta-induction'. Van Fraassen argued that theories succeed because unsuccessful ones are weeded out, as in natural selection.","etym":null,"example":"Physicists predicted the Higgs boson decades before it was detected; it seems a miracle if the theory were not tracking something real.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific realism","Instrumentalism","Induction"],"thinkers":["putnam","van_fraassen","worrall"],"tags":["no miracles argument","putnam","scientific realism","success of science","ultimate argument"]},{"term":"Non-identity problem","def":"The puzzle that some choices affecting future generations seem wrong even though they make no one worse off, because without them those particular people would never have existed.","more":"Parfit set out the problem in Reasons and Persons (1984). If a society uses up resources, the people born later will be different people from those who would otherwise have been born, and their lives may still be worth living. So who has been harmed? The puzzle troubles theories that say wrongness requires harm to a particular person.","etym":null,"example":"A woman told to wait a few months before conceiving to avoid a health risk does not wait; her child, who would not otherwise have existed, has a good but harder life.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Repugnant conclusion","Moral status","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"tags":["parfit","future generations","procreative ethics","wrongful life"]},{"term":"Non sequitur","def":"A conclusion that does not follow from what came before it.","more":"In logic the phrase can cover any invalid inference; in everyday use it often means a remark with no connection to what was said. Spotting a non sequitur often means finding a hidden premise that is needed but false. Many named fallacies are special kinds of non sequitur.","etym":"Latin non sequitur, 'it does not follow'.","example":"'She's a good singer, so she would make a good prime minister.'","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Valid","Premise"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["does not follow","irrelevant conclusion","invalid inference","fallacy"]},{"term":"Normative","def":"About how things ought to be, or what is good or right, rather than how things actually are.","more":"Normative claims include moral claims but also claims about rationality, evidence and taste. Normative ethics asks what we ought to do, as consequentialism, deontology and virtue ethics try to answer. The contrast between normative and descriptive is central to Hume's is-ought gap.","etym":"Latin norma, 'carpenter's square, rule'.","example":"'Most people tip in restaurants' is descriptive; 'you should tip' is normative.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'normal': normative is about what ought to be done, not about what is usual.","field":"Ethics","related":["Is–ought gap","Metaethics","Ethics"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["normativity","prescriptive","ought","evaluative","descriptive vs normative"]},{"term":"No true Scotsman","def":"The fallacy of protecting a claim from a counterexample by redefining terms, usually by declaring the counterexample not a 'real' or 'true' member of the group.","more":"The name was popularised by Antony Flew in the 1970s. It turns a factual claim into one that is true by definition, so that it can no longer be tested. It is a kind of ad hoc rescue of a generalisation.","etym":null,"example":"'No vegetarian eats fish.' 'My vegetarian friend eats fish.' 'Then she is not a true vegetarian.'","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Equivocation","Falsifiability"],"thinkers":["flew"],"tags":["no true scotsman fallacy","appeal to purity","moving the goalposts","ad hoc redefinition"]},{"term":"Noumenon","def":"Kant's term for a thing as it is in itself, apart from how it appears to us; we cannot know it.","more":"Kant argued that space, time and causation are forms our minds impose on experience, so we only ever know phenomena, things as they appear. Things in themselves lie behind phenomena but cannot be known. Critics, from Hegel onward, asked how Kant could say anything about something unknowable; Schopenhauer claimed we touch the thing in itself as will.","etym":"Greek nooumenon, 'that which is thought'.","example":"Wearing tinted glasses you cannot remove, you never see what colour things are without the tint.","notConfused":"Not the same as a phenomenon: phenomena are things as experienced; noumena are things as they are in themselves.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Idealism","Phenomenology","Skepticism"],"thinkers":["kant","schopenhauer"],"tags":["noumena","thing-in-itself","thing in itself","ding an sich","kant","phenomena"]},{"term":"Nyaya","def":"One of the classical schools of Indian philosophy, known for its careful theory of logic, argument and how we come to know things.","more":"Its founding text, the Nyaya Sutras, is traditionally credited to Akshapada Gautama. Nyaya lists four sources of knowledge (pramanas): perception, inference, comparison and testimony, and gives a detailed model of the valid five-step inference. It also defended realism about the external world and arguments for God against Buddhist critics.","etym":"Sanskrit nyaya, 'rule, method, reasoning'.","example":"Nyaya's standard inference: there is fire on the hill, because there is smoke there, and wherever there is smoke there is fire, as in a kitchen.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Logic","Induction","Epistemology"],"thinkers":["akshapada"],"tags":["nyāya","indian logic","gautama","pramana","hindu logic"]},{"term":"Objectivity","def":"Being true or real independently of anyone's opinions, feelings or point of view; also, a way of judging that sets personal bias aside.","more":"Thomas Nagel described objectivity as stepping back from our own perspective towards a 'view from nowhere', while warning that some things, such as what experience is like, are lost in that view. Philosophers debate whether ethics, beauty and even science are objective. Some feminist and pragmatist thinkers argue that all knowledge comes from somewhere.","etym":"Latin objectum, 'thing put before (the mind)'.","example":"The temperature in a room is objective; whether the room feels cosy is more subjective.","notConfused":"Not the same as neutrality: an objective judgement can take a firm side, as long as it is based on the facts.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Relativism","Moral realism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["nagel"],"tags":["objective","impartiality","mind-independence","view from nowhere","neutrality"]},{"term":"Occam’s razor","def":"The rule of thumb that, other things being equal, we should prefer the simpler explanation that assumes fewer things.","example":"If the milk is gone and your roommate was home, blame the roommate before blaming ghosts.","related":["Induction","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["ockham"],"more":"The principle is linked to the medieval logician William of Ockham, though the famous wording 'entities should not be multiplied beyond necessity' comes from later writers. It is a guide for choosing between theories, not a proof that the simpler one is true. Philosophers debate whether simplicity is a sign of truth or just a practical convenience.","etym":"Named after William of Ockham (c. 1287–1347); a 'razor' shaves away unneeded assumptions.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'the simplest explanation is always right': the razor only applies when rival explanations fit the evidence equally well.","field":"Method","tags":["ockham's razor","occam's razor","parsimony","principle of parsimony","simplicity","lex parsimoniae"]},{"term":"Occasionalism","def":"The view that created things have no real causal powers: God is the only true cause, and events in nature are merely the occasions on which God acts.","more":"Al-Ghazali argued that fire does not itself burn cotton; God creates the burning when fire and cotton meet, and could do otherwise. In 17th-century Europe Malebranche held a similar view, partly to explain how mind and body interact. Critics say it makes the regular order of nature, and our own agency, mysterious.","etym":"Latin occasio, 'occasion, opportunity'.","example":"When you flip a light switch, the occasionalist says God makes the light come on, using your action as the occasion.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Causation","Miracle","Mind-body problem"],"thinkers":["alghazali","malebranche","ashari"],"tags":["malebranche","al-ghazali","god as only cause","divine causation","causation"]},{"term":"Omnipotence","def":"Being all-powerful: the ability to do anything.","more":"The paradox of the stone asks whether God can create a stone too heavy for God to lift; either answer seems to name something God cannot do. Aquinas held that omnipotence means being able to do anything logically possible, so it is no limit that God cannot make a square circle. Mackie used puzzles about omnipotence in arguing against God.","etym":"Latin omnis, 'all', plus potens, 'powerful'.","example":"Can an all-powerful being make 2 + 2 = 5? Most theists answer that this is not a 'thing' at all.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Omniscience","Problem of evil","Paradox"],"thinkers":["aquinas","mackie"],"tags":["all-powerful","almighty","omnipotent","paradox of the stone","divine power"]},{"term":"Omniscience","def":"Knowing everything that can be known.","more":"Its most famous puzzle is whether God's foreknowledge is compatible with human free will: if God knows today what you will do tomorrow, can you do otherwise? Boethius answered that God is outside time and sees all moments at once, as an eternal present. Others, such as open theists, say even God cannot know undetermined future choices.","etym":"Latin omnis, 'all', plus sciens, 'knowing'.","example":"If God already knows what you will order at lunch tomorrow, is your choice really free?","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Omnipotence","Free will","Problem of evil"],"thinkers":["boethius","augustine"],"tags":["all-knowing","omniscient","divine knowledge","foreknowledge"]},{"term":"Ontological argument","def":"An argument that God exists from the very idea of God: a perfect being would lack something if it did not exist.","example":"Anselm: God is that than which nothing greater can be conceived, and such a being must be real, not just imagined.","related":["Cosmological argument","Design argument"],"thinkers":["anselm","descartes","kant"],"compass":"q19","more":"Anselm's version (c. 1078) was criticised at once by the monk Gaunilo, who said the same reasoning could prove a perfect island exists. Kant objected that existence is not a property that makes a thing greater. Modern versions using modal logic were developed by Plantinga and others, and the argument remains debated.","etym":"Greek on, 'being': an argument from the concept of being.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","tags":["anselm","descartes","god as greatest being","proof of god","a priori argument for god"]},{"term":"Ontology","def":"The part of metaphysics that asks what kinds of things exist. Do numbers exist? Do holes? Do nations?","example":"Arguing about whether a shadow is a real thing or just an absence of light is an ontology question.","related":["Metaphysics","Universals","Nominalism"],"thinkers":["quine","parmenides"],"more":"Ontology tries to draw up the most basic list of what there is, and to sort it into categories such as objects, properties, events and numbers. Quine's slogan was that to be is to be the value of a variable: roughly, you are committed to whatever your best theories have to talk about. Some philosophers think many ontology debates are merely verbal disputes about words.","etym":"Greek on (stem ont-), 'being', plus logos, 'study': a 17th-century coinage.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","tags":["being","what exists","existence","metaphysics","ontological commitment"]},{"term":"Open question argument","def":"Moore’s argument: for any natural property X, ‘Is X good?’ still seems an open question, so ‘good’ cannot simply mean X.","example":"‘This is pleasant, but is it good?’ makes sense, so ‘good’ does not just mean ‘pleasant’.","related":["Naturalistic fallacy","Moral realism"],"thinkers":["moore"],"more":"Moore used the argument to support his claim that goodness cannot be defined in natural terms. Critics reply that a definition can be true even when it is not obvious, just as 'water is H2O' was a discovery. The argument still shapes debates between naturalists and non-naturalists in metaethics.","etym":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","tags":["moore","naturalistic fallacy","definition of good","principia ethica","non-naturalism"]},{"term":"Original position","def":"John Rawls's thought experiment: imagine choosing the principles of justice for your society without knowing what position you will have in it.","more":"In A Theory of Justice (1971) the choosers sit behind a veil of ignorance that hides their class, talents, gender and ideas of the good life. Rawls argued they would choose equal basic liberties and the difference principle. Critics, such as Nozick and communitarians, dispute both the set-up and what would be chosen.","etym":null,"example":"Designing a game's rules before knowing which player you will be makes you want fair rules.","notConfused":"Not the same as the state of nature: the original position is a hypothetical device for testing fairness, not a story about life before government.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Veil of ignorance","Difference principle","Social contract","Justice"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"tags":["rawls","veil of ignorance","justice as fairness","social contract","a theory of justice"]},{"term":"Other minds","def":"The problem of how we can know that other people have thoughts and feelings at all, when we only ever see their behaviour.","more":"Mill gave the classic argument from analogy: others have bodies and behaviour like mine, so they probably have minds like mine. Critics note that this generalises from a single case, your own. Wittgenstein and others argued that we often see minds directly in faces and actions, so the problem rests on a mistaken picture.","etym":null,"example":"When a friend winces, you are sure they are in pain, but you never feel their pain yourself.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Skepticism","Zombie","Behaviorism"],"thinkers":["mill","wittgenstein"],"tags":["problem of other minds","other people's minds","solipsism","argument from analogy","skepticism"]},{"term":"Ought implies can","def":"The principle that you can only be morally required to do something if you are able to do it.","more":"It is often attributed to Kant, who uses the idea in several places. It explains why we excuse people who could not have helped. Some philosophers challenge it, pointing to moral dilemmas or to people who make themselves unable to do their duty.","etym":null,"example":"You cannot be obliged to save a drowning swimmer if you are a hundred miles away.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral dilemma","Moral responsibility","Free will"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["kant","moral obligation","ability","impossibility excuses","duty"]},{"term":"Pacifism","def":"The view that war and violence are always or almost always wrong.","more":"Absolute pacifists reject all violence, even in self-defence; others accept personal self-defence but reject war. Tolstoy and Gandhi drew on religious and moral sources to defend nonviolent resistance. Critics, often from just war theory, argue that refusing to fight can allow greater evils.","etym":"Latin pax, 'peace', plus facere, 'to make'.","example":"A conscientious objector who refuses to fight in any war is acting as a pacifist.","notConfused":"Not the same as passivity: many pacifists actively resist injustice using nonviolent means.","field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Deontology","Doctrine of double effect"],"thinkers":["tolstoy"],"tags":["pacifist","nonviolence","anti-war","peace","ahimsa","conscientious objection"]},{"term":"Panpsychism","def":"The view that consciousness, in some very simple form, is a basic and widespread feature of the physical world.","example":"A panpsychist might say an electron has an unimaginably faint inner glimmer of experience.","related":["Hard problem","Physicalism","Dualism"],"thinkers":["goff","gstrawson","leibniz"],"compass":"q6","more":"Panpsychism has an old history, with versions in ancient Greek thought and in Leibniz. It has returned because some think it avoids both the hard problem (how brain matter could produce experience) and the problems of dualism. Its main challenge is the combination problem: how tiny bits of experience could add up to a single unified mind like yours.","etym":"Greek pan, 'all', plus psyche, 'soul, mind'.","notConfused":"Not the same as saying rocks think: most panpsychists say only the smallest parts of matter have very simple experience.","field":"Mind","tags":["panpsychist","mind everywhere","universal consciousness","consciousness","combination problem"]},{"term":"Pantheism","def":"The view that God and the universe are the same thing: everything together is God.","more":"Spinoza's Ethics (1677) is the most famous philosophical version, identifying God with Nature, the one infinite substance. Pantheists usually reject a personal God who stands outside the world and acts on it. The word dates from the early 18th century.","etym":"Greek pan, 'all', plus theos, 'god'.","example":"A pantheist sees the starry sky not as God's creation but as part of God.","notConfused":"Not the same as panentheism, which says the world is in God but God is more than the world.","field":"Religion","related":["Monism","Brahman","Dao"],"thinkers":["spinoza"],"tags":["pantheist","god is nature","deus sive natura","spinoza","everything is god"]},{"term":"Paradigm","def":"Thomas Kuhn’s term for the shared framework of theories, methods and examples that a scientific community works within during ‘normal science’.","example":"Before Copernicus, astronomers worked within an Earth-centred paradigm.","related":["Scientific realism","Falsifiability"],"more":"In The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962) Kuhn argued that science does not grow by steady accumulation. Long periods of 'normal science' within a paradigm are broken by crises and revolutions, after which scientists see the world differently. Critics worried this made science seem irrational; Kuhn replied that theory choice still follows shared values such as accuracy and simplicity.","etym":"Greek paradeigma, 'pattern, example'.","notConfused":"Not the same as the business buzzword 'paradigm shift' meaning any big change: Kuhn meant a change in a whole scientific framework.","field":"Science","thinkers":["kuhn"],"tags":["paradigm shift","kuhn","normal science","scientific revolution","worldview"]},{"term":"Paradox","def":"An argument that seems to start from true premises and reason correctly, yet reaches a conclusion that seems false or contradictory.","more":"Paradoxes are useful because they show that at least one of our assumptions must go. Zeno's paradoxes of motion, the sorites paradox and the liar paradox have driven work in mathematics, logic and metaphysics. Russell's paradox about sets helped reshape the foundations of mathematics around 1900.","etym":"Greek paradoxon, 'contrary to expectation'.","example":"Zeno argued that a runner can never finish a race, since first they must cover half the distance, then half of what remains, and so on forever.","notConfused":"Not the same as a contradiction: a contradiction is plainly false, while a paradox is a puzzle about how good reasoning led there.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Liar paradox","Sorites paradox","Reductio ad absurdum"],"thinkers":["zeno_elea","russell"],"tags":["paradoxes","contradiction","puzzle","antinomy","self-contradiction"]},{"term":"Paradox of fiction","def":"The puzzle of how we can feel real emotions, such as fear or grief, for characters we know do not exist.","more":"Colin Radford set out the puzzle in 1975, and suggested our responses may be irrational. Others argue we feel 'quasi-emotions' in games of make-believe, or that emotions do not need belief in the existence of their object. The puzzle connects aesthetics to the philosophy of emotion.","etym":null,"example":"Crying when a character dies in a novel, while knowing perfectly well she never lived.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Mimesis","Paradox"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["emotions toward fiction","fictional characters","radford","anna karenina","fear of fiction"]},{"term":"Particularism (moral)","def":"The view that moral judgement does not depend on general principles: whether a feature counts for or against an action depends on the situation.","more":"Jonathan Dancy is its leading defender. He argues that a feature that counts in favour of an action in one case, such as that it gives pleasure, can count against it in another, such as pleasure in cruelty. Critics reply that without principles moral reasoning becomes unpredictable and hard to teach.","etym":"Latin particularis, 'concerning a part'.","example":"Telling the truth is usually good, but telling a murderer where your friend is hiding is not.","notConfused":"Not the same as relativism: particularists can believe there are right answers, just not ones captured by rules.","field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Deontology","Intuition"],"thinkers":["dancy"],"tags":["moral particularism","dancy","no moral principles","situational ethics","particularism"]},{"term":"Pascal’s wager","def":"Blaise Pascal’s argument that it is rational to bet on God: if God exists, the gain is infinite; if not, the loss is small.","example":"Like buying cheap insurance against an enormous possible loss.","related":["Problem of evil","Ontological argument"],"compass":"q19","more":"The argument appears in Pascal's Pensées, published in 1670, eight years after his death. It does not claim to prove God exists, only that betting on God is the wise choice. Critics raise the 'many gods' objection (which God should you bet on?) and doubt that you can choose to believe for practical reasons.","etym":"Named after Blaise Pascal (1623–1662).","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","thinkers":["pascal"],"tags":["pascal's wager","pascal","wager","betting on god","decision theory"]},{"term":"Paternalism","def":"Limiting someone’s freedom for their own good, against their will.","example":"Laws requiring seatbelts, or a tax on sugary drinks to protect drinkers’ health.","related":["Harm principle","Liberty (negative/positive)"],"thinkers":["mill"],"compass":"q27","more":"Mill's harm principle forbids paternalism towards competent adults, though he allowed stopping someone crossing an unsafe bridge they did not know was unsafe. 'Soft' paternalism steps in only when choices are not fully informed or voluntary. Nudges, a modern idea from Thaler and Sunstein, try to steer choices while leaving people free to opt out.","etym":"Latin pater, 'father': treating adults as a parent treats a child.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","tags":["paternalistic","nanny state","for your own good","soft paternalism","autonomy"]},{"term":"Perception","def":"Gaining awareness of the world through the senses: sight, hearing, touch, taste and smell.","more":"Naive or direct realists say we see objects themselves. Indirect realists, such as Locke, say we are directly aware only of ideas or sense data caused by objects. Illusions and hallucinations are the main test cases, and Berkeley and Reid gave very different responses.","etym":"Latin percipere, 'to seize, take in'.","example":"A straight stick looks bent in water: do you see the stick, or only an image of it?","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","Primary and secondary qualities","Idealism"],"thinkers":["locke","berkeley","reid"],"tags":["perceiving","senses","sense perception","seeing","sensation"]},{"term":"Perfectionism","def":"In ethics, the view that a good life consists in developing the excellent capacities of human nature, such as knowledge, skill and achievement.","more":"Its roots are in Aristotle's ethics; Thomas Hurka is a leading modern defender. In politics, perfectionism means that the state may promote good ways of living, a view defended by Joseph Raz and opposed by liberals who want the state to stay neutral. Critics ask who decides what human excellence is.","etym":"Latin perfectus, 'completed'.","example":"A perfectionist values mastering the violin even if it makes the player no happier.","notConfused":"Not the same as the everyday habit of perfectionism, being anxious that everything must be flawless.","field":"Ethics","related":["Eudaimonia","Virtue ethics","Telos"],"thinkers":["aristotle","hurka","raz"],"tags":["perfectionist","human excellence","flourishing","self-realisation","nietzsche"]},{"term":"Person","def":"A being with a certain level of mind, usually thought to include self-awareness, reasoning and the ability to plan, and often taken to have special moral status.","more":"Boethius defined a person as an individual substance of a rational nature, and Locke described a thinking being that can consider itself as itself across times and places. Philosophers debate whether some animals or future AIs could be persons, and whether all humans are. Frankfurt argued that persons can care about which desires they act on.","etym":"Latin persona, 'mask worn by an actor'.","example":"A sci-fi debate about whether an android has rights is a debate about whether it is a person.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'human being': a person is defined by mental capacities, so in principle a non-human could be one.","field":"Mind","related":["Personal identity","Moral status","Psychological continuity"],"thinkers":["boethius","locke","frankfurt"],"tags":["personhood","persons","self","moral status","rational being"]},{"term":"Personal identity","def":"The question of what makes you the same person over time, despite changes in body and mind.","example":"Are you the same person as the baby in your parents’ photos? What makes that true?","related":["Psychological continuity","Animalism","Ship of Theseus"],"thinkers":["locke","hume","parfit"],"compass":"q10","more":"Locke argued that a person persists through consciousness and memory, not through having the same body or soul. Animalists reply that you are a human animal and persist as long as the organism does. Parfit argued that identity is not what matters: what matters is psychological connection, which can come in degrees.","etym":"Latin persona, 'mask, character in a play'; identitas, 'sameness'.","notConfused":"Not the same as identity in the social sense (your sense of who you are, your culture or group): the philosophical question is about sameness over time.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["identity over time","self","same person","persistence","locke","psychological continuity"]},{"term":"Perspectivism","def":"Nietzsche's view that all knowing happens from some point of view, so there is no 'view from nowhere', only more or less rich perspectives.","more":"Nietzsche suggested that the more perspectives we can bring to a matter, the fuller our understanding of it becomes. Scholars disagree about whether this amounts to denying objective truth or only to denying a single God's-eye view. It influenced later postmodern and pragmatist thinkers.","etym":"Latin perspicere, 'to see through'.","example":"A historian, an engineer and a poet each describe the same bridge, and each notices things the others miss.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'anything goes' relativism: Nietzsche thought some perspectives are richer or healthier than others.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Relativism","Objectivity","Will to power"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"tags":["nietzsche","perspectives","no view from nowhere","relativism","interpretation"]},{"term":"Phenomenology","def":"A method of philosophy that starts by carefully describing experience exactly as it shows up to us, before any theory. Also the name of the movement founded by Husserl.","example":"Describing how a hammer ‘disappears’ into your hand while you use it, and only stands out when it breaks, is phenomenology.","related":["Existentialism","Qualia"],"thinkers":["husserl","heidegger","beauvoir"],"more":"Husserl asked philosophers to 'bracket', or set aside, questions about whether the world really exists, and describe the structures of experience itself, such as how every experience is directed at something. Heidegger, Sartre, Merleau-Ponty and Beauvoir took the method in new directions, focusing on everyday life, the body and freedom. In philosophy of mind the word is also used more loosely for 'what an experience is like'.","etym":"Greek phainomenon, 'that which appears', plus logos, 'study'.","notConfused":"Not the same as phenomenalism: phenomenalism is the view that physical objects are built out of sense experiences, while phenomenology is a method of describing experience.","field":"Method","tags":["husserl","heidegger","merleau-ponty","lived experience","first-person experience","epoché"]},{"term":"Physicalism","def":"The view that everything that exists is physical, or fixed by the physical, including minds.","example":"A physicalist says your feeling of joy just is (or is fully fixed by) brain activity.","related":["Materialism","Dualism","Identity theory","Functionalism"],"thinkers":["dennett","lewis"],"compass":"q6","more":"Physicalism is the main view in today's philosophy of mind, but it comes in forms. Reductive physicalists say mental states are brain states; non-reductive physicalists say mental properties are fixed by physical ones without being identical to them. The biggest challenge is consciousness: arguments like the zombie and Mary's room try to show that experience is something extra.","etym":"Greek phusis, 'nature'.","notConfused":"Not the same as believing only in what you can touch: physicalists accept fields, forces and anything physics needs.","field":"Mind","tags":["materialism","physicalist","everything is physical","naturalism","identity theory"]},{"term":"Platonism","def":"In general, the view that abstract objects like numbers really exist outside space and time. Named after Plato’s theory of Forms.","example":"A Platonist says the number 7 would exist even if no one ever counted anything.","related":["Forms","Universals","Nominalism"],"thinkers":["plato","frege"],"compass":"q12","more":"Mathematical Platonists such as Frege argued that numbers are real abstract objects, since true mathematical claims seem to be about something. The main objection, pressed by Paul Benacerraf, is how we could ever know about objects that have no causal contact with us. The capital-P word can also mean the broader philosophy of Plato and his followers.","etym":"From Plato (Greek Platon), c. 428–348 BCE.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'platonic love': that everyday phrase means a non-romantic friendship.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["plato","forms","platonic realism","abstract objects","realism about universals"]},{"term":"Possible worlds","def":"Complete ways things could have been. Talking about them helps make sense of ‘could’, ‘must’ and ‘might have’.","example":"There is a possible world where you were born in a different city.","related":["Necessary/contingent","Counterfactual"],"thinkers":["leibniz","lewis","kripke"],"more":"Leibniz said God chose this as the best of all possible worlds. In the 20th century Kripke and others used possible worlds to give precise meanings to 'necessary' (true in all worlds) and 'possible' (true in at least one). They disagree over what possible worlds are: Lewis held they are real concrete universes, while most philosophers treat them as abstract descriptions.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as the 'many worlds' of quantum physics: possible worlds are a tool for thinking about possibility, not a physical hypothesis.","field":"Metaphysics","tags":["possible world","modal logic","kripke","lewis","modal realism","possibility"]},{"term":"Post hoc ergo propter hoc","def":"The fallacy of assuming that because one thing happened after another, the first caused the second.","more":"It is a basic error about causation, often seen in superstitions and health claims. Ruling it out needs controlled comparison, which is why science uses experiments and control groups. Hume's analysis of causation shows why mere sequence is not enough to show a cause.","etym":"Latin post hoc ergo propter hoc, 'after this, therefore because of this'.","example":"'I wore my lucky socks and we won, so the socks made us win.'","notConfused":"Not the same as confusing correlation with causation in general, though the two are closely linked.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Causation","Non sequitur"],"thinkers":["hume"],"tags":["post hoc","false cause","after this therefore because of this","correlation causation","questionable cause"]},{"term":"Postmodernism","def":"A broad late-20th-century movement that is suspicious of grand stories claiming universal truth, and stresses how language, power and culture shape what counts as knowledge.","more":"Jean-François Lyotard's The Postmodern Condition (1979) described it as distrust of the big narratives of progress and reason. Thinkers such as Jacques Derrida and Michel Foucault are often grouped with it, though many rejected the label. Critics charge it with relativism and obscure writing; defenders say it exposes hidden assumptions.","etym":null,"example":"Asking whose voices are missing from a history textbook, and why, is a typically postmodern question.","notConfused":"Not the same as postmodern architecture or art, though they share a playful attitude to old rules.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Relativism","Perspectivism","Pragmatism"],"thinkers":["rorty"],"tags":["postmodern","lyotard","derrida","foucault","metanarratives","post-structuralism"]},{"term":"Pragmatism","def":"An American philosophical tradition that judges ideas by their practical consequences: what difference believing them makes in experience and action.","more":"Peirce founded it as a method for making ideas clear; William James popularised it and applied it to truth and religion; John Dewey extended it to education, democracy and ethics. Its critics say it confuses what is useful with what is true. Rorty revived it in the late 20th century.","etym":"Greek pragma, 'deed, thing done'.","example":"A pragmatist asks of a debate: if one side is right rather than the other, what would anyone ever do differently?","notConfused":"Not the same as being 'pragmatic' in the everyday sense of being practical or willing to compromise.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Fallibilism","Instrumentalism","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["peirce","james","dewey"],"tags":["pragmatist","peirce","william james","dewey","practical consequences","american pragmatism"]},{"term":"Prajna","def":"In Buddhism, wisdom: direct insight into the true nature of reality, especially impermanence, no-self and emptiness.","more":"Prajna is one of the three trainings, alongside ethics and meditation, and one of the perfections of a bodhisattva. The Perfection of Wisdom (Prajnaparamita) sutras, including the Heart Sutra, centre on it, teaching that all things are empty. It is seen as liberating knowledge, not just learning facts.","etym":"Sanskrit prajna, 'wisdom, understanding'.","example":"Knowing in theory that everything changes is learning; seeing it clearly enough that you stop clinging is prajna.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Nirvana","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna"],"tags":["prajñā","panna","paññā","wisdom","insight","perfection of wisdom"]},{"term":"Pratityasamutpada (dependent origination)","def":"The Buddhist teaching that everything arises in dependence on causes and conditions, and nothing exists entirely by itself.","more":"The Buddha used it to explain how suffering arises through a chain of twelve links, starting with ignorance, and how it can be stopped. Nagarjuna identified dependent origination with emptiness: because things depend on others, they lack any fixed nature of their own. It is central to almost all schools of Buddhism.","etym":"Sanskrit pratitya, 'depending on', plus samutpada, 'arising together'.","example":"A plant exists only through seed, soil, water and sunlight; take them away and there is no plant.","notConfused":"Not the same as simple cause and effect: it says nothing has an independent existence, not just that events have causes.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Karma","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna"],"tags":["dependent origination","paticcasamuppada","dependent arising","interdependence","conditioned arising"]},{"term":"Premise","def":"A claim used as a starting reason in an argument; the conclusion is the claim the premises are meant to support. If a premise is false, the argument can fail even if the logic is perfect.","example":"In ‘All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; so Socrates is mortal,’ the first two lines are premises.","related":["Argument","Sound"],"more":"Most philosophical arguments are laid out as numbered premises followed by a conclusion, so that readers can see exactly which claim to challenge. Critics usually attack either a premise (saying it is false) or the step from premises to conclusion (saying it does not follow). Hidden or unstated premises are a common source of bad reasoning.","etym":"Medieval Latin praemissa, 'things sent before'.","notConfused":"Not the same as an assumption in the everyday sense: a premise is stated openly so it can be tested, while an assumption is often unspoken.","field":"Logic and argument","thinkers":[],"tags":["premiss","assumption","reason","grounds","argument"]},{"term":"Presentism","def":"The view that only present things exist. The past no longer exists and the future does not exist yet.","example":"On presentism, dinosaurs do not exist at all: they did exist, but there are none.","related":["Eternalism"],"compass":"q17","more":"Presentism fits common sense and our feeling that time really passes. Its rival, eternalism, says past, present and future are all equally real, which many think fits Einstein's relativity better, since relativity suggests there is no single universal 'now'. Presentists also face the 'truthmaker' problem: what makes claims about the past true if the past does not exist?","etym":"Latin praesens, 'present'.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","thinkers":[],"tags":["only the present exists","a-theory of time","time","eternalism"]},{"term":"Pre-Socratics","def":"The early Greek thinkers before or around Socrates, from about 600 to 400 BCE, who first tried to explain nature by reason rather than myth.","more":"They include Thales, Heraclitus, Parmenides, Pythagoras, Zeno and Democritus. Most of their writings survive only in fragments quoted by later authors. They asked what the world is made of, whether change is real, and how reason relates to the senses: questions that shaped all later Western philosophy.","etym":null,"example":"Thales' suggestion that everything is made of water was wrong, but it was a new kind of answer.","notConfused":"Not a school with shared views: 'Pre-Socratics' is a modern label for very different thinkers.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Logos","Monism","Metaphysics"],"thinkers":["thales","heraclitus","parmenides"],"tags":["presocratics","presocratic","thales","heraclitus","parmenides","early greek philosophy"]},{"term":"Primary and secondary qualities","def":"Primary qualities, such as shape, size and motion, belong to objects themselves; secondary qualities, such as colour, taste and smell, exist only as effects objects produce in perceivers.","more":"Galileo, Boyle and Locke drew the distinction to fit the new mechanical science. Locke held that our ideas of primary qualities resemble the qualities in objects, while our ideas of secondary qualities do not. Berkeley argued the distinction collapses, since we cannot imagine shape without colour, so both are only in the mind.","etym":null,"example":"The same bowl of water feels warm to a cold hand and cool to a hot hand, but its shape does not change.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Perception","Empiricism","Idealism"],"thinkers":["locke","berkeley"],"tags":["primary qualities","secondary qualities","locke","colour","color","shape"]},{"term":"Principle of charity","def":"The rule that you should interpret what others say in its most reasonable and strongest form before criticising it.","more":"In argument it guards against attacking a straw man. Philosophers of language such as Quine and Donald Davidson used a version as a condition of understanding anyone at all: to translate a language we must assume its speakers mostly believe true things. It is now a standard rule of good philosophical method.","etym":null,"example":"If a friend says 'everyone is going', you take them to mean most of your friends, not every human on Earth.","notConfused":"Not the same as agreeing: charity is about interpreting fairly, not about accepting the view.","field":"Method","related":["Straw man","Argument","Dialectic"],"thinkers":["quine"],"tags":["charitable interpretation","interpretive charity","steelman","davidson","best interpretation"]},{"term":"Principle of sufficient reason","def":"The principle that everything has a reason or explanation for why it is so and not otherwise.","more":"Leibniz made it one of the two great principles of reasoning, alongside non-contradiction, and used it to argue for God as the reason for the world. Spinoza also relied on it heavily. Critics argue that it leads to the conclusion that nothing could have been different, or that quantum events show some things happen without reason.","etym":null,"example":"If you find a glowing ball in the woods, you assume there is some explanation for it being there.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Cosmological argument","Causation","Necessary/contingent"],"thinkers":["leibniz","spinoza"],"tags":["psr","leibniz","sufficient reason","everything has a reason","explanation"]},{"term":"Prisoner's dilemma","def":"A situation where two people each do better for themselves by not cooperating, yet both end up worse off than if they had both cooperated.","more":"The standard story has two suspects who each get a lighter sentence for betraying the other. It is a central model in game theory, developed around 1950, and is used to explain arms races, pollution and the need for trust and institutions. Many link it to Hobbes's argument that we need a sovereign to escape the state of nature.","etym":null,"example":"Two neighbouring cafés would both profit from keeping prices steady, but each is tempted to cut prices and both end up earning less.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Social contract","State of nature","Egoism"],"thinkers":["hobbes"],"tags":["prisoners dilemma","game theory","cooperation","defection","tit for tat"]},{"term":"Private language argument","def":"Wittgenstein's argument that there cannot be a language whose words refer to private sensations that only the speaker could ever understand.","more":"In Philosophical Investigations (1953) he imagines someone writing 'S' in a diary whenever a certain sensation occurs. With no public check, there is no difference between using 'S' correctly and merely thinking you do, so 'S' has no meaning. Scholars still disagree about exactly what the argument shows.","etym":null,"example":"Words like 'pain' are learned and checked through shared behaviour, such as wincing and crying, not by pointing inwards.","notConfused":"Not the same as a secret code: a code could in principle be taught to someone else; a private language could not.","field":"Language","related":["Language game","Other minds","Behaviorism"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"tags":["private language","wittgenstein","beetle in a box","rule-following","philosophical investigations"]},{"term":"Problem of evil","def":"The challenge that a God who is all-powerful, all-knowing and perfectly good would not allow the evil and suffering we see.","example":"Why would such a God allow a child to die of a painful disease?","related":["Theodicy","Free will defense"],"thinkers":["hume","augustine","mackie"],"compass":"q19","more":"The logical problem claims that God and evil are flatly inconsistent; the evidential problem claims that the amount of evil makes God unlikely. Replies include the free will defence and soul-making theodicies, and sceptical theism, which says we cannot expect to see God's reasons. Many philosophers regard it as the strongest argument against traditional theism.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as the question of why people do bad things: the problem is about reconciling suffering with a perfect God.","field":"Religion","tags":["evil","suffering","logical problem of evil","evidential problem of evil","theodicy","epicurus"]},{"term":"Problem of induction","def":"Hume’s puzzle: we cannot prove that the future will resemble the past without assuming it, so induction seems to have no non-circular justification.","example":"A turkey fed every morning for a year expects food on the day before Thanksgiving.","related":["Induction","Falsifiability","Causation"],"thinkers":["hume","popper","russell"],"more":"Hume pointed out that any argument for trusting induction would either be deductive (and cannot reach the future) or inductive (and so assume what it tries to prove). Popper responded that science does not need induction, only bold guesses and attempts to refute them. Others argue induction is justified pragmatically, or that being reasonable just means reasoning inductively.","etym":null,"notConfused":"Not the same as mathematical induction, which is a fully valid method of deductive proof.","field":"Knowledge","tags":["hume's problem of induction","induction","hume","inductive skepticism","uniformity of nature"]},{"term":"Property dualism","def":"The view that there is only one kind of substance, physical stuff, but it has two kinds of properties: physical ones and mental ones that cannot be reduced to them.","more":"It accepts that brains do the thinking, but denies that experience is nothing more than physical activity. Chalmers defends a version based on the hard problem and the conceivability of zombies. Its main challenge is mental causation: if mental properties are not physical, how do they make a difference?","etym":null,"example":"A property dualist says a brain is just a physical object, but the redness you experience is a non-physical feature of it.","notConfused":"Not the same as substance dualism, which says the mind is a separate thing, such as a soul.","field":"Mind","related":["Dualism","Physicalism","Epiphenomenalism","Hard problem"],"thinkers":["chalmers","jackson"],"tags":["non-reductive physicalism","dualism","chalmers","mental properties","emergence"]},{"term":"Proposition","def":"What a sentence says: the meaning that can be true or false, and can be shared by different sentences.","more":"'Snow is white' and 'La neige est blanche' express the same proposition. Philosophers treat propositions as what we believe and assert. Frege and Russell held different views about what they are made of, and some philosophers doubt that such abstract objects exist.","etym":"Latin propositio, 'a setting forth'.","example":"'Ali is taller than Ben' and 'Ben is shorter than Ali' express the same proposition.","notConfused":"Not the same as a sentence: a sentence is a string of words in a language; a proposition is its content.","field":"Language","related":["Premise","Analytic/synthetic","Language game"],"thinkers":["frege","russell"],"tags":["statement","claim","propositional content","meaning","truth-bearer"]},{"term":"Psychological continuity","def":"The view that you persist over time through overlapping chains of memory, character and intention.","example":"You are the same person as yesterday because you remember yesterday and carry on its plans.","related":["Personal identity","Animalism"],"thinkers":["locke","parfit"],"compass":"q10","more":"This view develops Locke's memory theory, which faced the objection that we forget most of our past. Modern versions only need overlapping chains of connection, not memory of everything. Puzzle cases such as teleporters and brain splitting test whether it gives the right answers.","etym":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","tags":["psychological connectedness","memory theory","locke","parfit","personal identity"]},{"term":"Pyrrhonism","def":"An ancient school of scepticism that aimed at peace of mind by suspending judgement on all disputed questions.","more":"It traces back to Pyrrho of Elis and is known mostly from Sextus Empiricus. Pyrrhonists set opposing arguments against each other until they seem equally strong, then suspend judgement (epoche); calm (ataraxia) was said to follow. They lived by appearances and custom without claiming to know how things really are.","etym":"Named after Pyrrho of Elis (c. 365–275 BCE).","example":"Asked whether honey is really sweet, a Pyrrhonist says it appears sweet to me, and leaves it there.","notConfused":"Not the same as denying all knowledge: a Pyrrhonist does not even assert that knowledge is impossible.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Skepticism","Stoicism","Epicureanism"],"thinkers":["pyrrho","sextus"],"tags":["pyrrho","sextus empiricus","pyrrhonian skepticism","ancient skepticism","epoché"]},{"term":"Qi","def":"In Chinese thought, the vital energy or basic 'stuff' that makes up all things, from rocks to feelings. Its flow and balance shape both health and nature.","more":"Qi appears across Confucian, Daoist and medical traditions. Mencius spoke of cultivating a 'flood-like qi' by living rightly. The Neo-Confucian Zhu Xi paired qi, the material force of things, with li 理, the pattern or principle that orders it, so that everything is a mix of stuff and structure. Scholars still debate whether qi is best read as matter, energy, or something that fits neither Western category.","etym":"Chinese 氣 qì, originally 'vapour' or 'breath'.","example":"Traditional Chinese medicine describes illness as qi that is blocked or out of balance.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Yin and yang","Dao","Xin"],"thinkers":["zhuxi","mencius"],"tags":["chi","ch'i","ki","vital energy","life force","breath"]},{"term":"Qualia","def":"The felt qualities of experience: what it is like to taste coffee or see red.","more":"Physicalists face the challenge of explaining qualia in terms of brain processes. Frank Jackson imagined Mary, a scientist who knows every physical fact about colour but has never seen red, and who seems to learn something new when she finally does. Daniel Dennett and others argue that the concept is confused and that there are no qualia in the sense intended.","etym":"Latin, plural of quale, 'of what kind'.","example":"You can describe the wavelength of red light, but the redness you see is a quale.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Hard problem","Functionalism","Zombie"],"thinkers":["jackson","nagel"],"compass":"q6","tags":["quale","raw feels","what it is like","subjective experience","phenomenal properties"]},{"term":"Rationalism","def":"The view that reason alone can give us important knowledge about the world, not just experience.","more":"The classic rationalists were Descartes, Spinoza and Leibniz in the seventeenth century, usually contrasted with the British empiricists Locke, Berkeley and Hume. Rationalists typically believed in innate ideas and took mathematics as a model of how reason can reveal necessary truths. Kant tried to combine the two camps, arguing that knowledge needs both concepts supplied by the mind and experience.","etym":"Latin ratio, 'reason, reckoning'.","example":"A rationalist says you can know every event has a cause just by thinking hard about it.","notConfused":"Not the same as being rational: an empiricist can be perfectly rational while denying that reason alone gives knowledge of the world.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","A priori"],"thinkers":["descartes","spinoza","leibniz"],"compass":"q13","tags":["rationalist","descartes","spinoza","leibniz","reason","innate ideas"]},{"term":"Realism","def":"The view that something exists, or is a certain way, independently of what anyone thinks, says or perceives about it.","more":"Realism is always realism about something: the external world, numbers, universals, moral facts or the unseen things science describes. A realist about X says X is there whether or not anyone knows it; an anti-realist says X depends in some way on minds, language or human practices. A person can be a realist about one thing, such as tables, and an anti-realist about another, such as morality.","etym":"Late Latin realis, 'actual', from res, 'thing'.","example":"A realist about the Moon thinks it was there before anyone looked, and would still be there if every mind vanished.","notConfused":"Not the same as being 'realistic' in everyday speech: philosophical realism is a claim about what exists, not about having modest expectations.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Anti-realism","Scientific realism","Moral realism","Idealism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["realist","mind-independence","external world","objective reality","anti-realism"]},{"term":"Reason","def":"The ability to think things through, draw conclusions and weigh evidence. Philosophers often contrast it with the senses, feelings, faith or tradition.","more":"Many philosophers have treated reason as what makes humans distinctive: Aristotle is traditionally credited with defining the human being as the rational animal. Rationalists such as Descartes trusted reason to reveal deep truths, while Hume argued that reason alone cannot move us to act and is 'the slave of the passions'. Kant's Critique of Pure Reason (1781) set out to map what reason can and cannot know.","etym":"Latin ratio, 'reckoning, calculation', via Old French raison.","example":"Working out that your keys must be in the car, because you had them when you drove home and have not been out since.","notConfused":"Not the same as rationalism: everyone uses reason, but rationalism is the stronger claim that reason alone can give us knowledge of the world.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Rationalism","Empiricism","Logic","Argument"],"thinkers":["aristotle","hume","kant"],"tags":["rationality","reasoning","logos","intellect","rational thought"]},{"term":"Red herring","def":"A fallacy in which an irrelevant topic is brought in to distract from the real question.","more":"A red herring does not answer the argument; it changes the subject, often to something more emotional or easier to discuss. It is a fallacy of relevance: the new point may even be true, but it does not bear on the claim at issue. To spot one, ask: even if that is right, does it settle what we were arguing about?","etym":"From the strong-smelling smoked fish; the idiom is traditionally linked to laying a false trail for hounds, though its exact origin is uncertain.","example":"Asked why her homework is late, a student replies: 'But think how much homework other schools set!'","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Straw man","Tu quoque"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["distraction","irrelevant point","diversion","changing the subject","ignoratio elenchi"]},{"term":"Reductio ad absurdum","def":"Showing a claim is false by showing that it leads to something absurd or contradictory.","more":"It is one of the oldest tools in philosophy and mathematics. Zeno of Elea used it to defend Parmenides, Socrates used it to refute definitions, and the classic proof that the square root of 2 is not a fraction assumes that it is and derives a contradiction. Its force depends on the result really being unacceptable: sometimes philosophers 'bite the bullet' and accept the strange conclusion instead.","etym":"Latin reductio ad absurdum, 'reduction to the absurd'.","example":"‘If everyone who ever lied were a liar forever, then every adult alive would be a liar, which is absurd.’","notConfused":"Not the same as reductionism: a reductio is a style of argument, while reductionism is a view about how levels of explanation relate.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Argument","Thought experiment"],"thinkers":["zeno_elea","socrates"],"tags":["reductio","proof by contradiction","reduction to absurdity","indirect proof"]},{"term":"Reductionism","def":"The view that something complex can be fully explained in terms of simpler parts or a more basic level, as when biology is explained by chemistry.","more":"Reductionists picture the sciences as a ladder, with psychology reducible to biology, biology to chemistry and chemistry to physics. Critics argue that some higher-level features cannot be reduced, for example because the same mental state, such as pain, might occur in very different physical systems. The word is sometimes used as an insult meaning 'oversimplifying', but in philosophy it names a serious and widely defended position.","etym":"Latin reducere, 'to lead back'.","example":"Saying that love is 'nothing but' certain chemicals in the brain is a reductionist claim.","notConfused":"Not the same as reductio ad absurdum: that is a style of argument, while reductionism is a view about how levels of explanation relate.","field":"Science","related":["Physicalism","Identity theory","Functionalism","Supervenience"],"thinkers":["pchurchland","churchland"],"tags":["reduction","reductive","reducing to parts","physicalism","emergence"]},{"term":"Reflective equilibrium","def":"A method of adjusting general principles and particular judgments against each other until they fit together.","more":"John Rawls named the method in A Theory of Justice (1971), building on Nelson Goodman's account of how we justify rules of reasoning. Neither general principles nor judgments about cases get the final say: each can be revised in light of the other. Critics worry it may only tidy up our existing prejudices; supporters reply that 'wide' reflective equilibrium also tests our views against background theories and other people's views.","etym":"Latin aequilibrium, 'equal balance'.","example":"You believe ‘never lie’ but also that lying to a murderer at the door is fine, so you revise your rule.","notConfused":null,"field":"Method","related":["Intuition","Thought experiment"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"tags":["rawls","goodman","coherence","considered judgements","considered judgments"]},{"term":"Relativism","def":"The view that truth or rightness depends on a culture or individual, so there is no single standard for everyone.","more":"Moral relativism faces strong objections: it seems to rule out moral progress, such as the abolition of slavery, and to stop us criticising practices in other cultures. Plato argued in the Theaetetus that Protagoras' relativism refutes itself. More modest forms say only that some kinds of judgment, such as those of etiquette or taste, are relative.","etym":"Latin relativus, 'having reference to something'.","example":"‘Eating with your left hand is rude’ may be true in one culture and false in another.","notConfused":"Not the same as tolerance: one can hold that tolerance is objectively right, which a thorough relativist cannot.","field":"Ethics","related":["Anti-realism","Moral realism"],"thinkers":[],"compass":"q4","tags":["moral relativism","cultural relativism","relativist","subjectivism","protagoras"]},{"term":"Religious experience","def":"An experience that seems, to the person having it, to be an awareness of God or of some ultimate reality.","more":"William James studied such experiences in The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902), noting features such as being hard to put into words and seeming to give insight. William Alston argued that they can justify belief in God much as sense perception justifies belief in the physical world, and Richard Swinburne proposed a 'principle of credulity': trust how things seem unless there is reason not to. Critics point to conflicting experiences across religions and to explanations from psychology and neuroscience.","etym":"Latin religio, 'reverence, obligation', and experientia, 'trial, experience'.","example":"Walking alone in the mountains, a person suddenly feels, overwhelmingly, that they are in the presence of something holy.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Revelation","Theism","Sufism"],"thinkers":["james","alston","swinburne"],"tags":["mystical experience","mysticism","encounter with god","william james","numinous"]},{"term":"Ren","def":"Confucian humaneness or benevolence: the central virtue of caring for others, rooted in family and extending outward.","more":"Ren is the highest virtue in the Analects, though Confucius rarely defines it and gives different answers to different students. It is first learned in the family through filial piety (xiao) and is expressed through ritual (li). Mencius argued that every person has the 'sprout' of ren in their natural feeling for others' suffering.","etym":"Chinese 仁 rén; the character is traditionally explained as combining 'person' and 'two'.","example":"Truly caring for your elderly grandparents, not just visiting out of duty.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Li (ritual propriety)","Virtue","Dao"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius"],"tags":["jen","humaneness","benevolence","goodness","confucius","humanity"]},{"term":"Repugnant conclusion","def":"Parfit’s name for the result that, on some views, a huge population of lives barely worth living could be better than a smaller population of very happy people.","more":"Derek Parfit set out the problem in Reasons and Persons (1984). If we judge outcomes by total wellbeing, adding enough lives that are barely worth living can outweigh any loss in quality of life. Parfit searched for a theory that avoids the conclusion and admitted he had not found one; some philosophers now argue we should accept it. The debate is central to population ethics, which asks how to value future people.","etym":null,"example":"Ten billion flourishing people versus a vast crowd whose lives are only just worth living.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Utility monster"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"tags":["parfit","population ethics","mere addition paradox","total utilitarianism"]},{"term":"Retributivism","def":"The view that punishment is justified because wrongdoers deserve it, not only because it prevents future crime.","more":"Retributivists hold that guilt is necessary for punishment and that the punishment should fit the crime. Kant argued that even a society about to disband should first execute its last convicted murderer, so that justice is done. Consequentialists see retribution as revenge in disguise and justify punishment instead by deterrence, protection of the public or reform of the offender.","etym":"Latin retribuere, 'to pay back'.","example":"A retributivist would punish a murderer even if it deterred no one.","notConfused":"Not the same as revenge: retribution is meant to be proportionate and imposed by a legitimate authority, not driven by personal anger.","field":"Ethics","related":["Desert","Moral responsibility"],"thinkers":["kant"],"compass":"q9","tags":["retribution","retributive justice","just deserts","punishment","eye for an eye","lex talionis"]},{"term":"Revelation","def":"Knowledge believed to be given directly by God, for example through scripture, prophets or visions, rather than discovered by human reason.","more":"Many religious thinkers distinguish natural theology, what reason can show about God, from revealed theology, what can be known only because God disclosed it. Aquinas held that reason can prove God exists, but that doctrines such as the Trinity are known only by revelation, and that faith and reason can never truly conflict. Sceptics ask how anyone can tell a genuine revelation from a mistaken or invented one, especially when religions report conflicting revelations.","etym":"Latin revelare, 'to unveil'.","example":"Believing the Ten Commandments are binding because they were given to Moses by God, not because you reasoned your way to them.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Religious experience","Theism","Sunna"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"tags":["divine revelation","scripture","revealed religion","prophecy","word of god"]},{"term":"Rhetoric","def":"The art of persuasion: using language to move an audience through argument, character and emotion.","more":"Aristotle's Rhetoric identified three means of persuasion: ethos (the speaker's credibility), pathos (the audience's emotions) and logos (the argument itself). Plato was suspicious of rhetoric, worrying in the Gorgias that it produces belief without knowledge. Today 'mere rhetoric' is an insult, but every argument has to be presented somehow, and good presentation can serve the truth as well as hide it.","etym":"Greek rhetorike (techne), 'the art of the public speaker'.","example":"A charity advert that shows one child's face instead of a table of statistics is using rhetoric, specifically pathos.","notConfused":"Not the same as logic: logic asks whether a conclusion follows from the premises, rhetoric asks whether an audience will be persuaded.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Sophists","Logic","Fallacy"],"thinkers":["aristotle","plato"],"tags":["persuasion","oratory","aristotle","ethos pathos logos","sophists"]},{"term":"Rights","def":"Entitlements a person has to act, or to be treated, in certain ways, which others are obliged to respect.","more":"Philosophers distinguish negative rights, to be left alone (such as the right not to be assaulted), from positive rights, to be provided with something (such as education). They also debate where rights come from: natural rights theorists like Locke think we have them simply as human beings, while Bentham dismissed natural rights as 'nonsense upon stilts' and saw rights as created by law. A further debate asks whether rights protect our choices or our important interests.","etym":"Old English riht, 'just, correct'.","example":"Your right to free speech means the government may not jail you for criticising it.","notConfused":"Not the same as what is right: having a right to do something, such as being rude, does not make doing it morally right.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Natural rights","Liberty (negative/positive)","Justice","Self-ownership"],"thinkers":["locke","bentham","nozick"],"tags":["human rights","natural rights","entitlements","claims","individual rights"]},{"term":"Rigid designator","def":"A word that picks out the same thing in every possible world in which that thing exists. Saul Kripke argued that proper names work this way.","more":"Kripke introduced the idea in lectures given in 1970 and published as Naming and Necessity (1980). 'Aristotle' refers to the same man in every possible situation, whereas 'the teacher of Alexander the Great' could have picked out someone else. This led Kripke to argue that some truths, such as 'water is H2O', are necessary even though they were discovered by experience.","etym":"Latin designare, 'to mark out'.","example":"'Barack Obama' would still name the same man in a world where he never entered politics, but 'the 44th US President' might name someone else.","notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Possible worlds","Necessary/contingent","Sense and reference","A posteriori"],"thinkers":["kripke"],"tags":["rigid designation","kripke","naming and necessity","proper names","possible worlds"]},{"term":"Rule of law","def":"The principle that everyone, including rulers, is subject to clear, public laws applied fairly, rather than to the will of whoever holds power.","more":"The idea goes back at least to Aristotle, who argued that it is better for the law to rule than any single citizen. Modern accounts list features such as laws being general, public, not retroactive, stable, and applied by independent courts. Joseph Raz argued that the rule of law is one virtue of a legal system but not the only one, so a system could respect it and still be unjust; others defend a 'thicker' idea that includes basic rights.","etym":null,"example":"A prime minister caught speeding gets the same fine as anyone else.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Legitimacy","Separation of powers","Justice","Democracy"],"thinkers":["aristotle","locke","raz"],"tags":["legality","law","constitutionalism","equality before the law","nobody above the law"]},{"term":"Rule utilitarianism","def":"The view that we should follow the rules whose general acceptance would produce the most wellbeing, rather than judging each act by its own consequences.","more":"It is contrasted with act utilitarianism, which judges each act directly. The rule version aims to avoid shocking verdicts, such as framing an innocent person to stop a riot, because a rule allowing that would do more harm than good. J. J. C. Smart objected that keeping to a rule when breaking it would clearly do more good is a kind of rule worship; others argue it collapses into act utilitarianism once rules gain enough exceptions. Brad Hooker defends a closely related rule consequentialism.","etym":null,"example":"Keeping a promise to meet a friend even when you could do slightly more good elsewhere, because a world where promises are kept goes better.","notConfused":"Not the same as deontology: rule utilitarians follow rules only because of their good consequences, while deontologists think some rules bind us regardless of consequences.","field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Consequentialism","Deontology"],"thinkers":["hooker","smart"],"tags":["rule consequentialism","utilitarianism","act utilitarianism","general rules","mill"]},{"term":"Samsara","def":"In Indian religions, the endless cycle of birth, death and rebirth, driven by karma, from which liberation is sought.","more":"Hindu, Buddhist and Jain traditions share the idea but explain it differently. In much Hindu thought an eternal self (atman) is reborn; Buddhists, who deny a fixed self, describe rebirth as a continuing causal stream rather than a soul moving between bodies. The goal in each tradition is release from the cycle: moksha in Hinduism and Jainism, nirvana in Buddhism.","etym":"Sanskrit saṃsāra, 'wandering' or 'flowing on'.","example":"A Buddhist might see a life spent chasing money and status as one more turn of the wheel of samsara.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Karma","Dukkha","Atman","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["saṃsāra","cycle of rebirth","reincarnation","wheel of life","rebirth","karma"]},{"term":"Satori","def":"In Zen Buddhism, a sudden experience of awakening, or insight into one's true nature.","more":"Satori is a Japanese term, often used alongside kenshō, 'seeing one's nature'. Zen teachers use meditation and puzzling questions called koans to break habitual thinking and open the way to it. Schools differ: the Rinzai school stresses sudden breakthroughs through koans, while Dōgen's Sōtō school teaches that sitting practice is itself enlightenment, not a means to some later flash.","etym":"Japanese 悟り satori, from satoru, 'to understand'.","example":"A Zen student who has struggled for months with the koan about the sound of one hand clapping suddenly sees the point, and the world looks different.","notConfused":"Not the same as nirvana: satori is a moment of awakening that can deepen over time, while nirvana is the final ending of craving and rebirth.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Zen","Zazen","Emptiness (śūnyatā)"],"thinkers":["dogen"],"tags":["kensho","enlightenment","awakening","sudden enlightenment","zen"]},{"term":"Scholasticism","def":"The method of philosophy and theology taught in medieval European universities, which used careful logic to reconcile Christian faith with ancient philosophy, especially Aristotle.","more":"Scholastics worked by posing a question, listing arguments on each side, giving an answer and then replying to every objection, the format of Aquinas's Summa Theologiae. Leading figures include Anselm, Aquinas, Duns Scotus and Ockham. Renaissance humanists and early modern thinkers such as Hobbes often mocked it as hair-splitting, but historians now credit it with high standards of precision and argument.","etym":"Latin scholasticus, 'of a school'.","example":"A medieval debate on whether God's knowledge of the future means our choices are not free, settled through a numbered list of objections and replies.","notConfused":null,"field":"History of philosophy","related":["Universals","Nominalism","Cosmological argument"],"thinkers":["aquinas","anselm","ockham"],"tags":["scholastic","schoolmen","aquinas","medieval philosophy","duns scotus","ockham"]},{"term":"Scientific realism","def":"The view that our best scientific theories are approximately true, including what they say about unseen things like electrons.","more":"The main argument for it, put forward by Hilary Putnam, is the 'no miracles' argument: the success of science would be a miracle if its theories were not at least roughly true. Opponents point to past theories, such as the ether theory of light, that were successful but turned out false. Bas van Fraassen's constructive empiricism holds that science need only aim at theories that fit what can be observed.","etym":"Latin realis, 'actual', from res, 'thing'.","example":"A realist thinks electrons really exist, not just that ‘electron’ talk makes good predictions.","notConfused":"Not the same as realism in general: one can be a realist about tables and chairs but not about electrons.","field":"Science","related":["Instrumentalism","Paradigm"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"compass":"q11","tags":["realism about science","unobservables","no-miracles argument","truth of theories","instrumentalism"]},{"term":"Secularism","def":"The view that government and public institutions should be separate from religion, neither enforcing nor favouring any faith.","more":"Secularism comes in stronger and weaker forms. The French model, laïcité, keeps religion largely out of public institutions, while other models simply require the state to treat all religions, and none, even-handedly. Defenders link it to freedom of conscience and toleration; critics argue that strict secularism can itself disadvantage religious citizens.","etym":"Latin saecularis, 'worldly', from saeculum, 'age, generation'.","example":"A state school that teaches pupils about several religions but leads no prayers.","notConfused":"Not the same as atheism: secularism is a view about the state, and many religious believers are secularists.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Toleration","Liberty (negative/positive)","Democracy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["secular","separation of church and state","laïcité","laicite","religious neutrality"]},{"term":"Self-ownership","def":"The idea that each person owns themselves, including their body and their labour, and so has strong rights over how these are used.","more":"Locke argued that every man has 'a property in his own person', and that by mixing our labour with unowned things we can come to own them. Robert Nozick built on this to argue that taxing earnings for redistribution is comparable to forced labour. G. A. Cohen took the idea seriously, argued it does not support the inequality libertarians think it does, and eventually rejected it.","etym":null,"example":"The belief that no one may force you to donate a kidney, even to save a life, draws on self-ownership.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Rights","Natural rights","Liberty (negative/positive)"],"thinkers":["locke","nozick","ga_cohen"],"tags":["ownership of oneself","nozick","locke","libertarianism","bodily autonomy"]},{"term":"Self-refutation","def":"A claim is self-refuting when it undermines itself: if it were true, it would have to be false, or could not be rationally believed.","more":"The classic charge, made in Plato's Theaetetus, is against Protagoras: if every belief is true for the person who holds it, then the belief that Protagoras is wrong is true as well. Similar objections are raised against the verification principle, which is neither true by definition nor checkable by experience, and against claims such as 'there is no truth'. Defenders usually reply by narrowing the claim so that it no longer applies to itself.","etym":null,"example":"'Never trust a generalisation' is itself a generalisation.","notConfused":"Not the same as two people contradicting each other: self-refutation happens within a single claim.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Relativism","Verification principle","Reductio ad absurdum"],"thinkers":["plato","protagoras"],"tags":["self-refuting","self-defeating","performative contradiction","self-undermining"]},{"term":"Sense and reference","def":"Gottlob Frege's distinction between what a word refers to (the object itself) and its sense (the way it presents that object).","more":"In an essay of 1892, Frege noticed that 'the Morning Star is the Evening Star' tells us something new, while 'the Morning Star is the Morning Star' does not, even though both names refer to the planet Venus. His explanation was that the two names share a reference but differ in sense. The distinction shaped modern philosophy of language, though Kripke and others later argued that proper names refer directly, without a sense to fix their target.","etym":"German Sinn ('sense') and Bedeutung ('meaning', usually translated 'reference').","example":"'Superman' and 'Clark Kent' refer to the same person, yet Lois Lane can believe Superman flies without believing Clark Kent flies.","notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Rigid designator","Language game","Analytic/synthetic"],"thinkers":["frege","kripke"],"tags":["sinn und bedeutung","sinn and bedeutung","frege","meaning","morning star evening star"]},{"term":"Sense data","def":"The immediate objects of perception, such as patches of colour or sounds, which some philosophers held we are directly aware of instead of physical objects themselves.","more":"Russell and Moore used the term in the early twentieth century. The argument from illusion supported it: when a straight stick looks bent in water, you are aware of something bent, which is not the stick, so it must be a sense datum. Critics such as J. L. Austin argued that the theory misdescribes ordinary perception, and many philosophers now prefer direct realism or other theories of perception.","etym":"Latin sensus, 'perception', and data, 'things given'. Singular: sense datum.","example":"Looking at a round coin from an angle, you see an oval shape: a sense-data theorist says the oval is a sense datum.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","Skepticism","Idealism","Qualia"],"thinkers":["russell","moore","ayer"],"tags":["sense-data","sense datum","sensa","appearances","russell","indirect realism"]},{"term":"Sentience","def":"The capacity to have experiences, especially to feel pleasure and pain.","more":"Many ethicists treat sentience as the key to moral status: if a being can suffer, its suffering counts. Bentham argued that what matters about animals is not whether they can reason or talk, but whether they can suffer, and Peter Singer built on this in arguing against speciesism. Hard cases include fish, insects and possible future AI, where it is uncertain whether anything is felt at all.","etym":"Latin sentire, 'to feel'.","example":"Debates about whether lobsters should be stunned before cooking turn on whether lobsters are sentient.","notConfused":"Not the same as intelligence: a being could feel pain without being able to reason well.","field":"Mind","related":["Speciesism","Qualia","Hard problem","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["bentham","singer"],"tags":["sentient","capacity to feel","feeling","consciousness","suffering","animal minds"]},{"term":"Separation of powers","def":"Dividing government into separate branches, usually lawmaking, executive and judicial, so that no single person or body holds all power.","more":"Locke distinguished legislative from executive power, and Montesquieu's The Spirit of the Laws (1748) gave the classic three-way version, which influenced the United States Constitution. The aim is that each branch checks the others, protecting liberty. Countries apply it differently: in the UK, government ministers sit in Parliament, while the US separates the branches more sharply.","etym":null,"example":"A court striking down a law passed by the legislature because it breaks the constitution.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Rule of law","Tyranny of the majority","Sovereignty"],"thinkers":["locke"],"tags":["checks and balances","montesquieu","trias politica","branches of government"]},{"term":"Ship of Theseus","def":"The puzzle of whether a ship whose parts are all gradually replaced is still the same ship.","more":"Plutarch reports that the Athenians preserved Theseus' ship by replacing its rotten planks, and that philosophers argued over whether it stayed the same ship. Hobbes added a twist: if someone kept the old planks and rebuilt the original, which of the two would be the Ship of Theseus? The puzzle tests theories of identity over time and applies to people too, whose cells are constantly replaced.","etym":"Named after Theseus, the mythical hero-king of Athens.","example":"If every part of your bike is replaced over ten years, do you still own the same bike?","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Personal identity","Vagueness"],"thinkers":["hobbes"],"tags":["theseus' ship","theseus paradox","grandfather's axe","identity over time","persistence"]},{"term":"Simulation hypothesis","def":"The idea that our whole world might be a computer simulation run by a more advanced civilisation.","more":"Nick Bostrom's 2003 simulation argument claims that at least one of three things is probably true: almost no civilisations reach the technology to run such simulations, almost none that could are interested in doing so, or we are very probably living in one. It updates older sceptical scenarios such as Descartes' evil demon and the brain in a vat. Critics question its assumptions, for example that consciousness can be simulated at all.","etym":null,"example":"A video-game character who starts to wonder whether the walls of their world are just code.","notConfused":"Not the same as solipsism: in a simulation, other people might be just as real as you.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Skepticism","Solipsism","Thought experiment"],"thinkers":["bostrom","descartes"],"tags":["simulation argument","bostrom","matrix","simulated reality","brain in a vat"]},{"term":"Skandhas","def":"In Buddhism, the five 'aggregates' or heaps that make up a person: form (the body), feeling, perception, mental formations and consciousness.","more":"The Buddha used the list to show that when we look for a self, we find only these changing processes, none of which is a permanent owner of the rest. This supports the teaching of anatta, or no-self. Later Buddhist schools, such as the Abhidharma tradition summarised by Vasubandhu, analysed the aggregates in fine detail.","etym":"Sanskrit skandha, 'heap, aggregate' (Pali khandha).","example":"Just as a chariot is nothing over and above its wheels, axle and frame, Buddhists say a person is nothing over and above the five aggregates.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Anatta (no-self)","Dukkha","Personal identity"],"thinkers":["buddha","vasubandhu"],"tags":["khandhas","five aggregates","aggregates","heaps","anatta"]},{"term":"Skepticism","def":"Doubt about whether we can know things: sometimes about one area, sometimes about everything.","more":"Ancient Pyrrhonian sceptics suspended judgment on every question in search of peace of mind, while Academic sceptics argued that nothing can be known for certain. Descartes used radical doubt, imagining an evil demon deceiving him, as a method for finding what is certain. Replies include G. E. Moore's appeal to common-sense certainties, such as knowing that he had hands, and contextualism, which says the standards for 'knowing' shift with the conversation.","etym":"Greek skeptikos, 'inquiring', from skepsis, 'inquiry'. British spelling: scepticism.","example":"‘How do you know you are not dreaming right now?’ is a skeptical challenge.","notConfused":"Not the same as cynicism in the everyday sense: a sceptic doubts claims to knowledge, not people's motives.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Fallibilism","Justification","Epistemology"],"thinkers":["pyrrho","sextus","descartes","hume"],"compass":"q14","tags":["scepticism","sceptic","skeptic","doubt","pyrrhonism","external world"]},{"term":"Slippery slope","def":"Claiming that one small step will inevitably lead to a chain of terrible results, without showing why each step must follow.","more":"Not every slippery slope argument is a fallacy. If there is good evidence that each step really does make the next one likely, the warning can be reasonable. The fallacy lies in assuming the chain without support. Philosophers discuss serious versions in debates about euthanasia and drug laws, where people disagree about whether a line can be held.","etym":"English metaphor: once you step onto a slippery slope, you cannot stop sliding.","example":"‘If we let students use phones at lunch, soon they will be on them all day and nobody will learn to read.’","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Sorites paradox"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["slippery slope argument","thin end of the wedge","domino effect","camel's nose"]},{"term":"Social construction","def":"The idea that some things exist, or have the features they do, because of human beliefs, practices and agreements rather than nature alone.","more":"Money and national borders are clear cases: they would not exist without shared human practices. The debate becomes heated over categories such as gender, race or mental illness, where some argue they are partly or wholly socially constructed and others stress natural differences. Ian Hacking's The Social Construction of What? (1999) urged that we always ask exactly what is said to be constructed, and noted that being constructed does not make something unreal.","etym":null,"example":"A £20 note is only printed paper, but it has value because people treat it as money.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'fake': a socially constructed thing, such as a marriage or a law, can be fully real and have real effects.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Relativism","Realism","Standpoint theory"],"thinkers":["hacking"],"tags":["social constructionism","socially constructed","constructivism","social constructivism","gender"]},{"term":"Social contract","def":"The idea that political authority comes from an agreement, real or imagined, among the people it governs.","more":"Hobbes argued in Leviathan (1651) that people would agree to an all-powerful ruler to escape a war of all against all. Locke (1689) held that government is limited by natural rights and may be resisted if it violates them, and Rousseau's The Social Contract (1762) located authority in the general will of the people. Hume objected that no such agreement ever took place, and later critics such as Carole Pateman and Charles Mills argued that the classic contracts left out women and non-white people.","etym":"Latin contractus, 'drawn together', hence 'agreement'.","example":"Accepting traffic laws because everyone is better off if we all agree to follow them.","notConfused":"Not the same as a legal contract: most social contract theorists use the agreement as a hypothetical test of legitimacy, not as a real historical event.","field":"Politics and society","related":["State of nature","Legitimacy","Veil of ignorance"],"thinkers":["hobbes","locke","rousseau","rawls"],"compass":"q23","tags":["social contract theory","contractarianism","hobbes","locke","rousseau","consent"]},{"term":"Socratic method","def":"Teaching and inquiry by asking a series of questions that test someone's beliefs and expose contradictions, rather than by giving answers.","more":"In Plato's early dialogues, Socrates asks someone to define a concept such as courage or justice, then draws out consequences until the definition fails. This testing is called the elenchus. It often ends in aporia, a state of puzzlement, which Socrates treated as progress, since recognising your ignorance is the start of wisdom. Law schools and seminars still use a version of it.","etym":"Named after Socrates; elenchus is Greek for 'refutation' or 'test'.","example":"A teacher asks, 'Is it always wrong to lie?', then, 'What about lying to a murderer at the door?', until the student refines their view.","notConfused":null,"field":"Method","related":["Dialectic","Reductio ad absurdum","Argument"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato"],"tags":["elenchus","socratic questioning","socratic dialogue","dialectic","socrates"]},{"term":"Solipsism","def":"The view that only one's own mind is sure to exist, or, in its extreme form, that only one's own mind exists at all.","more":"Solipsism arises as a worry from scepticism: all I directly know are my own experiences, so how can I prove that other minds or an outer world exist? Almost no philosopher defends it as true, but it is a useful challenge. Descartes faced it after his 'I think, therefore I am' and relied on God to escape it, while Wittgenstein argued that the solipsist cannot even state the view, since language is public.","etym":"Latin solus, 'alone', and ipse, 'self'.","example":"Wondering whether your friends really feel anything, or are just part of your own dream.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Skepticism","Idealism","Simulation hypothesis"],"thinkers":["descartes","wittgenstein"],"tags":["solipsist","only my mind exists","other minds","skepticism","egocentrism"]},{"term":"Sophists","def":"Paid teachers of rhetoric and practical skills in ancient Greece, especially in Athens in the fifth century BCE, who taught young men how to argue and succeed in public life.","more":"Leading Sophists included Protagoras and Gorgias. Many were sceptical about absolute truth and stressed that customs differ from city to city. Plato portrayed them as caring about winning rather than truth, which gave us the insult 'sophistry'. Modern historians take them more seriously, as thinkers about language, politics and relativism.","etym":"Greek sophistes, 'expert, wise man', from sophos, 'wise'.","example":"A modern equivalent might be a debate coach who, for a fee, teaches students to argue either side of any question.","notConfused":"Not the same as Socrates: Socrates took no fees and claimed to know nothing, though some Athenians lumped him in with the Sophists.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Rhetoric","Relativism","Socratic method"],"thinkers":["protagoras","plato"],"tags":["sophist","sophistry","protagoras","gorgias","rhetoric","relativism"]},{"term":"Sorites paradox","def":"The ‘heap’ puzzle: one grain of sand is not a heap, adding one grain never turns a non-heap into a heap, yet a million grains are a heap.","more":"The puzzle is traditionally credited to Eubulides of Miletus in the fourth century BCE. Each premise seems true and the reasoning seems valid, yet the conclusion seems false, so something has to give. Epistemicists say there is a sharp cut-off that we cannot know; supervaluationists and degree theorists change the logic; others conclude that vague words are not fully coherent.","etym":"Greek soros, 'heap'.","example":"When exactly does a person become ‘tall’ as they grow, millimetre by millimetre?","notConfused":"Not the same as the slippery slope fallacy: the sorites is a genuine logical puzzle, not a careless prediction about consequences.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Vagueness","Epistemicism","Supervaluationism"],"thinkers":[],"compass":"q16","tags":["paradox of the heap","heap paradox","sorites","bald man paradox","vagueness"]},{"term":"Soul","def":"The non-physical part of a person that many traditions regard as the true self and the source of thought and life, which may survive death.","more":"Plato argued that the soul is immortal and divided into reason, spirit and appetite. Aristotle used psyche more broadly for the life-principle of any living thing, so that plants have souls too, and it is unclear whether he thought any of it survives death. Descartes identified the soul with the thinking mind, a substance separate from the body. Many modern philosophers are physicalists who doubt there is a soul, while others, such as Richard Swinburne, defend it.","etym":"Old English sawol; it translates Greek psyche and Latin anima.","example":"Wondering whether you would still be you if your memories were moved into a new body is partly a question about the soul.","notConfused":"Not the same as the mind in modern philosophy: you can believe in minds without thinking they are non-physical souls.","field":"Mind","related":["Dualism","Personal identity","Atman","Physicalism"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle","descartes"],"tags":["psyche","spirit","anima","immortal soul","atman","mind"]},{"term":"Sound","def":"An argument is sound when it is valid and all its premises are true. A sound argument guarantees a true conclusion.","more":"Soundness is the gold standard for a deductive argument. If an argument is valid, the only way to resist its conclusion is to deny one of the premises. Much philosophical debate, for example over arguments for God's existence, is really about whether the premises of valid arguments are true.","etym":"Old English gesund, 'healthy, whole'.","example":"‘All squares have four sides; this is a square; so it has four sides’ is sound.","notConfused":"Not the same as valid: every sound argument is valid, but a valid argument with a false premise is unsound.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Valid","Premise"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["soundness","sound argument","valid with true premises","validity"]},{"term":"Sovereignty","def":"Supreme authority within a territory: the power to make final decisions that no other body inside that territory can override.","more":"Jean Bodin and Hobbes argued that sovereignty must be undivided and absolute to prevent civil war. Rousseau located it in the people as a whole, expressed in the general will. Today it is debated in international politics: membership of bodies such as the European Union involves pooling or limiting sovereignty, which some see as cooperation and others as a loss of self-government.","etym":"Old French soverain, from Latin super, 'above'.","example":"A country deciding its own immigration rules without needing anyone else's permission.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Legitimacy","Social contract","Democracy"],"thinkers":["hobbes","rousseau"],"tags":["sovereign","supreme authority","state power","popular sovereignty","hobbes","bodin"]},{"term":"Special pleading","def":"A fallacy in which someone applies a rule to others but makes an exception for themselves or their favoured case, without a good reason.","more":"Not every exception is special pleading: real differences can justify different treatment. The fallacy happens when the exception rests on no relevant difference. The charge is sometimes made against arguments in religion, for instance when everything is said to need a cause except God; defenders reply that God does differ relevantly, being necessary rather than contingent.","etym":null,"example":"'Cheating is wrong, but I only copied because I was really busy.'","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Cosmological argument","Universalisability"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["double standard","ad hoc exception","unjustified exception","fallacy"]},{"term":"Speciesism","def":"Giving less moral weight to a being's interests simply because it belongs to another species, in the way racism discounts interests on the basis of race.","more":"The psychologist Richard Ryder coined the word in 1970, and Peter Singer made it famous in Animal Liberation (1975). Singer argues that equal suffering deserves equal consideration whatever the species. Critics argue that species membership, or the capacities typical of a species, can be morally relevant, and that the comparison with racism is misleading.","etym":"English, coined by Richard Ryder on the model of 'racism'.","example":"Thinking it is fine to cause a pig great pain for a slightly tastier meal, when you would never do the same to a dog.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Sentience","Utilitarianism","Rights"],"thinkers":["singer"],"tags":["species bias","peter singer","animal rights","animal liberation","anti-speciesism"]},{"term":"Speech act","def":"An utterance that does something, such as promising, warning, ordering or marrying, rather than just describing the world.","more":"J. L. Austin's How to Do Things with Words (1962) noticed that saying 'I promise', or 'I do' at a wedding, is not reporting a fact but performing an act. He separated what is said, what is done in saying it, and what effects follow from it. John Searle developed this into a general theory, and it is now used in debates about free speech, hate speech and consent.","etym":null,"example":"A judge saying 'I sentence you to two years' is not describing anything: the words make it so.","notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Language game","Expressivism","Emotivism"],"thinkers":["searle"],"tags":["speech act theory","austin","searle","performative","illocutionary act","how to do things with words"]},{"term":"Standpoint theory","def":"The view, developed in feminist philosophy, that people's social position shapes what they can know, and that marginalised groups can sometimes see aspects of society more clearly.","more":"Thinkers such as Nancy Hartsock, Sandra Harding and Patricia Hill Collins drew on Marx's idea that workers can understand capitalism in ways owners may not. The claim is not that oppressed people are automatically right, but that their experience can reveal what the comfortable overlook. Critics worry that it slides into relativism or treats whole groups as sharing one view.","etym":null,"example":"A wheelchair user is likely to notice how hard a building is to get around long before its architect does.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Social construction","Relativism","Justification"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["feminist standpoint theory","standpoint epistemology","situated knowledge","harding","marginalised perspective"]},{"term":"State of nature","def":"An imagined condition of humans living without any government, used to show why we need (or do not need) the state.","more":"Hobbes pictured it as a war of all against all; Locke as a condition governed by natural law, where people have rights but no impartial judge; and Rousseau as relatively peaceful until property and society corrupted people. Most of these thinkers did not claim it was a real period of history, though Locke pointed to relations between independent nations as an example.","etym":null,"example":"Hobbes thought life there would be ‘solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short’.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Social contract","Natural rights"],"thinkers":["hobbes","locke","rousseau"],"tags":["hobbes","locke","rousseau","pre-political","war of all against all","social contract"]},{"term":"Steelman","def":"Presenting an opposing view in its strongest, most charitable form before criticising it. It is the opposite of a straw man.","more":"Steelmanning is closely related to the principle of charity, a rule of interpretation used by Quine and Donald Davidson: interpret others so that their claims make the best sense. Philosophers value it because defeating a weak version of a view proves little. If your objection still works against the best version, it is a strong objection.","etym":"English, a recent coinage formed in contrast to 'straw man'.","example":"Before arguing that school uniforms should be scrapped, first setting out the best case for them: equality, focus and less pressure over clothes.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Straw man","Fallacy","Argument"],"thinkers":["quine"],"tags":["steelmanning","steel man","principle of charity","strongest version","straw man"]},{"term":"Stoicism","def":"A Hellenistic school teaching that virtue is the only true good, and that we should focus on what is in our control: our judgments and choices. Its ideal of apatheia means freedom from destructive passions, not coldness.","more":"Founded by Zeno of Citium in Athens c. 300 BCE, Stoicism was later taught by Seneca, Epictetus and the emperor Marcus Aurelius. The Stoics held that the universe is rationally ordered and that living according to nature means living by reason. Their practical exercises, such as imagining losses in advance, influenced modern cognitive behavioural therapy.","etym":"Greek stoa poikile, the 'painted porch' in Athens where Zeno taught.","example":"Stuck in traffic, a Stoic reminds themself that the traffic is not up to them, but their reaction is.","notConfused":"Not the same as being 'stoical' in everyday speech: the Stoics did not aim to bottle up feelings but to correct the false judgments behind destructive emotions.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Virtue","Epicureanism"],"thinkers":["zeno_citium","seneca","epictetus","marcus_aurelius"],"tags":["stoic","stoics","epictetus","marcus aurelius","seneca","zeno of citium"]},{"term":"Straw man","def":"Replacing someone’s real view with a weaker, distorted version that is easy to knock down.","more":"A straw man can be deliberate or come from careless reading. It is common in political debate, where the other side's view is summed up in its least flattering form. The opposite habit, stating an opponent's view in its strongest form before criticising it, is called steelmanning or the principle of charity.","etym":"From a dummy stuffed with straw, which is easy to knock over.","example":"‘You want less homework? So you think students shouldn’t learn anything.’","notConfused":"Not the same as simplifying: a short summary of someone's view is fine as long as it is fair.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Ad hominem"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["strawman","straw-man","misrepresentation","steelman","fallacy"]},{"term":"Subjective and objective","def":"Something is subjective if it depends on a person's mind, feelings or point of view, and objective if it holds regardless of what anyone thinks.","more":"The pair is used in several ways: about facts (is beauty objective?), about judgments (is the referee being objective, meaning unbiased?), and about points of view. Thomas Nagel's The View from Nowhere (1986) explores the tension between seeing the world from inside our own perspective and stepping back to a detached view. Many debates in ethics and aesthetics turn on which side of this line a value falls.","etym":"Latin subiectum, 'that which lies under', and obiectum, 'that which is thrown before'.","example":"'Chocolate tastes better than vanilla' seems subjective; 'chocolate contains sugar' seems objective.","notConfused":"Not the same as opinion versus fact in everyday speech: a subjective experience, such as a headache, is still a real fact about the person who has it.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Relativism","Realism","Qualia","Taste"],"thinkers":["nagel"],"tags":["subjectivity","objectivity","subjective","objective","mind-dependent"]},{"term":"Sublime","def":"In aesthetics, the experience of something vast or powerful, such as a storm or a mountain range, that overwhelms us and mixes awe with a kind of fear or pleasure.","more":"Edmund Burke's A Philosophical Enquiry into the Origin of Our Ideas of the Sublime and Beautiful (1757) contrasted the beautiful (small, smooth, pleasing) with the sublime (vast, dark, terrifying when seen from safety). Kant argued in the Critique of Judgement (1790) that the sublime reveals the power of our reason, which can think of infinity even when our senses are overwhelmed. The idea shaped Romantic painting and poetry.","etym":"Latin sublimis, 'lofty, raised high'.","example":"Standing at the edge of the Grand Canyon and feeling tiny, uneasy and thrilled all at once.","notConfused":"Not the same as the beautiful: beauty charms and pleases, while the sublime overwhelms.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Taste","Tragedy"],"thinkers":["burke","kant"],"tags":["the sublime","awe","burke","kant","grandeur","overwhelming"]},{"term":"Substance","def":"In metaphysics, a thing that exists in its own right and has properties, rather than being a property of something else.","more":"Aristotle made substance the most basic category of being. Descartes said there are two kinds, mind and matter; Spinoza argued there can be only one, which he called God or Nature. Locke described the substance underlying properties as something we know not what, and Berkeley and Hume doubted that we need the idea at all.","etym":"Latin substantia, 'that which stands underneath', translating Greek ousia.","example":"A horse is a substance; its brownness is a property the horse has.","notConfused":"Not the same as a substance in chemistry, such as water or salt, though the two senses share a common root in Latin substantia.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Essence","Dualism"],"thinkers":["aristotle","descartes","spinoza"],"tags":["ousia","substratum","underlying reality","aristotle","descartes","spinoza"]},{"term":"Sufism","def":"The mystical tradition within Islam, which seeks direct, loving experience of God through prayer, remembrance (dhikr), music and spiritual discipline.","more":"Sufis often describe a path of stages in which the ego is purified until the seeker draws close to God, sometimes called fana, the 'passing away' of the self. Al-Ghazali, after a personal crisis, argued in his autobiography that Sufi experience gives a certainty that philosophical argument alone cannot. Poets such as Rumi spread Sufi ideas widely, while some Muslim scholars have criticised certain Sufi practices as departing from orthodox teaching.","etym":"Arabic sufi, probably from suf, 'wool', after the rough woollen clothes of early ascetics.","example":"A Sufi gathering where people repeat a name of God together to focus the heart.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Tawhid","Religious experience","Sunna"],"thinkers":["alghazali"],"tags":["sufi","islamic mysticism","tasawwuf","rumi","mysticism","dervish"]},{"term":"Sunna","def":"In Islam, the example of the Prophet Muhammad, his sayings, actions and approvals, which Muslims follow as a guide alongside the Qur'an.","more":"The sunna is known mainly through reports called hadith, which scholars graded by how reliable their chains of transmission seemed. With the Qur'an, it is a main source of Islamic law (sharia) and ethics. Debates continue about which hadith are authentic and how to apply old examples to new situations.","etym":"Arabic sunna, 'custom, normal practice, path'.","example":"Many Muslims break their fast with dates because the Prophet is reported to have done so.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Tawhid","Revelation","Sufism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["sunnah","hadith","prophetic tradition","muhammad","islamic law"]},{"term":"Supererogation","def":"An act that is morally good but goes beyond what duty requires, so that no one is blamed for not doing it.","more":"Heroes and saints seem to perform such acts: a soldier who throws himself on a grenade to save his comrades is praised, but no one says he was obliged to. J. O. Urmson's 1958 essay 'Saints and Heroes' brought the category into modern ethics. Some utilitarians struggle with it, since if the best act is always required, nothing can go beyond duty.","etym":"Latin supererogare, 'to pay out beyond what is owed'.","example":"Donating a kidney to a stranger is admirable, but you do nothing wrong by keeping it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Deontology","Virtue"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["supererogatory","beyond the call of duty","heroism","sainthood","above and beyond"]},{"term":"Supervaluationism","def":"The view that a vague sentence is true if it comes out true on every acceptable way of sharpening the word, and false if false on every way.","more":"Kit Fine gave an influential version in 1975. The view keeps classical logic: 'Bob is tall or Bob is not tall' comes out true even for a borderline Bob, because every sharpening makes one side true. Critics find it odd that 'there is a cut-off point' comes out true while no particular cut-off point does.","etym":"Latin super, 'above', and English valuation: a valuation over all the others.","example":"‘A 2-metre person is tall’ is true on every sensible cut-off, so it is simply true.","notConfused":"Not the same as epistemicism: supervaluationists deny there is a hidden sharp line, while epistemicists say there is one we cannot know.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Vagueness","Sorites paradox"],"thinkers":[],"compass":"q16","tags":["supervaluation","fine","vagueness","precisifications","borderline cases"]},{"term":"Supervenience","def":"One set of facts supervenes on another if there can be no difference in the first without some difference in the second.","more":"The mental is often said to supervene on the physical: two beings exactly alike physically could not differ mentally. Supervenience is useful because it states a dependence without claiming reduction. Moral philosophers also say moral facts supervene on natural ones: two situations alike in every non-moral way cannot differ in rightness. What explains supervenience, where it holds, is still debated.","etym":"Latin supervenire, 'to come upon, follow on'.","example":"The picture on a screen cannot change without some change in its pixels: the picture supervenes on the pixels.","notConfused":"Not the same as reductionism: X can depend on Y in this way without being nothing but Y.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Physicalism","Reductionism","Functionalism"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"tags":["supervene","dependence","no difference without a difference","physicalism","grounding"]},{"term":"Syllogism","def":"An argument with two premises and a conclusion, linking terms with words like 'all', 'some' and 'no', in patterns such as 'All A are B; all B are C; so all A are C'.","more":"Aristotle built the first system of logic around syllogisms in the Prior Analytics, cataloguing which forms are valid. Medieval students memorised the valid forms with names such as 'Barbara'. Syllogistic logic remained the standard for about two thousand years, until Frege and others in the late nineteenth century developed modern logic, which handles many more kinds of argument.","etym":"Greek syllogismos, 'reckoning together, inference'.","example":"All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; so Socrates is mortal.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Valid","Logic","Premise","Argument"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["syllogistic","categorical syllogism","aristotelian logic","all men are mortal","term logic"]},{"term":"Synthetic a priori","def":"Kant's name for truths that tell us something new about the world yet can be known without relying on experience.","more":"Hume had held that what we can know without experience is only true by definition or concerns relations of ideas. Kant argued in the Critique of Pure Reason (1781) that arithmetic, such as 7 + 5 = 12, and principles such as 'every event has a cause' are synthetic yet known a priori, because they reflect the structure our minds give to experience. The logical positivists rejected the category, while others argue it survives in some form.","etym":"Greek synthesis, 'putting together'; Latin a priori, 'from what comes before'.","example":"Knowing, without checking, that nothing can be red all over and green all over at the same time is often offered as a possible case.","notConfused":"Not the same as analytic a priori truths like 'all bachelors are unmarried', which are true just because of what the words mean.","field":"Knowledge","related":["A priori","Analytic/synthetic","Transcendental","Rationalism"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["synthetic apriori","kant","critique of pure reason","a priori","mathematics"]},{"term":"Tabula rasa","def":"Latin for 'blank slate': the view that the mind at birth contains no built-in ideas, and that all knowledge comes from experience.","more":"The image is ancient, since Aristotle compared the mind to a writing tablet, but it is linked above all with Locke's attack on innate ideas in An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689), where he compares the mind to white paper. Leibniz replied that the mind has built-in tendencies, like the veins in a block of marble that guide a sculptor. Psychology still debates how much is innate, for example in Noam Chomsky's claim that humans have an inborn capacity for grammar.","etym":"Latin tabula rasa, 'scraped tablet'.","example":"Picturing a newborn learning everything about faces, words and danger purely from experience, with nothing in place beforehand.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","Rationalism","A posteriori"],"thinkers":["locke","aristotle","leibniz"],"tags":["blank slate","clean slate","locke","empiricism","innate ideas"]},{"term":"Taste","def":"In aesthetics, the ability to judge beauty and artistic quality well.","more":"Eighteenth-century philosophers puzzled over how taste can be both personal and open to argument. Hume's essay 'Of the Standard of Taste' (1757) found a standard in the shared verdicts of 'true judges', people with delicacy, practice and freedom from prejudice. Kant argued that judgments of beauty rest on feeling yet claim everyone's agreement. The sociologist Pierre Bourdieu later argued that taste largely tracks social class.","etym":"Old French taster, 'to touch, feel, taste'.","example":"An experienced listener notices subtleties in a pianist's timing that a newcomer misses.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Sublime","Subjective and objective"],"thinkers":["hume","kant","bourdieu"],"tags":["aesthetic judgement","aesthetic judgment","good taste","hume","standard of taste"]},{"term":"Tautology","def":"A statement that is true in every possible case because of its logical form, such as 'either it is raining or it is not raining'.","more":"In logic, tautologies are the statements that come out true on every row of a truth table. Wittgenstein's Tractatus (1921) argued that all truths of logic are tautologies and so say nothing about the world. In everyday speech 'tautology' also means needless repetition, as in 'a free gift', a different but related use.","etym":"Greek tautologia, from tauto, 'the same', and logos, 'saying'.","example":"'Either you will pass the test or you will not' is guaranteed to be true, but tells you nothing.","notConfused":"Not the same as circular reasoning: a tautology is a single statement true by its form, while circular reasoning uses its conclusion as a premise.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Truth table","Analytic/synthetic","Begging the question"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"tags":["tautological","logical truth","necessary truth","redundancy","true in all cases"]},{"term":"Tawhid","def":"The Islamic doctrine of the oneness and uniqueness of God, the central belief of Islam.","more":"Tawhid holds that God is one, without partners, parts or equals, and that nothing else is divine. Medieval Muslim theologians debated what it implies, for example whether God's attributes, such as knowledge and power, are distinct from God's essence, which might seem to threaten God's oneness. Its opposite, associating partners with God (shirk), is regarded as the gravest sin.","etym":"Arabic tawhid, 'making one, unification'.","example":"The first part of the Muslim declaration of faith, that there is no god but God, expresses tawhid.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theism","Sunna","Sufism"],"thinkers":["ashari","alghazali"],"tags":["tawheed","oneness of god","divine unity","monotheism","islam"]},{"term":"Teleology","def":"Explaining things in terms of their purposes or goals rather than only their causes. The word is also used for ethical theories that judge actions by the ends they bring about.","more":"Aristotle saw purposes throughout nature: teeth exist for chewing, and acorns grow towards becoming oaks. The scientific revolution largely dropped such explanations from physics, and Darwin showed how the appearance of design can arise through natural selection. Philosophers still argue whether biological talk of functions, such as 'the heart is for pumping blood', is genuinely teleological. In ethics, teleological theories such as consequentialism are contrasted with deontology.","etym":"Greek telos, 'end, goal', and logos, 'account'.","example":"Saying 'birds have hollow bones so that they can fly' is a teleological explanation.","notConfused":"Not the same as telos: telos is the goal itself, while teleology is the style of explanation that appeals to goals.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Telos","Design argument","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["teleological","purpose","final cause","goal-directedness","telos","design"]},{"term":"Telos","def":"Greek for end or goal. Aristotle thought natural things have a built-in purpose they tend toward.","more":"Aristotle listed four kinds of cause or explanation, and the 'final cause' is what a thing is for. He applied the idea to ethics: the human telos is eudaimonia, flourishing through the active use of reason. Modern science mostly dropped purposes from its account of nature, especially after Darwin, though biology still talks of functions.","etym":"Greek telos, 'end, goal, completion'.","example":"An acorn’s telos is to become an oak tree.","notConfused":"Not the same as teleology: telos is the goal itself, while teleology is the practice of explaining things by their goals.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Eudaimonia","Design argument"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["purpose","goal","end","final cause","aristotle","teleology"]},{"term":"Theism","def":"Belief in a single, personal God who created the world and is usually held to be all-powerful, all-knowing and perfectly good.","more":"Judaism, Christianity and Islam are theistic religions. Theism is contrasted with atheism (there is no God), agnosticism (we do not or cannot know), deism (a creator who does not intervene) and pantheism (God is identical with the universe). Philosophers test theism through arguments for God's existence and challenges such as the problem of evil.","etym":"Greek theos, 'god'.","example":"A person who prays, believing that God hears and may answer, is a theist.","notConfused":"Not the same as deism: a deist's God created the world but does not act in it or answer prayers.","field":"Religion","related":["Ontological argument","Cosmological argument","Problem of evil","Theodicy"],"thinkers":["aquinas","swinburne","plantinga"],"tags":["theist","belief in god","monotheism","god","classical theism"]},{"term":"Theodicy","def":"An attempt to explain why a good God permits evil. The word was coined by Leibniz.","more":"Augustine argued that evil is a lack of good that comes from the misuse of free will. Leibniz argued in his Theodicy (1710) that ours is the best of all possible worlds, a view Voltaire mocked in Candide. John Hick's 'soul-making' theodicy, drawing on Irenaeus, says a world with suffering is needed for moral growth. Critics, from Hume onwards, doubt any explanation can account for the sheer amount of suffering.","etym":"Greek theos, 'god', and dike, 'justice'.","example":"Arguing that suffering builds courage and compassion that could not exist otherwise.","notConfused":"Not the same as a defence, such as Plantinga's free will defence, which only aims to show that God and evil are logically consistent, not to give God's actual reasons.","field":"Religion","related":["Problem of evil","Free will defense"],"thinkers":["leibniz","augustine"],"compass":"q19","tags":["problem of evil","justifying god","leibniz","best of all possible worlds","soul-making"]},{"term":"Theory-ladenness","def":"The idea that observation is never fully neutral, because what scientists see is shaped by the theories and concepts they already hold.","more":"N. R. Hanson, Thomas Kuhn and Paul Feyerabend pressed the point in the mid-twentieth century: an expert and a beginner looking at the same X-ray do not, in a sense, see the same thing. This challenges the simple picture in which observation is a neutral test of theories. Critics reply that observation can still correct theories, because many observations do not depend on the particular theory being tested.","etym":null,"example":"A trained doctor looks at an X-ray and sees a fracture; you see a blur of grey.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Paradigm","Underdetermination","Falsifiability"],"thinkers":["kuhn"],"tags":["theory-laden observation","theory laden","hanson","kuhn","observation"]},{"term":"Thought experiment","def":"An imagined scenario used to test an idea. It strips a question down to what matters, like a lab experiment done in the mind.","more":"Famous philosophical examples include Plato's ring of Gyges, Descartes' evil demon, Nozick's experience machine and Judith Jarvis Thomson's violinist. Scientists use them too, as when Galileo reasoned about a heavy and a light body falling tied together. Critics ask whether our intuitions about strange imagined cases are a reliable guide to real life.","etym":"German Gedankenexperiment, a term popularised by the physicist Ernst Mach.","example":"Imagining a machine that could give you any experience you want, to ask whether pleasure is all that matters.","notConfused":null,"field":"Method","related":["Intuition","Reflective equilibrium"],"thinkers":["nozick","parfit","thomson"],"tags":["gedankenexperiment","hypothetical scenario","imagined case","intuition pump"]},{"term":"Tian","def":"Chinese for 'Heaven': in early Chinese thought, the highest power, which grants rulers their mandate and underlies the moral order.","more":"Under the Zhou dynasty, Tian was a kind of supreme power that gave the Mandate of Heaven to virtuous rulers and withdrew it from wicked ones. Confucius spoke of Tian with reverence and Mencius linked it to human nature, while Xunzi treated it more as the regular working of nature, which does not respond to prayer. Scholars debate how personal a being Confucius took Tian to be.","etym":"Chinese 天 tiān, 'sky, heaven'.","example":"A dynasty losing power after floods and famine was traditionally read as having lost the Mandate of Heaven.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dao","Ren","Yi"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius","xunzi"],"tags":["t'ien","heaven","sky","mandate of heaven","confucianism"]},{"term":"Toleration","def":"Putting up with beliefs or practices you disapprove of, rather than suppressing them by force.","more":"The modern case for toleration grew in Europe after the religious wars of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. Locke's A Letter Concerning Toleration (1689) argued that the state cannot force true belief and should leave religion to conscience, though he excluded atheists, and probably Catholics, from toleration. Mill extended the case to opinions in general. A long-standing puzzle is whether we should tolerate the intolerant.","etym":"Latin tolerare, 'to bear, endure'.","example":"Disliking your neighbour's religion but defending their right to practise it.","notConfused":"Not the same as approval: you can only tolerate what you disapprove of.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Liberty (negative/positive)","Secularism","Harm principle"],"thinkers":["locke","mill"],"tags":["tolerance","religious toleration","locke","mill","pluralism"]},{"term":"Totalitarianism","def":"A form of government that seeks total control over every part of life, including thought, through a single party, an official ideology, mass propaganda and terror.","more":"The term is mostly applied to Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union under Stalin. Hannah Arendt's The Origins of Totalitarianism (1951) argued that it was a new form of rule, different from older tyranny, because it aimed to remake human beings and destroy the space for independent thought. Karl Popper, in The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945), traced intellectual roots of totalitarian thinking to Plato, Hegel and Marx, a reading many scholars dispute.","etym":"Italian totalitario, first used in the 1920s, originally by critics of Mussolini.","example":"A state that encourages children to report their parents' private conversations to the police.","notConfused":"Not the same as authoritarianism: an authoritarian state demands obedience, while a totalitarian one tries to control what people believe.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Tyranny of the majority","Liberty (negative/positive)","Legitimacy"],"thinkers":["arendt","popper"],"tags":["totalitarian","authoritarianism","dictatorship","arendt","fascism","tyranny"]},{"term":"Tragedy","def":"A drama in which a serious, often admirable person suffers a terrible downfall. Philosophers ask why audiences value watching such suffering.","more":"Aristotle's Poetics said that tragedy arouses pity and fear and brings about catharsis, a purging or clarifying of these emotions, and that the hero's fall often stems from hamartia, an error or flaw. Hume's essay 'Of Tragedy' asked why we take pleasure in what is painful. Nietzsche's The Birth of Tragedy (1872) saw Greek tragedy as a union of the Apollonian (order) and the Dionysian (frenzy).","etym":"Greek tragoidia, probably 'goat song', though the reason for the name is uncertain.","example":"Watching Romeo and Juliet die through a misunderstanding, you feel grief, yet you would happily watch the play again.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Sublime","Absurd"],"thinkers":["aristotle","hume","nietzsche"],"tags":["tragic","greek tragedy","aristotle's poetics","catharsis","tragic hero"]},{"term":"Tragedy of the commons","def":"A situation in which individuals, each acting in their own interest, overuse a shared resource and ruin it for everyone.","more":"The ecologist Garrett Hardin made the phrase famous in 1968, drawing on an 1833 example by the economist William Forster Lloyd about herders grazing a shared pasture. It shows how choices that make sense for each person can add up to collective disaster, which links it to game theory and to debates about pollution and climate change. The political economist Elinor Ostrom showed that communities often manage shared resources well through local rules, without either state control or private ownership.","etym":"English; 'commons' were pasture lands shared by a village.","example":"Each fishing boat takes a little extra, until the fish stock collapses and everyone loses their living.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Social contract","Egoism","State of nature"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["commons","hardin","overuse","shared resources","collective action problem","free rider problem"]},{"term":"Transcendental","def":"In Kant's sense, concerning the conditions that make experience and knowledge possible in the first place.","more":"A transcendental argument starts from something we cannot doubt, such as that we have experiences ordered in time, and works back to what must be true for that to be possible. Kant used this method to argue that space, time and causation are structures the mind brings to experience. Later philosophers, such as P. F. Strawson, used transcendental arguments against sceptics.","etym":"Latin transcendere, 'to climb over, go beyond'.","example":"Asking not 'Is this sentence true?' but 'What must be the case for any sentence to be true or false at all?' is a transcendental question.","notConfused":"Not the same as transcendent: a transcendent God is beyond the world, while transcendental conditions are about how experience is possible.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Synthetic a priori","Idealism","A priori"],"thinkers":["kant","pfstrawson"],"tags":["transcendental idealism","transcendental argument","kant","conditions of possibility","a priori"]},{"term":"Trolley problem","def":"A family of puzzles about whether it is right to cause one death to prevent five, and why our answers change from case to case.","more":"Philippa Foot introduced the case in 1967, in a paper on abortion and the doctrine of double effect, and Judith Jarvis Thomson named it and added variations such as the footbridge case. The puzzle is to find a principle that explains why diverting the trolley seems acceptable while pushing a person does not. It is now also discussed in debates about how self-driving cars should be programmed.","etym":"English; 'trolley' is the American word for a tram.","example":"Most people would pull a lever to divert the trolley, but would not push someone off a bridge to stop it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Doctrine of double effect","Consequentialism","Deontology"],"thinkers":["foot","thomson"],"compass":"q2","tags":["trolley dilemma","runaway trolley","footbridge case","foot","thomson","doctrine of double effect"]},{"term":"Truth","def":"The property a belief or statement has when it fits how things are. Philosophers disagree about what that fit consists in.","more":"The correspondence theory says a statement is true if it matches the facts; Aristotle gave an early version, saying that to say of what is that it is, is true. The coherence theory says truth is fitting into a consistent system of beliefs, and pragmatists such as William James linked truth to what works in experience. Deflationists argue there is little more to say than that 'snow is white' is true just if snow is white, while pluralists such as Michael Lynch say truth can take different forms in different areas.","etym":"Old English treowth, 'faithfulness, loyalty'.","example":"'Paris is the capital of France' is true because Paris is the capital of France.","notConfused":"Not the same as belief or certainty: a statement can be true when nobody believes it, and people can be certain of falsehoods.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Justification","Realism","Relativism","Epistemology"],"thinkers":["aristotle","james","lynch"],"tags":["true","theories of truth","veracity","fact","correspondence"]},{"term":"Truth table","def":"A chart that lists every possible combination of true and false for simple statements, showing when a compound statement such as 'A and B' or 'if A then B' is true.","more":"Truth tables let you check mechanically whether an argument in basic logic is valid, or whether a statement is a tautology. They were developed in the early twentieth century, notably by Wittgenstein in the Tractatus and by Emil Post, with earlier versions in Peirce. They also reveal surprising features of logic, such as the rule that 'if A then B' counts as true whenever A is false.","etym":null,"example":"'It is sunny and warm' is true on only one of the four rows: the one where it is both sunny and warm.","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Tautology","Valid","Logic"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein","peirce"],"tags":["truth tables","truth values","truth-functional","propositional logic","logical connectives"]},{"term":"Tu quoque","def":"A fallacy that dismisses a criticism by pointing out that the critic is guilty of the same thing.","more":"It is a form of ad hominem argument. Even a hypocrite can make a correct point: whether a criticism is right does not depend on the critic's own behaviour. Pointing out hypocrisy can be relevant to judging the critic's sincerity, but not to the truth of what they say.","etym":"Latin tu quoque, 'you too'.","example":"'You can't tell me to stop smoking, you smoked for years!'","notConfused":null,"field":"Logic and argument","related":["Ad hominem","Fallacy","Red herring"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["you too","appeal to hypocrisy","whataboutism","hypocrisy","ad hominem"]},{"term":"Turing test","def":"Alan Turing's proposed test for machine intelligence: if a judge chatting by text cannot reliably tell a computer from a human, the computer should count as thinking.","more":"Turing called it the 'imitation game' in his 1950 paper 'Computing Machinery and Intelligence', offering it as a replacement for the vague question of whether machines can think. Critics argue that imitation is not understanding: John Searle's Chinese room argument (1980) claims a system could pass by shuffling symbols without grasping what they mean. The arrival of large language models has revived the debate.","etym":"Named after Alan Turing.","example":"Chatting online with a customer-service agent and being unsure whether it is a person or a bot.","notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Behaviorism","Zombie"],"thinkers":["turing","searle"],"tags":["imitation game","alan turing","machine intelligence","artificial intelligence","can machines think"]},{"term":"Type and token","def":"A type is a general kind; a token is a particular instance of it. The word 'the' is one type, but it occurs as many tokens on this page.","more":"The distinction was introduced by C. S. Peirce. It matters in philosophy of mind: type identity theory says each kind of mental state is a kind of brain state, while token identity says only that each particular mental event is some physical event. It also clears up everyday puzzles, such as whether two people who drive 'the same car' share one thing or own two.","etym":"English; the terms are Peirce's.","example":"The sentence 'the cat sat on the mat' contains six word tokens but only five word types, since 'the' appears twice.","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Identity theory","Universals","Functionalism"],"thinkers":["peirce"],"tags":["type-token distinction","types","tokens","peirce","instance"]},{"term":"Tyranny of the majority","def":"The danger that, in a democracy, the majority uses its power to oppress minorities or individuals.","more":"Alexis de Tocqueville used the phrase in Democracy in America (1835), and John Stuart Mill took it up in On Liberty (1859), warning that social pressure and public opinion can be as tyrannical as law. Remedies include constitutional rights, independent courts and the separation of powers. Some democrats reply that these protections can themselves be undemocratic, giving unelected judges too much power.","etym":null,"example":"A majority voting to ban a minority's religious festival.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Democracy","Harm principle","Separation of powers","Rights"],"thinkers":["mill"],"tags":["majority tyranny","mill","tocqueville","majoritarianism","minority rights"]},{"term":"Ubuntu","def":"A Southern African ethical idea that a person becomes a person through other people, so that humanity is found in community, mutual care and relationship.","more":"Ubuntu is often summed up in the Nguni saying 'umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu', a person is a person through other persons. John Mbiti described African thought in similar terms, as holding that the individual exists because the community exists. Thaddeus Metz has developed ubuntu into a moral theory in which right actions honour communal relationships, and the idea played a public role in South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission. Critics ask whether it leaves enough room for individual rights.","etym":"Nguni (for example Zulu and Xhosa) ubuntu, 'humanness'.","example":"A village where one family's loss is felt as everyone's loss, and helping them is simply part of being a decent person.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Ren","Virtue ethics","Personal identity"],"thinkers":["mbiti","metz"],"tags":["i am because we are","humanness","african philosophy","community","interconnectedness"]},{"term":"Underdetermination","def":"The idea that the available evidence is never enough on its own to settle which theory is true, because more than one theory can fit the same data.","more":"Pierre Duhem argued that a scientific hypothesis is never tested alone but together with many background assumptions, so a failed prediction does not tell you which one to give up. Quine extended this into what is now called the Duhem-Quine thesis. Some take it to show that factors such as simplicity or social influence help decide between theories; others argue that it is less serious in practice than in principle.","etym":"Latin determinare, 'to set limits', with 'under-': not enough to fix the answer.","example":"A car will not start: a flat battery, an empty tank or a faulty starter could each explain it, and that fact alone cannot choose between them.","notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Theory-ladenness","Falsifiability","Scientific realism","Occam’s razor"],"thinkers":["duhem","quine"],"tags":["underdetermination of theory by evidence","quine-duhem","duhem","empirical equivalence","theory choice"]},{"term":"Universalisability","def":"The requirement that a moral rule must be one you could will everyone to follow in similar circumstances, not an exception made for yourself.","more":"It is the core of Kant's first formulation of the categorical imperative: act only on a principle that you could at the same time will to be a universal law. R. M. Hare later argued that universalisability is built into the meaning of moral words such as 'ought'. Critics say almost any rule can pass if it is described narrowly enough.","etym":"Latin universalis, 'of the whole'.","example":"Asking 'What if everyone skipped paying their bus fare?' to test whether you may do it.","notConfused":"Not the same as the Golden Rule: universalisability asks whether a rule could hold for everyone, not whether you would like to be treated that way.","field":"Ethics","related":["Categorical imperative","Deontology","Special pleading"],"thinkers":["kant","hare"],"tags":["universalizability","universalizable","universal law","kant","categorical imperative","hare"]},{"term":"Universals","def":"Features that many things can share at once, like redness or roundness. Philosophers argue about whether they really exist.","more":"Plato held that universals (his Forms) exist apart from the things that share them, while Aristotle thought they exist only in particular things. Nominalists such as Ockham deny that there are universals at all: only individual things exist, grouped by names or by resemblance. The debate matters for how we explain similarity, laws of nature and the meaning of general words.","etym":"Latin universalis, 'of the whole'.","example":"A fire truck and a strawberry both seem to have the same thing: redness.","notConfused":"Not the same as universal truths: a universal is a feature that many things share, not a claim that holds everywhere.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Nominalism","Platonism","Forms"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle","ockham"],"tags":["universal","problem of universals","properties","realism","nominalism","forms"]},{"term":"Unmoved mover","def":"Aristotle's idea of an eternal, unchanging first cause that sets everything else in motion without itself being moved.","more":"In the Physics and the Metaphysics, Aristotle argued that a chain of things moved by other things cannot go back forever, so there must be a first mover that is pure actuality. It moves things not by pushing them but as an object of love or desire. Aquinas adapted the reasoning as the first of his Five Ways of showing that God exists.","etym":"English rendering of Aristotle's Greek; Latin primum movens, 'first mover'.","example":"Like a person whom everyone admires, it causes movement in others without itself doing anything.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Cosmological argument","Causation","Theism"],"thinkers":["aristotle","aquinas"],"tags":["prime mover","first mover","aristotle","first cause","god","primum movens"]},{"term":"Upanishads","def":"Ancient Indian philosophical texts, the latest part of the Vedas, which explore the nature of the self (atman) and of ultimate reality (Brahman).","more":"The oldest Upanishads probably date from c. 700–500 BCE. A central teaching, on many readings, is that atman and Brahman are in some way one, summed up in the phrase tat tvam asi, 'that thou art'. They are the foundation of the Vedanta schools, which disagree about exactly how the self and ultimate reality are related.","etym":"Sanskrit, traditionally explained as upa ('near'), ni ('down') and sad ('sit'): sitting down near a teacher.","example":"A student asks a sage what lies behind all things and is told, in effect, that it is the same as their own deepest self.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Atman","Brahman","Vedanta","Samsara"],"thinkers":["shankara"],"tags":["upanisads","vedanta","vedas","atman","brahman","hindu scriptures"]},{"term":"Utilitarianism","def":"The best-known consequentialism: the right act is the one that produces the most overall wellbeing (‘utility’) counting everyone equally.","more":"Jeremy Bentham founded the modern theory, and John Stuart Mill refined it in Utilitarianism (1861), distinguishing higher from lower pleasures. Peter Singer applies it to global poverty and the treatment of animals. Critics argue that it can justify terrible acts for the greater good, neglects justice and rights, and demands too much of ordinary people.","etym":"Latin utilis, 'useful'.","example":"Spending £100 to save a stranger’s sight rather than on a gadget you barely want.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'utilitarian' in the everyday sense of plain and practical: the ethical theory is about maximising wellbeing.","field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Hedonism"],"thinkers":["bentham","mill","singer"],"compass":"q1","tags":["greatest happiness principle","greatest good","bentham","mill","utility","consequentialism"]},{"term":"Utility","def":"In ethics and economics, a measure of wellbeing or satisfaction: how good an outcome is for those affected by it.","more":"For Bentham, utility meant pleasure and the absence of pain; later thinkers equated it with getting what you prefer. Utilitarianism tells us to maximise total utility. A long-running problem is whether one person's utility can be compared with another's, and whether it can be measured at all.","etym":"Latin utilitas, 'usefulness'.","example":"An extra £100 probably adds more utility to a struggling student's life than to a billionaire's.","notConfused":"Not the same as usefulness in everyday speech: something useless, like a sunset, can bring a great deal of utility.","field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Wellbeing","Hedonism"],"thinkers":["bentham","mill"],"tags":["happiness","welfare","wellbeing","usefulness","utilitarianism","preference satisfaction"]},{"term":"Utility monster","def":"Nozick’s imagined being that gets vastly more pleasure from resources than anyone else, so utilitarianism seems to say everyone should be sacrificed to it.","more":"Robert Nozick introduced it in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) to show that maximising total happiness could permit extreme unfairness. Utilitarians reply that no such creature exists, or change their theory to give extra weight to those who are worse off. The example is sometimes revived in debates about future beings, such as advanced AI, with far greater capacity for wellbeing.","etym":null,"example":"A creature that enjoys each cookie a million times more than you do would get all the cookies.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["nozick"],"tags":["nozick","utilitarianism","aggregation","objection to utilitarianism"]},{"term":"Utopia","def":"An imagined perfect society, used to explore ideals or to criticise the society we have.","more":"Thomas More coined the word in his book Utopia (1516), and Plato's Republic is often counted as an earlier example. Karl Popper warned that trying to build a blueprint perfect society tends to lead to violence against those who do not fit, while Nozick's Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) defended a framework in which people freely form communities of their own choosing. Dystopias, such as Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four, imagine the opposite.","etym":"Greek ou, 'not', and topos, 'place': 'no-place', with a pun on eu-topos, 'good place'.","example":"A story about an island where no one owns private property and everyone works only six hours a day, as in More's Utopia.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Justice","Totalitarianism","Social contract"],"thinkers":["plato","nozick","popper"],"tags":["utopian","ideal society","thomas more","perfect society","dystopia"]},{"term":"Vagueness","def":"When a word has borderline cases where it is unclear whether it applies, with no sharp cut-off.","more":"Philosophers disagree about where vagueness lives: in our language, in our knowledge (epistemicism), or in the world itself. The question matters beyond philosophy, because law and ethics are full of vague lines, such as when a crowd becomes a riot or when a child becomes old enough to decide for themselves. Vague words are also the raw material of the sorites paradox.","etym":"Latin vagus, 'wandering, uncertain'.","example":"Is a 30-year-old ‘young’? Is a light-orange wall ‘red’?","notConfused":"Not the same as ambiguity: 'bank' is ambiguous because it has two meanings, while 'tall' is vague because it has no sharp boundary.","field":"Language","related":["Sorites paradox","Epistemicism","Supervaluationism"],"thinkers":[],"compass":"q16","tags":["vague","borderline cases","fuzziness","sorites paradox","imprecision"]},{"term":"Valid","def":"An argument is valid when the conclusion must be true if the premises are true. Validity is about form, not about whether the premises are actually true.","more":"Logicians judge validity by form: any argument with the same pattern as a valid argument is also valid. A valid argument can have false premises and a false conclusion; what it cannot have is true premises and a false conclusion. That is why validity alone does not make an argument good.","etym":"Latin validus, 'strong'.","example":"‘All fish can fly; Nemo is a fish; so Nemo can fly’ is valid but has a false premise.","notConfused":"Not the same as true: statements are true or false, while arguments are valid or invalid.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Sound","Argument","Fallacy"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["validity","valid argument","deductively valid","follows logically","logical consequence"]},{"term":"Value pluralism","def":"The view that there are many genuine values, such as liberty, equality, justice and mercy, which can conflict and cannot all be measured on one scale.","more":"Isaiah Berlin made the view central to his political thought, arguing that because values truly clash, some loss is unavoidable whatever we choose, and that the dream of a final harmony of all values has fuelled tyranny. Monists, such as utilitarians, reply that one master value can rank all the others.","etym":null,"example":"A judge must weigh mercy for a young offender against justice for the victim, and neither choice is free of cost.","notConfused":"Not the same as relativism: value pluralists think these values are real for everyone; there are just several of them.","field":"Ethics","related":["Relativism","Liberty (negative/positive)","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["berlin"],"tags":["moral pluralism","pluralism","isaiah berlin","incommensurable values","conflicting values"]},{"term":"Vedanta","def":"Often regarded as the most influential school of Hindu philosophy, based on the Upanishads, which asks how the self (atman) relates to ultimate reality (Brahman).","more":"It has several branches. Shankara's Advaita ('non-dual') Vedanta holds that atman and Brahman are identical and that the world of separate things is a kind of illusion (maya). Ramanuja's Vishishtadvaita ('qualified non-dualism') treats selves as real parts of Brahman, and Madhva's Dvaita ('dualism') keeps God and selves distinct.","etym":"Sanskrit veda, 'knowledge', and anta, 'end': 'the end of the Vedas'.","example":"Two Vedantins arguing whether a drop of water in the ocean is the ocean itself or only a part of it.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Upanishads","Atman","Brahman"],"thinkers":["shankara","ramanuja","madhva"],"tags":["vedānta","advaita vedanta","upanishads","shankara","brahman","hindu philosophy"]},{"term":"Veil of ignorance","def":"Rawls’ device for fairness: choose the rules of society without knowing who you will be in it: rich or poor, which gender, which talents. The imagined choosing situation is called the ‘original position’.","more":"Rawls argued in A Theory of Justice (1971) that people behind the veil would choose equal basic liberties and the difference principle, which protects the worst-off in case they turn out to be among them. The economist John Harsanyi had used a similar device earlier and concluded that people would choose to maximise average wellbeing instead. Critics ask whether reasoning so cautiously about risk is really rational.","etym":null,"example":"Cutting a cake fairly when you do not know which slice you will get.","notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Difference principle","Justice"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"compass":"q23","tags":["rawls","original position","justice as fairness","impartiality","a theory of justice"]},{"term":"Verification principle","def":"The logical positivists’ claim that a statement is meaningful only if it is true by definition or can be checked by experience.","more":"It was developed by the Vienna Circle in the 1920s and spread in English by A. J. Ayer's Language, Truth and Logic (1936), with the aim of clearing away metaphysics and theology as meaningless. Its main problem is self-application: the principle itself is neither true by definition nor checkable by experience, so by its own test it seems meaningless. Attempts to repair it ran into further difficulties, and it was largely abandoned.","etym":"Latin verus, 'true', and facere, 'to make'.","example":"By this test, ‘The Absolute is perfect’ is neither true nor false but meaningless.","notConfused":"Not the same as falsifiability: Popper offered falsifiability as a test of what counts as science, not as a test of meaning.","field":"Language","related":["Falsifiability","Analytic/synthetic","Emotivism"],"thinkers":["ayer"],"tags":["verificationism","verifiability criterion","logical positivism","ayer","meaningfulness"]},{"term":"Via negativa","def":"The 'negative way' in theology: describing God only by saying what God is not, because God is beyond all human concepts.","more":"Thinkers in Jewish, Christian and Islamic traditions used it. Maimonides argued that positive attributes such as 'wise' wrongly liken God to creatures, so we come closest to God through denials: God is not bodily, not limited, not ignorant. The Christian writer known as Pseudo-Dionysius was a major source for the approach. Critics worry that a God described only by negations is left with no content at all.","etym":"Latin via negativa, 'the negative way'; also called apophatic theology, from Greek apophasis, 'denial'.","example":"Saying 'God is not in time' rather than claiming to know what God's eternity is like.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theism","Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Dao"],"thinkers":["maimonides","plotinus"],"tags":["negative theology","apophatic theology","apophaticism","negative way"]},{"term":"Vienna Circle","def":"A group of philosophers and scientists in Vienna in the 1920s and 1930s who founded logical positivism, aiming to make philosophy as rigorous as science.","more":"Led by Moritz Schlick and including Rudolf Carnap and Otto Neurath, the group held that meaningful statements are either true by logic or checkable by experience, so most traditional metaphysics is meaningless. A. J. Ayer spread their ideas in English. The group broke up in the 1930s as Nazism rose, and Schlick was murdered in 1936. Their main doctrines were later widely abandoned, but their stress on logic and science shaped analytic philosophy.","etym":"German Wiener Kreis.","example":"A member would call a sentence like 'reality as a whole is spirit unfolding' not false but meaningless, since no experience could test it.","notConfused":null,"field":"History of philosophy","related":["Verification principle","Analytic/synthetic","Falsifiability"],"thinkers":["schlick","carnap","ayer"],"tags":["wiener kreis","logical positivism","carnap","schlick","neurath","logical empiricism"]},{"term":"Virtue","def":"A good trait of character, such as courage, honesty or generosity, built up through practice.","more":"Aristotle distinguished virtues of character, like courage, from intellectual virtues, like practical wisdom, and saw each virtue of character as a mean between two vices. The Greek word arete meant excellence of any kind, even the excellence of a good knife. The classical 'cardinal' virtues were wisdom, courage, temperance and justice, to which Christian thinkers added faith, hope and charity.","etym":"Latin virtus, 'excellence'; it translates the Greek arete.","example":"An honest person tells the truth easily, not because a rule forces them.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Golden mean","Ren"],"thinkers":["aristotle","confucius"],"tags":["virtues","arete","moral excellence","character","goodness"]},{"term":"Virtue epistemology","def":"An approach to knowledge that focuses on the qualities of the knower, such as careful perception, open-mindedness and intellectual honesty.","more":"Instead of asking only which beliefs are justified, it asks what makes someone a good thinker. Ernest Sosa treats knowledge as true belief that is reached through the believer's competence, like an archer's shot that hits the target because of skill rather than luck. Linda Zagzebski, drawing on virtue ethics, stresses character traits such as intellectual courage and humility.","etym":null,"example":"A detective who considers every possibility before reaching a conclusion is showing intellectual virtue.","notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Virtue ethics","Justification","Gettier problem"],"thinkers":["sosa","zagzebski"],"tags":["intellectual virtues","epistemic virtue","sosa","zagzebski","virtue reliabilism"]},{"term":"Virtue ethics","def":"The view that ethics is centred on character: being honest, brave, kind and wise, and living a flourishing life.","more":"Rooted in Aristotle, and with parallels in Confucius, it was revived in modern English-language philosophy after Elizabeth Anscombe's 1958 paper 'Modern Moral Philosophy' criticised rule-based ethics. Rosalind Hursthouse defines a right action as what a virtuous person would characteristically do in the circumstances. Critics say it gives too little guidance in hard cases; defenders reply that good judgment cannot be reduced to rules.","etym":"Latin virtus, 'excellence, courage'.","example":"Instead of asking ‘Which rule applies?’, a virtue ethicist asks ‘What would a good person do here?’","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue","Eudaimonia","Golden mean"],"thinkers":["aristotle","confucius","anscombe","foot"],"compass":"q1","tags":["aristotelian ethics","character ethics","aristotle","virtues","eudaimonia","character"]},{"term":"Wabi-sabi","def":"A Japanese aesthetic that finds beauty in things that are imperfect, impermanent, simple and worn.","more":"It grew from Zen Buddhism and the tea ceremony, which valued rough handmade bowls, moss and weathered wood, and it echoes the Buddhist teaching that all things are impermanent. Yoshida Kenkō's Essays in Idleness had earlier praised the incomplete and the faded. Scholars note that 'wabi-sabi' as a single paired term is largely modern; historically, wabi meant rustic simplicity and sabi the beauty of age.","etym":"Japanese wabi, 'rustic simplicity', and sabi, 'the patina of age'.","example":"A cracked tea bowl repaired with gold lacquer, valued more because its history shows.","notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetics","Zen","Dukkha"],"thinkers":["kenko","okakura"],"tags":["wabi sabi","imperfection","impermanence","japanese aesthetics","beauty of imperfection","rustic simplicity"]},{"term":"Wellbeing","def":"How well a person's life is going for them: what is good for someone, as opposed to what is good in general.","more":"Derek Parfit grouped theories into three families: hedonism (wellbeing is pleasure), desire-satisfaction theories (getting what you want) and objective list theories (some things, such as knowledge, friendship and achievement, are good for you whether or not you want them). Nozick's experience machine is a famous challenge to hedonism. Wellbeing is central to utilitarianism and to public policy.","etym":null,"example":"Someone who is rich and famous but lonely and anxious may still have low wellbeing.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Hedonism","Eudaimonia","Experience machine","Utility"],"thinkers":["parfit","nozick"],"tags":["well-being","welfare","flourishing","happiness","quality of life","prudential value"]},{"term":"Will to power","def":"Nietzsche’s idea that a basic drive in living things is to grow, overcome and expand their strength, not merely to survive.","more":"Nietzsche used the idea to explain far more than politics: knowledge, art and even morality were, for him, expressions of this drive. He rejected the view that living things seek only pleasure or self-preservation. The phrase was later misused, partly through a posthumous collection of his notes edited by his sister, to support nationalist and racist ideas that most scholars think he would have rejected.","etym":"German der Wille zur Macht.","example":"An artist pushing past old limits to create something new expresses will to power.","notConfused":"Not the same as a wish to dominate other people: for Nietzsche it includes self-mastery and creativity.","field":"Ethics","related":["Übermensch","Eternal recurrence"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"tags":["wille zur macht","nietzsche","power","self-overcoming","ubermensch"]},{"term":"Wu wei","def":"Daoist ‘non-action’ or effortless action: acting in harmony with things instead of forcing them.","more":"The Daodejing presents wu wei as the way of the sage and the good ruler, who governs lightly and lets things take their course. It does not mean doing nothing; it means acting without strain, as a skilled craftsperson does. The story of Cook Ding in the Zhuangzi is the classic illustration, and Confucians also used the idea for the effortless action of a fully virtuous person.","etym":"Chinese 無為 wúwéi, 'without action' or 'without striving'.","example":"A skilled cook carving meat by following the natural joints, so the knife never dulls.","notConfused":"Not the same as laziness or passivity: wu wei is skilful, responsive action that does not force things.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dao"],"thinkers":["laozi","zhuangzi"],"tags":["wu-wei","non-action","effortless action","non-doing","daoism","taoism"]},{"term":"Xiao","def":"Filial piety: the Confucian virtue of respect, care and loyalty towards one's parents and ancestors.","more":"In the Analects, xiao is treated as a root of ren (humaneness), since we first learn to love within the family. It means more than obedience: Confucius says that merely feeding your parents is not enough, since even animals are fed, without reverence. Later debates asked what to do when filial duty clashes with the law, as in Confucius' remark that fathers and sons cover for one another.","etym":"Chinese 孝 xiào.","example":"Taking time to care for an ageing parent, and honouring their memory after they die.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Ren","Li (ritual propriety)","Virtue"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius"],"tags":["hsiao","filial piety","respect for parents","family duty","confucianism"]},{"term":"Xin","def":"Chinese 'heart-mind': the organ that, in Chinese thought, both thinks and feels, the seat of reasoning, emotion and moral judgment.","more":"Because Chinese philosophy did not sharply separate thinking from feeling, xin is often translated 'heart-mind'. Mencius argued that every xin contains 'sprouts' of goodness, shown in the alarm anyone would feel on seeing a child about to fall into a well. The Neo-Confucian Wang Yangming later taught that moral knowledge lies within the heart-mind and cannot be separated from action.","etym":"Chinese 心 xīn; the character began as a picture of the heart.","example":"Feeling instant alarm when you see a toddler near a busy road, before any thinking, is xin at work according to Mencius.","notConfused":"Not the same as xin 信, 'trustworthiness', a different Confucian virtue written with a different character.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Ren","Yi","Qi"],"thinkers":["mencius","wangyangming"],"tags":["hsin","heart-mind","heart","mind","mencius","trustworthiness"]},{"term":"Yi","def":"Confucian 'righteousness': the sense of what is fitting and right, and the commitment to do it even at a cost to oneself.","more":"Mencius placed yi beside ren (humaneness) as a core virtue, and said that if he had to choose between life and yi, he would give up life. Yi is often contrasted with li 利, profit or advantage: the gentleman thinks of what is right, the petty person of what pays. The Mohists also prized yi, but grounded it in benefit to all.","etym":"Chinese 義 yì.","example":"Returning extra change a shopkeeper gave you by mistake, even though no one would ever know.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Ren","Xin","Li (ritual propriety)"],"thinkers":["mencius","confucius","mozi"],"tags":["righteousness","rightness","justice","mencius","confucianism"]},{"term":"Yin and yang","def":"In Chinese thought, two complementary forces, such as dark and light or passive and active, whose interplay produces all change in the world.","more":"Yin is associated with darkness, cold, softness and receptivity; yang with light, heat, hardness and activity. Neither is good or bad: each contains the seed of the other, and each turns into the other, as night turns into day. The idea became part of Daoist, Neo-Confucian and medical thought.","etym":"Chinese 陰 yīn, 'shady side of a hill', and 陽 yáng, 'sunny side'.","example":"The seasons: at its height, summer begins turning towards winter, and winter in turn towards spring.","notConfused":"Not the same as good and evil: yin and yang are partners in balance, not enemies.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dao","Qi","Wu wei"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["yin yang","yin-yang","complementary opposites","taiji","duality","daoism"]},{"term":"Yoga","def":"In Indian philosophy, a discipline of body and mind that aims to still thought and free the self, and also the name of one of the six classical schools of Hindu philosophy.","more":"Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, whose date is uncertain but often placed in the early centuries CE, define yoga as the stilling of the movements of the mind. They set out eight limbs, from ethical restraints and posture to breath control, meditation and absorption. The aim is to separate the pure witnessing self from the changing mind and body. Modern Western yoga focuses mainly on posture, a small part of the original system.","etym":"Sanskrit yoga, 'yoking, joining, discipline'.","example":"Sitting still and bringing your attention back to the breath each time the mind wanders is a basic yogic exercise.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Atman","Samsara","Zazen"],"thinkers":["patanjali"],"tags":["yoga sutras","patanjali","meditation","union","samkhya"]},{"term":"Zazen","def":"Seated meditation, the central practice of Zen Buddhism.","more":"Practitioners sit upright, often on a cushion, attending to posture and breath and letting thoughts come and go without following them. Dōgen, founder of the Japanese Sōtō school, taught shikantaza, 'just sitting', and held that zazen is not a means to enlightenment but enlightenment itself expressed. The Rinzai school combines zazen with koan practice.","etym":"Japanese za, 'sitting', and zen, 'meditation'.","example":"Sitting facing a wall for twenty minutes, noticing your breath and letting worries pass by.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Zen","Satori","Yoga"],"thinkers":["dogen"],"tags":["seated meditation","zen meditation","meditation","shikantaza","dogen"]},{"term":"Zen","def":"A school of Mahayana Buddhism, developed in China as Chan and later in Japan, that stresses meditation and direct insight over the study of texts.","more":"Zen traces itself to Bodhidharma, a semi-legendary Indian monk said to have brought it to China around the fifth or sixth century CE. It teaches that awakening comes from seeing directly into one's own nature, through meditation, koans and the guidance of a teacher. Japanese thinkers such as Dōgen, and later Nishida Kitarō, drew philosophical ideas from it.","etym":"Japanese zen, from Chinese chan, from Sanskrit dhyana, 'meditation'.","example":"A teacher answers a student's abstract question with an odd, concrete reply, to jolt them out of conceptual thinking.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'zen' in casual English, meaning calm or relaxed, which captures only a small part of the tradition.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Zazen","Satori","Emptiness (śūnyatā)"],"thinkers":["dogen","nishida"],"tags":["chan","ch'an","zen buddhism","seon","meditation","satori"]},{"term":"Zeno's paradoxes","def":"Arguments by Zeno of Elea that motion is impossible, such as the claim that a runner can never finish a race, because they must first cover half the distance, then half of what remains, and so on forever.","more":"Zeno, in the fifth century BCE, defended Parmenides' view that change is an illusion. In the most famous paradox, swift Achilles can never overtake a tortoise with a head start, because whenever he reaches where it was, it has moved a little further on. Mathematicians later showed that an infinite series can add up to a finite total, which answers much of the puzzle, though philosophers still debate whether that fully resolves it.","etym":"Greek paradoxa, 'contrary to expectation'.","example":"To walk to the door you must first get halfway, then half of what is left, then half again, endlessly: so how do you ever arrive?","notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Reductio ad absurdum","Sorites paradox"],"thinkers":["zeno_elea","parmenides"],"tags":["zeno","achilles and the tortoise","dichotomy paradox","arrow paradox","motion","infinity"]},{"term":"Zhengming","def":"Confucian 'rectification of names': the idea that social order requires words and titles to match reality, so that a ruler truly acts as a ruler and a father as a father.","more":"In the Analects, Confucius says that if he governed, he would begin by rectifying names, because when names are not correct, speech does not flow and affairs fail. Xunzi devoted a chapter to it, arguing that names are fixed by convention but must be kept stable to prevent confusion. It links ethics, politics and language: calling something by the wrong name lets wrongs pass unnoticed.","etym":"Chinese 正名 zhèngmíng, 'correcting names'.","example":"A Confucian would be troubled by a company calling mass sackings 'rightsizing' to hide what they are.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Li (ritual propriety)","Ren","Sense and reference"],"thinkers":["confucius","xunzi"],"tags":["rectification of names","zheng ming","cheng ming","confucius","correct names"]},{"term":"Zombie","def":"A philosophical zombie is an imagined being physically identical to a human, behaving the same, but with no inner experience at all.","more":"David Chalmers argued in The Conscious Mind (1996) that if such zombies are conceivable, and therefore possible, then consciousness is something over and above the physical, and physicalism is false. Critics reply that zombies only seem conceivable, or that what we can conceive is not a reliable guide to what is possible.","etym":"Haitian Creole zonbi, probably of African origin; philosophers borrowed the horror-film term.","example":"Your zombie twin would say ‘ouch’ and wince, but there would be nothing it felt like.","notConfused":"Not the same as a film zombie: a philosophical zombie looks and behaves exactly like a normal person.","field":"Mind","related":["Hard problem","Qualia","Physicalism"],"thinkers":["chalmers"],"compass":"q6","tags":["philosophical zombie","p-zombie","zombie argument","chalmers","consciousness","physicalism"]},{"term":"Disjunctive syllogism","def":"The valid argument form: either P or Q; not P; therefore Q.","more":"It is one of the oldest named inference patterns, studied by the Stoic logicians. Sextus Empiricus reports a Stoic story that even a hunting dog uses it: reaching a fork, it sniffs two paths, finds no scent, and runs down the third without sniffing. The form is only as good as its disjunction: if there were really more options than the two listed, the argument becomes a false dilemma. Some relevance and paraconsistent logicians restrict it, because combined with contradictions it lets you prove anything.","etym":"Latin disiunctivus, 'separating', and Greek syllogismos, 'reckoning together'.","example":"The keys are either in my coat or in my bag. They're not in my coat. So they're in my bag.","notConfused":"Not the same as a false dilemma: disjunctive syllogism is valid, while a false dilemma wrongly assumes there are only two options.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Syllogism","False dilemma","Valid"],"thinkers":["sextus"],"tags":["modus tollendo ponens","either or","process of elimination","rule of inference","disjunction elimination"]},{"term":"Hypothetical syllogism","def":"The valid argument form that chains conditionals: if P then Q; if Q then R; therefore if P then R.","more":"It lets reasoning travel along a series of links, which is why it is sometimes called a chain argument. Ancient logicians, including the Stoics and Aristotle's successor Theophrastus, studied arguments built from conditionals. It is valid for the material conditional of classical logic, but philosophers have found apparent counterexamples with everyday 'if' statements and counterfactuals, which suggests ordinary conditionals are more complicated. Slippery slope arguments often use this chain shape, and they fail when one link is weak.","etym":"Greek hypothesis, 'supposition', and syllogismos, 'reckoning together'.","example":"If I miss the bus, I'll be late. If I'm late, I'll miss the briefing. So if I miss the bus, I'll miss the briefing.","notConfused":"Not the same as a slippery slope fallacy: the form is valid, but a slippery slope argument relies on links that are doubtful or exaggerated.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Conditional","Slippery slope","Modus ponens"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["chain argument","transitivity of implication","chain rule","if-then chain","rule of inference"]},{"term":"Contrapositive","def":"The statement 'if not Q then not P', which is logically equivalent to 'if P then Q'.","more":"Swapping and negating both parts of a conditional preserves its truth, so a claim and its contrapositive stand or fall together. Logicians use this constantly in proofs: to show that P leads to Q, it is often easier to show that the absence of Q rules out P. Modus tollens is essentially reasoning with a contrapositive. Hempel's paradox of the ravens arises because 'all ravens are black' is equivalent to 'all non-black things are non-ravens'.","etym":"Latin contra, 'against', and ponere, 'to place'.","example":"'If it's a square, it has four sides' has the contrapositive 'if it doesn't have four sides, it isn't a square'.","notConfused":"Not the same as the converse ('if Q then P'), which does not follow: not everything with four sides is a square.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Conditional","Modus tollens","Paradox of the ravens"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["contraposition","if not q then not p","modus tollens","converse","conditional"]},{"term":"Paradoxes of material implication","def":"Odd-seeming results of the logical 'if...then', such as that a false statement implies anything and a true statement is implied by anything.","more":"In classical logic, 'if P then Q' (the material conditional) is false only when P is true and Q is false. This means 'if the moon is cheese, then Paris is in France' comes out true, which strikes most people as absurd. C. I. Lewis developed 'strict implication' and modal logic in the 1910s partly in response. Others, following H. P. Grice, argue the conditional is fine and the oddness comes from conversational expectations; relevance logicians instead demand a real connection between the parts.","etym":null,"example":"Logically, 'If 2 + 2 = 5, then I am the Pope' is true simply because its first part is false.","notConfused":"Not paradoxes in the sense of contradictions: nothing inconsistent follows, the results merely clash with how we use 'if'.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Conditional","Modal logic","Principle of explosion"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["material implication","material conditional","vacuous truth","relevance logic","conditional"]},{"term":"Quantifier","def":"A logical expression such as 'all' or 'some' that says how many things a statement is about.","more":"Gottlob Frege introduced the modern treatment of quantifiers in his Begriffsschrift of 1879, and C. S. Peirce developed a similar notation independently. The universal quantifier ('for all x') and the existential quantifier ('there is an x') let logic handle sentences like 'everyone loves someone', which traditional syllogistic logic struggled with. Quine famously took the existential quantifier as the key to ontology: 'to be is to be the value of a variable'. The order of quantifiers matters a great deal, as the quantifier shift fallacy shows.","etym":"Latin quantus, 'how much'.","example":"'Every student passed' uses a universal quantifier; 'some student passed' uses an existential one.","notConfused":"Not a number word in the ordinary sense: 'three' can be expressed with quantifiers, but quantifiers themselves just mark 'all' or 'at least one'.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Predicate logic","Quantifier shift fallacy","Quinean ontology"],"thinkers":["frege","peirce","quine"],"tags":["quantifiers","universal quantifier","existential quantifier","for all","there exists","predicate logic"]},{"term":"Predicate logic","def":"The system of logic that analyses sentences into objects, properties and relations, using quantifiers such as 'all' and 'some'.","more":"Also called first-order logic, it was created by Frege in 1879 and became the standard logic of analytic philosophy and mathematics. Unlike propositional logic, it can see inside sentences, so it can explain why 'Socrates is a man; all men are mortal; so Socrates is mortal' is valid. Gödel proved in 1929 that it is complete: every valid argument in it can be proved. Higher-order logics, which quantify over properties as well as objects, are more powerful but more controversial.","etym":"Latin praedicare, 'to proclaim' or 'assert'.","example":"'Everyone has a mother' becomes, roughly, 'for every person x there is some y such that y is the mother of x'.","notConfused":"Not the same as propositional logic, which treats whole sentences as unanalysed units joined by 'and', 'or', 'not' and 'if'.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Propositional logic","Quantifier","Classical logic"],"thinkers":["frege","godel"],"tags":["first-order logic","quantificational logic","predicate calculus","fol","quantifiers"]},{"term":"Propositional logic","def":"The part of logic that studies how whole statements combine using words like 'and', 'or', 'not' and 'if...then'.","more":"It treats each simple sentence as a single unit that is true or false and asks how the truth of complex sentences depends on their parts. The Stoics, especially Chrysippus, developed an early version in ancient Greece; George Boole gave it an algebraic form in the 19th century. Validity in propositional logic can be checked mechanically with truth tables. It is the foundation of digital circuits, where on and off play the role of true and false.","etym":null,"example":"From 'it is raining and it is cold' you can infer 'it is raining', whatever 'raining' and 'cold' mean.","notConfused":"Not the same as predicate logic, which looks inside sentences at subjects, properties and quantifiers.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Truth table","Predicate logic","Classical logic"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["sentential logic","propositional calculus","truth-functional logic","zeroth-order logic","truth tables"]},{"term":"Modal logic","def":"Logic that deals with necessity and possibility, using operators that mean 'it is necessary that' and 'it is possible that'.","more":"C. I. Lewis developed modern modal logic from about 1912 onwards, and Saul Kripke and others gave it a possible-worlds semantics around 1960: a statement is necessary if it is true in every accessible possible world. Variants handle knowledge (epistemic logic), obligation (deontic logic) and time (tense logic, pioneered by Arthur Prior). Modal logic is central to debates about essence, God's existence (in modal ontological arguments) and the nature of possibility itself.","etym":"Latin modus, 'manner': modal statements say in what manner something is true.","example":"'2 + 2 = 4' is necessarily true, while 'it is raining in Oslo' is at most possibly true.","notConfused":"Not the same as modal realism, which is a metaphysical view about whether possible worlds really exist; modal logic is the formal system itself.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Possible-worlds semantics","Necessary/contingent","Modal realism"],"thinkers":["kripke","prior"],"tags":["necessity and possibility","possible worlds","kripke semantics","box and diamond","modality"]},{"term":"Logical form","def":"The underlying structure of a statement or argument that determines what follows from it, regardless of its subject matter.","more":"Two arguments with the same logical form are valid or invalid together, which is why logic can study form rather than content. Bertrand Russell argued that the grammatical form of a sentence can mislead about its logical form; his theory of descriptions showed that 'the present king of France is bald' is really a claim about existence. Wittgenstein's Tractatus credits Russell with showing that apparent form need not be real form. Much analytic philosophy began as the project of uncovering hidden logical forms.","etym":null,"example":"'All dogs are animals; Rex is a dog; so Rex is an animal' has the same logical form as 'all poets are mortal; Keats is a poet; so Keats is mortal'.","notConfused":"Not the same as grammatical form: 'nobody came' looks like a sentence about a person called Nobody but logically says no one came.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Valid","Russell’s theory of descriptions","Argument"],"thinkers":["russell","wittgenstein"],"tags":["form of an argument","argument form","formalisation","formalization","structure"]},{"term":"Completeness (logic)","def":"The property of a logical system in which every valid argument or logical truth can actually be proved using its rules.","more":"A system is sound if it proves only valid things and complete if it proves all of them. Kurt Gödel proved in his 1929 doctoral thesis that first-order predicate logic is complete, a major result. Two years later his incompleteness theorems showed that no consistent formal system for arithmetic can prove every arithmetical truth. The two results concern different kinds of completeness and are often confused.","etym":"Latin completus, 'filled up'.","example":"A complete set of chess rules for checkmates would let you derive every position that genuinely is checkmate.","notConfused":"Not the same as the completeness denied by Gödel's incompleteness theorems: that concerns proving all truths of arithmetic, not all logically valid arguments.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Gödel’s incompleteness theorems","Predicate logic","Sound"],"thinkers":["godel"],"tags":["completeness theorem","gödel's completeness theorem","godel completeness","soundness","provability"]},{"term":"Gödel’s incompleteness theorems","def":"Two 1931 results showing that any consistent formal system strong enough for basic arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove, and cannot prove its own consistency.","more":"Kurt Gödel constructed a sentence that, in effect, says 'this sentence is not provable in this system'; if the system is consistent, the sentence is true but unprovable. The second theorem dealt a heavy blow to Hilbert's programme, which hoped to prove mathematics consistent by finitary means. Some philosophers, notably J. R. Lucas and Roger Penrose, argued the theorems show human minds are not computers; most logicians reject that inference. The theorems are often misquoted as showing that 'everything is uncertain', which they do not.","etym":null,"example":"However many new axioms you add to a consistent rulebook for arithmetic, there will still be a true sum-like statement the rulebook can't prove.","notConfused":"Not a claim that mathematics is inconsistent or that truth is relative: the theorems are precise results about formal proof systems.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Completeness (logic)","Hilbert’s finitism","Anti-mechanism"],"thinkers":["godel","hilbert","penrose"],"tags":["godel's incompleteness theorems","incompleteness","gödel","godel","unprovable truths","consistency"]},{"term":"Principle of explosion","def":"The rule of classical logic that from a contradiction, any statement whatsoever follows.","more":"Its Latin name, ex falso quodlibet or ex contradictione quodlibet, comes from medieval logic. If both P and not-P are true, then 'P or Q' is true for any Q, and since not-P, Q follows by disjunctive syllogism. This is why logicians treat a single contradiction as disastrous for a theory. Paraconsistent logicians, such as Graham Priest, reject explosion so that inconsistent information (in a database or a legal code) need not lead to total nonsense.","etym":"Latin ex falso quodlibet, 'from a falsehood, anything (follows)'.","example":"If a story says both that the hero is alive and that the hero is not alive, classical logic would let you 'prove' that the hero is a teapot.","notConfused":"Not the same as the law of non-contradiction, which says contradictions are never true; explosion says what would follow if one were accepted.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Contradiction","Paraconsistent logic","Dialetheism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["ex falso quodlibet","ex contradictione quodlibet","explosion","contradiction","paraconsistent logic"]},{"term":"Square of opposition","def":"A diagram showing the logical relations between four kinds of statement: 'all S are P', 'no S are P', 'some S are P' and 'some S are not P'.","more":"It derives from Aristotle's On Interpretation and was drawn as a diagram by later writers, including Boethius, whose version shaped medieval teaching. It marks contradictories (which can't both be true or both false), contraries (which can't both be true) and other relations. Modern logic, following Frege and Boole, drops some of its relations because 'all S are P' is treated as true when there are no S at all. The debate over this 'existential import' is still discussed.","etym":null,"example":"'All swans are white' and 'some swans are not white' are contradictories: exactly one must be true.","notConfused":"Not the same as a truth table, which deals with sentence connectives rather than 'all' and 'some' statements.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Syllogism","Contradiction","Quantifier"],"thinkers":["aristotle","boethius"],"tags":["aristotelian square","contraries","contradictories","subalternation","categorical propositions"]},{"term":"Fuzzy logic","def":"A logic in which statements can be true to a degree, anywhere between fully true (1) and fully false (0).","more":"It grew out of Lotfi Zadeh's 1965 theory of fuzzy sets, in which things can belong to a set partly. Engineers use it widely in control systems such as washing machines and cameras. In philosophy it is one proposed solution to vagueness and the sorites paradox, since 'tall' or 'heap' seem to come in degrees. Critics argue it replaces one sharp boundary (between true and false) with many sharp boundaries (between 0.6 and 0.61 true).","etym":"English fuzzy, 'blurred'.","example":"A thermostat using fuzzy logic treats 19 degrees as 'somewhat cold' rather than either cold or not cold.","notConfused":"Not the same as probability: a 0.7 probability means you don't know whether it is true, while 0.7 truth means it is partly true.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Degree theory","Vagueness","Sorites paradox"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["degrees of truth","many-valued logic","zadeh","vagueness","partial truth"]},{"term":"Russell’s paradox","def":"The contradiction that arises from the set of all sets that are not members of themselves: it is a member of itself if and only if it isn't.","more":"Bertrand Russell discovered it in 1901 and wrote to Frege about it in 1902, just as the second volume of Frege's Basic Laws of Arithmetic was going to press; Frege saw that his system was inconsistent. The paradox showed that you cannot assume every description defines a set. Responses include Russell's theory of types and the axioms of modern set theory, which restrict how sets can be formed. A popular version involves a barber who shaves all and only those who do not shave themselves.","etym":null,"example":"A village barber shaves everyone who doesn't shave themselves: does he shave himself? Either answer contradicts the rule.","notConfused":"Not the same as the liar paradox, though both involve self-reference: Russell's concerns sets, the liar concerns truth.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Liar paradox","Logicism","Paradox"],"thinkers":["russell","frege"],"tags":["russell's paradox","russell","set of all sets","barber paradox","set theory","frege"]},{"term":"Enthymeme","def":"An argument with an unstated premise or conclusion that the audience is expected to fill in.","more":"Aristotle discussed enthymemes in his Rhetoric as the kind of reasoning used in persuasive speech. Most everyday arguments are enthymemes, because spelling out every premise would be tedious. Evaluating them means supplying the missing premise, ideally in a charitable way. Hidden premises are also where weak assumptions often hide, so making them explicit is a key critical-thinking skill.","etym":"Greek enthymēma, 'a thing kept in mind'.","example":"'He's a politician, so he's probably lying' leaves out the questionable premise 'politicians usually lie'.","notConfused":"Not the same as an invalid argument: once the missing premise is added, an enthymeme can be perfectly valid.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Premise","Principle of charity","Rhetoric"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["suppressed premise","hidden premise","missing premise","implicit premise","rhetoric"]},{"term":"Cogent argument","def":"An inductive argument that is strong (its premises make the conclusion likely) and whose premises are actually true.","more":"It is the inductive counterpart of a sound deductive argument. Strength comes in degrees, so cogency does too: an argument from a large, varied sample is more cogent than one from a few cases. Even a cogent argument can have a false conclusion, because induction never guarantees its result. Textbook usage varies, and some writers use 'cogent' more loosely to mean simply 'convincing'.","etym":"Latin cogere, 'to compel'.","example":"'Every summer for 30 years this beach has had jellyfish in August, so it probably will this August' is cogent if the record is accurate.","notConfused":"Not the same as a sound argument: soundness applies to deductive arguments and guarantees a true conclusion; cogency only makes it probable.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Sound","Induction","Hasty generalisation"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["cogency","strong inductive argument","induction","good argument"]},{"term":"Formal fallacy","def":"A mistake in reasoning that comes from the structure of an argument itself, so that its form is invalid.","more":"Classic examples are affirming the consequent and denying the antecedent. Because the error lies in the form, you can show it by finding another argument with the same form and true premises but a false conclusion. Informal fallacies, by contrast, depend on content or context, such as ambiguity, irrelevance or unwarranted assumptions. Pointing out a formal fallacy shows that the argument fails, not that its conclusion is false.","etym":"Latin fallacia, 'deceit'.","example":"'If it's a cat, it has fur; my dog has fur; so my dog is a cat' commits a formal fallacy (affirming the consequent).","notConfused":"Not the same as an informal fallacy, such as ad hominem, where the problem lies in what is said rather than its logical shape.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy","Affirming the consequent","Invalid"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["logical fallacy","invalid form","deductive fallacy","non sequitur","invalidity"]},{"term":"Fallacy of composition","def":"Wrongly concluding that what is true of the parts of something must be true of the whole.","more":"Aristotle discussed fallacies of composition and division in his Sophistical Refutations, though his examples are partly linguistic. Sometimes properties do transfer from parts to wholes (if every brick is red, the wall is red), so the fallacy is assuming they always do. Economists cite the 'paradox of thrift': it may be wise for each household to save more, but if everyone does, demand falls and all may be worse off. The fallacy matters in debates about emergence, where wholes seem to have properties their parts lack.","etym":null,"example":"'Every player on this team is brilliant, so the team must be brilliant' ignores whether they play well together.","notConfused":"The reverse of the fallacy of division, which moves from the whole to the parts.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy of division","Emergence","Hasty generalisation"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["composition fallacy","part to whole","parts and wholes","fallacy of division"]},{"term":"Fallacy of division","def":"Wrongly concluding that what is true of a whole or a group must be true of each of its parts or members.","more":"Aristotle paired it with the fallacy of composition. It often arises with statistics about groups: a fact about an average does not tell you about any individual. It can also feed prejudice, as when a trait thought typical of a group is pinned on a particular person. Like composition, it is a fallacy only because the inference doesn't hold in general; some properties really do pass from whole to part.","etym":null,"example":"'This orchestra is world-famous, so its second oboist must be world-famous too.'","notConfused":"The reverse of the fallacy of composition, which moves from the parts to the whole.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Fallacy of composition","Hasty generalisation","Equivocation"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["division fallacy","whole to part","parts and wholes","fallacy of composition"]},{"term":"Gambler’s fallacy","def":"The mistaken belief that, in a series of independent random events, a run of one outcome makes the other outcome more likely next time.","more":"A fair coin has no memory, so after five heads the chance of tails on the next toss is still one half. The fallacy was famously displayed at a Monte Carlo casino in 1913, when black came up many times in a row and gamblers lost heavily betting on red. Psychologists such as Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman linked it to a belief that small samples should look like the long-run average. It matters for philosophy of probability and for understanding how people reason under uncertainty.","etym":null,"example":"After a family has had four boys, thinking the next baby is 'due' to be a girl.","notConfused":"Not the same as reasonably suspecting a rigged coin after many heads: that questions whether the events are fair and independent in the first place.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Base-rate fallacy","Hasty generalisation","Induction"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["gambler's fallacy","monte carlo fallacy","due for a win","maturity of chances","probability"]},{"term":"Appeal to popularity","def":"The fallacy of arguing that a claim is true, or a choice right, simply because many or most people believe or do it.","more":"Its Latin name is argumentum ad populum. Popular belief is not worthless evidence: if most experts agree, or many independent observers report the same thing, that can be good reason to believe. The fallacy lies in treating popularity itself as settling the question, especially where people may simply be copying each other. History offers many once-popular beliefs that turned out false.","etym":"Latin argumentum ad populum, 'argument to the people'.","example":"'Millions of people use this supplement, so it must work.'","notConfused":"Not the same as an appeal to expert consensus, which can be good evidence because it rests on the experts' reasons.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Appeal to authority","Fallacy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["argumentum ad populum","ad populum","bandwagon fallacy","bandwagon","majority belief","everyone does it"]},{"term":"Loaded question","def":"A question that sneaks in an unproven assumption, so that any direct answer seems to accept it.","more":"It is also called the fallacy of many questions or complex question. Aristotle discussed the trick of combining several questions as one in his Sophistical Refutations. The right response is usually to challenge the assumption rather than answer yes or no. Loaded questions appear in interrogations, debates and badly designed surveys.","etym":null,"example":"'Have you stopped cheating at cards?' Answering either yes or no admits you used to cheat.","notConfused":"Not the same as begging the question, which assumes the conclusion in an argument; a loaded question builds an assumption into a question.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Begging the question","False dilemma","Fallacy"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["complex question","plurium interrogationum","trick question","presupposition","have you stopped beating your wife"]},{"term":"Base-rate fallacy","def":"The error of ignoring how common something is in general when judging how likely it is in a particular case.","more":"Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman showed in the 1970s how often people neglect base rates. If a disease affects 1 in 1,000 people and a test is 99% accurate, most people who test positive will still not have it, because false positives from the many healthy people outnumber the true positives. Bayes' theorem shows how base rates and new evidence should be combined. The fallacy matters in medicine, law and the debate about profiling.","etym":null,"example":"Assuming a quiet, bookish stranger is more likely a librarian than a salesperson, ignoring that salespeople vastly outnumber librarians.","notConfused":"Not the same as the gambler's fallacy, which misjudges independent events in a sequence rather than ignoring background frequencies.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Bayesianism","Gambler’s fallacy","Hasty generalisation"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["base rate fallacy","base rate neglect","prior probability","false positives","bayes"]},{"term":"Undistributed middle","def":"The syllogistic fallacy in which the shared middle term never refers to all members of its class, so the two premises fail to connect.","more":"In a valid syllogism, the middle term links the two others. 'All A are C; all B are C; so all A are B' fails because the premises only say A and B are both somewhere inside C. Medieval logicians developed the theory of 'distribution' to state rules like this precisely. The fallacy underlies many guilt-by-association arguments.","etym":null,"example":"'All cats are mammals; all dogs are mammals; therefore all cats are dogs.'","notConfused":"Not the same as an ambiguous middle term (equivocation), where the middle term changes meaning between premises.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Syllogism","Formal fallacy","Equivocation"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["fallacy of the undistributed middle","undistributed middle term","syllogistic fallacy","formal fallacy"]},{"term":"Cum hoc ergo propter hoc","def":"The fallacy of concluding that because two things occur together, one must cause the other.","more":"It is the basis of the slogan 'correlation is not causation'. Two things can be correlated because a third factor causes both, because the causation runs the other way, or by coincidence. Scientists use controlled experiments and statistical methods to tell genuine causes from mere correlations. Philosophers of causation, from Hume onward, ask what more there is to causation than regular co-occurrence.","etym":"Latin, 'with this, therefore because of this'.","example":"Ice-cream sales and drownings rise together, but ice cream doesn't cause drowning: hot weather increases both.","notConfused":"Not the same as post hoc ergo propter hoc, which infers causation from one thing following another in time rather than from their occurring together.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Post hoc ergo propter hoc","Causation","Mill’s methods"],"thinkers":["hume"],"tags":["correlation is not causation","correlation implies causation","false cause","cum hoc","spurious correlation"]},{"term":"Motte-and-bailey","def":"An argumentative tactic of defending a bold, controversial claim by retreating, when challenged, to a modest claim that is easy to defend, then returning to the bold one.","more":"The philosopher Nicholas Shackel named it in 2005, comparing it to a medieval castle: the bailey is the desirable but exposed land, the motte the defensible tower. Once the critic gives up on attacking the motte, the arguer goes back to occupying the bailey. Spotting it requires noticing that two different claims are being run together under one phrase. It is related to equivocation, since the ambiguity of a word often makes the switch possible.","etym":"Norman French motte, 'mound', and bailey, 'enclosed courtyard'.","example":"Claiming 'astrology controls your destiny', then, when challenged, saying 'I only mean the stars influence the tides'.","notConfused":"Not the same as a straw man, which distorts an opponent's view; motte-and-bailey shifts between two versions of one's own view.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Equivocation","Straw man","Fallacy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["motte and bailey","bait and switch","equivocation","shifting claims","strategic retreat"]},{"term":"Quantifier shift fallacy","def":"The error of moving from 'everything has some X' to 'there is some X that everything has'.","more":"It confuses the order of 'every' and 'some', which in logic makes a big difference. Several famous philosophical arguments have been accused of it: some readers find it in the opening of Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics (every action aims at some good, so there is a good at which all actions aim), and some versions of the cosmological argument have been read the same way. Modern predicate logic, with its explicit quantifiers, makes the error easy to see.","etym":null,"example":"'Everyone has a mother, so there is someone who is everyone's mother.'","notConfused":"Not the same as the fallacy of composition, which moves from parts to wholes rather than swapping the order of 'every' and 'some'.","field":"Logic and argument","related":["Quantifier","Predicate logic","Cosmological argument"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["scope fallacy","quantifier scope","every-some fallacy","illicit quantifier shift"]},{"term":"Haecceity","def":"The 'thisness' of a thing: a property that makes it the very individual it is, rather than merely a thing of its kind.","more":"The medieval philosopher John Duns Scotus introduced the idea to explain what makes one individual distinct from another of exactly the same kind. In modern metaphysics Robert Merrihew Adams defended 'primitive thisness' in a 1979 paper, arguing that two worlds could differ only in which individual is which. Critics, often bundle theorists, deny that there is anything to an individual beyond its qualities and relations. The debate connects to the identity of indiscernibles and to questions about possible worlds.","etym":"Medieval Latin haecceitas, from haec, 'this'.","example":"Two identical new coins from the same mint share every quality, yet each has its own thisness that makes it this coin and not that one.","notConfused":"Not the same as quiddity or essence, which is what kind of thing something is; haecceity is which particular thing it is.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Principle of individuation","Identity of indiscernibles","Bundle theory"],"thinkers":["adams_rm"],"tags":["thisness","haecceitas","duns scotus","individuation","primitive identity"]},{"term":"Truthmaker","def":"Whatever in the world makes a true statement true, such as a fact, object or state of affairs.","more":"The idea is that truth depends on reality: 'the cat is on the mat' is true because of the cat's being on the mat. D. M. Armstrong developed truthmaker theory at length, and Kevin Mulligan, Peter Simons and Barry Smith wrote an influential 1984 paper on it. Hard cases include negative truths ('there are no unicorns') and truths about the past, which some say causes trouble for presentism. Truthmaker maximalism, the claim that every truth has a truthmaker, is disputed.","etym":null,"example":"The truthmaker for 'Paris is in France' is simply Paris's being located in France.","notConfused":"Not the same as the correspondence theory of truth, which defines what truth is; truthmaker theory only claims that truths depend on what exists.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Correspondence theory of truth","Presentism without truthmakers","Grounding"],"thinkers":["armstrong"],"tags":["truthmaker theory","truth-maker","truthmaking","what makes true","armstrong","correspondence"]},{"term":"Grounding","def":"A relation of metaphysical dependence in which some facts hold in virtue of other, more fundamental facts.","more":"Grounding became a major topic in the 2000s through work by Kit Fine, Jonathan Schaffer and Gideon Rosen. It is meant to capture the 'because' in claims such as 'the ball is coloured because it is red' or 'the set exists because its members do', which is not causal. Defenders think it gives metaphysics a precise way to talk about what is more fundamental. Sceptics doubt that one single relation covers all these cases.","etym":"English ground, used in the sense of 'basis' or 'reason'.","example":"A football match being exciting is grounded in particular players running, passing and shooting in particular ways.","notConfused":"Not the same as causation: grounding is a timeless dependence between facts, not one event bringing about another.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Fundamentality theory","Supervenience","Truthmaker"],"thinkers":["kit_fine","schaffer"],"tags":["metaphysical grounding","in virtue of","ontological dependence","fundamentality","supervenience"]},{"term":"Conatus","def":"A thing's striving or tendency to keep itself in existence and continue in its own way of being.","more":"Spinoza made it central to his Ethics (1677): 'each thing, as far as it can by its own power, strives to persevere in its being'. For Spinoza, a thing's conatus is its very essence, and human desire is conatus made conscious. Hobbes used the term for the small beginnings of motion behind appetite and aversion. The idea has been compared to self-preservation in biology.","etym":"Latin conatus, 'effort' or 'striving'.","example":"A plant turning toward the light and a person fighting off illness both express a conatus to keep going.","notConfused":"Not the same as Nietzsche's will to power, which aims at growth and mastery rather than simply continuing to exist.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Will to power","Essence","Pantheism"],"thinkers":["spinoza","hobbes"],"tags":["spinoza","striving","self-preservation","endeavour","hobbes"]},{"term":"Monad","def":"In Leibniz's philosophy, a simple, indivisible, mind-like substance; the ultimate building blocks of reality.","more":"Leibniz set out the theory in his Monadology (1714). Monads have no parts and no spatial extension; each has perceptions and appetites, and each mirrors the whole universe from its own point of view. Famously, monads 'have no windows': they do not causally interact, but God has arranged them in a pre-established harmony. The word was also used earlier by Pythagoreans and Neoplatonists for the number one or the source of all things.","etym":"Greek monas, 'unit'.","example":"On Leibniz's view, your mind is a dominant monad, and even the tiniest speck of matter is made of countless perceiving monads.","notConfused":"Not the same as an atom in Democritus' sense: atoms are tiny bits of matter, while monads are non-material and perceive.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Pre-established harmony","Substance","Atomism"],"thinkers":["leibniz"],"tags":["monads","monadology","leibniz","simple substance","windowless"]},{"term":"Principle of plenitude","def":"The principle that every genuine possibility is realised at some time or place: whatever can exist, does.","more":"The historian of ideas Arthur Lovejoy named it in The Great Chain of Being (1936) and traced it to Plato's Timaeus and through Neoplatonism, medieval thought and Leibniz. It was used to argue that a good creator would make a world full of every possible kind of creature. A modal version says that if something is never true, it is impossible. David Lewis's modal realism has been seen as a modern relative, since every possibility exists in some world.","etym":"Latin plenitudo, 'fullness'.","example":"Someone who believes in plenitude might reason that, in an infinite universe, every possible kind of planet must exist somewhere.","notConfused":"Not the same as modal realism, which says possibilities exist in other worlds rather than being realised in this one.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Modal realism","Neoplatonism","Possible worlds"],"thinkers":["plato","plotinus","leibniz"],"tags":["plenitude","great chain of being","lovejoy","all possibilities realised","possibility"]},{"term":"Causa sui","def":"Something that is its own cause, depending on nothing outside itself for its existence.","more":"Spinoza opens his Ethics by defining causa sui as 'that whose essence involves existence', and he identifies it with God or Nature. Many theologians preferred to say God is uncaused rather than self-caused, since causing oneself seems to require existing before one exists. Nietzsche mocked the idea in Beyond Good and Evil as 'the best self-contradiction', and attacked the related wish to be the ultimate cause of one's own will. The idea returns in arguments that ultimate moral responsibility is impossible.","etym":"Latin, 'cause of itself'.","example":"Asking 'what caused the universe?' and answering 'nothing outside it; it explains itself' treats the universe as causa sui.","notConfused":"Not the same as an uncaused cause, such as Aristotle's unmoved mover, which simply has no cause rather than causing itself.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Unmoved mover","Cosmological argument","Impossibility of ultimate responsibility"],"thinkers":["spinoza","nietzsche"],"tags":["self-caused","cause of itself","spinoza","god","uncaused cause"]},{"term":"Potentiality and actuality","def":"Aristotle's distinction between what a thing is capable of becoming or doing and what it actually is or does now.","more":"Aristotle developed it, especially in Metaphysics Book Theta, partly to answer Parmenides' argument that change is impossible: change is not something coming from nothing, but potential becoming actual. An acorn is potentially an oak; a sleeping mathematician is potentially solving equations. Aquinas used the distinction heavily, describing God as pure actuality with no unrealised potential. The modern study of dispositions and powers revives some of these ideas.","etym":"Greek dunamis, 'power', and energeia, 'being at work', translated into Latin potentia and actus.","example":"A block of marble is potentially a statue; once carved, it is actually one.","notConfused":"Not the same as possible and actual in modal logic: Aristotle's potentiality is a real capacity in a thing, not just a logical possibility.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Entelechy","Disposition","Four causes"],"thinkers":["aristotle","aquinas"],"tags":["potentiality","actuality","potency and act","dunamis","energeia","aristotle"]},{"term":"Entelechy","def":"The condition of a thing that has fully realised its potential, or the inner principle that drives it toward that realisation.","more":"Aristotle coined the term and defined the soul as the first entelechy of a living body. Leibniz later called his monads entelechies because they contain their own source of activity. In the early 20th century the biologist Hans Driesch used it for a vital force guiding embryo development, a use most scientists rejected. Today it mainly appears in histories of philosophy and discussions of teleology.","etym":"Greek entelecheia, from en, 'in', telos, 'end', and echein, 'to have': 'having one's end within'.","example":"A fully grown oak tree, in Aristotle's terms, is the entelechy of the acorn.","notConfused":"Not the same as telos, which is the goal itself; entelechy is the state of having reached it or the principle that aims at it.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Potentiality and actuality","Telos","Vitalism"],"thinkers":["aristotle","leibniz"],"tags":["entelecheia","actuality","realised potential","aristotle","vital force","telos"]},{"term":"Categories","def":"The most general kinds or classes into which everything that exists, or everything we can think about, can be sorted.","more":"Aristotle listed ten categories, including substance, quantity, quality, relation, place and time, treating them as the basic types of being. Kant instead proposed twelve categories of the understanding, such as cause and unity, which he took to be concepts the mind must apply to any experience. Later philosophers, including Gilbert Ryle, used the idea of category differences to diagnose confusions. Modern ontology still debates which categories, if any, are fundamental.","etym":"Greek katēgoria, 'accusation' or 'predication', from katēgorein, 'to speak against' or 'to assert'.","example":"'Five metres' falls under quantity, 'red' under quality and 'yesterday' under time in Aristotle's scheme.","notConfused":"Not the same as a category mistake, which is an error of putting something in the wrong category.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Category mistake","Substance","Ontology"],"thinkers":["aristotle","kant"],"tags":["aristotle's categories","kant's categories","kinds of being","classification","ontological categories"]},{"term":"Intrinsic and extrinsic properties","def":"An intrinsic property is one a thing has in itself, regardless of anything else; an extrinsic property depends on its relations to other things.","more":"The distinction seems obvious but is hard to define precisely. David Lewis worked on it in the 1980s, and Rae Langton and Lewis proposed a well-known definition in 1998 based on whether a property could be had by something alone in the universe. It matters for debates about value, causation and personal identity. Some argue that even mass or shape is less intrinsic than it appears.","etym":"Latin intrinsecus, 'inwardly', and extrinsecus, 'outwardly'.","example":"Your height is intrinsic; being the tallest person in the room is extrinsic, since it depends on who else is there.","notConfused":"Not the same as intrinsic value, which concerns what is good for its own sake; intrinsic properties are about what a thing is like in itself.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Intrinsic value","Essence","Accident (property)"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"tags":["intrinsic properties","extrinsic properties","relational properties","duplicates","lewis"]},{"term":"Temporal parts","def":"Parts of an object that exist at different times, just as spatial parts exist in different places.","more":"Those who believe in temporal parts say an object such as a person stretches through time like a worm, with a childhood part and an adult part. Quine, David Lewis and Theodore Sider (in Four-Dimensionalism, 2001) have defended such views. The idea solves some puzzles of change and coincidence, but critics find it strange to say that only a part of you is reading this sentence. It is the central claim of perdurantism and stage theory.","etym":null,"example":"The 2010 part of a city and the 2020 part of the same city are its temporal parts, like scenes in a film.","notConfused":"Not the same as stages in the everyday sense of phases of life: temporal parts are meant to be literal parts of an object.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Perdurantism","Endurantism","Stage theory"],"thinkers":["quine","lewis","sider"],"tags":["perdurance","four-dimensionalism","perdurantism","time slices","persistence"]},{"term":"Problem of the many","def":"The puzzle that any ordinary object, such as a cloud or a cat, seems to have many equally good candidates for being it, differing only slightly at the edges.","more":"Peter Unger presented it in a 1980 paper; Peter Geach gave a related example the same year about a cat and its hairs. Since a cloud has no sharp boundary, many slightly different collections of droplets seem equally qualified to be the cloud, so there seem to be many clouds where we count one. Responses include treating it as a problem of vagueness, accepting that there are many overlapping objects, or denying that ordinary objects exist. It links questions of vagueness with questions of material composition.","etym":null,"example":"Removing one hair at the edge of a cat leaves something with an equally good claim to be the cat; so how many cats are on the mat?","notConfused":"Not the same as the sorites paradox, which concerns gradual change over a series; the problem of the many concerns many candidates at a single moment.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Vagueness","Sorites paradox","Mereology"],"thinkers":["unger"],"tags":["unger","vagueness","cloud","composition","how many cats"]},{"term":"Special composition question","def":"The question: under what conditions do several things together make up a further single thing?","more":"Peter van Inwagen posed it in this form in Material Beings (1990). Answers range from mereological nihilism (they never do; there are only simples) to universalism (they always do, so any collection, however scattered, makes an object). Van Inwagen's own answer is that things compose something only when their activity constitutes a life, so organisms exist but chairs and tables do not. Common sense wants a middle answer, but it is hard to state one that is not arbitrary.","etym":null,"example":"Do your nose and the Eiffel Tower together form a single object? Universalists say yes; nihilists say there aren't even noses.","notConfused":"Not the same as the question of personal identity over time, which asks when an object at one time is the same as an object at another.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Mereology","Mereological nihilism","Mereological universalism"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen"],"tags":["composition","mereology","when do parts make a whole","van inwagen","mereological nihilism"]},{"term":"Counterpart theory","def":"David Lewis's view that each thing exists in only one possible world, and that what it could have done depends on its counterparts: similar individuals in other worlds.","more":"Lewis introduced it in 1968 to go with his belief in many concrete possible worlds. On this view, 'Humphrey could have won the election' is true because someone very like Humphrey wins in another world. Saul Kripke objected that Humphrey cares about whether he himself wins, not whether someone else similar to him does. Counterpart theory allows similarity to vary with context, which some see as a strength.","etym":null,"example":"There is a possible world where someone just like you became a chef; that counterpart makes it true that you could have been a chef.","notConfused":"Not the same as transworld identity, the view that one and the same individual exists in many worlds.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Modal realism","Transworld identity","Possible worlds"],"thinkers":["lewis","kripke"],"tags":["counterparts","david lewis","modal realism","transworld identity","possible worlds"]},{"term":"Transworld identity","def":"The idea that one and the same individual exists in more than one possible world.","more":"If we say Socrates could have been a carpenter, it seems that Socrates himself exists in a world where he is a carpenter. Roderick Chisholm raised puzzles in 1967 about how we could identify an individual across worlds if everything about it might differ. Kripke replied that possible worlds are stipulated, not discovered through a telescope, so there is no special problem of identification. Lewis rejected transworld identity in favour of counterpart theory.","etym":null,"example":"Saying 'I could have been born in Brazil' treats you as existing in another possible world under different circumstances.","notConfused":"Not the same as identity over time, which concerns the same thing at different times in one world.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Counterpart theory","Rigid designator","Possible worlds"],"thinkers":["chisholm","kripke","plantinga"],"tags":["trans-world identity","cross-world identity","possible worlds","counterpart theory","kripke"]},{"term":"Actualism","def":"The view that everything that exists is actual: there are no merely possible things.","more":"Actualists deny that there are unrealised possible people or objects in any sense. They often treat possible worlds as abstract descriptions or states of affairs, as Alvin Plantinga and Robert Merrihew Adams did, rather than concrete places. The rival view, possibilism, says that merely possible things also exist in some sense, as in Lewis's modal realism. The debate turns on how to make sense of statements like 'there could have been a person who doesn't exist'.","etym":"Latin actualis, 'active' or 'real'.","example":"An actualist says that the child you might have had does not exist at all; there is only a true description of how things could have been.","notConfused":"Not the same as presentism, which says only present things exist; actualism concerns the actual versus the merely possible, not present versus past.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Modal realism","Ersatzism","Possible worlds"],"thinkers":["plantinga","adams_rm"],"tags":["actualist","only actual things exist","possible worlds","possibilism","modal realism"]},{"term":"Humean supervenience","def":"David Lewis's thesis that everything about the world depends on nothing more than the local distribution of particular qualities across space and time.","more":"Lewis named and defended it in the 1980s, drawing on Hume's denial of necessary connections between distinct events. On this view, laws of nature, causation and chances are not extra ingredients but are fixed by the overall pattern of events. Critics argue that quantum entanglement involves features that are not local, and that laws seem to govern events rather than summarise them. It is closely tied to the best-system account of laws.","etym":null,"example":"Like a mosaic: once every tile's colour and position is fixed, the whole picture, including any patterns in it, is fixed too.","notConfused":"Not the same as supervenience in general, which is any relation of 'no difference in A without a difference in B'.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Supervenience","Best system account of laws","Regularity theory of causation"],"thinkers":["lewis","hume"],"tags":["david lewis","supervenience","mosaic","local qualities","physicalism","laws of nature"]},{"term":"Fission","def":"A thought experiment in which one person splits into two people who are each psychologically continuous with the original.","more":"David Wiggins discussed such cases in 1967, and Derek Parfit made them famous in 'Personal Identity' (1971) and Reasons and Persons (1984). If each of your brain hemispheres were transplanted into a different body, both resulting people would remember your life, but they cannot both be you. Parfit concluded that identity is not what matters in survival; what matters is psychological continuity, which can hold to two people at once. The case is a key challenge for psychological theories of personal identity.","etym":"Latin fissio, 'splitting'.","example":"Like an amoeba dividing: if you split into Lefty and Righty, which one is you?","notConfused":"Not the same as teletransportation, which involves a single copy; fission produces two equally good candidates.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Personal identity","Non-branching view","Teletransportation"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"tags":["personal identity","splitting","brain bisection","derek parfit","survival","duplication"]},{"term":"Teletransportation","def":"A thought experiment in which a machine records your body's exact structure, destroys it, and builds a perfect copy elsewhere, raising the question of whether you survive.","more":"Derek Parfit used it in Reasons and Persons (1984) to test our beliefs about personal identity. Many people feel the replica would not really be them but a new person with false memories; others think the copy has everything that matters. Parfit varied the case: if the machine fails to destroy the original, there seem to be two people, which suggests the replica is not you. The scenario is familiar from science fiction, such as Star Trek's transporter.","etym":"Greek tēle, 'far', and Latin transportare, 'to carry across'.","example":"Would you step into a machine that disassembles you in London and builds an exact copy in Tokyo?","notConfused":"Not the same as travel: the matter isn't moved, only the information, so the question is whether pattern alone is enough for survival.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Personal identity","Psychological continuity","Fission"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"tags":["teleportation","teleporter","star trek","personal identity","derek parfit","duplication"]},{"term":"Third man argument","def":"An objection to Plato's Forms: if large things share a Form of Largeness, the large things plus the Form seem to need a further Form, and so on forever.","more":"Plato raises the objection himself in the Parmenides; the name comes from Aristotle, who used a version with the Form of Man. The argument depends on assuming that the Form has the property it explains (self-predication) and that nothing is explained by itself. Scholars still debate whether Plato thought the objection fatal. It is a classic example of an infinite regress argument.","etym":null,"example":"If tall people are tall because they share in Tallness, and Tallness is itself tall, what do Tallness and the tall people share?","notConfused":"Not the same as the 'third man' in the film noir sense; the name refers to a third 'Man' (a further Form) beyond individual men and the Form of Man.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Forms","Infinite regress","Platonism about universals"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle"],"tags":["tma","infinite regress","plato's forms","theory of forms","parmenides","regress argument"]},{"term":"Ex nihilo nihil fit","def":"The principle that nothing comes from nothing: whatever exists or begins must come from something.","more":"Parmenides and other early Greek thinkers relied on it, and Lucretius made it the first principle of his poem On the Nature of Things. It underlies many versions of the cosmological argument: if the universe began, something must have caused it. Christian theology's doctrine of creation out of nothing (creatio ex nihilo) holds that God made the world without pre-existing material, while still treating God as the cause. Some physicists argue that quantum theory allows things to arise from 'nothing', though critics say a quantum vacuum is not truly nothing.","etym":"Latin, 'nothing comes from nothing'.","example":"A detective assumes the muddy footprints must have come from somewhere; they cannot just have appeared.","notConfused":"Not the same as the principle of sufficient reason, which says everything has an explanation, not merely a material or causal source.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Principle of sufficient reason","Cosmological argument","Kalam"],"thinkers":["parmenides","lucretius"],"tags":["nothing comes from nothing","creation from nothing","ex nihilo","principle of sufficient reason","causation"]},{"term":"Grandfather paradox","def":"The puzzle that if you travelled back in time and killed your grandfather before your parent was born, you would never exist to make the trip.","more":"It is the best-known argument that backward time travel is impossible. David Lewis replied in 'The Paradoxes of Time Travel' (1976) that the time traveller can kill grandfather in one sense (she has the ability) but cannot in another (it is inconsistent with what already happened), so something will always prevent it. Others appeal to branching timelines or many worlds, so the traveller changes a different history. The paradox shows how time travel stories must respect consistency.","etym":null,"example":"Tim travels to 1921 with a rifle to kill his grandfather, but something, perhaps a slipped foot, must stop him.","notConfused":"Not the same as the bootstrap paradox, in which an object or piece of information has no origin because it loops through time.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Fatalism","Many-worlds interpretation","Paradox"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"tags":["time travel","time travel paradox","causal loop","backward causation","temporal paradox"]},{"term":"Why is there something rather than nothing?","def":"The question of why any world exists at all, rather than there being nothing whatsoever.","more":"Leibniz posed it explicitly in 1714, answering that God is the sufficient reason for existence. Heidegger called it the fundamental question of metaphysics in his 1935 lectures. Some philosophers think it has no answer, or that 'nothing' is not a genuine alternative; others argue that a necessary being, or simple probability (there are many ways for there to be something but only one way for there to be nothing), explains it. Scientific cosmology can describe the early universe but arguably cannot answer the question.","etym":null,"example":"Looking up at the night sky and wondering not how the stars formed, but why there is anything to form at all.","notConfused":"Not the same as asking what caused the Big Bang: that question assumes something already exists to do the causing.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Principle of sufficient reason","Cosmological argument","Brute facts view"],"thinkers":["leibniz","heidegger"],"tags":["why anything exists","existence of the world","leibniz","principle of sufficient reason","nothingness","primordial existence question"]},{"term":"Disposition","def":"A property that consists in a tendency to behave in a certain way under certain conditions, such as fragility or solubility.","more":"A glass is fragile even if it never breaks; its fragility is about what would happen if it were struck. Philosophers debate whether dispositions are real properties or simply grounded in a thing's structure, and whether they can be defined by conditional statements. C. B. Martin's 'finkish' cases, in which a disposition would vanish the moment its trigger occurred, challenged simple conditional definitions. Some philosophers, such as Stephen Mumford, argue dispositions or powers are the basic features of reality.","etym":"Latin dispositio, 'arrangement'.","example":"Sugar is soluble even while sitting dry in the bowl: it would dissolve if put in water.","notConfused":"Not the same as a disposition in the sense of temperament (a cheerful disposition), though that is a psychological case of the same idea.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Dispositional essentialism","Powers theory of causation","Potentiality and actuality"],"thinkers":["mumford","goodman"],"tags":["dispositional property","power","capacity","tendency","propensity","fragility"]},{"term":"Arche","def":"In early Greek philosophy, the first principle or basic stuff from which everything comes and of which everything is made.","more":"Aristotle, in Metaphysics Book A, described the earliest philosophers as seeking an arche. Thales proposed water; Anaximander the apeiron, the boundless; Anaximenes air; Heraclitus emphasised fire. This search for a single underlying principle is often seen as the beginning of Western philosophy and science, because it replaced mythological explanations with natural ones. The word also means 'rule', as in 'monarchy' and 'anarchy'.","etym":"Greek archē, 'beginning', 'origin' or 'rule'.","example":"Thales looking at rivers, rain and living things and concluding that everything is ultimately water.","notConfused":"Not the same as an archetype in Jung's sense, a recurring image in the human psyche.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Pre-Socratics","Monism","Logos"],"thinkers":["thales","aristotle","heraclitus"],"tags":["arkhe","arché","first principle","origin","presocratics","basic stuff","thales"]},{"term":"Principle of individuation","def":"Whatever it is that makes an individual thing the particular individual it is and distinct from others of the same kind.","more":"Medieval philosophers debated it intensely. Aquinas held that matter, considered with its dimensions, individuates things of the same species; Duns Scotus proposed haecceity; others said each thing is individual by itself. Locke discussed it in his chapter on identity, saying that existence at a particular time and place individuates. Schopenhauer used the Latin phrase principium individuationis for space and time as what splits the one will into many things.","etym":"Latin principium individuationis.","example":"Two copies of the same book have the same words and design; what makes this copy different from that one?","notConfused":"Not the same as a criterion of identity over time, which asks what makes something the same thing later; individuation asks what distinguishes it now.","field":"Metaphysics","related":["Haecceity","Identity of indiscernibles","Substratum theory"],"thinkers":["aquinas","locke","schopenhauer"],"tags":["individuation","haecceity","thisness","identity","particularity","bundle theory"]},{"term":"Defeater","def":"A piece of evidence or reasoning that removes or weakens the justification you had for a belief.","more":"John Pollock distinguished two kinds: a rebutting defeater gives reason to think the belief is false, while an undercutting defeater attacks the link between your evidence and the belief without showing the belief false. Defeaters explain why justification can be lost when new information arrives. Alvin Plantinga's evolutionary argument against naturalism claims that naturalism provides a defeater for all of a naturalist's beliefs. 'Defeasibility' theories of knowledge say you know only if there is no undefeated defeater for your belief.","etym":"Old French desfaire, 'to undo'.","example":"You believe the wall is red because it looks red; learning that it is lit by a red lamp undercuts that reason.","notConfused":"Not the same as a counterexample, which shows a general claim false; a defeater can simply remove support for a belief without disproving it.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Defeasible","Defeasibility theory","Justification"],"thinkers":["plantinga"],"tags":["undercutting defeater","rebutting defeater","defeasibility","counterevidence","justification"]},{"term":"Testimony","def":"Coming to believe something because someone else told you, in speech or writing.","more":"Most of what we know, from history to our own birthdays, rests on testimony. Hume argued that we are justified in trusting testimony only because experience shows it is generally reliable, a reductionist view. Thomas Reid instead held that we have a natural, basic entitlement to trust others unless there is reason not to. C. A. J. Coady's Testimony (1992) revived the debate, and recent work connects it to epistemic injustice and trust in experts.","etym":"Latin testimonium, from testis, 'witness'.","example":"You know Australia exists even if you've never been there, because many sources have told you so.","notConfused":"Not limited to courtroom testimony: in epistemology it covers any knowledge passed on by others' word, including books and websites.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Reductionism about testimony","Anti-reductionism about testimony","Epistemic injustice"],"thinkers":["hume","reid"],"tags":["word of others","hearsay","trust","social epistemology","testimonial knowledge","reports"]},{"term":"Lottery paradox","def":"The puzzle that it seems rational to believe of each lottery ticket that it will lose, yet irrational to believe that all tickets will lose when one must win.","more":"Henry Kyburg introduced it in 1961. If rational belief means believing whatever is highly probable, then you should believe each ticket loses, and if rational beliefs can be combined, you should believe no ticket wins, which you know is false. Responses include denying that high probability is enough for belief, denying that rational beliefs always combine, or thinking in terms of degrees of belief instead. Many also hold that you cannot know your ticket will lose, however likely it is.","etym":null,"example":"With a million tickets, you're almost certain your ticket will lose, but does that mean you know you won't win?","notConfused":"Not the same as the preface paradox, which involves beliefs you hold on the basis of evidence about each claim rather than pure probability.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Preface paradox","Credence","Bayesian epistemology"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["kyburg","rational belief","probability","lottery","high probability belief","closure"]},{"term":"Preface paradox","def":"The puzzle that an author can rationally believe each claim in her book while also rationally believing that at least one claim in it is false.","more":"D. C. Makinson presented it in 1965. Authors often write in a preface that any errors remaining are their own, and this modesty seems perfectly reasonable. Yet the set of beliefs, each claim plus 'at least one claim is false', cannot all be true. The paradox challenges the idea that rational beliefs must be consistent with each other.","etym":null,"example":"A historian checks each of 400 facts carefully, but given her past record she reasonably expects a few mistakes slipped through.","notConfused":"Not the same as the lottery paradox: here each belief is based on specific evidence rather than on a statistical chance.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Lottery paradox","Fallibilism","Contradiction"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["makinson","inconsistent beliefs","rational belief","fallibility","humility","closure"]},{"term":"Fake barn cases","def":"Thought experiments in which someone correctly identifies a real barn in an area full of convincing fake barn facades, raising the question of whether they know it is a barn.","more":"Alvin Goldman presented the case in 1976, crediting it to Carl Ginet. Henry looks at the one real barn and truly believes 'that's a barn', but he could easily have been fooled by a fake. Most people judge he does not know, which supports theories requiring knowledge to be safe from easy error or produced by a reliable process in the environment. Some experimental philosophers have found that ordinary people's intuitions about the case are less uniform than philosophers assumed.","etym":null,"example":"Buying the only genuine designer bag at a market full of near-perfect fakes: you're right that it's real, but did you know it?","notConfused":"Not the same as a classic Gettier case, in which the believer's evidence is itself misleading; in fake barn cases the perception is fine, but the environment is risky.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Gettier problem","Safety theory of knowledge","Process reliabilism"],"thinkers":["goldman","ginet"],"tags":["fake barns","barn facade county","goldman","gettier problem","epistemic luck","reliabilism"]},{"term":"Epistemic luck","def":"Luck that makes a belief come out true in a way that seems to prevent it from being knowledge.","more":"Gettier cases show that a justified true belief can be true by luck, and most philosophers think such luck rules out knowledge. Duncan Pritchard distinguished veritic luck, which undermines knowledge, from harmless kinds, such as being lucky to have the evidence at all. Anti-luck epistemology tries to state exactly which conditions remove the bad kind of luck. Debates over safety and sensitivity are largely about how to do this.","etym":"Greek epistēmē, 'knowledge'.","example":"Glancing at a stopped clock that happens to show the right time: you are right, but only by luck.","notConfused":"Not the same as moral luck, which concerns praise and blame for outcomes beyond our control rather than knowledge.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Gettier problem","Anti-luck virtue epistemology","Safety theory of knowledge"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["lucky belief","gettier problem","veritic luck","safety","knowledge","luck"]},{"term":"Doxastic and propositional justification","def":"Propositional justification is having good reason to believe something; doxastic justification is actually believing it for that good reason.","more":"The distinction is often traced to Roderick Firth in 1978. You can have strong evidence for a claim but believe it for a bad reason, such as wishful thinking; then your belief is propositionally but not doxastically justified. The distinction matters for the 'basing relation': what it is for a belief to be held because of certain evidence. It also helps clarify debates about whether knowledge requires believing for the right reasons.","etym":"Greek doxa, 'belief' or 'opinion'.","example":"A juror has good evidence the defendant is guilty, but believes it only because he dislikes the defendant's face.","notConfused":"Not the same as the distinction between justification and truth: both kinds of justification can be present while the belief is false.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Justification","Evidentialism","Belief"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["propositional justification","doxastic justification","basing relation","well-founded belief","justification"]},{"term":"Basic belief","def":"A belief that is justified without being inferred from other beliefs.","more":"Foundationalists hold that some beliefs must be basic to stop an infinite regress of reasons. Candidates include beliefs about one's own experiences ('I seem to see red'), simple truths of logic, and, for some, ordinary perceptual beliefs. Alvin Plantinga argued that belief in God can be 'properly basic', not needing argument, much as belief in other minds is. Coherentists deny that any belief is basic in this sense.","etym":null,"example":"Your belief that you have a headache usually rests on nothing but the headache itself, not on an argument.","notConfused":"Not the same as a belief that is merely simple or obvious: what matters is that its justification doesn't come from inference.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Foundationalism","Reformed epistemology","Coherentism"],"thinkers":["plantinga","descartes"],"tags":["properly basic belief","foundational belief","foundationalism","non-inferential justification","self-evident"]},{"term":"Evil demon","def":"Descartes' imagined deceiver of great power who might be making all your experiences of the world false.","more":"In his Meditations (1641), Descartes uses the hypothesis of a malicious demon, sometimes called the evil genius, to doubt everything that could be doubted, including the external world and his own body. The demon cannot, however, make him doubt that he exists while he is being deceived, which leads to the cogito. Brain-in-a-vat scenarios are a modern version. Epistemologists still use the 'new evil demon problem' to test reliabilism: a deceived person's beliefs seem justified even though they are unreliable.","etym":null,"example":"Imagine everything you see, hear and touch is a show staged by a being who wants you to believe false things.","notConfused":"Not the same as the problem of evil, which concerns why a good God allows suffering.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Brain in a vat","Cogito","Radical skepticism"],"thinkers":["descartes"],"tags":["malicious demon","evil genius","deceiving demon","descartes","radical scepticism","skepticism"]},{"term":"Dream argument","def":"The sceptical argument that since you cannot be sure you are not dreaming, you cannot be sure your experiences show you the real world.","more":"Plato's Theaetetus raises the question of how to prove we are awake, and Zhuangzi's butterfly dream poses a similar puzzle. Descartes used it in the first Meditation to doubt the existence of his body and surroundings. A common reply is that dreams are less coherent than waking life, though critics note we don't always notice this while dreaming. Others question whether dream experiences are really like waking perceptions at all.","etym":null,"example":"You dream you are reading this sentence; how do you know, right now, that you aren't?","notConfused":"Not the same as the simulation hypothesis, which supposes a constant artificial world rather than an occasional dream.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Butterfly dream","Evil demon","Radical skepticism"],"thinkers":["descartes","plato","zhuangzi"],"tags":["dreaming argument","dreaming","descartes","scepticism","skepticism","zhuangzi"]},{"term":"Agrippa’s trilemma","def":"The sceptical argument that any attempt to justify a belief must end in an infinite regress, a circle, or an unjustified assumption.","more":"It comes from the 'modes' of the ancient sceptic Agrippa, reported by Sextus Empiricus. Every reason seems to need a further reason, so justification either goes on forever, loops back on itself, or stops somewhere arbitrary. The German philosopher Hans Albert later called it the Münchhausen trilemma, after the baron who claimed to pull himself out of a swamp by his own hair. Infinitism, coherentism and foundationalism can each be seen as accepting one of the three horns.","etym":"Greek tri-, 'three', and lēmma, 'premise' or 'assumption'.","example":"A child asking 'why?' after every answer eventually forces you to repeat yourself, give up, or say 'because that's just how it is'.","notConfused":"Not the same as a false dilemma: the sceptic argues that the three options really are exhaustive.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Infinite regress","Foundationalism","Infinitism"],"thinkers":["sextus"],"tags":["agrippa's trilemma","munchhausen trilemma","münchhausen trilemma","regress problem","infinite regress","scepticism","skepticism"]},{"term":"Problem of the criterion","def":"The puzzle that to know which beliefs are true we need a criterion, but to know which criterion is correct we already need to know which beliefs are true.","more":"Ancient sceptics, as recorded by Sextus Empiricus, used it to argue we can never get started in knowledge. Roderick Chisholm revived it in 1973, distinguishing 'methodists', who start with a criterion, from 'particularists', who start with particular things they know, such as that they have hands. Chisholm himself sided with particularism. It remains a basic challenge for any theory of knowledge.","etym":"Greek kritērion, 'means of judging'.","example":"To check whether a news site is reliable, you compare it with facts you trust; but how did you decide those facts were reliable?","notConfused":"Not the same as the problem of induction, which concerns justifying inferences about the unobserved rather than choosing a standard of truth.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Agrippa’s trilemma","Common-sense particularism","Pyrrhonism"],"thinkers":["sextus","chisholm"],"tags":["criterion problem","wheel","chisholm","particularism","methodism","scepticism"]},{"term":"Peer disagreement","def":"Disagreement with someone who is as well-informed and as good a reasoner as you on the question at hand.","more":"Since the 2000s, philosophers such as Richard Feldman, Thomas Kelly, David Christensen and Adam Elga have asked whether learning of such disagreement should make you less confident. Conciliationists say you should move toward your peer's view; steadfast views say you may keep your belief if your reasoning was in fact correct. The issue matters for religious, political and philosophical disagreement, where intelligent people often differ. It also raises the worry that if conciliationism is right, it must apply to disagreement about conciliationism itself.","etym":null,"example":"You and a friend, equally good at maths, split a restaurant bill and get different answers.","notConfused":"Not the same as relativism: those debating peer disagreement assume there is a right answer and ask how to respond when you can't tell who has it.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Conciliationism","Steadfastness","Higher-order evidence"],"thinkers":["feldman_r"],"tags":["disagreement","epistemic peer","conciliationism","steadfastness","equal weight view"]},{"term":"Higher-order evidence","def":"Evidence about your own reasoning or evidence, such as information that you may be tired, biased or drugged, rather than evidence about the world directly.","more":"David Christensen and others have discussed it since around 2010. Higher-order evidence seems to matter: a pilot told that high altitude impairs judgment should doubt her calculations even if they are in fact correct. Yet it is puzzling, because it does not change the first-order evidence itself. It plays a key role in debates about peer disagreement and self-doubt.","etym":null,"example":"You solve a puzzle, then learn the drink you had contains a drug that makes people confidently wrong.","notConfused":"Not the same as a rebutting defeater: it does not show the belief false but casts doubt on your ability to assess the evidence.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Peer disagreement","Defeater","Conciliationism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["evidence about evidence","self-doubt","epistemic humility","reasoning errors","defeat"]},{"term":"Credence","def":"A degree of belief: how confident you are that something is true, often expressed as a probability between 0 and 1.","more":"Frank Ramsey, in 'Truth and Probability' (1926), proposed measuring degrees of belief by the bets a person would accept. Bayesian epistemology studies how credences should fit together and change with evidence. Philosophers debate how credences relate to all-or-nothing belief: is belief just a high credence, or something different? Credence is central to formal epistemology and decision theory.","etym":"Latin credentia, from credere, 'to believe'.","example":"Being 70% sure it will rain tomorrow is a credence of 0.7.","notConfused":"Not the same as objective probability: a credence is how confident a person is, which may differ from how likely something actually is.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Bayesian epistemology","Dutch book argument","Belief"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["degree of belief","subjective probability","confidence","partial belief","bayesianism"]},{"term":"Dutch book argument","def":"An argument that degrees of belief must obey the laws of probability, because otherwise a bookie could offer you a set of bets you'd accept that guarantee you lose.","more":"Frank Ramsey and Bruno de Finetti developed the idea in the 1920s and 1930s. If you are 60% sure it will rain and also 60% sure it won't, you would accept bets that together lose money whatever happens. Defenders take this to show that probabilistic coherence is a requirement of rationality. Critics reply that being vulnerable to clever bookies is a practical flaw, not obviously an epistemic one.","etym":"From 'book', a set of bets taken by a bookmaker; the origin of 'Dutch' is unclear.","example":"Paying 60p for a bet that pays £1 if it rains and another 60p for one that pays £1 if it doesn't: you spend £1.20 to win £1.","notConfused":"Not the same as the gambler's fallacy, which concerns misjudging chances in a sequence rather than inconsistent degrees of belief.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Credence","Bayesianism","Subjective Bayesianism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["dutch book","sure loss","coherence","probabilism","bayesianism","betting"]},{"term":"Principle of indifference","def":"The principle that, if you have no reason to favour any of several possibilities, you should give them all equal probability.","more":"John Maynard Keynes named it in 1921; earlier versions, called the principle of insufficient reason, go back to Jacob Bernoulli and Laplace. It seems obviously right for dice and coins. But Joseph Bertrand and others showed that it gives conflicting answers depending on how you describe the possibilities, as in Bertrand's paradox. Objective Bayesians try to refine it; subjective Bayesians generally reject it.","etym":null,"example":"With no information about a three-horse race, you give each horse a one-in-three chance.","notConfused":"Not the same as the principle of sufficient reason, which says everything has an explanation, not how to assign probabilities.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Objective Bayesianism","Bayesian epistemology","Credence"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["principle of insufficient reason","equal probability","laplace","bertrand's paradox","uniform prior"]},{"term":"Moore’s paradox","def":"The absurdity of saying something like 'it is raining, but I don't believe it is raining', even though both parts could be true.","more":"G. E. Moore pointed it out in the 1940s, and Wittgenstein, who gave it the name, thought it revealed something deep about belief and assertion. The sentence is not contradictory, since it could be true that it rains while you fail to believe it. The oddness lies in asserting or believing it. Explanations appeal to the rules of assertion, to the nature of belief, or to the idea that sincerely asserting P expresses belief in P.","etym":null,"example":"'The train has left, but I don't believe it has.'","notConfused":"Not the same as a contradiction: the two halves are consistent, and the paradox concerns why one can't sensibly say them together.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Belief","Contradiction","Speech act"],"thinkers":["moore","wittgenstein"],"tags":["moore's paradox","moorean absurdity","g. e. moore","assertion","belief"]},{"term":"Value problem of knowledge","def":"The question of why knowledge is more valuable than mere true belief.","more":"Plato raised it in the Meno: a true belief about the road to Larissa gets you there as well as knowledge does. His answer was that knowledge is 'tied down' and less likely to run away. Linda Zagzebski and Jonathan Kvanvig sharpened the problem, arguing that if knowledge is just reliably produced true belief, the reliability adds no extra value (the 'swamping problem'). Virtue epistemologists answer that knowledge is an achievement that credits the knower.","etym":null,"example":"A good espresso from a reliable machine is no tastier than an equally good one from a random machine; so why value reliable belief more?","notConfused":"Not the same as asking whether truth is valuable: the puzzle assumes truth matters and asks what knowledge adds.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Virtue epistemology","Process reliabilism","Justified true belief"],"thinkers":["plato","zagzebski"],"tags":["meno problem","swamping problem","value of knowledge","true belief","virtue epistemology"]},{"term":"Episteme","def":"The ancient Greek word for knowledge in the full sense, especially systematic, well-grounded understanding.","more":"Plato contrasted episteme with doxa, mere opinion, which can be true but lacks a grasp of reasons. Aristotle used it for scientific knowledge of necessary truths derived from first principles, distinguishing it from techne (craft) and phronesis (practical wisdom). The word gives us 'epistemology'. In the 20th century Michel Foucault used it for the underlying assumptions of thought in a given historical period.","etym":"Greek epistēmē, 'knowledge' or 'understanding'.","example":"Knowing that the angles of a triangle add up to 180 degrees because you can prove it, not just because a teacher said so.","notConfused":"Not the same as doxa, opinion or belief, which can be true without being understood or justified.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemology","Belief","Justified true belief"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle"],"tags":["epistêmê","scientific knowledge","knowledge","doxa","epistemology","understanding"]},{"term":"Clear and distinct ideas","def":"Descartes' standard for truth: whatever the mind perceives with complete clarity and sharp separation from everything else must be true.","more":"In the Meditations (1641), after establishing the cogito, Descartes takes clear and distinct perception as his general rule of truth, guaranteed by a non-deceiving God. A perception is clear when it is present and evident to an attentive mind, and distinct when it is sharply separated from other perceptions. Critics, starting with his contemporaries, complained that the standard is subjective, since people can feel sure of falsehoods. It also leads to the worry known as the Cartesian circle.","etym":null,"example":"Descartes would say you clearly and distinctly perceive that 2 + 3 = 5, unlike your hazy sense of a distant shape in the fog.","notConfused":"Not the same as feeling confident: Descartes meant a special kind of intellectual perception, not just a strong feeling.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Cartesian circle","Cogito","Rationalism"],"thinkers":["descartes"],"tags":["clear and distinct perception","descartes","certainty","rationalism","criterion of truth"]},{"term":"Copy principle","def":"Hume's thesis that every simple idea in the mind is a faint copy of an earlier impression from sensation or feeling.","more":"Hume stated it at the start of his Treatise (1739) and Enquiry (1748). He used it as a test for meaningful terms: if we cannot find the impression from which an idea is copied, the term may be empty. This led him to question ideas such as necessary connection and substance. Hume himself noted a counterexample, the 'missing shade of blue' that someone might imagine without ever having seen it.","etym":null,"example":"You can't form an idea of pineapple's taste, Hume says, without having tasted one.","notConfused":"Not the same as the verification principle, though the logical positivists' meaning test was inspired by it.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","Tabula rasa","Hume's fork"],"thinkers":["hume"],"tags":["impressions and ideas","hume","empiricism","missing shade of blue","copy thesis"]},{"term":"Cartesian circle","def":"The objection that Descartes reasons in a circle: he proves God exists using clear and distinct ideas, then relies on God to guarantee that clear and distinct ideas are true.","more":"Antoine Arnauld raised it in the Fourth Objections to the Meditations (1641). Descartes replied that God guarantees only remembered conclusions, not the clear perceptions we have at the moment, though scholars disagree whether this works. The problem illustrates a general difficulty: any check on our reasoning seems to require reasoning. It is a famous case of the problem of the criterion.","etym":null,"example":"Like checking a ruler's accuracy by measuring it with itself.","notConfused":"Not the same as an ordinary circular argument in daily life: here the circle concerns the foundations of all knowledge.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Clear and distinct ideas","Begging the question","Problem of the criterion"],"thinkers":["descartes"],"tags":["arnauld's circle","descartes","circular reasoning","circularity","clear and distinct ideas"]},{"term":"Confirmation bias","def":"The tendency to seek out, notice and remember evidence that supports what you already believe, while overlooking evidence against it.","more":"Francis Bacon described the tendency in his Novum Organum (1620), noting that people count the hits and forget the misses. In the 1960s the psychologist Peter Wason studied it experimentally, notably with his selection task. Philosophers of science see it as a reason for methods such as blind trials and Popper's emphasis on testing theories against attempts to refute them. Epistemologists ask how far such biases undermine the rationality of ordinary belief.","etym":null,"example":"Remembering the times your 'lucky' socks preceded a win, but forgetting the losses.","notConfused":"Not the same as confirmation in philosophy of science, which is the legitimate support evidence gives a theory.","field":"Knowledge","related":["Falsificationism","Confirmation","Theory-ladenness"],"thinkers":["bacon","popper"],"tags":["myside bias","cognitive bias","motivated reasoning","selective evidence","belief perseverance"]},{"term":"Explanatory gap","def":"The apparent gap between physical facts about the brain and facts about conscious experience: even full knowledge of the brain seems not to explain why it feels like something.","more":"Joseph Levine coined the phrase in 1983. He argued that even if pain is identical to some brain process, we cannot see why that process should feel painful, in the way we can see why water's molecular structure makes it boil at 100°C. Levine treated the gap as a problem for our understanding, not necessarily proof that physicalism is false. Later, the hard problem of consciousness and the phenomenal concept strategy developed from these ideas.","etym":null,"example":"You can learn everything about the chemistry of chocolate and the neurons it excites, and still wonder why it tastes like that.","notConfused":"Not the same as the hard problem, which asks why consciousness exists at all; the explanatory gap focuses on our inability to deduce experience from physical facts.","field":"Mind","related":["Hard problem","Phenomenal concept strategy","Qualia"],"thinkers":["chalmers"],"tags":["levine","hard problem","qualia","consciousness","mind-body problem","physicalism"]},{"term":"Causal closure of the physical","def":"The principle that every physical event that has a cause has a sufficient physical cause.","more":"Many physicalists take it to be well supported by the success of physics and physiology, which find no non-physical forces at work in the body. David Papineau has argued that it is the key premise for physicalism. Combined with the claim that mental events cause physical ones, it seems to imply that mental events are physical. Interactionist dualists deny it, and some argue that quantum indeterminacy leaves room for non-physical influence.","etym":null,"example":"When you raise your arm, every step from nerve signal to muscle contraction appears to have a complete physical explanation.","notConfused":"Not the same as determinism: closure says physical events have physical causes, not that those causes fix everything in advance.","field":"Mind","related":["Mental causation","Causal exclusion argument","Physicalism"],"thinkers":["papineau"],"tags":["physical closure","completeness of physics","causal completeness","physicalism","mental causation"]},{"term":"Causal exclusion argument","def":"The argument that if every physical effect already has a sufficient physical cause, then non-physical mental properties have no causal work left to do.","more":"Jaegwon Kim developed it from the late 1980s, especially against non-reductive physicalism. If my decision to drink causes my arm to move, but my brain state is already sufficient, the decision seems either identical to the brain state or causally idle, unless every action is overdetermined by two separate causes. Responses include accepting reduction, accepting systematic overdetermination, or arguing that causation works differently at different levels. The argument is central to debates over mental causation.","etym":null,"example":"If the physical chain from neurons to muscles fully explains why you reached for water, what does your thirst add?","notConfused":"Not the same as epiphenomenalism, which is one possible conclusion; the exclusion argument is a reason offered for some such conclusion.","field":"Mind","related":["Mental causation","Causal closure of the physical","Epiphenomenalism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["exclusion problem","kim","jaegwon kim","mental causation","overdetermination","epiphenomenalism"]},{"term":"Propositional attitude","def":"A mental state, such as believing, hoping, fearing or desiring, directed at a proposition that something is the case.","more":"The term owes to Bertrand Russell. Propositional attitudes are usually reported with 'that' clauses: 'Ana believes that it will rain.' They are central to folk psychology, our everyday way of explaining behaviour through beliefs and desires. Philosophers argue about whether they are real inner states, as Jerry Fodor held, useful patterns, as Daniel Dennett suggests, or, as eliminative materialists claim, likely to be discarded by neuroscience.","etym":null,"example":"Hoping that the bus comes on time and believing that it won't are two attitudes toward the same proposition.","notConfused":"Not the same as an attitude in the everyday sense of a general outlook or mood.","field":"Mind","related":["Folk psychology","Intentionality","Proposition"],"thinkers":["russell","dennett"],"tags":["attitude","belief","desire","intentionality","folk psychology","mental state"]},{"term":"Language of thought","def":"The hypothesis that thinking takes place in a mental language, with symbols that combine according to rules, like a computer's internal code.","more":"Jerry Fodor defended it in The Language of Thought (1975). It explains why thought is productive (we can think endlessly many new thoughts) and systematic (anyone who can think 'John loves Mary' can think 'Mary loves John'). The mental language, sometimes called Mentalese, is not English or any spoken language. Connectionists and others reject it, arguing that the brain does not process symbols in this way.","etym":null,"example":"On this view, a baby who cannot yet speak still thinks in an inner 'Mentalese' about faces and milk.","notConfused":"Not the same as inner speech, the voice in your head speaking a natural language, which is a conscious experience rather than the underlying code.","field":"Mind","related":["Connectionism","Computational functionalism","Modularity of mind"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["mentalese","lot","fodor","jerry fodor","computational theory of mind","representational theory of mind"]},{"term":"Modularity of mind","def":"The view that the mind contains specialised modules, such as for vision or language, that work fast, automatically and independently of our other beliefs.","more":"Jerry Fodor proposed it in The Modularity of Mind (1983), limiting modules to input systems like perception and language. A sign of modularity is that illusions persist even when we know they are illusions. Evolutionary psychologists later argued for 'massive modularity', the idea that most of the mind consists of modules, which Fodor himself rejected. The debate matters for whether what we see is influenced by what we believe.","etym":"Latin modulus, 'small measure'.","example":"The two lines in the Müller-Lyer illusion still look unequal even after you measure them and know they're the same.","notConfused":"Not the same as phrenology, the discredited idea that mental faculties live in particular bumps of the skull.","field":"Mind","related":["Theory-ladenness","Language of thought","Computational functionalism"],"thinkers":["carruthers"],"tags":["modules","mental modules","fodor","massive modularity","cognitive architecture","encapsulation"]},{"term":"Intentional stance","def":"Daniel Dennett's strategy of predicting a system's behaviour by treating it as a rational agent with beliefs and desires.","more":"Dennett contrasts it with the physical stance (predicting from physics) and the design stance (predicting from what something is designed to do). He developed the idea from 1971 and in The Intentional Stance (1987). For Dennett, to have beliefs just is to be a system whose behaviour is reliably predictable from this stance. Critics argue this makes belief too cheap, or merely a matter of interpretation.","etym":null,"example":"Predicting a chess computer's next move by thinking 'it wants to protect its queen'.","notConfused":"Not the same as intentionality: the stance is a method of interpretation, while intentionality is the property of mental states being about something.","field":"Mind","related":["Intentionality","Folk psychology","Interpretivism"],"thinkers":["dennett"],"tags":["dennett","daniel dennett","design stance","physical stance","interpretation","intentionality"]},{"term":"Cartesian theatre","def":"Dennett's mocking name for the idea that there is a single place in the brain where everything 'comes together' for consciousness, as if watched on a screen.","more":"Daniel Dennett introduced it in Consciousness Explained (1991). He argued that the brain has no central viewing point and no finish line where experiences become conscious. In its place he proposed a 'multiple drafts' model, in which many parallel processes compete. The image draws on Descartes' suggestion that the pineal gland is where mind and body meet.","etym":null,"example":"Imagining a tiny you sitting behind your eyes, watching the world on a screen.","notConfused":"Not the same as Cartesian dualism: Dennett thinks even materialists can fall into the theatre picture.","field":"Mind","related":["Homunculus fallacy","Global workspace theory","Dualism"],"thinkers":["dennett","descartes"],"tags":["cartesian theater","dennett","daniel dennett","multiple drafts","homunculus","consciousness"]},{"term":"Privileged access","def":"The special, first-person way in which each of us seems to know our own mental states, more directly and reliably than others can.","more":"Descartes treated knowledge of one's own mind as more certain than knowledge of anything else. Gilbert Ryle and later behaviourists challenged this, arguing that we learn about our minds much as we learn about others'. Psychological research on confabulation, and Eric Schwitzgebel's work on the unreliability of introspection, suggests we often get our own minds wrong. Most philosophers now accept some special access while denying that it is infallible.","etym":null,"example":"You can know you're feeling anxious without looking in a mirror or asking anyone else.","notConfused":"Not the same as infallibility: access can be special and direct without being immune from error.","field":"Mind","related":["Other minds","Cogito","Infallibilism"],"thinkers":["descartes","schwitzgebel"],"tags":["first-person authority","introspection","self-knowledge","incorrigibility","first-person access"]},{"term":"Access consciousness","def":"Ned Block's term for information in the mind that is available for reasoning, reporting and controlling behaviour.","more":"Block drew the distinction in 1995 between access consciousness and phenomenal consciousness, the felt quality of experience. He argued that scientists studying 'consciousness' often measure access and wrongly assume they have explained experience. Blindsight seems to show some access without experience, and Block suggested the reverse might also occur. Critics argue the two cannot really come apart.","etym":null,"example":"A fact you can use in conversation, such as the time on your watch, is access-conscious; the redness of a sunset is a matter of phenomenal consciousness.","notConfused":"Not the same as phenomenal consciousness, which concerns what experience is like rather than what information is available to use.","field":"Mind","related":["Consciousness","What it is like","Blindsight"],"thinkers":["block"],"tags":["a-consciousness","phenomenal consciousness","ned block","block","availability","global workspace"]},{"term":"What it is like","def":"The phrase used to pick out conscious experience: a creature is conscious if there is something it is like to be that creature.","more":"Thomas Nagel made it famous in 'What Is It Like to Be a Bat?' (1974). Bats navigate by echolocation, and Nagel argued that no amount of physical knowledge would tell us what that is like from the bat's own point of view. The phrase is now the standard way to define phenomenal consciousness. Critics such as Dennett argue it hides confusions behind an intuitive formula.","etym":null,"example":"There is something it is like to taste coffee; presumably nothing it is like to be a toaster.","notConfused":"Not the same as asking what something resembles: it asks how an experience feels from the inside.","field":"Mind","related":["Qualia","Hard problem","Consciousness"],"thinkers":["nagel"],"tags":["what it's like","what is it like to be a bat","nagel","thomas nagel","phenomenal consciousness","qualia","subjective experience"]},{"term":"Swampman","def":"Donald Davidson's thought experiment in which lightning strikes a swamp and creates an exact physical copy of a person, with no history.","more":"Davidson introduced it in 'Knowing One's Own Mind' (1987). Swampman behaves just like Davidson, but Davidson argued that, lacking a causal history with the things around him, his words and thoughts would not at first mean anything. The case is used to test theories that make mental content depend on history, such as teleosemantics. Many people find the conclusion surprising, while others accept it as the cost of a good theory.","etym":null,"example":"A perfect duplicate appears and says 'I need to feed my cat'; does it refer to a cat it has never encountered?","notConfused":"Not the same as a philosophical zombie, which lacks consciousness; Swampman's question is about meaning and content.","field":"Mind","related":["Teleosemantics","Externalism","Zombie"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["swamp man","davidson","donald davidson","teleosemantics","content externalism","duplicate"]},{"term":"Blindsight","def":"A condition in which people with damage to the visual cortex can respond accurately to things in their blind field while reporting that they see nothing.","more":"The neuropsychologist Lawrence Weiskrantz studied it in the 1970s and gave it its name. Patients asked to 'guess' the location or movement of objects do far better than chance. Philosophers use blindsight to argue that visual information can guide behaviour without conscious experience. It features in debates about access versus phenomenal consciousness and about what consciousness is for.","etym":null,"example":"A patient insists he sees nothing on his left, yet steps around a chair placed there.","notConfused":"Not the same as ordinary blindness, or simply poor eyesight: the eyes work, but conscious seeing in part of the field is lost.","field":"Mind","related":["Access consciousness","Consciousness","Higher-order theory of consciousness"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["blind sight","unconscious perception","visual cortex","phenomenal consciousness","neuroscience"]},{"term":"Homunculus fallacy","def":"The error of explaining a mental ability by assuming a little person inside the head who already has that ability.","more":"Anthony Kenny named it in a 1971 paper. Saying that we see because an image is projected inside the brain only moves the problem: who sees the inner image? Such explanations lead to an infinite regress. Daniel Dennett argued that homunculi are acceptable if each one is stupider than the last, until they become simple mechanisms.","etym":"Latin homunculus, 'little man'.","example":"Explaining memory by saying the brain contains a librarian who files and fetches memories.","notConfused":"Not the same as the ghost in the machine, which is Ryle's criticism of mind-body dualism specifically.","field":"Mind","related":["Cartesian theatre","Infinite regress","Ghost in the machine"],"thinkers":["kenny","dennett"],"tags":["homunculus","little man in the head","regress","cartesian theatre","inner observer"]},{"term":"Simulation theory (of mind)","def":"The view that we understand other people's minds by imaginatively putting ourselves in their position, rather than by applying a theory.","more":"Robert Gordon and Jane Heal proposed it in the mid-1980s, and Alvin Goldman developed it in Simulating Minds (2006). Its rival, the 'theory-theory', holds that we use an implicit folk-psychological theory to infer others' mental states. Mirror neurons have sometimes been cited as support for simulation, though their role is disputed. Many researchers now accept a hybrid of both.","etym":null,"example":"Predicting how nervous your friend is before her driving test by imagining how you'd feel.","notConfused":"Not the same as the simulation hypothesis, the idea that we might be living in a computer simulation.","field":"Mind","related":["Other minds","Folk psychology","Direct perception of other minds"],"thinkers":["goldman"],"tags":["simulation theory","mindreading","mind reading","empathy","theory of mind","theory theory"]},{"term":"Pre-established harmony","def":"Leibniz's view that mind and body do not affect each other, but God set them up from the start to run in perfect step.","more":"Leibniz proposed it in the 1690s and developed it in his Monadology (1714). He compared mind and body to two clocks made so precisely that they always agree without being connected. The view avoids the problem of how mind and matter could interact, but at the cost of making their apparent interaction an illusion. It extends to all monads, each of which unfolds its own states in harmony with all the others.","etym":null,"example":"You decide to wave and your arm rises, not because the decision moved the arm, but because both were scheduled in advance.","notConfused":"Not the same as occasionalism, in which God intervenes at every moment; in pre-established harmony God arranges everything once at creation.","field":"Mind","related":["Parallelism","Occasionalism","Monad"],"thinkers":["leibniz"],"tags":["preestablished harmony","leibniz","parallelism","occasionalism","mind-body problem","monads"]},{"term":"Easy problems of consciousness","def":"David Chalmers' term for the problems of explaining the brain's functions, such as attention, memory and verbal report, which science can in principle solve.","more":"Chalmers introduced the distinction in 1995. The 'easy' problems are hard in practice but involve explaining abilities and mechanisms. The hard problem, by contrast, asks why any of these functions are accompanied by experience. Critics such as Dennett argue that once all the easy problems are solved, nothing will be left over.","etym":null,"example":"Explaining how the brain picks out a friend's voice in a noisy room is an easy problem; explaining why it sounds like anything is the hard one.","notConfused":"Not 'easy' in the sense of simple: they may take decades of research, but they raise no special puzzle of principle.","field":"Mind","related":["Hard problem","Meta-problem of consciousness","Access consciousness"],"thinkers":["chalmers","dennett"],"tags":["easy problem","chalmers","david chalmers","hard problem","cognitive functions"]},{"term":"Meta-problem of consciousness","def":"The problem of explaining why we think and say that there is a hard problem of consciousness.","more":"David Chalmers set it out in 2018. Because our reports and judgments are behaviour, explaining them is in principle an 'easy' problem. Illusionists, such as Keith Frankish, think solving it will show that consciousness, as we imagine it, is an illusion. Chalmers thinks solving it may give clues to the hard problem without dissolving it.","etym":"Greek meta, 'beyond' or 'about'.","example":"Why do so many people, across cultures, feel there is something mysterious about the redness of red?","notConfused":"Not the same as the hard problem itself: the meta-problem is about our beliefs concerning consciousness, not consciousness directly.","field":"Mind","related":["Hard problem","Illusionism","Easy problems of consciousness"],"thinkers":["chalmers","frankish"],"tags":["meta-problem","chalmers","illusionism","hard problem","problem intuitions"]},{"term":"Neural correlates of consciousness","def":"The minimal brain activity that is sufficient for a particular conscious experience.","more":"Francis Crick and Christof Koch made the search for them a scientific programme from 1990. Researchers compare conditions in which a stimulus is consciously seen with ones in which it is not, as in binocular rivalry. Philosophers point out that finding correlates is not the same as explaining why they produce experience. Theories such as global workspace theory and integrated information theory make different predictions about where the correlates lie.","etym":null,"example":"Scanning brains while people view an image that flips between a face and a vase, to see what changes when perception switches.","notConfused":"Not the same as a cause or identity: a correlate may accompany experience without explaining or being it.","field":"Mind","related":["Hard problem","Global workspace theory","Integrated information theory"],"thinkers":["koch","seth"],"tags":["ncc","neuroscience of consciousness","brain correlates","neural basis","consciousness"]},{"term":"Combination problem","def":"The problem for panpsychism of explaining how tiny conscious subjects, such as particles, could combine to form a single larger mind like ours.","more":"William James raised the core worry in The Principles of Psychology (1890): twelve people each thinking one word do not add up to one mind thinking the whole sentence. William Seager named it the combination problem in 1995. It is widely seen as the most serious objection to panpsychism. Philip Goff and others have proposed solutions, while cosmopsychists reverse the direction, starting with one cosmic mind.","etym":null,"example":"A choir of thousands still isn't one person hearing the whole song.","notConfused":"Not the same as the binding problem in neuroscience, which asks how the brain unites colour, shape and motion into a single perception.","field":"Mind","related":["Panpsychism","Cosmopsychism","Constitutive micropsychism"],"thinkers":["james","goff"],"tags":["panpsychism","micro-experience","subject summing","cosmopsychism","combination"]},{"term":"Connectionism","def":"An approach to the mind that models thinking as activity in networks of simple interconnected units, like neurons, rather than as rule-based symbol manipulation.","more":"It rose to prominence with David Rumelhart and James McClelland's work on parallel distributed processing in 1986. Knowledge is stored in the strength of connections, learned from examples. Jerry Fodor and Zenon Pylyshyn argued in 1988 that connectionism cannot explain the systematic structure of thought. Today's deep learning systems are descendants of connectionist models, which has revived the debate.","etym":null,"example":"A network that learns to recognise handwritten digits by adjusting its connections after thousands of examples, without anyone writing rules for each digit.","notConfused":"Not the same as the connectome, the actual map of neural connections in a particular brain.","field":"Mind","related":["Language of thought","Computational functionalism","Eliminative materialism"],"thinkers":["churchland","pchurchland"],"tags":["neural networks","parallel distributed processing","pdp","artificial neural network","deep learning"]},{"term":"Frame problem","def":"The problem of how a thinking system can efficiently tell which of its beliefs need updating, and which consequences matter, when something changes.","more":"John McCarthy and Patrick Hayes identified it in artificial intelligence in 1969 as a technical problem about representing what doesn't change. Daniel Dennett's 1984 essay broadened it: his robot, told to rescue its battery from a room with a bomb, wastes time deducing irrelevant consequences. Philosophers such as Fodor saw it as a deep problem about relevance in general reasoning. Humans seem to solve it effortlessly, which is part of the puzzle.","etym":null,"example":"When you move a cup, you don't stop to check whether the walls changed colour; how does a mind know not to?","notConfused":"Not the same as framing effects in psychology, where the wording of a choice changes what people decide.","field":"Mind","related":["Strong AI","Computational functionalism","Embedded cognition"],"thinkers":["dennett"],"tags":["frame problem in ai","relevance","belief updating","artificial intelligence","mccarthy"]},{"term":"Strong AI","def":"John Searle's term for the claim that a suitably programmed computer would literally have a mind and understand, not merely simulate understanding.","more":"Searle introduced the label in 'Minds, Brains, and Programs' (1980) alongside his Chinese room argument against it. 'Weak AI' holds only that computers are useful tools for studying the mind. The term is now often used loosely for human-level or general artificial intelligence, which is a different question about ability rather than understanding. Advances in large language models have renewed the debate.","etym":null,"example":"Claiming that a chatbot doesn't just produce sentences about sadness, but genuinely understands what sadness is.","notConfused":"Not the same as artificial general intelligence: a system might match human abilities while still, on Searle's view, understanding nothing.","field":"Mind","related":["Chinese room","Computational functionalism","Turing test"],"thinkers":["searle"],"tags":["strong artificial intelligence","searle","chinese room","machine consciousness","thinking machines","artificial general intelligence"]},{"term":"Argument from hallucination","def":"The argument that since hallucinations can feel just like real perceptions, even in normal perception we are directly aware only of something mental, such as sense data.","more":"It is a close relative of the argument from illusion and was used by sense-data theorists in the early 20th century. If a hallucinated dagger and a real one look exactly alike, it seems the experience must be the same kind of thing in both cases. Since the hallucination involves no dagger, the argument concludes, neither does the real experience directly. Disjunctivists reply that genuine perception and hallucination are fundamentally different, even if they cannot be told apart from the inside.","etym":"Latin hallucinari, 'to wander in mind'.","example":"Macbeth's vision of a dagger seems as real to him as a dagger on the table would.","notConfused":"Not the same as the argument from illusion, which uses cases where a real object looks different from how it is, such as a straight stick looking bent in water.","field":"Mind","related":["Argument from illusion","Sense data","Direct realism"],"thinkers":["ayer"],"tags":["hallucination","argument from illusion","sense data","disjunctivism","naive realism","perception"]},{"term":"Transparency of experience","def":"The claim that when you try to attend to your experience itself, you find only the things experienced and their features, as if looking through glass.","more":"G. E. Moore described consciousness as 'diaphanous' in 1903, and Gilbert Harman revived the point in 1990. If introspection reveals only properties of objects, such as the redness of a tomato, this seems to support representationalism, the view that experience's character is fixed by what it represents. Critics point to blurry vision, afterimages and moods as experiences with features not obviously belonging to objects. The debate bears directly on whether qualia exist.","etym":"Latin transparere, 'to show through'.","example":"Asked to describe your experience of a blue sky, you can only describe the sky's blueness.","notConfused":"Not the same as transparency in the sense of honesty or openness about one's motives.","field":"Mind","related":["Representationalism (about experience)","Qualia","Privileged access"],"thinkers":["moore","harman"],"tags":["transparency","diaphanousness","representationalism","intentionalism","perception"]},{"term":"Use/mention distinction","def":"The difference between using a word to talk about something and mentioning the word itself, usually marked by quotation marks.","more":"'Paris is a city' uses the word Paris; ''Paris' has five letters' mentions it. Confusing the two can create apparent paradoxes and bad arguments. Quine stressed the importance of careful quotation in his Mathematical Logic (1940), and the distinction became standard in analytic philosophy. Self-reference paradoxes, such as the liar, often depend on how language can talk about itself.","etym":null,"example":"'Cats have whiskers' is about animals; ''cats' has four letters' is about a word.","notConfused":"Not the same as the sense/reference distinction, which concerns two aspects of a word's meaning rather than whether the word is being talked about.","field":"Language","related":["Sense and reference","Liar paradox","Type and token"],"thinkers":["quine"],"tags":["use and mention","use-mention distinction","quotation marks","quotation","metalanguage"]},{"term":"De re and de dicto","def":"A de dicto claim is about a statement or description; a de re claim is about a thing itself, however it is described.","more":"The distinction comes from medieval logic and was revived by Quine in the 1950s for beliefs and necessity. 'Ralph believes someone is a spy' can mean Ralph believes there are spies (de dicto) or there is a particular person Ralph suspects (de re). In modal logic, 'the number of planets is necessarily greater than 7' is false de dicto, since there could have been fewer planets, but arguably true de re of the number 8. Many puzzles about belief, necessity and essentialism turn on this ambiguity.","etym":"Latin de re, 'about the thing', and de dicto, 'about what is said'.","example":"'Maria wants to marry a millionaire' could mean any millionaire will do (de dicto) or that there's a particular millionaire she loves (de re).","notConfused":"Not the same as the distinction between literal and figurative meaning; both readings are literal.","field":"Language","related":["Referential opacity","Modal logic","Propositional attitude"],"thinkers":["quine"],"tags":["de dicto","de re","modality","scope ambiguity","belief ascription","de re modality"]},{"term":"Referential opacity","def":"The feature of some sentence positions where replacing a term with another that refers to the same thing can change the sentence's truth.","more":"Quine developed the idea in the 1950s, drawing on Frege. 'Lois believes Superman can fly' is true, but 'Lois believes Clark Kent can fly' seems false, even though Superman is Clark Kent. Contexts involving belief, necessity and quotation are typically opaque, while ordinary contexts are transparent. Quine's own example: 'Giorgione was so-called because of his size' is true, but substituting 'Barbarelli', another name for the painter, makes nonsense.","etym":"Latin opacus, 'shaded' or 'dark'.","example":"'Oedipus wanted to marry Jocasta' is true; 'Oedipus wanted to marry his mother' is not, though Jocasta was his mother.","notConfused":"Not the same as vagueness or ambiguity: the terms are clear, but substituting them fails.","field":"Language","related":["De re and de dicto","Frege’s puzzle","Leibniz's law"],"thinkers":["quine","frege"],"tags":["opacity","intensional context","substitutivity","opaque context","quine"]},{"term":"Twin Earth","def":"Hilary Putnam's thought experiment of a planet exactly like Earth except that its 'water' is a different chemical, XYZ, used to argue that meanings 'ain't in the head'.","more":"Putnam presented it in 'The Meaning of 'Meaning'' (1975). Oscar on Earth and his twin on Twin Earth are in identical mental states, yet Oscar's word 'water' refers to H2O and his twin's to XYZ. So, Putnam concluded, meaning depends partly on the environment, not just on the speaker's mind. The case launched semantic externalism and was extended by Tyler Burge to thoughts themselves.","etym":null,"example":"Two people raised on different planets both say 'pass the water', drinking identical-seeming but chemically different liquids.","notConfused":"Not the same as the brain in a vat, which concerns scepticism; Twin Earth is about what determines meaning.","field":"Language","related":["Semantic externalism (Putnam’s reply)","Natural kind externalism","Externalism"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"tags":["twin earth thought experiment","putnam","hilary putnam","semantic externalism","xyz","meaning ain't in the head"]},{"term":"Indexical","def":"A word whose reference shifts with the context of use, such as 'I', 'here', 'now' or 'this'.","more":"C. S. Peirce introduced the related notion of an 'index', a sign that points to its object. David Kaplan's work on demonstratives, circulated from the 1970s, distinguished an indexical's constant meaning (its 'character') from what it refers to on a given occasion (its 'content'). John Perry argued that some indexical beliefs, such as 'I am making a mess', cannot be captured without indexicals. They pose challenges for any theory that equates meaning with reference.","etym":"Latin index, 'pointer' or 'forefinger'.","example":"'I am hungry' is true when you say it before lunch and false when your well-fed friend says it.","notConfused":"Not the same as an ambiguous word: 'I' always means the speaker; it is the referent, not the meaning, that changes.","field":"Language","related":["Sense and reference","Rigid designator","Epistemic contextualism"],"thinkers":["peirce"],"tags":["indexicals","context sensitivity","demonstrative","deixis","kaplan"]},{"term":"Connotation and denotation","def":"In John Stuart Mill's terms, a word's denotation is the things it applies to, and its connotation is the attributes it implies about them.","more":"Mill set out the distinction in A System of Logic (1843). He held that proper names, such as 'Dartmouth', denote without connoting: the name would still apply if the river moved its mouth. General terms like 'white' denote white things and connote whiteness. The idea anticipates later distinctions such as Frege's sense and reference and Carnap's intension and extension.","etym":"Latin connotare, 'to mark along with', and denotare, 'to mark out'.","example":"'Bachelor' denotes all unmarried men and connotes being male and unmarried.","notConfused":"Not the same as connotation in everyday speech, meaning a word's emotional associations, as when 'cheap' connotes poor quality.","field":"Language","related":["Extension and intension","Sense and reference","Descriptivism about names"],"thinkers":["mill"],"tags":["denotation","connotation","mill","john stuart mill","reference","meaning"]},{"term":"Extension and intension","def":"A term's extension is the set of things it applies to; its intension is the meaning or criterion that determines which things those are.","more":"Rudolf Carnap made the distinction standard in Meaning and Necessity (1947), building on Frege and earlier logicians. Two terms can share an extension but differ in intension: Quine's example is 'creature with a heart' and 'creature with kidneys', which happen to pick out the same animals. In possible-worlds semantics, an intension is treated as a function giving an extension in each possible world. Contexts where only extension matters are called extensional.","etym":"Latin extensio, 'stretching out', and intensio, 'stretching' or 'tension'.","example":"'Morning star' and 'evening star' have the same extension, Venus, but different intensions.","notConfused":"Not the same as intention (with a t), which means purpose; intension with an s concerns meaning.","field":"Language","related":["Connotation and denotation","Sense and reference","Possible-worlds semantics"],"thinkers":["carnap","quine"],"tags":["extension","intension","reference","meaning","sense","carnap"]},{"term":"Pragmatics","def":"The study of how context and the speaker's purposes shape what is communicated, beyond the literal meaning of the words.","more":"Charles Morris in 1938 divided the study of signs into syntax (relations between signs), semantics (signs and what they stand for) and pragmatics (signs and their users). H. P. Grice's theory of implicature and J. L. Austin's speech act theory are central to pragmatics. A key debate concerns where semantics ends and pragmatics begins, for example whether 'some' literally means 'not all'.","etym":"Greek pragma, 'deed' or 'affair'.","example":"'Can you pass the salt?' is literally a question about ability, but pragmatically a request.","notConfused":"Not the same as pragmatism, the philosophical movement of Peirce, James and Dewey.","field":"Language","related":["Implicature","Speech act theory","Truth-conditional semantics"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["pragmatic","context","speech act","implicature","semantics","linguistics"]},{"term":"Implicature","def":"What a speaker suggests or communicates beyond the literal meaning of what they say.","more":"H. P. Grice introduced the term in lectures given in 1967 and published 'Logic and Conversation' in 1975. He argued that conversation follows a cooperative principle with maxims of quantity, quality, relation and manner, and that listeners infer extra meaning when speakers seem to flout them. Implicatures can be cancelled without contradiction, which distinguishes them from what is strictly said. Grice used the idea to defend the logical analysis of words like 'and' and 'or'.","etym":"From implicate, Latin implicare, 'to fold in'.","example":"A reference letter saying only 'Mr X has excellent handwriting and always arrived on time' implies he is not a good candidate.","notConfused":"Not the same as logical implication or entailment: an implicature can be cancelled ('...and in fact he's brilliant'), but an entailment cannot.","field":"Language","related":["Pragmatics","Entailment","Presupposition"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["conversational implicature","grice","implied meaning","implication","innuendo","cooperative principle"]},{"term":"Presupposition","def":"Something taken for granted by a statement or question, which must be true for it to be either true or false in the normal way.","more":"Frege noted that 'Kepler died in misery' presupposes that 'Kepler' refers to someone. P. F. Strawson, in 'On Referring' (1950), argued against Russell that 'the present king of France is bald' is neither true nor false, because its presupposition fails. Presuppositions survive negation: 'the king of France is not bald' still assumes there is one. Linguists study how presuppositions are triggered by words like 'again', 'stop' and 'the'.","etym":"Latin prae, 'before', and supponere, 'to place under'.","example":"'Jo has stopped smoking' and 'Jo hasn't stopped smoking' both presuppose that Jo used to smoke.","notConfused":"Not the same as an implicature: a presupposition survives negation, while an implicature typically doesn't.","field":"Language","related":["Definite description","Implicature","Loaded question"],"thinkers":["pfstrawson","russell","frege"],"tags":["presuppositions","presupposition failure","assumption","loaded question","strawson"]},{"term":"Performative utterance","def":"An utterance that does something rather than describing something, such as promising, naming or marrying, simply by being said in the right circumstances.","more":"J. L. Austin introduced the idea in his 1955 lectures, published as How to Do Things with Words (1962). Performatives are not true or false but 'felicitous' or 'infelicitous', depending on whether conditions are met (you can't name a ship you don't own). Austin went on to argue that all utterances perform acts, leading to speech act theory. Judith Butler later borrowed the idea of performativity in her theory of gender.","etym":"From perform, Old French parfournir, 'to complete'.","example":"Saying 'I promise to pay you back' doesn't report a promise; it makes one.","notConfused":"Not the same as a performance in the theatrical sense: a performative is a real act, such as making a bet, not pretending.","field":"Language","related":["Speech act theory","Speech act","Ordinary Language Philosophy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["performative","speech act","illocutionary act","austin","j. l. austin","performativity"]},{"term":"Compositionality","def":"The principle that the meaning of a complex expression is determined by the meanings of its parts and how they are combined.","more":"It is often attributed to Frege, though he never stated it in exactly this form. Compositionality explains how we can understand sentences we have never heard before, since we know the parts and the rules. Donald Davidson argued that a learnable language must be compositional. Idioms ('kick the bucket') and context-sensitive words pose challenges, and debates continue over how strictly natural languages obey the principle.","etym":"Latin componere, 'to put together'.","example":"You've never heard 'the purple giraffe juggled teacups', but you understand it instantly.","notConfused":"Not the same as composition in the metaphysical sense, which concerns how parts make up physical objects.","field":"Language","related":["Fregean semantics (sense and reference)","Truth-conditional semantics","Davidsonian semantics"],"thinkers":["frege"],"tags":["principle of compositionality","frege's principle","productivity","systematicity","semantics"]},{"term":"Linguistic turn","def":"The 20th-century shift in philosophy toward treating questions about language as the key to many traditional philosophical problems.","more":"The phrase was popularised by Richard Rorty's anthology The Linguistic Turn (1967), having been coined by Gustav Bergmann. Frege, Russell, the early and later Wittgenstein, the logical positivists and ordinary language philosophers all, in different ways, treated philosophy as the analysis of language. Some argued that many philosophical problems are confusions about words. Later analytic philosophy partly turned away from this, returning to metaphysics and the mind directly.","etym":null,"example":"Instead of asking 'what is goodness?', asking 'what do we mean when we call something good?'","notConfused":"Not the same as linguistics, the scientific study of language; the linguistic turn is about philosophical method.","field":"Language","related":["Analytic philosophy","Ordinary Language Philosophy","Logical positivism"],"thinkers":["rorty","wittgenstein","frege"],"tags":["turn to language","analytic philosophy","rorty","language","philosophy of language"]},{"term":"Picture theory of meaning","def":"The early Wittgenstein's view that a sentence is meaningful by picturing a possible state of affairs, sharing a logical form with it.","more":"Wittgenstein set it out in the Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921); he is reported to have been inspired by a model of a car accident used in a court case. Just as toy cars can represent real cars, names in a sentence stand for objects, and their arrangement shows how the objects are arranged. A consequence was that ethics, aesthetics and much of philosophy cannot be said, only shown. Wittgenstein later rejected the theory in favour of meaning as use.","etym":null,"example":"A map of the London Underground pictures the network because the arrangement of its lines matches the arrangement of the stations.","notConfused":"Not the same as the idea that words call up mental images: the 'picture' is a shared logical structure, not an image.","field":"Language","related":["Use theory of meaning","Logical form","Language game"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"tags":["picture theory","tractatus","wittgenstein","logical atomism","early wittgenstein"]},{"term":"Family resemblance","def":"Wittgenstein's idea that things called by the same word may share no single common feature, but are linked by a network of overlapping similarities.","more":"Wittgenstein introduced it in the Philosophical Investigations (1953), using the example of games: board games, ball games and solitaire share various features, but no one feature runs through all of them. Like members of a family, they resemble each other in different ways. The idea challenged the assumption that definitions must give necessary and sufficient conditions. It has been applied to concepts such as art, religion and disease.","etym":null,"example":"A sister may share her brother's nose and her father's eyes, while her brother and father look nothing alike.","notConfused":"Not the same as vagueness: a family resemblance concept can have clear cases without any common essence.","field":"Language","related":["Necessary and sufficient conditions","Language game","Cluster account of art"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"tags":["family resemblances","wittgenstein","philosophical investigations","concepts","definition"]},{"term":"Rule-following problem","def":"The puzzle of what makes it the case that someone is following one rule rather than another, since any finite past behaviour fits many different rules.","more":"Wittgenstein raised it in the Philosophical Investigations (1953), and Saul Kripke's Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language (1982) made it famous. Kripke's example: nothing in your past use of 'plus' seems to settle that you meant addition rather than 'quus', which agrees with addition for numbers below 57 but gives 5 otherwise. If no fact about a person fixes the rule, meaning itself seems threatened. Responses appeal to dispositions, community practice, or the claim that meaning is simply primitive.","etym":null,"example":"A child who has always continued '2, 4, 6, 8' correctly might, at 1,000, start adding 4 instead, and insist that's what 'add 2' meant.","notConfused":"Not the same as the private language argument, which it partly supports; the rule-following problem concerns all meaning, private or public.","field":"Language","related":["Kripke’s skeptical solution","Meaning skepticism","Private language argument"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein","kripke"],"tags":["rule following","kripkenstein","kripke","wittgenstein","quus","private language"]},{"term":"T-schema","def":"Alfred Tarski's pattern for defining truth: 'S' is true if and only if S, as in ''snow is white' is true if and only if snow is white.","more":"Tarski introduced it in 1933 as part of his semantic theory of truth, requiring any adequate definition of truth to imply every instance (his 'Convention T'). The schema looks trivial, but it gives truth a precise logical role. Tarski showed that a language cannot define its own truth predicate without paradox, leading to a hierarchy of languages. Deflationists take the T-schema to say nearly everything there is to say about truth.","etym":null,"example":"''The kettle is boiling' is true if and only if the kettle is boiling.'","notConfused":"Not the same as the correspondence theory of truth, though some read it that way; Tarski said his theory was neutral between philosophical views.","field":"Language","related":["Tarskian hierarchy","Deflationism about truth","Liar paradox"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["t schema","convention t","tarski","disquotation","semantic theory of truth","snow is white"]},{"term":"Frege’s puzzle","def":"The puzzle of how 'Hesperus is Phosphorus' can be informative while 'Hesperus is Hesperus' is trivial, when both names refer to the same planet.","more":"Gottlob Frege posed it in 'On Sense and Reference' (1892). Ancient astronomers learned something important when they discovered that the evening star and the morning star were both Venus. Frege's solution was that the names share a reference but differ in sense, or mode of presentation. Direct reference theorists, who hold that a name's meaning is just its referent, have to find other solutions.","etym":null,"example":"Learning that 'Lady Gaga' and 'Stefani Germanotta' are the same person tells you something new, even though it's just one person.","notConfused":"Not the same as the identity of indiscernibles, which is a metaphysical principle about objects rather than a puzzle about informative statements.","field":"Language","related":["Sense and reference","Russellian semantics (direct reference)","Referential opacity"],"thinkers":["frege"],"tags":["frege's puzzle","hesperus and phosphorus","morning star","cognitive significance","sense and reference"]},{"term":"Empty names","def":"Names that refer to nothing, such as 'Pegasus' or 'Vulcan', which nonetheless seem to be meaningful.","more":"If a name's meaning is just what it refers to, an empty name should be meaningless, and 'Pegasus doesn't exist' should say nothing. Russell's theory of descriptions treated such names as disguised descriptions; Alexius Meinong held that non-existent objects have a kind of being. Astronomers once named a planet, Vulcan, that was believed to orbit inside Mercury; it does not exist. Empty names remain a key problem for direct reference theories.","etym":null,"example":"'Sherlock Holmes lived at 221B Baker Street' seems true in some sense, though there was no Sherlock Holmes.","notConfused":"Not the same as a definite description that fails, such as 'the present king of France', though Russell thought ordinary names work like such descriptions.","field":"Language","related":["Russell’s theory of descriptions","Definite description","Make-believe theory"],"thinkers":["russell"],"tags":["empty name","non-referring terms","vacuous names","fiction","pegasus","negative existentials"]},{"term":"Metaphor","def":"A figure of speech that describes one thing in terms of another, such as calling time a thief.","more":"Aristotle discussed metaphor in the Poetics and Rhetoric, seeing a talent for it as a sign of genius. Max Black's 'interaction' theory (1955) held that metaphors create new meaning by bringing two systems of ideas together. Donald Davidson argued in 1978 that metaphors mean nothing beyond their literal (usually false) meaning; they simply make us notice things. George Lakoff and Mark Johnson argued in 1980 that much ordinary thought is structured by metaphor.","etym":"Greek metaphora, 'carrying over'.","example":"'Juliet is the sun' doesn't claim she is a ball of gas, but invites us to see her as radiant and life-giving.","notConfused":"Not the same as a simile, which makes the comparison explicit with 'like' or 'as'.","field":"Language","related":["Pragmatics","Implicature","Rhetoric"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["metaphors","figurative language","figure of speech","analogy","simile"]},{"term":"Natural and non-natural meaning","def":"H. P. Grice's distinction between meaning as natural sign (smoke means fire) and meaning as intended communication (a word or gesture someone uses to convey something).","more":"Grice drew the distinction in his 1957 paper 'Meaning'. Natural meaning is factive: if spots mean measles, the person has measles. Non-natural meaning depends on a speaker's intention to produce a belief in a listener by getting them to recognise that intention. This became the basis of intention-based semantics and influenced theories of communication generally.","etym":null,"example":"'Those clouds mean rain' is natural meaning; 'three rings of the bell mean the bus is full' is non-natural meaning.","notConfused":"Not the same as literal and figurative meaning: both kinds of non-natural meaning depend on intentions.","field":"Language","related":["Intention-based semantics","Implicature","Pragmatics"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["natural meaning","non-natural meaning","grice","meaning nn","speaker meaning"]},{"term":"Universal grammar","def":"Noam Chomsky's hypothesis that all human languages share a deep structure built into the human mind, which makes language learning possible.","more":"Chomsky developed the idea from the 1960s. He argued that children acquire complex grammar quickly and with limited data, so they must have an innate language faculty with built-in principles, set differently by each language's 'parameters'. The view revived rationalist ideas of innate knowledge. Critics argue that general learning abilities and rich input can explain language acquisition, and point to the great diversity of languages.","etym":null,"example":"Children everywhere seem to know without being taught that a question is formed by moving the 'is' of the main clause, not simply the first 'is' in the sentence.","notConfused":"Not the same as a universal language like Esperanto, which is an invented language, not a structure in the mind.","field":"Language","related":["Poverty of the stimulus","Innate ideas","Linguistic universalism"],"thinkers":["chomsky","pinker"],"tags":["chomsky","noam chomsky","innate grammar","language acquisition device","nativism","generative grammar"]},{"term":"Poverty of the stimulus","def":"The argument that children learn grammar too richly and quickly for it to come from experience alone, so some linguistic knowledge must be innate.","more":"Noam Chomsky made the argument central to linguistics, and used this name for it in 1980. Children rarely hear examples of certain errors being corrected, yet they don't make them. Chomsky saw this as a modern version of Plato's and Descartes' arguments for innate ideas. Empiricist critics argue children receive more data than Chomsky assumed and learn statistical patterns very efficiently.","etym":null,"example":"A toddler, never told the rule, still says 'is the man who is tall here?' rather than 'is the man who tall is here?'","notConfused":"Not the same as Meno's paradox, though both argue from learning to innate knowledge.","field":"Language","related":["Universal grammar","Innate ideas","Rationalism"],"thinkers":["chomsky","pinker"],"tags":["poverty of stimulus","chomsky","language acquisition","nativism","innateness"]},{"term":"Signifier and signified","def":"Ferdinand de Saussure's two sides of a linguistic sign: the signifier is the sound or written form, and the signified is the concept it stands for.","more":"Saussure presented the distinction in lectures published after his death as the Course in General Linguistics (1916). He emphasised that the link between them is arbitrary: nothing about the sound 'tree' resembles trees. He also argued that signs get their value from their differences from other signs in the system. These ideas founded structuralism and were developed by Roland Barthes and criticised by Jacques Derrida.","etym":"French signifiant and signifié, 'signifying' and 'signified'.","example":"'Dog', 'chien' and 'Hund' are different signifiers for the same signified concept.","notConfused":"Not the same as Frege's sense and reference: the signified is a concept, not the thing in the world.","field":"Language","related":["Deconstruction","Sense and reference","Postmodernism"],"thinkers":["barthes"],"tags":["signifier","signified","saussure","semiotics","sign","structuralism"]},{"term":"Normal science","def":"Thomas Kuhn's term for the ordinary, puzzle-solving work scientists do within an accepted paradigm, without questioning its foundations.","more":"Kuhn introduced it in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962). Most scientists, he argued, are not testing their basic theory but extending it, measuring constants and solving problems it defines. Anomalies that resist solution can build up into a crisis, which may end in a scientific revolution and a new paradigm. Popper objected that normal science, so described, is uncritical and even dangerous.","etym":null,"example":"Chemists using the periodic table to predict and measure the properties of new compounds, without doubting the table itself.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'normal' meaning ordinary or average: it is Kuhn's technical term for paradigm-guided research.","field":"Science","related":["Paradigm","Kuhn’s paradigm view","Incommensurability"],"thinkers":["kuhn","popper"],"tags":["kuhn","thomas kuhn","paradigm","puzzle-solving","scientific revolutions"]},{"term":"Demarcation problem","def":"The problem of finding a principled way to distinguish science from non-science and pseudoscience.","more":"Karl Popper made it central, proposing falsifiability as the criterion: a theory is scientific only if some observation could show it false. The logical positivists had used verifiability instead. Larry Laudan argued in 1983 that no single criterion works and the problem should be abandoned, but many philosophers now favour multi-criteria approaches. The question matters for law, education and public health, for example in debates over teaching creationism.","etym":"Spanish demarcar, 'to mark out boundaries'.","example":"Is astrology science? It makes predictions, but its practitioners tend to explain away failures rather than accept them.","notConfused":"Not the same as the distinction between good and bad science: a flawed experiment can still be science.","field":"Science","related":["Falsifiability","Pseudoscience","Multi-criteria demarcation"],"thinkers":["popper"],"tags":["demarcation","science vs pseudoscience","popper","falsifiability","what is science"]},{"term":"Pseudoscience","def":"A body of claims or practices presented as scientific that does not follow the methods or standards of science.","more":"Popper's examples included astrology and, more controversially, psychoanalysis and some versions of Marxism, which he thought could explain away any outcome. Common signs of pseudoscience include immunity to refutation, reliance on anecdote, lack of progress and hostility to criticism. Philosophers disagree about whether there is a sharp line, which is the demarcation problem. Calling something pseudoscience is a serious charge, since it claims the mantle of science falsely.","etym":"Greek pseudēs, 'false', and Latin scientia, 'knowledge'.","example":"Homeopathy claims scientific backing, but its central idea, that extreme dilution increases potency, has failed repeated controlled tests.","notConfused":"Not the same as non-science, such as poetry or ethics, which doesn't claim to be science at all.","field":"Science","related":["Demarcation problem","Falsifiability","Ad hoc hypothesis"],"thinkers":["popper","freud"],"tags":["fake science","junk science","quackery","pseudo-science","demarcation problem"]},{"term":"Paradox of the ravens","def":"Carl Hempel's puzzle that, by plausible principles, seeing a green apple counts as evidence that all ravens are black.","more":"Hempel presented it in 1945. 'All ravens are black' is logically equivalent to 'all non-black things are non-ravens', and a green apple is a non-black non-raven, so it seems to confirm both. Hempel himself accepted the conclusion and argued our intuition is mistaken. Bayesian responses say the apple does confirm the hypothesis, but only to a tiny degree, because there are so many non-black things.","etym":null,"example":"An ornithologist claims to be studying ravens while sitting indoors noting the colours of her shoes and books.","notConfused":"Not the same as Goodman's grue problem, which concerns which predicates can be projected, not which observations confirm.","field":"Science","related":["Confirmation","Contrapositive","Grue"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["raven paradox","hempel's paradox","hempel","confirmation paradox","black ravens","green apple"]},{"term":"Pessimistic meta-induction","def":"The argument that since most successful scientific theories of the past turned out false, our current successful theories are probably false too.","more":"Larry Laudan gave the classic version in 1981, listing theories such as the ether theory of light and the caloric theory of heat that were successful yet referred to nothing. It is a major challenge to scientific realism and to the no-miracles argument. Realists respond that modern theories are more successful, or that the parts of old theories doing the real work survived, as in structural realism. Others reply that the historical sample is biased.","etym":"Greek meta, 'beyond': an induction about past inductions.","example":"Doctors once confidently treated disease by rebalancing the 'four humours'; might today's medicine look as odd in 300 years?","notConfused":"Not the same as general scepticism about science: it targets the claim that successful theories are approximately true, not science's practical success.","field":"Science","related":["Scientific realism","No-miracles argument","Structural realism"],"thinkers":["worrall"],"tags":["pessimistic induction","laudan","anti-realism","scientific realism","theory change"]},{"term":"Auxiliary hypothesis","def":"A background assumption needed, alongside a main theory, to derive testable predictions from it.","more":"Pierre Duhem pointed out that a theory never meets experiment alone: predictions depend on assumptions about instruments, conditions and other theories. So when a prediction fails, logic does not tell us whether to blame the main theory or an auxiliary. This underlies the Duhem-Quine thesis and confirmation holism. A famous success came in 1846, when astronomers, instead of rejecting Newton's theory over Uranus's odd orbit, added the hypothesis of an unseen planet and found Neptune.","etym":"Latin auxilium, 'help'.","example":"If a recipe fails, you might blame the recipe or assume your oven runs cold; the oven assumption is an auxiliary hypothesis.","notConfused":"Not the same as an ad hoc hypothesis: an auxiliary can be independently tested and well supported.","field":"Science","related":["Confirmation holism","Ad hoc hypothesis","Underdetermination"],"thinkers":["duhem","quine"],"tags":["auxiliary assumption","background assumptions","duhem-quine thesis","quine-duhem","holism","underdetermination"]},{"term":"Ad hoc hypothesis","def":"An assumption added to a theory just to save it from contrary evidence, without any independent support.","more":"Popper held that adding ad hoc hypotheses makes a theory less falsifiable and is a sign of bad science. Whether a hypothesis is ad hoc is often only clear later, depending on whether it leads to new confirmed predictions. Imre Lakatos distinguished progressive from degenerating research programmes on this basis. The postulated planet Neptune turned out well; a similar planet, Vulcan, proposed to explain Mercury's orbit, was never found, and Einstein's general relativity explained the orbit instead.","etym":"Latin ad hoc, 'for this (purpose)'.","example":"When a psychic's prediction fails, saying 'the sceptic in the room disturbed the energy'.","notConfused":"Not the same as an auxiliary hypothesis in general: auxiliaries are needed everywhere, but ad hoc ones have no support beyond rescuing the theory.","field":"Science","related":["Auxiliary hypothesis","Falsificationism","Lakatos’s methodology of research programmes"],"thinkers":["popper"],"tags":["ad hoc","post hoc rationalization","special pleading","moving the goalposts","falsifiability"]},{"term":"Crucial experiment","def":"An experiment that decisively settles which of two competing theories is true.","more":"Francis Bacon spoke of 'crucial instances', and Isaac Newton described his prism experiment of 1672 as an experimentum crucis. Pierre Duhem argued that true crucial experiments are impossible in physics, because a failed prediction can always be blamed on auxiliary assumptions, and because there may be further theories no one has considered. Still, some experiments, such as the 1919 eclipse observations of light bending, are treated as turning points in practice.","etym":"Latin experimentum crucis, 'experiment of the cross', from crux, a signpost at a crossroads.","example":"Sending light through a second prism to test whether prisms add colour or merely separate colours already in white light.","notConfused":"Not the same as a decisive proof: even 'crucial' experiments can later be reinterpreted.","field":"Science","related":["Auxiliary hypothesis","Confirmation holism","Underdetermination"],"thinkers":["bacon","duhem"],"tags":["experimentum crucis","decisive experiment","critical experiment","duhem","falsification"]},{"term":"Observational and theoretical terms","def":"The distinction between words for things we can directly observe, like 'red' or 'heavy', and words for things posited by theory, like 'electron' or 'gene'.","more":"Logical empiricists such as Rudolf Carnap relied on it, hoping to explain the meaning of theoretical terms through their links to observation. Critics, including N. R. Hanson and Thomas Kuhn, argued that all observation is theory-laden, so the line is blurry. Bas van Fraassen redrew it as a distinction between observable and unobservable things rather than terms, as the basis of constructive empiricism. Whether we should believe in unobservables is the core of the realism debate.","etym":null,"example":"'The needle points to 5' is observational; 'the current is 5 amps' uses a theoretical term.","notConfused":"Not the same as the distinction between facts and opinions: both kinds of term can occur in factual claims.","field":"Science","related":["Theory-ladenness","Constructive empiricism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["carnap","van_fraassen"],"tags":["observation terms","theoretical terms","observational-theoretical distinction","logical positivism","theory-ladenness"]},{"term":"Theoretical virtues","def":"Features that make a scientific theory preferable apart from fitting the data, such as simplicity, scope, coherence and fruitfulness.","more":"Thomas Kuhn listed five in a 1977 essay: accuracy, consistency, broad scope, simplicity and fruitfulness. He noted they can conflict and be weighed differently, so theory choice is not purely algorithmic. Realists argue these virtues are signs of truth; anti-realists such as van Fraassen say they are merely pragmatic. They play a key role in inference to the best explanation and in philosophical theory choice too.","etym":"Latin virtus, 'excellence'.","example":"The Sun-centred model of the solar system was preferred partly because it was simpler and explained more with fewer assumptions.","notConfused":"Not the same as moral or intellectual virtues of scientists, such as honesty; these are qualities of theories.","field":"Science","related":["Occam’s razor","Inference to the best explanation","Underdetermination"],"thinkers":["kuhn","van_fraassen"],"tags":["theory choice","simplicity","parsimony","explanatory virtues","elegance","fruitfulness"]},{"term":"Ceteris paribus laws","def":"Laws or generalisations that hold only 'other things being equal', allowing for exceptions when interfering factors are present.","more":"Most laws in biology, economics and psychology, and arguably physics, seem to be like this: demand falls as price rises unless something else changes. Nancy Cartwright argued in How the Laws of Physics Lie (1983) that even fundamental physical laws hold only in idealised conditions. Critics worry that ceteris paribus clauses make laws empty, because any exception can be blamed on 'other things'. Defenders try to specify what makes such laws testable.","etym":"Latin ceteris paribus, 'other things being equal'.","example":"'Heating water makes it boil at 100°C', unless you're up a mountain or the water is salty.","notConfused":"Not the same as a statistical law, which says something happens with a certain probability rather than unless interfered with.","field":"Science","related":["Laws of nature","Autonomy of the special sciences","Idealisation"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["ceteris paribus","other things being equal","hedged laws","exceptions","laws of nature"]},{"term":"Vitalism","def":"The view that living things are alive because of a special non-physical force or principle, not explicable by physics and chemistry alone.","more":"Versions go back to Aristotle's idea of the soul as the form of a living body. In the 19th and early 20th centuries, thinkers such as Hans Driesch and Henri Bergson (with his élan vital) defended it against mechanism. The synthesis of urea from inorganic chemicals in 1828 is often cited as a blow to vitalism, though historians think its impact has been exaggerated. Molecular biology has left little room for vital forces, and the term is now mostly used critically.","etym":"Latin vita, 'life'.","example":"Believing that a cell is more than its molecules because of a 'life force' that leaves at death.","notConfused":"Not the same as emergentism, which allows that life has new properties without positing a special non-physical force.","field":"Science","related":["Mechanical philosophy","Emergence","Entelechy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["vital force","elan vital","élan vital","life force","bergson","anti-mechanism"]},{"term":"Mechanical philosophy","def":"The 17th-century view that nature works like a machine, explicable by matter in motion and contact between particles, without hidden purposes or occult qualities.","more":"Descartes, Pierre Gassendi, Robert Boyle and Hobbes developed versions of it, replacing Aristotelian forms and final causes. It helped shape the Scientific Revolution. Newton's gravity, acting at a distance without contact, troubled strict mechanists, who thought it brought back occult forces. The ideal of mechanistic explanation remains influential in biology and neuroscience.","etym":"Greek mēchanē, 'device' or 'machine'.","example":"Explaining a magnet not by its 'attractive virtue' but by tiny particles streaming between it and the iron.","notConfused":"Not the same as mechanics, the branch of physics: the mechanical philosophy was a general picture of nature.","field":"Science","related":["Vitalism","Materialism","Four causes"],"thinkers":["descartes","hobbes"],"tags":["mechanism","mechanistic philosophy","corpuscularianism","clockwork universe","boyle","descartes"]},{"term":"Laplace’s demon","def":"An imagined intellect that knows the position and force of every particle at one moment and could therefore calculate the entire past and future.","more":"Pierre-Simon Laplace described it in 1814 to illustrate determinism in Newtonian physics. If the laws are deterministic, such a being would find nothing uncertain. Quantum indeterminacy, chaos theory and limits on computation have all been offered as reasons such a demon is impossible in practice or principle. The idea is still used to frame debates about determinism and free will.","etym":null,"example":"If the demon knew every particle's state when you woke up, it could predict what you'll have for dinner in ten years.","notConfused":"Not the same as Descartes' evil demon, which is a sceptical deceiver; Laplace's demon is a perfect predictor.","field":"Science","related":["Causal determinism","Determinism","Indeterminism (physical)"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["laplace's demon","laplace","determinism","causal determinism","clockwork universe","predictability"]},{"term":"Measurement problem","def":"The problem in quantum mechanics of explaining why measurements have definite outcomes when the theory describes systems as being in combinations (superpositions) of possibilities.","more":"Quantum theory's equations evolve smoothly, yet measurements give single results. Erwin Schrödinger's 1935 cat thought experiment dramatised the problem: a cat linked to a quantum event seems to be both alive and dead until observed. Interpretations such as Copenhagen, many-worlds, pilot-wave theory and collapse theories offer different solutions. Philosophers of physics regard it as one of the deepest unsolved problems about reality.","etym":null,"example":"Schrödinger's cat: a sealed box where a cat's life depends on whether an atom decays.","notConfused":"Not the same as the observer effect, where measuring something disturbs it; the measurement problem is about why there is a single definite result at all.","field":"Science","related":["Copenhagen interpretation","Many-worlds interpretation","Objective collapse theories"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["quantum measurement","wave function collapse","schrodinger's cat","schrödinger's cat","quantum mechanics","interpretations of quantum mechanics"]},{"term":"Context of discovery and context of justification","def":"The distinction between how a scientist comes up with an idea and how that idea is tested and justified.","more":"Hans Reichenbach introduced the terms in Experience and Prediction (1938). Logical empiricists and Popper held that philosophy of science should study only justification, leaving discovery to psychology. Kekulé's reported daydream of a snake biting its tail, said to have led to the ring structure of benzene, is a stock example: the dream doesn't justify the theory. Kuhn and later historians argued that the two contexts are more intertwined than this suggests.","etym":null,"example":"An idea may come to you in the shower, but you justify it in the lab.","notConfused":"Not the same as the genetic fallacy, though related: the distinction warns against judging an idea by its origins.","field":"Science","related":["Genetic fallacy","Hypothetico-deductive method","Falsificationism"],"thinkers":["popper"],"tags":["context of discovery","context of justification","reichenbach","discovery","justification"]},{"term":"Confirmation","def":"The relation in which evidence supports a hypothesis, making it more credible.","more":"Carl Hempel developed a logical theory of confirmation in the 1940s, which ran into the paradox of the ravens; Goodman's grue problem showed further difficulties. Bayesian confirmation theory says evidence confirms a hypothesis when it raises its probability. Popper argued that theories cannot really be confirmed, only corroborated by surviving tests. Questions about how much a given piece of evidence confirms, and why, remain central to philosophy of science.","etym":"Latin confirmare, 'to strengthen'.","example":"Finding fossils in the predicted rock layers confirms evolutionary theory without proving it.","notConfused":"Not the same as verification: confirmation can be partial and increase gradually, while verification would settle the matter conclusively.","field":"Science","related":["Bayesianism","Paradox of the ravens","Confirmation holism"],"thinkers":["goodman","popper"],"tags":["evidential support","inductive support","confirmation theory","corroboration","evidence"]},{"term":"Replication crisis","def":"The discovery, especially since the 2010s, that many published scientific findings, notably in psychology and medicine, fail when other researchers repeat the studies.","more":"A large 2015 project by the Open Science Collaboration repeated 100 psychology studies and successfully replicated fewer than half. Causes include publication bias, small samples, flexible data analysis ('p-hacking') and incentives that reward striking results. Philosophers of science have discussed what it shows about statistical methods, scientific objectivity and trust in experts. Reforms include pre-registering studies and sharing data.","etym":null,"example":"A famous study claims a 'power pose' boosts confidence hormones, but later larger studies fail to find the effect.","notConfused":"Not the same as fraud: most failed replications come from honest but flawed methods and incentives.","field":"Science","related":["Error statistics","Confirmation bias","Objectivity"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["reproducibility crisis","replicability","replication","p-hacking","publication bias"]},{"term":"Fine-tuning","def":"The observation that some physical constants seem to fall within the narrow range that allows life, so that small changes would have made life impossible.","more":"Physicists have noted that the strength of gravity, the cosmological constant and other values appear finely balanced. Some, such as Richard Swinburne, use this as evidence of a designer; others suggest a multiverse in which every value occurs somewhere. Still others argue that we lack a meaningful way to assign probabilities to constants, or that life might arise under other conditions. It is the main modern form of the design argument.","etym":null,"example":"Like finding that a dial controlling the universe had to be set within a hair's breadth for stars and chemistry to exist.","notConfused":"Not the same as the anthropic principle, which is one proposed explanation of why we observe fine-tuning.","field":"Science","related":["Design argument","Anthropic principle","Many-worlds interpretation"],"thinkers":["swinburne"],"tags":["fine tuning","fine-tuning argument","cosmological constants","design argument","multiverse"]},{"term":"Anthropic principle","def":"The principle that what we can observe about the universe must be compatible with the existence of observers like us.","more":"The physicist Brandon Carter introduced the term in 1973. The weak version is a selection effect: we couldn't find ourselves in a universe that couldn't support life, so we shouldn't be surprised that ours does. Stronger versions, claiming the universe must produce life, are much more controversial. Combined with a multiverse, the weak principle offers a non-theistic explanation of fine-tuning, though critics say it explains too little.","etym":"Greek anthrōpos, 'human being'.","example":"Fish in a pond shouldn't be amazed that their world contains water; they couldn't exist in one without it.","notConfused":"Not the same as anthropocentrism, the view that humans are the centre of value or of the universe.","field":"Science","related":["Fine-tuning","Design argument","Anthropocentrism"],"thinkers":["bostrom"],"tags":["anthropic reasoning","weak anthropic principle","strong anthropic principle","observation selection","fine-tuning"]},{"term":"Mill’s methods","def":"Five methods set out by John Stuart Mill for discovering causes by comparing cases: agreement, difference, joint agreement and difference, residues, and concomitant variation.","more":"Mill presented them in A System of Logic (1843). The method of agreement looks for a factor common to all cases of an effect; the method of difference compares cases with and without the effect, which is the logic of controlled experiments. Concomitant variation looks for factors that change together. Critics note that the methods work only if we have already identified the relevant possible causes.","etym":null,"example":"Everyone who got food poisoning ate the prawns, and no one who skipped them got ill: the methods of agreement and difference point to the prawns.","notConfused":"Not the same as Mill's harm principle or his utilitarianism, which belong to his ethics and politics.","field":"Science","related":["Causation","Induction","Cum hoc ergo propter hoc"],"thinkers":["mill"],"tags":["mill's methods","method of agreement","method of difference","causal inference","john stuart mill"]},{"term":"Scientific model","def":"A simplified representation of a system, such as a diagram, equation set or physical object, used to understand, explain or predict its behaviour.","more":"Examples range from the Bohr model of the atom to climate simulations and the use of fruit flies as model organisms. Philosophers ask how models represent, given that they are deliberately inaccurate in many respects. Mary Morgan and Margaret Morrison argued in 1999 that models are partly independent of both theory and data, 'mediating' between them. Using several incompatible models of the same thing is common, which raises questions for realism.","etym":"Latin modulus, 'small measure'.","example":"A map of the Underground leaves out real distances, but it is the right model for planning your route.","notConfused":"Not the same as a model in logic, which is a structure that makes a set of sentences true.","field":"Science","related":["Idealisation","Scientific perspectivism","Scientific pluralism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["model","models","modelling","modeling","representation","simulation"]},{"term":"Idealisation","def":"Deliberately simplifying a scientific description by assuming conditions that are known to be false, such as frictionless planes or perfectly rational agents.","more":"Galileo made idealisation central to physics, studying motion as it would be without air resistance. Idealised laws and models can be highly useful even though nothing exactly satisfies them. Philosophers debate whether idealisations are approximations to the truth, harmless fictions or tools that reveal real tendencies. Nancy Cartwright argued that this shows laws of physics, taken literally, are false.","etym":"Greek idea, 'form' or 'pattern'.","example":"Physics problems that tell you to 'ignore air resistance' when dropping a ball from a tower.","notConfused":"Not the same as idealism, the metaphysical view that reality is fundamentally mental.","field":"Science","related":["Scientific model","Ceteris paribus laws","Instrumentalism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["idealization","abstraction","simplification","frictionless plane","models"]},{"term":"Problem of old evidence","def":"The puzzle that, on Bayesian theory, evidence already known when a theory is proposed cannot confirm it, though in practice it often seems to.","more":"Clark Glymour raised it in Theory and Evidence (1980). Einstein's general relativity was strongly supported by explaining the odd orbit of Mercury, which astronomers had known about for decades. But if the evidence already has probability 1, Bayes' theorem says it cannot raise the theory's probability. Proposed solutions appeal to the discovery that the theory implies the evidence, or to counterfactual degrees of belief.","etym":null,"example":"A detective's new theory is convincing partly because it explains a clue everyone noticed weeks ago.","notConfused":"Not the same as predictivism, the view that novel predictions count more than accommodated data, though the two debates are related.","field":"Science","related":["Bayesianism","Confirmation","Predictivism"],"thinkers":["einstein"],"tags":["old evidence","glymour","bayesianism","mercury perihelion","confirmation"]},{"term":"Transcendental argument","def":"An argument that something must be true because it is a necessary condition for something we cannot doubt, such as having experience or thought at all.","more":"Kant is the model: in the Critique of Pure Reason (1781) he argued that concepts like causation must apply to experience because experience would otherwise be impossible. P. F. Strawson revived such arguments in Individuals (1959) against scepticism. Barry Stroud argued in 1968 that they can at most show how we must think, not how the world must be. They remain a distinctive tool for answering sceptics.","etym":"Latin transcendere, 'to climb over'.","example":"Arguing that a sceptic who doubts language has meaning must rely on meaningful language to state that doubt.","notConfused":"Not the same as transcendence in religion: 'transcendental' here means concerning the conditions that make experience possible.","field":"Method","related":["Transcendental","Kantian synthesis (transcendental idealism)","Skepticism"],"thinkers":["kant","pfstrawson"],"tags":["transcendental arguments","kant","transcendental deduction","necessary conditions","anti-scepticism"]},{"term":"Conceptual engineering","def":"The project of assessing and improving our concepts, rather than only describing them as they are.","more":"The term became common in the 2010s, with Herman Cappelen's Fixing Language (2018) a key text. Sally Haslanger's proposals for defining 'gender' and 'race' in ways that serve social justice are influential examples. Carnap's earlier idea of explication is often seen as a forerunner. Critics ask whether we can really change concepts at will and how to keep debates from becoming merely verbal.","etym":null,"example":"Scientists redefining 'planet' in 2006 so that Pluto no longer qualified.","notConfused":"Not the same as conceptual analysis, which aims to describe the concepts we already have.","field":"Method","related":["Conceptual analysis","Explication","Stipulative definition"],"thinkers":["carnap"],"tags":["concept engineering","ameliorative analysis","conceptual ethics","concept revision","haslanger"]},{"term":"Method of doubt","def":"Descartes' strategy of setting aside every belief that could possibly be doubted, in order to find something absolutely certain.","more":"Descartes used it in the Discourse on the Method (1637) and the Meditations (1641), doubting the senses, then mathematics with the help of an evil demon hypothesis. The method led him to the cogito. Critics like C. S. Peirce argued that real doubt needs a real reason, and that we cannot doubt everything simply by deciding to. It is often called methodical or hyperbolic doubt, because it is deliberately exaggerated.","etym":null,"example":"Clearing out a cupboard by taking everything out and putting back only what you're sure is good.","notConfused":"Not the same as scepticism as a final position: Descartes doubted in order to reach certainty, not to stay in doubt.","field":"Method","related":["Cogito","Evil demon","Foundationalism"],"thinkers":["descartes","peirce"],"tags":["cartesian doubt","methodical doubt","hyperbolic doubt","descartes","cogito","foundationalism"]},{"term":"Hermeneutic circle","def":"The idea that we understand the parts of a text or situation through the whole, and the whole through its parts, moving back and forth between them.","more":"Friedrich Schleiermacher and Wilhelm Dilthey developed it for interpreting texts and history. Heidegger and Hans-Georg Gadamer argued that all understanding is circular in this way, because we always begin with prior expectations that are then revised. They held that the circle is not vicious, but the way understanding deepens. It is central to hermeneutics and to debates about interpretation in the humanities.","etym":"Greek hermēneuein, 'to interpret'.","example":"You can't fully understand a novel's first chapter until you've read the end, but you can't understand the end without the first chapter.","notConfused":"Not the same as circular reasoning: it describes how interpretation develops, not an argument assuming its conclusion.","field":"Method","related":["Hermeneutics","Verstehen","Begging the question"],"thinkers":["schleiermacher","heidegger"],"tags":["hermeneutics","interpretation","gadamer","heidegger","understanding"]},{"term":"Explication","def":"Rudolf Carnap's method of replacing a vague everyday concept with a more precise one that serves the same purposes better.","more":"Carnap set it out in Logical Foundations of Probability (1950). A good explication should resemble the original concept, be exact, be fruitful and be simple. His example is replacing 'fish' with the zoological concept piscis, which excludes whales. Explication is a forerunner of conceptual engineering, and it raises the question of whether philosophy should describe concepts or improve them.","etym":"Latin explicare, 'to unfold'.","example":"Replacing the everyday idea of 'warm' with the precise concept of temperature measured in degrees.","notConfused":"Not the same as explanation: explication clarifies a concept, while explanation says why something happens.","field":"Method","related":["Conceptual engineering","Conceptual analysis","Logical positivism"],"thinkers":["carnap"],"tags":["carnap","rational reconstruction","conceptual analysis","precisification","explicandum"]},{"term":"Experimental philosophy","def":"An approach that uses methods from psychology, such as surveys and experiments, to study people's intuitions about philosophical questions.","more":"It grew in the early 2000s. Jonathan Weinberg, Shaun Nichols and Stephen Stich reported in 2001 that intuitions about Gettier cases might vary across cultural groups, though later studies have challenged this. Joshua Knobe found in 2003 that people are more likely to say a side effect was intentional when it is harmful than when it is helpful, the 'Knobe effect'. Critics argue that philosophers' trained intuitions, not untrained ones, are what matter.","etym":null,"example":"Asking hundreds of people whether a CEO who knowingly harms the environment for profit did so intentionally.","notConfused":"Not the same as thought experiments, which philosophers consider from the armchair, though experimental philosophy often studies responses to them.","field":"Method","related":["Intuition","Thought experiment","Method of cases"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["x-phi","xphi","experimental philosophers","intuitions","surveys"]},{"term":"Moorean shift","def":"The move of turning an opponent's argument around: since you are more certain the conclusion is false than that all the premises are true, you reject a premise instead.","more":"It is named after G. E. Moore, who argued that he was more certain he had hands than he could ever be of any sceptical premise. William Rowe called it the 'G. E. Moore shift' in his 1979 paper on the problem of evil. It reflects the logical point that a valid argument can be used to prove the conclusion or, equally, to reject a premise. Philosophers sum it up as 'one person's modus ponens is another's modus tollens'.","etym":null,"example":"A sceptic argues: 'If you can't rule out dreaming, you don't know you're in a café.' The Moorean replies: 'I do know I'm in a café, so I can rule out dreaming.'","notConfused":"Not the same as dogmatism or refusing to listen: the shift accepts the argument is valid and asks which premise is least credible.","field":"Method","related":["Mooreanism (common sense)","Modus tollens","Modus ponens"],"thinkers":["moore","rowe"],"tags":["g. e. moore","here is a hand","modus tollens","common sense"]},{"term":"Ostensive definition","def":"Defining a word by pointing to examples of what it applies to, rather than by using other words.","more":"It seems to be how we first learn many words, and some empiricists thought it ties language to the world. Wittgenstein's Philosophical Investigations (1953) opens by criticising Augustine's picture of learning words this way. He argued that pointing is ambiguous: when you point at a red ball and say 'red', the learner could take you to mean ball, round or toy. Understanding ostension, he concluded, already requires a background of practice.","etym":"Latin ostendere, 'to show'.","example":"Pointing at a giraffe and saying 'giraffe' to a toddler.","notConfused":"Not the same as a stipulative definition, which introduces a new meaning in words rather than showing examples.","field":"Method","related":["Stipulative definition","Language game","Private language argument"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein","augustine"],"tags":["definition by pointing","ostension","pointing","examples","wittgenstein"]},{"term":"Stipulative definition","def":"A definition that fixes a meaning for a word by decision, for the purposes of a particular discussion, rather than reporting how the word is already used.","more":"Philosophers often stipulate technical meanings to avoid ambiguity, as when they define 'knowledge' or 'person' for an argument. A stipulative definition cannot be true or false, only useful or unhelpful. Problems arise when someone stipulates a meaning and then slides back to the ordinary meaning, a kind of equivocation. Lexical or reportive definitions, by contrast, aim to describe actual usage and can be wrong.","etym":"Latin stipulari, 'to bargain' or 'demand a formal promise'.","example":"'In this essay, by 'wealthy' I'll mean anyone earning over £100,000 a year.'","notConfused":"Not the same as a persuasive definition, which uses a definition to win an argument by exploiting a word's emotional force.","field":"Method","related":["Persuasive definition","Ostensive definition","Equivocation"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["stipulation","defined term","technical term","definition by fiat"]},{"term":"Persuasive definition","def":"A definition that gives a familiar, emotionally loaded word a new meaning in order to shift people's attitudes.","more":"Charles L. Stevenson introduced the idea in a 1938 paper, as part of his emotivist account of ethical language. A persuasive definition keeps a word's positive or negative feeling while changing what it refers to. Spotting it is important in political and ethical argument, where terms like 'freedom', 'violence' or 'art' are often redefined. It shows how definitions can carry hidden value judgments.","etym":"Latin persuadere, 'to convince'.","example":"'True courage is refusing to fight' redefines courage so that the admiration attached to it goes to pacifists.","notConfused":"Not the same as a stipulative definition made openly for clarity: a persuasive definition aims to shift feelings without saying so.","field":"Method","related":["Stipulative definition","Emotivism","Rhetoric"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["loaded definition","stevenson","emotive meaning","redefinition","rhetoric"]},{"term":"Method of cases","def":"The practice of testing philosophical theories against intuitive judgments about particular real or imagined cases.","more":"Much philosophy proceeds this way: a theory says X, a case seems to show not-X, and the theory is revised. Gettier's 1963 cases against the justified-true-belief analysis are a famous success; trolley cases in ethics are another example. Experimental philosophers question whether such intuitions are reliable or shared. Defenders argue that we cannot do without judgments about cases, and that reflective equilibrium balances them against principles.","etym":null,"example":"Testing a definition of 'lying' against the case of an actor reading a false line on stage.","notConfused":"Not the same as casuistry in ethics, which reasons from cases to decide what to do, rather than to test general theories.","field":"Method","related":["Thought experiment","Counterexample","Reflective equilibrium"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["thought experiments","intuitions","case method","counterexamples","reflective equilibrium"]},{"term":"Disputatio","def":"The formal method of debate used in medieval universities, in which a question is posed, objections are stated, an answer is given and each objection is replied to.","more":"It was central to scholastic education from the 13th century. Aquinas's Summa Theologiae follows its structure: each article lists objections, an authority on the other side ('on the contrary'), Aquinas's own answer ('I answer that') and replies to the objections. The method trained students to state opposing views fairly before rejecting them. Many modern philosophy papers still follow a similar objection-and-reply pattern.","etym":"Latin disputatio, 'discussion' or 'debate'.","example":"Asking 'Does God exist?', first setting out the best arguments that he does not, then answering and replying to each.","notConfused":"Not the same as a dispute in the sense of a quarrel: it was a structured academic exercise.","field":"Method","related":["Scholasticism","Dialectic","Steelman"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"tags":["disputation","scholasticism","quaestio","medieval university","debate","scholastic method"]},{"term":"Aphorism","def":"A short, pointed saying that expresses a thought or truth in a memorable form.","more":"Philosophers from Heraclitus to Pascal and Nietzsche have written in fragments or aphorisms. Nietzsche thought aphorisms could provoke readers to think for themselves rather than follow a system, though he warned they must be read slowly and 'chewed'. The form suits insight but can make arguments hard to reconstruct. Wittgenstein's later work, written in numbered remarks, has a similar style.","etym":"Greek aphorismos, 'definition' or 'distinction'.","example":"Pascal: 'The heart has its reasons, which reason does not know.'","notConfused":"Not the same as a proverb, which is a traditional saying of unknown authorship; an aphorism usually has a known author.","field":"Method","related":["Rhetoric","Philosophical dialogue","Perspectivism"],"thinkers":["nietzsche","pascal","heraclitus"],"tags":["aphorisms","maxim","saying","epigram","adage","nietzsche"]},{"term":"Philosophical dialogue","def":"A form of philosophical writing presented as a conversation between characters who argue for different views.","more":"Plato's dialogues, with Socrates as the main speaker, are the founding examples. Later classics include Berkeley's Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous (1713) and Hume's Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (published 1779). The form lets an author explore objections vividly and leave questions open, though it can make it hard to tell what the author thinks. Modern authors still use it for teaching.","etym":"Greek dialogos, 'conversation'.","example":"Two characters, one believing in free will and one not, debating over a series of evenings.","notConfused":"Not the same as dialectic, which is a method of reasoning, though dialogues often use it.","field":"Method","related":["Socratic method","Dialectic","Aphorism"],"thinkers":["plato","berkeley","hume"],"tags":["dialogue","dialogues","platonic dialogue","socratic dialogue","conversation"]},{"term":"Regulative ideal","def":"An ideal we can never fully reach but that guides and organises our inquiry or action.","more":"Kant distinguished regulative from constitutive principles in the Critique of Pure Reason: ideas such as a complete, unified science cannot be given in experience, but they rightly direct our search for ever more systematic knowledge. C. S. Peirce treated truth as what inquiry would converge on in the long run, a similar guiding ideal. The notion has been applied to ethics, democracy and objectivity.","etym":"Latin regula, 'rule'.","example":"No map is perfectly accurate, but the ideal of a perfect map guides cartographers to improve theirs.","notConfused":"Not the same as a goal one expects to achieve: a regulative ideal is valuable precisely as a direction, not a destination.","field":"Method","related":["Transcendental","Objectivity","Pragmatist theory of truth"],"thinkers":["kant","peirce"],"tags":["regulative ideals","kant","regulative principle","ideal","guiding ideal"]},{"term":"Socratic irony","def":"Socrates' habit of claiming ignorance and praising others' wisdom while questioning them in ways that expose their confusions.","more":"In Plato's dialogues, Socrates often says he knows nothing and asks to be taught, which draws others into confident claims they can't defend. Scholars debate how sincere his claims of ignorance were. Søren Kierkegaard wrote his dissertation on the concept of irony with continual reference to Socrates (1841). The technique survives in teaching, where feigning puzzlement can make students think more carefully.","etym":"Greek eirōneia, 'dissimulation' or 'feigned ignorance'.","example":"A teacher asking innocently, 'So, if heavier things fall faster, what happens when you tie a light stone to a heavy one?'","notConfused":"Not the same as sarcasm: Socratic irony is less about mockery and more about inviting reflection.","field":"Method","related":["Socratic method","Aporia","Dialectic"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato","kierkegaard"],"tags":["irony","feigned ignorance","eironeia","socrates","socratic method"]},{"term":"Verstehen","def":"Understanding human action from the inside, by grasping the meanings and intentions it has for those involved, as a method of the human sciences.","more":"Wilhelm Dilthey argued that the human sciences aim at Verstehen (understanding), while the natural sciences aim at Erklären (explanation by causal laws). Max Weber made interpretive understanding central to sociology, while also wanting causal explanation. Positivists argued that understanding is a heuristic, not a separate method. The debate continues about whether social science needs methods different from natural science.","etym":"German verstehen, 'to understand'.","example":"Explaining why someone bows in Japan by grasping what bowing means to them, not by measuring their muscle movements.","notConfused":"Not the same as empathy in the everyday sense: Verstehen aims at understanding meanings and reasons, not simply sharing feelings.","field":"Method","related":["Hermeneutic circle","Hermeneutics","Ideal type"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["understanding","interpretive understanding","weber","max weber","dilthey","interpretive sociology"]},{"term":"Ideal type","def":"Max Weber's tool of a deliberately exaggerated, simplified model of a social phenomenon, used to compare and analyse real cases.","more":"Weber described it in his essays on method from 1904. Examples include his ideal types of bureaucracy and of charismatic, traditional and legal authority. An ideal type is not an average or a moral ideal; real cases are measured by how far they deviate from it. Critics question whether such models capture reality or simply impose the researcher's interests.","etym":null,"example":"Comparing a real company to a 'pure bureaucracy' with fixed rules and strict hierarchy, to see where it departs from the type.","notConfused":"Not the same as an ideal in the moral sense: 'ideal' here means existing as an idea, not being best.","field":"Method","related":["Verstehen","Idealisation","Methodological individualism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["ideal types","weber","max weber","model","pure type"]},{"term":"Methodological individualism","def":"The principle that social phenomena should be explained in terms of the actions, beliefs and interactions of individuals.","more":"Max Weber is often seen as its founder, and the economist Joseph Schumpeter coined the phrase in 1908. Karl Popper and Friedrich Hayek defended it against theories that treat nations, classes or history as having their own laws or purposes. Holists reply that social structures, such as institutions and norms, have causal powers of their own. It is a principle about explanation, not necessarily about ethics or politics.","etym":null,"example":"Explaining a bank run by the decisions of individual depositors worried about their savings, rather than by 'the market panicking'.","notConfused":"Not the same as political individualism or egoism: one can be a methodological individualist and still value community.","field":"Method","related":["Holism","Reductionism","Ideal type"],"thinkers":["popper","hayek"],"tags":["individualism","weber","popper","holism","reductionism"]},{"term":"Methodological naturalism","def":"The principle that science should seek only natural explanations, appealing to natural causes and laws rather than supernatural ones.","more":"It is distinguished from metaphysical naturalism, which claims that nothing supernatural exists. The term became common from the 1980s, especially in debates over intelligent design. Defenders say science can only test natural causes, so it must proceed as if there are only such causes. Critics, including Alvin Plantinga, argue that this assumption is unjustified or biased against religious belief.","etym":"Latin natura, 'nature'.","example":"A doctor investigating a sudden recovery looks for medical causes, not miracles, even if she is religious.","notConfused":"Not the same as metaphysical naturalism: one can accept the method in science while believing in God.","field":"Method","related":["Naturalism","Naturalism about religion","Demarcation problem"],"thinkers":["plantinga"],"tags":["naturalism","natural explanations","science and religion","intelligent design"]},{"term":"Heuristic","def":"A rule of thumb or strategy that helps solve problems or make decisions efficiently, without guaranteeing a correct answer.","more":"George Pólya's How to Solve It (1945) popularised heuristics in mathematics, such as working backwards or solving a simpler problem first. Herbert Simon, and later Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, studied how people use heuristics, which are useful but can produce systematic biases. In philosophy of science, Imre Lakatos spoke of a research programme's positive and negative heuristics. Many philosophers argue that intuitions themselves function as heuristics.","etym":"Greek heuriskein, 'to find', as in 'eureka'.","example":"'If in doubt, take the route the locals use' is a heuristic for finding your way in a new city.","notConfused":"Not the same as an algorithm, which guarantees an answer if followed correctly.","field":"Method","related":["Lakatos’s methodology of research programmes","Intuition","Confirmation bias"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["heuristics","rule of thumb","shortcut","mental shortcut","cognitive bias","kahneman"]},{"term":"Prima facie","def":"At first sight; holding or appearing to hold unless outweighed or defeated by something else.","more":"Philosophers use it for reasons, evidence and duties that count in favour of something without settling it. W. D. Ross's theory of prima facie duties (1930) holds that duties like keeping promises bind us unless a stronger duty conflicts. In epistemology, a prima facie justified belief can be defeated by new evidence. The phrase is also standard in law, where a prima facie case is one strong enough to require an answer.","etym":"Latin prima facie, 'at first face' or 'at first appearance'.","example":"A promise to meet a friend gives a prima facie duty to go, which is outweighed if you need to help someone injured on the way.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'apparent' in the sense of illusory: a prima facie duty is real, just capable of being outweighed.","field":"Method","related":["Rossian pluralism","Defeasible","Defeater"],"thinkers":["ross"],"tags":["at first sight","on its face","at first glance","defeasible","presumption","prima facie evidence"]},{"term":"Operational definition","def":"A definition of a concept in terms of the procedure used to measure or detect it.","more":"The physicist Percy Bridgman proposed operationalism in The Logic of Modern Physics (1927), partly inspired by Einstein's analysis of simultaneity in terms of measurement. Strict operationalism, which identifies a concept with its measuring operations, was widely rejected, since it implies that different thermometers measure different 'temperatures'. Operational definitions remain useful in psychology and social science, where abstract concepts must be made measurable.","etym":"Latin operari, 'to work'.","example":"Defining 'hunger' in an experiment as 'hours since last meal'.","notConfused":"Not the same as a dictionary definition: an operational definition tells you how to measure, not what the word ordinarily means.","field":"Method","related":["Verificationist theory of meaning","Logical positivism","Explication"],"thinkers":["einstein"],"tags":["operationalism","operationalization","operationalisation","bridgman","measurement"]},{"term":"Summum bonum","def":"The highest good: the one thing that is good in itself and for whose sake everything else is pursued.","more":"Ancient ethics was largely an argument about what the highest good is: Aristotle said eudaimonia, Epicurus said pleasure understood as tranquillity, and the Stoics said virtue. Cicero's dialogue On Ends compares the rival answers. Kant used the term for the union of virtue and happiness in proportion to it, and argued that we must postulate God and immortality to make that union possible.","etym":"Latin summum bonum, 'the highest good'.","example":"Asking 'And why do you want that?' about money, then health, then happiness, until you reach something that is wanted for nothing beyond itself.","notConfused":"Not the same as intrinsic value in general: many things may be intrinsically good, but the summum bonum is the single highest or most complete one.","field":"Ethics","related":["Eudaimonia","Intrinsic value","Telos"],"thinkers":["aristotle","cicero","kant"],"tags":["highest good","supreme good","ultimate good","final end","eudaimonia","telos"]},{"term":"Phronesis","def":"Practical wisdom: the ability to see what matters in a particular situation and to work out how to act well in it.","more":"Aristotle, in the Nicomachean Ethics, treats phronesis as the intellectual virtue that every moral virtue needs: courage without it can turn into recklessness. It cannot be fully captured in rules, because it involves perceiving the details of a case, and it is learned through experience. Modern virtue ethicists such as Rosalind Hursthouse make it central to their accounts of right action.","etym":"Greek phronēsis, 'thought, practical understanding', from phronein, 'to think'.","example":"An experienced nurse who knows when a frightened patient needs the full truth and when they need reassurance first.","notConfused":"Not the same as cleverness: Aristotle says cleverness can serve any goal, even bad ones, while practical wisdom aims at what is genuinely good.","field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Arete","Golden mean"],"thinkers":["aristotle","hursthouse"],"tags":["practical wisdom","prudence","phronēsis","aristotle","practical reason","virtue ethics"]},{"term":"Sophrosyne","def":"The Greek virtue of moderation and self-control: a well-ordered soul whose desires agree with reason.","more":"Plato's Charmides tries and fails to define it, and in the Republic it is the harmony in which every part of the soul (or city) agrees about who should rule. Aristotle treats it more narrowly as the mean regarding bodily pleasures such as food, drink and sex. It became one of the four cardinal virtues, usually translated 'temperance'.","etym":"Greek sōphrosynē, from sōs, 'safe, sound', and phrēn, 'mind': 'soundness of mind'.","example":"Enjoying a good dinner without either refusing all pleasure or eating until you feel ill, and without having to fight yourself to stop.","notConfused":"Not the same as enkrateia (self-control in the narrow sense): the self-controlled person resists strong bad desires, while the person with sophrosyne does not have them to the same degree.","field":"Ethics","related":["Cardinal virtues","Akrasia","Golden mean"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle"],"tags":["sophrosune","moderation","temperance","self-control","self-restraint","virtue"]},{"term":"Agape","def":"Unconditional, self-giving love directed at others for their own sake, especially as described in Christian ethics.","more":"In the New Testament agape names God's love for humanity and the love Christians are commanded to show to neighbours and even enemies. The Swedish theologian Anders Nygren sharply contrasted it with eros, desire that seeks its own fulfilment. Kierkegaard's Works of Love explores neighbour-love as a duty owed to every person equally, which raises the question of how it fits with special love for friends and family.","etym":"Greek agapē, 'love, affection'.","example":"Helping a stranger who can never repay you and whom you do not especially like, simply because they need it.","notConfused":"Not the same as philia (friendship) or eros (desire): agape is not based on liking or attraction and does not depend on being returned.","field":"Ethics","related":["Philia","Altruism","Golden rule"],"thinkers":["kierkegaard","augustine"],"tags":["agapē","charity","caritas","unconditional love","christian love","love of neighbour"]},{"term":"Philia","def":"The Greek word for friendship or affectionate love, which Aristotle treated as a central part of a good human life.","more":"Aristotle devotes two books of the Nicomachean Ethics to philia. He distinguishes friendships of utility, of pleasure and of virtue, and argues that only the last, between good people who love each other's character, is complete and lasting. He calls a friend 'another self', and holds that even a happy person needs friends. Philosophers still debate whether friendship's partiality can be squared with impartial moral theories.","etym":"Greek philia, 'friendship, fondness'; compare philosophy, 'love of wisdom'.","example":"A friendship that survives one person moving away, because it was never just about convenience or having fun together.","notConfused":"Not the same as eros: philia is mutual goodwill and companionship, not romantic or sexual desire.","field":"Ethics","related":["Agape","Eudaimonia","Special obligations"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["philía","friendship","brotherly love","love","aristotle","friends"]},{"term":"Ressentiment","def":"Nietzsche's term for a festering resentment felt by the weak toward the strong, which he believed shaped the values of traditional morality.","more":"In On the Genealogy of Morality (1887) Nietzsche argues that people unable to act on their hostility turned it into a value system, calling the powerful 'evil' and their own weakness 'good'. He saw this 'slave revolt in morality' behind Christian ideals of meekness and pity. Max Scheler later wrote a book on ressentiment, agreeing it was real but denying that Christian love grew from it.","etym":"French ressentiment, 'resentment'; Nietzsche used the French word.","example":"Someone who cannot afford a fancy car insisting that everyone who drives one must be shallow and greedy.","notConfused":"Not the same as ordinary resentment at being wronged: ressentiment is a lasting, often hidden attitude that rewrites what counts as good and bad.","field":"Ethics","related":["Master morality and slave morality","Genealogy","Will to power"],"thinkers":["nietzsche","scheler"],"tags":["resentment","nietzsche","slave morality","envy","genealogy of morals"]},{"term":"Master morality and slave morality","def":"Nietzsche's contrast between values created by the strong, who call themselves 'good', and values created by the weak, who start by calling others 'evil'.","more":"In Beyond Good and Evil and On the Genealogy of Morality, Nietzsche says master morality praises pride, strength and nobility, while slave morality praises humility, pity and equality. He thought modern Western morality was largely slave morality, born from ressentiment. Readers disagree about how literally to take this history and about whether Nietzsche simply wished to restore master morality.","etym":null,"example":"One culture admires the proud warrior who takes what he wants; another admires the humble person who turns the other cheek.","notConfused":"Not a claim about actual slaves and masters as social classes, but about two psychological sources of values.","field":"Ethics","related":["Ressentiment","Genealogy","Übermensch"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"tags":["master morality","slave morality","nietzsche","genealogy of morals","ressentiment","slave revolt in morality"]},{"term":"Amor fati","def":"Nietzsche's ideal of 'love of fate': embracing everything that happens, including suffering, as something you would not want otherwise.","more":"Nietzsche introduces the phrase in The Gay Science (1882) and calls it his 'formula for greatness' in Ecce Homo. It goes beyond putting up with what happens: one is to affirm it. It is closely linked to his thought experiment of eternal recurrence. Readers often compare it with Stoic acceptance of fate, though Nietzsche's tone is more celebratory than calm.","etym":"Latin amor fati, 'love of fate'.","example":"Looking back on a painful failure and saying, not 'I wish it hadn't happened', but 'I would choose it again, because it made me who I am'.","notConfused":"Not the same as fatalism: amor fati is an attitude of affirmation, not a claim that our choices make no difference.","field":"Ethics","related":["Eternal recurrence","Stoicism","Fatalism"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"tags":["love of fate","nietzsche","acceptance","eternal recurrence","eternal return","stoicism"]},{"term":"Prima facie duty","def":"A duty that really counts but can be outweighed by a stronger duty in a particular situation.","more":"W. D. Ross introduced the idea in The Right and the Good (1930). He listed several such duties, including fidelity, reparation, gratitude, justice, beneficence, self-improvement and non-maleficence. When they conflict, we must judge which is weightier in the case at hand; there is no fixed ranking. Ross later said 'conditional duty' might have been a better name.","etym":"Latin prima facie, 'at first sight'.","example":"You promised to meet a friend, but on the way you see someone injured; the duty to help outweighs the duty to keep the promise, though you should still apologise.","notConfused":"Not a merely apparent duty: it is a real moral reason, which is why breaking it can leave something to regret or make up for.","field":"Ethics","related":["Rossian pluralism","Duty","Moral residue"],"thinkers":["ross"],"tags":["prima facie duties","ross","w. d. ross","pluralism","conditional duty","moral conflict"]},{"term":"Moral agent","def":"A being that can act for moral reasons and can therefore be held responsible for what it does.","more":"Moral agency is usually thought to require the ability to understand right and wrong and to act on that understanding. Adult humans are the standard case; young children and most animals are not usually counted as full moral agents. Debates over AI systems and corporations often turn on whether they can be moral agents.","etym":"Latin agens, 'doing', from agere, 'to act'.","example":"We blame an adult who smashes a vase in anger, but not a cat who knocks it off a shelf.","notConfused":"Not the same as a moral patient: a newborn baby is a moral patient (it can be wronged) without being a moral agent.","field":"Ethics","related":["Moral patient","Moral responsibility","Moral status"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["moral agency","agent","moral responsibility","responsible agent","moral agents"]},{"term":"Moral patient","def":"A being whose interests count morally, so that it can be wronged or harmed in a way that matters.","more":"Many philosophers hold that sentience, the capacity to feel pleasure and pain, is enough to make a being a moral patient; this is the basis of Peter Singer's case for animals. Others link moral patiency to rationality, relationships or being a living thing. The concept separates the question 'who counts?' from 'who is responsible?'.","etym":"Latin patiens, 'suffering, undergoing'.","example":"A dog cannot be blamed for anything, but kicking it for fun still wrongs it.","notConfused":"Not the same as a moral agent: agents act morally; patients are those whom moral action is about.","field":"Ethics","related":["Moral agent","Moral status","Sentience"],"thinkers":["singer"],"tags":["moral patiency","moral status","moral considerability","moral patients","animal rights"]},{"term":"Agent-relative and agent-neutral reasons","def":"Reasons that are tied to a particular person (such as 'my child') versus reasons that apply to everyone equally (such as 'reduce suffering').","more":"Thomas Nagel developed the distinction, and Derek Parfit used related terms in Reasons and Persons (1984). Consequentialism is usually formulated in purely agent-neutral terms, giving everyone the same goal. Common-sense morality includes agent-relative reasons, such as special duties to family and constraints against harming that bind me even if my harming would prevent others' harms.","etym":null,"example":"My reason to look after my own mother is agent-relative; my reason to support cancer research is agent-neutral.","notConfused":"Not the same as subjective versus objective reasons: an agent-relative reason can be perfectly objective, just indexed to a person.","field":"Ethics","related":["Agent-centred constraints","Special obligations","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["nagel","parfit"],"tags":["agent-relative reasons","agent-neutral reasons","partiality","impartiality","nagel","parfit"]},{"term":"Thick and thin concepts","def":"Thin ethical concepts like 'good' and 'right' are general; thick ones like 'cruel', 'brave' or 'tactful' mix evaluation with description.","more":"Bernard Williams brought the distinction to prominence in Ethics and the Limits of Philosophy (1985). Thick concepts seem to be both world-guided (you need facts to apply them) and action-guiding (they imply praise or blame). Philosophers argue about whether they can be split into a neutral description plus an attitude, which matters for the fact/value distinction.","etym":null,"example":"Calling a remark 'rude' tells you both what kind of remark it was and that it was bad; calling it 'wrong' only tells you the second.","notConfused":"Not the same as vague versus precise: 'cruel' can be quite precise, but it is still thick because it carries evaluation.","field":"Ethics","related":["Is–ought gap","Virtue","Metaethics"],"thinkers":["williams"],"tags":["thick concepts","thin concepts","bernard williams","thick ethical concepts","fact-value distinction"]},{"term":"Moral residue","def":"The lingering regret, guilt or duty to make amends that remains after you rightly chose one obligation over another.","more":"Bernard Williams used the example of moral regret to argue against theories that say a conflict between duties simply disappears once you know which one wins. If the losing duty leaves nothing behind, it is hard to explain why an apology or compensation is called for. The phenomenon is often used to support the existence of genuine moral dilemmas.","etym":"Latin residuum, 'what is left over'.","example":"A doctor who could save only one of two patients chose rightly, yet still feels she owes the other family an explanation and sorrow.","notConfused":"Not the same as guilt for wrongdoing: the agent may have done nothing wrong, yet something is still owed.","field":"Ethics","related":["Moral dilemma","Prima facie duty","Dirty hands"],"thinkers":["williams"],"tags":["moral remainder","regret","moral dilemma","guilt","remorse","tragic choice"]},{"term":"Dirty hands","def":"The problem of whether political leaders must sometimes do something morally wrong in order to do what is politically right.","more":"The phrase comes from Sartre's play Dirty Hands (1948), and Michael Walzer's 1973 article 'Political Action: The Problem of Dirty Hands' set the philosophical debate. Walzer argued that a politician might rightly order the torture of a terrorist to prevent a bombing yet still be guilty. The idea goes back to Machiavelli's claim that a prince must learn how not to be good.","etym":null,"example":"A leader who lies to the public to prevent a panic during a crisis, and believes both that it was necessary and that it was wrong.","notConfused":"Not the same as hypocrisy or simple corruption: the dirty-hands agent acts for a genuinely public good and feels the moral cost.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Moral residue","Moral dilemma","Political realism"],"thinkers":["walzer","sartre","machiavelli"],"tags":["problem of dirty hands","walzer","michael walzer","machiavelli","political realism","necessary evil"]},{"term":"Moral saint","def":"A person whose every action is as morally good as possible; Susan Wolf argued that such a life would not be ideal.","more":"In 'Moral Saints' (1982) Wolf argued that a person dominated by morality would have no room for playing the oboe, cooking gourmet food or enjoying a good joke at someone's expense. She concluded that moral perfection is not obviously the best way to live, and that morality is not the only standard of a good life. The paper sparked wide debate about the demandingness of ethics.","etym":null,"example":"Someone who gives every spare penny to charity and spends every free hour volunteering, never learning an instrument because it would be less useful to others.","notConfused":"Not the same as a religious saint: Wolf's target is an imagined person maximally devoted to moral improvement, not a figure venerated by a church.","field":"Ethics","related":["Supererogation","Effective altruism","Wellbeing"],"thinkers":["wolf"],"tags":["moral saints","susan wolf","moral perfection","saintliness","supererogation"]},{"term":"Impartial spectator","def":"Adam Smith's imagined fair-minded observer, whose view of our conduct we take up in order to judge ourselves morally.","more":"In The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759) Smith argued that conscience develops as we learn to see our own actions as an impartial, well-informed onlooker would. We feel approval or disapproval through sympathy, imagining ourselves in another's place. The idea influenced later 'ideal observer' theories of morality.","etym":null,"example":"Before sending an angry email, imagining how it would look to a calm, fair person who knew all the facts.","notConfused":"Not the same as a real third party or public opinion: the spectator is idealised and can disagree with what everyone around us thinks.","field":"Ethics","related":["Moral sense theory","Conscience","Moral sentimentalism"],"thinkers":["smith"],"tags":["ideal observer","adam smith","moral sentiments","spectator","impartiality","sympathy"]},{"term":"Paradox of hedonism","def":"The observation that people who aim directly at pleasure or happiness often get less of it than people who aim at other things.","more":"Henry Sidgwick named the paradox in The Methods of Ethics (1874), and Joseph Butler and John Stuart Mill made similar points: Mill said happiness is found by those who 'have their minds fixed on some object other than their own happiness'. It does not refute hedonism as a theory of what is good, but suggests hedonists should not always think like hedonists. Some philosophers reply that it is really a point about strategy: a hedonist can recommend caring about friends, work and causes precisely because that produces the most pleasure.","etym":null,"example":"Someone who spends a holiday constantly checking whether they are having fun enjoys it less than someone absorbed in exploring.","notConfused":"Not a logical paradox: it is a psychological observation about how pleasure is best obtained.","field":"Ethics","related":["Hedonism","Utilitarianism","Wellbeing"],"thinkers":["sidgwick","mill"],"tags":["hedonism paradox","pleasure paradox","pursuit of happiness","sidgwick","happiness"]},{"term":"Conscience","def":"The inner sense of right and wrong by which people judge their own actions and feel obliged or guilty.","more":"Medieval thinkers such as Aquinas distinguished synderesis, a built-in grasp of basic moral principles, from conscience, the act of applying them to particular cases. Joseph Butler treated conscience as the supreme authority in human nature. Freud explained it as the superego, internalised parental authority, and others ask whether a conscience can be mistaken and still binding.","etym":"Latin conscientia, 'knowledge within oneself', from con-, 'with', and scire, 'to know'.","example":"Feeling uneasy for days after keeping extra change a cashier gave you by mistake.","notConfused":"Not the same as consciousness: conscience is moral self-judgment, consciousness is awareness in general.","field":"Ethics","related":["Impartial spectator","Moral sense theory","Natural law"],"thinkers":["aquinas","freud"],"tags":["moral sense","inner voice","guilt","synderesis","moral conscience"]},{"term":"Lex talionis","def":"The law of retaliation: a punishment should match the harm done, as in 'an eye for an eye'.","more":"The principle appears in the ancient Code of Hammurabi and in the Hebrew Bible. Many scholars think it originally limited revenge, forbidding punishment greater than the offence. Kant defended a version of it as the only principle that sets punishment by justice rather than by usefulness; critics say it cannot be applied literally to crimes like fraud.","etym":"Latin lex talionis, 'law of like for like', from talis, 'such'.","example":"Saying that someone who smashed your window should pay exactly the cost of a new window, no more and no less.","notConfused":"Not the same as revenge: lex talionis is a principle of proportion applied by a public authority, not private retaliation.","field":"Ethics","related":["Retributivism","Desert","Justice"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["eye for an eye","law of retaliation","retaliation","retribution","retributivism","talion"]},{"term":"Forgiveness","def":"Giving up justified resentment or blame toward someone who has wronged you, without denying that the wrong happened.","more":"Bishop Joseph Butler described forgiveness as overcoming resentment, and much later work builds on his account. Philosophers debate whether forgiveness requires the wrongdoer to repent, whether some deeds are unforgivable, and whether one can forgive on behalf of others. Hannah Arendt argued in The Human Condition that forgiveness is essential to political life because it frees people from the consequences of the past.","etym":"Old English forgiefan, 'to give up, grant'.","example":"A friend breaks a promise and apologises; you stop holding it against them, though you both still agree it was wrong.","notConfused":"Not the same as excusing (deciding it was not really wrong) or forgetting: forgiveness keeps the wrong in view but lets go of resentment.","field":"Ethics","related":["Reactive attitudes view","Restorative justice","Moral responsibility"],"thinkers":["arendt"],"tags":["forgive","pardon","mercy","reconciliation","resentment","absolution"]},{"term":"Prescriptivism","def":"R. M. Hare's theory that moral statements are a kind of universal command rather than descriptions of facts.","more":"In The Language of Morals (1952) and Freedom and Reason (1963), Hare argued that saying 'You ought to do X' prescribes X, and that moral 'oughts' must be universalisable: you must be willing to apply them to everyone in similar circumstances, including yourself. He held that this logic leads to a form of utilitarianism. Critics argue it struggles to explain moral error and weakness of will.","etym":"Latin praescribere, 'to write before, to order'.","example":"If you say 'People should return lost wallets', you commit yourself to wanting the same if you find one, not just if you lose one.","notConfused":"Not the same as emotivism: emotivists say moral statements express feelings; Hare insisted they are rational, universal prescriptions.","field":"Ethics","related":["Emotivism","Universalisability","Expressivism"],"thinkers":["hare"],"tags":["universal prescriptivism","hare","r. m. hare","non-cognitivism","moral language","imperatives"]},{"term":"Normative ethics","def":"The branch of ethics that asks what makes actions right or wrong and what makes lives good, developing general theories to answer.","more":"The main traditions are consequentialism, deontology and virtue ethics, with contractualism and care ethics as further options. Normative ethics is usually distinguished from metaethics, which asks what moral claims mean and whether they can be true, and from applied ethics, which tackles particular issues. In practice the three levels constantly inform each other.","etym":"Latin norma, 'carpenter's square, rule'.","example":"Asking not 'Is lying to my boss OK?' but 'What general principle settles when lying is OK?'.","notConfused":"Not the same as metaethics: normative ethics asks which acts are wrong; metaethics asks what 'wrong' means and whether moral facts exist.","field":"Ethics","related":["Metaethics","Applied ethics","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["ethical theory","moral theory","normative theory","consequentialism","deontology","virtue ethics"]},{"term":"Applied ethics","def":"The part of ethics that tackles specific practical questions, such as abortion, animal welfare, war, or the use of new technologies.","more":"Applied ethics grew rapidly from the late 1960s, partly driven by medical advances and political movements. Peter Singer's 'Famine, Affluence, and Morality' (1972) and Judith Jarvis Thomson's 'A Defense of Abortion' (1971) are classic examples. It includes bioethics, business ethics, environmental ethics and more, and often uses thought experiments and mid-level principles rather than a single grand theory.","etym":null,"example":"Debating whether self-driving cars should be programmed to protect passengers or pedestrians first.","notConfused":"Not the same as normative ethics: applied ethics uses theories and principles on particular cases rather than building the theories.","field":"Ethics","related":["Normative ethics","Bioethics","Casuistry"],"thinkers":["singer","thomson"],"tags":["practical ethics","applied morality","moral issues","bioethics","contemporary moral problems"]},{"term":"Bioethics","def":"The ethics of medicine and the life sciences, covering issues such as consent, end-of-life care, genetic technologies and research on humans and animals.","more":"The field took shape in the 1960s and 1970s, prompted by scandals in medical research and by new technologies such as organ transplants and life support. Beauchamp and Childress's Principles of Biomedical Ethics (first edition 1979) proposed four guiding principles: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice. Today bioethicists also advise hospitals and governments.","etym":"Greek bios, 'life', plus ethics.","example":"A hospital committee deciding whether to honour a patient's earlier request to refuse a blood transfusion.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Applied ethics","Principlism","Euthanasia"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["medical ethics","biomedical ethics","healthcare ethics","research ethics","informed consent"]},{"term":"Environmental ethics","def":"The study of our moral relations to the natural world, including animals, plants, species, ecosystems and future generations.","more":"The field emerged in the 1970s; Richard Routley's 1973 paper asking whether we need 'a new, an environmental ethic' is often cited as a starting point. Central debates include whether only sentient beings have moral standing or whether whole ecosystems do, and how much we owe future people. Positions range from human-centred views to biocentrism and deep ecology.","etym":null,"example":"Asking whether it would be wrong to wipe out a species of beetle that nobody uses or cares about.","notConfused":"Not the same as environmental science: science tells us what is happening to nature; environmental ethics asks what we ought to do about it.","field":"Ethics","related":["Biocentrism","Deep ecology","Land ethic"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["ecological ethics","ethics of nature","green ethics","environmentalism","eco-ethics"]},{"term":"Land ethic","def":"Aldo Leopold's idea that humans should see themselves as members of a biotic community and treat the land with respect.","more":"Leopold set it out in A Sand County Almanac (1949): 'A thing is right when it tends to preserve the integrity, stability, and beauty of the biotic community. It is wrong when it tends otherwise.' It extends moral concern from individuals to soils, waters, plants and animals as a whole. Critics have worried that it could justify sacrificing individuals for the good of the ecosystem.","etym":null,"example":"A farmer leaving a wetland on their property untouched because draining it would harm the whole local ecosystem, even though it would pay.","notConfused":"Not the same as animal rights: the land ethic focuses on ecological wholes, and may permit culling individual animals to protect them.","field":"Ethics","related":["Environmental ethics","Deep ecology","Biocentrism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["aldo leopold","leopold","biotic community","sand county almanac","ecocentrism"]},{"term":"Deep ecology","def":"An environmental philosophy holding that all living things have value in themselves, regardless of their usefulness to humans.","more":"The Norwegian philosopher Arne Naess coined the term in 1973, contrasting it with 'shallow' ecology, which cares about pollution mainly for human health and wealth. He and George Sessions drew up an eight-point platform in 1984. Critics argue it can be misanthropic or that its call for a smaller human population is troubling.","etym":null,"example":"Opposing a dam not because it would hurt tourism, but because the river and its life matter in their own right.","notConfused":"Not the same as environmentalism in general: deep ecology rejects the human-centred reasons that many environmentalists rely on.","field":"Ethics","related":["Environmental ethics","Land ethic","Biocentrism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["naess","arne naess","ecosophy","ecocentrism","biocentrism","radical environmentalism"]},{"term":"Sanctity of life","def":"The principle that human life has a special, inviolable value, so that it is always wrong to kill an innocent person intentionally.","more":"The principle has deep roots in Jewish, Christian and Islamic thought, which see human life as a gift from God. It is central in debates on abortion, euthanasia and capital punishment. Critics such as Peter Singer argue it should be replaced by a 'quality of life' approach, while defenders warn that this makes some lives seem less worth protecting.","etym":"Latin sanctitas, 'holiness, inviolability'.","example":"A doctor refusing to give a lethal injection to a terminally ill patient who asks for it, while still doing everything to relieve their pain.","notConfused":"Not the same as vitalism, the view that life must be prolonged at any cost: many sanctity-of-life defenders accept stopping burdensome treatment.","field":"Ethics","related":["Euthanasia","Doctrine of double effect","Moral status"],"thinkers":["singer"],"tags":["sanctity of human life","sacredness of life","right to life","pro-life","inviolability of life"]},{"term":"Euthanasia","def":"Deliberately ending a person's life, or allowing them to die, for their own sake, usually to end suffering from incurable illness.","more":"Philosophers distinguish voluntary, non-voluntary and involuntary euthanasia, and active (killing) from passive (letting die). James Rachels argued in 1975 that the active/passive distinction has no moral weight in itself. Opponents appeal to the sanctity of life, the risk of pressure on vulnerable people, and the doctrine of double effect.","etym":"Greek eu, 'good', and thanatos, 'death': 'a good death'.","example":"A patient with terminal cancer, in great pain and of sound mind, asking a doctor to help end their life.","notConfused":"Not the same as assisted suicide: in euthanasia someone else performs the final act; in assisted suicide the patient does it with help.","field":"Ethics","related":["Sanctity of life","Acts and omissions","Autonomy"],"thinkers":["rachels"],"tags":["mercy killing","assisted dying","assisted suicide","physician-assisted suicide","right to die","dignity in dying"]},{"term":"Heteronomy","def":"Kant's term for a will that is governed by something outside reason itself, such as desires, authority or rewards.","more":"In the Groundwork (1785) Kant contrasted heteronomy with autonomy, the will's giving the moral law to itself. He argued that any morality based on happiness, feeling or even God's commands is heteronomous, and so cannot ground unconditional obligation. Critics reply that Kant's demand is too austere and that feelings properly play a part in moral life.","etym":"Greek heteros, 'other', and nomos, 'law': 'law from another'.","example":"Being honest only because you fear getting caught is heteronomous; being honest because you see you must is autonomous.","notConfused":"Not the same as lack of freedom in a political sense: a heteronomous agent may be free to do as they like, yet be ruled by their inclinations.","field":"Ethics","related":["Autonomy","Kantian ethics","Categorical imperative"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["heteronomous will","kant","autonomy","external authority","heteronomy of the will"]},{"term":"Maxim","def":"In Kant's ethics, the personal rule or principle behind an action, which the categorical imperative tests.","more":"Kant thought every intentional action has a maxim of the form 'In situation S, I will do A in order to achieve E'. The first formula of the categorical imperative asks whether you could will your maxim to become a universal law. A recurring problem is that the same act can be described by many maxims, some of which pass the test and some of which fail.","etym":"Medieval Latin maxima propositio, 'greatest premise'.","example":"'When I need money, I will borrow it by promising to repay, knowing I cannot' is the maxim Kant uses to show that lying promises are wrong.","notConfused":"Not the same as a proverb or saying: a Kantian maxim is the agent's own working rule, not a piece of popular wisdom.","field":"Ethics","related":["Categorical imperative","Universalisability","Kantian ethics"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["maxims","kant","universalizability","categorical imperative","subjective principle"]},{"term":"Perfect and imperfect duties","def":"Perfect duties must be kept every time (like not lying); imperfect duties leave room to choose when and how to fulfil them (like helping others).","more":"The distinction is older, but Kant made it famous in the Groundwork. He argued that maxims which cannot even be conceived as universal laws break perfect duties, while maxims that can be conceived but not consistently willed break imperfect duties. Imperfect duties allow 'latitude', which helps explain why going beyond them can be supererogatory.","etym":null,"example":"You must never break your promise to repay a loan (perfect), but you can choose which charity to support and when (imperfect).","notConfused":"Not a contrast between important and unimportant duties: imperfect duties are fully binding; they just do not specify exact actions.","field":"Ethics","related":["Imperfect duty of beneficence","Duty","Supererogation"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["perfect duties","imperfect duties","kant","duty","beneficence","strict duties"]},{"term":"Ethics of belief","def":"The question of whether there are moral duties about what we believe, such as a duty to believe only on good evidence.","more":"W. K. Clifford's 1877 essay 'The Ethics of Belief' argued that 'it is wrong always, everywhere, and for anyone, to believe anything upon insufficient evidence', using the example of a shipowner who convinces himself his ship is seaworthy. William James replied in 'The Will to Believe' (1896) that in some live, forced and momentous choices we may believe without conclusive evidence. A puzzle is whether we can control our beliefs enough for duties to apply.","etym":null,"example":"Is it wrong to believe a rumour about a coworker just because you dislike them, even if you never act on it?","notConfused":"Not the same as evidentialism alone: evidentialism says what justifies belief; the ethics of belief asks whether failing to follow evidence is a moral fault.","field":"Ethics","related":["Evidentialism","The will to believe","Doxastic involuntarism"],"thinkers":["clifford","james"],"tags":["clifford","w. k. clifford","doxastic responsibility","william james","will to believe","epistemic duty"]},{"term":"Expanding moral circle","def":"The idea that moral concern has gradually spread from family and tribe to wider groups, and perhaps should extend further still.","more":"The historian W. E. H. Lecky described the image in the 19th century, and Peter Singer's The Expanding Circle (1981) gave it a philosophical treatment, arguing that reason pushes us to give equal consideration to more and more beings. Debates today concern whether the circle should include animals, future generations, ecosystems, or even artificial minds.","etym":null,"example":"Practices once accepted, such as slavery or cruelty to animals for sport, came to be widely condemned as more beings were seen as mattering.","notConfused":"Not a claim that moral progress is automatic: it describes a pattern and a challenge, not a law of history.","field":"Ethics","related":["Moral status","Speciesism","Equal consideration of interests"],"thinkers":["singer"],"tags":["moral circle","expanding circle","peter singer","singer","moral progress","circle of concern"]},{"term":"Self-deception","def":"Getting yourself to believe something you have reason to think is false, usually because the truth is unwelcome.","more":"It is puzzling: how can one person be both the deceiver who knows the truth and the deceived who does not? Some philosophers split the mind into parts; others, like Alfred Mele, say self-deception is simply motivated bias, not literal lying to oneself. Sartre's 'bad faith' and Joseph Butler's sermon on self-deceit treat it as a moral failing as well as a cognitive one.","etym":null,"example":"Someone who keeps telling themselves their partner is just busy, while ignoring every sign that the relationship is ending.","notConfused":"Not the same as wishful thinking in general: self-deception usually involves some awareness of contrary evidence that is pushed aside.","field":"Ethics","related":["Bad faith","Akrasia","Ethics of belief"],"thinkers":["sartre"],"tags":["self deception","denial","wishful thinking","bad faith","motivated belief"]},{"term":"Oikeiosis","def":"The Stoic idea that living beings naturally recognise themselves, and then others, as their own and care for them.","more":"According to the Stoics, a newborn creature's first impulse is toward self-preservation. In humans, reason extends this sense of belonging outward to family, fellow citizens and finally all humanity. Hierocles pictured it as a set of concentric circles we should draw inward. The idea underpins Stoic cosmopolitanism.","etym":"Greek oikeiōsis, 'making one's own, appropriation', from oikos, 'household'.","example":"Learning to feel about a stranger's suffering as you would about a cousin's, by treating them as part of your wider family.","notConfused":"Not the same as selfishness: it begins with self-concern but is meant to grow into concern for everyone.","field":"Ethics","related":["Stoicism","Cosmopolitanism","Expanding moral circle"],"thinkers":["zeno_citium","cicero"],"tags":["oikeiōsis","appropriation","familiarization","stoicism","circles of concern","hierocles"]},{"term":"Adiaphora","def":"In Stoic ethics, 'indifferent things' such as health, wealth and reputation, which are neither good nor bad in themselves.","more":"The Stoics held that only virtue is good and only vice is bad; everything else is indifferent to happiness. They added that some indifferents are 'preferred' (health) and others 'dispreferred' (illness), so it is natural to choose them, but losing them cannot make a virtuous person's life worse. Later Christian writers used the word for practices neither commanded nor forbidden.","etym":"Greek adiaphora, 'things not differing'.","example":"A Stoic who loses their savings in a market crash treats it as unwelcome but insists it does not harm what really matters: their character.","notConfused":"Not the same as things we do not care about: Stoics may sensibly pursue preferred indifferents, they just do not stake their happiness on them.","field":"Ethics","related":["Stoicism","Apatheia","Virtue"],"thinkers":["epictetus","seneca","zeno_citium"],"tags":["indifferents","stoicism","preferred indifferents","indifference","moral neutrality"]},{"term":"Hubris","def":"Excessive pride or arrogance, especially overstepping human limits, which in Greek thought invites downfall.","more":"In Athenian law hubris meant degrading insult or assault that shamed the victim; in tragedy and later usage it came to mean overweening pride that defies the gods. Aristotle discusses it in the Rhetoric as insulting others for the pleasure of feeling superior. It remains a theme in ethics of technology and leadership, warning against overconfidence.","etym":"Greek hybris, 'insolence, outrage'.","example":"A company boss who dismisses every safety warning because they are sure nothing can go wrong under their leadership.","notConfused":"Not the same as healthy self-respect or justified confidence: hubris involves contempt for others or for natural limits.","field":"Ethics","related":["Tragedy","Virtue","Arete"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["arrogance","pride","excessive pride","nemesis","overconfidence","tragic flaw"]},{"term":"Hegemony","def":"Rule maintained less by force than by consent, as a dominant group's ideas come to seem like plain common sense to everyone.","more":"The Italian Marxist Antonio Gramsci developed the concept in his Prison Notebooks (written 1929–1935). He asked why workers in the West had not revolted, and answered that the ruling class shaped culture, schools, churches and media so that its outlook seemed natural. Challenging hegemony, he argued, requires building a rival culture, not just seizing state power.","etym":"Greek hēgemonia, 'leadership', from hēgemōn, 'leader'.","example":"When people assume without question that 'there is no alternative' to the current economic system, that assumption may be a sign of hegemony.","notConfused":"Not the same as domination by force or by a single country: in Gramsci's sense hegemony works through consent and culture.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Ideology","False consciousness","Marxism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["cultural hegemony","gramsci","antonio gramsci","domination","consent","ideology"]},{"term":"Commodity fetishism","def":"Marx's idea that in capitalism relations between people appear as relations between things, as if goods had value by themselves.","more":"Marx introduced it in the first chapter of Capital, volume 1 (1867). He argued that a product's price really reflects the social labour that went into it, but in the market we see only things exchanging at certain prices. The workers, conditions and relationships behind a product disappear from view. Later critical theorists, such as Lukács with his idea of reification, developed the notion further.","etym":"Latin commoditas, 'convenience'; Portuguese feitiço, 'charm, sorcery'.","example":"Buying a cheap T-shirt and thinking only about its price and style, with no thought of the people who sewed it or how they were paid.","notConfused":"Not the same as being materialistic or loving shopping: it is a claim about how social relations are hidden in market exchange.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Reification","Marxism","Alienation"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["fetishism of commodities","marx","karl marx","reification","capitalism","alienation"]},{"term":"Surplus value","def":"In Marx's theory, the extra value workers produce beyond what they are paid in wages, which the capitalist keeps as profit.","more":"Marx argued in Capital that labour power is a special commodity: it creates more value than it costs to maintain. If a worker produces enough in four hours to cover their wage but works eight, the remaining four hours produce surplus value. For Marx this was the hidden source of profit and the core of exploitation. Most mainstream economists reject the labour theory of value it relies on.","etym":null,"example":"A bakery worker paid £80 a day bakes bread that sells for £300 more than the flour and ovens cost; for Marx, much of that gap is surplus value.","notConfused":"Not the same as profit in the accountant's sense: profit is one form surplus value takes, alongside rent and interest.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Labour theory of value","Exploitation","Marxism"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["surplus-value","marx","profit","exploitation","unpaid labour","unpaid labor"]},{"term":"Labour theory of value","def":"The theory that the value of a good is determined by the amount of labour needed to produce it.","more":"Versions appear in John Locke's account of property, in Adam Smith and especially in David Ricardo. Marx built his economics on it, refining it as 'socially necessary labour time'. From the 1870s most economists replaced it with theories based on marginal utility and supply and demand, though it remains debated in political philosophy.","etym":null,"example":"Thinking that a hand-carved chair is worth more than a mass-produced one mainly because it took far more hours to make.","notConfused":"Not the same as Locke's labour theory of property, which concerns how we come to own things, not what they are worth.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Surplus value","Lockean theory of property","Marxism"],"thinkers":["smith","marx","locke"],"tags":["labor theory of value","ltv","value theory","marx","ricardo","adam smith"]},{"term":"Exploitation","def":"Taking unfair advantage of someone, especially by benefiting from their work or vulnerability without giving them a fair return.","more":"Marx gave the concept a technical meaning: workers are exploited when they produce more value than they receive in wages. Philosophers today, such as Alan Wertheimer, analyse exploitation more broadly as unfair transactions, which can happen even when both sides gain and agree. A central puzzle is how a voluntary exchange can still be wrong.","etym":"French exploiter, 'to make use of, work (land)'.","example":"A company paying desperate workers in a disaster zone far below normal wages because it knows they have no alternative.","notConfused":"Not the same as using someone: we use bus drivers to get around without exploiting them, as long as the deal is fair.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Surplus value","Alienation","Justice"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["exploit","unfair advantage","oppression","wage labour","surplus value"]},{"term":"Class consciousness","def":"Awareness by members of a social class of their shared position and interests, especially as against other classes.","more":"Marx distinguished a class 'in itself' (people sharing an economic position) from a class 'for itself' (people aware of it and organised to act). Georg Lukács developed the concept in History and Class Consciousness (1923). Marxists argued that capitalism would be overthrown only when workers became conscious of their common exploitation.","etym":null,"example":"Workers at different factories realising they face the same problems and forming a union together.","notConfused":"Not the same as snobbery or awareness of status: class consciousness concerns economic position and collective interest.","field":"Politics and society","related":["False consciousness","Marxism","Hegemony"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["class awareness","marx","proletariat","working class","class struggle","solidarity"]},{"term":"False consciousness","def":"Beliefs held by oppressed people that hide their oppression from them and serve the interests of those in power.","more":"The phrase comes from a letter by Friedrich Engels (1893), though the idea is rooted in Marx's account of ideology. It suggests people can be wrong about their own interests in systematic ways. Critics warn that it can become a way of dismissing anyone who disagrees, since their disagreement can always be blamed on false consciousness.","etym":null,"example":"Low-paid workers opposing a minimum wage rise because they believe it will mainly hurt them, a belief spread by those who would pay for it.","notConfused":"Not the same as simply being misinformed: false consciousness is a pattern of belief produced by social structures and serving them.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Ideology","Hegemony","Class consciousness"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["ideology","marx","engels","self-deception","internalized oppression"]},{"term":"Base and superstructure","def":"Marx's model in which a society's economy (the base) shapes its law, politics, religion and culture (the superstructure).","more":"Marx sketched it in the preface to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy (1859): the 'relations of production' form the real foundation on which legal and political structures arise. How strictly the base determines the superstructure is fiercely debated; Engels himself said the economy is determining only 'in the last instance'. Later Marxists such as Gramsci gave culture more independent power.","etym":null,"example":"A Marxist might explain why property law protects factory owners by pointing to the economic system it grew out of.","notConfused":"Not the claim that people are always motivated by money: it concerns how social structures, not individual motives, are shaped.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Historical materialism","Ideology","Marxism"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["base","superstructure","marx","historical materialism","mode of production","economic determinism"]},{"term":"Dialectical materialism","def":"The philosophy, developed from Marx and Engels, that reality is material and changes through conflicts of opposing forces.","more":"Marx took Hegel's dialectic, the idea that development happens through contradiction, and applied it to material and economic conditions rather than to Spirit. Engels set out general 'laws of dialectics' in works such as Anti-Dühring (1878). The term itself was popularised later, by Georgi Plekhanov, and became the official philosophy of the Soviet Union. Many Marxist scholars distinguish Marx's own historical approach from this later system.","etym":null,"example":"Explaining the rise of capitalism as the outcome of tensions within feudal society, not as the result of new ideas alone.","notConfused":"Not the same as historical materialism: that is the theory of history in particular, while dialectical materialism claimed to be a general philosophy of nature too.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Historical materialism","Dialectic","Marxism"],"thinkers":["marx","hegel"],"tags":["diamat","dialectics","marxism","engels","marx","historical materialism"]},{"term":"Reification","def":"Treating something abstract or socially made, such as the market or a social relationship, as if it were a natural, fixed thing.","more":"Georg Lukács developed the concept in History and Class Consciousness (1923), building on Marx's commodity fetishism. He argued that capitalism makes people experience society as a set of objective, unchangeable laws, and even treat themselves as things. The Frankfurt School took up the idea. In logic, 'reification' also names the fallacy of treating an abstraction as a concrete thing.","etym":"Latin res, 'thing', and facere, 'to make': 'making into a thing'.","example":"Saying 'the market demanded layoffs' as if the market were a person, hiding the decisions of the people involved.","notConfused":"Not the same as objectification in feminist theory, which concerns treating persons as objects, though the ideas are related.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Commodity fetishism","Alienation","Critical theory"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["verdinglichung","lukacs","lukács","objectification","commodity fetishism","concretism","hypostatization"]},{"term":"Praxis","def":"Purposeful human action, especially action that changes the world, as opposed to pure theory.","more":"Aristotle distinguished praxis (action done for its own sake, like ethical and political life) from poiesis (making things) and theoria (contemplation). Marx used the word for the unity of theory and practice in changing society, famously writing that philosophers have only interpreted the world, 'the point is to change it'. Paulo Freire later described praxis as reflection and action aimed at transforming the world.","etym":"Greek praxis, 'action, doing'.","example":"A group that not only studies housing inequality but organises tenants to win better conditions, then reflects on what worked.","notConfused":"Not the same as practice in the sense of rehearsal: praxis means theory-informed action that transforms things.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Marxism","Critical theory","Phronesis"],"thinkers":["aristotle","marx"],"tags":["practice","theory and practice","marx","action","gramsci","praxis philosophy"]},{"term":"Divine right of kings","def":"The doctrine that monarchs receive their authority directly from God and so are answerable to God alone, not to their subjects.","more":"It flourished in 17th-century Europe; James VI and I of England wrote in its defence, and Robert Filmer's Patriarcha argued that kings inherited Adam's God-given authority. Bishop Bossuet defended it for Louis XIV. John Locke's First Treatise is a point-by-point attack on Filmer, and the doctrine faded as theories of the social contract and consent took hold.","etym":null,"example":"A king declaring that rebelling against him is rebelling against God.","notConfused":"Not the same as the Mandate of Heaven: the Chinese mandate could be lost by a ruler who governed badly, while divine right denied subjects any such judgment.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Mandate of Heaven","Social contract","Legitimacy"],"thinkers":["filmer","bossuet","locke"],"tags":["divine right","absolute monarchy","absolutism","filmer","monarchy","royal absolutism"]},{"term":"Popular sovereignty","def":"The principle that ultimate political authority belongs to the people, who are the source of government's legitimacy.","more":"Ideas of it go back to medieval thinkers such as Marsilius of Padua, but it was developed by social contract theorists, especially Rousseau, for whom sovereignty is the general will of the people and cannot be handed over. It shaped the American and French revolutions: the US Constitution opens 'We the People'. Debates continue over who 'the people' are and how their will is to be expressed.","etym":"Latin popularis, 'of the people'; Old French soverain, 'supreme'.","example":"A country holding a referendum to change its constitution because only the people can authorise such a change.","notConfused":"Not the same as direct democracy: popular sovereignty can be exercised through elected representatives.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Sovereignty","General will","Democracy"],"thinkers":["rousseau"],"tags":["sovereignty of the people","people's sovereignty","consent of the governed","democracy","rousseau","self-government"]},{"term":"Political obligation","def":"The moral duty, if there is one, to obey the law and support the state simply because it is your state.","more":"Socrates in Plato's Crito gave early arguments for it, including gratitude and implied agreement. Later theories appeal to consent, fair play, natural duties of justice or membership. A. John Simmons has argued that none of these succeed for most citizens, leading to philosophical anarchism: the view that there is no general duty to obey, though there may be good reasons to obey many laws.","etym":"Latin obligare, 'to bind'.","example":"Asking whether you are morally bound to obey a pointless law against crossing an empty road on a red light.","notConfused":"Not the same as legal obligation: the law says you must obey it; the question is whether you have a moral reason to.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Consent theory of political obligation","Philosophical anarchism","Legitimacy"],"thinkers":["socrates","simmons"],"tags":["duty to obey the law","obligation to obey","political authority","legitimacy","consent","civil disobedience"]},{"term":"Distributive justice","def":"Justice in how a society shares out benefits and burdens such as income, wealth, opportunities and duties.","more":"Aristotle distinguished distributive justice, giving to each according to merit, from corrective justice. In modern debates John Rawls's justice as fairness, Robert Nozick's entitlement theory, utilitarianism, luck egalitarianism and sufficientarianism offer rival principles. Questions include what is to be distributed (resources, welfare, capabilities) and whether patterns of distribution matter at all.","etym":null,"example":"Arguing about whether tax should take a larger share from high earners in order to fund public schools.","notConfused":"Not the same as corrective justice, which concerns repairing a specific wrong between two parties.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Justice as fairness","Entitlement theory","Corrective justice"],"thinkers":["aristotle","rawls","nozick"],"tags":["economic justice","social justice","fair distribution","redistribution","rawls","equality"]},{"term":"Corrective justice","def":"Justice that restores the balance when one person has wrongfully harmed or gained at another's expense.","more":"Aristotle described it in Nicomachean Ethics Book V as 'arithmetical': the judge takes from the one who gained and gives to the one who lost, regardless of who is the better person. It underlies much thinking about tort law and compensation. Some legal philosophers, such as Ernest Weinrib and Jules Coleman, see it as the core principle of private law.","etym":null,"example":"A court ordering a driver who crashed into your fence to pay for its repair.","notConfused":"Not the same as retributive justice, which concerns punishing the wrongdoer, or distributive justice, which concerns overall shares in society.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Distributive justice","Restorative justice","Reparations"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["rectificatory justice","restorative justice","compensation","aristotle","tort","rectification"]},{"term":"Reparations","def":"Payments or other measures to make amends to groups who were wronged, often for historical injustices such as slavery or colonial rule.","more":"Arguments for reparations appeal to corrective justice, to unjust enrichment, or to continuing harms that past wrongs still cause. Opponents raise the non-identity problem, the difficulty of identifying victims and perpetrators, and the fairness of charging people today. Ta-Nehisi Coates's 2014 essay 'The Case for Reparations' brought the issue back into public debate in the USA.","etym":"Latin reparare, 'to restore'.","example":"Germany's payments to survivors of the Holocaust and to Israel after the Second World War.","notConfused":"Not the same as charity or aid: reparations are owed as a matter of justice, because of a wrong, not just because someone is in need.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Corrective justice","Non-identity problem","Restorative justice"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["reparation","restitution","compensation","historical injustice","slavery reparations","redress"]},{"term":"Biopower","def":"Michel Foucault's term for modern power that manages populations' lives, health and bodies, rather than simply threatening death.","more":"Foucault introduced it in The History of Sexuality, Volume 1 (1976). He argued that, from the 18th century, states increasingly governed by measuring and regulating birth rates, public health, sexuality and life expectancy. This power works through statistics, medicine and norms rather than just laws and punishment. The idea has been influential in debates about public health, surveillance and medicine.","etym":"Greek bios, 'life', plus power.","example":"Government campaigns that track obesity rates and nudge whole populations to eat differently.","notConfused":"Not the same as biotechnology or the power of biology: it is a form of political power over life.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Panopticon","Power/knowledge","Paternalism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["biopolitics","bio-power","foucault","michel foucault","governmentality","discipline"]},{"term":"Panopticon","def":"A prison design in which one guard can watch every inmate unseen, later used by Foucault as a model of modern disciplinary power.","more":"Jeremy Bentham designed it in the 1780s and 1790s: cells arranged in a ring around a central tower, so inmates never know when they are being watched and so behave as if they always are. Foucault's Discipline and Punish (1975) used it as a symbol of how schools, factories and hospitals produce self-regulating individuals. It is now a common image in discussions of digital surveillance.","etym":"Greek pan, 'all', and optikon, 'for seeing'.","example":"Employees who never relax at their desks because their computer activity might be monitored at any moment.","notConfused":"Not just any surveillance system: the key point is that uncertainty about being watched makes people police themselves.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Biopower","Power/knowledge","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["bentham"],"tags":["panopticism","bentham","jeremy bentham","surveillance","foucault","discipline and punish"]},{"term":"Power/knowledge","def":"Foucault's claim that power and knowledge are bound together: power produces knowledge, and knowledge creates new forms of power.","more":"Foucault argued in works such as Discipline and Punish (1975) that sciences like psychiatry, criminology and medicine did not just discover facts about people; they arose with institutions that classified, examined and controlled them. He did not claim knowledge is simply false, but that what counts as knowledge is shaped by power relations. Critics worry that this blurs the line between truth and domination.","etym":null,"example":"The category 'juvenile delinquent' both describes certain young people and gives courts and social workers new ways to manage them.","notConfused":"Not the same as the saying 'knowledge is power' (from Francis Bacon): Foucault means the two are produced together, not that knowing things gives you control.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Biopower","Panopticon","Genealogy"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["power-knowledge","savoir-pouvoir","foucault","michel foucault","discourse","knowledge is power"]},{"term":"Recognition","def":"Being acknowledged by others as a person, an equal, or as having worth, which many philosophers see as a basic human need and a matter of justice.","more":"Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit (1807) made recognition central to self-consciousness, especially in the master–slave dialectic. In the 1990s Charles Taylor's 'The Politics of Recognition' and Axel Honneth's The Struggle for Recognition revived the idea. Nancy Fraser argued that justice requires both recognition and the redistribution of resources.","etym":"Latin recognoscere, 'to know again, examine'.","example":"A minority group campaigning not for more money but for its language to be taught in schools and respected in public life.","notConfused":"Not the same as fame or praise: recognition is about being treated as a full and equal member, not about being admired.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Master–slave dialectic","Double consciousness","Dignity view of hate speech"],"thinkers":["hegel"],"tags":["politics of recognition","honneth","axel honneth","charles taylor","hegel","respect","identity politics"]},{"term":"Public sphere","def":"The space in society where private people come together to discuss public matters and form public opinion, independent of the state.","more":"Jürgen Habermas traced its rise in The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere (1962): coffee houses, salons and newspapers in 18th-century Europe let citizens debate politics as equals. He argued that commercial mass media later degraded it. Critics such as Nancy Fraser pointed out that it excluded women and workers, who formed their own 'counterpublics'.","etym":null,"example":"Letters pages in newspapers, town hall meetings and online forums where citizens argue about policy.","notConfused":"Not the same as the public sector (government services): the public sphere is citizens' discussion, outside the state.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Deliberative democracy","Civil society","Critical theory"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["habermas","jürgen habermas","public opinion","deliberative democracy","public debate","civil society"]},{"term":"Banality of evil","def":"Hannah Arendt's phrase for the way great evil can be committed by ordinary, thoughtless people doing their jobs rather than by monsters.","more":"Arendt coined it in Eichmann in Jerusalem (1963), her report on the trial of Adolf Eichmann, a key organiser of the Holocaust. She found him not demonic but shallow and careerist, speaking in clichés and seemingly unable to think from others' points of view. The phrase caused fierce controversy, and later historians have argued that Eichmann was more ideologically committed than Arendt believed.","etym":null,"example":"An official who processes deportation orders efficiently, thinking only about meeting targets and pleasing superiors.","notConfused":"Not a claim that the evil itself was minor or that the perpetrator was innocent: Arendt supported Eichmann's death sentence.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Totalitarianism","Moral responsibility","Self-deception"],"thinkers":["arendt"],"tags":["arendt","hannah arendt","eichmann","eichmann in jerusalem","thoughtlessness","ordinary evil"]},{"term":"Political realism","def":"The view that politics is driven by power and interest, so political action should be judged by its results, not by ideal moral standards.","more":"Thucydides' Melian Dialogue ('the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must'), Machiavelli and Hobbes are often seen as founders. In international relations, Hans Morgenthau and others argued that states pursue national interest in an anarchic world. In political philosophy Bernard Williams and Raymond Geuss criticised 'moralistic' theories like Rawls's for ignoring how politics actually works.","etym":null,"example":"A government allying with a brutal regime because it is a counterweight to a more dangerous rival.","notConfused":"Not the same as moral realism (the view that there are objective moral facts): political realism is about the role of morality in politics.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Dirty hands","Virtù","State of nature"],"thinkers":["machiavelli","hobbes","williams"],"tags":["realpolitik","realism","machiavelli","hobbes","power politics","raison d'etat"]},{"term":"Virtù","def":"Machiavelli's term for the energy, skill and boldness that let a leader master fortune and achieve great things.","more":"In The Prince (1513) and the Discourses, Machiavelli contrasts virtù with fortuna, chance, which he likens to a river that can be channelled by those prepared for it. Virtù may include ruthlessness and deception when the situation requires. Scholars debate whether Machiavelli rejected Christian ethics or simply described a separate political excellence.","etym":"Italian virtù, from Latin virtus, 'manliness, excellence'.","example":"A leader who acts decisively in a sudden crisis, taking risks that more cautious rivals avoid, and comes out stronger.","notConfused":"Not the same as virtue in the moral sense: Machiavelli's virtù can involve acts that conventional morality condemns.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Political realism","Dirty hands","Civic humanism"],"thinkers":["machiavelli"],"tags":["virtu","virtue","machiavelli","fortuna","the prince","prowess"]},{"term":"Philosopher king","def":"Plato's ideal ruler, someone who has both political power and true philosophical knowledge of the Good.","more":"In the Republic, Socrates argues that cities will have no rest from troubles until philosophers become kings or kings become philosophers. Rulers would be selected and trained for decades, live without private property, and rule for the good of all. Karl Popper's The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945) attacked the idea as a blueprint for authoritarian rule.","etym":null,"example":"Arguing that a country should be run by its wisest experts rather than whoever wins a popularity contest.","notConfused":"Not the same as technocracy: Plato's rulers are guided by knowledge of the Good itself, not by technical expertise alone.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Epistocracy","Noble lie","Forms"],"thinkers":["plato","popper"],"tags":["philosopher-king","philosopher kings","philosopher ruler","plato","republic","guardians"]},{"term":"Noble lie","def":"In Plato's Republic, a myth told to citizens to make them accept their place in society and care for the city.","more":"Socrates proposes telling citizens that they were born from the earth and that the gods mixed gold, silver or bronze into their souls, marking them as rulers, guardians or producers. The aim is social harmony. Critics, notably Popper, saw it as propaganda; defenders read it as a reflection on the founding stories every society relies on.","etym":"Greek gennaion pseudos, 'noble (or well-born) falsehood'.","example":"A government spreading a comforting national myth about its founding to keep citizens united.","notConfused":"Not the same as a white lie told to spare someone's feelings: the noble lie is a political tool aimed at a whole society.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Philosopher king","Ideology","Paternalism"],"thinkers":["plato","popper"],"tags":["noble falsehood","myth of the metals","plato","republic","propaganda"]},{"term":"Ring of Gyges","def":"A myth in Plato's Republic about a ring of invisibility, used to ask whether anyone would be just if they could never be caught.","more":"Glaucon tells the story of a shepherd who finds a ring that makes him invisible, then seduces the queen, kills the king and takes the throne. He argues that people are just only out of fear of punishment. The rest of the Republic tries to show that justice is good for the soul in itself, so the just person is happier even unseen.","etym":null,"example":"Asking yourself what you would do if you knew for certain that no one would ever find out.","notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Justice","Egoism","Thought experiment"],"thinkers":["plato"],"tags":["gyges","ring of invisibility","invisibility","plato","republic","why be moral"]},{"term":"Political animal","def":"Aristotle's description of human beings as creatures whose nature is fulfilled only by living in a political community.","more":"In the Politics, Aristotle says that a human is by nature a political animal (zōon politikon) and that anyone who can live outside a city is either a beast or a god. Because humans have speech, they can discuss justice and the good together. The idea contrasts with social contract theories that picture people as independent individuals who choose to form states.","etym":"Greek zōon politikon, 'animal of the polis (city)'.","example":"People who feel that taking part in their town's decisions is part of a full life, not a chore.","notConfused":"Not the same as someone obsessed with party politics: Aristotle meant that living in community is part of human nature.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","State of nature","Eudaimonia"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["zoon politikon","political animals","social animal","aristotle","polis","human nature"]},{"term":"Civil society","def":"The realm of voluntary associations, such as clubs, charities, churches and unions, that sits between individuals and the state.","more":"For early modern thinkers like Locke the term simply meant political society as opposed to the state of nature. Hegel used it for the sphere of economic and social life between family and state. Tocqueville praised Americans' many associations as a defence against tyranny, and Robert Putnam's Bowling Alone (2000) worried about their decline.","etym":"Latin societas civilis, 'society of citizens'.","example":"A neighbourhood food bank run by volunteers, independent of both government and business.","notConfused":"Not the same as the state or the market, though some thinkers, including Hegel, included economic life within it.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Public sphere","Communitarianism","State of nature"],"thinkers":["hegel","locke"],"tags":["civil society organizations","voluntary associations","ngos","associational life","hegel","tocqueville"]},{"term":"Invisible hand","def":"Adam Smith's metaphor for how people pursuing their own interests in a market can, without intending it, promote the good of society.","more":"Smith uses the phrase only a few times, most famously in The Wealth of Nations (1776), where a merchant investing at home is 'led by an invisible hand to promote an end which was no part of his intention'. Later economists turned it into a general case for free markets. Many scholars argue Smith was more cautious, and his Theory of Moral Sentiments stresses sympathy and virtue.","etym":null,"example":"A baker bakes bread to earn a living, not to feed the town, yet the town is fed.","notConfused":"Not the same as laissez-faire: Smith supported some government roles, such as public works and education.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Enlightened self-interest","Classical liberalism","Tragedy of the commons"],"thinkers":["smith"],"tags":["adam smith","free market","laissez-faire","spontaneous order","self-interest","market economy"]},{"term":"Intersectionality","def":"The idea that forms of discrimination such as racism and sexism interact, creating distinct experiences that cannot be understood by looking at each separately.","more":"Legal scholar Kimberlé Crenshaw coined the term in 1989, showing how anti-discrimination law failed Black women whose claims fit neither 'race' nor 'sex' cases alone. The idea draws on earlier Black feminist thought, including the Combahee River Collective and Angela Davis. Debates concern how to handle many overlapping categories and whether it is best seen as a theory or a method.","etym":"Latin intersectio, 'a cutting across'.","example":"A company that hires Black men and white women might still exclude Black women, a pattern hidden if race and gender are examined separately.","notConfused":"Not the same as simply adding up disadvantages: the claim is that combinations create new, specific forms of experience.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Feminist philosophy","Standpoint theory","Patriarchy"],"thinkers":["davis_angela"],"tags":["intersectional","crenshaw","kimberlé crenshaw","kimberle crenshaw","multiple oppression","black feminism"]},{"term":"Patriarchy","def":"A social system in which men hold most power and authority, and male dominance is built into institutions and norms.","more":"Originally the term meant rule by fathers, and Robert Filmer used patriarchal authority to defend absolute monarchy. Second-wave feminists such as Kate Millett in Sexual Politics (1970) redefined it as systematic male domination across society. Philosophers debate whether it is a useful single concept or too broad to explain varied forms of gender inequality.","etym":"Greek patriarchēs, 'chief of a family', from patēr, 'father', and archein, 'to rule'.","example":"A society where women can vote but are still expected to do most childcare and are rarely seen in top leadership roles.","notConfused":"Not the same as a claim that all men are bad or all men benefit equally: it describes a structure, not individual attitudes.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Feminist philosophy","Ideology","Intersectionality"],"thinkers":["filmer","beauvoir"],"tags":["male dominance","patriarchal society","sexism","feminism","gender oppression","androcentrism"]},{"term":"Double consciousness","def":"W. E. B. Du Bois's term for the experience of seeing oneself both through one's own eyes and through the eyes of a society that looks on with contempt.","more":"Du Bois introduced it in The Souls of Black Folk (1903), describing Black Americans' 'two-ness: an American, a Negro; two souls, two thoughts, two unreconciled strivings'. It links to his image of 'the veil' separating Black and white worlds. The concept has been widely applied to colonised peoples and other marginalised groups, and influenced Frantz Fanon.","etym":null,"example":"Someone constantly checking how their accent, hair or clothes will be judged by others who see them as outsiders.","notConfused":"Not the same as split personality or self-consciousness in general: it is a socially imposed divided self-awareness.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Recognition","Postcolonialism","Alienation"],"thinkers":["du_bois","fanon"],"tags":["du bois","w. e. b. du bois","souls of black folk","the veil","twoness","race"]},{"term":"Orientalism","def":"Edward Said's term for the Western tradition of portraying 'the East' as exotic, backward and irrational, in ways that supported colonial power.","more":"Said's Orientalism (1978) examined European scholarship, literature and art about the Middle East and Asia, arguing that it created an image of 'the Orient' as the West's opposite. Drawing on Foucault, he argued that this knowledge was bound up with domination. The book helped found postcolonial studies, though critics accused Said of oversimplifying Western scholarship.","etym":"Latin oriens, 'rising (sun), east'.","example":"Films that show Middle Eastern cities only as deserts, bazaars and danger, with no ordinary modern life.","notConfused":"Before Said, 'orientalism' simply meant the academic study of Asian and Middle Eastern languages and cultures.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Postcolonialism","Power/knowledge","Ideology"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["said","edward said","postcolonialism","the east","exoticism","eurocentrism"]},{"term":"Human rights","def":"Rights that every person has simply by being human, regardless of nationality, status or the laws of their country.","more":"They descend from earlier natural rights theories, but the modern framework was set by the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948). Philosophers debate their basis (human dignity, agency, needs, or political practice) and scope, for example whether rights to healthcare or holidays are genuine human rights. Critics from Bentham ('nonsense upon stilts', aimed at natural rights) to Marx questioned the whole idea.","etym":null,"example":"Condemning torture in any country, even where it is legal there, on the grounds that it violates human rights.","notConfused":"Not the same as legal rights: a right granted by a particular country's law might not be a human right, and a human right may not be protected by law.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Natural rights","Rights","Cosmopolitanism"],"thinkers":["bentham","marx"],"tags":["universal rights","natural rights","rights of man","udhr","universal declaration of human rights","human dignity"]},{"term":"Legal positivism","def":"The theory that what the law is depends on social facts, such as what has been enacted or accepted, not on whether it is morally good.","more":"Jeremy Bentham and John Austin developed early versions, with Austin defining law as the commands of a sovereign. H. L. A. Hart's The Concept of Law (1961) refined it, explaining law as a system of rules unified by a 'rule of recognition'. Ronald Dworkin and natural law theorists like John Finnis argue against it that moral principles are part of the law.","etym":"Latin positum, 'laid down, posited'.","example":"Saying that a racist law passed by a legitimate parliament really is law, while insisting that it is a bad law that should be repealed.","notConfused":"Not the claim that people should obey every law: positivists separate what law is from whether we ought to follow it.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Natural law theory","Rule of law","Legitimacy"],"thinkers":["bentham","hart","dworkin","finnis"],"tags":["positivism (law)","hart","h. l. a. hart","austin","kelsen","law as social fact"]},{"term":"Overlapping consensus","def":"John Rawls's idea that people with different religious and moral views can each endorse the same political principles for their own reasons.","more":"Rawls introduced it in Political Liberalism (1993) to explain how a just society could be stable despite 'reasonable pluralism'. Catholics, secular Kantians and utilitarians might all support equal liberty, each from within their own doctrine. Critics question whether such a consensus is realistic, or whether it excludes too many people as 'unreasonable'.","etym":null,"example":"A religious believer and an atheist both supporting freedom of conscience, one because faith must be freely chosen, the other because of respect for autonomy.","notConfused":"Not the same as a compromise or truce: each side genuinely endorses the shared principles, not just tolerates them.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Political liberalism","Public reason liberalism","Value pluralism"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"tags":["rawls","john rawls","political liberalism","reasonable pluralism","public reason","consensus"]},{"term":"Maximin","def":"The rule of choosing the option whose worst possible outcome is better than the worst outcome of any other option.","more":"The rule comes from decision theory. Rawls argued in A Theory of Justice (1971) that parties in the original position, ignorant of their place in society, would reason in a maximin way and so choose principles that protect the worst-off. John Harsanyi objected that rational choosers would instead maximise average expected utility, leading to utilitarianism.","etym":"From 'maximum' and 'minimum': maximise the minimum.","example":"Choosing a job with a modest but guaranteed salary over one that might pay a fortune but might pay nothing.","notConfused":"Not the same as the difference principle itself: maximin is a decision rule; the difference principle is a principle of justice Rawls thought it supports.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Original position","Difference principle","Veil of ignorance"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"tags":["maximin rule","minimax","worst case","rawls","difference principle","decision under uncertainty"]},{"term":"Civil religion","def":"A set of shared public beliefs, symbols and rituals that bind citizens to their political community, somewhat like a religion.","more":"Rousseau introduced the term in The Social Contract (1762), proposing a minimal civil creed including belief in God, an afterlife, the sanctity of the social contract and tolerance. In 1967 the sociologist Robert Bellah argued that the USA has a civil religion expressed in presidential speeches, national holidays and reverence for the flag. Critics worry it can turn patriotism into idolatry.","etym":null,"example":"Citizens standing in solemn silence for a national anthem, or treating the founding documents almost as sacred texts.","notConfused":"Not the same as an established state church: civil religion is shared across denominations and focuses on the nation.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Secularism","General will","Toleration"],"thinkers":["rousseau"],"tags":["civic religion","rousseau","bellah","robert bellah","patriotism","national rituals"]},{"term":"Habitus","def":"Pierre Bourdieu's term for the deep-seated habits, tastes and ways of acting that people absorb from their social background.","more":"Bourdieu developed the idea in works such as Distinction (1979). Habitus makes certain choices feel natural, from what food we like to what careers seem realistic, and so helps class differences reproduce themselves without anyone planning it. The word had been used before by Aristotle's Latin translators and by Marcel Mauss, but Bourdieu gave it its modern sociological sense.","etym":"Latin habitus, 'condition, bearing, dress'.","example":"A student from a family with no university graduates feeling out of place at a fancy college dinner without being able to say exactly why.","notConfused":"Not the same as personal habit: habitus is a socially shaped set of dispositions shared by people of similar background.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Cultural capital","Sociological critique of taste","Social construction"],"thinkers":["bourdieu"],"tags":["bourdieu","pierre bourdieu","dispositions","habits","embodied habits","social conditioning"]},{"term":"Cultural capital","def":"Bourdieu's term for non-financial social assets, such as education, tastes, manners and knowledge, that give people advantages.","more":"Bourdieu, with Jean-Claude Passeron, introduced the concept in the 1970s to explain why children of educated parents do better at school even when money is equal. Cultural capital can be embodied (ways of speaking), objectified (books and art) or institutionalised (qualifications). Critics argue it can underplay individual talent and effort.","etym":null,"example":"A child who has grown up visiting museums finding it easier to impress teachers in art class.","notConfused":"Not the same as economic capital (money) or social capital (useful networks), though Bourdieu thought they can be converted into each other.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Habitus","Sociological critique of taste","Equal opportunity for welfare"],"thinkers":["bourdieu"],"tags":["bourdieu","pierre bourdieu","social capital","symbolic capital","taste","social class"]},{"term":"Nationalism","def":"The view that nations are important political communities and that each nation should have its own state or self-government.","more":"Modern nationalism took shape in the late 18th and 19th centuries, with Herder and Fichte stressing shared language and culture. Ernest Gellner and Benedict Anderson (who called nations 'imagined communities') argued nations are modern creations. Philosophers such as David Miller defend a liberal nationalism, while critics point to its role in wars and exclusion.","etym":"Latin natio, 'birth, people'.","example":"A movement campaigning for a region with its own language to become an independent country.","notConfused":"Not the same as patriotism: patriotism is love of one's country; nationalism is a political doctrine about nations and states.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Liberal nationalism","Cosmopolitanism","Patriotism"],"thinkers":["david_miller"],"tags":["nation","national identity","national self-determination","nation-state","patriotism"]},{"term":"Patriotism","def":"Love of and loyalty to one's country, often including special concern for its people and pride in its achievements.","more":"Alasdair MacIntyre's 1984 lecture 'Is Patriotism a Virtue?' argued that patriotism conflicts with impartial liberal morality. Martha Nussbaum's essay 'Patriotism and Cosmopolitanism' (1994) argued that our primary loyalty should be to humanity as a whole. Others defend a moderate patriotism that loves one's country while criticising its faults.","etym":"Greek patriōtēs, 'fellow countryman', from patris, 'fatherland'.","example":"Feeling pride when a national team wins, or shame when your government does something unjust.","notConfused":"Not the same as nationalism: one can love one's country without believing every nation must have its own state.","field":"Politics and society","related":["Nationalism","Cosmopolitanism","Special obligations"],"thinkers":["macintyre","nussbaum"],"tags":["patriot","love of country","national loyalty","nationalism","civic patriotism","loyalty"]},{"term":"Cataphatic theology","def":"Speaking about God positively, by saying what God is (good, wise, powerful), rather than only what God is not.","more":"It is usually paired with apophatic or negative theology, which says that God transcends all our concepts. The 6th-century writer known as Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite discussed both ways and argued that the negative way is higher. Aquinas tried to steer between them with his doctrine of analogy, holding that positive terms apply to God in a stretched but meaningful sense.","etym":"Greek kataphasis, 'affirmation'.","example":"Saying 'God is love' is cataphatic; saying 'God is not limited by time' is apophatic.","notConfused":"Not the same as anthropomorphism: cataphatic theologians usually insist that positive terms apply to God only in an exalted or analogical sense.","field":"Religion","related":["Negative theology","Analogy of being","Via negativa"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"tags":["kataphatic theology","affirmative theology","positive theology","via positiva","apophatic theology"]},{"term":"Theosis","def":"The Christian teaching, especially in Eastern Orthodoxy, that human beings can be transformed by grace to share in the divine life.","more":"It is often summed up in a saying attributed to Athanasius: God became human so that humans might become divine. Orthodox theologians such as Gregory Palamas distinguished God's essence, which remains unknowable, from God's energies, in which humans can participate. Theosis does not mean that people become God by nature, but that they come to share God's life.","etym":"Greek theōsis, 'deification', from theos, 'god'.","example":"A monk who spends years in prayer, aiming not just to be forgiven but to be gradually transformed in likeness to God.","notConfused":"Not the same as pantheism: humans do not merge into God or lose their identity; the creature-creator distinction remains.","field":"Religion","related":["Grace","Mysticism","Imago Dei"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["deification","divinization","theōsis","eastern orthodoxy","sanctification"]},{"term":"Imago Dei","def":"The teaching in Judaism and Christianity that human beings are made in the image of God, which gives them special dignity.","more":"It comes from Genesis 1:27. Thinkers have identified the image with reason (Augustine, Aquinas), with the capacity for relationship, or with humanity's role as stewards of creation. The idea has been used to ground human equality and human rights, and to argue against treating any person as a mere thing.","etym":"Latin imago Dei, 'image of God'.","example":"A religious activist arguing that every prisoner, however terrible their crime, retains a dignity that forbids torture.","notConfused":"Not a claim that God has a human-shaped body: most interpreters take the image to be spiritual or relational.","field":"Religion","related":["Moral status","Human rights","Soul"],"thinkers":["augustine","aquinas"],"tags":["image of god","human dignity","genesis","tselem elohim"]},{"term":"Original sin","def":"The Christian doctrine that humanity inherited a damaged, sin-prone condition from the first humans' disobedience.","more":"Augustine gave the doctrine its classic shape in his debates with Pelagius in the early 5th century, arguing that people cannot be good without God's grace. It was central for Luther and Calvin, while Eastern Orthodoxy usually speaks of inheriting mortality and corruption rather than guilt. Philosophers have questioned how anyone could be responsible for an ancestor's act.","etym":null,"example":"An explanation of why even small children can be selfish, and why even good people struggle to do what they know is right.","notConfused":"Not the same as the first sin itself: original sin is the inherited state, not Adam and Eve's act.","field":"Religion","related":["Grace","Problem of evil","Felix culpa"],"thinkers":["augustine"],"tags":["ancestral sin","the fall","fall of man","adam and eve","augustine","sin"]},{"term":"Grace","def":"In Christian theology, God's free and undeserved favour, which saves people and enables them to do good.","more":"Augustine argued against Pelagius that humans cannot earn salvation or even turn to God without grace. During the Reformation, Luther and Calvin stressed salvation by grace through faith alone, while the Council of Trent held that grace works with human free will. Philosophers discuss how grace can be reconciled with human freedom and responsibility.","etym":"Latin gratia, 'favour, kindness, thanks'.","example":"A believer who says their recovery from addiction was not their own achievement but a gift they did not deserve.","notConfused":"Not the same as reward: grace is by definition unearned.","field":"Religion","related":["Original sin","Predestination","Faith"],"thinkers":["augustine","aquinas"],"tags":["divine grace","sola gratia","salvation","unmerited favour","justification","augustine"]},{"term":"Predestination","def":"The doctrine that God has decided in advance who will be saved, and perhaps who will be lost.","more":"Augustine taught that God chooses the elect by grace, not because of their foreseen merits. John Calvin held a 'double predestination' in which God also chooses who will be damned. Arminians and others reply that God's choice depends on foreseen free responses. In Islam, a parallel debate about divine decree (qadar) divided Mu'tazilites from Ash'arites.","etym":"Latin praedestinare, 'to determine beforehand'.","example":"A Calvinist wondering whether her love of God is a sign that she is among the elect.","notConfused":"Not the same as foreknowledge: God might know what you will choose without having caused or decided it.","field":"Religion","related":["Theological determinism","Grace","Providence"],"thinkers":["augustine"],"tags":["predestination doctrine","double predestination","calvinism","calvin","election","foreordination"]},{"term":"Providence","def":"God's care for and guidance of the world, by which all events are directed toward good ends.","more":"Stoics spoke of providence (pronoia) as the rational order of the cosmos. Christian, Jewish and Islamic thinkers such as Boethius, Maimonides and Aquinas developed accounts of how God governs the world while leaving room for human freedom. The problem of evil sharpens the question of how a providential God can permit terrible events.","etym":"Latin providentia, 'foresight', from providere, 'to see ahead'.","example":"Someone who missed a flight that later crashed saying, 'It was providence.'","notConfused":"Not the same as fate: providence is the purposeful care of a personal God, whereas fate can be blind and impersonal.","field":"Religion","related":["Problem of evil","Predestination","Molinism"],"thinkers":["boethius","aquinas","maimonides"],"tags":["divine providence","god's plan","fate","sovereignty of god","divine guidance"]},{"term":"Eschatology","def":"The part of theology concerned with the 'last things': death, judgment, the end of the world and the final destiny of souls.","more":"Jewish, Christian and Islamic traditions all teach a final judgment and resurrection, though they differ in detail. Twentieth-century theologians debated whether the Kingdom of God is future, already present, or both. Philosophers ask whether resurrection is coherent and whether eternal punishment could be just.","etym":"Greek eschatos, 'last', and logos, 'study'.","example":"Debating whether the world is heading toward a final restoration or simply toward the heat death of the universe.","notConfused":"Not the same as apocalypticism, a type of eschatology expecting an imminent, dramatic end revealed in visions.","field":"Religion","related":["Resurrection","Immortality","Beatific vision"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["end times","last things","apocalypse","judgment day","afterlife","eschaton"]},{"term":"Creatio ex nihilo","def":"The doctrine that God created the universe out of nothing, not from pre-existing matter.","more":"It became standard in Christian, Jewish and Islamic theology by the 2nd and 3rd centuries AD, distinguishing God from Plato's Demiurge, who shaped existing material. Aquinas held that creation from nothing can be known by reason but that a beginning in time is known only by revelation. Defenders of the kalam cosmological argument appeal to it.","etym":"Latin creatio ex nihilo, 'creation from nothing'.","example":"Contrasting a sculptor shaping clay with the idea of bringing both the clay and the sculptor into being.","notConfused":"Not the same as the idea that something came from nothing without a cause: in creatio ex nihilo, God is the cause.","field":"Religion","related":["Kalam","Cosmological argument","Classical theism"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"tags":["creation from nothing","ex nihilo","creation","genesis","creator god"]},{"term":"Beatific vision","def":"The direct vision of God that, in Christian theology, makes up the ultimate happiness of the blessed in heaven.","more":"Aquinas argued in the Summa Theologiae that perfect human happiness can consist only in seeing God's essence, something impossible in this life without divine help. The idea joins Aristotle's view that contemplation is the highest activity with Christian hope of union with God. Debates concern whether finite minds can truly see an infinite God.","etym":"Latin beatus, 'blessed, happy', and visio, 'seeing'.","example":"A medieval monk describing heaven not as a reward of pleasures but as the endless joy of knowing God face to face.","notConfused":"Not the same as a mystical vision in this life: the beatific vision is the final, permanent state of the saved.","field":"Religion","related":["Eudaimonia","Summum bonum","Eschatology"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"tags":["vision of god","heaven","beatitude","aquinas","eternal happiness"]},{"term":"Mysticism","def":"The pursuit or experience of direct union with or awareness of God or ultimate reality, beyond ordinary thought and perception.","more":"Mystical traditions exist in Christianity (Meister Eckhart, Teresa of Ávila), Islam (Sufism), Judaism (Kabbalah), Hinduism and Buddhism. William James listed four marks of mystical experience in The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902): ineffability, a sense of knowledge, transience and passivity. Philosophers debate whether mystics across traditions experience the same thing and whether such experiences give knowledge.","etym":"Greek mystikos, 'secret, connected with the mysteries', from myein, 'to close (the eyes or lips)'.","example":"A person meditating who feels for a moment that the boundary between themselves and everything else has vanished.","notConfused":"Not the same as occultism or magic: mysticism seeks union with ultimate reality, not secret powers.","field":"Religion","related":["Religious experience","Ineffable","Sufism"],"thinkers":["james"],"tags":["mystic","mystical experience","union with god","contemplation","sufism","meister eckhart"]},{"term":"Natural evil","def":"Suffering not caused by any free agent, such as that from earthquakes, disease or animal predation.","more":"Philosophers of religion distinguish it from moral evil, the suffering caused by wrong human choices. The free will defence explains moral evil more easily than natural evil. Responses include the soul-making theodicy, the claim that natural laws must be regular to allow free choice, and Alvin Plantinga's suggestion that some natural evil might come from fallen non-human free agents.","etym":null,"example":"The 1755 Lisbon earthquake, which killed tens of thousands and shook Voltaire's faith in a good providence.","notConfused":"Not the same as moral evil: a murder is a moral evil; a tsunami is a natural evil.","field":"Religion","related":["Problem of evil","Free will defence","Soul-making theodicy"],"thinkers":["voltaire","plantinga"],"tags":["natural evils","problem of evil","suffering","animal suffering","theodicy"]},{"term":"Felix culpa","def":"The 'happy fault': the idea that the Fall of humanity was fortunate because it led to the greater good of redemption.","more":"The phrase comes from the Exsultet, an ancient Easter hymn: 'O happy fault that earned for us so great a Redeemer.' Thinkers including Augustine and Aquinas suggested that God permits evil in order to bring greater good from it. Alvin Plantinga has defended a 'felix culpa theodicy', arguing that a world with incarnation and atonement is better than any world without sin.","etym":"Latin felix culpa, 'happy fault'.","example":"Saying of a painful breakup that led you to find your true partner, 'It was the best thing that ever happened to me.'","notConfused":"Not a claim that sin is good in itself: it is a claim about the good that God brings out of it.","field":"Religion","related":["Original sin","Theodicy","Problem of evil"],"thinkers":["augustine","aquinas","plantinga"],"tags":["happy fault","fortunate fall","o felix culpa","the fall","original sin","theodicy"]},{"term":"Best of all possible worlds","def":"Leibniz's thesis that, since God is perfectly good, wise and powerful, God must have created the best world possible.","more":"Leibniz argued for it in the Theodicy (1710): the evils in this world are part of the best overall combination, as shadows are part of a fine painting. Voltaire mocked the idea in Candide (1759) through the endlessly optimistic Dr Pangloss. Critics also question whether there is a best possible world at all, or whether for any world one could imagine a better.","etym":null,"example":"Dr Pangloss insisting, after every disaster, that 'all is for the best in the best of all possible worlds'.","notConfused":"Not the claim that this world contains no evil or that everything in it is good: only that no better whole was possible.","field":"Religion","related":["Theodicy","Problem of evil","Possible worlds"],"thinkers":["leibniz","voltaire"],"tags":["best possible world","leibniz","optimism","candide","voltaire","theodicy"]},{"term":"Fine-tuning argument","def":"The argument that the physical constants of the universe are set within the narrow range that allows life, which is better explained by a designer than by chance.","more":"Physicists have noted that small changes in constants like the strength of gravity would make stars, chemistry or life impossible. Defenders such as Richard Swinburne and Robin Collins argue this is far more probable if God exists. Critics reply with the multiverse hypothesis, with the anthropic principle (we could only observe a life-permitting universe), and with doubts about how to assign probabilities to constants.","etym":null,"example":"Comparing the universe to a radio dial that had to be set to exactly the right frequency out of billions to pick up any signal.","notConfused":"Not the same as the classical design argument from biology: fine-tuning concerns the laws and constants of physics, which evolution cannot explain.","field":"Religion","related":["Design argument","Natural theology","Inference to the best explanation"],"thinkers":["swinburne"],"tags":["fine tuning argument","teleological argument","design argument","anthropic principle","intelligent design"]},{"term":"Omnibenevolence","def":"Perfect goodness: the attribute of being wholly good and loving, traditionally ascribed to God.","more":"Together with omnipotence and omniscience, it makes up the classical triad used in the problem of evil. Philosophers ask whether perfect goodness means maximising happiness, or something else such as perfect love or justice. Some theologians, influenced by classical theism, prefer to say God is goodness itself rather than a being who is good.","etym":"Latin omnis, 'all', and benevolentia, 'goodwill'.","example":"Someone asking how a perfectly loving God could allow children to suffer is appealing to omnibenevolence.","notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Omnipotence","Omniscience","Problem of evil"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["all-loving","all-good","omnibenevolent","divine goodness","perfect goodness","god's goodness"]},{"term":"Aseity","def":"The property of existing from oneself: depending on nothing else for one's existence, traditionally attributed to God.","more":"Anselm and Aquinas treated God as self-existent, the uncaused source of all other being. Aseity raises puzzles about abstract objects: if numbers and properties exist necessarily, do they depend on God? Some theists answer that they are ideas in God's mind; others accept that they exist independently.","etym":"Latin a se, 'from oneself'.","example":"Everything you can point to, from a tree to a star, exists because of something else; aseity is the claim that God alone does not.","notConfused":"Not the same as eternity: something might exist forever yet still depend on something else.","field":"Religion","related":["Necessary being","Classical theism","Cosmological argument"],"thinkers":["anselm","aquinas"],"tags":["self-existence","a se","independence","necessary being","divine simplicity","uncaused"]},{"term":"Analogy of being","def":"The view, associated with Aquinas, that words like 'good' and 'exists' apply to God and creatures neither in exactly the same nor in a completely different sense, but analogically.","more":"Aquinas argued that if the words meant exactly the same thing, God would be reduced to a creature; if they meant something totally different, we could say nothing true about God. Analogy steers between these extremes. Duns Scotus defended univocity instead, and the 20th-century theologian Karl Barth criticised analogia entis as a human attempt to reach God.","etym":"Latin analogia entis, 'analogy of being'.","example":"We call both a person and a policy 'healthy', in related but different senses; similarly 'wise' applies differently to a human and to God.","notConfused":"Not the same as metaphor: analogy is meant to be literally true, though not in the same sense in both uses.","field":"Religion","related":["Cataphatic theology","Univocity of being","Negative theology"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"tags":["analogia entis","analogical predication","aquinas","thomas aquinas","religious language"]},{"term":"Univocity of being","def":"John Duns Scotus's view that 'being' has exactly the same basic meaning when said of God and of creatures.","more":"Scotus argued around 1300 that unless 'being' meant the same thing in both cases, we could not reason from creatures to God at all. He held that God and creatures differ not in the meaning of 'being' but in degree, God being infinite. Aquinas's followers defended analogy instead, and some modern thinkers, notably Gilles Deleuze, took univocity in a very different, non-theological direction.","etym":"Latin univocus, 'having one voice or meaning'.","example":"Saying 'God exists' and 'my cat exists' use 'exists' in the same sense, though God's existence is infinite and the cat's is not.","notConfused":"Not the claim that God and creatures are equal or alike in every way: the shared concept is minimal.","field":"Religion","related":["Analogy of being","Scholasticism","Haecceity"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["univocal","univocity","duns scotus","scotus","deleuze","religious language"]},{"term":"Numinous","def":"Rudolf Otto's term for the experience of the holy as an overwhelming mystery that is both terrifying and fascinating.","more":"Otto introduced it in The Idea of the Holy (1917), describing a 'mysterium tremendum et fascinans' that cannot be reduced to moral or rational ideas. He thought it was the core of all religious experience. The concept influenced religious studies and writers such as C. S. Lewis. Critics doubt that experiences across religions share a single essence.","etym":"Latin numen, 'divine power or presence'.","example":"The sense of awe and smallness some people feel entering a vast ancient cathedral or standing under a night sky.","notConfused":"Not the same as the sublime, though related: the numinous is specifically a sense of the holy or divine.","field":"Religion","related":["Religious experience","Sublime","Sacred and profane"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["holy","numen","mysterium tremendum","rudolf otto","otto","the sacred"]},{"term":"Sacred and profane","def":"The distinction between things set apart as holy and things belonging to ordinary, everyday life.","more":"The sociologist Émile Durkheim made it central to his definition of religion in The Elementary Forms of Religious Life (1912), arguing that sacred things express society's power over individuals. The historian of religion Mircea Eliade's The Sacred and the Profane (1957) described how sacred times and places break into ordinary experience. Scholars debate whether the distinction is universal.","etym":"Latin sacer, 'holy'; profanus, 'outside the temple'.","example":"Taking off your shoes before entering a mosque or temple marks the move from profane space into sacred space.","notConfused":"Not the same as good and evil: profane things are not wicked, just ordinary.","field":"Religion","related":["Numinous","Religious experience","Secularism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["sacred","profane","holy","durkheim","eliade","mircea eliade"]},{"term":"Monotheism","def":"Belief in exactly one God, as in Judaism, Christianity and Islam.","more":"Monotheism is usually contrasted with polytheism (many gods) and with henotheism (worship of one god while accepting others exist). Philosophical arguments for God usually aim at a single being with perfect attributes. Christianity's doctrine of the Trinity and some Hindu and Sikh traditions raise questions about what counts as one God.","etym":"Greek monos, 'single', and theos, 'god'.","example":"The opening of the Jewish Shema: 'Hear, O Israel: the Lord our God, the Lord is one.'","notConfused":"Not the same as theism, which in philosophy usually means belief in a personal God; theism is typically monotheistic, but monotheism is a count of gods.","field":"Religion","related":["Theism","Polytheism","Tawhid"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["one god","monotheist","abrahamic religions","theism","judaism","islam"]},{"term":"Ground of being","def":"Paul Tillich's description of God not as a being among others but as the power of being itself, on which everything depends.","more":"In his Systematic Theology (1951–1963), Tillich argued that calling God 'a being', even the highest, makes God one thing alongside others. Instead God is being-itself or the ground of being. He also said that to argue for God's existence is, in a way, to deny God. Critics argue this makes God so abstract that it is unclear what is left to believe.","etym":null,"example":"Rather than asking whether there is a God 'out there', asking what makes it the case that anything exists at all.","notConfused":"Not the same as pantheism: Tillich held that God is the ground of the world, not identical with it.","field":"Religion","related":["Ultimate concern","Classical theism","Necessary being"],"thinkers":["tillich"],"tags":["tillich","paul tillich","being itself","ultimate reality","god beyond god"]},{"term":"Ultimate concern","def":"Paul Tillich's definition of faith as being grasped by what concerns a person unconditionally.","more":"Tillich argued in Dynamics of Faith (1957) that everyone has some ultimate concern, whether God, nation, success or something else. When a finite thing like money or a nation is treated as ultimate, he called it idolatry. The idea helps explain how non-religious commitments can function like religions in people's lives.","etym":null,"example":"For some people, career success is their ultimate concern: everything else is sacrificed to it.","notConfused":"Not the same as a strong interest: an ultimate concern is unconditional and claims a person's whole life.","field":"Religion","related":["Ground of being","Faith","Leap of faith"],"thinkers":["tillich"],"tags":["tillich","paul tillich","faith","dynamics of faith","ultimate concerns"]},{"term":"I–Thou","def":"Martin Buber's term for a direct, whole-hearted relationship of mutual presence with another, contrasted with treating them as an object (I–It).","more":"Buber set out the idea in I and Thou (1923). In I–It relations we use, classify and experience things; in I–Thou relations we meet the other as a whole being. Buber held that every genuine I–Thou relationship gives a glimpse of the 'eternal Thou', God. The book influenced theology, education, psychotherapy and the ethics of care.","etym":"German Ich und Du, 'I and you' (familiar form).","example":"The difference between talking to a shop assistant only as a means to a purchase and truly meeting them as a person.","notConfused":"Not a claim that I–It relations are bad: Buber thought they are necessary, but that a life of only I–It is not fully human.","field":"Religion","related":["The Other","Recognition","Ethics of care"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["i-thou","i and thou","ich und du","buber","martin buber","i-it"]},{"term":"Necessary being","def":"A being that could not fail to exist, whose non-existence is impossible.","more":"Avicenna developed the distinction between necessary and possible (contingent) being and argued that contingent things must ultimately depend on a being necessary in itself. Aquinas's third way and Leibniz's argument from contingency build on similar ideas. Critics such as Hume and Kant questioned whether the idea of a necessarily existing being even makes sense.","etym":null,"example":"Your existence depends on your parents, theirs on theirs, and so on; a necessary being would be one whose existence depends on nothing.","notConfused":"Not the same as an eternal being: something could exist forever by chance, but a necessary being exists in every possible world.","field":"Religion","related":["Aseity","Cosmological argument","Necessary/contingent"],"thinkers":["avicenna","aquinas","leibniz"],"tags":["necessary existence","necessary existent","contingency argument","cosmological argument","aseity","wajib al-wujud"]},{"term":"Privation theory of evil","def":"The view that evil is not a positive thing in itself but the absence of a good that ought to be there.","more":"Plotinus and especially Augustine developed it; Augustine used it to argue that God, who created only good things, did not create evil. Aquinas adopted it, describing evil as privatio boni, the privation of good. Critics argue that pain and cruelty seem all too real to be mere absences.","etym":"Latin privatio, 'deprivation, a taking away'.","example":"Blindness is not an extra thing in the eye but the lack of sight; on this view evil in general works the same way.","notConfused":"Not the claim that evil is unreal or an illusion: privations, like holes, are real lacks.","field":"Religion","related":["Problem of evil","Theodicy","Neoplatonism"],"thinkers":["augustine","plotinus","aquinas"],"tags":["privatio boni","privation","absence of good","augustine","evil as privation"]},{"term":"Sensus divinitatis","def":"John Calvin's term for an inborn sense of the divine that he thought all humans have.","more":"Calvin argued in the Institutes of the Christian Religion (1536, later expanded) that God implanted in every person an awareness of God's existence. Alvin Plantinga revived the idea in Warranted Christian Belief (2000), arguing that belief in God produced by such a faculty could be justified without arguments, like perception or memory. Critics ask why, if it exists, so many people lack belief in God.","etym":"Latin sensus divinitatis, 'sense of the divine'.","example":"Someone gazing at a starry sky and finding themselves simply believing that God made it, without reasoning to that conclusion.","notConfused":"Not the same as religious experience in general: it is a proposed universal faculty, not a particular unusual experience.","field":"Religion","related":["Reformed epistemology","Natural theology","Religious experience"],"thinkers":["plantinga"],"tags":["sense of the divine","calvin","john calvin","reformed epistemology","plantinga","innate knowledge of god"]},{"term":"Wahdat al-wujud","def":"The 'oneness of being': the Sufi doctrine that only God truly exists and all things are manifestations of the one divine reality.","more":"It is associated with the Andalusian mystic Ibn Arabi (1165–1240), although the phrase itself was popularised by later followers. Created things have existence only as reflections or self-disclosures of God. Critics such as Ibn Taymiyya condemned it as pantheism, and in the 17th century Ahmad Sirhindi proposed a 'oneness of witnessing' instead.","etym":"Arabic waḥdat al-wujūd, 'unity of existence'.","example":"A Sufi seeing a sunset, a beggar and their own reflection as different mirrors of the same divine light.","notConfused":"Not the same as pantheism in the Western sense, according to its defenders: God is not simply the sum of things, but the reality they manifest.","field":"Religion","related":["Sufism","Tawhid","Mysticism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["wahdat al wujud","unity of being","oneness of being","ibn arabi","ibn 'arabi","sufism","pantheism"]},{"term":"Deus sive Natura","def":"Spinoza's phrase 'God, or Nature', expressing his view that God and Nature are one and the same infinite substance.","more":"Spinoza used it in the Ethics (published 1677). He held that there is only one substance, which can be called God or Nature, and that everything is a mode of it. This God has no personal will or purposes and does not answer prayers. Contemporaries called Spinoza an atheist; others saw him as deeply religious, a 'God-intoxicated man' in Novalis's phrase.","etym":"Latin Deus sive Natura, 'God, or Nature'.","example":"Feeling reverence toward the laws of physics and the whole universe, not toward a person who designed them.","notConfused":"Not the same as believing nature was made by God: for Spinoza, Nature is God.","field":"Religion","related":["Pantheism","Monism","Substance"],"thinkers":["spinoza"],"tags":["god or nature","spinoza","pantheism","substance","natura naturans"]},{"term":"Apologetics","def":"The reasoned defence of a religious faith against objections, and the presentation of arguments in its favour.","more":"Early Christian 'apologists' like Justin Martyr wrote defences of Christianity to Roman authorities in the 2nd century. Later examples include parts of Aquinas's Summa Contra Gentiles, Pascal's Pensées and C. S. Lewis's Mere Christianity. Islamic kalam has a similar defensive role. Critics sometimes use the word to suggest arguing for a fixed conclusion rather than following the evidence.","etym":"Greek apologia, 'a speech in defence'.","example":"A believer explaining to a sceptical friend why they think the universe's existence points to God.","notConfused":"Not the same as apologising: the word comes from the Greek for a defence, as in Plato's Apology of Socrates.","field":"Religion","related":["Natural theology","Kalam","Fideism"],"thinkers":["aquinas","pascal"],"tags":["apologist","christian apologetics","defence of faith","defense of faith","natural theology"]},{"term":"Aesthetic attitude","def":"A special way of attending to something purely for how it looks, sounds or feels, setting aside practical concerns.","more":"The idea grew from 18th-century theories of disinterested taste, especially Kant's, and was developed by Schopenhauer, Edward Bullough and Jerome Stolnitz. Defenders claim that almost anything can be appreciated aesthetically if approached with the right attitude. George Dickie's 1964 paper 'The Myth of the Aesthetic Attitude' argued there is no special attitude, only paying attention or not.","etym":null,"example":"Looking at a rusty old gate not as something that needs repair but for the patterns and colours of the rust.","notConfused":"Not the same as the aesthetic experience itself: the attitude is the way of approaching something that is supposed to make such experience possible.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Disinterestedness","Aesthetic distance","Aesthetic experience"],"thinkers":["kant","schopenhauer","dickie"],"tags":["disinterestedness","disinterested attention","aesthetic contemplation","stolnitz","aesthetic distance"]},{"term":"Aesthetic experience","def":"The distinctive kind of experience we have when appreciating beauty or art, often described as absorbing, unified and valued for its own sake.","more":"John Dewey's Art as Experience (1934) argued that aesthetic experience is a heightened, complete form of ordinary experience. Monroe Beardsley proposed marks such as focused attention, a sense of freedom and coherence. Critics doubt there is one distinctive kind of experience, pointing to the great variety of ways we engage with art and nature.","etym":"Greek aisthēsis, 'perception, sensation'.","example":"Losing all sense of time while listening to a piece of music and feeling, afterwards, that it was complete in itself.","notConfused":"Not the same as pleasure in general: eating when hungry is pleasant, but not usually an aesthetic experience.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic attitude","Beauty","Disinterestedness"],"thinkers":["dewey","beardsley"],"tags":["aesthetic appreciation","beauty","aesthetic pleasure","art appreciation","aesthetic response"]},{"term":"Aesthetic distance","def":"The psychological separation from practical concerns that lets us appreciate something aesthetically, such as a tragedy or a storm.","more":"Edward Bullough introduced 'psychical distance' in a 1912 paper, using the example of a fog at sea: frightening if you think of danger, beautiful if you set that aside. Too little distance and the work is too personal to enjoy; too much and it leaves us cold. Bullough called the ideal the 'antinomy of distance'. Dickie later criticised the idea as part of the 'myth' of the aesthetic attitude.","etym":null,"example":"A jealous husband watching Othello may be too caught up in his own life to appreciate the play.","notConfused":"Not the same as emotional coldness: good distance allows strong feeling, just not practical self-concern.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic attitude","Disinterestedness","Paradox of fiction"],"thinkers":["dickie"],"tags":["psychical distance","bullough","edward bullough","disinterestedness","aesthetic attitude"]},{"term":"Art for art's sake","def":"The slogan that art needs no moral, political or useful purpose and should be valued only for its own beauty or form.","more":"The French phrase l'art pour l'art spread in the 1830s, linked to writers such as Théophile Gautier. In England it inspired the Aesthetic movement and Oscar Wilde, who wrote that 'All art is quite useless'. It opposed views that art should teach morals or serve a cause. Critics, including Tolstoy and many Marxists, argued it cut art off from life.","etym":"French l'art pour l'art.","example":"A painter refusing to explain their abstract work's 'message', insisting it is simply meant to be looked at.","notConfused":"Not the same as formalism: art for art's sake is a slogan about art's purpose, while formalism is a theory about what makes art valuable.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aestheticism","Formalism (art)","Autonomism"],"thinkers":["wilde","tolstoy"],"tags":["l'art pour l'art","aestheticism","art for arts sake","autonomy of art","formalism","oscar wilde"]},{"term":"Kitsch","def":"Art or objects that offer cheap, ready-made emotion and prettiness, imitating the effects of art without its depth.","more":"Clement Greenberg's essay 'Avant-Garde and Kitsch' (1939) attacked kitsch as mass-produced culture that predigests art for the viewer. Milan Kundera described it in The Unbearable Lightness of Being as 'the absolute denial of shit', a refusal to admit unpleasant reality. Philosophers debate whether kitsch is morally as well as aesthetically bad, and whether enjoying it ironically changes anything.","etym":"German Kitsch, 19th-century Munich art-dealers' slang for cheap sketches.","example":"A painting of a big-eyed crying puppy designed to make you go 'aww' without making you feel or think anything new.","notConfused":"Not the same as camp: kitsch is earnest and unaware; camp is a knowing, playful appreciation, often of kitsch.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Camp","Avant-garde","Taste"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["kitschy","tacky","bad taste","sentimentality","greenberg","cheesy"]},{"term":"Picturesque","def":"An 18th-century aesthetic category for scenes that are rough, irregular and varied, between the calm beautiful and the overwhelming sublime.","more":"William Gilpin popularised it from the 1760s through guidebooks teaching travellers to see landscapes as if they were paintings. Uvedale Price and Richard Payne Knight debated its nature in the 1790s, with Price treating it as a category alongside the beautiful and the sublime. It shaped English landscape gardening and tourism.","etym":"Italian pittoresco, 'in the manner of a painter'.","example":"A crumbling, ivy-covered ruin beside a winding river, with a shepherd and sheep in the foreground.","notConfused":"Not the same as merely pretty: picturesque scenes are deliberately rough and irregular.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Sublime","Beauty","Environmental aesthetics"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["picturesque landscape","gilpin","uvedale price","sublime","beautiful","landscape aesthetics"]},{"term":"Genius","def":"In aesthetics, an exceptional natural talent for creating original art that cannot be produced simply by following rules.","more":"Kant defined genius in the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790) as 'the innate mental aptitude through which nature gives the rule to art'. A genius's works are original and exemplary, setting standards others learn from, yet the genius cannot fully explain how they made them. The Romantics celebrated the idea; later critics questioned its links to myths of solitary, often male, creators.","etym":"Latin genius, 'guardian spirit', related to gignere, 'to beget'.","example":"A composer whose music breaks the rules of their time yet later becomes the model that music students are taught to follow.","notConfused":"Not the same as high intelligence: Kant thought there could be no genius in science, since scientific discoveries can be learned step by step.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Romanticism","Kant's theory of aesthetic ideas","Free play"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["artistic genius","creativity","originality","kant","romanticism","talent"]},{"term":"Free play","def":"Kant's term for the harmonious, unforced interaction of imagination and understanding that he thought underlies judgments of beauty.","more":"In the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), Kant argued that when we find something beautiful, our imagination and understanding work together without being fixed by any concept. This free play is pleasurable, and because all humans share these faculties, we can expect others to agree. Scholars still debate exactly what the free play is.","etym":"German freies Spiel.","example":"Enjoying the shifting patterns of flames in a fireplace without needing to classify or understand them.","notConfused":"Not the same as Schiller's 'play drive', though Schiller developed his idea from Kant's.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Kantian theory of taste","Purposiveness without purpose","Disinterestedness"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["free play of the faculties","imagination and understanding","kant","judgement of taste","beauty"]},{"term":"Purposiveness without purpose","def":"Kant's description of beautiful things as seeming designed or ordered, yet without our attributing any actual purpose to them.","more":"In the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), Kant said the form of a beautiful object fits our faculties as if made for them, yet we judge it without any concept of what it is for. A flower appears perfectly arranged, but to find it beautiful we need not think of its role in reproduction. The idea also connects to Kant's treatment of living organisms.","etym":"German Zweckmäßigkeit ohne Zweck.","example":"A seashell's spiral looks as if someone designed it to please the eye, though we know no one did.","notConfused":"Not the same as functional beauty: functional beauty depends on knowing what a thing is for; Kant's free beauty does not.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Free play","Kantian theory of taste","Teleology"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["finality without end","purposiveness","kant","critique of judgment","beauty","formal purposiveness"]},{"term":"Aura","def":"Walter Benjamin's term for the unique presence and authority of an original artwork in its particular time and place.","more":"In 'The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction' (1935–36), Benjamin argued that photography and film remove art's aura by making endless copies. He saw this loss as liberating: art could be freed from ritual and tradition and used for politics. The essay remains central to discussions of originality, mass culture and digital media.","etym":"Greek aura, 'breeze, breath'.","example":"Seeing the Mona Lisa in person feels different from seeing it on a poster, even though the poster shows the image more clearly.","notConfused":"Not the same as a mystical energy field: Benjamin meant a social and historical quality of uniqueness.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Culture industry critique","Problem of forgery","Avant-garde"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["walter benjamin","benjamin","mechanical reproduction","age of mechanical reproduction","authenticity","original artwork"]},{"term":"Avant-garde","def":"Artists and movements that deliberately break with established forms and tastes, pushing art in new directions.","more":"The military term ('vanguard') was applied to art and politics in 19th-century France. Movements such as Cubism, Futurism, Dada and Surrealism in the early 20th century are classic examples. Clement Greenberg contrasted avant-garde art with kitsch, and Peter Bürger argued that the historical avant-garde aimed to reunite art with life. Critics ask whether the avant-garde can survive once its shocks are absorbed by museums and markets.","etym":"French avant-garde, 'advance guard'.","example":"Composers in the 1950s using silence or random chance to make music, upsetting audiences used to melody.","notConfused":"Not the same as simply new or fashionable: avant-garde art challenges the very rules of what art should be.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Readymade","Kitsch","Artworld"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["avant garde","vanguard","modernism","experimental art","innovation"]},{"term":"Readymade","def":"An ordinary manufactured object presented as an artwork with little or no alteration, a practice begun by Marcel Duchamp.","more":"Duchamp's Fountain (1917), a urinal signed 'R. Mutt', is the most famous. Readymades challenged the idea that art requires skill or beauty and shifted attention to the artist's choice and the art context. Philosophers such as Arthur Danto and George Dickie built theories of art partly to explain why such objects can be artworks.","etym":"English ready-made, Duchamp's own term.","example":"An artist exhibiting an unmodified snow shovel in a gallery under the title 'In Advance of the Broken Arm' (another Duchamp work).","notConfused":"Not the same as found-object art in general: in a readymade, the object is presented essentially unchanged, and the gesture of choosing is the point.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Institutional theory of art","Artworld","Avant-garde"],"thinkers":["danto","dickie"],"tags":["ready-made","found object","objet trouvé","duchamp","marcel duchamp","fountain"]},{"term":"Problem of forgery","def":"The puzzle of why a perfect forgery seems less valuable than the original if the two look exactly the same.","more":"Nelson Goodman argued in Languages of Art (1968) that knowing a painting is a forgery can change how we look at it, so the difference matters aesthetically. Alfred Lessing argued that forgery is a moral or historical offence rather than an aesthetic one. Denis Dutton stressed that art is a performance and that a forgery misrepresents the achievement. The case of Han van Meegeren's fake Vermeers is often discussed.","etym":null,"example":"A painting hailed as a masterpiece loses most of its price overnight when revealed as a modern fake, though nothing on the canvas has changed.","notConfused":"Not the same as reproduction or copying: forgeries deceive about their origin, whereas an openly labelled copy does not.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aura","Achievement view of art","Authenticity"],"thinkers":["goodman","dutton"],"tags":["forgery","forgeries","fake art","authenticity","art fraud","van meegeren"]},{"term":"Death of the author","def":"Roland Barthes's claim that a text's meaning should not be tied to its author's intentions or biography, but is made by its readers.","more":"Barthes's 1967 essay 'The Death of the Author' argued that a text is 'a tissue of quotations' drawn from many sources, and that 'the birth of the reader must be at the cost of the death of the Author'. It challenged traditional criticism that explained works through the writer's life. Michel Foucault's 'What Is an Author?' (1969) took up related questions.","etym":null,"example":"Reading a children's book as a story about grief, even if the author says they meant nothing of the kind.","notConfused":"Not the same as the intentional fallacy, though similar: Wimsatt and Beardsley still sought a correct reading in the text, while Barthes celebrated many readings.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Intentional fallacy","Reader-response theory","Post-structuralism"],"thinkers":["barthes"],"tags":["barthes","roland barthes","authorial intent","intentional fallacy","reader response"]},{"term":"Fusion of horizons","def":"Hans-Georg Gadamer's description of understanding as a meeting between the reader's perspective and the perspective of a text or another culture.","more":"In Truth and Method (1960), Gadamer argued that we never read from nowhere: our 'prejudices' (pre-judgments) shaped by history make understanding possible. Real understanding happens when our horizon and the text's horizon merge into a wider view. Jürgen Habermas criticised Gadamer for not leaving enough room to criticise tradition.","etym":"German Horizontverschmelzung.","example":"A modern reader of an ancient Greek play coming to see both the play and their own assumptions in a new light.","notConfused":"Not the same as simply agreeing with the text: a fusion of horizons can include seeing where the text is wrong.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Hermeneutic circle","Hermeneutics","Interpretivism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["horizon","gadamer","hans-georg gadamer","hermeneutics","truth and method","understanding"]},{"term":"Uncanny","def":"The unsettling feeling when something familiar seems strangely unfamiliar, or something lifeless seems alive.","more":"The psychiatrist Ernst Jentsch wrote on it in 1906, and Sigmund Freud's essay 'The Uncanny' (1919) argued it arises when repressed or surmounted ideas return. Freud discussed doubles, automata and repetition, drawing on E. T. A. Hoffmann's story 'The Sandman'. The concept is used in aesthetics, literary theory and, through the 'uncanny valley', in robotics.","etym":"German unheimlich, literally 'un-homely'.","example":"The eerie feeling of looking at a lifelike wax figure or a humanoid robot that is almost, but not quite, human.","notConfused":"Not the same as horror or fear in general: the uncanny is specifically the strange-within-the-familiar.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Sublime","Tragedy","Paradox of fiction"],"thinkers":["freud"],"tags":["unheimlich","the uncanny","freud","uncanny valley","eerie","strange"]},{"term":"Apollonian and Dionysian","def":"Nietzsche's contrast between two artistic forces: Apollonian order, form and clarity, and Dionysian frenzy, intoxication and loss of self.","more":"Nietzsche set out the contrast in The Birth of Tragedy (1872), naming the forces after the Greek gods Apollo and Dionysus. He argued that Greek tragedy united them, and that Socratic rationalism later killed tragedy by favouring the Apollonian. The pair has since been widely used to describe tensions in art and culture.","etym":"From the Greek gods Apollo and Dionysus.","example":"A classical sculpture's calm proportion is Apollonian; the ecstatic crowd at a rave is Dionysian.","notConfused":"Not a simple contrast between good and bad, or reason and emotion: Nietzsche thought great art needs both.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Tragedy","Catharsis","Friedrich Nietzsche"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"tags":["apollonian","dionysian","apollo and dionysus","nietzsche","birth of tragedy","tragedy"]},{"term":"Yūgen","def":"A Japanese aesthetic ideal of profound, mysterious grace: beauty felt as a deep, subtle suggestion rather than shown outright.","more":"The term, borrowed from Chinese Buddhist texts, became central in medieval Japanese poetics and in the Noh theatre. The playwright Zeami (c. 1363–1443) made yūgen the highest aim of Noh performance. It is linked to Buddhist awareness of impermanence and to a preference for what is hinted at over what is fully displayed.","etym":"Japanese, from Chinese youxuan, 'dark, deep, mysterious'.","example":"Watching geese disappear into a misty sky at dusk, feeling a depth of meaning you cannot put into words.","notConfused":"Not the same as mono no aware: mono no aware is a poignant sense of passing things; yūgen emphasises mystery and depth.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Mono no aware","Wabi-sabi","Ineffable"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["yugen","japanese aesthetics","mysterious beauty","mono no aware","wabi-sabi","subtlety"]},{"term":"Camp","def":"A playful sensibility that delights in artifice, exaggeration and style, often enjoying things as 'so bad they're good'.","more":"Susan Sontag's essay 'Notes on “Camp”' (1964) brought the term into criticism, describing camp as 'love of the unnatural: of artifice and exaggeration' and noting its links to gay culture. Camp turns away from seriousness and judges by style rather than content. Critics debate whether it is politically subversive or merely ironic.","etym":"Uncertain; possibly from French se camper, 'to strike a pose'.","example":"Fans gleefully watching a melodramatic old film with ridiculous costumes and overacting, loving it for exactly those flaws.","notConfused":"Not the same as kitsch: kitsch is earnest bad taste; camp is a knowing appreciation that may find delight in it.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Kitsch","Aestheticism","Taste"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["camp aesthetic","susan sontag","sontag","notes on camp","kitsch","artifice"]},{"term":"Male gaze","def":"Film theorist Laura Mulvey's term for the way visual art and film often present women as objects for a presumed heterosexual male viewer.","more":"Mulvey introduced it in her 1975 essay 'Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema', drawing on psychoanalysis. She argued that classic Hollywood cameras dwell on women's bodies while male characters drive the story. John Berger's Ways of Seeing (1972) had made a related point: 'men act and women appear'. The idea has been widely applied and also criticised for assuming a single kind of viewer.","etym":null,"example":"An action film that introduces its female lead with a slow camera pan up her body, while the male lead is introduced by what he does.","notConfused":"Not a claim about individual men's eyes: it describes a viewpoint built into how images are made.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Feminist philosophy","Ideology","Mimesis"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["mulvey","laura mulvey","objectification","scopophilia","feminist film theory","gaze"]},{"term":"Everyday aesthetics","def":"The study of aesthetic qualities in ordinary life, such as cooking, cleaning, clothing, weather and household objects.","more":"The field grew in the 2000s, with books such as Yuriko Saito's Everyday Aesthetics (2007), and draws on John Dewey's view of art as continuous with experience. It challenges the focus of traditional aesthetics on fine art and nature. Debates concern whether everyday experiences are truly aesthetic and whether familiarity dulls them.","etym":null,"example":"The satisfaction of a freshly made bed, or the pleasure of a well-arranged lunchbox.","notConfused":"Not the same as design theory: everyday aesthetics includes experiences, like the smell of rain, that nobody designed.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic experience","Environmental aesthetics","Wabi-sabi"],"thinkers":["dewey"],"tags":["aesthetics of everyday life","everyday life","ordinary aesthetics","yuriko saito","daily life"]},{"term":"Environmental aesthetics","def":"The study of the aesthetic appreciation of natural and human-shaped environments, from wilderness to cities.","more":"Ronald Hepburn's 1966 essay on the neglect of natural beauty helped launch the field, and Allen Carlson argued that appreciating nature properly requires scientific knowledge of it. Others stress engagement, emotion or imagination instead. Questions include whether all untouched nature is beautiful, a view called positive aesthetics.","etym":null,"example":"Appreciating a marsh more once you learn how its plants and birds depend on each other.","notConfused":"Not the same as environmental ethics: one asks what is beautiful in nature, the other what we owe it, though the two often connect.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Positive aesthetics","Picturesque","Sublime"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["aesthetics of nature","natural beauty","landscape","nature appreciation","allen carlson"]},{"term":"Pathetic fallacy","def":"Attributing human feelings to nature or objects, such as describing the sea as 'cruel' or the sky as 'weeping'.","more":"John Ruskin coined the phrase in Modern Painters, volume 3 (1856), criticising poets whose strong emotion led them to see their own feelings in nature. He thought great poets used it sparingly. Today the term is often used neutrally for a common literary device.","etym":"Greek pathos, 'feeling, suffering', plus fallacy.","example":"A film in which the sky starts to rain just as the hero's heart is broken.","notConfused":"Not a logical fallacy: it is a figure of speech. Nor is it exactly personification, which can give objects any human trait, not only feelings.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Expression theory of art","Mimesis","Romanticism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["personification","anthropomorphism","ruskin","john ruskin","projection"]},{"term":"Negative capability","def":"The poet John Keats's term for the ability to stay with uncertainty, mystery and doubt without rushing to resolve them.","more":"Keats used it in a letter of December 1817, praising the capacity of 'being in uncertainties, Mysteries, doubts, without any irritable reaching after fact & reason', which he found in Shakespeare. The idea has been taken up in literary criticism, psychoanalysis and leadership studies. It contrasts with the philosopher's drive to settle every question.","etym":null,"example":"A writer who lets a character remain morally ambiguous instead of forcing the story to say whether they were good or bad.","notConfused":"Not the same as scepticism: Keats meant a creative openness, not a philosophical doubt about knowledge.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Romanticism","Aporia","Genius"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["keats","john keats","uncertainty","tolerance of ambiguity","doubt"]},{"term":"Defamiliarization","def":"The artistic technique of making familiar things seem strange so that we perceive them freshly.","more":"The Russian critic Viktor Shklovsky introduced the term (ostranenie) in his 1917 essay 'Art as Device'. He argued that habit makes us stop really seeing things, and that art exists 'to make the stone stony'. He cited Tolstoy, who described events like an opera through the eyes of someone who did not know the conventions. Brecht's 'alienation effect' in theatre is a related idea.","etym":"Russian ostranenie, 'making strange'.","example":"A poem describing a staircase as a 'stack of wooden waves' makes you notice stairs you climb every day without thinking.","notConfused":"Not the same as alienation in Marx's sense: it is a deliberate artistic effect, not a social condition.","field":"Beauty and art","related":["Formalism (art)","Avant-garde","Mimesis"],"thinkers":["tolstoy"],"tags":["ostranenie","estrangement","making strange","shklovsky","viktor shklovsky","russian formalism"]},{"term":"Avidya","def":"Ignorance: in Indian philosophy, the basic misunderstanding of reality that keeps beings trapped in suffering and rebirth.","more":"In Buddhism, avidya is the first link in the chain of dependent origination, especially ignorance of the Four Noble Truths and of no-self. In Advaita Vedanta, Shankara used it for the error of taking the self to be separate from Brahman. Both traditions hold that liberation comes through knowledge that removes it, not merely through good deeds.","etym":"Sanskrit a-, 'not', and vidyā, 'knowledge'.","example":"Mistaking a coiled rope for a snake in dim light, a classic Vedanta image for how ignorance projects a false world.","notConfused":"Not the same as lack of information: avidya is a deep misperception of what one is and what the world is.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dependent origination","Maya","Moksha"],"thinkers":["buddha","shankara"],"tags":["avijja","avidyā","ignorance","delusion","maya","illusion"]},{"term":"Tanha","def":"Craving or thirst: the Buddhist term for the grasping desire that the Buddha identified as the cause of suffering.","more":"The second of the Four Noble Truths states that dukkha arises from tanha. The Buddha distinguished craving for sensual pleasure, craving for existence and craving for non-existence. The third Noble Truth says suffering ends when craving ends, which is nirvana. Buddhists distinguish tanha from wholesome wishes such as the wish to become enlightened.","etym":"Pali taṇhā, Sanskrit tṛṣṇā, 'thirst'.","example":"Finally getting the new phone you wanted and, within a week, already wanting the next model.","notConfused":"Not the same as all desire: wanting to help others or to practise the path is not usually classed as tanha.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dukkha","Four Noble Truths","Buddhist non-attachment"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["tanhā","trishna","craving","thirst","desire","attachment"]},{"term":"Bhakti","def":"Loving devotion to a personal God, which Hindu traditions treat as a path to liberation.","more":"The Bhagavad Gita presents bhakti yoga alongside the paths of knowledge and action, and Krishna promises that those who devote themselves to him will reach him. Medieval bhakti movements, with poet-saints such as Mirabai and Kabir, stressed personal love of God over caste and ritual. Ramanuja and Madhva gave bhakti a central philosophical role, against Shankara's emphasis on knowledge.","etym":"Sanskrit bhakti, 'devotion, participation', from bhaj, 'to share, worship'.","example":"A devotee singing hymns to Krishna every morning, seeking not rewards but closeness to God.","notConfused":"Not the same as ritual worship: bhakti is an inner attitude of love, which may or may not involve ritual.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Moksha","Nishkama karma","Vishishtadvaita"],"thinkers":["ramanuja","madhva"],"tags":["devotion","bhakti yoga","devotionalism","love of god","krishna"]},{"term":"Purusha and prakriti","def":"In Samkhya philosophy, the two basic realities: purusha, pure consciousness, and prakriti, matter or nature, including the mind.","more":"Samkhya, systematised in Ishvarakrishna's Samkhyakarika (around the 4th–5th century AD), holds that there are many purushas, which only witness, while prakriti evolves into everything else, including thought and ego. Suffering comes from confusing purusha with prakriti; liberation (kaivalya) is their discrimination. The Yoga school of Patanjali adopted this framework.","etym":"Sanskrit puruṣa, 'person, spirit'; prakṛti, 'nature, primary matter'.","example":"Watching your own anger and realising that you, the witness, are not the anger itself, a move Samkhya would describe in these terms.","notConfused":"Not the same as Descartes's mind–body dualism: for Samkhya, thinking belongs to matter, and only pure awareness belongs to purusha.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Samkhya","Gunas","Yoga"],"thinkers":["patanjali"],"tags":["purusha","prakriti","puruṣa","prakṛti","samkhya","consciousness and matter"]},{"term":"Gunas","def":"The three qualities that make up nature in Samkhya and later Hindu thought: sattva (clarity), rajas (activity) and tamas (inertia).","more":"Samkhya holds that everything in prakriti is a mix of these three strands, and change arises from their shifting balance. The Bhagavad Gita applies them to food, faith, action and character. The goal is often described as cultivating sattva and ultimately going beyond all three gunas.","etym":"Sanskrit guṇa, 'strand, quality'.","example":"Feeling calm and clear after a morning walk (sattva), restless and driven before a deadline (rajas), and heavy and sluggish after a huge meal (tamas).","notConfused":"Not the same as moral virtues: the gunas are features of all matter, including the mind, not choices of character.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Purusha and prakriti","Samkhya","Yoga"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["guna","sattva","rajas","tamas","three gunas","three qualities"]},{"term":"Lila","def":"Divine play: the Hindu idea that God creates and sustains the world not out of need but spontaneously, as play.","more":"The Brahma Sutras say creation is 'mere sport (lila), as in ordinary life', answering the objection that a complete God would have no reason to create. Vaishnava traditions celebrate Krishna's playful deeds as lila. The idea raises the problem of evil in a new form: how can a world with so much suffering be play?","etym":"Sanskrit līlā, 'play, sport'.","example":"A child building sandcastles purely for joy, not to achieve anything, is an image used for lila.","notConfused":"Not the same as maya: maya concerns the illusory or veiling power of the world, lila the spontaneous freedom of creation.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Maya","Brahman","Bhakti"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["leela","līlā","divine play","cosmic play","play"]},{"term":"Rta","def":"The cosmic order in the Vedic tradition, governing the regular course of nature, ritual and right conduct.","more":"In the Rigveda, rta is the order behind the dawn, the seasons and the rivers, upheld by gods like Varuna. Truth and proper sacrifice keep it intact. Over time the concept was largely replaced by dharma, which carried its sense of rightful order into ethics and law.","etym":"Sanskrit ṛta, 'what has been properly joined, order, truth'.","example":"The sun rising every morning and the seasons turning in order were, for Vedic poets, signs of rta.","notConfused":"Not the same as karma: rta is the overall cosmic order, karma the law of action and its consequences within it.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Dharma","Logos","Dao"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["rita","ṛta","cosmic order","vedic order","dharma","natural order"]},{"term":"Pramana","def":"A valid means of knowledge in Indian philosophy, such as perception, inference or reliable testimony.","more":"Indian schools debated how many pramanas there are: the Carvakas accepted only perception, Buddhists such as Dignaga and Dharmakirti accepted perception and inference, and the Nyaya school added comparison and testimony. Mimamsa and Vedanta added further ones. Theories of pramana are the core of Indian epistemology.","etym":"Sanskrit pramāṇa, 'measure, means of correct knowing'.","example":"You know there is a fire because you see it (perception), because you see smoke (inference), or because a trusted friend tells you (testimony).","notConfused":"Not the same as a belief or piece of knowledge: a pramana is the source or method that produces knowledge.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Nyaya","Inference","Perception"],"thinkers":["akshapada","dharmakirti"],"tags":["pramāṇa","pramanas","means of knowledge","sources of knowledge","nyaya","indian epistemology"]},{"term":"Two truths doctrine","def":"The Buddhist teaching that there are two levels of truth: conventional truth about everyday things, and ultimate truth about how things really are.","more":"Nagarjuna made it central to Madhyamaka: conventionally there are persons, chairs and causes; ultimately all things are empty of independent essence. He insisted that the ultimate cannot be taught without relying on the conventional. Different Buddhist schools understand the relation between the two levels in different ways.","etym":"Sanskrit saṃvṛti-satya, 'conventional truth', and paramārtha-satya, 'ultimate truth'.","example":"Saying 'I'm hungry' is conventionally true, though ultimately there is no fixed, independent 'I' that is hungry.","notConfused":"Not the same as saying everyday statements are lies: conventional truths are genuinely true at their level.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Madhyamaka","Emptiness (śūnyatā)","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["nagarjuna"],"tags":["two truths","conventional truth","ultimate truth","nagarjuna","madhyamaka","samvrti and paramartha"]},{"term":"Upaya","def":"Skilful means: the Buddhist idea that teachings are adapted to the needs and abilities of the people hearing them.","more":"The concept is central to Mahayana texts such as the Lotus Sutra, which tells of a father who lures his children from a burning house by promising them toy carts. It explains why the Buddha seems to say different things to different people. Critics worry that it could justify deception; defenders reply that its aim is always the listener's liberation.","etym":"Sanskrit upāya, 'means, approach'.","example":"A teacher explaining a hard idea to a child with a simple story, knowing a fuller account will come later.","notConfused":"Not the same as relativism: upaya assumes there is a real goal, while the routes to it differ.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Mahayana","Bodhisattva","Two truths doctrine"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["upāya","skillful means","skilful means","expedient means","lotus sutra","mahayana"]},{"term":"Buddha-nature","def":"The Mahayana teaching that all beings have the potential, or the inner nature, to become Buddhas.","more":"It developed from Indian ideas of the tathagatagarbha, the 'womb' or 'embryo' of the Buddha, found in texts such as the Mahaparinirvana Sutra. It became central to East Asian Buddhism, including Chan and Zen; Dōgen reinterpreted it to say that all existence is Buddha-nature. Critics within Buddhism asked whether it smuggles back a permanent self.","etym":"Sanskrit buddha-dhātu or tathāgatagarbha.","example":"A Zen teacher telling a struggling student that enlightenment is not something to acquire but their own true nature to uncover.","notConfused":"Not the same as an atman or permanent soul, according to most Buddhist interpreters, though the comparison is debated.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Mahayana","Zen Buddhism","Mu"],"thinkers":["dogen"],"tags":["buddha nature","tathagatagarbha","tathāgatagarbha","bussho","original enlightenment","mahayana"]},{"term":"Karuna","def":"Compassion: the Buddhist wish that all beings be free from suffering, considered essential to the path.","more":"Karuna is one of the four brahmaviharas and, in Mahayana Buddhism, paired with wisdom (prajna) as the two wings of enlightenment. The bodhisattva vows to save all beings out of compassion. Buddhist writers such as Shantideva argued that once one sees there is no fixed self, there is no reason to prefer one's own suffering over anyone else's.","etym":"Sanskrit karuṇā, 'compassion'.","example":"A meditation in which you picture someone who is suffering and wish sincerely for them to be free of it.","notConfused":"Not the same as pity, which can look down on others: karuna sees suffering as shared and seeks to relieve it.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Brahmaviharas","Bodhisattva","Prajna"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["karuṇā","compassion","mahakaruna","brahmaviharas","metta"]},{"term":"Brahmaviharas","def":"The four 'divine abodes' of Buddhism: loving-kindness, compassion, sympathetic joy and equanimity, cultivated toward all beings.","more":"The four are metta (loving-kindness), karuna (compassion), mudita (joy at others' happiness) and upekkha (equanimity). They are also called the four immeasurables because they are to be extended without limit. Buddhaghosa's Visuddhimagga (5th century) gives detailed meditation methods for them. They appear in Hindu yoga texts too.","etym":"Sanskrit and Pali brahma, 'divine, sublime', and vihāra, 'dwelling'.","example":"Feeling genuinely glad when a rival wins a prize, rather than envious, practises mudita.","notConfused":"Not the same as feelings that simply happen to us: they are attitudes deliberately trained through practice.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Karuna","Buddhism","Buddhist non-attachment"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["brahmavihara","four immeasurables","divine abodes","metta","loving-kindness","equanimity"]},{"term":"Anicca","def":"Impermanence: the Buddhist teaching that all conditioned things are constantly changing and none lasts.","more":"Anicca is one of the three marks of existence, along with dukkha and anatta. The Buddha taught that clinging to impermanent things leads to suffering, and that seeing their impermanence clearly loosens attachment. Later Buddhist philosophers developed doctrines of momentariness, holding that things exist only for an instant.","etym":"Pali anicca, Sanskrit anitya, 'not permanent'.","example":"Watching a flower bloom, wilt and fall over a single week.","notConfused":"Not the same as Heraclitus's flux, though often compared: anicca is tied to the practical goal of ending suffering.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Three marks of existence","Dukkha","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["anitya","impermanence","transience","three marks of existence","change"]},{"term":"Three marks of existence","def":"The Buddhist teaching that all conditioned things are impermanent (anicca), unsatisfactory (dukkha) and without a fixed self (anatta).","more":"The Buddha taught that seeing these three features clearly is the key insight that leads to liberation. In Pali texts the argument often runs: what is impermanent is unsatisfactory, and what is impermanent and unsatisfactory cannot be one's self. Some lists add nirvana as a fourth seal, since it alone is not conditioned.","etym":"Pali ti-lakkhaṇa, 'three characteristics'.","example":"Reflecting that your favourite mug will one day break, that owning it will never fully satisfy you, and that it is not part of who you are.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Anicca","Dukkha","Anatta (no-self)"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["three marks","tilakkhana","anicca","dukkha","anatta","three characteristics"]},{"term":"Arhat","def":"In early and Theravada Buddhism, a person who has reached nirvana and will not be reborn.","more":"The arhat has eliminated craving and ignorance and is considered a fully liberated disciple of the Buddha. Mahayana Buddhists criticised the arhat ideal as aiming only at personal liberation, holding up the bodhisattva, who delays final nirvana to help all beings, as a higher goal. Theravadins reject this characterisation.","etym":"Sanskrit arhat, Pali arahant, 'worthy one'.","example":"A monk who, after years of meditation, is said to have no remaining craving and to be at peace whatever happens.","notConfused":"Not the same as a bodhisattva: the arhat has finished the path; the bodhisattva vows to keep working for others' liberation.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Bodhisattva","Theravada","Nirvana"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["arahant","arhant","arahat","enlightened one","nirvana","theravada"]},{"term":"Three Jewels","def":"The three things in which Buddhists take refuge: the Buddha, the Dharma (his teaching) and the Sangha (the community).","more":"Reciting 'I take refuge in the Buddha, the Dharma and the Sangha' is the basic act of becoming a Buddhist in nearly all traditions. The Buddha is the teacher and example, the Dharma the truth and path, and the Sangha the community of practitioners, especially the monastic order. Some traditions also treat them as qualities within one's own mind.","etym":"Sanskrit triratna, 'three jewels'.","example":"A newcomer at a meditation centre reciting the refuge formula at a ceremony marking their commitment.","notConfused":null,"field":"Asian traditions","related":["Buddhism","Dharma","Eightfold Path"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["triple gem","three refuges","triratna","buddha dharma sangha","refuge"]},{"term":"Samadhi","def":"A state of deep, unified concentration in meditation, important in both Hindu yoga and Buddhism.","more":"In Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, samadhi is the eighth and final limb of yoga, where the mind becomes completely absorbed in its object. In Buddhism, right concentration (samma samadhi) is the eighth factor of the Eightfold Path. Both traditions distinguish many levels and debate how samadhi relates to liberating insight.","etym":"Sanskrit samādhi, 'putting together, concentration'.","example":"A musician so absorbed in playing that all sense of self and surroundings drops away gives a faint everyday echo of samadhi.","notConfused":"Not the same as liberation itself in Buddhism: concentration supports insight but is not enlightenment by itself.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Yoga","Eightfold Path","Zazen"],"thinkers":["patanjali"],"tags":["samādhi","concentration","meditative absorption","jhana","dhyana","meditation"]},{"term":"Nishkama karma","def":"Desireless action: acting from duty without attachment to the fruits or results, as taught in the Bhagavad Gita.","more":"In the Gita, Krishna tells the warrior Arjuna, 'You have a right to your actions, but never to their fruits.' Acting this way lets one fulfil one's dharma without generating binding karma. Gandhi took the Gita as his guide and saw selfless action as central to his ethics. Critics ask whether one can act purposefully while truly not caring about outcomes.","etym":"Sanskrit niṣkāma, 'without desire', and karma, 'action'.","example":"A doctor doing her best for every patient, without letting pride in success or fear of failure drive her.","notConfused":"Not the same as not caring about anything: one still acts carefully, but without self-interested attachment to the result.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Karma","Dharma","Bhakti"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["niskama karma","desireless action","karma yoga","bhagavad gita","detachment","selfless action"]},{"term":"Syadvada","def":"The Jain doctrine of 'maybe': every statement about reality is true only from a certain standpoint and should be qualified.","more":"Syadvada belongs with anekantavada, the Jain view that reality has many aspects. Jain logicians developed a sevenfold scheme (saptabhangi): something 'in some respect is', 'in some respect is not', 'in some respect is and is not', 'in some respect is inexpressible', and three combinations. It aims to capture partial truths and promote intellectual humility.","etym":"Sanskrit syāt, 'perhaps, in some respect', and vāda, 'doctrine'.","example":"A glass of water is 'in some respect hot' compared with ice, and 'in some respect cold' compared with tea.","notConfused":"Not the same as relativism or scepticism: Jains held that each qualified statement can be fully true from its standpoint.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Anekantavada","Jainism","Perspectivism"],"thinkers":["mahavira","umasvati"],"tags":["syādvāda","anekantavada","anekāntavāda","jainism","perspectivism","sevenfold predication"]},{"term":"Jiva","def":"In Jainism and Hindu thought, the living soul: the conscious, individual life-principle in each being.","more":"Jainism holds that countless jivas exist, not only in humans and animals but in plants, water and earth, and that karma clings to them as fine matter. Liberation means freeing the jiva from all karma so it rises to the top of the universe. In Vedanta, jiva is the individual self, whose relation to Brahman divides schools such as Advaita and Dvaita.","etym":"Sanskrit jīva, 'living, life'.","example":"A Jain monk sweeping the path before walking to avoid harming tiny creatures, each believed to have a jiva.","notConfused":"Not the same as atman in Advaita: the jiva is the individual embodied self; Advaitins hold its true nature is the universal atman.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Jainism","Atman","Ahimsa"],"thinkers":["mahavira","umasvati"],"tags":["jīva","jiva soul","soul","atman","jainism","living being"]},{"term":"Zhong and shu","def":"Loyalty or doing one's best (zhong) and reciprocity or sympathetic understanding (shu), which Confucius's disciple called the single thread of his teaching.","more":"In Analects 4.15, Confucius says his way has one thread running through it, and his disciple Zengzi explains it as zhong and shu. Shu is defined elsewhere (15.24) as 'Do not impose on others what you yourself do not desire'. Scholars debate how the two relate, with some reading zhong as commitment to one's role and shu as imaginative extension to others.","etym":"Chinese 忠 zhōng, 'loyalty, wholeheartedness'; 恕 shù, 'reciprocity, consideration'.","example":"Before criticising a colleague's mistake in public, imagining how you would feel if they did the same to you.","notConfused":"Not identical to the golden rule: Confucius's formulation is negative, and it works within relationships and roles.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Golden rule","Ren","Confucian ethics"],"thinkers":["confucius"],"tags":["zhong","shu","loyalty and reciprocity","confucius","golden rule","analects"]},{"term":"Liangzhi","def":"Innate moral knowing: Wang Yangming's term for the inborn awareness of good and evil that every person has.","more":"Wang took the term from Mencius, who spoke of knowing the good without having to learn it. For Wang, extending liangzhi is the core of moral cultivation, rather than studying the principles of things one by one as Zhu Xi had taught. He held that everyone, even a thief, has this knowing, though selfish desires can obscure it.","etym":"Chinese 良知 liángzhī, 'good knowing'.","example":"The immediate unease a child feels when about to take something that is not theirs.","notConfused":"Not the same as Western intuitionism in ethics: liangzhi is inseparable from action and feeling, not a faculty for grasping moral truths.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Unity of knowledge and action","Four sprouts","Neo-Confucianism"],"thinkers":["wangyangming","mencius"],"tags":["liang zhi","innate knowledge","wang yangming","moral intuition","good knowing","conscience"]},{"term":"Unity of knowledge and action","def":"Wang Yangming's doctrine that true knowledge and action are one: if you do not act on what you know, you do not really know it.","more":"Wang (1472–1529) taught that knowing filial piety means already being moved to act filially, just as seeing a beautiful colour already involves liking it. He opposed Zhu Xi's school, which he thought separated study from practice. The doctrine was influential in China and in Japan, where Wang's school was known as Yōmeigaku.","etym":"Chinese 知行合一 zhī xíng hé yī.","example":"Someone who says they know smoking is harmful but keeps smoking would, for Wang, not truly know it yet.","notConfused":"Not the same as Socrates' claim that no one does wrong knowingly, though often compared: Wang stresses that knowing itself involves feeling and willing.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Liangzhi","Akrasia","Neo-Confucianism"],"thinkers":["wangyangming"],"tags":["zhixing heyi","knowledge and action","wang yangming","unity of knowing and acting","neo-confucianism"]},{"term":"Four sprouts","def":"Mencius's four natural moral beginnings in every human: compassion, shame, deference and the sense of right and wrong.","more":"In Mencius 2A6, he argues that anyone who suddenly saw a child about to fall into a well would feel alarm and compassion, proving that humans have the sprouts of goodness. With cultivation they grow into the virtues of benevolence (ren), righteousness (yi), propriety (li) and wisdom (zhi). Xunzi rejected the view, holding that human nature is bad and must be reshaped by ritual.","etym":"Chinese 四端 sì duān, 'four beginnings'.","example":"Feeling an instant jolt of alarm when you see a toddler wandering toward a busy road.","notConfused":"Not the claim that people are already good: the sprouts must be nurtured, or they wither.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Mencian Confucianism","Ren","Xunzian Confucianism"],"thinkers":["mencius","xunzi"],"tags":["four beginnings","mencius","mengzi","human nature is good","ren","moral sprouts"]},{"term":"Jian ai","def":"Impartial care, or 'universal love': the Mohist teaching that we should care for all people equally, not favour our own.","more":"Mozi (5th century BC) argued that the world's disorder comes from partiality: people care for their own families and states at others' expense. If everyone cared for others' families as their own, conflict would cease, and he argued this would benefit everyone. Mencius attacked jian ai as denying one's father, and the debate prefigures modern disputes over impartialism.","etym":"Chinese 兼愛 jiān ài, 'inclusive care'.","example":"Deciding to give the same weight to a stranger's child's education as to your own nephew's.","notConfused":"Not the same as Christian agape: Mozi argued for impartial care largely on the grounds of its beneficial consequences.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Mohism","Impartialism","Filial piety"],"thinkers":["mozi","mencius"],"tags":["jianai","universal love","impartial care","mozi","mohism","universal care"]},{"term":"Ziran","def":"Naturalness or spontaneity: the Daoist ideal of things being 'so of themselves', without force or artificial interference.","more":"The Daodejing says that the Dao models itself on ziran (chapter 25), and the sage lets things follow their own course. The Zhuangzi celebrates the natural spontaneity of animals and skilled craftsmen. Later Neo-Daoist (Xuanxue) thinkers such as Guo Xiang developed the idea that each thing arises spontaneously on its own.","etym":"Chinese 自然 zìrán, 'self-so'.","example":"A tree growing in its own shape on a hillside, compared with a hedge clipped into a box.","notConfused":"Not the same as 'nature' in the sense of the natural world, though the modern Chinese word for nature comes from it.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Wu wei","Dao","Pu"],"thinkers":["laozi","zhuangzi"],"tags":["tzu-jan","spontaneity","naturalness","daoism","taoism","wu wei"]},{"term":"Pu","def":"The 'uncarved block': the Daoist image of original simplicity, before desire and social convention shape a person.","more":"The Daodejing repeatedly uses pu for the simple, unadorned state the sage returns to. Cutting the block makes useful vessels but loses its wholeness, just as learning and ambition make people clever but divided. The image supports Daoist criticism of Confucian ritual and of a society that encourages endless desire.","etym":"Chinese 樸 pǔ, 'unworked wood'.","example":"A person who enjoys simple pleasures without needing to impress anyone, compared with someone constantly reshaping themselves to fit in.","notConfused":"Not the same as ignorance or stupidity: pu is a chosen simplicity that avoids the distortions of excessive cleverness.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Ziran","Wu wei","Daoism"],"thinkers":["laozi"],"tags":["p'u","uncarved block","simplicity","daoism","taoism","laozi"]},{"term":"Wuxing","def":"The Five Phases of Chinese thought: wood, fire, earth, metal and water, which generate and overcome one another in cycles.","more":"The theory was developed during the Warring States period, with the thinker Zou Yan (3rd century BC) associated with it, and was combined with yin–yang thought in the Han dynasty. It was used to explain seasons, medicine, politics and the rise and fall of dynasties. The 'phases' are processes rather than fixed substances.","etym":"Chinese 五行 wǔxíng, 'five movements'.","example":"Traditional Chinese medicine linking the liver to wood and spring, and the heart to fire and summer.","notConfused":"Not the same as the four elements of Greek philosophy: the wuxing are dynamic phases of change, not basic building materials.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Yin and yang","Qi","Yijing"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["wu xing","five phases","five elements","five agents","yin and yang","chinese cosmology"]},{"term":"Yijing","def":"The Book of Changes: an ancient Chinese divination text of 64 hexagrams, later a foundational classic of Chinese philosophy.","more":"Its core goes back to the early Zhou dynasty, and appended commentaries called the Ten Wings, traditionally attributed to Confucius, made it a philosophical work about change, yin and yang, and the patterns of the cosmos. Neo-Confucians such as Zhu Xi drew on it heavily. Leibniz noticed parallels between its hexagrams and binary numbers.","etym":"Chinese 易經 Yìjīng, 'Classic of Changes'; often written I Ching.","example":"Someone tossing coins six times to build a hexagram and reading its text for guidance on a decision.","notConfused":"Not merely a fortune-telling book: Chinese thinkers read it as a guide to understanding change and acting wisely.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Yin and yang","Taiji","Wuxing"],"thinkers":["confucius","zhuxi","leibniz"],"tags":["i ching","yi jing","book of changes","hexagrams","zhouyi","divination"]},{"term":"Taiji","def":"The Supreme Ultimate: in Chinese thought, the source from which yin and yang and all things arise.","more":"The term appears in the Yijing commentaries. Zhou Dunyi's Explanation of the Diagram of the Supreme Ultimate (11th century) made it central to Neo-Confucianism, and Zhu Xi identified taiji with the totality of li (principle). The familiar yin–yang symbol is often called the taiji diagram.","etym":"Chinese 太極 tàijí, 'great ridgepole' or 'supreme pole'.","example":"The swirling black-and-white circle on a martial arts studio's sign is a taiji symbol.","notConfused":"Not the same as Tai chi the exercise, though the name of the martial art (taijiquan) comes from it.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Yin and yang","Li (principle)","Neo-Confucianism"],"thinkers":["zhuxi"],"tags":["tai chi","t'ai chi","supreme ultimate","taijitu","yin and yang","neo-confucianism"]},{"term":"Hundred Schools of Thought","def":"The flourishing of rival philosophical schools in China between roughly the 6th and 3rd centuries BC.","more":"During the late Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, thinkers travelled between competing states offering advice. The main schools included Confucians, Daoists, Mohists, Legalists, the School of Names and the Yin-Yang school. The period ended with the Qin unification in 221 BC, and the Han later established Confucianism as state orthodoxy.","etym":"Chinese 諸子百家 zhūzǐ bǎijiā, 'the various masters, the hundred families'.","example":"Like the ancient Greek world of rival schools, but with the arguments aimed at rulers seeking to win wars and order society.","notConfused":"'Hundred' is a figure of speech meaning 'many': there were not literally a hundred schools.","field":"Asian traditions","related":["Confucianism","Mohism","Legalism"],"thinkers":["confucius","mozi","mencius","xunzi","zhuangzi","hanfeizi"],"tags":["hundred schools","warring states","zhuzi baijia","confucianism","mohism","legalism"]},{"term":"Apeiron","def":"The 'boundless' or 'unlimited': Anaximander's name for the endless, indefinite source from which all things arise.","more":"Anaximander of Miletus (6th century BC), a successor of Thales, argued that the basic principle could not be any one element such as water, since the elements oppose one another. Instead it must be something unlimited and indefinite. Opposites like hot and cold separate out from it and 'pay penalty' to one another for injustice, in the oldest surviving fragment of Western philosophy.","etym":"Greek apeiron, 'without limit', from a-, 'not', and peras, 'limit'.","example":"Imagining a formless, endless source out of which hot and cold, wet and dry, all separate like weather from the sky.","notConfused":"Not the same as the mathematical infinite: apeiron is an indefinite, qualityless stuff, not a quantity.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Arche","Pre-Socratics","Milesian school"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["boundless","the unlimited","indefinite","anaximander","arche","infinite"]},{"term":"Nous","def":"Mind or intellect, especially the highest, intuitive kind of understanding in Greek philosophy.","more":"Anaxagoras (5th century BC) held that Nous set the cosmos in motion and ordered it. Plato and Aristotle treated nous as the faculty that grasps first principles directly, and Aristotle called God thought thinking itself. In Plotinus, Nous is the second level of reality, emanating from the One.","etym":"Greek nous, 'mind, intellect'.","example":"Grasping, in a flash, why a geometric proof must be true, rather than just following its steps.","notConfused":"Not the same as everyday 'common sense', which is what the British slang 'nous' means.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Logos","Neoplatonism","Intuition"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle","plotinus"],"tags":["intellect","mind","reason","anaxagoras","plotinus"]},{"term":"Physis and nomos","def":"The ancient Greek contrast between nature (physis) and human convention or law (nomos).","more":"In 5th-century BC Athens, Sophists such as Antiphon argued that laws are mere conventions, often at odds with nature. In Plato's Gorgias, Callicles claims that by nature the strong should rule and that conventional justice protects the weak. Plato and Aristotle tried to show that justice and the polis are rooted in nature. The contrast lives on in debates about nature versus nurture and social construction.","etym":"Greek physis, 'nature, growth'; nomos, 'custom, law'.","example":"Asking whether wearing clothes, driving on the right or marriage are natural to humans or just customs that could be otherwise.","notConfused":"Not the same as the modern distinction between nature and culture, though related: nomos covers laws and morals as well as customs.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Sophists","Natural law","Social construction"],"thinkers":["plato","protagoras"],"tags":["physis","nomos","nature vs convention","nature and convention","sophists","natural law"]},{"term":"Elenchus","def":"Socrates' method of cross-examination: testing someone's belief by drawing out consequences that contradict it.","more":"In Plato's early dialogues, Socrates asks someone to define a virtue, such as courage or piety, then leads them through questions to see that their definition conflicts with other things they believe. The usual result is aporia, puzzlement. Scholars debate whether the elenchus can prove anything true or only expose inconsistency.","etym":"Greek elenchos, 'refutation, scrutiny'.","example":"Someone says courage is standing firm in battle; Socrates asks whether a wise retreat can be courageous, and they admit it can.","notConfused":"Not identical to the 'Socratic method' as used in classrooms today, which often means any teaching by questions.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Socratic method","Aporia","Reductio ad absurdum"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato"],"tags":["elenchos","socratic method","cross-examination","refutation","socrates","aporia"]},{"term":"Daimonion","def":"The inner divine sign or voice that Socrates said warned him against certain actions.","more":"In Plato's Apology, Socrates says it came to him from childhood and only ever held him back, never urged him on. Notably, it did not stop him from defending himself at his trial, which he took as a sign that death was not bad. Interpreters have read it as a religious experience, as conscience, or as intuition.","etym":"Greek daimonion, 'divine thing', from daimōn, 'spirit, divinity'.","example":"A sudden strong sense that you should not take a certain job, even though you cannot say why.","notConfused":"Not the same as a demon in the later sense of an evil spirit: for the Greeks a daimon could be a guardian divinity.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Socrates","Conscience","Intuition"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato"],"tags":["daemon","daimon","socrates","inner voice","divine sign","guardian spirit"]},{"term":"Know thyself","def":"The ancient Greek maxim inscribed at the temple of Apollo at Delphi, which Socrates made central to philosophy.","more":"In Plato's dialogues Socrates treats the maxim as a call to examine one's own beliefs and ignorance. In the Phaedrus he says he has no time for clever explanations of myths while he still does not know himself. Later thinkers, from the Stoics to Montaigne, made self-knowledge a key philosophical task.","etym":"Greek gnōthi seauton.","example":"Taking time to ask why you really want a certain career, rather than just following what is expected of you.","notConfused":"Not originally a call to introspection about personality: it may first have meant 'know that you are mortal, not a god'.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Socrates","Socratic method","Hubris"],"thinkers":["socrates","plato","montaigne"],"tags":["gnothi seauton","gnōthi seauton","self-knowledge","delphi","delphic maxim","socrates"]},{"term":"Divided line","def":"Plato's image in the Republic of a line divided into four sections, representing levels of reality and corresponding levels of knowledge.","more":"In Republic Book VI, the lowest section is images and shadows (imagination), then physical things (belief), then mathematical objects (thought), and at the top the Forms (understanding). It comes just before the allegory of the cave, which presents the same ascent as a story. Scholars debate how exactly the two images map onto each other.","etym":null,"example":"Moving from a reflection of a tree in a pond, to the tree, to the geometry of its growth, to the very idea of life.","notConfused":null,"field":"History of philosophy","related":["Allegory of the cave","Forms","Platonism"],"thinkers":["plato"],"tags":["analogy of the divided line","plato","republic","levels of knowledge","forms","doxa"]},{"term":"Prime matter","def":"In Aristotelian thought, pure matter without any form, the ultimate underlying subject of all physical change.","more":"Aristotle's hylomorphism analyses things as matter plus form. If elements can change into one another, something must persist that is not itself any element: prime matter. Medieval Aristotelians, such as Aquinas, developed the idea, though scholars debate whether Aristotle himself held it. It cannot exist on its own, only as informed by some form.","etym":"Latin materia prima, 'first matter'.","example":"If water turns to air, something must stay the same through the change; prime matter is meant to be that something.","notConfused":"Not the same as atoms or elementary particles: prime matter has no properties of its own at all.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Hylomorphism","Substance","Potentiality and actuality"],"thinkers":["aristotle","aquinas"],"tags":["materia prima","first matter","hyle","aristotle","matter and form","hylomorphism"]},{"term":"Idols of the mind","def":"Francis Bacon's four kinds of mental error that distort human understanding and block true knowledge of nature.","more":"In the Novum Organum (1620), Bacon named the Idols of the Tribe (errors common to human nature), the Cave (individual biases), the Marketplace (confusions from language) and the Theatre (false philosophical systems). He believed science must clear these away and proceed by careful observation and induction. The list anticipates modern research on cognitive biases.","etym":"Latin idola, 'images, phantoms'.","example":"Remembering the times your horoscope came true and forgetting the misses is an Idol of the Tribe.","notConfused":"Not religious idols: Bacon meant false images or illusions in the mind.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Empiricism","Induction","British Empiricism"],"thinkers":["bacon"],"tags":["idols","francis bacon","bacon","novum organum","idols of the tribe","bias"]},{"term":"Esse est percipi","def":"Berkeley's principle 'to be is to be perceived': physical objects exist only as ideas in minds.","more":"George Berkeley defended it in A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) and Three Dialogues (1713). He argued that we never perceive anything but ideas, and that the notion of unperceived material substance is meaningless. Things go on existing when we look away because God always perceives them. Samuel Johnson famously 'refuted' Berkeley by kicking a stone.","etym":"Latin esse est percipi.","example":"For Berkeley, the apple in the fridge consists of the colour, taste and feel you experience, and God's perceiving keeps it there when the door is closed.","notConfused":"Not the claim that things vanish when nobody looks: Berkeley held that God perceives everything continuously.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Berkeleian idealism","Idealism","Phenomenalism"],"thinkers":["berkeley"],"tags":["to be is to be perceived","berkeley","george berkeley","idealism","immaterialism","subjective idealism"]},{"term":"Impressions and ideas","def":"Hume's division of perceptions into vivid impressions (sensations and feelings) and fainter ideas (copies of them in thought and memory).","more":"In A Treatise of Human Nature (1739) and the Enquiry (1748), Hume argued that every simple idea is copied from an earlier impression. This 'copy principle' became a tool for testing concepts: if no impression lies behind a term like 'substance' or 'necessary connection', it may be empty. He used it to criticise metaphysical ideas.","etym":null,"example":"The burn you feel touching a hot stove is an impression; recalling it later is an idea.","notConfused":"Not the same as Locke's use of 'idea', which covered every object of thought, including sensations.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Empiricism","Hume's fork","Bundle theory"],"thinkers":["hume","locke"],"tags":["impressions","ideas","hume","copy principle","empiricism","perception"]},{"term":"Sapere aude","def":"'Dare to know': the motto Kant gave to the Enlightenment, urging people to think for themselves.","more":"Kant used the Latin phrase, taken from the poet Horace, in his essay 'An Answer to the Question: What Is Enlightenment?' (1784). He defined enlightenment as humanity's emergence from self-incurred immaturity, the inability to use one's own understanding without another's guidance. He blamed laziness and cowardice for that immaturity, not lack of intelligence.","etym":"Latin sapere aude, 'dare to be wise'.","example":"Checking the evidence for a popular claim yourself rather than simply accepting what an authority says.","notConfused":"Not a call to reject all authority: Kant distinguished free public reasoning from obedience in one's official duties.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Enlightenment","Autonomy","Kantian ethics"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["dare to know","kant","enlightenment","what is enlightenment","think for yourself","horace"]},{"term":"Copernican revolution","def":"Kant's description of his own reversal in philosophy: instead of our knowledge conforming to objects, objects conform to the structure of our minds.","more":"In the preface to the second edition of the Critique of Pure Reason (1787), Kant compares his move to Copernicus's idea that the observer moves rather than the stars. He argued that space, time and categories like causation are forms our minds impose on experience. This explains how synthetic a priori knowledge is possible, but limits knowledge to appearances.","etym":null,"example":"Coloured glasses would make everything look tinted; Kant suggests space and time are like built-in 'glasses' through which we must see everything.","notConfused":"Not the same as the original Copernican revolution in astronomy, which Kant used as an analogy.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Transcendental","Synthetic a priori","Noumenon"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["kant's copernican revolution","copernican turn","kant","transcendental idealism","critique of pure reason"]},{"term":"Zeitgeist","def":"The 'spirit of the age': the characteristic ideas, mood and outlook of a particular period.","more":"The concept is associated with German thought around 1800, especially Herder and Hegel. Hegel used related phrases, such as the spirit of a time, and held that each age expresses a stage in the development of Spirit, so that philosophy is 'its own time comprehended in thought'. Critics warn that the idea can hide the diversity and conflict within any period.","etym":"German Zeit, 'time', and Geist, 'spirit'.","example":"Calling the 1960s a time of rebellion and optimism, as if a single mood shaped everything from music to politics.","notConfused":"Not the same as fashion: zeitgeist refers to deep shared assumptions of a period, not passing trends.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Geist","German Idealism","Historical materialism"],"thinkers":["hegel"],"tags":["spirit of the age","spirit of the times","hegel","mood of the era"]},{"term":"Geist","def":"Spirit or mind: in Hegel's philosophy, the collective, developing rational life of humanity that comes to know itself through history.","more":"In the Phenomenology of Spirit (1807) and later works, Hegel describes Geist as unfolding through stages, from individual consciousness to social life, art, religion and finally philosophy. He divides it into subjective, objective (law, morality, the state) and absolute spirit. Interpreters disagree whether Geist is a kind of cosmic mind or simply the shared life of human communities.","etym":"German Geist, 'spirit, mind, ghost'.","example":"Hegel would see the growth of the idea that all humans are free, from ancient slavery to modern rights, as Geist coming to know itself.","notConfused":"Not the same as a ghost or a personal soul: Geist is collective and rational.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Absolute idealism","Aufhebung","Zeitgeist"],"thinkers":["hegel"],"tags":["spirit","mind","hegel","absolute spirit","weltgeist","world spirit"]},{"term":"Aufhebung","def":"Hegel's term for the way a conflict is resolved by both cancelling and preserving its sides, raising them to a higher level.","more":"Hegel exploited the double meaning of the German verb aufheben: to abolish and to keep (and also to lift up). In his dialectic, an earlier stage is not simply destroyed by its opposite but taken up into a richer whole. It is usually translated 'sublation'. Hegel himself rarely used the formula 'thesis–antithesis–synthesis', which is often wrongly attributed to him.","etym":"German aufheben, 'to cancel, preserve, lift up'.","example":"An adult's mature personality does not erase their teenage rebellion but carries its independence forward in a calmer form.","notConfused":"Not the same as compromise: both sides are transformed, not just split down the middle.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Dialectic","Geist","Master–slave dialectic"],"thinkers":["hegel"],"tags":["sublation","sublate","hegel","dialectic","aufheben","synthesis"]},{"term":"Owl of Minerva","def":"Hegel's image for the claim that philosophy understands an era only when it is ending, too late to guide it.","more":"In the preface to the Philosophy of Right (1820), Hegel wrote that 'the owl of Minerva spreads its wings only with the falling of the dusk'. Minerva was the Roman goddess of wisdom. He meant that philosophy cannot tell the world how it ought to be in advance; it grasps a form of life once it has matured. Marx's demand that philosophy change the world was partly a reply.","etym":null,"example":"Historians and philosophers only fully understanding a revolution decades after it is over.","notConfused":null,"field":"History of philosophy","related":["Geist","Zeitgeist","Praxis"],"thinkers":["hegel","marx"],"tags":["hegel","philosophy of right","owl","dusk","hindsight","minerva"]},{"term":"Master–slave dialectic","def":"Hegel's story of two self-consciousnesses struggling for recognition, in which the master ends up dependent and the slave gains true independence through work.","more":"In the Phenomenology of Spirit (1807), two people each seek recognition; one risks death and becomes master, the other fears death and becomes slave. But the master's recognition is worthless, coming from someone he does not respect, while the slave, through labour, shapes the world and discovers his own power. The passage deeply influenced Marx, Sartre, Beauvoir and Fanon.","etym":"German Herrschaft und Knechtschaft, 'lordship and bondage'.","example":"An employer who has come to depend completely on a skilled employee who knows how everything actually works.","notConfused":"Not a historical account of real slavery but a philosophical story about recognition and self-consciousness.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Recognition","Aufhebung","Alienation"],"thinkers":["hegel","fanon","beauvoir"],"tags":["master-slave dialectic","lord and bondsman","lordship and bondage","hegel","recognition","phenomenology of spirit"]},{"term":"Will to live","def":"Schopenhauer's name for the blind, endless striving that he believed is the inner nature of all reality, and the root of suffering.","more":"In The World as Will and Representation (1818), Schopenhauer argued that behind the world as we perceive it lies a single irrational Will, appearing in us as the urge to survive and reproduce. Because desire is never satisfied for long, life oscillates between pain and boredom. Relief comes through art, compassion and, finally, denial of the will, an idea he linked to Buddhist and Hindu thought.","etym":"German Wille zum Leben.","example":"Falling in love and believing it is a free personal choice, when Schopenhauer would say the species' urge to reproduce is working through you.","notConfused":"Not the same as Nietzsche's will to power: Nietzsche rejected Schopenhauer's pessimism and saw striving as something to affirm.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Philosophical pessimism","Will to power","Conatus"],"thinkers":["schopenhauer","nietzsche"],"tags":["wille zum leben","will","schopenhauer","arthur schopenhauer","pessimism","world as will"]},{"term":"Death of God","def":"Nietzsche's announcement that belief in the Christian God had become unbelievable in modern Europe, with vast consequences for morality and meaning.","more":"The phrase appears in The Gay Science (1882), where a madman declares, 'God is dead... And we have killed him.' Nietzsche meant that science and secular thought had undermined faith, though Europeans had not yet faced what followed: the collapse of the values built on it and the threat of nihilism. Later, 1960s 'death of God' theologians took up the phrase in their own way.","etym":"German Gott ist tot.","example":"Someone who no longer believes in God but still assumes that morality, human equality and life's meaning are simply secure.","notConfused":"Not a claim that a God who once existed has literally died: it is a claim about the loss of a cultural belief.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Nihilism","Übermensch","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"tags":["god is dead","nietzsche","nihilism","gay science","the madman","secularization"]},{"term":"Lifeworld","def":"Husserl's term for the everyday world of shared, lived experience that we take for granted before any science or theory.","more":"Husserl developed the concept (Lebenswelt) in The Crisis of European Sciences (1936). He argued that modern science, beginning with Galileo, replaced the lifeworld with mathematical models and then forgot that its meaning depends on it. The idea influenced later phenomenology and Habermas, who contrasted the lifeworld with economic and bureaucratic 'systems'.","etym":"German Lebenswelt, 'life-world'.","example":"The sunset you watch is red and warm in your lifeworld, even though physics describes it in wavelengths.","notConfused":"Not the same as the private world of one individual: the lifeworld is shared and social.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Phenomenology","Being-in-the-world","Public sphere"],"thinkers":["husserl"],"tags":["lebenswelt","life-world","life world","husserl","everyday world","phenomenology"]},{"term":"Being-in-the-world","def":"Heidegger's term for human existence as always already engaged with a world of things, people and tasks, not a mind looking at objects from outside.","more":"In Being and Time (1927), Heidegger rejected the Cartesian picture of a subject separate from an external world. We are first absorbed in practical activity, using things as 'ready-to-hand', like a hammer we hardly notice while hammering. Only when something breaks do we see it as a mere object. The hyphens signal a single, unified phenomenon.","etym":"German In-der-Welt-sein.","example":"While typing, you are not aware of your keyboard as an object; it becomes one only when a key sticks.","notConfused":"Not simply being located in a place, as water is in a glass: it means being involved in a meaningful world.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Dasein","Thrownness","Phenomenology"],"thinkers":["heidegger"],"tags":["in-der-welt-sein","being in the world","heidegger","dasein","being and time","engagement"]},{"term":"Thrownness","def":"Heidegger's term for the fact that we find ourselves already existing in a particular situation we did not choose.","more":"In Being and Time (1927), Heidegger says Dasein is 'thrown' into the world: born at a certain time and place, with a body, a language and a history it did not select. We must take up these conditions as our own. Sartre's idea of facticity develops a similar point.","etym":"German Geworfenheit.","example":"You did not choose your native language, family or century, yet you have to make your life from them.","notConfused":"Not the same as fatalism: thrownness is the starting point for choice, not a denial of it.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Being-in-the-world","Facticity","Dasein"],"thinkers":["heidegger"],"tags":["geworfenheit","throwness","heidegger","dasein","facticity","being and time"]},{"term":"Das Man","def":"Heidegger's term for 'the They' or 'the One': the anonymous public way of thinking and acting that we fall into without choosing.","more":"In Being and Time (1927), Heidegger describes how we do what 'one' does, enjoy what 'one' enjoys, and judge as 'one' judges. This relieves us of responsibility but leads to an inauthentic existence. Authenticity requires owning our choices, especially in facing our own death.","etym":"German das Man, from the impersonal pronoun man, 'one'.","example":"Choosing a career, clothes and opinions mainly because 'that's what people do'.","notConfused":"Not a particular group of other people: Das Man is a way of being that includes ourselves.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Authenticity","Being-toward-death","Bad faith"],"thinkers":["heidegger"],"tags":["the they","the one","heidegger","inauthenticity","conformism"]},{"term":"Facticity","def":"The given facts of a person's existence, such as their body, past, place and circumstances, which their freedom must work with.","more":"Heidegger used the term in Being and Time, and Sartre made it central in Being and Nothingness (1943), contrasting facticity with transcendence, our ability to go beyond what is given through choice. Bad faith, for Sartre, is pretending to be only facticity (a fixed thing) or only transcendence (pure freedom). Beauvoir applied the idea to women's situation.","etym":"French facticité, from Latin factum, 'what has been done'.","example":"You cannot change that you failed an exam last year, but you can decide what that fact will mean for your future.","notConfused":"Not the same as fate: facticity is the situation freedom starts from, not a script it must follow.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Thrownness","Bad faith","Situated freedom"],"thinkers":["heidegger","sartre","beauvoir"],"tags":["factical","sartre","heidegger","thrownness","situation","givenness"]},{"term":"Being-for-itself and being-in-itself","def":"Sartre's contrast between conscious beings, who are free and always incomplete (for-itself), and mere things, which simply are what they are (in-itself).","more":"In Being and Nothingness (1943), Sartre describes the in-itself as solid, full and unchanging, while the for-itself, consciousness, involves nothingness: it can question, negate and project itself toward possibilities. Human beings long to be both at once, free yet fixed, which Sartre calls the desire to be God, an impossible project. The distinction underlies Sartre's account of bad faith, in which a person pretends to be a fixed thing in order to escape the burden of freedom.","etym":"French être-pour-soi and être-en-soi.","example":"A table is simply a table; a person who works as a waiter is never just a waiter, because they could always choose otherwise.","notConfused":"Not the same as mind–body dualism: Sartre is contrasting two modes of being, not two substances.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["Bad faith","Existence precedes essence","Facticity"],"thinkers":["sartre"],"tags":["pour-soi","en-soi","for-itself","in-itself","sartre","being and nothingness"]},{"term":"The look","def":"Sartre's account of how being seen by another person makes us aware of ourselves as objects in someone else's world.","more":"In Being and Nothingness (1943), Sartre describes a man peeping through a keyhole who suddenly hears footsteps and feels shame: he is now seen. The look reveals that others are free subjects who can judge us and define us in ways we cannot control. It underlies his line from No Exit, 'Hell is other people.'","etym":"French le regard, 'the look, the gaze'.","example":"The sudden self-consciousness when you realise someone has been watching you sing to yourself.","notConfused":"Not the same as the male gaze, though related: Sartre's look is a general feature of human relations, not specifically gendered.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["The Other","Bad faith","Being-for-itself and being-in-itself"],"thinkers":["sartre"],"tags":["le regard","the gaze","sartre","shame","being-for-others","objectification"]},{"term":"The Other","def":"In continental philosophy, another person (or group) considered as a separate consciousness whose existence shapes our own self-understanding.","more":"Hegel's master–slave dialectic made the other's recognition central to self-consciousness. Sartre described the Other through the look, Beauvoir argued in The Second Sex (1949) that society has made woman 'the Other' to man, and Emmanuel Levinas held that the face of the Other places an infinite ethical demand on us. Postcolonial thinkers analyse how colonised peoples were cast as the Other.","etym":null,"example":"Defining 'us' by what 'we' are not, such as a nation describing itself by contrast with foreigners.","notConfused":"Not the same as the problem of other minds: that asks how we know others have minds; the Other concerns how others shape our existence and ethics.","field":"History of philosophy","related":["The look","Recognition","Orientalism"],"thinkers":["hegel","sartre","beauvoir"],"tags":["other","otherness","alterity","levinas","the other person","othering"]},{"term":"Milesian school","def":"The first Greek philosophers, Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes, from the city of Miletus in the 6th century BC.","more":"They sought natural explanations for the world, asking what single basic stuff (arche) everything comes from: water for Thales, the boundless (apeiron) for Anaximander, air for Anaximenes. Anaximenes explained change through condensation and rarefaction of air. Aristotle treated them as the first natural philosophers, and they are often credited with beginning both philosophy and science in the West.","etym":"From Miletus, a Greek city on the coast of present-day Turkey.","example":"Explaining earthquakes by the earth floating on water, as Thales reportedly did, rather than by the anger of a god.","notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":["Pre-Socratics","Arche","Apeiron"],"thinkers":["thales"],"tags":["milesians","miletus","thales","anaximander","anaximenes","ionian school"]},{"term":"Pythagoreanism","def":"The philosophical and religious movement founded by Pythagoras, which held that number is the key to reality and taught the transmigration of souls.","more":"Pythagoras founded a community in Croton in southern Italy in the late 6th century BC, with rules about diet and silence. Later Pythagoreans such as Philolaus taught that the cosmos is ordered by number and harmony, inspired partly by the mathematical ratios of musical intervals. Their ideas strongly influenced Plato. Because they wrote little early on, much about Pythagoras himself is legend.","etym":"From Pythagoras of Samos.","example":"Noticing that a string half as long plays a note exactly an octave higher, and concluding that the universe is made of mathematical harmony.","notConfused":"Not the same as Pythagoras's theorem, which was known to the Babylonians long before and whose link to Pythagoras is uncertain.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Pre-Socratics","Platonism","Rebirth"],"thinkers":["pythagoras","plato"],"tags":["pythagoreans","pythagoras","pythagorean","number mysticism","harmony of the spheres","transmigration"]},{"term":"Cyrenaics","def":"An ancient Greek school, founded by Aristippus of Cyrene, which held that immediate bodily pleasure is the highest good.","more":"Aristippus was a follower of Socrates, and the school flourished in the 4th and 3rd centuries BC. Unlike Epicurus, who valued tranquillity and the absence of pain, the Cyrenaics prized present, active pleasures and doubted we can know anything beyond our own sensations. Later members such as Hegesias became deeply pessimistic about whether happiness is achievable.","etym":"From Cyrene, a Greek city in present-day Libya.","example":"Choosing the delicious meal tonight over saving for a future you might not live to enjoy.","notConfused":"Not the same as Epicureanism: Epicureans prized calm and the absence of pain, Cyrenaics prized the intensity of present pleasure.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Hedonism","Epicureanism","Socrates"],"thinkers":["socrates"],"tags":["cyrenaic","cyrenaic school","aristippus","hedonism","pleasure","cyrene"]},{"term":"Peripatetic school","def":"The school of philosophy founded by Aristotle at the Lyceum in Athens, and the tradition of his followers.","more":"Aristotle founded the Lyceum around 335 BC; the name 'Peripatetic' probably comes from the peripatos, a covered walkway there, though a legend says Aristotle lectured while walking. His successor Theophrastus continued research in botany, ethics and logic. Later Peripatetics, notably Alexander of Aphrodisias (c. 200 AD), wrote influential commentaries on Aristotle.","etym":"Greek peripatētikos, 'given to walking about'.","example":"Students gathered under a colonnade discussing the classification of plants and animals with their teacher.","notConfused":"Not the same as Aristotelianism in general, which includes medieval and modern followers far beyond the ancient school.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Aristotelianism","Scholasticism","Stoicism"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["peripatetics","lyceum","aristotle","aristotelianism","peripatetic","theophrastus"]},{"term":"Transcendentalism","def":"A 19th-century American movement that held that truth is found through intuition and nature, and that each individual has a spark of the divine.","more":"Centred in New England in the 1830s and 1840s, it was led by Ralph Waldo Emerson, whose essay Nature (1836) is a founding text, and Henry David Thoreau, author of Walden (1854) and 'Civil Disobedience' (1849). Influenced by Kant, Romanticism and Indian scriptures, they stressed self-reliance, non-conformity and the moral value of nature. Margaret Fuller was another leading figure.","etym":"From Kant's term 'transcendental', borrowed loosely.","example":"Leaving the city to live simply in a cabin in the woods to discover what is truly essential in life, as Thoreau did.","notConfused":"Not the same as Kant's transcendental idealism, which the movement only loosely drew on.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Romanticism","Civil disobedience","Idealism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["transcendentalist","emerson","ralph waldo emerson","thoreau","henry david thoreau","self-reliance"]},{"term":"Yogācāra","def":"A Mahayana Buddhist school holding that what we experience as the external world is the construction of consciousness.","more":"Founded in India around the 4th century AD by the brothers Asanga and Vasubandhu, it is also called 'Mind-Only' (cittamātra). It analyses eight kinds of consciousness, including a 'storehouse consciousness' that carries karmic seeds. Vasubandhu's Twenty Verses uses dream arguments to show objects need not exist outside the mind. Scholars debate whether Yogācāra is idealism in the Western sense.","etym":"Sanskrit yogācāra, 'practice of yoga'.","example":"In a dream you see mountains and feel fear, though nothing exists outside your mind; Yogācāra argues waking experience can be understood similarly.","notConfused":"Not the same as Madhyamaka: Madhyamaka says everything, including mind, is empty; Yogācāra gives consciousness a special explanatory role.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Madhyamaka","Idealism","Mahayana"],"thinkers":["vasubandhu"],"tags":["yogacara","mind-only","consciousness-only","vijnanavada","cittamatra","vasubandhu"]},{"term":"Falsafa","def":"The tradition of Greek-inspired philosophy in the medieval Islamic world, developed by thinkers such as al-Kindi, al-Farabi, Avicenna and Averroes.","more":"Falsafa grew from the 9th century, after Greek works by Aristotle, Plato and the Neoplatonists were translated into Arabic in Baghdad. Its philosophers (falasifa) worked on logic, metaphysics, ethics and politics, often trying to harmonise reason with Islam. Al-Ghazali's The Incoherence of the Philosophers attacked some of their views, and Averroes replied with The Incoherence of the Incoherence.","etym":"Arabic falsafa, from Greek philosophia.","example":"Avicenna's 'floating man' thought experiment, asking whether a person suspended in air without sensations would still know they exist.","notConfused":"Not the same as kalam: kalam is theology that defends religious doctrine by argument, while falsafa grew from the Greek philosophical tradition.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Kalam","Philosophy in the Islamic World","Aristotelianism"],"thinkers":["alfarabi","avicenna","averroes","alghazali"],"tags":["islamic philosophy","arabic philosophy","al-kindi","al-farabi","avicenna","averroes"]},{"term":"Structuralism","def":"A 20th-century approach that explains language, culture and the mind through underlying systems of relations rather than individual elements.","more":"It began with Ferdinand de Saussure's Course in General Linguistics (1916), which argued that a word's meaning comes from its differences from other words in a system. Claude Lévi-Strauss applied it to myths and kinship, Roland Barthes to popular culture, and Jacques Lacan to psychoanalysis. It dominated French thought in the 1950s and 1960s before post-structuralist critiques.","etym":"Latin structura, 'a fitting together, building'.","example":"Understanding a traffic light: red means 'stop' not because of anything in redness but because of its contrast with green and amber.","notConfused":"Not the same as structural realism in philosophy of science or mathematical structuralism, though they share an emphasis on relations.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Post-structuralism","Deconstruction","Language game"],"thinkers":["barthes"],"tags":["structuralist","saussure","levi-strauss","lévi-strauss","structures","semiotics"]},{"term":"Post-structuralism","def":"A movement of the late 1960s onward that criticised structuralism for assuming stable systems of meaning, stressing instability, power and difference.","more":"Thinkers often grouped under the label include Jacques Derrida, Michel Foucault, Gilles Deleuze, Julia Kristeva and the later Roland Barthes, though most rejected the label. They questioned fixed meanings, unified selves and grand explanations, and examined how language and knowledge are bound up with power. Critics accuse it of obscurity and relativism.","etym":null,"example":"Showing how the meaning of a word like 'normal' shifts over time and serves to exclude certain people.","notConfused":"Not the same as postmodernism, though overlapping: post-structuralism is mainly a set of theories of language and knowledge, postmodernism a wider cultural condition and style.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Structuralism","Deconstruction","Postmodernism"],"thinkers":["barthes"],"tags":["poststructuralism","post structuralism","derrida","foucault","deconstruction","postmodernism"]},{"term":"Neo-Kantianism","def":"A broad revival of Kant's philosophy in Germany from about 1860 to the 1920s, focused on the foundations of science and values.","more":"Under the slogan 'Back to Kant', the Marburg School (Hermann Cohen, Paul Natorp, Ernst Cassirer) studied the logical foundations of mathematics and natural science. The Southwest or Baden School (Wilhelm Windelband, Heinrich Rickert) distinguished the natural sciences from the cultural sciences and studied values. Its influence reached phenomenology, logical positivism and sociologists such as Max Weber.","etym":null,"example":"Asking not what the world is really like, but what conditions make scientific knowledge of it possible.","notConfused":"Not the same as Kantian ethics today: Neo-Kantianism was a historical movement, mainly in epistemology and philosophy of science.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Kantian synthesis (transcendental idealism)","German Idealism","Logical positivism"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["neokantianism","neo-kantian","marburg school","baden school","cassirer","back to kant"]},{"term":"Positivism","def":"Auguste Comte's 19th-century philosophy that genuine knowledge comes only from science, and that society should be studied and organised scientifically.","more":"Comte argued in his Course of Positive Philosophy (1830–1842) that human thought passes through three stages: theological, metaphysical and finally positive (scientific). He coined the word 'sociology' for the science of society. Late in life he founded a secular 'Religion of Humanity'. Logical positivism in the 20th century took over the name but added new tools from logic.","etym":"French positif, 'given, laid down', meaning based on observable facts.","example":"Explaining a drought by measurable weather patterns rather than angry gods (theological) or hidden forces (metaphysical).","notConfused":"Not the same as logical positivism: Comte's positivism was a theory of history and society, without the verification principle.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Logical positivism","Empiricism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["positivist","auguste comte","comte","scientism","law of three stages","logical positivism"]},{"term":"Logical atomism","def":"The view, held by Russell and the early Wittgenstein, that the world consists of simple facts and that language can be analysed into matching simple statements.","more":"Russell lectured on 'The Philosophy of Logical Atomism' in 1918, and Wittgenstein's Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921) presented its most famous version: the world is the totality of facts, and propositions are pictures of them. Complex statements are truth-functions of atomic ones. Wittgenstein later rejected the view in his Philosophical Investigations.","etym":"Greek atomos, 'uncuttable'.","example":"Analysing 'The cat is on the mat and the dog is barking' into two simple statements whose truth settles the whole.","notConfused":"Not the same as atomism in physics: the atoms here are logical facts, not particles.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Analytic philosophy","Logical positivism","Linguistic turn"],"thinkers":["russell","wittgenstein"],"tags":["atomic facts","russell","bertrand russell","wittgenstein","tractatus","logical analysis"]},{"term":"Thomism","def":"The philosophical and theological tradition based on the thought of Thomas Aquinas.","more":"Aquinas combined Aristotle's philosophy with Christian theology. His thought was adopted by the Dominican order and, after Pope Leo XIII's encyclical Aeterni Patris (1879), promoted as a foundation for Catholic education. Twentieth-century Thomists included Jacques Maritain and Étienne Gilson, and 'analytic Thomists' such as Anscombe's circle apply modern logical tools to Thomist ideas.","etym":"From Thomas Aquinas.","example":"A philosopher defending natural law ethics by arguing that human reason can grasp the goods that fulfil human nature.","notConfused":"Not the same as Scholasticism as a whole: Thomism is one school within the broader scholastic method.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Scholasticism","Thomistic natural law","Aristotelianism"],"thinkers":["aquinas","anscombe"],"tags":["thomist","aquinas","thomas aquinas","scholasticism","neo-thomism","st thomas"]},{"term":"Averroism","def":"A medieval European movement inspired by Averroes's commentaries on Aristotle, known for teaching that all humans share one intellect.","more":"In 13th-century Paris, masters such as Siger of Brabant followed Averroes in reading Aristotle closely, including on controversial points like the eternity of the world and the unity of the intellect. Aquinas wrote On the Unity of the Intellect against the Averroists, and the bishop of Paris condemned related theses in 1270 and 1277. Critics accused them of holding a 'double truth', though this was largely a caricature.","etym":"From Averroes (Ibn Rushd).","example":"A medieval teacher saying, 'This is what Aristotle's reasoning proves, though faith teaches otherwise.'","notConfused":"Not identical to Averroes's own thought: Latin Averroists developed and sometimes distorted his views.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Falsafa","Scholasticism","Aristotelianism"],"thinkers":["averroes","aquinas"],"tags":["averroist","averroes","ibn rushd","latin averroism","double truth","unity of the intellect"]},{"term":"Mu'tazila","def":"An early school of Islamic theology that stressed reason, God's justice and unity, and human free will.","more":"The Mu'tazila flourished in Basra and Baghdad from the 8th to 10th centuries. They held that the Qur'an was created rather than eternal, that reason can know good and evil without revelation, and that God, being just, must reward and punish humans for actions they freely choose. They were briefly backed by the Abbasid caliphs, and later lost ground to the Ash'ari school.","etym":"Arabic muʿtazila, 'those who withdraw'.","example":"Arguing that lying is wrong because reason shows it to be wrong, not simply because God forbids it.","notConfused":"Not the same as falsafa: the Mu'tazila were theologians (practitioners of kalam), not followers of the Greek philosophical system.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Kalam","Ash'arism","Free will"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["mutazila","mu'tazilites","mutazilites","kalam","rationalism","free will"]},{"term":"Ash'arism","def":"The school of Islamic theology founded by al-Ash'ari, holding that God directly creates every event and that good and evil are set by God's command.","more":"Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari (d. 935/936) left the Mu'tazila and developed a middle path between strict traditionalism and rationalism. Ash'arites held that God is the only true cause and humans 'acquire' (kasb) actions God creates, and that moral truths depend on God's will. Al-Ghazali was an Ash'arite, and the school became the main Sunni theology.","etym":"From Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari.","example":"Saying fire does not really burn cotton by its own power; God creates the burning each time the two meet.","notConfused":"Not the same as divine command theory in Christian ethics, though similar in grounding morality in God's will.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Mu'tazila","Occasionalism","Kalam"],"thinkers":["ashari","alghazali"],"tags":["asharism","ash'ari","ashari","asharite","occasionalism","kalam"]},{"term":"Illuminationism","def":"A school of Islamic philosophy founded by Shihab al-Din Suhrawardi, which describes reality as degrees of light and knowledge as direct presence.","more":"Suhrawardi (1154–1191) set out the system in The Philosophy of Illumination, criticising parts of Avicenna's Aristotelianism and drawing on Platonic and ancient Persian ideas. Reality is a hierarchy of lights flowing from the Light of Lights, and the deepest knowledge is 'knowledge by presence', not by concepts. He was executed in Aleppo, but his school greatly influenced later Persian philosophy, including Mulla Sadra.","etym":"Arabic ishrāq, 'illumination, the rising of the sun'.","example":"Knowing your own existence directly and immediately, without needing a concept or argument, is the model of knowledge by presence.","notConfused":"Not the same as the European Enlightenment, despite the similar image of light.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Falsafa","Sufism","Neoplatonism"],"thinkers":["avicenna"],"tags":["ishraq","hikmat al-ishraq","philosophy of illumination","suhrawardi","light","ishraqi"]},{"term":"Gnosticism","def":"A group of religious movements in the early centuries AD that taught salvation through secret knowledge and saw the material world as flawed or evil.","more":"Gnostic texts, many found at Nag Hammadi in Egypt in 1945, often describe a lesser creator god, the Demiurge, who made the physical world, while sparks of the true divine are trapped in human beings. Church fathers such as Irenaeus attacked these teachings as heresy. Scholars now debate whether 'Gnosticism' names a single movement at all.","etym":"Greek gnōsis, 'knowledge'.","example":"Believing that your true self is a spark of light imprisoned in a body, and that awakening to this secret frees it.","notConfused":"Not the same as agnosticism: the agnostic says we cannot know whether God exists; the Gnostic claims special saving knowledge.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Problem of evil","Neoplatonism","Manichaeism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["gnostic","gnostics","gnosis","demiurge","secret knowledge","valentinus"]},{"term":"Manichaeism","def":"A religion founded by the Persian prophet Mani in the 3rd century AD, teaching a cosmic struggle between a good realm of light and an evil realm of darkness.","more":"Mani (c. 216–274/277) combined elements of Zoroastrianism, Christianity and Buddhism. Manichaeans believed light is trapped in matter and must be freed through ascetic practice. The religion spread from the Roman Empire to China. Augustine was a Manichaean for about nine years before converting to Christianity, and his later theory of evil as privation was partly a reply.","etym":"From Mani.","example":"Explaining every evil in the world by an independent evil power at war with God.","notConfused":"In everyday speech 'Manichaean' means seeing things in black and white, but the religion was a specific, complex system.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Gnosticism","Privation theory of evil","Problem of evil"],"thinkers":["augustine"],"tags":["manicheism","manichean","manichaean","mani","dualism","light and darkness"]},{"term":"Samkhya","def":"One of the oldest schools of Hindu philosophy, a dualism of pure consciousness (purusha) and matter (prakriti).","more":"Its classic text is Ishvarakrishna's Samkhyakarika (around the 4th–5th century AD), though Samkhya ideas appear much earlier, including in the Bhagavad Gita. It holds that matter evolves into the mind, senses and elements through the interplay of the three gunas, and that liberation comes from discriminating purusha from prakriti. Classical Samkhya does not posit a creator God.","etym":"Sanskrit sāṃkhya, 'enumeration, reckoning'.","example":"Listing the twenty-five basic principles of reality, from consciousness down to the five elements, is a Samkhya exercise.","notConfused":"Not the same as Yoga, though closely paired: Yoga accepts Samkhya metaphysics but adds a personal God (Ishvara) and meditation practices.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Purusha and prakriti","Gunas","Yoga"],"thinkers":["patanjali"],"tags":["sankhya","sāṃkhya","samkhya philosophy","purusha and prakriti","dualism","kapila"]},{"term":"Vaisheshika","def":"A Hindu school of natural philosophy that classified reality into categories and held that physical things are made of eternal atoms.","more":"It is traditionally founded by Kanada, whose Vaisheshika Sutra may date from the last centuries BC. It listed categories (padarthas) such as substance, quality, action, universal and particularity, and was later merged with the Nyaya school of logic. Its atomism developed independently of Greek atomism.","etym":"Sanskrit viśeṣa, 'distinction, particularity'.","example":"Explaining that a pot is made of tiny, indivisible particles of earth combined in pairs and threes.","notConfused":"Not the same as Greek atomism: Vaisheshika atoms are of different elemental kinds and were often thought to be arranged by God's will.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Nyaya","Atomism","Samkhya"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["vaiseshika","vaisesika","vaiśeṣika","kanada","atomism","categories"]},{"term":"Mimamsa","def":"The Hindu school devoted to interpreting the Vedas, especially their ritual commands, and to defending their authority.","more":"Founded on Jaimini's Mimamsa Sutra (around the 2nd century BC or later), it developed sophisticated theories of language, meaning and interpretation. Its leading thinkers, Kumarila Bhatta and Prabhakara (7th century AD), argued that the Vedas are authorless and eternal, and that dharma is known through them. Its rules of interpretation influenced Hindu law.","etym":"Sanskrit mīmāṃsā, 'investigation, inquiry'.","example":"Debating whether a Vedic command to perform a sacrifice means it must be done, or only describes a ritual's benefits.","notConfused":"Not the same as Vedanta (sometimes called Uttara Mimamsa, 'later inquiry'): Mimamsa focuses on ritual and action, Vedanta on knowledge of Brahman.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Vedanta","Dharma","Hermeneutics"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["mīmāṃsā","purva mimamsa","mimansa","vedic exegesis","kumarila","dharma"]},{"term":"Carvaka","def":"The materialist school of ancient India, which rejected the afterlife, karma and the gods, and accepted only perception as a source of knowledge.","more":"Also called Lokayata, it is known mostly through its opponents' summaries, since its own texts are lost. Carvakas held that consciousness arises from the combination of material elements, as intoxicating power arises from fermented ingredients, and that it ends at death. They doubted inference because universal rules cannot be checked by perception. Some reports attribute to them a pleasure-seeking ethic.","etym":"Sanskrit; traditionally from the name of a founder, Carvaka; Lokāyata means 'worldly'.","example":"Telling a priest that since no one has seen a soul leave the body, there is no reason to pay for rituals for the dead.","notConfused":"Not simply a school of hedonists: its main contributions were sceptical arguments about inference and the soul.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Materialism","Pramana","Inductive skepticism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["charvaka","cārvāka","lokayata","materialism","indian materialism","atheism"]},{"term":"Dvaita Vedanta","def":"Madhva's school of Vedanta, which holds that God, individual souls and matter are eternally distinct.","more":"Madhva (13th century) argued against Shankara's Advaita that difference is real: God (Vishnu) differs from souls, souls from each other, and all from matter. Liberation comes by God's grace through devotion (bhakti), and souls remain distinct from God even then. Madhva also held that some souls are eternally bound, an unusual doctrine in Hindu thought.","etym":"Sanskrit dvaita, 'duality'.","example":"Seeing yourself as a servant of God forever, never merging into God, as a drop of water might into the sea.","notConfused":"Not the same as Western mind–body dualism: Dvaita is about the distinction between God, souls and the world.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Vedanta","Advaita Vedanta","Vishishtadvaita"],"thinkers":["madhva"],"tags":["dvaita","dualism","madhva","madhvacharya","vedanta"]},{"term":"Vishishtadvaita","def":"Ramanuja's school of Vedanta, 'qualified non-dualism', in which souls and the world are real parts of Brahman, like a body of which God is the soul.","more":"Ramanuja (traditionally 1017–1137) argued against Shankara that the world is not illusion and that Brahman is a personal God (Vishnu) with real qualities. Selves and matter are distinct from God but inseparable from God, who is their inner controller. Liberation comes through devotion and God's grace, and the liberated soul remains an individual enjoying God.","etym":"Sanskrit viśiṣṭa, 'qualified', and advaita, 'non-dual'.","example":"A person's body is truly theirs and distinct from their soul, yet they are one person; Ramanuja saw the world's relation to God similarly.","notConfused":"Not the same as Advaita: Advaita holds that individual selves are ultimately identical with Brahman and their difference is illusory.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Vedanta","Dvaita Vedanta","Bhakti"],"thinkers":["ramanuja"],"tags":["visistadvaita","qualified non-dualism","qualified nondualism","ramanuja","vedanta"]},{"term":"Pure Land Buddhism","def":"A Mahayana tradition centred on faith in Amitabha Buddha and the hope of rebirth in his 'Pure Land', where enlightenment is easy to reach.","more":"It is based on sutras describing Amitabha's vow to welcome all who call on him. It became widespread in China and Japan, where Hōnen (1133–1212) and his disciple Shinran (1173–1263) taught that sincere recitation of Amida's name, the nembutsu, relying on his 'other-power' rather than one's own effort, is enough. It is today one of the largest forms of Buddhism in East Asia.","etym":"Chinese jingtu, Japanese jōdo, 'pure land'.","example":"A Japanese grandmother repeating 'Namu Amida Butsu' throughout the day as an expression of trust.","notConfused":"Not the same as a Christian heaven: the Pure Land is a place where enlightenment is easier, not the final goal itself.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Mahayana","Grace","Bodhisattva"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["pure land","amitabha","amida","nembutsu","jodo shinshu","shinran"]},{"term":"Theravada","def":"The 'Way of the Elders': the oldest surviving school of Buddhism, dominant in Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia, based on the Pali Canon.","more":"Theravada traces itself to the early monastic community and preserves the Pali scriptures. Its 5th-century commentator Buddhaghosa wrote the Visuddhimagga, a systematic guide to the path. It emphasises monastic discipline, meditation and the ideal of the arhat. In the 20th century, its vipassana (insight) meditation became influential worldwide.","etym":"Pali thera, 'elder', and vāda, 'doctrine'.","example":"Young men in Thailand spending a few months as monks in a local monastery, a common Theravada tradition.","notConfused":"Not the same as all non-Mahayana Buddhism: Theravada is the one surviving school out of many early Buddhist schools.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Mahayana","Arhat","Buddhism"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["theravāda","way of the elders","hinayana","pali canon","southeast asian buddhism","arhat"]},{"term":"Mahayana","def":"The 'Great Vehicle': the branch of Buddhism that emphasises the bodhisattva path of seeking enlightenment for the sake of all beings.","more":"Mahayana arose in India around the start of the common era with new sutras such as the Perfection of Wisdom and Lotus Sutras. It includes the philosophical schools of Madhyamaka and Yogācāra and spread to China, Tibet, Korea and Japan, giving rise to Zen and Pure Land. Its teachings include emptiness, skilful means and Buddha-nature.","etym":"Sanskrit mahā, 'great', and yāna, 'vehicle'.","example":"A practitioner vowing not to rest in their own liberation until every suffering being is free.","notConfused":"Not the same as Vajrayana (Tibetan tantric Buddhism), which is usually considered a development within Mahayana.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Bodhisattva","Theravada","Madhyamaka"],"thinkers":["nagarjuna","vasubandhu"],"tags":["mahāyāna","great vehicle","bodhisattva","mahayana buddhism","emptiness","sunyata"]},{"term":"School of Names","def":"An ancient Chinese school of thinkers, including Hui Shi and Gongsun Long, known for paradoxes about language and logic.","more":"Active in the 4th and 3rd centuries BC, its thinkers explored how names relate to things. Gongsun Long argued that 'a white horse is not a horse', and Hui Shi, a friend and debating partner of Zhuangzi, offered paradoxes about space and time. Many contemporaries dismissed them as sophists, but modern scholars see serious work on reference and categories.","etym":"Chinese 名家 míngjiā, 'school of names'.","example":"Arguing that 'white horse' and 'horse' mean different things, so a white horse is not (identical to) a horse.","notConfused":"Not the same as the Confucian doctrine of rectifying names (zhengming), which is about names fitting social roles.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Zhengming","Hundred Schools of Thought","Zhuangzi"],"thinkers":["zhuangzi"],"tags":["mingjia","logicians","hui shi","gongsun long","white horse paradox"]},{"term":"Process philosophy","def":"The view that reality is fundamentally made of events and processes, not fixed things or substances.","more":"It has ancient roots in Heraclitus, but its classic modern statement is Alfred North Whitehead's Process and Reality (1929), which describes reality as 'actual occasions' of experience. Henri Bergson and Charles Hartshorne developed related views, and process theology applies them to God. Critics question whether processes can exist without things that undergo them.","etym":null,"example":"Thinking of a person not as a solid object that changes, but as an ongoing flow of experiences and activities.","notConfused":"Not the same as process theism, which is one application of process philosophy to God.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Process theism","Heraclitus","Substance"],"thinkers":["whitehead","hartshorne","heraclitus"],"tags":["process metaphysics","process thought","whitehead","alfred north whitehead","becoming","heraclitus"]},{"term":"Personalism","def":"A family of philosophies that make the human person, with dignity, freedom and relationships, the central concept of thought.","more":"American personalism grew at Boston University under Borden Parker Bowne in the late 19th century, and influenced Martin Luther King Jr., who studied there. French personalism, led by Emmanuel Mounier in the 1930s, opposed both individualism and collectivism. Max Scheler and later Karol Wojtyła (Pope John Paul II) developed phenomenological personalism.","etym":"Latin persona, 'mask, character, person'.","example":"Arguing that an economy should be judged by whether it lets each person flourish in community, not only by output.","notConfused":"Not the same as individualism: personalists stress that persons are essentially relational.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Person","Moral status","I–Thou"],"thinkers":["scheler"],"tags":["personalist","person","human dignity","mounier","emmanuel mounier","borden parker bowne"]},{"term":"Transhumanism","def":"The movement holding that humans should use technology to overcome biological limits such as ageing, disease and limited intelligence.","more":"The biologist Julian Huxley used the word in 1957, and the movement grew from the 1980s and 1990s, with philosophers such as Nick Bostrom and Julian Savulescu defending human enhancement. Critics such as Michael Sandel and Francis Fukuyama argue that it threatens human dignity, equality or an appreciation of life as a gift. Defenders reply that medicine already enhances humans, through vaccines and glasses for example, so the line between therapy and enhancement is hard to draw.","etym":"Latin trans, 'beyond', plus human.","example":"Supporting research on gene editing to raise intelligence, or brain implants to improve memory.","notConfused":"Not the same as posthumanism, a different critical tradition that questions the boundaries of the human rather than enhancing it.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Humanism","Immortality is desirable","Longtermism"],"thinkers":["bostrom","sandel"],"tags":["transhumanist","human enhancement","h+","posthumanism","life extension","bostrom"]},{"term":"Negritude","def":"A literary and philosophical movement of the 1930s onward that affirmed the value of Black African culture and identity against colonial racism.","more":"It was founded in Paris by students from French colonies: Aimé Césaire of Martinique, Léopold Sédar Senghor of Senegal and Léon-Gontran Damas of French Guiana. Césaire coined the word in his poem Notebook of a Return to the Native Land (1939). Sartre praised it in 'Black Orpheus' (1948), while Fanon and later African philosophers criticised Senghor's version for essentialism.","etym":"French négritude, from nègre, 'Black person', reclaimed from a slur.","example":"A poet celebrating African rhythms, ancestors and landscapes as a source of pride rather than shame.","notConfused":"Not the same as Pan-Africanism, a political movement for African unity, though the two influenced each other.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Postcolonialism","Africana Philosophy","Recognition"],"thinkers":["sartre","fanon"],"tags":["négritude","negritude movement","senghor","léopold senghor","aimé césaire","cesaire","black identity"]},{"term":"Postcolonialism","def":"The study of the cultural, political and intellectual effects of colonialism and of how formerly colonised peoples resist and reshape them.","more":"Frantz Fanon's The Wretched of the Earth (1961) and Edward Said's Orientalism (1978) are founding texts; Gayatri Spivak's 'Can the Subaltern Speak?' and Homi Bhabha's work extended the field. Postcolonial thinkers examine how colonial categories shape knowledge, identity and literature even after independence. Debates concern how to decolonise philosophy curricula that focus only on European thinkers.","etym":null,"example":"Noticing that a school history book describes colonisers as 'discovering' lands where people already lived.","notConfused":"Not simply the period after colonies became independent: it is a critical approach to the lasting legacy of colonialism.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Orientalism","Negritude","Double consciousness"],"thinkers":["fanon"],"tags":["postcolonial theory","post-colonialism","postcolonial studies","decolonization","colonialism","fanon","said"]},{"term":"Scottish School of Common Sense","def":"An 18th-century Scottish movement, led by Thomas Reid, that defended ordinary beliefs about the world against the scepticism of Hume.","more":"Reid's An Inquiry into the Human Mind on the Principles of Common Sense (1764) argued that the 'theory of ideas' shared by Descartes, Locke, Berkeley and Hume led to absurd scepticism. Instead, certain principles, such as that the external world exists and that memory is generally reliable, are part of our constitution and need no proof. Dugald Stewart continued the school, which influenced American and French philosophy.","etym":null,"example":"Answering a sceptic by saying that it is more reasonable to trust your eyes than any clever argument that you cannot know the table is there.","notConfused":"Not the same as appealing to popular opinion: Reid meant principles built into human nature, not whatever most people believe.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Mooreanism (common sense)","Direct realism","Skepticism"],"thinkers":["reid"],"tags":["common sense philosophy","scottish common sense realism","thomas reid","reid","common sense realism"]},{"term":"Young Hegelians","def":"A group of radical German thinkers in the 1830s and 1840s who used Hegel's ideas to criticise religion and the Prussian state.","more":"After Hegel's death in 1831, his followers split. The 'Old' or 'Right' Hegelians saw his system as supporting Christianity and the existing order; the 'Young' or 'Left' Hegelians, including David Strauss, Bruno Bauer, Ludwig Feuerbach, Max Stirner and the young Karl Marx, drew radical conclusions. Feuerbach's The Essence of Christianity (1841) argued that God is a projection of human qualities.","etym":null,"example":"Arguing that people worship a God who is really an idealised image of human love and reason, as Feuerbach did.","notConfused":"Not 'young' in age necessarily: the name marks their radical politics, as opposed to the conservative Old Hegelians.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["German Idealism","Marxism","Alienation"],"thinkers":["hegel","marx","stirner"],"tags":["left hegelians","young hegelian","feuerbach","bruno bauer","max stirner","marx"]},{"term":"British idealism","def":"An influential movement in late 19th-century Britain that drew on Hegel to argue that reality is a spiritual whole and that individuals are fulfilled in community.","more":"Leading figures included T. H. Green, F. H. Bradley, Bernard Bosanquet and J. M. E. McTaggart. Green's ethics of self-realisation shaped a more socially active liberalism, and Bradley's Appearance and Reality (1893) argued for a single Absolute. Russell and Moore's revolt against it around 1900 helped launch analytic philosophy.","etym":null,"example":"Arguing that the state should provide education because citizens can only realise their best selves when given real opportunities.","notConfused":"Not the same as Berkeley's idealism: the British idealists were influenced mainly by Kant and Hegel.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Absolute idealism","German Idealism","Analytic philosophy"],"thinkers":["bradley","mctaggart","russell","moore"],"tags":["british idealists","bradley","f. h. bradley","t. h. green","absolute idealism","mctaggart"]},{"term":"Cambridge Platonists","def":"A group of 17th-century English philosophers and theologians at Cambridge who defended reason, tolerance and Platonic ideas within Christianity.","more":"Leading members were Benjamin Whichcote, Ralph Cudworth and Henry More. They opposed both Calvinist predestination and Hobbes's materialism, arguing that moral truths are eternal and knowable by reason. Cudworth's True Intellectual System of the Universe (1678) attacked atheism. Anne Conway, a friend of More's, developed her own original metaphysics.","etym":null,"example":"Arguing that the difference between right and wrong is as unchangeable as a mathematical truth, not just a command from God or the state.","notConfused":"Not followers of Plato only: they drew heavily on Plotinus and Neoplatonism as well.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Platonism","Neoplatonism","Moral rationalism"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["cambridge platonism","henry more","ralph cudworth","cudworth","benjamin whichcote","platonism"]},{"term":"Utopian socialism","def":"Early 19th-century socialist ideas, such as those of Robert Owen, Charles Fourier and Henri de Saint-Simon, that sought to build ideal cooperative communities.","more":"These thinkers responded to the hardships of early industrial capitalism with plans for model societies: Owen ran the New Lanark mills and founded New Harmony in Indiana, and Fourier designed communal 'phalansteries'. Marx and Engels respected them but labelled them 'utopian' because they relied on moral appeals rather than class struggle, contrasting them with their own 'scientific' socialism. Many of their experimental communities collapsed within a few years, though cooperative movements and ideas such as shorter working hours outlasted them.","etym":null,"example":"A factory owner building good housing, schools and shorter hours for workers, hoping to show the world a better way to live.","notConfused":"Not the same as utopianism in general: it refers to a specific group of early socialist thinkers.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Socialism","Utopia","Marxism"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["utopian socialist","robert owen","fourier","charles fourier","saint-simon","socialism"]},{"term":"Liberation theology","def":"A Christian movement, born in Latin America in the late 1960s, that reads the gospel as demanding liberation of the poor from social and economic oppression.","more":"Gustavo Gutiérrez's A Theology of Liberation (1971) is its founding text, with the idea of a 'preferential option for the poor'. Liberation theologians drew on some Marxist social analysis, which led to criticism from the Vatican in the 1980s. Related movements include Black theology in the USA, associated with James Cone, and feminist theology.","etym":null,"example":"A priest in a poor neighbourhood organising residents to demand clean water, seeing this as part of his faith, not separate from it.","notConfused":"Not the same as Marxism: liberation theologians use social analysis but remain grounded in Christian faith.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Marxism","Praxis","Distributive justice"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["liberation theologians","gustavo gutierrez","gutiérrez","preferential option for the poor","base communities","social justice"]},{"term":"Ecofeminism","def":"A movement linking the domination of women with the domination of nature, arguing that both stem from the same patterns of thought.","more":"The term was coined by Françoise d'Eaubonne in 1974. Philosophers such as Val Plumwood and Karen Warren argued that Western thought ranks reason over emotion, culture over nature and men over women, and that these hierarchies support each other. Critics within feminism worried that some versions risked identifying women with nature in an essentialist way.","etym":"Greek oikos, 'household', plus feminism.","example":"Arguing that treating forests as raw material and treating women's care work as worthless reflect the same contempt for what sustains life.","notConfused":"Not the claim that women are naturally closer to nature: many ecofeminists reject that idea.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Feminist philosophy","Environmental ethics","Care ethics"],"thinkers":[],"tags":["eco-feminism","ecofeminist","feminism","environmentalism","françoise d'eaubonne","carolyn merchant"]},{"term":"Romanticism","def":"A late 18th- and 19th-century movement in art and thought that prized emotion, imagination, nature, individuality and the sublime over Enlightenment rationalism.","more":"In Germany, the Jena circle around Friedrich Schlegel and Novalis developed Romantic philosophy in the 1790s, influencing Schelling, Hegel and Schleiermacher. In Britain, Wordsworth and Coleridge championed imagination and nature. Isaiah Berlin's The Roots of Romanticism argued that it transformed Western values by stressing will and self-expression. Critics have seen in it roots of both modern individualism and nationalism.","etym":"From 'romance', originally a story in the vernacular romance languages.","example":"A poet wandering alone in the mountains, valuing the overwhelming feelings this stirs over any scientific description.","notConfused":"Not the same as being romantic in love: the movement concerns imagination, emotion and nature in general.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Sublime","Genius","Enlightenment"],"thinkers":["schleiermacher","berlin"],"tags":["romantic movement","romantics","romantic","rousseau","schelling","emotion and imagination"]},{"term":"Aestheticism","def":"A late 19th-century movement, especially in Britain and France, that put beauty at the centre of life and art, apart from moral or social purpose.","more":"Drawing on the slogan 'art for art's sake', it was associated with Walter Pater, whose Studies in the History of the Renaissance (1873) urged readers 'to burn always with this hard, gem-like flame', and with Oscar Wilde, James McNeill Whistler and the decorative arts. Critics such as John Ruskin had argued that art and morality are linked, and Wilde's 1895 trials made the movement scandalous. Defenders saw it as a protest against Victorian moralism and industrial ugliness, and it shaped later movements in design and modernist art.","etym":"Greek aisthētikos, 'perceptive'.","example":"Designing your whole home, clothes and daily routine as works of art, valuing them for their beauty alone.","notConfused":"Not the same as aesthetics, the philosophical study of beauty and art: aestheticism is a movement advocating a way of life.","field":"Schools and movements","related":["Art for art's sake","Camp","Romanticism"],"thinkers":["wilde"],"tags":["aesthetic movement","art for art's sake","oscar wilde","walter pater","pater","decadence"]},{"term":"Moral pluralism","def":"Several basic moral considerations count, and none always wins.","more":"Moral pluralism says that there is more than one basic source of right and wrong. Results matter, but so do promises, rights, fairness, gratitude and character, and these cannot all be reduced to one master principle. When they conflict, no fixed formula settles it: we must use judgment in the particular case. The classic version is W. Also called Value pluralism. Held by W. D. Ross, Isaiah Berlin, Thomas Nagel, Robert Audi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Rossian pluralism","Ethical intuitionism","Reflective equilibrium"],"thinkers":["ross","berlin","nagel"],"position":"moral_pluralism","tags":["value pluralism","ethical pluralism","pluralism","isaiah berlin","ross","moral conflict"]},{"term":"Moral constructivism","def":"Moral truths are real, but they are built by correct reasoning rather than discovered out there.","more":"Moral constructivism says that moral claims can be true, but what makes them true is not a fact independent of all minds. Instead, moral truths are what would come out of an idealised process of reasoning or agreement: what rational agents would accept, or what follows from the standpoint of anyone who values things at all. Kantian constructivists like Christine Korsgaard and John Rawls (in his later work) argue that the basic principles bind every reasoner, since they are built into what it is to act rationally. Humean constructivists like Sharon Street argue that what you have reason to do depends on the values you already hold, so truths may differ between very different agents. Also called Constructivism in ethics. Held by Christine Korsgaard, John Rawls, Sharon Street.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractualism","Moral rationalism"],"thinkers":["korsgaard","rawls","street"],"position":"moral_constructivism","tags":["constructivism","ethical constructivism","metaethical constructivism","rawls","korsgaard","street","constructivism in ethics"]},{"term":"Moral relativism","def":"Moral claims are true or false only relative to a culture’s or person’s standards.","more":"Moral relativism says that whether a moral claim is true depends on some framework: the standards of a culture, a society, or an individual. No framework is the single correct one. So “eating meat is wrong” might be true relative to one culture’s standards and false relative to another’s. Relativism is different from the plain fact that cultures disagree, which everyone accepts; it is a claim about what makes moral statements true. Also called Metaethical relativism. Held by Gilbert Harman, David B. Wong.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Expressivism"],"thinkers":["harman","wong"],"position":"moral_relativism","tags":["ethical relativism","relativism","cultural relativism","moral subjectivism","who's to say","metaethical relativism"]},{"term":"Moral error theory","def":"Moral claims try to state objective facts, but there are none, so they are all untrue.","more":"Moral error theory says that ordinary moral judgments aim to state objective facts (that cruelty really is wrong, whatever anyone thinks), but no such facts exist. So every positive moral claim is untrue, rather as claims about witches are untrue. The view was made famous by J. L. Also called Error theory. Held by J. L. Mackie, Richard Joyce, Jonas Olson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral skepticism"],"thinkers":["mackie","joyce","olson_jonas"],"position":"moral_error_theory","tags":["error theory","mackie","j. l. mackie","argument from queerness","moral anti-realism","nihilism"]},{"term":"Ethical intuitionism","def":"Some basic moral truths are self-evident and known by rational intuition.","more":"Ethical intuitionism holds that we know some moral truths directly, without inferring them from anything else. Just as you can see that “if A is taller than B and B is taller than C, then A is taller than C” simply by understanding it, a mature, reflective person can see that it is wrong to cause pain for fun, or that promises create obligations. Such intuitions are not hunches or gut feelings: they are intellectual seemings that hold up under careful thought. Classic intuitionists include Henry Sidgwick, G. Also called Intuitionism (in ethics). Held by W. D. Ross, G. E. Moore, H. A. Prichard, Michael Huemer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral realism","Moral pluralism","Rossian pluralism"],"thinkers":["ross","moore","huemer"],"position":"ethical_intuitionism","tags":["intuitionism","moral intuitionism","moral intuition","self-evidence","ross","moore","intuitionism (in ethics)"]},{"term":"Moral sense theory","def":"We know right and wrong through feelings of approval and disapproval, a kind of moral sense.","more":"Moral sense theory holds that moral distinctions are known through feeling rather than pure reason. Just as we perceive colours through sight, we perceive virtue and vice through a sense that responds with approval or disapproval when we contemplate actions and characters. The view was developed in the 18th century by the Earl of Shaftesbury and Francis Hutcheson, and taken up, with changes, by David Hume, who argued that reason alone can never tell us what to value. Adam Smith gave a related account built on sympathy and the imagined “impartial spectator”. Also called Sentimentalism. Held by Francis Hutcheson, Earl of Shaftesbury, David Hume, Adam Smith.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Expressivism","Care ethics"],"thinkers":["hume","smith","slote"],"position":"moral_sense_theory","tags":["moral sense","hutcheson","francis hutcheson","shaftesbury","hume","sentimentalism"]},{"term":"Moral skepticism","def":"We do not have, and perhaps cannot have, moral knowledge.","more":"Moral skepticism, in the sense used here, is a view about knowledge: whatever the truth about right and wrong, our moral beliefs are not justified well enough to count as knowledge. The ancient Pyrrhonist sceptics, as reported by Sextus Empiricus, set opposing arguments about good and bad against each other and suspended judgment. Walter Sinnott-Armstrong defends a modern version: we cannot rule out rival hypotheses, such as moral nihilism (the view that nothing is right or wrong), so we cannot be fully justified in our moral beliefs. Moral skepticism differs from error theory, which confidently says moral claims are false; the sceptic only says we do not know. Also called Moral scepticism (epistemic). Held by Sextus Empiricus, Walter Sinnott-Armstrong.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral error theory"],"thinkers":["sextus"],"position":"moral_skepticism","tags":["moral scepticism","ethical skepticism","moral nihilism","error theory","moral knowledge","moral scepticism (epistemic)"]},{"term":"Autonomy of morality","def":"Right and wrong do not depend on God’s will; they would hold whether or not God commanded them.","more":"The autonomy of morality is the view that morality stands on its own: what makes acts right or wrong does not depend on anyone’s commands, God’s included. If God exists and commands kindness, that is because kindness is already good, and a good God would recognise it. People of many beliefs hold this view: atheists, who think morality needs no God, and many religious believers, who think God’s goodness consists in perfectly knowing and loving what is independently good. The view draws on the first horn of the dilemma Socrates put to Euthyphro in Plato’s dialogue. Also called Independence of morality from God. Held by Immanuel Kant, Erik Wielenberg, James Rachels.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral realism"],"thinkers":["kant","rachels"],"position":"autonomy_of_morality","tags":["euthyphro dilemma","autonomy of ethics","secular morality","independence of morality from religion","morality without god","independence of morality from god"]},{"term":"Modified divine command theory","def":"Goodness is grounded in God’s perfectly good nature, and moral duties in the commands of that good God.","more":"Modified divine command theory, developed by Robert Merrihew Adams, aims to keep what is attractive about grounding morality in God while avoiding the charge of arbitrariness. It separates goodness from obligation. Goodness is grounded in God’s nature: God is the Good itself, and other things are good insofar as they resemble God. Obligation, what we are required to do, is grounded in God’s commands, as duties in a relationship are created by the reasonable demands of the other party. Held by Robert Merrihew Adams, William Lane Craig, C. Stephen Evans.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Moral argument","Classical theism","Divine command theory","Natural law theory"],"thinkers":["adams_rm","craig"],"position":"modified_divine_command_theory","tags":["divine command theory","robert adams","adams","divine nature","euthyphro dilemma","god's commands"]},{"term":"Natural law theory","def":"Right and wrong follow from human nature and the goods it seeks, which God made and reason can know.","more":"Natural law theory, in its classic form from Thomas Aquinas, holds that morality is rooted in human nature. Humans naturally seek certain basic goods, such as life, knowledge, friendship, family and living in community, and right action is action that respects and pursues these goods reasonably. Because God created human nature, the natural law is a sharing in God’s eternal law. But its content is not arbitrary: God commands what is good because it fits the nature God created, and reason can discover it without revelation. Also called Thomistic natural law, Natural law ethics. Held by Thomas Aquinas, John Finnis, Mark C. Murphy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism","Moral argument","Virtue ethics","Doctrine of double effect"],"thinkers":["aquinas","finnis"],"position":"natural_law_theory","tags":["natural law","natural law ethics","aquinas","human nature","basic goods","lex naturalis","thomistic natural law"]},{"term":"Two-level consequentialism","def":"The right act has the best results, but in daily life you should mostly follow good rules and habits.","more":"Two-level consequentialism separates what makes acts right from how we should decide. What makes an act right is still its results. But people who try to calculate results every time tend to do badly: they lack time and information, and they talk themselves into self-serving exceptions. So at the everyday, “intuitive” level we should rely on firm rules and dispositions, like honesty and keeping promises, which reliably lead to good outcomes. Also called Two-level utilitarianism, Sophisticated consequentialism. Held by R. M. Hare, Henry Sidgwick, Peter Railton, Peter Singer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Act consequentialism","Rule consequentialism","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["hare","sidgwick","singer"],"position":"two_level_consequentialism","tags":["two-level utilitarianism","hare","r. m. hare","rules of thumb","critical level","intuitive level","sophisticated consequentialism"]},{"term":"Act consequentialism","def":"Each act is right or wrong depending only on its own results.","more":"Act consequentialism is the simplest form of consequentialism: an act is right if and only if it produces at least as good results as any alternative available to you at that moment. Each act is judged on its own merits. Rules such as “don’t lie” are useful rules of thumb, but if breaking one would truly lead to better results in a particular case, then breaking it is right. When its measure of good is wellbeing counted equally for all, it is act utilitarianism, defended by J. Also called Act utilitarianism (its best-known form). Held by J. J. C. Smart, Shelly Kagan, Peter Singer, Alastair Norcross.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Maximising consequentialism","Equivalence thesis"],"thinkers":["smart","kagan","singer","norcross","feldman"],"position":"act_consequentialism","tags":["act utilitarianism","act-consequentialism","direct consequentialism","utilitarianism","consequences","act utilitarianism (its best-known form)"]},{"term":"Rule consequentialism","def":"An act is right if it follows the rules whose general acceptance would have the best results.","more":"Rule consequentialism judges acts by rules, and rules by results. An act is right if it is allowed by the set of rules that would have the best consequences if (nearly) everyone accepted them. The ideal code would include rules against murder, theft, lying and breaking promises, and a rule requiring us to prevent disasters. Brad Hooker, its leading defender, argues that it fits our moral convictions better than act consequentialism: it forbids framing innocents or harvesting organs, because a society that accepted such practices would be worse. Also called Rule utilitarianism. Held by Brad Hooker, Richard Brandt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Two-level consequentialism","Consequentialism","Contractualism"],"thinkers":["hooker"],"position":"rule_consequentialism","tags":["rule utilitarianism","rule-consequentialism","brad hooker","hooker","rules","indirect consequentialism"]},{"term":"Maximising consequentialism","def":"Only the best available act is right; anything less is wrong.","more":"Maximising consequentialism says that you are required to do the best you can: an act is right only if no alternative open to you would have produced a better outcome. This is the classical form found in Bentham, Mill and Sidgwick, who spoke of producing “the greatest happiness” or the greatest balance of good over bad. It has the attraction of being simple and clear. It is also very demanding: on this view, spending an evening watching films when you could have done more good is, strictly, wrong. Also called Maximising utilitarianism. Held by Jeremy Bentham, Henry Sidgwick, Shelly Kagan, Peter Singer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Act consequentialism","Strict duty of beneficence"],"thinkers":["bentham","sidgwick","kagan","singer"],"position":"maximising_consequentialism","tags":["maximizing consequentialism","maximization","optimific","demandingness","best outcome","maximising utilitarianism"]},{"term":"Scalar consequentialism","def":"Acts are only better or worse; there is no sharp line between required and not required.","more":"Scalar consequentialism says that the basic moral facts are comparative: one act is better than another, to some degree, depending on its results. Words like “required”, “permitted” and “wrong” draw sharp lines that the underlying reality does not contain. Moving from giving nothing to charity to giving a little, to giving more, makes things progressively better; there is no special point at which you cross from wrong to right. Alastair Norcross is its best-known defender. Also called Scalar utilitarianism. Held by Alastair Norcross.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Act consequentialism"],"thinkers":["norcross"],"position":"scalar_consequentialism","tags":["scalar utilitarianism","degrees of rightness","better and worse","alastair norcross","norcross"]},{"term":"Satisficing consequentialism","def":"An act is right if its results are good enough, even when better was possible.","more":"Satisficing consequentialism says that you are not required to produce the best possible outcome, only one that is good enough. The word “satisfice”, a blend of “satisfy” and “suffice”, comes from the economist Herbert Simon, who used it for decision-making that settles for a satisfactory option. Michael Slote brought it into ethics in a 1984 symposium with Philip Pettit, who replied to him. Just as a sensible person need not hunt for the very best sandwich when a good one is at hand, a moral agent need not squeeze out every last unit of good. Held by Michael Slote.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Agent-centred prerogative","Fair-share view"],"thinkers":["slote"],"position":"satisficing_consequentialism","tags":["satisficing","good enough","michael slote","slote","demandingness","satisficing utilitarianism"]},{"term":"Objective list theory","def":"Some things, like friendship and knowledge, are good for you whether or not you want or enjoy them.","more":"Objective list theory holds that a life goes well for a person to the extent that it contains certain objectively good things, such as friendship, knowledge, achievement, health, pleasure and the exercise of one’s abilities. These things are good for you whether or not you want them or enjoy them. The name comes from Derek Parfit’s classification of theories of wellbeing. Defenders differ about what is on the list: John Finnis lists basic goods like life, knowledge, play, friendship and religion; Guy Fletcher and others defend different lists. Held by John Finnis, Guy Fletcher, Thomas Hurka, Martha Nussbaum.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Hybrid theory of wellbeing","Natural law theory","Virtue ethics","Objectivism about meaning"],"thinkers":["finnis","hurka","nussbaum","arneson"],"position":"objective_list_theory","tags":["objective list","objective goods","wellbeing","well-being","perfectionism","parfit"]},{"term":"Desire-satisfaction theory","def":"Your life goes well when what you want actually comes about.","more":"Desire-satisfaction theory says that what is good for you is getting what you want, in the sense that the state of affairs you desire actually obtains, whether or not you know about it or feel pleasure. Simple versions count your actual desires; “informed desire” versions count the desires you would have if you were fully informed and thinking clearly, as defended in different forms by Richard Brandt, James Griffin and Peter Railton. Preference utilitarians such as R. M. Also called Preference satisfaction theory, Desire theory of wellbeing, Desire theory of well-being. Held by R. M. Hare, Richard Brandt, Chris Heathwood.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Reasons internalism","Subjectivism about meaning","Hybrid theory of well-being"],"thinkers":["hare"],"position":"desire_satisfaction_theory","tags":["desire satisfaction","preference satisfaction","desire fulfilment","preference utilitarianism","wellbeing","preference satisfaction theory","desire theory of wellbeing","desire theory of well-being"]},{"term":"Hybrid theory of wellbeing","def":"A life goes best when you enjoy things that are really good.","more":"Hybrid theories say that neither experience alone nor objective goods alone make a life go well; what matters is the combination. The best-known version says wellbeing consists in enjoying, or taking pleasure in, things that are objectively good, such as real friendships, knowledge and achievement. Derek Parfit suggested this kind of view in an appendix to Reasons and Persons, Robert Merrihew Adams defended wellbeing as enjoyment of the excellent, and Shelly Kagan argued for “well-being as enjoying the good”. The view aims to capture the truth in hedonism (a life you don’t enjoy is not going well for you) and the truth in objective list theory (pleasure in fake or worthless things is not enough). Also called Enjoying the good. Held by Shelly Kagan, Robert Merrihew Adams, Derek Parfit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Objective list theory","Hedonism"],"thinkers":["kagan","adams_rm","parfit"],"position":"hybrid_theory_of_wellbeing","tags":["hybrid theory","hybrid view","wellbeing","well-being","enjoyment","objective goods","enjoying the good"]},{"term":"Total view","def":"One outcome is better than another if it contains more total wellbeing, adding across everyone who exists.","more":"The total view in population ethics (the study of how to compare outcomes containing different numbers of people) says that the value of an outcome is the sum of the wellbeing of everyone in it. Adding a person whose life is worth living adds value. Henry Sidgwick accepted this form of utilitarianism. Its most notorious implication was named by Derek Parfit the “repugnant conclusion”: for any population of very happy people, there is some much larger population of people with lives barely worth living that is better. Also called Totalism, Total utilitarianism. Held by Henry Sidgwick, Michael Huemer, Torbjörn Tännsjö.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Hedonism","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["sidgwick","huemer"],"position":"total_view","tags":["totalism","total utilitarianism","repugnant conclusion","population ethics","parfit"]},{"term":"Average view","def":"One outcome is better than another if people in it are better off on average.","more":"The average view says that what makes an outcome good is the average level of wellbeing in it, not the total. So a smaller world of very happy people is better than a vast world of people whose lives are barely worth living, which avoids the repugnant conclusion. John Harsanyi gave an influential argument for using average utility to judge social arrangements: if you did not know which person you would be, it would be rational to choose the society with the highest average. In population ethics, though, the average view has few defenders in its pure form, because it has odd results when the number of people changes. Also called Average utilitarianism. Held by John Harsanyi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"average_view","tags":["averagism","average utilitarianism","population ethics","mere addition","average wellbeing"]},{"term":"Critical-level view","def":"A new life adds value only if its wellbeing is above a certain positive level.","more":"Critical-level views say that adding a person to the world makes it better only if that person’s wellbeing is above a set “critical level”, which is above the point at which life is barely worth living. A life below that level, even if still worth living for the person, counts as neutral or negative for the value of the outcome. This blocks the repugnant conclusion: a huge population of lives barely worth living adds little or nothing, or even lowers the value. The economists Charles Blackorby, Walter Bossert and David Donaldson developed and defended critical-level utilitarianism. Also called Critical-level utilitarianism. Held by Charles Blackorby, Walter Bossert, David Donaldson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Total view"],"thinkers":[],"position":"critical_level_view","tags":["critical level","critical-level utilitarianism","population ethics","repugnant conclusion","blackorby"]},{"term":"Person-affecting view","def":"An outcome can only be better if it is better for someone.","more":"Person-affecting views say that morality is about making people better off, not about making more good in the abstract. An outcome is better only if it is better for some person. Since a merely possible person who is never created is not worse off for not existing, we have no duty to create happy people just to increase the total. Jan Narveson put it memorably: we are in favour of making people happy, but neutral about making happy people. Also called The person-affecting restriction. Held by Jan Narveson, Melinda Roberts.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractualism"],"thinkers":["narveson"],"position":"person_affecting_view","tags":["person-affecting","person affecting principle","non-identity problem","population ethics","parfit","narveson","the person-affecting restriction"]},{"term":"Threshold deontology","def":"Duties hold against ordinary gains, but can give way when a catastrophe is at stake.","more":"Threshold deontology says that duties such as not killing or torturing the innocent are real constraints: you may not break them simply because doing so would produce somewhat better results, such as saving five people at the cost of one. But constraints are not absolute. Once the stakes pass a very high threshold, such as preventing the deaths of thousands, the duty can be overridden. Michael S. Also called Moderate deontology. Held by Michael S. Moore, Charles Fried, F. M. Kamm.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Rossian pluralism"],"thinkers":["michael_moore","fried","kamm"],"position":"threshold_deontology","tags":["moderate deontology","catastrophe","deontology","constraints"]},{"term":"Rossian pluralism","def":"There are several basic duties, each binding unless outweighed by a stronger duty in the situation.","more":"Rossian pluralism is W. D. Ross’s theory of prima facie duties. A prima facie duty is a duty that holds unless a stronger duty outweighs it in the circumstances. Also called Prima facie duties, Ross’s intuitionism. Held by W. D. Ross, Robert Audi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral pluralism","Ethical intuitionism","Threshold deontology"],"thinkers":["ross"],"position":"rossian_pluralism","tags":["ross","w. d. ross","prima facie duties","pluralistic deontology","intuitionism","the right and the good","ross’s intuitionism"]},{"term":"Moral absolutism","def":"Some acts are always wrong, whatever the consequences.","more":"Moral absolutism, in the sense used here, holds that certain kinds of act, such as deliberately killing the innocent, torture, or (for some) lying, are wrong without exception. No amount of good that might result can justify them. ) Thomas Aquinas, Immanuel Kant and G. E. Also called Deontological absolutism, Absolutism about constraints. Held by Thomas Aquinas, Immanuel Kant, G. E. M. Anscombe, John Finnis.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Doctrine of double effect","Natural law theory"],"thinkers":["aquinas","kant","anscombe","finnis"],"position":"moral_absolutism","tags":["absolutism","absolute prohibitions","exceptionless rules","intrinsically evil acts","deontology","deontological absolutism","absolutism about constraints"]},{"term":"Doing/allowing distinction","def":"Causing harm is, in itself, morally worse than allowing harm to happen.","more":"The doctrine of doing and allowing says that there is a morally important difference between doing harm (for example, killing) and allowing harm (for example, letting someone die). Other things being equal, the duty not to harm is stronger than the duty to help. Philippa Foot defended this in terms of negative duties (not to harm) and positive duties (to aid), arguing that negative duties are generally stricter. Warren Quinn, F. Also called Doctrine of doing and allowing, Killing vs letting die. Held by Philippa Foot, Warren Quinn, F. M. Kamm, Fiona Woollard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Doctrine of double effect","Negative-duties view"],"thinkers":["foot","quinn_warren","kamm"],"position":"doing_allowing_distinction","tags":["doing and allowing","doing versus allowing","killing vs letting die","acts and omissions","negative duties","doctrine of doing and allowing"]},{"term":"Equivalence thesis","def":"Killing and letting die are equally bad in themselves, other things being equal.","more":"The equivalence thesis says that the difference between doing and allowing harm has no moral weight in itself. If two cases are alike in every other way (motive, risk, cost, consent), then killing someone is no worse than letting them die. James Rachels defended it with his bathtub cases, in a famous paper on euthanasia arguing that letting a suffering patient die slowly is not morally better than ending their life quickly. Shelly Kagan argued more generally that common-sense distinctions like doing and allowing cannot bear the weight placed on them. Also called Moral equivalence of killing and letting die. Held by James Rachels, Shelly Kagan, Peter Singer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Act consequentialism","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["rachels","kagan","singer"],"position":"equivalence_thesis","tags":["killing and letting die","rachels","james rachels","acts and omissions","active and passive euthanasia","moral equivalence of killing and letting die"]},{"term":"Principle of permissible harm","def":"You may redirect an existing threat so it harms fewer, but not use a new person to stop it.","more":"The principle of permissible harm, developed by F. M. Kamm, tries to explain trolley-type cases better than double effect. Roughly, it says that you may harm someone when the harm comes about through the very good you produce, or through means that do not themselves harm, such as redirecting a threat away from more people. Also called Redirection principle. Held by F. M. Kamm, Judith Jarvis Thomson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Doctrine of double effect"],"thinkers":["kamm","thomson"],"position":"principle_of_permissible_harm","tags":["trolley problem","kamm","frances kamm","redirecting harm","permissible harm","redirection principle"]},{"term":"Bodily autonomy view","def":"A right to life does not include a right to use someone else’s body to stay alive.","more":"This view, made famous by Judith Jarvis Thomson’s 1971 paper, says that a person’s right to life is a right not to be killed unjustly, not a right to be given whatever they need to survive. In particular, it does not give anyone a right to use another person’s body without their consent. Thomson’s violinist case is meant to show this: you may unplug yourself, even though he will die, because he has no right to use your kidneys. Staying plugged in would be very generous, the act of a “Good Samaritan”, but it is not owed. Also called Thomson’s argument from bodily autonomy. Held by Judith Jarvis Thomson, F. M. Kamm, David Boonin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Doing/allowing distinction","Negative-duties view"],"thinkers":["thomson","kamm"],"position":"bodily_autonomy_view","tags":["bodily autonomy","thomson","judith jarvis thomson","violinist","abortion","bodily integrity","thomson’s argument from bodily autonomy"]},{"term":"Responsibility view","def":"You may owe someone the use of your body if you are responsible for their depending on you.","more":"The responsibility view grants that the violinist has no right to your body, since you were kidnapped and did nothing to create his need. But it holds that things change when you are responsible for someone’s being in a position where they depend on you. Just as someone who knocks a person into a pond acquires a special duty to rescue them, a person who knowingly brings about another’s dependence may owe them support, even support involving the use of their body. In the debate over Thomson’s argument about abortion, critics such as Francis Beckwith have pressed this point, arguing that the violinist case matches only cases where no voluntary act created the dependence. Also called The responsibility objection. Held by Francis Beckwith.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Special obligations"],"thinkers":[],"position":"responsibility_view","tags":["responsibility objection","abortion","violinist","consent","tacit consent","responsibility","the responsibility objection"]},{"term":"Strict duty of beneficence","def":"If you can prevent something very bad without sacrificing anything comparably important, you must.","more":"This view, defended most famously by Peter Singer, says that we are seriously required to help those in need, and that this requirement is very demanding. In his 1972 paper, Singer argued that if we can prevent something bad from happening without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, we ought to do it. Since money spent on luxuries could prevent deaths from poverty and disease, we must give much of it away. Distance and the number of other people who could also help make no moral difference. Also called Singer’s principle, Demanding beneficence. Held by Peter Singer, Peter Unger, William Godwin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Maximising consequentialism","Impartialism","Equal consideration of interests"],"thinkers":["singer","unger","godwin"],"position":"strict_duty_of_beneficence","tags":["beneficence","peter singer","singer","drowning child","famine affluence and morality","duty to aid","singer’s principle","demanding beneficence"]},{"term":"Imperfect duty of beneficence","def":"You must make helping others one of your ends, but you have latitude in how, when and whom.","more":"Kant distinguished perfect duties, which forbid specific acts (like lying or breaking promises), from imperfect duties, which require you to adopt an end without fixing exactly what you must do. Beneficence, helping others in need, is an imperfect duty: you must make others’ happiness one of your goals, and you may not adopt a policy of never helping. But you have “latitude” in how much to give, when and to whom. This avoids the extreme demands of Singer’s principle while still saying that helping is a real duty, not just a nice extra. Also called Kantian beneficence. Held by Immanuel Kant, Barbara Herman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology"],"thinkers":["kant"],"position":"imperfect_duty_of_beneficence","tags":["beneficence","imperfect duty","kant","latitude","charity","duty to help","kantian beneficence"]},{"term":"Fair-share view","def":"You must do your fair share of helping, not make up for others who do less.","more":"The fair-share view, defended by Liam Murphy, says that the demands of beneficence should be judged by what each person would have to do if everyone did their part. If ending a great evil requires each well-off person to give a certain amount, then that amount is your duty. You are not required to make up the shortfall when others fail to do their share, though doing more is admirable. Murphy argues that this is the right response to the demandingness problem: it keeps beneficence a serious duty, while recognising that it is unfair for the burden to grow simply because others shirk. Also called Cooperative principle of beneficence. Held by Liam Murphy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Satisficing consequentialism","Imperfect duty of beneficence"],"thinkers":[],"position":"fair_share_view","tags":["fair share","liam murphy","murphy","demandingness","cooperative principle","duty to aid","cooperative principle of beneficence"]},{"term":"Negative-duties view","def":"We owe others only that we not harm them; helping is good but never required.","more":"The negative-duties view says that the only duties we owe others are negative ones: not to kill, harm, steal from or deceive them. Positive duties to help, like giving to charity, are not strictly owed, even if helping is admirable and generous. Jan Narveson defends this view from a libertarian standpoint, arguing that everyone has a right to their own life and property, and that any enforceable duty to aid others would violate that right. On this view, refusing to donate to save a stranger far away does not wrong them, though it may reflect a lack of generosity. Also called Libertarian view of aid. Held by Jan Narveson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Doing/allowing distinction","Bodily autonomy view"],"thinkers":["narveson"],"position":"negative_duties_only","tags":["negative duties","libertarianism","no duty to aid","non-harm","harm principle","narveson","libertarian view of aid"]},{"term":"Equal consideration of interests","def":"Equal interests count equally, whichever species the being belongs to.","more":"The principle of equal consideration of interests says that we must give equal weight to the similar interests of all beings affected by our actions. If a pig and a person feel the same pain, the pain matters equally. Jeremy Bentham put the key question as whether animals can suffer, not whether they can reason or talk. Peter Singer developed the principle in Animal Liberation, arguing that giving less weight to the interests of other species simply because of their species is “speciesism”, a prejudice like racism or sexism. Held by Peter Singer, Jeremy Bentham, James Rachels.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Impartialism","Strict duty of beneficence"],"thinkers":["singer","bentham","rachels"],"position":"equal_consideration_of_interests","tags":["equal consideration","singer","peter singer","speciesism","animal liberation","sentience"]},{"term":"Hierarchical view of moral status","def":"Animals matter in their own right, but beings with richer mental lives matter more.","more":"The hierarchical view says that animals have genuine moral status: their suffering matters in itself, not just because of its effects on humans. But moral status comes in degrees, depending on psychological capacities such as self-awareness, the ability to plan for the future and to form deep relationships. So a given amount of pain counts for more in a person than in a pig, and more in a pig than in a fish. Shelly Kagan defends this view in How to Count Animals, More or Less, arguing that it fits widely shared intuitions better than either strict equality or the denial of animal status. Also called Hierarchical view, Degrees of moral status. Held by Shelly Kagan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Animal rights view"],"thinkers":["kagan"],"position":"hierarchical_view_of_moral_status","tags":["hierarchy of moral status","degrees of moral status","moral status","animals","two-tier view","hierarchical view"]},{"term":"Animal rights view","def":"Many animals have rights, such as a right not to be used merely as resources.","more":"The animal rights view, developed by Tom Regan, holds that animals who are “subjects-of-a-life” (beings with beliefs, desires, memory, a sense of their own future, and a welfare that matters to them) have inherent value and moral rights. Like humans, they may not be treated merely as means to others’ ends, whatever the benefits. This is a deontological view: it rejects the utilitarian approach of weighing animal suffering against human benefit, because rights cannot be traded away for greater total good. Regan concluded that factory farming, animal experimentation and commercial hunting should be abolished, not merely reformed. Also called Rights view. Held by Tom Regan, Christine Korsgaard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology"],"thinkers":["korsgaard"],"position":"animal_rights_view","tags":["animal rights","tom regan","regan","subject-of-a-life","abolitionism","inherent value","rights view"]},{"term":"Indirect duty view","def":"We have no duties to animals themselves, only duties about them, grounded in effects on people.","more":"The indirect duty view says that animals do not have moral standing in their own right. We should not be cruel to them, but the reason is not that the animal is wronged. Kant held that cruelty to animals is wrong because it damages our humane character and makes us more likely to be cruel to people. Peter Carruthers defended a contractualist version: morality is an agreement among rational agents, and animals cannot be parties to it, so our duties regarding them come from human concerns, such as other people’s feelings and the risk of hardening our character. Also called Indirect duties to animals. Held by Immanuel Kant, Peter Carruthers.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractarianism","Contractualism"],"thinkers":["kant","carruthers"],"position":"indirect_duty_view","tags":["indirect duties","kant","cruelty to animals","animals as means","indirect duty","indirect duties to animals"]},{"term":"Special obligations","def":"Relationships like parenthood and friendship create real duties toward particular people.","more":"The view that there are special obligations holds that our relationships with particular people, such as our children, parents, friends and fellow members of communities, give us duties toward them that we do not have toward strangers. These are not just useful habits: they are genuine moral requirements, valuable in themselves. Bernard Williams famously argued that a man who saves his drowning wife rather than a stranger should not need to consult an impartial principle; if he did, he would have “one thought too many”. Samuel Scheffler and Simon Keller have defended accounts of why relationships generate reasons. Also called Associative duties, Partialism. Held by Bernard Williams, Simon Keller, Diane Jeske.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Care ethics","Graded love","Responsibility view"],"thinkers":["williams"],"position":"special_obligations","tags":["special duties","associative duties","partiality","family obligations","loyalty","relationships","partialism"]},{"term":"Agent-centred prerogative","def":"You are permitted, though not required, to give extra weight to your own life and loves.","more":"An agent-centred prerogative is a permission to give your own interests, projects and relationships more weight than they would have from an impartial point of view. Samuel Scheffler introduced the idea as part of a “hybrid” theory: morality does not require you always to produce the best outcome, because you may give somewhat greater weight to your own concerns. But you are permitted to do the best, too, if you choose. So you may save your own child rather than two strangers, and you also would not act wrongly if you saved the two. Also called Agent-relative permission. Held by Samuel Scheffler.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Satisficing consequentialism","Special obligations"],"thinkers":["scheffler"],"position":"agent_centred_prerogative","tags":["agent-centered prerogative","personal prerogative","scheffler","partiality","demandingness","agent-relative permission"]},{"term":"Impartialism","def":"From the moral point of view, everyone’s interests count equally, including strangers’.","more":"Impartialism says that, morally speaking, no one counts for more because they are related to you, live near you or share your nationality. Everyone’s wellbeing matters equally. William Godwin gave a notorious example: if you could save either Archbishop Fénelon, whose work would benefit thousands, or his chambermaid, who might be your mother, you should save Fénelon. The Chinese philosopher Mozi taught “impartial care” (jian ai), arguing that partiality causes conflict. Also called Strict impartiality. Held by William Godwin, Mozi, Peter Singer, Henry Sidgwick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Strict duty of beneficence","Equal consideration of interests"],"thinkers":["godwin","mozi","singer","sidgwick"],"position":"impartialism","tags":["impartiality","impartial","equal concern","universalism","point of view of the universe","strict impartiality"]},{"term":"Graded love","def":"Care rightly begins with family and extends outward, in decreasing degrees, to all.","more":"Graded love is the Confucian view that care properly differs in degree depending on relationship. Moral development starts with filial piety (love and respect for parents) and care within the family, and then extends outward to neighbours, community and all people. Mencius defended this against Mozi’s teaching of impartial care, arguing that loving everyone equally ignores the special place of one’s parents. But graded love is not indifference to strangers: the point is that the capacity for care, learned at home, can be extended outward. Also called Love with distinctions, Confucian partiality. Held by Confucius, Mencius.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Special obligations","Care ethics","Virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius"],"position":"graded_love","tags":["love with distinctions","confucian love","partiality","mencius","ren","confucian partiality"]},{"term":"Moral rationalism","def":"Basic moral truths are known by reason, and moral requirements bind anyone who can reason.","more":"Moral rationalism makes reason, not feeling or observation, the source of morality. In method, it says the most basic moral principles can be known a priori (by thinking, without needing experience) and should govern our judgments about cases. Henry Sidgwick, in The Methods of Ethics (1874), sought a few self-evident axioms, such as that one person’s good counts no more than another’s from the point of view of the universe, and let them overrule common-sense rules. Kant derived duties from the categorical imperative, a command of reason binding unconditionally. Also called Rationalism about morality. Held by Immanuel Kant, Henry Sidgwick, Thomas Nagel, Derek Parfit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Ethical intuitionism","Moral constructivism","Moral realism","Deontology"],"thinkers":["kant","sidgwick","nagel","parfit","korsgaard"],"position":"moral_rationalism","tags":["ethical rationalism","rationalism","kant","moral reason","categorical requirements","rationalism about morality"]},{"term":"Eudaimonism","def":"A life goes well when you flourish by developing and exercising your human abilities well.","more":"Eudaimonism comes from the Greek word eudaimonia, usually translated as happiness or flourishing. Aristotle argued that every living thing has a characteristic way of thriving: an oak flourishes by growing strong and producing acorns. Humans flourish by exercising what is distinctive in us, especially reason, in an excellent way over a whole life. That means acting with virtues (good traits of character) such as courage, justice and practical wisdom, with friends and in a community. Also called Perfectionism about well-being, Flourishing view, Virtue pays. Held by Aristotle, Rosalind Hursthouse, Richard Kraut, Plato.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Essentialism about human nature","Objective list theory","Stoicism","Virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["aristotle","hursthouse","plato","foot"],"position":"eudaimonism","tags":["eudaemonism","eudaimonia","flourishing","aristotle","virtue ethics","happiness","perfectionism about well-being","flourishing view","virtue pays"]},{"term":"Reasons internalism","def":"Your reasons depend on what you care about, so someone who doesn’t care may lack moral reasons.","more":"Reasons internalism, made prominent by Bernard Williams, says that you have a reason to do something only if there is a sound route of reasoning from what you already care about (your “motivational set”) to doing it. Reasons are rooted in desires and commitments, as David Hume suggested. This means that most people do have reasons to be moral, since most care about others, fairness and how they are seen. But a person who truly cares about none of these may have no reason to be moral, though we can still call them cruel or wicked. Also called Humean theory of reasons, Internalism about reasons. Held by Bernard Williams, David Hume, Sharon Street.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Expressivism","Desire-satisfaction theory"],"thinkers":["williams","hume","street"],"position":"reasons_internalism","tags":["internal reasons","bernard williams","williams","humean theory of reasons","motivational set","externalism","internalism about reasons"]},{"term":"Contractarianism","def":"Morality is the set of rules it pays each of us to agree to for mutual benefit.","more":"Contractarianism says that morality consists of the rules that rational, self-interested people would agree to because everyone does better under them. Thomas Hobbes argued that without such rules, life would be a war of all against all, “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short”. So it pays each of us to accept rules against killing, stealing and cheating, as long as others do too. David Gauthier developed this into a detailed theory, arguing that a rational person would become a “constrained maximiser”, disposed to cooperate with other cooperators, because such people are trusted and do better than those known to cheat. Also called Hobbesian contractarianism. Held by Thomas Hobbes, David Gauthier, Jan Narveson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractualism","Ethical egoism","Indirect duty view"],"thinkers":["hobbes","narveson"],"position":"contractarianism","tags":["contractarian","hobbes","gauthier","david gauthier","social contract","mutual advantage","hobbesian contractarianism"]},{"term":"Ethical egoism","def":"You ought to do whatever is best for you in the long run.","more":"Ethical egoism says that each person ought to do what is in their own long-term interest. It is a claim about what we should do, not psychological egoism, the separate claim that people always do act selfishly. Ethical egoists usually argue that enlightened self-interest recommends honesty, cooperation and kindness much of the time, since these tend to pay. But when morality and self-interest truly conflict, self-interest wins. Also called Rational egoism. Held by Ayn Rand, Jesse Kalin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractarianism"],"thinkers":["rand"],"position":"ethical_egoism","tags":["egoism","self-interest","rational egoism","selfishness","ayn rand"]},{"term":"Prioritarianism","def":"Everyone’s wellbeing counts, but benefits to the worse-off count for more.","more":"Prioritarianism says that making people better off matters more the worse off they are. A given gain in wellbeing has more moral value if it goes to someone badly off than if it goes to someone already doing well. ”, distinguishing it from egalitarianism (the view that inequality itself is bad). For a prioritarian, what matters is not how people compare with each other but how badly off each person is in absolute terms. Also called The priority view. Held by Derek Parfit, Richard Arneson, Nils Holtug.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["parfit","arneson"],"position":"prioritarianism","tags":["priority view","prioritarian","priority to the worse off","parfit","equality","worst off","the priority view"]},{"term":"Self-referential altruism","def":"Everyone’s good counts, but those closer to you, like family and friends, count for more.","more":"Self-referential altruism is a name given by C. D. Broad to the view that we have duties to promote the good of others, but that the strength of these duties depends on how others are related to us. Your family, friends, neighbours and fellow citizens make stronger claims on you than strangers do, while strangers still count for something. Held by C. D. Broad.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Special obligations","Graded love","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["broad"],"position":"self_referential_altruism","tags":["broad","c. d. broad","partiality","graded concern","special obligations"]},{"term":"Global consequentialism","def":"Everything we can evaluate, from acts to motives, rules and characters, is judged directly by its results.","more":"Global consequentialism applies the test of results to everything, not just to acts. The best act is the act with the best results; the best motive is the motive whose having has the best results; the best rule, character or institution likewise. Philip Pettit and Michael Smith gave the view its name in 2000, and Toby Ord defended it in his doctoral work. It differs from act consequentialism, which puts acts first and judges other things by how they lead to good acts, and from rule consequentialism, which judges acts by the rules they follow. Held by Philip Pettit, Michael Smith, Toby Ord.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Two-level consequentialism","Act consequentialism","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["pettit"],"position":"global_consequentialism","tags":["global utilitarianism","pettit","smith","kagan","indirect consequentialism","consequentialism"]},{"term":"Negative utilitarianism","def":"The only thing we should try to bring about is less suffering; happiness has no positive weight of its own.","more":"Negative utilitarianism says that the aim of morality is to reduce suffering, not to increase happiness. In its strong form, only suffering counts: a world with no suffering at all is as good as any world can be. The idea is often traced to a note in Karl Popper’s The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945), where he suggested that public policy should aim to minimise avoidable suffering rather than maximise happiness, though Popper offered this as a principle for politics rather than a complete moral theory. The view appeals to those who think agony is far more urgent than pleasure, and that it is wrong to let some suffer so that others may have more fun. Also called Strong negative utilitarianism. Held by Karl Popper.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Suffering-focused ethics","Utilitarianism","Anti-natalism"],"thinkers":["popper"],"position":"negative_utilitarianism","tags":["negative utilitarian","minimize suffering","popper","suffering","antinatalism","strong negative utilitarianism"]},{"term":"Suffering-focused ethics","def":"Preventing suffering, especially extreme suffering, matters more than creating an equal amount of happiness.","more":"Suffering-focused ethics is a family of views that give priority to reducing suffering without claiming that only suffering counts. Some versions simply weigh suffering more heavily than happiness. Others say that extreme suffering, such as torture, cannot be outweighed by any amount of mild pleasure. Jamie Mayerfeld defended the special moral importance of relieving suffering in Suffering and Moral Responsibility (1999), and Magnus Vinding gathered the case in Suffering-Focused Ethics (2020). Also called Weak negative utilitarianism, Suffering-focused views. Held by Jamie Mayerfeld, Magnus Vinding.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Negative utilitarianism","Prioritarianism","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"suffering_focused_ethics","tags":["suffering focused ethics","s-risks","negative utilitarianism","reducing suffering","compassion","weak negative utilitarianism","suffering-focused views"]},{"term":"Variable value view","def":"Adding happy people adds value, but less and less as the population grows, so quality eventually beats quantity.","more":"The variable value view is a theory in population ethics, the study of how to compare outcomes containing different numbers of people. It tries to steer between the total view, which adds up all wellbeing, and the average view, which looks only at how well people live on average. On the variable value view, when a population is small, adding more people with good lives adds a lot of value, much as the total view says. But as the population grows, each extra person adds less, so that in a huge population quality matters far more than numbers. Also called Variable value theory. Held by Thomas Hurka, Yew-Kwang Ng.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Average view","Critical-level view"],"thinkers":["hurka"],"position":"variable_value_view","tags":["variable value","population ethics","ng","yew-kwang ng","hurka","repugnant conclusion","variable value theory"]},{"term":"The procreation asymmetry","def":"There is strong reason not to create a miserable life, but no comparable reason to create a happy one.","more":"The procreation asymmetry is the pair of beliefs most people hold about creating new people. First, if a child would have a life of terrible suffering, that is a strong reason not to create it, for the child’s own sake. Second, if a child would have a happy life, that is not in itself a reason to create it, or at most a weak one. Jan Narveson defended the second idea in 1967, and Jeff McMahan named the pair “the Asymmetry” in 1981. Also called The Asymmetry. Held by Jan Narveson, Melinda Roberts, Johann Frick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Person-affecting view","Procreative neutrality"],"thinkers":[],"position":"procreation_asymmetry","tags":["asymmetry","procreative asymmetry","procreation","population ethics","narveson","antinatalism","the asymmetry"]},{"term":"Full aggregation","def":"All benefits and harms add up, so enough small harms to many people can outweigh a great harm to one.","more":"Full aggregation is the view that the good and bad in an outcome are added up across people, with no limit on what can outweigh what. Many small benefits, if there are enough of them, can outweigh one large one. This is how classical utilitarians like Bentham and Sidgwick compared outcomes, and Alastair Norcross has defended it directly in “Comparing Harms: Headaches and Human Lives” (1997). It implies that, in Scanlon’s transmitter room case, enough viewers’ annoyance at missing a match could outweigh one man’s agony, and even that enough mild headaches could outweigh a death. Also called Aggregationism. Held by Alastair Norcross, Jeremy Bentham, Henry Sidgwick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Total view"],"thinkers":["norcross","bentham","sidgwick"],"position":"full_aggregation","tags":["aggregation","aggregationism","utilitarianism","transmission line","scanlon","summing harms"]},{"term":"Limited aggregation","def":"Harms can be added together only against harms of a comparable size, so no number of trivial harms outweighs a grave one.","more":"Limited aggregation tries to keep what is plausible in adding up numbers while avoiding its worst results. It says that harms or benefits can be combined only when they are relevant to each other, meaning roughly comparable in size. Many deaths outweigh one death, and many broken legs might outweigh one death, but no number of headaches or spoiled football matches can outweigh one person’s agony. F. Also called Partial aggregation, Relevance view. Held by F. M. Kamm, Alex Voorhoeve, T. M. Scanlon.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractualism"],"thinkers":["kamm","scanlon"],"position":"limited_aggregation","tags":["partial aggregation","relevance view","scanlon","aggregation","transmission line case"]},{"term":"Anti-aggregation","def":"Harms to different people cannot be added into a bigger harm, so the numbers alone never decide whom to help.","more":"Anti-aggregation is the view that we should not add up harms or benefits across different people. Pain is always felt by someone, and there is no one who feels the sum of five people’s pain. ” (1977). Faced with a choice between saving one person or five, he argued, we have no reason to prefer the five because there are more of them; he suggested flipping a coin, so that each person gets an equal chance. Also called Non-aggregation, Taurek’s view. Held by John Taurek, G. E. M. Anscombe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractualism","Deontology"],"thinkers":["anscombe"],"position":"anti_aggregation","tags":["anti-aggregationism","non-aggregation","taurek","john taurek","should the numbers count","scanlon","taurek’s view"]},{"term":"Longtermism","def":"Positively influencing the long-term future is a key moral priority of our time, because future people count and there may be vast numbers of them.","more":"Longtermism is the view that improving the long-run future, over thousands or even millions of years, is among the most important things we can do. It rests on three ideas: people who will live in the future matter just as much as people alive now; there could be enormous numbers of them; and what we do now can make a lasting difference, especially by reducing the risk of human extinction or of a permanent bad turn. Nick Bostrom’s “Astronomical Waste” (2003) and Parfit’s nuclear-war comparison are early sources. Toby Ord’s The Precipice (2020) and William MacAskill’s What We Owe the Future (2022) brought it to a wide audience, and Hilary Greaves and MacAskill defended a strong version in an academic paper. Also called Strong longtermism. Held by William MacAskill, Hilary Greaves, Toby Ord, Nick Bostrom.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Total view","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["bostrom"],"position":"longtermism","tags":["long-termism","future generations","effective altruism","existential risk","macaskill","the long-term future","strong longtermism"]},{"term":"Objective consequentialism","def":"The right act is the one that actually turns out best, whether or not the agent could know it.","more":"Objective consequentialism says that what makes an act right is how things actually turn out. If a doctor gives a drug that, unknown to anyone, happens to be the cure, she did the right thing; if a well-meant act turns out badly, it was wrong. G. E. Also called Actual-consequence consequentialism. Held by G. E. Moore, Peter Railton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Act consequentialism","Two-level consequentialism"],"thinkers":["moore"],"position":"objective_consequentialism","tags":["actualism","objective rightness","actual consequences","consequentialism","objective ought","actual-consequence consequentialism"]},{"term":"Expected-value consequentialism","def":"The right act is the one with the best expected results, given the evidence the agent has.","more":"Expected-value consequentialism holds that the right act is the one whose expected results are best. The expected value of an option is found by weighing each possible outcome by how likely it is, on the agent’s evidence, and adding up. Frank Jackson defended this view in 1991 with the case of a doctor who must choose between a safe drug that will help and two risky drugs, one of which will cure and one of which will kill. Most people agree she should give the safe drug, even though one risky drug would in fact have been better. Also called Expectabilism, Decision-theoretic consequentialism. Held by Frank Jackson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Act consequentialism","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["jackson"],"position":"expected_value_consequentialism","tags":["expected value","expected utility","expectabilism","probable consequences","subjective rightness","decision-theoretic consequentialism"]},{"term":"Anti-natalism","def":"Coming into existence is always a harm, however good the life, so it is better never to have been.","more":"Anti-natalism is the view that bringing new people into existence is always bad for them. Its best-known defender, David Benatar, argues from an asymmetry. The presence of pain is bad and the presence of pleasure is good. But the absence of pain is good even if there is no one to enjoy that absence, while the absence of pleasure is not bad unless there is someone deprived of it. Also called Benatar’s asymmetry. Held by David Benatar, Peter Wessel Zapffe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Philosophical pessimism"],"thinkers":["benatar"],"position":"anti_natalism","tags":["antinatalism","anti natalism","benatar","david benatar","better never to have been","procreation","benatar’s asymmetry"]},{"term":"Computational functionalism","def":"A mind is a kind of computation: run the right program on any hardware and you get a mind.","more":"Computational functionalism says that a mind is a kind of computation: having a mind is a matter of processing information in the right way, whatever physical stuff does the processing. John Searle called the claim that a suitably programmed computer would literally have a mind “strong AI”, contrasting it with “weak AI”, the claim that computers are useful tools for studying minds. Alan Turing argued in 1950 that if a machine’s conversation could not be told apart from a person’s, we would have no good reason to deny that it thinks. On this view the brain is one kind of hardware for minds, and there could be others: silicon chips, or anything else that carries out the same computations. Also called strong AI, the systems reply. Held by Alan Turing, Hilary Putnam, Daniel Dennett, David Chalmers.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Functionalism","Robot reply"],"thinkers":["turing","putnam","dennett","chalmers"],"position":"computational_functionalism","tags":["functionalism","computationalism","computational theory of mind","machine functionalism","strong ai","substrate independence","the systems reply"]},{"term":"Biological naturalism","def":"Minds are real biological features of brains, caused by their physical powers, and no program alone can produce one.","more":"Biological naturalism is John Searle’s view that consciousness and other mental states are real, subjective, biological features of the brain, caused by neurobiological processes and realised in the brain, much as digestion is a biological process of the stomach. Mental states are as natural as photosynthesis, so there is no soul and nothing supernatural. But they cannot be reduced to, or explained away as, mere functions or computations: they depend on the specific causal powers of biological brains, or of anything with equivalent powers. Running the right program is not enough for a mind, as Searle’s Chinese room argument aims to show. Held by John Searle.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Identity theory","Understanding without consciousness","Robot reply"],"thinkers":["searle"],"position":"biological_naturalism","tags":["searle","john searle","chinese room","biological brain","anti-computationalism"]},{"term":"Sufficientarianism","def":"Justice requires that everyone has enough for a decent life; above that line, inequality is not itself a problem.","more":"Sufficientarianism says that what matters morally is not that people have the same, but that each person has enough. Harry Frankfurt argued that the badness of poverty lies in people lacking what they need, not in their having less than others. Two claims are central. The positive claim: it is especially important that everyone reaches a threshold of sufficiency (enough food, health, education and security to live a decent life). Also called the doctrine of sufficiency. Held by Harry Frankfurt, Roger Crisp.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["The capability approach"],"thinkers":["frankfurt"],"position":"sufficientarianism","tags":["sufficiency","sufficiency principle","frankfurt","harry frankfurt","enough","distributive justice","the doctrine of sufficiency"]},{"term":"Kantian ethics","def":"Right action is acting on principles every rational being could accept, and treating people never merely as tools.","more":"Kantian ethics is the moral theory of Immanuel Kant and his followers. Its centre is the categorical imperative: a command of reason that binds you whatever you happen to want. Kant gave several formulas of it. One says to act only on a rule (a “maxim”) that you could will everyone to follow. Also called Kantianism. Held by Immanuel Kant, Christine Korsgaard, Onora O’Neill, Barbara Herman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Moral rationalism","Moral constructivism","Contractualism"],"thinkers":["kant","korsgaard"],"position":"kantian_ethics","tags":["kantianism","kant","deontology","categorical imperative","duty","respect for persons"]},{"term":"Rights-based deontology","def":"Morality’s deepest limits are the rights of individuals, which others may not cross even to do good.","more":"Rights-based deontology says that the basic moral facts are the rights each person has, such as rights not to be killed, assaulted, robbed or enslaved. These rights explain our duties: you must not kill me because I have a right not to be killed. Robert Nozick called such rights “side constraints”: they are not goals to promote, but limits on how anyone may pursue any goal. So you may not violate one person’s rights even to prevent several other violations. Also called Side-constraint theory, Natural rights theory. Held by Robert Nozick, John Locke, Judith Jarvis Thomson, Murray Rothbard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Negative-duties view","Victim-centred constraints"],"thinkers":["nozick","locke","thomson","rothbard"],"position":"rights_based_deontology","tags":["rights theory","rights","side constraints","nozick","deontology","individual rights","side-constraint theory","natural rights theory"]},{"term":"Agent-centred constraints","def":"You may not kill one to prevent five killings because each person is responsible first for what they themselves do.","more":"This is one answer to the paradox of deontology: why is it wrong to kill one person to prevent five other killings? Agent-centred views say the explanation lies with you, the person acting. A constraint is “agent-relative”: it tells each agent not to kill, rather than telling everyone to make sure there is as little killing as possible. Thomas Nagel argued that when you intentionally harm someone, your will is guided by evil: you aim at the very thing that morality says is bad, and that is what the constraint forbids. Also called Agent-relative explanation of constraints. Held by Thomas Nagel, Charles Fried.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Doctrine of double effect"],"thinkers":["nagel","fried"],"position":"agent_centred_constraints","tags":["agent-centered constraints","agent-relative constraints","deontological constraints","clean hands","deontology","agent-relative explanation of constraints"]},{"term":"Victim-centred constraints","def":"You may not kill one to save five because each person has a status that forbids their being used, even for more lives.","more":"This is another answer to the paradox of deontology. Victim-centred views say constraints are explained not by the agent’s responsibility but by the worth of the potential victim. Frances Kamm argued that constraints express a high moral status: each person is inviolable, meaning it is not permissible to harm them in certain ways even to prevent greater harm to others. This status is a good that belongs to everyone, including the five. Also called Inviolability view, Status view. Held by F. M. Kamm, Warren Quinn.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Deontology","Rights-based deontology","Principle of permissible harm"],"thinkers":["kamm","quinn_warren"],"position":"victim_centred_constraints","tags":["victim-centered constraints","patient-centred constraints","inviolability","kamm","deontology","inviolability view","status view"]},{"term":"Doctrine of triple effect","def":"You may act because a harm will occur, as long as you do not act in order to bring that harm about.","more":"The doctrine of triple effect is Frances Kamm’s refinement of the doctrine of double effect. Double effect contrasts intending harm with merely foreseeing it. Kamm adds a third relation: acting because of an effect. Her example is the party case. Also called DTE. Held by F. M. Kamm.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Doctrine of double effect","Principle of permissible harm"],"thinkers":["kamm"],"position":"doctrine_of_triple_effect","tags":["triple effect","kamm","frances kamm","loop case","double effect","because of vs in order to","dte"]},{"term":"Means principle","def":"It is wrong to use a person merely as a means, for example by making harm to them your way of achieving a goal.","more":"The means principle says that harming someone as a means to your goal is especially wrong, more than harming them as a side effect. It comes from Kant’s formula of humanity, which forbids treating people merely as means. In the trolley problem, it explains why you may not push a large man off a bridge to stop a trolley: his body is your tool. In the ordinary switch case, the one person on the side track is not needed; if he leapt clear, the five would still be saved. Also called Mere means principle. Held by Immanuel Kant, Victor Tadros.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Doctrine of double effect","Kantian ethics"],"thinkers":["kant"],"position":"means_principle","tags":["mere means","kant","formula of humanity","using people","treating as an end","mere means principle"]},{"term":"Will theory of rights","def":"To have a right is to have control over someone else’s duty: the power to demand, waive or enforce it.","more":"The will theory says that the point of a right is to give its holder a kind of small-scale sovereignty over another person’s duty. If you have a right that I repay you, you can insist on repayment, forgive the debt or sue me: the choice is yours. H. L. Also called Choice theory of rights. Held by H. L. A. Hart, Hillel Steiner.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Rights-based deontology"],"thinkers":["hart","steiner"],"position":"will_theory_of_rights","tags":["choice theory","will theory","hart","h. l. a. hart","rights","waiver","choice theory of rights"]},{"term":"Interest theory of rights","def":"To have a right is to have an interest important enough to place others under a duty.","more":"The interest theory says that rights exist to protect important interests. Joseph Raz put it this way: you have a right when some aspect of your wellbeing is a sufficient reason for holding someone else to be under a duty. On this view, the mother in Hart’s promise case may also have a right, since the promise exists for her benefit. The theory easily explains rights held by those who cannot choose, such as babies, people in comas and, some argue, animals. Also called Benefit theory of rights. Held by Joseph Raz, Neil MacCormick, Matthew Kramer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Rights-based deontology","Animal rights view"],"thinkers":["raz"],"position":"interest_theory_of_rights","tags":["benefit theory","interest theory","raz","joseph raz","rights","interests","benefit theory of rights"]},{"term":"Direct duty view","def":"We owe duties to animals themselves, not only duties about them.","more":"The direct duty view says that animals can be wronged in their own right: when you are cruel to a dog, the dog is the one you wrong, not just yourself or other people. Many traditions reach this conclusion. Utilitarians such as Peter Singer ground it in animals’ capacity to suffer, and Tom Regan in their being the subject of a life. Christine Korsgaard reached it from inside Kantian ethics. Also called Direct duties to animals. Held by Christine Korsgaard, Peter Singer, Tom Regan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Animal rights view","Equal consideration of interests","Hierarchical view of moral status"],"thinkers":["korsgaard","singer"],"position":"direct_duty_view","tags":["direct duties","moral status of animals","animal ethics","animal welfare","animals","direct duties to animals"]},{"term":"Rawlsian contractualism","def":"The right rules are those we would choose if we did not know who we would be.","more":"Rawlsian contractualism takes John Rawls’s method in A Theory of Justice and applies it to morality. Rawls imagined people choosing principles in an “original position”, behind a “veil of ignorance” that hides their sex, race, talents, wealth and even their idea of a good life. Because no one knows who they will be, no one can tilt the rules in their own favour, so the choice is fair. Rawls called the result for institutions “justice as fairness”, and suggested that principles for individuals could be chosen in the same way, “rightness as fairness”. Also called Rightness as fairness, Kantian contractualism (Rawls’s version). Held by John Rawls.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractualism","Kantian ethics","Prioritarianism"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"position":"rawlsian_contractualism","tags":["rawls","john rawls","veil of ignorance","original position","justice as fairness","social contract","rightness as fairness","kantian contractualism (rawls’s version)"]},{"term":"Constrained maximisation","def":"It pays to be the kind of person who keeps agreements with others who keep them, even when a single breach would pay.","more":"Constrained maximisation is David Gauthier’s answer to the question why a self-interested person should keep moral agreements. A “straightforward maximiser” does whatever pays best in each case, so breaks agreements whenever cheating pays. A “constrained maximiser” is disposed to cooperate with others who are likewise disposed, even when a particular breach would pay. Gauthier argued that if people’s dispositions are at least partly visible (he called this “translucency”), constrained maximisers will be trusted and invited into profitable cooperation, while straightforward maximisers will be shut out. Also called Gauthier’s reply to the Foole. Held by David Gauthier.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Contractarianism","Ethical egoism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"constrained_maximisation","tags":["gauthier","david gauthier","morals by agreement","contractarianism","prisoner's dilemma","cooperation","gauthier’s reply to the foole"]},{"term":"Scheffler’s hybrid view","def":"You may always do the most good, but you may also sometimes put your own concerns first.","more":"In The Rejection of Consequentialism (1982), Samuel Scheffler proposed a middle path between consequentialism and deontology. He kept the consequentialist idea that it is always permissible to bring about the best outcome, so there are no side constraints (absolute limits) forbidding, say, killing one to save five. But he added an agent-centred prerogative: a permission to give your own interests and projects more weight than they have from an impartial point of view, so you need not always do the best. The motivation is that each person has a life of their own, lived from their own point of view, and morality should respect that. Also called Hybrid theory, Consequentialism with an agent-centred prerogative. Held by Samuel Scheffler.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Agent-centred prerogative","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["scheffler"],"position":"scheffler_hybrid","tags":["scheffler","samuel scheffler","agent-centred prerogative","hybrid theory","rejection of consequentialism","consequentialism with an agent-centred prerogative"]},{"term":"Triple Theory","def":"An act is wrong when it is forbidden by principles that would be best, that everyone could rationally will, and that no one could reasonably reject.","more":"Derek Parfit argued in On What Matters (2011) that the great traditions of modern ethics are like climbers on different sides of the same mountain. Kantian ethics asks which principles everyone could rationally will to be universal laws. Rule consequentialism asks which principles would make things go best if everyone accepted them. T. Also called Parfit’s convergence thesis, Kantian rule consequentialism. Held by Derek Parfit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Rule consequentialism","Contractualism","Deontology"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"position":"triple_theory","tags":["parfit","derek parfit","on what matters","kantian consequentialism","convergence","parfit’s convergence thesis","kantian rule consequentialism"]},{"term":"Virtue consequentialism","def":"A character trait is a virtue because it reliably produces good results.","more":"Virtue consequentialism agrees with virtue ethics that character matters deeply, but explains virtue in consequentialist terms. On Julia Driver’s version in Uneasy Virtue (2001), a virtue is a character trait that systematically produces more good than bad in the world; what you know or intend is not what makes it a virtue. This lets the view count traits like modesty as virtues, even though, on Driver’s analysis, modesty involves not fully appreciating your own worth. The view differs from Aristotelian virtue ethics, which grounds virtue in the agent’s own flourishing and practical wisdom, and from act consequentialism, which assesses single acts rather than lasting traits. Also called Consequentialist virtue theory. Held by Julia Driver, Ben Bradley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Consequentialism","Virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["driver"],"position":"virtue_consequentialism","tags":["julia driver","driver","consequentialism","virtue","consequentialist virtue theory"]},{"term":"Moral naturalism","def":"Moral facts are real, and they are natural facts of the kind science can study.","more":"Moral naturalism is the form of moral realism that places moral facts in the natural world. Goodness might be, roughly, what we would want if we were fully informed and rational (Peter Railton’s view), or whatever natural property best explains our use of the word “good” (the view of the “Cornell realists” Richard Boyd, Nicholas Sturgeon and David Brink). Naturalists compare moral facts to facts about health: there is no single physical test for being healthy, yet health is real and can be studied. The view promises to fit ethics into a scientific picture of the world and to explain how we can learn moral truths from experience. Also called Naturalist moral realism, Ethical naturalism. Held by Peter Railton, Richard Boyd, Nicholas Sturgeon, David Brink.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral realism"],"thinkers":["foot"],"position":"moral_naturalism","tags":["ethical naturalism","naturalism","cornell realism","moral realism","naturalistic ethics","naturalist moral realism"]},{"term":"Moral non-naturalism","def":"Moral facts are real, but they are not natural facts and cannot be reduced to anything science studies.","more":"Non-naturalism holds that there are objective moral facts, and that they are a distinct kind of fact, not identical to anything physical, biological or psychological. G. E. Moore argued that goodness is a simple property that cannot be defined in natural terms. Also called Non-naturalist moral realism, Robust realism. Held by G. E. Moore, Derek Parfit, David Enoch, Russ Shafer-Landau.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral realism","Ethical intuitionism"],"thinkers":["moore","parfit","enoch"],"position":"moral_non_naturalism","tags":["non-naturalism","ethical non-naturalism","moore","g. e. moore","moral realism","open question argument","non-naturalist moral realism","robust realism"]},{"term":"Quietist realism","def":"Moral claims can be objectively true, but this needs no special metaphysics: only moral arguments can support or undermine them.","more":"Quietist realism agrees with other realists that moral claims such as “torture for fun is wrong” can be true, and true whatever anyone thinks. But it denies that this commits us to strange entities, and it declines the debate about what moral facts are made of. Ronald Dworkin argued that every claim about morality, even the skeptic’s claim that nothing is really wrong, is itself a moral claim and must be defended with moral reasons. T. Also called Quietism about metaethics, Non-metaphysical realism. Held by Ronald Dworkin, T. M. Scanlon.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral realism","Moral non-naturalism"],"thinkers":["dworkin","scanlon"],"position":"quietist_realism","tags":["quietism","relaxed realism","dworkin","scanlon","parfit","non-metaphysical realism","quietism about metaethics"]},{"term":"Theistic moral realism","def":"Moral facts are objective, and they are grounded in God.","more":"Theistic moral realism holds that there are objective moral facts and that they depend on God: on God’s nature, God’s commands, or both. Robert Merrihew Adams, in Finite and Infinite Goods (1999), argued that God is the Good itself, and that other things are good insofar as they resemble God; obligations, in turn, come from the commands of a loving God. William Lane Craig argues that without God there could be no objective moral values at all. The view shares realism’s confidence that cruelty is wrong whatever anyone thinks, and adds an account of where this objectivity and authority come from. Also called Theistic ethics, Theistic realism. Held by Robert Merrihew Adams, William Lane Craig, C. Stephen Evans.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral realism","Modified divine command theory","Divine command theory"],"thinkers":["adams_rm","craig"],"position":"theistic_moral_realism","tags":["theistic ethics","moral argument","god and morality","moral realism","divine command","theistic realism"]},{"term":"Quasi-realism","def":"Moral judgments express attitudes, yet we are entitled to talk of moral truth, error and objectivity.","more":"Quasi-realism is Simon Blackburn’s version of expressivism. It starts like emotivism: moral judgments project our attitudes onto the world rather than describe facts in it, an idea Blackburn traces to Hume. But it then argues that almost everything realists like to say can be earned on this basis. We can call moral claims true, meaning roughly that we endorse them. Also called Blackburn’s quasi-realism. Held by Simon Blackburn.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Expressivism","Norm expressivism"],"thinkers":["blackburn"],"position":"quasi_realism","tags":["quasi realism","blackburn","simon blackburn","expressivism","projectivism","non-cognitivism","blackburn’s quasi-realism"]},{"term":"Norm expressivism","def":"To make a moral judgment is to accept a norm or plan about what to do and feel.","more":"Norm expressivism is Allan Gibbard’s version of expressivism. In Wise Choices, Apt Feelings (1990) he argued that calling an act wrong expresses your acceptance of norms about it, in particular norms that make it fitting for the agent to feel guilt and for others to feel resentment. In Thinking How to Live (2003) he recast this as plan expressivism: normative judgments are like decisions or plans, including plans for situations you will never face, such as what to do if you were in someone else’s shoes. Because plans can be consistent or inconsistent, this gives moral reasoning a logic. Also called Plan expressivism, Gibbard’s expressivism. Held by Allan Gibbard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Expressivism","Quasi-realism"],"thinkers":["gibbard"],"position":"norm_expressivism","tags":["norm-expressivism","gibbard","allan gibbard","plan expressivism","expressivism","non-cognitivism","gibbard’s expressivism"]},{"term":"Kantian constructivism","def":"Moral principles are those that any rational agent, reasoning correctly about what to do, is committed to.","more":"Kantian constructivism holds that moral truths are not discovered in the world but constructed by practical reason (reasoning about what to do), and that the construction starts from features every rational agent shares. John Rawls used the label in “Kantian Constructivism in Moral Theory” (1980), describing principles of justice as what free and equal persons would agree to under fair conditions. Christine Korsgaard argues in The Sources of Normativity (1996) that to act at all you must value your own humanity, your capacity to choose for reasons, and that consistency then commits you to valuing everyone’s. So morality binds every rational agent, not only those who already care about it. Held by Christine Korsgaard, John Rawls, Onora O’Neill.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral constructivism","Moral rationalism","Deontology"],"thinkers":["korsgaard","rawls"],"position":"kantian_constructivism","tags":["rawls","korsgaard","christine korsgaard","constructivism","kant","rational agency"]},{"term":"Humean constructivism","def":"What you have reason to do is fixed by what follows from the values you already hold.","more":"Humean constructivism, defended above all by Sharon Street, agrees with Kantians that normative truths are built by reasoning rather than discovered, but denies that reasoning alone yields a morality binding on everyone. Street argues that what is true for you about reasons is whatever follows, on careful and consistent reflection, from your own evaluative attitudes together with the non-moral facts. Most humans share many values, so in practice we agree on a great deal. But an ideally coherent Caligula, who values torturing others for fun, need not be making any mistake by his own lights. Held by Sharon Street.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral constructivism","Reasons internalism"],"thinkers":["street"],"position":"humean_constructivism","tags":["sharon street","street","constructivism","hume","practical reasons","subjectivism"]},{"term":"Moral abolitionism","def":"Since moral claims are all untrue, we would do better to stop making them.","more":"Moral abolitionism is one answer to the question error theorists face: if there are no moral facts, what should we do with moral talk? Abolitionists such as Ian Hinckfuss and Richard Garner argue that we should give it up. In The Moral Society (1987), Hinckfuss argued that moral thinking does more harm than good, feeding self-righteousness, conflict and elitism. Garner, in Beyond Morality (1994), argues that we can still be kind, honest and cooperative, and can state our preferences and feelings openly, without dressing them up as objective demands. Also called Moral eliminativism. Held by Richard Garner, Ian Hinckfuss.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral error theory"],"thinkers":["garner"],"position":"moral_abolitionism","tags":["abolitionism","error theory","joel marks","moral eliminativism","amoralism"]},{"term":"Moral fictionalism","def":"We should keep using moral language as a kind of useful make-believe, without believing it.","more":"Moral fictionalism, in the form Richard Joyce defends in The Myth of Morality (2001), accepts that moral claims are untrue but recommends keeping them as a fiction. We would go on saying and thinking “stealing is wrong”, but without truly believing it, much as a storyteller says that Sherlock Holmes lives on Baker Street. Joyce argues that moral thoughts work as a bulwark against weakness of will: framing a choice as “wrong” helps you resist short-term temptations you would later regret. The fiction gives us these benefits without asking us to believe what is false. Also called Revolutionary fictionalism. Held by Richard Joyce.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral error theory"],"thinkers":["joyce"],"position":"moral_fictionalism","tags":["fictionalism","error theory","richard joyce","make-believe","moral fiction","revolutionary fictionalism"]},{"term":"Moral conservationism","def":"Even though moral claims are untrue, we should go on believing them in everyday life.","more":"Moral conservationism is Jonas Olson’s answer to the error theorist’s question about what to do next. In Moral Error Theory: History, Critique, Defence (2014), Olson argues that once moral beliefs are seen to be untrue, the best policy is usually to keep holding them in everyday life, rather than abandoning them or merely pretending. Fictionalism asks too much: switching to make-believe is hard and may not motivate. Abolitionism risks losing the real benefits of moral thinking for cooperation and self-control. Held by Jonas Olson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral error theory"],"thinkers":["olson_jonas"],"position":"moral_conservationism","tags":["conservationism","error theory","jonas olson","moral belief"]},{"term":"Cultural relativism","def":"Right and wrong are fixed by the moral code of a culture, and no code is objectively better.","more":"Cultural relativism holds that moral claims are true or false only relative to the standards of a culture. In one society “eating meat is wrong” may be true; in another it may be false, and there is no neutral standard to rank the two codes. The view grew from anthropology. William Graham Sumner argued in Folkways (1906) that the customs of a group can make anything right, and Ruth Benedict, in Patterns of Culture (1934) and other work, argued that what counts as normal or abnormal varies from culture to culture. Also called Cultural moral relativism, Conventionalism. Held by Ruth Benedict, William Graham Sumner, Melville Herskovits.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral relativism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"cultural_relativism","tags":["cultural relativist","relativism","moral relativism","anthropology","ethnocentrism","tolerance","cultural moral relativism","conventionalism"]},{"term":"Moral subjectivism","def":"A moral claim is true or false only relative to the standards or feelings of the person who makes it.","more":"Moral subjectivism, in the sense meant here, makes each individual the measure of moral truth. When I say “eating meat is wrong”, my claim is true if it fits my own moral standards or feelings, and when you say “eating meat is fine”, yours may be true too, relative to yours. In its simplest form, a moral claim reports the speaker’s attitude: “X is wrong” means “I disapprove of X”. The Finnish philosopher and anthropologist Edward Westermarck defended a view of this broad kind in Ethical Relativity (1932), grounding moral judgments in emotions of approval and disapproval. Also called Individual relativism, Speaker relativism. Held by Edward Westermarck.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral relativism","Expressivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"moral_subjectivism","tags":["subjectivism","ethical subjectivism","personal opinion","relativism","feelings","individual relativism","speaker relativism"]},{"term":"Agreement relativism","def":"Moral demands hold only among people who share an implicit agreement, so they are relative to such agreements.","more":"Gilbert Harman argued in “Moral Relativism Defended” (1975) that morality arises when a group of people reach an implicit agreement, a tacit understanding, about how they will treat one another. Judgments such as “he ought not to have done that” make sense only if the person judged shares the relevant agreement with the speaker. Harman’s example is a professional killer employed by a criminal organisation: it seems odd to say he ought not to have killed his victim, since nothing he accepts gives him a reason not to. We can call him evil or dangerous, but not say he failed by standards he shares. Also called Harman’s relativism. Held by Gilbert Harman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral relativism","Contractarianism"],"thinkers":["harman"],"position":"agreement_relativism","tags":["harman","gilbert harman","relativism","implicit agreement","moral relativism","harman’s relativism"]},{"term":"Moral sentimentalism","def":"Moral knowledge and motivation come, at bottom, from feelings such as sympathy, approval and disapproval.","more":"Moral sentimentalism holds that morality rests on human feeling rather than on pure reason. David Hume argued that reason alone can only tell us what is true or false; it is our sentiments of approval and disapproval, felt from a general and impartial point of view, that mark virtue and vice. Francis Hutcheson spoke of a moral sense, and Adam Smith, in The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759), built ethics around sympathy and an imagined impartial spectator. Today Michael Slote and Jesse Prinz defend sentimentalist views. Also called Sentimentalism. Held by David Hume, Adam Smith, Francis Hutcheson, Michael Slote.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral sense theory","Care ethics","Expressivism"],"thinkers":["hume","smith","slote"],"position":"moral_sentimentalism","tags":["sentimentalism","hume","moral sense","sympathy","emotions","feelings"]},{"term":"Debunking of moral intuitions","def":"Many moral intuitions about cases come from evolution or bias, so we should trust well-grounded principles over them.","more":"This method starts from a question about where our intuitions come from. Peter Singer argued in “Ethics and Intuitions” (2005) that many intuitions, such as that pushing someone to their death is far worse than diverting a trolley onto them, are left over from our evolutionary past, when harm done up close was the kind of violence that mattered. Joshua Greene’s brain-imaging studies suggested that such judgments are driven by fast emotional responses, while consequentialist judgments involve slower reasoning, though that reading of the data is disputed. If an intuition is best explained by something that does not track moral truth, it loses its authority. Also called Principle-first method, Evolutionary debunking of intuitions. Held by Peter Singer, Joshua Greene, Katarzyna de Lazari-Radek.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral rationalism","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["singer","greene"],"position":"debunking_of_moral_intuitions","tags":["evolutionary debunking","debunking arguments","moral intuitions","singer","greene","joshua greene","principle-first method","evolutionary debunking of intuitions"]},{"term":"Moral particularism","def":"There are no reliable moral principles: what matters morally depends on the particular situation.","more":"Moral particularism, defended by Jonathan Dancy in Moral Reasons (1993) and Ethics Without Principles (2004), denies that morality can be captured in general principles. Its key idea is holism about reasons: a feature that is a reason in favour of an act in one situation may be no reason, or even a reason against, in another. That I borrowed a book from you is normally a reason to give it back to you; but if I learn you stole it from the library, it gives me no such reason. So no rule of the form “always do X”, or even “X always counts in favour”, is safe. Also called Particularism. Held by Jonathan Dancy, Margaret Little.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Moral pluralism"],"thinkers":["dancy"],"position":"moral_particularism","tags":["particularism","jonathan dancy","dancy","holism of reasons","situational ethics","anti-theory"]},{"term":"My favourite theory","def":"When unsure which moral theory is right, act on the one you think most likely to be true.","more":"Moral uncertainty is uncertainty not about the facts but about which moral view is correct. “My favourite theory” is the simplest answer: work out which theory you have the highest credence in (that is, which you think most probably true), and do what it says. Johan Gustafsson and Olle Torpman defended a version of it in “In Defence of My Favourite Theory” (2014). Its attractions are that it is easy to apply and that it never asks you to compare the stakes of rival theories on a common scale, which many think cannot be done, since theories disagree about what matters. Held by Johan E. Gustafsson, Olle Torpman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":[],"thinkers":[],"position":"my_favourite_theory","tags":["my favorite theory","moral uncertainty","gustafsson","normative uncertainty"]},{"term":"Maximising expected choiceworthiness","def":"Under moral uncertainty, weigh each theory’s verdict by your confidence in it and by the stakes, and choose the best overall.","more":"This view applies the logic of decisions under risk to moral uncertainty. Each moral theory you take seriously rates each option by its choiceworthiness: how strongly the theory favours or opposes it. You multiply each theory’s rating by your credence in that theory, add these up, and choose the option with the highest total. So a theory you think less likely can still decide the matter if far more is at stake on it: if you are unsure whether animal suffering matters much, the chance that factory farming is a great wrong may outweigh the small benefit of cheap meat. Also called MEC, Moral hedging. Held by Will MacAskill, Toby Ord, Krister Bykvist, Ted Lockhart.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral parliament"],"thinkers":[],"position":"maximise_expected_choiceworthiness","tags":["mec","moral uncertainty","expected choiceworthiness","macaskill","normative uncertainty","moral hedging"]},{"term":"Moral parliament","def":"Handle moral uncertainty by imagining your rival theories as delegates in a parliament who bargain and vote.","more":"Nick Bostrom proposed the parliamentary model in 2009 as a way to act under moral uncertainty without forcing all theories onto one scale. Imagine each moral theory you take seriously sending delegates to a parliament, with seats in proportion to your credence in it. The delegates bargain and then vote. Because they can trade, a smaller theory can win on the issues it cares about most, in return for supporting a larger theory elsewhere. Also called Parliamentary model. Held by Nick Bostrom, Toby Newberry, Toby Ord.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Maximising expected choiceworthiness"],"thinkers":["bostrom"],"position":"moral_parliament","tags":["parliamentary model","moral uncertainty","bostrom","toby ord","normative uncertainty"]},{"term":"Normative externalism","def":"What you should do depends on what is actually right, not on your uncertain beliefs about morality.","more":"Normative externalism denies that moral uncertainty calls for any special decision rule. Brian Weatherson, in Normative Externalism (2019), and Elizabeth Harman, in “The Irrelevance of Moral Uncertainty” (2015), argue that the norms that matter are the true moral norms, whether or not you believe them. If eating meat is permissible, you do nothing wrong by eating it, however uncertain you are; if it is wrong, your uncertainty does not excuse you. Hedging between theories, Weatherson argues, treats a good person as someone who cares about “doing what is right” in the abstract, rather than about what actually matters: other people, honesty, suffering. Also called The irrelevance of moral uncertainty. Held by Brian Weatherson, Elizabeth Harman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":[],"thinkers":["weatherson"],"position":"normative_externalism","tags":["externalism","moral uncertainty","weatherson","harman","objective ought","the irrelevance of moral uncertainty"]},{"term":"Quantitative hedonism","def":"Pleasures differ only in amount: how intense and how long-lasting they are decides how much each is worth.","more":"Quantitative hedonism holds that the value of a pleasure depends only on how much pleasure it is. Jeremy Bentham listed the dimensions that matter: intensity, duration, certainty and nearness, and, when judging acts, fruitfulness (whether it leads to more pleasure), purity (whether it is free of later pain) and extent (how many people it reaches). Whether pleasure comes from poetry or from a simple game makes no difference in itself; Bentham remarked that if the children’s game of push-pin gives more pleasure than poetry, it is more valuable. Henry Sidgwick also held that pleasures differ only in how desirable they are as felt. Also called Benthamite hedonism. Held by Jeremy Bentham, Henry Sidgwick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Hedonism","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["bentham","sidgwick"],"position":"quantitative_hedonism","tags":["hedonism","bentham","jeremy bentham","hedonic calculus","felicific calculus","pleasure","benthamite hedonism"]},{"term":"Qualitative hedonism","def":"Pleasure is the only good, but some kinds of pleasure are higher and more valuable than others.","more":"Qualitative hedonism is John Stuart Mill’s answer to the charge that hedonism is “a doctrine worthy only of swine”. In Utilitarianism (1861), Mill agreed that pleasure and freedom from pain are the only things desirable as ends, but argued that pleasures differ in quality as well as quantity. The pleasures of the intellect, the feelings, the imagination and the moral sentiments are higher than those of mere sensation. His test: if nearly everyone who has experienced both prefers one kind, even with more discontent attached, that kind is higher. Also called Millian hedonism, Higher-pleasures hedonism. Held by John Stuart Mill.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Hedonism","Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["mill"],"position":"qualitative_hedonism","tags":["higher pleasures","mill","john stuart mill","higher and lower pleasures","hedonism","millian hedonism","higher-pleasures hedonism"]},{"term":"Attitudinal hedonism","def":"What is good for you is taking pleasure in things: being glad, or enjoying, that something is so.","more":"Attitudinal hedonism, developed by Fred Feldman in Pleasure and the Good Life (2004), distinguishes two kinds of pleasure. Sensory pleasures are feelings, like the warmth of a bath. Attitudinal pleasures are attitudes: being pleased that something is so, such as being glad your friend has recovered. Feldman argues that attitudinal pleasure is the basic good. Also called Feldman’s hedonism. Held by Fred Feldman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Hedonism"],"thinkers":["feldman"],"position":"attitudinal_hedonism","tags":["attitudinal pleasure","feldman","fred feldman","hedonism","enjoyment","feldman’s hedonism"]},{"term":"Actual-desire theory","def":"Your life goes well to the extent that the desires you actually have are satisfied.","more":"The simplest desire theory says that what is good for you is getting what you actually want, whatever that is. No one else, however wise, can overrule your preferences about your own life. This respects people’s authority over their own lives and avoids paternalism (deciding for others what is good for them). It also explains why wellbeing differs between people: what is good for a keen climber may not be good for you. Also called Simple desire satisfactionism. Held by Chris Heathwood.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Desire-satisfaction theory"],"thinkers":[],"position":"actual_desire_theory","tags":["desire satisfaction","preference satisfaction","actual desires","wellbeing","well-being","simple desire satisfactionism"]},{"term":"Informed-desire theory","def":"Your life goes well when you get what you would want if you knew the relevant facts.","more":"Informed-desire theories keep desire at the centre but correct it for ignorance. What is good for you is what you would want if you had accurate information about your options and their effects. If you want to drink from a glass because you think it holds gin, but it holds petrol, you would not want to drink it if you knew, so drinking it is not good for you. James Griffin developed an influential informed-desire account in Well-Being (1986), on which wellbeing is the fulfilment of desires formed with a proper appreciation of what their objects are like. Also called Informed-preference theory. Held by James Griffin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Desire-satisfaction theory","Idealised-desire theory"],"thinkers":[],"position":"informed_desire_theory","tags":["informed desire","full information","fully informed preferences","wellbeing","well-being","informed-preference theory"]},{"term":"Idealised-desire theory","def":"Your good is what you would want if you were fully informed and reasoning perfectly.","more":"Idealised-desire theories go further than correcting factual mistakes. They ask what you would want after ideal reflection: with full information, vividly appreciated, and perfect rationality. Richard Brandt held that rational desires are those that would survive “cognitive psychotherapy”, repeated vivid reflection on all relevant facts. Peter Railton proposed an ideal advisor test: your good is what a fully informed and rational version of you would want you, as you actually are, to want. Also called Ideal-advisor theory, Rational-desire theory. Held by Richard Brandt, Peter Railton, John Harsanyi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Desire-satisfaction theory","Informed-desire theory"],"thinkers":[],"position":"idealised_desire_theory","tags":["ideal desire","ideal advisor","full information account","rational desire","wellbeing","ideal-advisor theory","rational-desire theory"]},{"term":"Global desire theory","def":"What counts is whether your desires about your life as a whole, your plans and aims for it, are fulfilled.","more":"Global desire theories count only, or mainly, desires about your life as a whole, rather than every passing want. Derek Parfit, in Reasons and Persons (1984), described a global version: what matters is whether you get what you want about how your life goes overall. He noted that it handles his addiction case well, since few people would want a life of addiction even if each craving were met. John Rawls held a related view in A Theory of Justice (1971): a person’s good is the successful carrying out of a rational plan of life. Also called Life-plan theory, Success theory. Held by John Rawls.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Desire-satisfaction theory"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"position":"global_desire_theory","tags":["global desires","life plan","desire satisfaction","parfit","life as a whole","life-plan theory","success theory"]},{"term":"New natural law theory","def":"A short list of basic goods is self-evident to practical reason, and we must never act directly against any of them.","more":"New natural law theory was developed from the 1960s by Germain Grisez, John Finnis and Joseph Boyle, and later Robert P. George. It holds that there are a handful of basic goods, which Finnis lists as life, knowledge, play, aesthetic experience, friendship, practical reasonableness and religion. These are self-evident: not proved from facts about human nature, but grasped by practical reason as worth pursuing for their own sake. Also called New classical natural law, Grisez–Finnis theory. Held by John Finnis, Germain Grisez, Robert P. George.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Natural law theory","Moral absolutism"],"thinkers":["finnis"],"position":"new_natural_law_theory","tags":["new natural law","grisez","germain grisez","finnis","john finnis","basic goods","new classical natural law","grisez–finnis theory"]},{"term":"Capabilities approach","def":"What matters is what people are really able to do and be, such as living a healthy life, thinking and loving.","more":"The capabilities approach, founded by the economist and philosopher Amartya Sen and developed by Martha Nussbaum, judges how well people’s lives go by their capabilities: their real freedom to do and be things they have reason to value. Sen deliberately left the list open, to be settled by public discussion. Nussbaum, in Women and Human Development (2000) and Creating Capabilities (2011), proposed ten central capabilities, among them life, bodily health, bodily integrity, practical reason, affiliation with others, play and control over one’s environment. As an answer to what goes on the list, it focuses on opportunities rather than achieved states: a person who fasts by choice and one who starves are equally hungry, but have very different capabilities. Also called Capability approach. Held by Martha Nussbaum, Amartya Sen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Objective list theory"],"thinkers":["nussbaum"],"position":"capabilities_approach","tags":["capability approach","capabilities","sen","amartya sen","nussbaum","martha nussbaum"]},{"term":"Anthropocentrism","def":"Full moral standing belongs to human beings as such.","more":"Anthropocentrism, in ethics, holds that moral standing in the full sense belongs to human beings because they are human. Animals and the rest of nature may deserve kind treatment, but they lack the rights and standing every human has. Unlike the Kantian view, which grounds status in the capacity to reason, anthropocentrism grounds it in membership of the human kind: so newborn babies and people with severe cognitive disabilities have full moral status simply because they are human. Carl Cohen defended a view of this kind, arguing that rights belong to beings of a kind capable of moral judgment, even when some members lack the capacity. Also called Human exceptionalism, Human-centred ethics. Held by Carl Cohen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Indirect duty view","Hierarchical view of moral status"],"thinkers":[],"position":"anthropocentrism","tags":["human-centred ethics","human-centered","speciesism","anthropocentric","human exceptionalism"]},{"term":"Biocentrism","def":"Every living thing, plant or animal, has moral worth in its own right.","more":"Biocentrism extends moral standing to all living things, not only to beings that can feel. Albert Schweitzer, the doctor and theologian, founded his ethics on “reverence for life”: every living thing has a will to live, and goodness consists in preserving and furthering life. Paul W. Taylor gave a systematic defence in Respect for Nature (1986): each organism is a “teleological centre of life”, pursuing its own good in its own way, and so has inherent worth equal to every other’s. Also called Reverence for life, Life-centred ethics. Held by Albert Schweitzer, Paul W. Taylor, Robin Attfield.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Equal consideration of interests"],"thinkers":[],"position":"biocentrism","tags":["biocentric ethics","reverence for life","paul taylor","albert schweitzer","life-centred ethics"]},{"term":"Dualism of practical reason","def":"Reason endorses both your own good and the general good as ultimate aims, and cannot settle a clash between them.","more":"Henry Sidgwick ended The Methods of Ethics (1874) with what he called the most profound problem of ethics. Careful reflection, he argued, shows that two principles are each self-evident: that you should pursue your own greatest good (rational egoism), and that you should promote the good of everyone, counting each person equally (rational benevolence, the root of utilitarianism). In ordinary life the two usually point the same way, because helping others tends to serve you too. But sometimes they clearly conflict, as when giving up your life or fortune would do much more good for others. Also called Sidgwick’s dualism. Held by Henry Sidgwick, Roger Crisp.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Utilitarianism","Ethical egoism","Rational egoism (Objectivist ethics)"],"thinkers":["sidgwick"],"position":"dualism_of_practical_reason","tags":["sidgwick","henry sidgwick","egoism vs utilitarianism","methods of ethics","sidgwick’s dualism"]},{"term":"Theological voluntarism","def":"Things are good or right simply because God wills or commands them.","more":"Theological voluntarism (from the Latin for “will”) takes the first horn of the Euthyphro dilemma. In Plato’s dialogue Euthyphro, Socrates asks whether the pious is loved by the gods because it is pious, or pious because it is loved by the gods. Asked of morality: is an act right because God commands it, or does God command it because it is right? The voluntarist answers that God’s will makes it right. Also called Divine command theory (strong), Ethical voluntarism. Held by William of Ockham, Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari, Philip L. Quinn.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Modified divine command theory","Theological determinism"],"thinkers":["ockham","ashari"],"position":"theological_voluntarism","tags":["divine command theory","voluntarism","ockham","euthyphro dilemma","god's will","divine command theory (strong)","ethical voluntarism"]},{"term":"Aristotelian virtue ethics","def":"The virtues are the traits a human being needs to flourish, and practical wisdom shows how to use them.","more":"Aristotelian virtue ethics follows Aristotle’s Nicomachean Ethics. Every living thing has a way of flourishing; for humans it is eudaimonia, a life of rational activity done well, lived among friends and fellow citizens. The virtues are stable traits of character, such as courage, justice, generosity and temperance, that make that life possible. Each is a mean between two vices: courage lies between cowardice and recklessness. Also called Neo-Aristotelian virtue ethics, Eudaimonist virtue ethics. Held by Aristotle, Philippa Foot, Rosalind Hursthouse, Alasdair MacIntyre.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Eudaimonism","Aristotelian naturalism","Qualified-agent account of right action"],"thinkers":["aristotle","foot","hursthouse","macintyre","nussbaum"],"position":"aristotelian_virtue_ethics","tags":["aristotelian ethics","aristotle","nicomachean ethics","eudaimonia","golden mean","phronesis","neo-aristotelian virtue ethics","eudaimonist virtue ethics"]},{"term":"Confucian ethics","def":"A good life is cultivated through family, ritual and learning, until care and propriety become second nature.","more":"Confucian ethics comes from Confucius (Kongzi) and his followers, above all Mencius and Xunzi. Its ideal is the junzi, the exemplary person, who has cultivated ren (humaneness, warm concern for others), yi (rightness), li (ritual propriety, the forms of respectful conduct) and zhi (wisdom). Moral life begins in the family, in filial piety toward parents, and widens outward. Mencius held that human nature contains ‘sprouts’ of goodness, such as the alarm anyone feels seeing a child about to fall into a well, which need cultivating; Xunzi held that our nature is crude and must be shaped by ritual and teachers. Also called Ru ethics. Held by Confucius, Mencius, Xunzi, Zhu Xi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Confucian role ethics","Graded love"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius","xunzi","zhuxi"],"position":"confucian_ethics","tags":["confucianism","confucius","ren","li","filial piety","junzi","ru ethics"]},{"term":"Sentimentalist virtue ethics","def":"Virtues are the traits of character that people, through sympathy, naturally approve of.","more":"Sentimentalist virtue ethics grounds the virtues in human feeling rather than in reason or human function. David Hume argued that we call a trait a virtue when, viewing it from a general point of view, it arouses approval in us through sympathy, our capacity to feel with others. The traits we approve of are those useful or agreeable to their owner or to others: benevolence, justice, cheerfulness, wit, prudence. Adam Smith added the figure of the impartial spectator, an imagined fair observer whose sympathy we test ourselves against. Also called Humean virtue ethics. Held by David Hume, Adam Smith, Michael Slote.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Moral sense theory","Care ethics"],"thinkers":["hume","smith","slote"],"position":"sentimentalist_virtue_ethics","tags":["sentimentalist virtue","hume","michael slote","slote","sympathy","empathy","humean virtue ethics"]},{"term":"Target-centred virtue ethics","def":"An act is right when it hits the targets of the virtues, the things that kindness, honesty and the rest aim at.","more":"Target-centred virtue ethics was developed by Christine Swanton. Each virtue has a field and a target. The field of generosity is giving; its target is giving the right amount, to the right people, in the right way. An act is virtuous when it hits the target, and right when it is overall virtuous in its situation, hitting the targets of all the relevant virtues together. Also called Pluralistic virtue ethics. Held by Christine Swanton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Qualified-agent account of right action"],"thinkers":[],"position":"target_centred_virtue_ethics","tags":["target-centered virtue ethics","christine swanton","swanton","virtue ethics","targets of virtue","pluralistic virtue ethics"]},{"term":"Agent-based virtue ethics","def":"An act is right because it comes from a good motive; the goodness of motives comes first.","more":"Agent-based virtue ethics, defended by Michael Slote in Morals from Motives (2001), is the most radical form of virtue ethics. Most theories explain good motives in terms of something else, such as good results or right rules. Agent-based ethics reverses this: the basic moral facts are about inner motives, like benevolence or caring, and an act is wrong if it expresses a bad or deficient motive, and right if it does not. Slote first built this on ‘universal benevolence’ and later on caring and empathy, linking it to care ethics. Also called Motive-based virtue ethics. Held by Michael Slote, Linda Zagzebski.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Sentimentalist virtue ethics","Care ethics"],"thinkers":["slote","zagzebski"],"position":"agent_based_virtue_ethics","tags":["agent-based","michael slote","slote","motives","virtue ethics","motive-based virtue ethics"]},{"term":"Qualified-agent account of right action","def":"An act is right if it is what a virtuous person would characteristically do in your circumstances.","more":"’. Rosalind Hursthouse stated it as: an action is right if and only if it is what a virtuous agent would characteristically (that is, acting in character) do in the circumstances. A virtuous agent is one who has and exercises the virtues, such as honesty, justice and kindness. The account parallels its rivals: consequentialists define right action through best results, deontologists through correct rules, and virtue ethicists through the virtuous agent. Also called Hursthouse’s criterion, V1. Held by Rosalind Hursthouse.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Virtue ethics","Aristotelian virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["hursthouse"],"position":"qualified_agent_account","tags":["qualified agent","hursthouse","rosalind hursthouse","what would a virtuous person do","virtuous person","hursthouse’s criterion","v1"]},{"term":"Unity of the virtues","def":"You cannot fully have one virtue without the others, because each needs practical wisdom.","more":"The unity (or reciprocity) of the virtues is the thesis that the virtues come as a package. In Plato’s Protagoras, Socrates argues that the virtues are in some way one, all forms of knowledge of good and bad. Aristotle gave the most influential version: no one can have a full virtue without practical wisdom, the skill of judging what matters in a situation, and anyone with practical wisdom will have all the virtues. So someone brave in war but unjust lacks real courage: they face danger for the wrong ends, in the wrong way. Also called Reciprocity of the virtues. Held by Socrates, Aristotle, Thomas Aquinas.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Aristotelian virtue ethics","Stoicism"],"thinkers":["socrates","aristotle","aquinas"],"position":"unity_of_virtues","tags":["unity of virtue","reciprocity of the virtues","socrates","aristotle","practical wisdom"]},{"term":"Disunity of the virtues","def":"The virtues are separate traits; a person can have some without the others.","more":"The disunity thesis holds that the virtues are distinct traits that can come apart. A soldier can be truly brave while serving an unjust cause; a fiercely honest person can be unkind. Owen Flanagan, among others, argued that the classical unity thesis fits neither psychology nor ordinary moral experience, and that people have different moral personalities, each with its own strengths. On this view, we should praise real virtues where we find them, even in otherwise bad people, and we should not expect moral perfection. Also called Independence of the virtues. Held by Owen Flanagan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Situationism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"disunity_of_virtues","tags":["disunity of virtue","virtue ethics","fragmented character","separate virtues","independence of the virtues"]},{"term":"Situationism","def":"Behaviour depends far more on small features of situations than on stable character traits.","more":"Situationism is a challenge to virtue ethics from social psychology. In famous experiments, small features of a situation predicted behaviour better than character: shoppers who had just found a coin in a phone booth were much more likely to help a stranger; seminary students in a hurry often walked past a man in distress, even on the way to talk about the Good Samaritan; ordinary people in Stanley Milgram’s study obeyed orders to give what they believed were dangerous shocks. Gilbert Harman concluded that there may be no character traits of the kind virtue ethics assumes. John Doris argued more moderately that traits are ‘local’ (honest about exams, not about taxes) and that we should manage situations, not rely on character. Also called Situationist critique of virtue ethics. Held by Gilbert Harman, John M. Doris.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Disunity of the virtues"],"thinkers":["harman"],"position":"situationism","tags":["situationist","character skepticism","doris","harman","milgram","character traits","situationist critique of virtue ethics"]},{"term":"Confucian role ethics","def":"Morality consists in living your roles well, as a child, parent, friend or neighbour.","more":"Confucian role ethics is a reading of Confucianism developed by Roger Ames and Henry Rosemont. They argue that early Confucians did not think of persons as separate individuals who then enter relationships; rather, a person simply is the web of roles they live: son or daughter, sibling, friend, teacher, neighbour. Living these roles well, with appropriate feeling and ritual, is what morality is. Ames and Rosemont say this makes Confucianism a distinct kind of ethics, not a version of Western virtue ethics, which still centres on traits of individual agents. Also called Role ethics. Held by Roger T. Ames, Henry Rosemont Jr..","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Confucian ethics","Special obligations","Care ethics"],"thinkers":[],"position":"confucian_role_ethics","tags":["role ethics","roger ames","ames","henry rosemont","confucianism","roles"]},{"term":"Moral generalism","def":"A feature that is a moral reason in one case keeps its moral pull in every case.","more":"Moral generalism holds that moral thinking depends on general principles. In its classic form, atomism about reasons, a feature that counts morally in one place counts the same way everywhere: causing pain is always a reason against an act, keeping a promise always a reason for it, even when other reasons outweigh it. W. D. Also called Atomism about reasons, Principled ethics. Held by W. D. Ross, Brad Hooker, Sean McKeever, Michael Ridge.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Rossian pluralism"],"thinkers":["ross","hooker"],"position":"moral_generalism","tags":["generalism","moral principles","particularism","principles","moral rules","atomism about reasons","principled ethics"]},{"term":"Thomistic natural law","def":"Natural law is our reasoned share in God’s order, read off from the goods our nature inclines us to.","more":"Thomistic natural law follows Thomas Aquinas. Aquinas held that God governs the universe by an eternal law, and that rational creatures share in it through the natural law, which they grasp by reason. Its first principle is ‘good is to be done and pursued, and evil avoided’. We know what is good by attending to our natural inclinations: like all beings we seek to preserve ourselves; like animals we seek to mate and raise offspring; as rational beings we seek to know the truth, including about God, and to live in society. Also called Classical natural law, Thomism. Held by Thomas Aquinas, Ralph McInerny, Anthony J. Lisska.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Natural law theory","Aristotelian naturalism","Doctrine of double effect"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"position":"thomistic_natural_law","tags":["natural law","aquinas","thomas aquinas","thomism","eternal law","lex naturalis","classical natural law"]},{"term":"Aristotelian naturalism","def":"Moral goodness is the natural goodness of a human being, judged as we judge plants and animals by their kind.","more":"Aristotelian naturalism, developed by Philippa Foot, Michael Thompson and Rosalind Hursthouse, holds that moral evaluation is a special case of evaluating living things. We say an oak with shallow roots is defective, or a wolf that does not hunt with its pack is a poor wolf, by reference to how members of the species live. Human beings, too, have a form of life, and it includes practical reason, cooperation, promise-keeping and care for children. A person lacking justice or courage is defective as a human being in a similar way. Also called Neo-Aristotelian naturalism, Natural goodness. Held by Philippa Foot, Rosalind Hursthouse, Michael Thompson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Aristotelian virtue ethics","Natural law theory","Moral realism"],"thinkers":["foot","hursthouse"],"position":"aristotelian_naturalism","tags":["neo-aristotelian naturalism","natural goodness","philippa foot","foot","hursthouse","thompson"]},{"term":"Divine simplicity view of ethics","def":"God’s will and God’s goodness are not two things, so asking which comes first is a confusion.","more":"The divine simplicity view applies an old doctrine about God to ethics. According to divine simplicity, defended by Augustine, Anselm and Thomas Aquinas, God has no parts: God does not have goodness, will and wisdom as separate properties but simply is them. If so, the Euthyphro question, whether God wills things because they are good or they are good because God wills them, rests on a false split. God’s will is God’s goodness. Also called Divine simplicity. Held by Thomas Aquinas, Anselm of Canterbury, Eleonore Stump.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Modified divine command theory","Natural law theory"],"thinkers":["aquinas","anselm","stump"],"position":"divine_simplicity_view","tags":["divine simplicity","euthyphro dilemma","aquinas","god's nature","god's goodness"]},{"term":"Teleological suspension of the ethical","def":"Faith can require a person to go beyond the ethical, in a direct relation to God that cannot be justified to others.","more":"The teleological suspension of the ethical is an idea from Søren Kierkegaard’s Fear and Trembling (1843), written under the pen name Johannes de silentio. Ethics, in the sense of universal duties that can be explained to anyone, forbids a father to kill his son. Yet Abraham is honoured as the father of faith for being willing to sacrifice Isaac at God’s command. Kierkegaard argues that either there is a ‘teleological suspension of the ethical’, in which the individual stands in a higher, direct relation to God (the ‘telos’, or goal), or Abraham is simply a murderer. Also called Kierkegaard on Abraham. Held by Søren Kierkegaard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Divine command theory","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["kierkegaard"],"position":"teleological_suspension_of_the_ethical","tags":["teleological suspension","kierkegaard","søren kierkegaard","abraham and isaac","fear and trembling","knight of faith","kierkegaard on abraham"]},{"term":"Psychological egoism","def":"Everyone always acts, in the end, only for their own benefit.","more":"Psychological egoism is a claim about human motivation, not about morality: it says that every voluntary action is ultimately aimed at the agent’s own good, whether pleasure, safety, reputation or a quiet conscience. Even apparent sacrifice, on this view, is really self-interested: the rescuer wants to feel good or avoid guilt. Thomas Hobbes is often read as holding it, though some scholars dispute that reading, and François de La Rochefoucauld’s maxims expose self-love behind virtues. It differs from ethical egoism, which says we ought to pursue our own good: if psychological egoism were true, ethical egoism would be trivially easy or pointless. Held by Thomas Hobbes, François de La Rochefoucauld.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Ethical egoism"],"thinkers":["hobbes"],"position":"psychological_egoism","tags":["egoism","selfishness","self-interest","hobbes","altruism","butler"]},{"term":"Epicurean ethics","def":"Your own pleasure, understood as a calm life free from pain and fear, is the goal of life.","more":"Epicurean ethics comes from Epicurus, who taught in his Garden in Athens around 300 BCE. He held that pleasure is the goal of life, but by pleasure he meant mainly ataraxia (peace of mind) and freedom from bodily pain, not luxury. Simple food, friends and philosophy give the most reliable pleasure; ambition, fame and great wealth bring anxiety. Since the aim is the agent’s own tranquil life, Epicureanism is usually classed as egoistic hedonism, though Epicurus prized friendship very highly and some scholars think he valued friends for their own sake. Also called Epicureanism, Epicurean egoism. Held by Epicurus, Lucretius.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Ethical egoism","Hedonism"],"thinkers":["epicurus","lucretius"],"position":"epicureanism","tags":["epicureanism","epicurus","ataraxia","aponia","tranquility","pleasure","epicurean egoism"]},{"term":"Rational egoism (Objectivist ethics)","def":"Each person’s own life is their highest value; reason, productiveness and independence are the virtues it requires.","more":"Rational egoism, in Ayn Rand’s Objectivist form, holds that each person’s own life, as a rational being, is their ultimate value, and that morality is a code of values for living it. Its central virtues are rationality, independence, integrity, honesty, justice, productiveness and pride. Rand rejected altruism, which she understood as the doctrine that self-sacrifice for others is the essence of virtue, and also rejected ‘whim-worship’ hedonism and the use of force. On her view, rational interests do not conflict: people deal with each other as traders, exchanging value for value. Also called Objectivism, Randian egoism. Held by Ayn Rand, Tara Smith.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Ethical egoism","Eudaimonism"],"thinkers":["rand"],"position":"rational_egoism","tags":["objectivism","ayn rand","rand","virtue of selfishness","rational self-interest","egoism","randian egoism"]},{"term":"Enlightened self-interest","def":"Pursue your own interest, but wisely: in the long run, honesty and cooperation usually serve you best.","more":"Enlightened self-interest is the view that people should act for their own good, but with foresight: since we live among others, keeping promises, being honest and helping people usually pays in the long run, through trust, reputation and the help we get back. Thomas Hobbes argued that rational people seeking their own safety would agree to rules of peace and keep them. Alexis de Tocqueville described Americans as explaining their good deeds by ‘self-interest rightly understood’. David Gauthier argued that a rational person would become a ‘constrained maximiser’, disposed to keep fair agreements even when cheating would pay in the moment, because such people are trusted and do better overall. Also called Self-interest rightly understood. Held by Thomas Hobbes, David Gauthier.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Ethical egoism","Contractarianism"],"thinkers":["hobbes"],"position":"enlightened_self_interest","tags":["self-interest rightly understood","tocqueville","rational self-interest","long-term interest","cooperation"]},{"term":"Ethics of ambiguity","def":"To truly will your own freedom, you must also will the freedom of others.","more":"The ethics of ambiguity is Simone de Beauvoir’s existentialist ethics, set out in The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947). Human beings are ambiguous: free subjects who make meaning, yet also bodies and objects in a world of others, limited and mortal. There are no values given in advance, but that does not mean anything goes. Since my freedom is realised only through projects in a shared world, and others can take up or destroy what I do, to will myself free is also to will others free. Also called Beauvoir’s existentialist ethics. Held by Simone de Beauvoir.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Existentialism","Situated freedom"],"thinkers":["beauvoir"],"position":"ethics_of_ambiguity","tags":["de beauvoir","simone de beauvoir","beauvoir","existentialist ethics","freedom","ambiguity","beauvoir’s existentialist ethics"]},{"term":"Situated freedom","def":"Freedom is real but always exercised within a situation that can genuinely limit it, especially for the oppressed.","more":"Situated freedom is the view that human freedom is always freedom in a situation: a body, a history, a society, other people. Maurice Merleau-Ponty argued against the early Sartre that freedom is not absolute; our situation gives our choices their shape and weight. Simone de Beauvoir argued in The Second Sex (1949) that women had been made into ‘the Other’ by social conditions, so their apparent passivity was not simply bad faith but the result of oppression that limited their possibilities. On this view, it is wrong to blame the oppressed for not choosing freely, and right to change the conditions that confine them. Also called Ethics of ambiguity. Held by Simone de Beauvoir, Maurice Merleau-Ponty.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Ethics of ambiguity","Existentialism","Radical freedom","Socially made human nature"],"thinkers":["beauvoir"],"position":"situated_freedom","tags":["beauvoir","simone de beauvoir","merleau-ponty","existentialism","oppression","ethics of ambiguity"]},{"term":"Nietzschean perfectionism","def":"Morality should serve the flourishing of higher, creative human beings, who create and live by their own values.","more":"Nietzschean perfectionism is a reading of Friedrich Nietzsche’s ethics. Nietzsche attacked what he called ‘morality’, especially the Christian and Kantian ethics of pity, equality and self-denial, arguing in On the Genealogy of Morality (1887) that it grew from the resentment of the weak against the strong. In its place he praised ‘higher types’ who create their own values, affirm life, including suffering, and would will their lives to recur eternally. On the perfectionist reading, defended by Thomas Hurka among others, Nietzsche holds that some lives are genuinely higher than others and that excellence, not equal welfare, is what matters. Also called Nietzschean value creation, Revaluation of values. Held by Friedrich Nietzsche.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Existentialism"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"position":"nietzschean_perfectionism","tags":["nietzsche","perfectionism","ubermensch","übermensch","overman","higher types","nietzschean value creation","revaluation of values"]},{"term":"Temporal neutrality","def":"You should care equally about every part of your future, giving no less weight to your old age than to tomorrow.","more":"Temporal neutrality is the classic view of prudence (rational self-interest): a pain or pleasure matters just as much whether it comes today or in 40 years, as long as it is yours. Being later is not in itself a reason to care less, though uncertainty can be. Henry Sidgwick defended this as a basic principle of reason, and Thomas Nagel argued that prudence requires seeing your present as just one time among others in your life. This view grounds ordinary advice such as saving for retirement, not smoking and studying for exams. Also called Self-interest theory, Equal concern for the future. Held by Henry Sidgwick, Thomas Nagel.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Identity matters"],"thinkers":["sidgwick","nagel"],"position":"temporal_neutrality","tags":["time neutrality","prudence","future self","discounting","parfit","self-interest theory","equal concern for the future"]},{"term":"Present-aim theory","def":"What you have reason to do depends on what you care about now; caring about your future is rational only if you do care.","more":"The present-aim theory says that the rational thing to do is what best serves your present aims, whatever they are. If you now care about your future, you have reason to save and stay healthy. If you do not, reason alone does not force you to. David Hume held that it is not contrary to reason to prefer a lesser good to a greater one, since reason is the servant of our desires, not their judge. Also called Critical present-aim theory. Held by David Hume.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Discounting by connectedness"],"thinkers":["hume"],"position":"present_aim_theory","tags":["present aim theory","parfit","present desires","prudence","future self","self-interest theory","critical present-aim theory"]},{"term":"Practice-based care ethics","def":"Care is above all a practice: the work of noticing and meeting needs, which morality and politics should value.","more":"Practice-based care ethics treats care first as work, not a feeling or a trait. Joan Tronto and Berenice Fisher defined caring broadly as everything we do to maintain, continue and repair our world so that we can live in it as well as possible. Tronto distinguishes four phases (caring about, taking care of, care-giving and care-receiving) with matching moral qualities: attentiveness, responsibility, competence and responsiveness. Virginia Held likewise calls care both a practice and a value. Also called care as a practice. Held by Joan Tronto, Virginia Held, Berenice Fisher.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Care ethics","Relational care ethics","Virtue-based care ethics"],"thinkers":["tronto","held"],"position":"practice_based_care_ethics","tags":["care ethics","ethics of care","joan tronto","tronto","virginia held","care work","care as a practice"]},{"term":"Relational care ethics","def":"Care is a relation between two people, completed when the cared-for receives and responds to the carer’s attention.","more":"Relational care ethics, developed by Nel Noddings in Caring (1984), puts the caring relation at the centre of ethics. In caring, the carer (the ‘one-caring’) is engrossed in the other person and her motivation is displaced toward his needs; the cared-for completes the relation by receiving and responding to that care. Noddings distinguishes natural caring, which we do from love, from ethical caring, which we summon when love fails, guided by our memory of being cared for and by an ideal of ourselves as carers. Moral life, on this view, is not about applying principles but about meeting particular others. Also called Noddings’s ethics of caring. Held by Nel Noddings.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Care ethics","Practice-based care ethics"],"thinkers":["noddings"],"position":"relational_care_ethics","tags":["care ethics","ethics of care","nel noddings","noddings","caring relation","cared-for","noddings’s ethics of caring"]},{"term":"Virtue-based care ethics","def":"Care is a virtue: a trait of empathic concern for others, part of a good character.","more":"Virtue-based care ethics treats caring as a trait of character. Michael Slote argues that empathic caring is the basic moral motive: an act is right if it expresses, or at least does not show a lack of, fully developed empathic concern for others. He has tried to build a complete ethics, justice included, on this basis. Raja Halwani (2003) takes a more modest line: care is a central virtue, but it belongs inside an Aristotelian virtue ethics, where practical wisdom and other virtues such as justice and honesty guide and limit it. Also called care as a virtue. Held by Michael Slote, Raja Halwani.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Care ethics","Sentimentalist virtue ethics","Agent-based virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["slote"],"position":"virtue_based_care_ethics","tags":["care ethics","ethics of care","michael slote","slote","empathy","empathic caring","care as a virtue"]},{"term":"Neo-Rossian pluralism","def":"Ross was right that there are several basic duties, but his list needs extending and his view needs firmer foundations.","more":"Neo-Rossian pluralism keeps W. D. Ross’s main idea: there are several basic prima facie duties (duties that hold unless a stronger duty outweighs them), known by intuition, with no single master principle. But it revises his list and its grounding. Also called Kantian intuitionism. Held by Robert Audi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Rossian pluralism","Moral pluralism","Principlism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"neo_rossian_pluralism","tags":["ross","w. d. ross","audi","robert audi","prima facie duties","pluralism","kantian intuitionism"]},{"term":"Utilitarianism for animals, Kantianism for people","def":"Animals’ welfare counts and may be traded off for the greater good, but persons have rights that may never be traded off.","more":"This view splits morality in two. For animals, what matters is their pleasure and pain, so we must weigh their suffering, but one animal may be sacrificed if that produces more good overall. For persons, beings who can reflect on their lives and shape them, there are side constraints: limits on what may be done to them, whatever the gains. Robert Nozick named and floated the view in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), without fully endorsing it. Also called two-tier view of moral status, the morality of respect and the morality of interests. Held by Jeff McMahan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Rights-based deontology","Hierarchical view of moral status"],"thinkers":["mcmahan"],"position":"utilitarianism_for_animals_kantianism_for_people","tags":["nozick","robert nozick","animals","animal ethics","side constraints","utilitarianism","two-tier view of moral status","the morality of respect and the morality of interests"]},{"term":"Mencian Confucianism","def":"Human nature is good: we are born with sprouts of the virtues, which need cultivating.","more":"Mencian Confucianism follows Mencius (Mengzi, 4th century BCE), the most influential Confucian after Confucius himself. Its central claim is that human nature is good. Everyone has four ‘sprouts’ (beginnings): compassion, shame at wrongdoing, deference to others, and a sense of right and wrong. These grow into the four virtues of humaneness (ren), rightness (yi), ritual propriety (li) and wisdom (zhi). Also called Mencius’s theory that human nature is good. Held by Mencius, Zhu Xi, Wang Yangming.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Confucian ethics","Xunzian Confucianism"],"thinkers":["mencius","zhuxi","wangyangming"],"position":"mencian_confucianism","tags":["mencius","mengzi","four sprouts","human nature is good","confucianism","moral cultivation","mencius’s theory that human nature is good"]},{"term":"Xunzian Confucianism","def":"Human nature is bad: goodness is made through ritual, teachers and deliberate effort.","more":"Xunzian Confucianism follows Xunzi (3rd century BCE), whose chapter ‘Human Nature Is Bad’ answers Mencius directly. Left to itself, Xunzi says, human nature seeks profit, envies others and craves pleasures, and if followed it leads to strife and disorder. Goodness is the product of deliberate effort (wei): the sages created ritual (li) and standards of right, and through teachers, study and long practice people are reshaped, as crooked wood is steamed against a straightening board or clay is shaped by a potter. Xunzi is still a Confucian: he prizes ritual, learning and the exemplary person. Also called Xunzi’s theory that human nature is bad. Held by Xunzi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Confucian ethics","Mencian Confucianism"],"thinkers":["xunzi"],"position":"xunzian_confucianism","tags":["xunzi","hsun tzu","human nature is bad","ritual","li","confucianism","xunzi’s theory that human nature is bad"]},{"term":"Principlism","def":"Hard cases are settled by specifying and weighing a few basic principles, such as respecting autonomy and doing no harm.","more":"Principlism is the method made famous by Tom Beauchamp and James Childress in Principles of Biomedical Ethics (1979). It holds that four principles, respect for autonomy, non-maleficence (not harming), beneficence (doing good) and justice, capture the core of common morality for medicine. Like W. D. Also called the four-principles approach. Held by Tom Beauchamp, James Childress.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Rossian pluralism","Neo-Rossian pluralism","Moral pluralism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"principlism","tags":["beauchamp and childress","four principles","bioethics","autonomy","beneficence","the four-principles approach"]},{"term":"Casuistry","def":"Reason about hard moral cases by comparing them with clear cases, not by applying theories.","more":"Casuistry (from the Latin casus, ‘case’) is the method of reasoning about moral problems by comparing a new case with clear ‘paradigm’ cases whose answers no one doubts, and reasoning by analogy. It flourished among Catholic moral theologians in the 16th and 17th centuries, then fell into disrepute after Blaise Pascal mocked its excesses. Albert Jonsen and Stephen Toulmin revived it in The Abuse of Casuistry (1988), drawing on their work for a US national commission on medical research ethics. They noticed that members who disagreed about theory often agreed about particular cases. Held by Albert Jonsen, Stephen Toulmin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Moral particularism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"casuistry","tags":["casuistic reasoning","case-based reasoning","jonsen","toulmin","cases","jesuits"]},{"term":"Proportionalism","def":"A harmful act can be right if there is a proportionate reason: enough good at stake to justify it.","more":"Proportionalism is a view developed by Catholic moral theologians from the 1960s, among them Peter Knauer, Louis Janssens, Josef Fuchs and Richard McCormick. Traditional Catholic teaching held that some kinds of act, such as lying or directly killing an innocent person, are always wrong whatever the circumstances. Proportionalists distinguished ‘pre-moral’ (or ontic) evils, such as death, pain and deception, from moral evil. Causing a pre-moral evil is wrong, they said, only when there is no proportionate reason for it, judged by the whole act in its context. Held by Richard McCormick, Peter Knauer, Josef Fuchs, Louis Janssens.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Ethics","related":["Natural law theory"],"thinkers":[],"position":"proportionalism","tags":["proportionate reason","catholic moral theology","knauer","double effect","consequentialism"]},{"term":"Egalitarian liberalism","def":"People should have strong rights and freedoms, and society should be arranged so that bad luck of birth does not decide how their lives go.","more":"Egalitarian liberalism is the family of views that combines two commitments. The first is liberal: each person has basic rights (to speak, worship, vote and live as they choose) that no majority may override. The second is egalitarian (from “equal”): a just society does not let wealth, power and opportunity depend on the luck of who your parents were. Its most famous statement is John Rawls’s theory of “justice as fairness”. Also called liberal egalitarianism, left liberalism, social liberalism. Held by John Rawls, Ronald Dworkin, Martha Nussbaum, Elizabeth Anderson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["The difference principle (justice as fairness)","Luck egalitarianism","Liberal neutrality"],"thinkers":["rawls","dworkin","nussbaum","anderson_e","scanlon"],"position":"egalitarian_liberalism","tags":["liberal egalitarianism","rawls","john rawls","dworkin","ronald dworkin","luck egalitarianism","left liberalism","social liberalism"]},{"term":"Classical liberalism","def":"Government should be limited: it protects rights, enforces the rule of law and leaves most of economic life to free markets.","more":"Classical liberalism grew from thinkers such as John Locke, Adam Smith and the defenders of constitutional government in the 1700s and 1800s. It holds that individual liberty, private property, free trade and the rule of law (clear rules applying equally to everyone, including rulers) are the foundations of a free and prosperous society. The state has real jobs: defence, police, courts, some public goods such as roads, and often a basic safety net. But it should not try to plan the economy or impose a pattern of equal outcomes. Also called market liberalism. Held by John Locke, Adam Smith, Friedrich Hayek, Benjamin Constant.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Libertarianism","Negative liberty","Lockean theory of property"],"thinkers":["locke","smith","hayek","constant"],"position":"classical_liberalism","tags":["limited government","laissez-faire","free market","individual rights","rule of law","locke","adam smith","market liberalism"]},{"term":"Socialism","def":"Land, factories and major industries should be owned and controlled in common, not by private owners who profit from others’ work.","more":"Socialism is a family of views holding that the means of production (land, factories, machines and major industries) should be owned in common, by the public or by the workers, rather than privately. Karl Marx argued that under capitalism owners profit from the labour of workers who own nothing but their ability to work, so the system is built on exploitation, and predicted it would give way to a classless society. Not all socialists are Marxists. Democratic socialists seek common ownership through elections and law; market socialists keep markets but make firms worker-owned; some, such as Julius Nyerere, drew on local traditions of shared life. Also called Marxism (one form), democratic socialism (one form). Held by Karl Marx, G. A. Cohen, Eduard Bernstein, Julius Nyerere.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Political radicalism","Relational egalitarianism"],"thinkers":["marx","ga_cohen","bernstein","nyerere"],"position":"socialism","tags":["common ownership","public ownership","collectivism","marxism","workers","equality","nationalisation","marxism (one form)","democratic socialism (one form)"]},{"term":"Entitlement theory","def":"A distribution is just if everyone got what they have through fair acquisition and free transfer, however unequal the result.","more":"Entitlement theory, set out by Robert Nozick, says justice in holdings is historical, not patterned. A patterned theory says a just distribution must match some formula: equal shares, shares by need, shares that help the worst-off. Nozick’s theory has three parts. Justice in acquisition: how unowned things may first come to be owned. Also called historical theory of justice, Nozickian justice. Held by Robert Nozick, Murray Rothbard, Jan Narveson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Libertarianism","Lockean theory of property"],"thinkers":["nozick","rothbard","narveson"],"position":"entitlement_theory","tags":["nozick","historical entitlement","just acquisition","just transfer","libertarianism","property rights","historical theory of justice","nozickian justice"]},{"term":"Utilitarian theory of justice","def":"The just distribution is whichever produces the greatest total wellbeing, counting everyone equally.","more":"Utilitarianism judges actions and institutions by how much wellbeing they produce. Applied to justice, it says the best way to share out income, wealth and opportunities is the one that maximises wellbeing overall, with each person’s gains counting the same. This often supports a lot of redistribution, because of diminishing marginal utility: an extra hundred dollars matters far more to a poor family than to a millionaire. But equality is valued only as a means. Also called welfarist justice. Held by Jeremy Bentham, John Stuart Mill, Henry Sidgwick, Peter Singer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Deterrence theory of punishment"],"thinkers":["bentham","mill","sidgwick","singer"],"position":"utilitarian_justice","tags":["utilitarianism","greatest happiness","welfare maximisation","distributive justice","wellbeing","welfarist justice"]},{"term":"Luck egalitarianism","def":"It is unjust for some to be worse off than others through bad luck, but not through their own choices.","more":"Luck egalitarianism holds that inequalities are fair when they reflect people’s choices and unfair when they reflect luck they did not choose. It distinguishes “brute luck” (being born with a disability, a fire, the family you are born into) from “option luck” (the results of gambles you chose to take). Society should compensate people for bad brute luck, but people should bear the costs of their own choices: someone who chooses to work less, or to take a risky bet, cannot demand that others make up the difference. Richard Arneson and G. Also called responsibility-sensitive egalitarianism. Held by Ronald Dworkin, Richard Arneson, G. A. Cohen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Egalitarian liberalism","Equality of resources","Equal opportunity for welfare"],"thinkers":["dworkin","arneson","ga_cohen"],"position":"luck_egalitarianism","tags":["brute luck","option luck","equality","responsibility","dworkin","cohen","fairness","responsibility-sensitive egalitarianism"]},{"term":"Relational egalitarianism","def":"Justice means people relate to each other as equals, without domination or social hierarchy; equal shares matter only as a means to that.","more":"Relational egalitarianism says the point of equality is not that everyone gets the same amount of something, but that people stand in relations of equals. Elizabeth Anderson argued that egalitarian movements historically fought against oppression: aristocracy, slavery, caste and racial hierarchy, where some people could command, exclude or look down on others. A just society, on this view, is one where no one is dominated, exploited, marginalised or treated as inferior, and where everyone can function as a full citizen. That requires a lot of material provision: enough income, education and healthcare to take part in society. Also called social egalitarianism, democratic equality. Held by Elizabeth Anderson, Samuel Scheffler, Iris Marion Young.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Republicanism (freedom as non-domination)","The capability approach","Democratic proceduralism (intrinsic view)"],"thinkers":["anderson_e","scheffler"],"position":"relational_egalitarianism","tags":["social equality","democratic equality","anderson","non-domination","equal respect","hierarchy","social egalitarianism"]},{"term":"Lockean theory of property","def":"People come to own things by working on them, and these property rights exist before and independently of government.","more":"John Locke argued that God gave the earth to humanity in common, yet individuals can come to own parts of it. Each person owns their own body and labour. When you mix your labour with something unowned, picking apples or clearing a field, you make it yours, because no one else may take your labour. Locke added conditions, later called the Lockean proviso: you must leave “enough and as good” for others, and must not take more than you can use before it spoils (though he thought money relaxed this limit). Also called labour theory of property, natural property rights. Held by John Locke, Robert Nozick, Murray Rothbard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Libertarianism","Entitlement theory","Classical liberalism"],"thinkers":["locke","nozick","rothbard"],"position":"lockean_property","tags":["labour theory of property","mixing labour","locke","lockean proviso","original appropriation","property rights","natural property rights"]},{"term":"Conventionalism about property","def":"Property rights are created by law and social rules, so the real question is which property rules are fair.","more":"Conventionalism says that property is not a natural fact but a set of rules people make. David Hume argued that the rules of property arise from convention: people come to see that stable possession serves everyone, so they agree, often without any formal agreement, to respect each other’s holdings. Jeremy Bentham put it more bluntly: before laws there is no property. In modern form, Liam Murphy and Thomas Nagel argue that there is no such thing as your “pre-tax income” that taxes take from you; what you own is defined by the whole legal system, including tax law. Also called legal conventionalism about property. Held by David Hume, Jeremy Bentham, Thomas Nagel, Liam Murphy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Egalitarian liberalism","Utilitarian theory of justice"],"thinkers":["hume","bentham","nagel"],"position":"conventionalism_about_property","tags":["property conventionalism","legal positivism about property","property rights","murphy and nagel","myth of ownership","legal conventionalism about property"]},{"term":"Left-libertarianism","def":"You fully own yourself, but natural resources belong to everyone, so those who use them owe others a fair share of their value.","more":"Left-libertarianism combines a libertarian view of persons with an egalitarian view of the earth. Like libertarians, left-libertarians hold that each person fully owns their own body and labour, so the state may not force anyone to work for others. But unlike right-libertarians, they deny that anyone can simply take natural resources (land, minerals, the airwaves) for free. Nobody made these, so they belong in some sense to everyone. Also called Georgism (one form), geolibertarianism. Held by Hillel Steiner, Henry George, Thomas Paine, Michael Otsuka.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Libertarianism","Lockean theory of property"],"thinkers":["steiner","henry_george","paine"],"position":"left_libertarianism","tags":["self-ownership","common ownership of resources","georgism","steiner","vallentyne","libertarianism","georgism (one form)","geolibertarianism"]},{"term":"Primary goods","def":"Justice should share out the basic goods any person needs whatever their plans: rights, liberties, opportunities, income, wealth and self-respect.","more":"John Rawls proposed measuring how well people fare, for purposes of justice, by their share of primary goods: things any rational person would want, whatever else they want. His list: basic rights and liberties; freedom of movement and choice of occupation; powers and positions of office; income and wealth; and the social bases of self-respect. Rawls preferred this to measuring happiness because people in a free society have different ideas of the good life, and the state should not decide which is best. Primary goods are things everyone can use for their own aims, and they are public and measurable. Also called Rawlsian primary goods, the primary goods metric. Held by John Rawls.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["The difference principle (justice as fairness)","Liberal neutrality"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"position":"primary_goods","tags":["social primary goods","rawls","basic goods","rights and liberties","income and wealth","self-respect","rawlsian primary goods","the primary goods metric"]},{"term":"The capability approach","def":"Justice should be measured by what people are actually able to do and be, not by what they have or how happy they feel.","more":"” with capabilities: people’s real freedoms to achieve valuable “functionings”, such as being well nourished, being healthy, reading, moving about, taking part in community life. ”, arguing that both resources and happiness are poor measures. Resources ignore that people differ in their ability to use them; happiness can adapt to deprivation, so the oppressed may report being content. Nussbaum went further and proposed a list of central capabilities that every just society should guarantee up to a threshold. Also called capabilities approach. Held by Amartya Sen, Martha Nussbaum.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Effective freedom","Sufficientarianism","Relational egalitarianism"],"thinkers":["nussbaum"],"position":"capability_approach","tags":["capabilities approach","capabilities","functionings","amartya sen","martha nussbaum","human development"]},{"term":"Equality of resources","def":"Everyone should start with an equal share of resources, insured against bad luck, and then live with the results of their own choices.","more":"Ronald Dworkin argued that equality should be measured by resources, not by wellbeing. To explain what an equal division would be, he imagined shipwreck survivors on an island holding an auction where everyone has the same number of clamshells to bid with. If, at the end, no one would prefer anyone else’s bundle (the “envy test”), the division is equal. People then make different choices about work and consumption; the results reflect their choices and are fair. Also called Dworkinian equality. Held by Ronald Dworkin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Luck egalitarianism"],"thinkers":["dworkin"],"position":"equality_of_resources","tags":["dworkin","resource egalitarianism","envy test","auction","hypothetical insurance","equality","dworkinian equality"]},{"term":"Equal opportunity for welfare","def":"Everyone should have equally good chances to achieve wellbeing, so that differences in how well people end up come only from their choices.","more":"Equal opportunity for welfare, proposed by Richard Arneson, tries to keep what was attractive in equality of welfare (wellbeing is what really matters) while answering Dworkin’s objection about responsibility. It does not require that everyone end up equally well off. Instead, each person should face an array of options, a decision tree of possible life paths, that gives them the same expected wellbeing as everyone else, given their abilities and circumstances. If two people have equal opportunities and one makes worse choices, the result is fair. Also called equal opportunity for wellbeing. Held by Richard Arneson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Luck egalitarianism","Equal access to advantage"],"thinkers":["arneson"],"position":"equal_opportunity_for_welfare","tags":["arneson","welfare egalitarianism","equal opportunity","luck egalitarianism","wellbeing","equal opportunity for wellbeing"]},{"term":"Equal access to advantage","def":"Justice requires that no one be disadvantaged, in resources or in wellbeing, through no fault or choice of their own.","more":"G. A. Cohen argued that neither resources nor welfare alone is the right currency of egalitarian justice. “Advantage” is a broad notion that includes both resources and wellbeing, as well as other things that matter to how a person fares. Also called Cohen’s currency of egalitarian justice. Held by G. A. Cohen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Luck egalitarianism","Equal opportunity for welfare"],"thinkers":["ga_cohen"],"position":"equal_access_to_advantage","tags":["g.a. cohen","cohen","luck egalitarianism","advantage","equality","cohen’s currency of egalitarian justice"]},{"term":"Consent theory of political obligation","def":"We are bound to obey the state because we have agreed to, openly or by our conduct.","more":"Consent theory says that the state’s authority over you, and your duty to obey it, come from your agreement. The idea is old: in Plato’s Crito, the Laws of Athens tell Socrates he agreed to obey them by choosing to stay in the city all his life. John Locke made consent the foundation of legitimate government: people are born free and equal, so no one can be put under another’s power without consent. Since few people ever sign anything, Locke appealed to tacit consent (agreement shown by conduct rather than words): by owning land, living in a country, or even travelling on its roads, you accept its laws. Also called social contract (consent version). Held by John Locke, Jean-Jacques Rousseau.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Classical liberalism"],"thinkers":["locke","rousseau"],"position":"consent_theory","tags":["consent","social contract","express consent","political obligation","locke","duty to obey","social contract (consent version)"]},{"term":"Natural duty of justice","def":"We must support reasonably just institutions that apply to us, whether or not we agreed to them or benefit from them.","more":"The natural duty view, defended by John Rawls in A Theory of Justice and later by Jeremy Waldron, says that political obligation does not depend on anything we have done, such as consenting or accepting benefits. Everyone has a natural duty (one owed to all people simply as people) to support and comply with just institutions that apply to them, and to help create them where they do not exist. Since a reasonably just state’s laws and courts are such institutions, you owe them obedience, even if you never agreed and would prefer to be left alone. This avoids the main problem for consent theory: most people never consented. Also called natural duty theory. Held by John Rawls, Jeremy Waldron.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Egalitarian liberalism","Liberal account of civil disobedience"],"thinkers":["rawls","waldron"],"position":"natural_duty_of_justice","tags":["natural duty","rawls","political obligation","duty to obey","just institutions","waldron","natural duty theory"]},{"term":"Fair play theory of political obligation","def":"Those who benefit from a cooperative system in which others obey the rules owe it their fair share of obedience.","more":"The fair play theory, first set out by H. L. A. Hart and developed by John Rawls and George Klosko, grounds political obligation in fairness. Also called principle of fairness. Held by H. L. A. Hart, John Rawls, George Klosko.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Natural duty of justice"],"thinkers":["hart","rawls"],"position":"fair_play_theory","tags":["fair play","fairness principle","hart","free rider","political obligation","reciprocity","principle of fairness"]},{"term":"Associative political obligation","def":"Being a member of a political community, like being part of a family, brings obligations you never chose.","more":"Associative theories say political obligation comes from membership, not from consent, benefits or a general duty. We already accept unchosen obligations of this kind: children owe things to their parents, siblings to each other, without ever agreeing to it. Ronald Dworkin argued that a political community can be a “true community” in which members owe each other special obligations, including obedience to its laws, provided the community treats each member with equal concern. John Horton argued that political obligations are part of the very idea of being a member of a polity (an organised political community), just as family obligations are part of being in a family. Also called communitarian theory of obligation, membership theory. Held by Ronald Dworkin, John Horton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Liberal nationalism"],"thinkers":["dworkin"],"position":"associative_political_obligation","tags":["associative obligation","membership","dworkin","political obligation","community","communitarianism","communitarian theory of obligation","membership theory"]},{"term":"Philosophical anarchism","def":"No existing state has a general right to be obeyed, though we need not try to abolish states.","more":"Philosophical anarchism denies that there is a general moral duty to obey the law just because it is the law. , has no special moral grip on us. Robert Paul Wolff argued that authority conflicts with autonomy: a responsible person must decide for herself what to do and cannot hand that over to a ruler. A. Also called political obligation skepticism. Held by Robert Paul Wolff, A. John Simmons, Michael Huemer, Joseph Raz.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Anarchism"],"thinkers":["rp_wolff","simmons","huemer","raz"],"position":"philosophical_anarchism","tags":["anarchism","political obligation","legitimacy","authority","simmons","wolff","political obligation skepticism"]},{"term":"Democratic proceduralism (intrinsic view)","def":"Democracy is valuable in itself because giving everyone an equal say is what treating people as equals requires.","more":"Proceduralists hold that democracy is justified not mainly by its results but by its fairness as a way of deciding. People disagree deeply about justice and the good, and they must still live under shared laws. Thomas Christiano argued that the only way to treat everyone as an equal in such conditions is to give each an equal say: “public equality”, equality everyone can see is being respected. Jeremy Waldron argued that when citizens disagree in good faith about rights, a majority vote among equals is more respectful than leaving the decision to judges or experts. Also called intrinsic justification of democracy, democratic equality. Held by Thomas Christiano, Jeremy Waldron.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Relational egalitarianism","Positive liberty"],"thinkers":["christiano","waldron"],"position":"democratic_proceduralism","tags":["proceduralism","intrinsic value of democracy","political equality","equal say","democracy","fair procedure","intrinsic justification of democracy","democratic equality"]},{"term":"Protective theory of democracy","def":"Democracy’s main value is protection: it lets people remove bad rulers peacefully and stops anyone holding unchecked power.","more":"The protective view justifies democracy by what it prevents. Since no ruler or group can be trusted with unchecked power, the key question is how to limit and remove rulers. ” Democracy, with regular elections, is the answer. Joseph Schumpeter gave a minimal account: democracy is a competition among elites for the people’s vote, not rule by the people in any deep sense. Also called protective democracy, minimalist democracy. Held by Karl Popper, James Madison, Joseph Schumpeter.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Classical liberalism","Piecemeal reform"],"thinkers":["popper"],"position":"protective_democracy","tags":["protective democracy","instrumental democracy","accountability","schumpeter","removing rulers","democracy","minimalist democracy"]},{"term":"Epistemic democracy","def":"Democracy is justified because large, varied groups of ordinary people tend to make good decisions.","more":"Epistemic democrats (from the Greek for knowledge) defend democracy because it tends to get things right. Aristotle suggested that many people together can judge better than a few, as a meal to which many contribute can be better than one provided by a single host. The Marquis de Condorcet proved his jury theorem: if each voter is more likely than not to be right on a yes-or-no question, and votes independently, the majority’s chance of being right rises towards certainty as the group grows. Hélène Landemore argues that diversity of perspectives matters even more than individual expertise, so inclusive democracy outperforms rule by the few. Also called epistemic justification of democracy, democratic reason. Held by Hélène Landemore, David Estlund, Marquis de Condorcet.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Democratic proceduralism (intrinsic view)"],"thinkers":["landemore","estlund"],"position":"epistemic_democracy","tags":["wisdom of crowds","condorcet jury theorem","collective intelligence","democracy","landemore","epistemic justification of democracy","democratic reason"]},{"term":"Political meritocracy","def":"Political leaders should be selected for ability and good character through exams and track records, not chiefly by popular vote.","more":"Political meritocracy holds that political power should go to those best able to use it well. The idea has deep roots in Confucian thought, which held that rulers should be people of virtue and learning, and in imperial China’s civil service examinations, which for centuries selected officials partly by examination. Contemporary defenders such as Daniel A. Bell and Tongdong Bai argue that ordinary elections often reward charisma and short-term promises, while good governance needs knowledge, experience and concern for the long term, including future generations and foreigners who cannot vote. Also called Confucian meritocracy, rule by the virtuous and able. Held by Daniel A. Bell, Tongdong Bai.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Epistocracy"],"thinkers":["daniel_bell"],"position":"political_meritocracy","tags":["meritocracy","rule by the able","confucian meritocracy","daniel bell","china model","technocracy","rule by the virtuous and able"]},{"term":"Epistocracy","def":"People who know more about politics should have more political power, because bad decisions harm everyone.","more":"Epistocracy (from the Greek for “rule by the knowledgeable”) holds that political power should be distributed partly by competence. In Plato’s Republic, rule belongs to philosopher-kings. John Stuart Mill, though a democrat, proposed giving extra votes to the better educated. Jason Brennan’s modern defence keeps universal participation but suggests options such as voting tests, plural votes for those who pass knowledge exams, or “enfranchisement lotteries”, in which a randomly chosen group studies an issue before voting. Also called rule of the knowledgeable. Held by Jason Brennan, John Stuart Mill, Plato.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Political meritocracy"],"thinkers":["brennan","mill","plato"],"position":"epistocracy","tags":["rule of the knowledgeable","knowledge-based voting","jason brennan","against democracy","plural voting","technocracy"]},{"term":"Liberal account of civil disobedience","def":"In a nearly just society, open, non-violent law-breaking to protest serious injustice can be justified if legal means have failed and you accept the penalty.","more":"The liberal account, set out most clearly by John Rawls, defines civil disobedience as a public, non-violent, conscientious act contrary to law, done to bring about a change in law or policy. It appeals to the majority’s own sense of justice. Rawls held it can be justified in a nearly just democracy when there is serious and clear injustice (especially violations of equal basic liberties), when normal legal channels have been tried and failed, and when it does not threaten the working of the constitution. By acting openly and accepting the legal consequences, disobedients show fidelity to law in general while protesting one law. Also called Rawlsian civil disobedience. Held by John Rawls, Ronald Dworkin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Natural duty of justice","Satyagraha (non-violent resistance)"],"thinkers":["rawls","dworkin"],"position":"liberal_civil_disobedience","tags":["civil disobedience","rawls","nonviolent protest","law-breaking","conscientious protest","dissent","rawlsian civil disobedience"]},{"term":"Satyagraha (non-violent resistance)","def":"Unjust laws may and sometimes must be disobeyed, through disciplined non-violence that accepts suffering and seeks to win over opponents.","more":"Satyagraha, a term coined by Mohandas Gandhi, roughly means “holding firmly to truth” or “truth-force”. Gandhi developed it in South Africa and the Indian independence movement as a method of resisting injustice without violence. Its followers refuse to obey unjust laws openly, accept arrest and suffering without retaliating, and aim to convert their opponents rather than defeat or humiliate them. Martin Luther King Jr. Also called Gandhian non-violent resistance, truth-force. Held by Mohandas Gandhi, Martin Luther King Jr..","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Liberal account of civil disobedience"],"thinkers":[],"position":"satyagraha","tags":["non-violent resistance","nonviolence","gandhi","ahimsa","civil disobedience","passive resistance","truth-force","gandhian non-violent resistance"]},{"term":"Strict obedience view","def":"Citizens should never break the law, even to protest injustice, but should seek change only by legal means.","more":"The strict obedience view holds that disobeying the law is never permissible, or almost never, because the law’s authority is what holds society together. Thomas Hobbes argued that without an undivided sovereign power, life returns to a state of war, so subjects must obey (though he allowed that no one can be obliged to give up their own life). Immanuel Kant argued there is no right to rebellion or forcible resistance against the supreme authority, since such a right would destroy the very basis of law. In Plato’s Crito, Socrates refuses to escape his death sentence out of respect for the laws, though how far this reflects a general view is contested, since in the Apology he says he would refuse an order to stop philosophising. Also called no right of resistance. Held by Thomas Hobbes, Immanuel Kant.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Consent theory of political obligation"],"thinkers":["hobbes","kant"],"position":"strict_obedience","tags":["obedience","socrates","crito","rule of law","duty to obey","law and order","no right of resistance"]},{"term":"Principled uncivil disobedience","def":"Against serious injustice, resistance that is covert, disruptive or damages property can sometimes be justified, not only polite civil disobedience.","more":"This view, defended most fully by Candice Delmas, argues that the liberal model of civil disobedience is too narrow. Delmas holds that the same principles that ground political obligation (the natural duty of justice, fairness, the duty to rescue people in danger, and the ties of political membership) can sometimes ground a duty to resist injustice. That resistance need not always be public, non-violent toward property or willing to accept punishment. Covert acts like helping escaped slaves along the Underground Railroad, leaking secret evidence of government wrongdoing, or sabotaging equipment used for abuse can be justified. Also called uncivil disobedience, duty to resist. Held by Candice Delmas.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Satyagraha (non-violent resistance)"],"thinkers":[],"position":"uncivil_disobedience","tags":["uncivil disobedience","covert resistance","sabotage","delmas","resistance","civil disobedience","duty to resist"]},{"term":"Negative liberty","def":"You are free to the extent that no one interferes with your choices.","more":"Negative liberty is freedom understood as the absence of interference: the area in which a person can act without being stopped or coerced by others. Thomas Hobbes defined a free person as one not hindered from doing what they have the will and ability to do. Isaiah Berlin’s essay “Two Concepts of Liberty” made the term famous, distinguishing negative liberty (freedom from) from positive liberty (freedom to be one’s own master). On the negative view, poverty, ill health or lack of skill are not in themselves lacks of freedom, though they may be bad for other reasons. Also called freedom as non-interference. Held by Thomas Hobbes, Isaiah Berlin, Benjamin Constant, Friedrich Hayek.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Classical liberalism","Libertarianism"],"thinkers":["hobbes","berlin","constant","hayek"],"position":"negative_liberty","tags":["freedom from","non-interference","isaiah berlin","liberty","freedom","freedom as non-interference"]},{"term":"Effective freedom","def":"To be free you need real options: the money, health and skills to do what you choose, not just an absence of interference.","more":"Effective freedom says that being free means actually being able to do and be the things you have reason to value. On this view, a person who is formally permitted to travel, study or start a business, but lacks the money, health or education to do so, is not really free to do those things. Amartya Sen developed this idea through the capability approach, describing development as the expansion of people’s real freedoms. Philippe Van Parijs argued for “real freedom for all”, which led him to defend an unconditional basic income (regular cash paid to every person). Also called real freedom, freedom as capability. Held by Amartya Sen, Philippe Van Parijs.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["The capability approach","Egalitarian liberalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"effective_freedom","tags":["real freedom","effective liberty","freedom to","resources","positive freedom","van parijs","freedom as capability"]},{"term":"Republicanism (freedom as non-domination)","def":"You are free when no one has the power to interfere with you at will, whether or not they actually do.","more":"Republicanism here is a tradition of political thought, not any modern party. Its central idea is freedom as non-domination. Drawing on Roman law, Cicero, Machiavelli’s Discourses and English writers of the 1600s, Quentin Skinner and Philip Pettit argue that the opposite of freedom is slavery: being subject to another’s arbitrary will. A slave whose master happens to be kind is still unfree, because the master could interfere at any time. Also called neo-republicanism, civic republicanism, freedom as non-domination. Held by Philip Pettit, Quentin Skinner, Marcus Tullius Cicero, Niccolò Machiavelli.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Relational egalitarianism","Democratic proceduralism (intrinsic view)"],"thinkers":["pettit","skinner","cicero","machiavelli"],"position":"republicanism","tags":["non-domination","freedom as non-domination","pettit","skinner","neo-republicanism","liberty","civic republicanism"]},{"term":"Positive liberty","def":"You are free when you are your own master, guided by your reason and deepest values, and share in governing your community.","more":"Positive liberty understands freedom as self-mastery. Isaiah Berlin described it as the wish to be one’s own master: to be moved by one’s own reasons and purposes, not by external forces or by one’s passions. Jean-Jacques Rousseau held that obeying a law we prescribe for ourselves is freedom, so citizens who share in making the laws through the general will are free. T. Also called freedom as self-mastery, freedom as self-government. Held by Jean-Jacques Rousseau, T. H. Green, Charles Taylor.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Democratic proceduralism (intrinsic view)","Political perfectionism"],"thinkers":["rousseau"],"position":"positive_liberty","tags":["freedom to","self-mastery","autonomy","isaiah berlin","self-government","liberty","freedom as self-mastery","freedom as self-government"]},{"term":"Liberal neutrality","def":"The state should not favour any religion or view of the good life, but rely on reasons that people of any reasonable view could accept.","more":"Liberal neutrality holds that in a free society, where people reasonably disagree about religion and the good life, the state should not take sides on these questions. Its laws should be justified by reasons all reasonable citizens could accept, such as rights, fairness and security, rather than by claims about which religion is true or which way of life is best. John Rawls called this “public reason” and developed it in Political Liberalism, arguing that the “burdens of judgement” (the many sources of honest disagreement) make such disagreement permanent. Ronald Dworkin and Charles Larmore defended related views. Also called political liberalism, neutralism. Held by John Rawls, Ronald Dworkin, Charles Larmore, Bruce Ackerman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Egalitarian liberalism","Primary goods"],"thinkers":["rawls","dworkin"],"position":"liberal_neutrality","tags":["state neutrality","neutrality","liberalism","rawls","conceptions of the good","secular state","political liberalism","neutralism"]},{"term":"Political perfectionism","def":"The state may promote what genuinely makes lives go well, such as knowledge, health and the arts, and discourage what wastes them.","more":"Political perfectionism holds that the state may, and sometimes should, take sides about what makes human lives go well. The name comes from the idea of perfecting human nature. Aristotle held that the purpose of the political community is to make good lives possible for its members. Joseph Raz developed a liberal perfectionism: personal autonomy, the ability to shape one’s own life, is itself a central good, but it requires a range of valuable options. Also called liberal perfectionism (one form), state perfectionism. Held by Joseph Raz, Aristotle, John Finnis, Thomas Hurka.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Positive liberty","Coercive paternalism"],"thinkers":["raz","aristotle","finnis","hurka"],"position":"political_perfectionism","tags":["perfectionism","state perfectionism","raz","good life","liberal perfectionism","liberal perfectionism (one form)"]},{"term":"Religious traditionalism (political)","def":"Law should reflect a moral order given by God or a sacred tradition, and the state may honour and support religion.","more":"Religious traditionalism holds that political authority should recognise a moral order with a religious source. Thomas Aquinas argued that just human laws derive from natural law, which itself reflects the eternal law of God; a human law that departs from it is a corruption of law. Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet and Joseph de Maistre defended the close union of throne and altar. Similar ideas appear across many traditions, including established churches in Christian countries and views in Islamic, Jewish, Hindu and Confucian thought that law should express a sacred moral order. Also called religious establishment, theonomic politics (strong forms). Held by Thomas Aquinas, Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet, Joseph de Maistre.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Conservatism","Communitarianism","Political perfectionism"],"thinkers":["aquinas","bossuet","de_maistre"],"position":"religious_traditionalism","tags":["traditionalism","theocracy","divine law","religious law","natural law","religious conservatism","religious establishment","theonomic politics (strong forms)"]},{"term":"Statism (about global justice)","def":"Demanding duties of distributive justice hold among fellow citizens of a state; to outsiders we owe human rights and humanitarian help.","more":"Statism here is a view about the scope of justice, not a view favouring a big state. It holds that egalitarian principles of justice, like Rawls’s difference principle, apply within states but not between them. Thomas Nagel argued that such principles arise because citizens are jointly subject to a coercive legal system imposed in their name, which they are expected to accept as their own. That special relationship calls for special justification, including fair shares. Also called political conception of global justice. Held by Thomas Nagel, Michael Blake, John Rawls.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Qualified right to exclude"],"thinkers":["nagel","rawls"],"position":"statism_about_global_justice","tags":["statism","global justice","nagel","cosmopolitanism","borders","associative duties","political conception of global justice"]},{"term":"Liberal nationalism","def":"Nations are real ethical communities whose members owe each other special loyalty, within limits set by human rights.","more":"Liberal nationalism defends national loyalty while accepting liberal principles. David Miller argued that nations are communities of people who share a history, a public culture and a sense of belonging, and who wish to govern themselves. Such communities are ethically significant: their members owe each other more than they owe outsiders, as family members do. Shared national identity also supports trust and willingness to share, which welfare states and democracy need. Also called nationalism (liberal form). Held by David Miller, Yael Tamir.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Associative political obligation","Qualified right to exclude"],"thinkers":["david_miller"],"position":"liberal_nationalism","tags":["nationalism","national identity","tamir","david miller","self-determination","patriotism","nationalism (liberal form)"]},{"term":"Open borders","def":"People have a strong right to move and settle where they choose, so borders should generally be open.","more":"The open borders view holds that restrictions on immigration are a serious injustice that needs strong justification, and that in most cases there is none. Joseph Carens argued that citizenship in a rich Western country is like feudal privilege: an inherited status that greatly improves one’s life chances and is protected by keeping others out. If we reject feudal birth privilege, we should reject this too. Michael Huemer, from a libertarian angle, argued that preventing someone from coming to trade and work with willing employers and landlords is a harmful coercive act that requires justification. Also called freedom of movement view. Held by Joseph Carens, Michael Huemer, Chandran Kukathas.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Cosmopolitanism (about justice)","Libertarianism"],"thinkers":["huemer"],"position":"open_borders","tags":["free movement","freedom of movement","immigration","carens","migration","no borders","freedom of movement view"]},{"term":"Qualified right to exclude","def":"A self-governing people may control immigration, but must admit a fair share of refugees and treat migrants decently.","more":"The qualified view holds that states have a real right to control who settles in their territory, but that this right has limits. Michael Walzer compared political communities to clubs and neighbourhoods: they may shape their membership to preserve their character, but must offer mutual aid to those in desperate need, especially refugees. David Miller argued that a nation’s right to self-determination includes deciding how large its population will be and what its public culture should be like. But states must admit refugees whose human rights cannot be protected elsewhere, must not discriminate on grounds like race, and must give long-term residents a path to citizenship. Also called moderate immigration view. Held by David Miller, Michael Walzer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Liberal nationalism","Statism (about global justice)","Communitarianism"],"thinkers":["david_miller","walzer"],"position":"qualified_right_to_exclude","tags":["immigration","border control","migration","right to exclude","refugees","moderate immigration view"]},{"term":"Strong right to exclude","def":"A legitimate state, like a club, may refuse entry to anyone, even refugees, so long as it helps them in other ways.","more":"Christopher Heath Wellman argued that legitimate states have a strong right to exclude would-be immigrants, grounded in freedom of association. Just as individuals have the right to choose whom to marry or which clubs to join, and to refuse association with others, legitimate political communities have a right to decide whom to admit as members. This right holds even against people in great need, though states still have duties to help them. Wellman argued that a state could meet its duties to refugees by helping them elsewhere, for example by funding protection in other countries or intervening against the persecuting regime, rather than by admitting them. Also called freedom of association view of immigration. Held by Christopher Heath Wellman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Qualified right to exclude"],"thinkers":[],"position":"right_to_exclude","tags":["right to exclude","immigration restriction","closed borders","wellman","freedom of association","border control","freedom of association view of immigration"]},{"term":"Deterrence theory of punishment","def":"Punishment is a harm justified only by its good effects: deterring crime, protecting the public and reforming offenders.","more":"The deterrence theory, a consequentialist or forward-looking view, holds that punishment is justified only by the good it does. Jeremy Bentham argued that all punishment is itself an evil, since it causes suffering, so it can be justified only if it prevents greater evil. It does this by deterring the offender (specific deterrence) and others (general deterrence), by incapacitating dangerous people, and by rehabilitating offenders. Cesare Beccaria argued that certainty and promptness of punishment deter better than severity, and that cruel punishments are both wrong and ineffective. Also called consequentialist theory of punishment, utilitarian theory of punishment. Held by Jeremy Bentham, Cesare Beccaria, John Stuart Mill.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Utilitarian theory of justice","Mixed theory of punishment"],"thinkers":["bentham","mill"],"position":"deterrence_theory","tags":["deterrence","consequentialism about punishment","utilitarian punishment","crime prevention","bentham","punishment","consequentialist theory of punishment","utilitarian theory of punishment"]},{"term":"Mixed theory of punishment","def":"The aim of punishment is to reduce crime, but it may fall only on the guilty and never exceed what they deserve.","more":"Mixed theories try to combine the strengths of deterrence theory and retributivism. H. L. A. Also called hybrid theory, Hart’s theory of punishment. Held by H. L. A. Hart, John Rawls.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Retributivism","Deterrence theory of punishment"],"thinkers":["hart","rawls"],"position":"mixed_theory_of_punishment","tags":["hart","hybrid theory of punishment","retributivism","deterrence","punishment","hybrid theory","hart’s theory of punishment"]},{"term":"Restorative justice","def":"Responding to crime should focus on repairing harm by bringing victims, offenders and communities together, with punishment as a last resort.","more":"Restorative justice treats crime primarily as harm to people and relationships, rather than as an offence against the state. Instead of asking what law was broken and what punishment is deserved, it asks who was harmed, what they need, and who is responsible for repairing it. Its practices include victim-offender meetings, family group conferences and community circles, where the offender hears the harm done, takes responsibility and agrees on ways to make amends. Howard Zehr developed the framework in the 1990s, drawing on Mennonite practice and on Indigenous traditions such as those in New Zealand. Also called reparative justice. Held by Howard Zehr, John Braithwaite, Nils Christie.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Penal abolitionism"],"thinkers":["christie"],"position":"restorative_justice","tags":["restoration","reconciliation","victim-offender mediation","repairing harm","criminal justice","punishment","reparative justice"]},{"term":"Penal abolitionism","def":"Punishment, and prisons above all, cannot be justified, and should be replaced by ways of preventing harm, meeting needs and resolving conflict.","more":"Penal abolitionism holds that the institution of punishment, especially imprisonment, is unjustified and should be abolished rather than reformed. Thomas Mathiesen and Nils Christie in Scandinavia argued that prisons fail on their own terms (they do not rehabilitate and often increase crime) and that deliberately inflicting pain is not a proper response to conflict. Angela Y. Davis argued that prisons in the United States are bound up with racial and economic injustice and that society should invest in education, health care, housing and community support that address the causes of harm. Also called prison abolitionism, abolitionism (penal). Held by Angela Y. Davis, Nils Christie, Thomas Mathiesen, Ruth Wilson Gilmore.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Restorative justice","Anarchism"],"thinkers":["davis_angela","christie"],"position":"penal_abolitionism","tags":["prison abolition","abolitionism","prisons","punishment","criminal justice","decarceration","prison abolitionism","abolitionism (penal)"]},{"term":"The offense principle","def":"Besides harmful speech, the law may restrict expression that causes serious, hard-to-avoid offence, weighed against its value.","more":"Joel Feinberg argued that the harm principle alone is too narrow. Some conduct does not harm anyone in the strict sense, but causes serious offence: disgust, shock, shame or alarm. Feinberg imagined being trapped on a bus with passengers doing deeply offensive things, and suggested the law may sometimes protect people from such experiences. His offense principle allows restriction only after weighing several factors: how intense and widespread the offence is, how easily it can be avoided, whether those offended consented, how valuable the expression is to the speaker and society, and whether there are other places it could happen. Also called Feinberg’s offense principle. Held by Joel Feinberg.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["The harm principle"],"thinkers":["feinberg"],"position":"offense_principle","tags":["offence principle","offensiveness","feinberg","harm principle","free speech","censorship","feinberg’s offense principle"]},{"term":"Dignity view of hate speech","def":"Besides directly harmful speech, the law may restrict hate speech because it attacks some people’s standing as equal members of society.","more":"The dignity view, defended by Jeremy Waldron, argues that hate speech, such as public statements that a racial or religious group is subhuman or should be driven out, damages something important: the assurance that every member of society is treated as an equal citizen. In a well-ordered society, members of minority groups should be able to go about their lives knowing that their basic standing is secure. Hate speech in public places, Waldron argued, is like a group libel that undermines this assurance and tells some people they are not welcome. Mari Matsuda and other critical race theorists argued that racist speech inflicts real injury on its targets. Also called dignity principle, the harm in hate speech. Held by Jeremy Waldron, Mari Matsuda.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Relational egalitarianism","Communitarianism"],"thinkers":["waldron"],"position":"dignity_principle","tags":["hate speech","dignity","waldron","free speech","harm in hate speech","censorship","dignity principle","the harm in hate speech"]},{"term":"Free speech skepticism","def":"There is no single neutral principle of free speech; every society limits speech, and the real question is where to draw the line, for political reasons.","more":"Free speech skepticism doubts that “free speech” names one clear principle that can settle disputes. Stanley Fish argued that every society and institution limits speech: no one thinks fraud, perjury or threats are protected. Every principle of free speech, therefore, already contains exceptions shaped by judgements about which speech matters and which causes harm. Calling one side’s position “free speech” disguises a political choice as neutral. Also called skepticism about free speech principles. Held by Stanley Fish.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":[],"thinkers":["fish"],"position":"free_speech_skepticism","tags":["free speech","freedom of expression","stanley fish","censorship","speech regulation","skepticism about free speech principles"]},{"term":"Anti-paternalism","def":"Over their own lives competent adults are in charge, and the state may not restrict their choices for their own good.","more":"Anti-paternalism holds that the state may not interfere with competent adults’ choices merely to protect them from themselves. Paternalism (from the Latin for “father”) means limiting someone’s liberty for their own good, as a parent does with a child. Mill argued that a person’s own good, physical or moral, is not sufficient reason for interference. Joel Feinberg distinguished hard paternalism (overriding fully voluntary choices for the person’s good), which he rejected, from soft paternalism (stepping in to check that a choice is informed and voluntary), which he accepted. Also called Millian anti-paternalism. Held by John Stuart Mill, Joel Feinberg.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["The harm principle","Libertarianism","Negative liberty"],"thinkers":["mill","feinberg"],"position":"anti_paternalism","tags":["paternalism","autonomy","harm principle","mill","self-regarding","liberty","millian anti-paternalism"]},{"term":"Libertarian paternalism","def":"The state may steer people toward better choices, for example with sensible defaults, as long as they remain free to choose otherwise.","more":"Libertarian paternalism was proposed by the economist Richard Thaler and the legal scholar Cass Sunstein. It is “paternalist” because it aims to make people better off by their own standards, and “libertarian” because it leaves them free to choose. Its main tool is the nudge: changing the way choices are presented (the “choice architecture”) without forbidding any option or significantly changing incentives. Examples include automatically enrolling workers in pension schemes while letting them opt out, or placing healthy food at eye level in a cafeteria. Also called nudge theory, nudging. Held by Cass Sunstein, Richard Thaler.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Coercive paternalism"],"thinkers":["sunstein"],"position":"libertarian_paternalism","tags":["nudge","nudging","choice architecture","thaler","sunstein","paternalism","behavioural economics","nudge theory"]},{"term":"Coercive paternalism","def":"When people predictably harm themselves through common errors, the law may require or ban things for their own good.","more":"Coercive paternalism holds that the state may sometimes use the force of law to protect people from their own choices. Sarah Conly argued, drawing on behavioural economics, that people regularly fail to act on their own deepest goals because of predictable biases: overconfidence, short-sightedness, susceptibility to framing. When the stakes are high and the costs of interference are low, laws requiring or banning certain conduct can help people achieve what they themselves want. Seatbelt laws and motorcycle helmet laws are familiar examples; Conly argued that banning cigarettes could be justified on similar grounds. Also called hard paternalism (political). Held by Sarah Conly, Gerald Dworkin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Political perfectionism","Libertarian paternalism"],"thinkers":["conly"],"position":"coercive_paternalism","tags":["paternalism","sarah conly","against autonomy","hard paternalism","state coercion","hard paternalism (political)"]},{"term":"Piecemeal reform","def":"Institutions should be improved step by step, testing each reform and correcting mistakes, rather than redesigned all at once.","more":"Piecemeal reform holds that social change should proceed through limited, testable reforms rather than total redesign. Karl Popper called this “piecemeal social engineering” and contrasted it with “utopian engineering”, which aims to build an ideal society according to a blueprint. Because society is complex and our knowledge limited, any reform will have unexpected effects. Small reforms let us see those effects and correct course; sweeping ones make mistakes huge and hard to undo. Also called piecemeal social engineering, incrementalism. Held by Karl Popper, Eduard Bernstein.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Protective theory of democracy","Conservatism"],"thinkers":["popper","bernstein"],"position":"piecemeal_reform","tags":["incrementalism","gradualism","piecemeal social engineering","popper","reform","open society"]},{"term":"Political radicalism","def":"The present system is unjust at its roots, so it needs sweeping transformation rather than patching.","more":"Political radicalism (from the Latin radix, “root”) holds that the injustices of a society spring from its basic structure, and so require fundamental change rather than gradual reform. Radicals come from many traditions. Thomas Paine defended the American and French revolutions against Burke, arguing that each generation has the right to remake its government. Karl Marx argued that capitalism could not be reformed into justice, since exploitation is built into it. Also called radicalism. Held by Karl Marx, Thomas Paine, Frantz Fanon.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Socialism","Anarchism"],"thinkers":["marx","paine","fanon"],"position":"political_radicalism","tags":["radicalism","revolution","systemic change","structural injustice","revolutionary politics"]},{"term":"Democratic theory of legitimacy","def":"A state has the right to rule when its laws come from a fair process in which all citizens have an equal say.","more":"This view holds that what makes political authority legitimate is democratic procedure. When laws are made through free elections, equal votes, open debate and majority decision, all citizens are bound by them, including those who voted against. Defenders differ on why. Thomas Christiano argues that democracy publicly treats people as equals when they disagree about justice; Jeremy Waldron that majority rule respects each person as a reasoner whose view counts; deliberative democrats such as Jürgen Habermas that only laws all could accept in free discussion are legitimate. Held by Thomas Christiano, Jeremy Waldron, Jürgen Habermas.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Democratic proceduralism (intrinsic view)","Deliberative democracy","Epistemic democracy"],"thinkers":["christiano","waldron"],"position":"democratic_legitimacy","tags":["democratic legitimacy","legitimacy","authority","democracy","consent","fair procedure"]},{"term":"Public reason liberalism","def":"Coercive laws are legitimate only if they can be justified to every reasonable citizen, by reasons all can accept.","more":"Public reason liberalism starts from the fact of reasonable pluralism: free people, reasoning carefully, come to different religions and philosophies, and this will not go away. If the state coerces citizens on the basis of one of these views, it treats those who reject it as subjects rather than equals. So, the view holds, political power is legitimate only when exercised according to principles that all reasonable citizens could endorse. John Rawls set out this “liberal principle of legitimacy” in Political Liberalism (1993). Held by John Rawls, Gerald Gaus, Charles Larmore, Jonathan Quong.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Liberal neutrality","Political liberalism","Convergence liberalism"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"position":"public_reason_liberalism","tags":["public reason","political liberalism","rawls","gaus","justificatory liberalism","legitimacy"]},{"term":"Instrumentalism about legitimacy","def":"A state’s right to rule depends on how well it secures justice and its people’s good, not on how it came to power.","more":"Instrumentalism holds that political authority is justified by what it achieves. A state is legitimate if, and to the extent that, it does a good enough job of securing peace, protecting rights, providing public goods and helping people act as they have reason to. Joseph Raz’s service conception of authority (1986) is the most influential version. Richard Arneson argues that even democracy has no value in itself: it is justified only because, and where, it produces better results than the alternatives. Also called the service view. Held by Joseph Raz, Richard Arneson, David Hume.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Service conception of authority","Natural duty of justice"],"thinkers":["raz","arneson","hume"],"position":"legitimacy_instrumentalism","tags":["legitimacy","instrumentalism","authority","justice","good outcomes","the service view"]},{"term":"Prescriptive legitimacy","def":"Authority grows from long, peaceful and accepted rule: a government that has stood for generations has a title to obedience.","more":"Prescriptive legitimacy is the traditionalist answer to the question of authority. Just as long, undisturbed possession of land can give a title to it (“prescription” in law), long and peaceful rule gives a government a title to obedience. Edmund Burke argued in a 1782 speech that the British constitution is a prescriptive constitution, whose authority rests on its having existed time out of mind. David Hume observed that almost every government began in conquest or usurpation, yet time and custom reconcile people to it and give it a rightful claim. Also called traditional authority. Held by Edmund Burke, David Hume, Roger Scruton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Conservatism","Burkean conservatism"],"thinkers":["burke","hume","scruton"],"position":"prescriptive_legitimacy","tags":["prescription","burke","tradition","legitimacy","established authority","conservatism","traditional authority"]},{"term":"Tacit consent theory","def":"By living in a state and enjoying its protection and property laws, you consent to its authority without saying so.","more":"Tacit consent theory answers the obvious problem for consent theories: most people never explicitly agree to obey their state. John Locke argued in his Second Treatise of Government (1689) that anyone who owns land or enjoys any part of a government’s territory, even just travelling freely on the highway, gives tacit consent and is obliged to obey its laws while there. An older form appears in Plato’s Crito, where the Laws of Athens tell Socrates that by staying in the city all his life he agreed to obey them. The view keeps consent at the root of authority while explaining how ordinary citizens could have given it. Held by John Locke, Plato.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Consent theory of political obligation"],"thinkers":["locke","plato"],"position":"tacit_consent_theory","tags":["tacit consent","implied consent","locke","hume","social contract","political obligation"]},{"term":"Hypothetical consent theory","def":"A state is legitimate if free and reasonable people would agree to it, whether or not anyone actually did.","more":"Hypothetical consent theory moves from what people did agree to, to what they would agree to under fair conditions. Immanuel Kant, in his essay “On the Common Saying: That May Be Correct in Theory, But It Is of No Use in Practice” (1793), described the original contract as an idea of reason: a test that a law is legitimate if a whole people could have consented to it. Social contract theorists in this line, including Rawls in his account of justice, use imagined agreements to model what fair terms of cooperation would be. The appeal is that it keeps the core idea of consent theory, that authority must be acceptable to those it governs, without requiring a historical contract no one ever signed. Held by Immanuel Kant, John Rawls.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Consent theory of political obligation","Public reason liberalism"],"thinkers":["kant","rawls"],"position":"hypothetical_consent_theory","tags":["hypothetical contract","social contract","contractualism","rawls","kant","legitimacy"]},{"term":"Normative consent theory","def":"If it would be wrong for you to refuse consent to a just authority, you are bound as if you had consented.","more":"Normative consent is David Estlund’s idea, developed in Democratic Authority (2008). Ordinary consent theory says you are bound only if you actually agree. Estlund points out that in other areas we sometimes think non-consent can be void: if a flight attendant in an emergency directs you to help, and you refuse when you ought to agree, your refusal does not free you from the authority. Similarly, if a democratic authority is fair and reasonably reliable, and refusing to accept it would be wrong, you are under its authority as though you had consented. Held by David Estlund.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Consent theory of political obligation","Epistemic democracy"],"thinkers":["estlund"],"position":"normative_consent_theory","tags":["normative consent","estlund","consent","authority","legitimacy"]},{"term":"Deliberative democracy","def":"Democratic decisions are legitimate when they come out of free and equal public reasoning, not merely from counting votes.","more":"Deliberative democracy holds that democracy is more than adding up preferences. A decision is legitimate when it results from public discussion among free and equal citizens who give one another reasons that others could accept. Jürgen Habermas, in Between Facts and Norms (1992), argued that laws are legitimate when all those affected could agree to them in rational discourse. Joshua Cohen’s essay “Deliberation and Democratic Legitimacy” (1989) set out an ideal deliberative procedure. Held by Jürgen Habermas, Joshua Cohen, Amy Gutmann.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Democratic theory of legitimacy","Epistemic democracy","Public reason liberalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"deliberative_democracy","tags":["deliberation","public deliberation","habermas","discursive democracy","democracy","legitimacy"]},{"term":"Political liberalism","def":"Basic political questions should be settled by public reasons that citizens of every reasonable faith and philosophy can share.","more":"Political liberalism is the view John Rawls set out in Political Liberalism (1993). It accepts that reasonable citizens will always hold different “comprehensive doctrines”, religious and philosophical views of life. A stable and fair society cannot be built on any one of them. Instead, citizens can each endorse a shared political conception of justice from within their own doctrines, an overlapping consensus. Held by John Rawls, Jonathan Quong, Charles Larmore.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Public reason liberalism","Liberal neutrality","Justice as fairness"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"position":"political_liberalism","tags":["rawls","public reason","overlapping consensus","reasonable pluralism","liberalism"]},{"term":"Convergence liberalism","def":"A law is justified if each reasonable citizen has sufficient reason, from within their own view, to accept it, even if their reasons differ.","more":"Convergence liberalism is a version of public reason liberalism defended by Gerald Gaus in The Order of Public Reason (2011) and by Kevin Vallier. Rawls asked citizens to justify laws by reasons all can share. Convergence theorists reply that this is too demanding and unfair to religious citizens. A law is justified to you if you have sufficient reason to accept it given your own values, whatever they are. Held by Gerald Gaus, Kevin Vallier.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Public reason liberalism","Classical liberalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"convergence_liberalism","tags":["gaus","convergence","public reason","justificatory liberalism","liberalism"]},{"term":"Service conception of authority","def":"Someone has authority over you when following their directives helps you act better on the reasons that already apply to you.","more":"The service conception of authority is Joseph Raz’s account, set out in The Morality of Freedom (1986). Its core is the “normal justification thesis”: a person or body has legitimate authority over you if you would better conform to the reasons that apply to you by following its directives than by trying to work out what to do yourself. A state can serve people in this way by having expertise, by solving coordination problems such as which side of the road to drive on, or by being free of the biases that affect individual judgment. Authority is therefore piecemeal: a state may have authority over some matters and some people, but not others. Held by Joseph Raz.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Instrumentalism about legitimacy","Philosophical anarchism"],"thinkers":["raz"],"position":"service_conception_of_authority","tags":["raz","normal justification thesis","authority","legitimate authority","pre-emption"]},{"term":"Samaritan theory of legitimacy","def":"States may coerce citizens because coercion is needed to rescue everyone from the grave dangers of life without a state.","more":"The samaritan theory was proposed by Christopher Heath Wellman in “Liberalism, Samaritanism, and Political Legitimacy” (1996). It starts from duties of rescue: you may impose a modest cost on someone to save another from grave peril, as when you take a stranger’s car without permission to rush a dying person to hospital. Without a state, Wellman argues, people would face exactly such peril, from violence and insecurity. A state that protects everyone from this imposes only modest costs on each citizen, so its coercion is permissible. Held by Christopher Heath Wellman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Instrumentalism about legitimacy","Natural duty of justice"],"thinkers":[],"position":"samaritan_theory_of_legitimacy","tags":["wellman","samaritanism","legitimacy","political obligation","rescue"]},{"term":"A priori philosophical anarchism","def":"No state could ever have a right to be obeyed, because authority conflicts with each person’s moral autonomy.","more":"A priori philosophical anarchism is the view defended by Robert Paul Wolff in In Defense of Anarchism (1970). A morally responsible person, Wolff argued, must take responsibility for her actions by deciding for herself what she ought to do. Authority, by contrast, is a right to be obeyed simply because one commands. To obey on that ground is to give up autonomy. Held by Robert Paul Wolff.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Philosophical anarchism","Anarchism"],"thinkers":["rp_wolff"],"position":"a_priori_philosophical_anarchism","tags":["philosophical anarchism","wolff","autonomy","authority","anarchism"]},{"term":"A posteriori philosophical anarchism","def":"A state could in principle have authority, but every actual state fails every test for it.","more":"A posteriori philosophical anarchism is A. John Simmons’s view, first argued in Moral Principles and Political Obligations (1979). Rather than claiming that authority is impossible, Simmons examined each proposed ground of political obligation (consent, fair play, gratitude, natural duty and membership) and argued that none applies to most citizens of real states. Few people have truly consented; benefits forced on us do not create fair-play duties; natural duties are owed to just institutions anywhere, not to our own state in particular. Held by A. John Simmons, Michael Huemer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Philosophical anarchism","Consent theory of political obligation"],"thinkers":["simmons","huemer"],"position":"a_posteriori_philosophical_anarchism","tags":["philosophical anarchism","simmons","political obligation","legitimacy","anarchism"]},{"term":"Desert-based justice","def":"A just distribution gives people what they deserve for their effort, contribution or merit.","more":"Desert-based theories of distributive justice hold that people should receive income and wealth in proportion to what they deserve, usually for their effort, the value of their contribution, or the sacrifices they make. The idea is old: Aristotle held that just distribution is proportional to merit, though different regimes judge merit differently. Many ordinary people judge pay this way. Modern defenders include George Sher, in Desert (1987), and Fred Feldman, who argues that a distribution is better the more closely people’s wellbeing matches what they deserve. Held by Aristotle, George Sher, Fred Feldman, David Miller.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Entitlement theory","Utilitarian theory of justice"],"thinkers":["aristotle","david_miller"],"position":"desert_based_justice","tags":["desert","merit","deserving","distributive justice","reward","meritocracy"]},{"term":"Distribution according to need","def":"Goods should go to people according to their needs, while each contributes according to their ability.","more":"Distribution according to need holds that the just way to share goods is to give each person what they need, not what they earn, deserve or bargain for. Its best-known statement is the slogan “from each according to his ability, to each according to his needs”, which Marx, in his Critique of the Gotha Programme (1875), reserved for a higher stage of communism, after scarcity had been overcome; the slogan was already used by earlier socialists. Anarcho-communists such as Peter Kropotkin made sharing by need the basis of their free communes. Many people apply the idea to particular goods, such as health care and emergency aid, even when they reject it for income in general. Held by Karl Marx, Peter Kropotkin, David Miller.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Socialism","Marxism","Anarcho-communism","Sufficientarianism"],"thinkers":["marx","kropotkin","david_miller"],"position":"distribution_according_to_need","tags":["to each according to need","needs","marx","from each according to ability","distributive justice"]},{"term":"Justice as fairness","def":"A just society secures equal basic liberties and fair opportunity for all, and allows inequalities only when they benefit the worst-off.","more":"Justice as fairness is John Rawls’s theory, set out in A Theory of Justice (1971) and later works. Rawls asked what principles free and equal people would choose if they did not know their own place in society, a test he called the “original position” behind a “veil of ignorance”. He argued they would choose two principles. First, everyone has an equal right to a full set of basic liberties, compatible with the same for all. Also called Rawlsian liberalism. Held by John Rawls, Samuel Freeman, Thomas Scanlon.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Egalitarian liberalism","The difference principle (justice as fairness)","Primary goods","Political liberalism"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"position":"justice_as_fairness","tags":["rawls","difference principle","original position","veil of ignorance","fairness","theory of justice","rawlsian liberalism"]},{"term":"Hayekian liberalism","def":"Free markets and the rule of law beat central planning, because the knowledge a society needs is spread among millions of people.","more":"Hayekian liberalism follows the Austrian-British economist and philosopher Friedrich Hayek (1899–1992). Its central idea is that the knowledge needed to run an economy (about local conditions, changing needs, what can be made and how) is dispersed among millions of people, and much of it can’t be written down. Prices, markets and inherited customs coordinate this knowledge in a “spontaneous order” that no one designed. Central planning must fail, Hayek argued in The Road to Serfdom (1944), and it concentrates power in dangerous ways. Held by Friedrich Hayek, Michael Polanyi, Gerald Gaus.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Classical liberalism","Negative liberty"],"thinkers":["hayek"],"position":"hayekian_liberalism","tags":["hayek","spontaneous order","knowledge problem","free market","road to serfdom","rule of law"]},{"term":"Millian liberalism","def":"Government should leave people free to live as they choose, except to prevent harm to others, because freedom makes people and societies flourish.","more":"Millian liberalism takes its name from John Stuart Mill (1806–1873). In On Liberty (1859) Mill defended the “harm principle”: the only purpose for which power can rightfully be used over anyone against their will is to prevent harm to others. His grounds were not natural rights but human wellbeing, understood broadly to include the development of each person’s individuality. People usually know their own interests best; free discussion exposes errors; and “experiments in living” show which ways of life work. Held by John Stuart Mill, Wilhelm von Humboldt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Classical liberalism","The harm principle","Anti-paternalism"],"thinkers":["mill"],"position":"millian_liberalism","tags":["john stuart mill","mill","harm principle","on liberty","individuality","liberalism"]},{"term":"Ordoliberalism","def":"A free market needs a strong state that sets and enforces the rules of competition, but does not plan outcomes.","more":"Ordoliberalism grew up in the 1930s around the Freiburg School of economists and lawyers, led by Walter Eucken and Franz Böhm, with allies such as Wilhelm Röpke and Alexander Rüstow. Having watched the Weimar Republic collapse and the Nazis take control of the economy, they concluded that markets are not self-sustaining: left alone, firms form cartels and monopolies, and private power captures the state. Their answer was an “economic constitution”: a strong but limited state that guards competition, stable money and open markets, while leaving prices and production to firms and consumers. After 1945 these ideas shaped West Germany’s “social market economy” under Ludwig Erhard. Held by Walter Eucken, Franz Böhm, Wilhelm Röpke, Alexander Rüstow.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Classical liberalism","Social democracy"],"thinkers":[],"position":"ordoliberalism","tags":["freiburg school","social market economy","eucken","competition policy","german liberalism"]},{"term":"Neoclassical liberalism","def":"Economic liberties are basic rights, and free markets are the best way to meet the demands of social justice.","more":"Neoclassical liberalism is a recent attempt to join classical liberal economics with liberal concern for the poor. In Free Market Fairness (2012), John Tomasi argued that economic liberties (to choose your work, own productive property, start a business and keep much of what you earn) are basic liberties, as important to living by your own lights as freedom of religion or speech. He also accepted a Rawlsian test of justice: institutions should be judged by how the least advantaged fare. Tomasi’s claim is that a market-based society, which he calls market democracy, passes that test better than a welfare state does. Also called market democracy, bleeding-heart libertarianism. Held by John Tomasi, Jason Brennan, Matt Zwolinski.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Classical liberalism","Justice as fairness"],"thinkers":["brennan"],"position":"neoclassical_liberalism","tags":["bleeding heart libertarianism","tomasi","economic liberty","free markets","liberalism","market democracy","bleeding-heart libertarianism"]},{"term":"Minarchism","def":"The only legitimate state is a minimal one that protects people against force, theft and fraud.","more":"Minarchism is the libertarian view that a state is justified but must be minimal: a “night-watchman” that provides police, courts and national defence to protect people’s rights, and does nothing else. It may not redistribute income, run schools or ban drugs, since these force some people to serve others or override choices about their own lives. Robert Nozick gave the best-known defence in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974): a minimal state could arise from a state of nature by steps that violate no one’s rights, but anything more extensive would violate them. Minarchists differ from anarcho-capitalists, who reject even the minimal state, and from classical liberals, who accept a wider state on the strength of its good effects. Also called the night-watchman state. Held by Robert Nozick, Ayn Rand, Tibor Machan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Libertarianism","Entitlement theory"],"thinkers":["nozick","rand"],"position":"minarchism","tags":["minimal state","night-watchman state","limited government","nozick","libertarianism","the night-watchman state"]},{"term":"Anarcho-capitalism","def":"Every service the state provides, including police, courts and law, should be supplied by competing private firms instead.","more":"Anarcho-capitalism holds that no state is legitimate, not even a minimal one, because every state forces people to pay taxes for its services and forbids anyone else to offer them. Instead, protection, dispute resolution and even law could be supplied in a free market by private agencies and courts that people choose to pay. Murray Rothbard argued from natural rights: taxation is theft, and the state is a band of robbers on a large scale. David Friedman argued on grounds of good results, in The Machinery of Freedom (1973), that competing agencies would produce better and cheaper law. Also called market anarchism. Held by Murray Rothbard, David D. Friedman, Hans-Hermann Hoppe, Michael Huemer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Libertarianism","Anarchism","Individualist anarchism"],"thinkers":["rothbard","david_friedman","huemer"],"position":"anarcho_capitalism","tags":["ancap","anarchocapitalism","rothbard","private law","free market anarchism","stateless society","libertarianism","market anarchism"]},{"term":"Burkean conservatism","def":"Inherited institutions carry the wisdom of many generations, so reform should be gradual and respect what has grown up over time.","more":"Burkean conservatism takes its lead from Edmund Burke’s Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790), written against the French revolutionaries’ plan to rebuild society on abstract principles. Burke argued that institutions such as the constitution, the church, property and the family embody practical knowledge built up over generations, far more than any one person’s reasoning can grasp. Society, he wrote, is a partnership between the living, the dead and those yet to be born. Change is necessary, since a state that cannot change cannot preserve itself, but it should be careful repair, not demolition. Held by Edmund Burke, Roger Scruton, Russell Kirk.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Conservatism","Piecemeal reform"],"thinkers":["burke","scruton"],"position":"burkean_conservatism","tags":["burke","edmund burke","tradition","conservatism","prudence","organic society"]},{"term":"Sceptical conservatism","def":"Political knowledge is practical and limited, so government should be modest and prefer the familiar to the untried.","more":"Sceptical conservatism rests less on reverence for tradition than on doubt about political reason. Michael Oakeshott argued in “Rationalism in Politics” (1947) that most of the knowledge needed to govern is practical know-how, learned by doing, that cannot be fully put into rules or plans; rationalists who try to run politics from a book get it wrong. In “On Being Conservative” (1956) he described conservatism as a disposition: to prefer the familiar to the unknown, the tried to the untried, present laughter to utopian bliss. Government, on this view, is like an umpire: it keeps general rules so that people can pursue their own projects, rather than steering society toward a goal. Also called Oakeshottian conservatism, the politics of imperfection. Held by Michael Oakeshott, Anthony Quinton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Conservatism","Piecemeal reform"],"thinkers":["oakeshott"],"position":"sceptical_conservatism","tags":["oakeshott","scepticism","skeptical conservatism","conservatism","practical knowledge","rationalism in politics","oakeshottian conservatism","the politics of imperfection"]},{"term":"National conservatism","def":"The independent nation, with its shared culture and loyalties, is the foundation of a free and stable political order.","more":"National conservatism holds that the nation (a people bound by language, history, religion or custom) is the natural home of political loyalty, and that independent nation-states are the best basis for freedom and self-government. It is wary of global institutions, of immigration faster than newcomers can be integrated, and of free trade that weakens national industries, and it values religion and the family as sources of national cohesion. Yoram Hazony’s The Virtue of Nationalism (2018) defends a world of independent nations against “imperial” projects of universal order. Roger Scruton earlier argued that liberal democracy needs a pre-political sense of “we”, a national loyalty, to sustain trust and sacrifice. Held by Yoram Hazony, Roger Scruton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Conservatism","Liberal nationalism","Communitarianism"],"thinkers":["scruton"],"position":"national_conservatism","tags":["nationalism","national identity","sovereignty","hazony","conservatism","patriotism"]},{"term":"Democratic socialism","def":"The main means of production should be owned in common and run democratically, with change won through elections and civil liberties fully protected.","more":"Democratic socialism aims at a socialist economy, with key industries, services and resources owned in common, but insists that it be reached and run democratically, with free elections, free speech and the rule of law. It rejects both one-party rule on the Soviet model and the view that a welfare state on top of capitalism is enough. R. H. Held by R. H. Tawney, Michael Harrington, George Orwell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Socialism","Social democracy","Piecemeal reform"],"thinkers":[],"position":"democratic_socialism","tags":["socialism","democracy","common ownership","workers","social ownership"]},{"term":"Market socialism","def":"Firms should be owned by their workers or the public, while markets set prices and coordinate production.","more":"Market socialism tries to combine the efficiency of markets with socialist ownership. Prices, competition and consumer choice remain, but firms are not owned by private shareholders. In some versions they are self-managed cooperatives, as David Schweickart proposes under the name “economic democracy”. In others, as in John Roemer’s A Future for Socialism (1994), citizens receive equal shares in firms that they cannot sell for cash, so profits are spread across the population. Held by John Roemer, David Schweickart, David Miller, Oskar Lange.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Socialism","Liberal socialism"],"thinkers":["david_miller"],"position":"market_socialism","tags":["socialism","worker cooperatives","workers ownership","markets","roemer","socialism with markets"]},{"term":"Complex equality","def":"Each social good should be shared out by its own standards, so that success in one sphere, like money, cannot buy dominance in others.","more":"Complex equality is Michael Walzer’s theory in Spheres of Justice (1983). Walzer argued that there is no single formula for distributive justice. Goods such as health care, political office, education, honours, love and money have meanings that members of a society share, and those meanings imply how each good should be distributed: medical care by need, office by qualification, honours by desert. Injustice occurs when one good dominates the others, as when money buys votes, exemption from military service or a better verdict. Also called Walzer’s spheres of justice. Held by Michael Walzer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Relational egalitarianism"],"thinkers":["walzer"],"position":"complex_equality","tags":["walzer","spheres of justice","distributive justice","equality","pluralism","walzer’s spheres of justice"]},{"term":"Neo-Aristotelian communitarianism","def":"A good human life is learned and lived within communities and traditions of practice, so politics should serve the shared human good.","more":"This strand of communitarianism draws on Aristotle and on Alasdair MacIntyre’s After Virtue (1981). MacIntyre argued that modern moral debate is chaotic because it uses fragments of older traditions without the shared idea of the human good that made sense of them. Virtues are the qualities we need to achieve the goods internal to practices (such as farming, medicine, chess or raising a family) and to live a coherent life within a tradition. Politics, on this view, should not be neutral about the good life, as many liberals ask. Held by Alasdair MacIntyre, Aristotle.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Political perfectionism"],"thinkers":["macintyre","aristotle"],"position":"aristotelian_communitarianism","tags":["aristotle","macintyre","virtue","communitarianism","community","tradition"]},{"term":"Responsive communitarianism","def":"A good society balances individual rights with shared responsibilities, strengthened mainly through moral dialogue and civil society, not coercion.","more":"Responsive communitarianism was launched in the early 1990s by the sociologist Amitai Etzioni, together with a public platform signed by scholars and public figures. It held that Western societies, especially the United States, had come to stress individual rights and neglect responsibilities to family, neighbours and community. The cure is not to cancel rights but to rebalance them: rights come with matching responsibilities. Etzioni stressed that a community’s values should be promoted mainly by persuasion, example and the “moral voice” of neighbours and civil society, rather than by law. Held by Amitai Etzioni, Mary Ann Glendon.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Liberal communitarianism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"responsive_communitarianism","tags":["etzioni","communitarianism","rights and responsibilities","common good","community"]},{"term":"Confucian communitarianism","def":"People flourish through relationships, roles and rituals, so politics should cultivate virtue and social harmony.","more":"Confucian communitarianism draws on Confucius, Mencius and later Confucian thinkers to offer an alternative to Western liberalism. It sees persons as constituted by relationships (parent and child, ruler and subject, friend and friend) and holds that people flourish by cultivating virtues such as humaneness (ren) and by observing rituals (li) that express respect. Government should lead by moral example and promote the people’s welfare and education, not merely protect rights. Modern defenders, such as Daniel A. Also called political Confucianism. Held by Confucius, Mencius, Daniel A. Bell, Joseph Chan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Political meritocracy","Political perfectionism"],"thinkers":["confucius","mencius","daniel_bell"],"position":"confucian_communitarianism","tags":["confucianism","confucius","communitarianism","ritual","li","harmony","roles","political confucianism"]},{"term":"Neo-republicanism","def":"Freedom is non-domination, so a just state protects citizens from arbitrary power while staying under their control.","more":"Neo-republicanism is a revival, since the 1990s, of the Roman and early-modern idea of freedom as not living under another’s arbitrary power. Philip Pettit (Republicanism, 1997) and Quentin Skinner (Liberty before Liberalism, 1998) argued that a slave with a kind master is unfree, even if he is never interfered with, because he lives at the master’s mercy. The state’s job is to reduce such domination in private life, for example by employers or husbands, through law and social support. But the state itself must not dominate: its power must be checked by the rule of law, the separation of powers, and a “contestatory” democracy in which citizens can challenge decisions. Also called neo-Roman republicanism. Held by Philip Pettit, Quentin Skinner, Maurizio Viroli.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Republicanism (freedom as non-domination)"],"thinkers":["pettit","skinner"],"position":"neo_republicanism","tags":["republicanism","non-domination","pettit","civic republicanism","freedom","neo-roman republicanism"]},{"term":"Civic humanism","def":"Taking part in governing together is a central part of a good human life, not only a protection for private freedom.","more":"Civic humanism, sometimes called classical republicanism, holds that human beings realise themselves partly through active citizenship: debating, deciding and acting together on the affairs of their city. The idea goes back to Aristotle’s claim that humans are political animals, and was revived in Renaissance Florence; historians such as Hans Baron and J. G. A. Also called classical republicanism. Held by Aristotle, Hannah Arendt, Niccolò Machiavelli.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Republicanism (freedom as non-domination)","Positive liberty"],"thinkers":["aristotle","machiavelli"],"position":"civic_humanism","tags":["civic republicanism","republicanism","political participation","active citizenship","renaissance humanism","machiavelli","classical republicanism"]},{"term":"Labour republicanism","def":"Workers who depend on employers for their living are dominated, so a free republic needs economic independence and cooperative work.","more":"Labour republicanism applies the republican idea of freedom as non-domination to work. In the nineteenth century, American labour activists, most notably the Knights of Labor, argued that “wage slavery” was incompatible with republican citizenship: a worker who must obey a boss to survive is dependent, as a servant is. Alex Gourevitch recovered this tradition in From Slavery to the Cooperative Commonwealth (2015). Its remedy is not only fair wages but a “cooperative commonwealth” in which workers own and govern their workplaces, backed by strong unions and the right to strike. Also called radical republicanism. Held by Alex Gourevitch.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Republicanism (freedom as non-domination)","Socialism","Democratic socialism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"labour_republicanism","tags":["workplace democracy","wage slavery","republicanism","domination","labour","worker freedom","radical republicanism"]},{"term":"Anarcho-communism","def":"Abolish the state, money and private property, and let free communes share goods according to need.","more":"Anarcho-communism holds that both the state and capitalism should be abolished and replaced by a federation of free, self-governing communes. Land, buildings and tools would be held in common, and goods shared out according to need, without money or wages. Peter Kropotkin, its best-known theorist, argued in The Conquest of Bread (1892) that modern production could already supply everyone’s needs, and in Mutual Aid (1902) that cooperation is as natural as competition. Unlike Marxists, anarcho-communists reject any transitional workers’ state, warning that it would become a new ruling class. Also called anarchist communism. Held by Peter Kropotkin, Errico Malatesta, Emma Goldman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Anarchism","Distribution according to need","Libertarian socialism"],"thinkers":["kropotkin"],"position":"anarcho_communism","tags":["anarchist communism","kropotkin","anarchism","communism","mutual aid","stateless society"]},{"term":"Anarcho-syndicalism","def":"Workers organised in revolutionary unions should take over industry and run society through federations of workplaces.","more":"Anarcho-syndicalism holds that labour unions (syndicates, from the French syndicat) are both the means of overthrowing capitalism and the basis of a free society afterwards. Workers organised by industry would use direct action, above all the general strike, to take over workplaces, and then run production through democratic councils federated locally and nationally, with no state. Rudolf Rocker set out the theory in Anarcho-Syndicalism (1938). The largest experiment came in Spain, where the CNT union, by its own count over a million strong by late 1936, collectivised factories and farms in Catalonia and Aragon, until rivals within the Republic and then Franco’s victory in 1939 ended the experiment. Held by Rudolf Rocker, Fernand Pelloutier, Noam Chomsky.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Anarchism","Anarcho-communism","Libertarian socialism"],"thinkers":["chomsky"],"position":"anarcho_syndicalism","tags":["syndicalism","rocker","anarchism","trade unions","general strike","workers control"]},{"term":"Mutualism","def":"People should own what they personally use and trade fairly, with free credit from cooperative banks replacing landlords’ and lenders’ unearned income.","more":"Mutualism is the anarchism of Pierre-Joseph Proudhon (1809–1865), the first writer to call himself an anarchist. In What Is Property? (1840) he declared that “property is theft”, meaning the right to draw rent, interest and profit from things that others work; but he defended “possession”, the use of land and tools by those who work them. Mutualists keep markets and exchange, but want them made fair: goods should trade at roughly their labour cost, and a people’s bank should lend at cost so that workers can own their means of production. Held by Pierre-Joseph Proudhon, Benjamin Tucker, Kevin Carson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Anarchism","Individualist anarchism","Market socialism"],"thinkers":["proudhon","tucker"],"position":"mutualism","tags":["proudhon","anarchism","mutual banking","fair exchange","cooperatives","possession"]},{"term":"Individualist anarchism","def":"Each individual is sovereign over themselves, and every association must be voluntary and open to exit.","more":"Individualist anarchism puts the sovereignty of the individual at the centre. It rejects the state because the state imposes obligations people never chose. Max Stirner’s The Ego and Its Own (1844) went furthest, treating the state, morality and even “humanity” as phantoms that rule over the unique individual. American individualists, including Josiah Warren, Lysander Spooner and Benjamin Tucker, argued for individual sovereignty, voluntary association and free markets purged of monopolies granted by the state over money, land, tariffs and patents. Held by Max Stirner, Benjamin Tucker, Lysander Spooner, Josiah Warren.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Anarchism","Mutualism","Anarcho-capitalism"],"thinkers":["stirner","tucker"],"position":"individualist_anarchism","tags":["anarchist individualism","stirner","tucker","self-sovereignty","anarchism","egoism"]},{"term":"Social democracy","def":"Keep a market economy, but tame it with a strong welfare state, public services, progressive taxes and powerful unions, reached by gradual reform.","more":"Social democracy grew out of the socialist movement but came to accept a mainly private market economy, aiming to make it serve everyone through democratic reform. Eduard Bernstein argued in 1899 that socialism should advance gradually through parliaments and unions, not revolution. After 1945, social democratic parties, especially in Scandinavia, built universal welfare states with public health care and education, progressive taxation, strong unions and organised bargaining between employers and labour. Anthony Crosland argued in The Future of Socialism (1956) that equality, not public ownership, was the real socialist goal. Held by Eduard Bernstein, Anthony Crosland.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Egalitarian liberalism","Democratic socialism","Piecemeal reform"],"thinkers":["bernstein"],"position":"social_democracy","tags":["welfare state","mixed economy","social democrats","regulated capitalism","nordic model","socialism"]},{"term":"Liberal socialism","def":"Keep liberal rights and democracy in full, but replace private ownership of the main means of production with social ownership.","more":"Liberal socialism holds that liberal values, such as individual rights, free speech and democracy, are best realised under socialist economic arrangements. The Italian anti-fascist Carlo Rosselli gave the idea its name in Liberal Socialism (Socialismo liberale, 1930), arguing that socialism is liberalism extended to the economy. John Rawls later listed liberal socialism, alongside property-owning democracy, as a regime that could satisfy his principles of justice, while welfare-state capitalism could not, because it lets a small class control the economy and politics. Liberal socialists usually favour markets with worker-owned or publicly owned firms, combined with strong protection for civil liberties. Held by Carlo Rosselli, Norberto Bobbio.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Egalitarian liberalism","Market socialism","Justice as fairness"],"thinkers":[],"position":"liberal_socialism","tags":["socialism","liberalism","rosselli","democratic socialism","market socialism"]},{"term":"Libertarian socialism","def":"The means of production should be owned in common and managed from below by workers and communities, without a central state.","more":"Libertarian socialism is a broad family that joins socialist ownership with a deep suspicion of state power. It holds that workers and communities should own and manage production themselves, through councils, cooperatives and federations, rather than through a central planning state. The term covers social anarchists, council communists such as Anton Pannekoek, and guild socialists such as G. D. Held by Noam Chomsky, G. D. H. Cole, Anton Pannekoek.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Socialism","Anarchism","Anarcho-communism","Anarcho-syndicalism"],"thinkers":["chomsky"],"position":"libertarian_socialism","tags":["socialist anarchism","anarchism","socialism","self-management","workers control","anti-authoritarian"]},{"term":"Liberal conservatism","def":"A free economy and limited state flourish best when supported by tradition, religion and the family.","more":"Liberal conservatism combines classical liberal economics with conservative respect for tradition and moral order. In the United States it took the name “fusionism” from the writer Frank Meyer, whose In Defense of Freedom (1962) argued that virtue must be freely chosen, so the state should protect liberty while churches, families and communities cultivate virtue. Many European centre-right parties have held similar views. Liberal conservatives favour free markets, low taxes and the rule of law, together with strong families, national institutions and caution about rapid social change. Also called fusionism, conservative liberalism. Held by Frank Meyer, William F. Buckley Jr..","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Conservatism","Classical liberalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"liberal_conservatism","tags":["conservative liberalism","conservatism","liberalism","free market","tradition","fusionism"]},{"term":"Liberal communitarianism","def":"Liberal rights and fairness come first, but they depend on shared ways of life that politics must also sustain.","more":"Liberal communitarianism accepts the core of liberalism (equal rights, democracy and toleration) while taking on board the communitarian point that persons are shaped by communities, and that a liberal society depends on shared values and attachments. Charles Taylor argued in “Cross-Purposes: The Liberal–Communitarian Debate” (1989) that one can hold a communitarian picture of the self and still defend liberal rights. Michael Walzer described communitarianism as a recurring corrective within liberalism, not a rival theory. Liberal communitarians tend to support public recognition of minority cultures, civic education, national service and strong local institutions, while rejecting coercive enforcement of one way of life. Held by Charles Taylor, Michael Walzer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Communitarianism","Egalitarian liberalism","Responsive communitarianism"],"thinkers":["walzer"],"position":"liberal_communitarianism","tags":["communitarianism","liberalism","community","common good"]},{"term":"Republican liberalism","def":"Liberal rights need citizens with civic virtue, so a liberal state should cultivate public spirit and participation.","more":"Republican liberalism, a phrase Richard Dagger used in Civic Virtues (1997), joins liberal commitments to rights and autonomy with the republican insistence on civic virtue and active citizenship. Liberals and republicans, on this view, are not rivals: a regime of rights survives only if citizens are willing to vote, serve on juries, follow public debate and resist abuses of power. The state may therefore encourage civic education, public service and participation, without dictating anyone’s personal idea of the good life. Rawls himself accepted classical republicanism in this sense as compatible with his political liberalism, while rejecting the civic humanist claim that participation is the highest good. Held by Richard Dagger.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Politics and society","related":["Republicanism (freedom as non-domination)","Egalitarian liberalism","Neo-republicanism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"republican_liberalism","tags":["republicanism","civic virtue","liberalism","citizenship","civic republicanism"]},{"term":"Substance dualism","def":"You are, or have, a non-physical mind or soul, distinct from your body.","more":"Substance dualism says that a person is, or has, two different kinds of thing: a physical body and a non-physical mind or soul. The mind is what thinks, feels and chooses; it has no size, shape or location in the way a brain does, and it could in principle exist without the body. René Descartes gave the classic modern version in the 1640s, though the idea is far older and appears in Plato. Most versions say mind and body causally affect each other. Also called Cartesian dualism, Mind-body dualism. Held by René Descartes, Plato, Richard Swinburne, William Hasker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Interactionism","Anti-physicalism","Theism"],"thinkers":["descartes","plato","swinburne","hasker","avicenna"],"position":"substance_dualism","tags":["dualism","cartesian dualism","mind-body dualism","soul","descartes","immaterial mind"]},{"term":"Neutral monism","def":"Mind and matter are two ways of arranging one underlying stuff that is neither mental nor physical.","more":"Neutral monism says there is one basic kind of stuff, but it is neither mental nor physical in itself. Mind and matter are two different arrangements, or groupings, of this neutral stuff. William James compared it to paint: the same paint can sit in a shop as a substance for sale, or be arranged on a canvas as a picture. In the same way, one event can belong to the story of a physical object and also to the story of someone’s mind. Also called dual-aspect monism (a close relative). Held by William James, Bertrand Russell, Ernst Mach.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Physicalism","Idealism","Panpsychism"],"thinkers":["james","russell","mach"],"position":"neutral_monism","tags":["russell","spinoza","dual aspect","monism","mind and matter","william james","dual-aspect monism (a close relative)"]},{"term":"Reductive physicalism","def":"Consciousness is a brain process, and science will explain it the way biology explained life.","more":"Reductive physicalism, as an answer to the hard problem, says that conscious experience is real and is nothing over and above certain brain processes, so a mature brain science will explain it in the same way biology explained life. A century and a half ago, “life” also seemed to need an extra spark (vitalism, the idea of a special life-force). Yet once metabolism, reproduction and growth were explained, nothing was left over. Reductive physicalists expect the same for consciousness: explain how the brain discriminates, attends, reports, controls behaviour and models its own states, and the hard problem will shrink and disappear. Held by Patricia Churchland, Anil Seth, Stanislas Dehaene, David Papineau.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism","Identity theory","Type-B physicalism"],"thinkers":["pchurchland","seth","papineau"],"position":"reductive_physicalism","tags":["physicalism","materialism","identity theory","mind-brain identity","reductionism"]},{"term":"Non-reductivism about consciousness","def":"The hard problem is real: no explanation of brain mechanisms can explain why experience feels like something.","more":"Non-reductivism says the hard problem is real and cannot be solved by explaining brain functions, however thoroughly. The “easy problems” (David Chalmers’ term, 1995) are explaining how the brain distinguishes colours, focuses attention and produces speech; they are hard work but standard science. The hard problem is why any of this is accompanied by experience: why there is “something it is like”, in Thomas Nagel’s phrase, to see red or feel pain. Non-reductivists say no account of mechanisms can answer this, because experience is not itself a mechanism. Held by David Chalmers, Thomas Nagel, Philip Goff, Galen Strawson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Property dualism","Panpsychism","Anti-physicalism"],"thinkers":["chalmers","nagel","goff","gstrawson"],"position":"non_reductivism_about_consciousness","tags":["hard problem","explanatory gap","chalmers","anti-reductionism","consciousness"]},{"term":"Illusionism","def":"The private, felt quality of experience is an illusion created by the brain’s model of itself.","more":"Illusionism says that phenomenal consciousness, the supposed private, inner, felt quality of experience (qualia), does not really exist. It only seems to. Your brain builds a simplified model of its own processing, and that model represents your states as having ineffable, glowing inner qualities. The model is useful but inaccurate, a bit like a computer desktop that shows neat folders while hiding the circuits. Held by Keith Frankish, Daniel Dennett.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Eliminative materialism","Type-A physicalism","Physicalism"],"thinkers":["frankish","dennett"],"position":"illusionism","tags":["illusion","eliminativism about qualia","frankish","dennett","qualia","consciousness"]},{"term":"Mysterianism","def":"Consciousness is natural, but human minds may be built in a way that stops us ever understanding how brains produce it.","more":"Mysterianism says that consciousness is a natural feature of the brain, nothing supernatural, but that human beings may be permanently unable to understand how the brain produces it. Colin McGinn argues that our minds, like every animal’s, have limits: a dog cannot understand calculus, not because calculus is magic but because dog brains lack the concepts. Perhaps the link between brain and experience is like that for us, “cognitively closed”, in McGinn’s term. We grasp the brain through perception and science, and experience through introspection, but neither route reveals the bridge between them. Also called new mysterianism, cognitive closure. Held by Colin McGinn, Noam Chomsky.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism","Non-reductivism about consciousness"],"thinkers":["mcginn","chomsky"],"position":"mysterianism","tags":["new mysterianism","mcginn","cognitive closure","mystery of consciousness","consciousness"]},{"term":"Interactionism","def":"Mind and body causally affect each other in both directions.","more":"Interactionism says mind and body causally affect each other in both directions. Stubbing your toe causes a feeling of pain; your decision to move causes your arm to move. This is how things seem to everyone, and it was René Descartes’ view: he suggested the soul acts on the body through the pineal gland, a small structure deep in the brain. Today the term appears mostly in discussions of dualism, since for physicalists mental causation is simply brain causation and needs no special name. Also called interactionist dualism. Held by René Descartes, Karl Popper, John Eccles, Richard Swinburne.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Substance dualism","Property dualism"],"thinkers":["descartes","popper","swinburne"],"position":"interactionism","tags":["dualist interactionism","mental causation","descartes","dualism","mind-body problem","interactionist dualism"]},{"term":"Parallelism","def":"Mind and body never affect each other but are arranged to run in perfect step.","more":"Parallelism says mind and body do not causally affect each other at all, yet they always run in step. When you decide to raise your arm, your arm rises, but the decision did not cause it; the two series of events were set up in advance to correspond. Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz called this “pre-established harmony” and compared mind and body to two clocks made by a perfect craftsman to keep exactly the same time. For Leibniz, God arranged the harmony at creation. Also called pre-established harmony. Held by Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Occasionalism","Substance dualism"],"thinkers":["leibniz"],"position":"psychophysical_parallelism","tags":["psychophysical parallelism","leibniz","pre-established harmony","occasionalism","dualism","mind-body problem"]},{"term":"Phenomenal concept strategy","def":"Mary gains a new way of thinking about something physical she already knew, not knowledge of a new non-physical fact.","more":"The phenomenal concept strategy is a physicalist reply to the knowledge argument and the hard problem. It says that when Mary, who knew every physical fact about colour, first sees red, she gains a new concept: a “phenomenal concept” of what seeing red is like, which can only be acquired by having the experience. With it she can think new thoughts (“so this is what seeing red is like”), but those thoughts pick out an old fact, a brain state she already knew about under scientific concepts. Compare learning that the Morning Star is the Evening Star: one planet, two ways of thinking about it. Held by Brian Loar, David Papineau, Katalin Balog.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Type-B physicalism","Physicalism","Acquaintance hypothesis"],"thinkers":["papineau"],"position":"phenomenal_concept_strategy","tags":["phenomenal concepts","knowledge argument","mary's room","physicalism","explanatory gap"]},{"term":"Ability hypothesis","def":"Mary learns no new fact; she gains new abilities to recognise, imagine and remember red.","more":"The ability hypothesis says that when Mary first sees red, she learns no new fact. Instead she gains new abilities: to recognise red things by sight, to imagine red, and to remember what seeing red was like. “Knowing what it’s like” is a kind of know-how, like knowing how to ride a bike, not knowledge that some fact is true. Laurence Nemirow (from 1980) and David Lewis (1988) developed the idea. Held by David Lewis, Laurence Nemirow.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism","Acquaintance hypothesis"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"position":"ability_hypothesis","tags":["know-how","lewis","nemirow","knowledge argument","mary's room","physicalism"]},{"term":"Acquaintance hypothesis","def":"Mary gains direct acquaintance with an experience she already knew all the facts about.","more":"The acquaintance hypothesis says that what Mary gains when she first sees red is neither a new fact nor merely a set of abilities, but acquaintance: direct, first-hand contact with something she previously knew only by description. Earl Conee proposed it in 1994, drawing on Bertrand Russell’s distinction between knowledge by acquaintance (knowing a thing directly, as you know a taste) and knowledge by description (knowing facts about it). You can know every fact about a city from books and still gain something by visiting it, without learning any new fact about it. Mary meets the experience of red face to face. Held by Earl Conee.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Ability hypothesis","Phenomenal concept strategy","Physicalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"acquaintance_hypothesis","tags":["acquaintance","conee","knowledge argument","mary's room","physicalism"]},{"term":"Type-A physicalism","def":"Once every brain function is explained, nothing about consciousness is left to explain, even in principle.","more":"Type-A physicalism (a label from David Chalmers’ classification of views) says there is no deep gap between physical facts and facts about consciousness, not even in our concepts. Once you understand every function the brain performs, how it discriminates, reports, remembers and reacts, there is nothing further to explain, and anyone who fully grasped the physical facts could in principle work out the facts about experience. So zombies, physical duplicates with no inner life, are not really conceivable: people who think they imagine one are not imagining a full physical duplicate. And Mary, knowing everything physical, would not be surprised by red; Daniel Dennett suggested she could even spot a trick if shown a blue banana. Also called a priori physicalism. Held by Daniel Dennett, David Lewis.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism","Illusionism","Functionalism"],"thinkers":["dennett","lewis"],"position":"type_a_physicalism","tags":["type a materialism","analytic functionalism","deflationary physicalism","chalmers","physicalism","a priori physicalism"]},{"term":"Type-B physicalism","def":"Experiences are brain states, but this is a discovery, so zombies are imaginable yet impossible.","more":"Type-B physicalism (another label from Chalmers’ classification) says that conscious states are physical states, but that this is something we discover, not something we could work out by reasoning alone. There is a real gap in our concepts: nothing in the idea of a brain process logically guarantees that it feels like anything, which is why zombies seem conceivable. But the gap is not in reality. Compare “water is H2O”: before chemistry, you could imagine water not being H2O, yet water couldn’t actually be anything else. Also called a posteriori physicalism. Held by David Papineau, Ned Block, Brian Loar.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Phenomenal concept strategy","Identity theory","Physicalism"],"thinkers":["papineau","block"],"position":"type_b_physicalism","tags":["type b materialism","a posteriori physicalism","identity theory","zombies","physicalism"]},{"term":"Anti-physicalism","def":"Physical facts do not fix the facts about consciousness, so physicalism is false.","more":"Anti-physicalism is the conclusion that physicalism is false: the facts about consciousness are not fixed by the physical facts. Its most famous route is David Chalmers’ zombie argument. A zombie, in this sense, is a being physically identical to you, atom for atom, that behaves exactly as you do but has no inner experience at all. If such a being is conceivable, carefully and without hidden contradiction, then it is possible in some world; and if it is possible, then the physical facts about you do not by themselves guarantee your experience. Also called the zombie argument, the conceivability argument. Held by David Chalmers, Philip Goff.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Property dualism","Non-reductivism about consciousness","Panpsychism"],"thinkers":["chalmers","goff"],"position":"anti_physicalism","tags":["anti-materialism","dualism","knowledge argument","zombie argument","physicalism","consciousness","the zombie argument","the conceivability argument"]},{"term":"Understanding without consciousness","def":"A machine could really think and understand while feeling nothing at all.","more":"This view separates two things that usually go together in humans: intelligence (the capacity to understand, reason and solve problems) and consciousness (there being something it is like to be you). It holds that a machine could genuinely have the first without the second. A future AI might truly understand language and grasp ideas, in the functional sense in which we test understanding in people, while there is nothing it feels like to be it. Some think consciousness depends on biology, on being a living, self-maintaining body; Anil Seth argues along these lines. Also called intelligence without consciousness. Held by Christof Koch, Anil Seth.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Biological naturalism","Computational functionalism"],"thinkers":["koch","seth"],"position":"understanding_without_consciousness","tags":["chinese room","strong ai","machine understanding","artificial intelligence","searle","intelligence without consciousness"]},{"term":"Anti-mechanism","def":"No machine running a program could ever have a genuine mind.","more":"Anti-mechanism says that no machine of the kind we know how to build, a computer following a program, could ever genuinely have a mind. In 1961 J. R. Lucas argued from Kurt Gödel’s incompleteness theorem, which shows that any consistent formal system strong enough for arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove. Also called no machine minds, the Lucas-Penrose argument. Held by J. R. Lucas, Roger Penrose.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Substance dualism"],"thinkers":["penrose"],"position":"anti_mechanism","tags":["lucas","penrose","godel argument","computationalism","artificial intelligence","machine minds","no machine minds","the lucas-penrose argument"]},{"term":"Agnosticism about machine minds","def":"We have no reliable way to tell whether a machine really understands or feels, and may never have one.","more":"Agnosticism about machine minds says that we lack, and may never have, a reliable way to tell whether a machine genuinely understands or feels. Behaviour is not enough: AI systems trained on human writing can produce talk of feelings whether or not they have any. Jonathan Birch calls this the “gaming problem”: the very data that make a system convincing also make its behaviour poor evidence. And every theory of consciousness that might settle the question is itself controversial, so different theories give different verdicts about the same machine. Held by Eric Schwitzgebel, Ned Block.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Argument from analogy","Inference to the best explanation (other minds)"],"thinkers":["schwitzgebel","block"],"position":"agnosticism_about_machine_minds","tags":["ai consciousness","machine consciousness","other minds","turing test","artificial intelligence"]},{"term":"Robot reply","def":"Symbols alone may not give understanding, but a program connected to the world through a robot body would.","more":"The robot reply is a response to John Searle’s Chinese room. Searle imagined a man in a room answering Chinese questions by following an English rulebook for matching Chinese symbols; he passes for a Chinese speaker but understands nothing. The robot reply grants that shuffling symbols on its own may not give understanding, because the symbols are connected to nothing. But put the program in a robot with cameras, arms and sensors, so that its symbols are linked by perception and action to the things they stand for, and it would genuinely understand. Also called symbol grounding view. Held by Stevan Harnad.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Computational functionalism","Informational semantics","Enactivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"robot_reply","tags":["chinese room","symbol grounding","embodied cognition","searle","robotics","artificial intelligence","symbol grounding view"]},{"term":"Broad animal sentience","def":"Many animals, probably including fish and some invertebrates, have conscious experiences.","more":"This view holds that consciousness is widespread in the animal kingdom: not only mammals and birds but probably fish, and quite possibly some invertebrates such as octopuses, crabs and bees, have experiences, at least simple ones like pain and pleasure. ) Evidence includes animals trading off pain against reward, guarding wounds, learning to avoid places where they were harmed, and responding to painkillers in ways that mirror humans. In 2012 a group of scientists signed the Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness, stating that many non-human animals, including all mammals and birds and also octopuses, have the neural basis for conscious states. Philosophers Peter Godfrey-Smith, Michael Tye and Jonathan Birch defend versions of the view. Held by Peter Godfrey-Smith, Michael Tye, Jonathan Birch.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Panpsychism","Inference to the best explanation (other minds)"],"thinkers":[],"position":"broad_animal_sentience","tags":["animal sentience","animal consciousness","fish pain","invertebrates","sentience","animal minds"]},{"term":"Cortical requirement view","def":"Conscious experience needs a cortex or something like it, so fish and most invertebrates probably feel nothing.","more":"This view holds that conscious experience requires particular brain structures, especially a neocortex (the folded outer layer of the mammalian brain) or something closely equivalent. Since fish and most invertebrates lack these structures, their responses to harmful stimuli are likely unconscious, like the reflex that pulls your hand from a flame before you feel anything. The neuroscientist Brian Key argued in a 2016 paper titled “Why Fish Do Not Feel Pain” that fish lack the neural circuits needed for pain experience, and James D. Rose made earlier arguments along similar lines. Held by Brian Key, James D. Rose.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Identity theory","Higher-order theory of consciousness"],"thinkers":[],"position":"cortical_requirement_view","tags":["cortex","neocortex","animal consciousness","fish pain","animal minds"]},{"term":"Higher-order theory of consciousness","def":"A state is conscious when you are aware of being in it, so consciousness needs the ability to represent your own mind.","more":"Higher-order theories say that a mental state is conscious when the creature is aware of being in it, through a further “higher-order” thought or perception about that state. A pain you feel is a pain you are, in a minimal way, aware of having; an unconscious state, like the processing that lets you drive on “autopilot”, lacks such awareness. David Rosenthal and Peter Carruthers developed leading versions. The theory has a striking consequence for animals: consciousness requires the capacity to represent one’s own mental states, so creatures without it would have no conscious experiences. Also called HOT theory. Held by David Rosenthal, Peter Carruthers.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism","Cortical requirement view"],"thinkers":["carruthers"],"position":"higher_order_theory_of_consciousness","tags":["higher-order thought","hot theory","rosenthal","higher-order perception","meta-cognition","consciousness"]},{"term":"Animal automatism","def":"Animals are natural machines without thought or feeling.","more":"Animal automatism says that non-human animals are complex natural machines without thought or feeling: their behaviour, however clever, runs on mechanical processes, as a clock tells time without knowing it. René Descartes is the classic source. In his Discourse on the Method (1637) he argued that animals lack real language and flexible reason, the signs of a thinking soul. How far Descartes denied animals feeling, as opposed to thought, is contested among scholars; some of his followers, such as Nicolas Malebranche, more clearly denied animal sensation. Also called the beast-machine view. Held by René Descartes, Nicolas Malebranche.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Substance dualism"],"thinkers":["descartes","malebranche"],"position":"animal_automatism","tags":["beast-machine","descartes","animals as machines","bête machine","animal minds","the beast-machine view"]},{"term":"Wittgensteinian criterial view","def":"Behaviour is not mere evidence of a hidden mind: it is part of what our mental words mean.","more":"The Wittgensteinian view says the problem of other minds rests on a confused picture: that minds are hidden inner objects, and behaviour only outward evidence from which we must infer them. Following Ludwig Wittgenstein’s later work, it holds that certain behaviour is a criterion for mental states: part of what our words “pain”, “joy” and “expect” mean is tied to their typical expression in crying out, laughing and waiting. When you see someone writhing with a broken leg, you don’t form a hypothesis about pain; you see that they are in pain, and doubting it would show you had not grasped the concept. Norman Malcolm and P. Also called Wittgensteinian view of other minds. Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein, Norman Malcolm, P. F. Strawson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Direct perception of other minds","Interpretivism"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein","pfstrawson"],"position":"criterial_view_of_other_minds","tags":["criteria","wittgenstein","other minds","private language","behaviourism","wittgensteinian view of other minds"]},{"term":"Direct perception of other minds","def":"We can see others’ feelings in their faces and actions, without inferring them.","more":"This view, developed mainly in the phenomenological tradition (philosophy that starts from careful description of experience), says we can directly perceive other people’s mental states in their expressions. When a friend’s face lights up, you don’t see a moving face and then infer joy; you see the joy in the face, as you see anger in a clenched fist. Max Scheler argued this in the early 20th century, and today Dan Zahavi and Shaun Gallagher defend versions, often citing developmental psychology: very young infants respond to faces and emotional expressions long before they could reason by analogy. The view does not claim we see everything in another mind, only that minds are partly visible in bodies. Held by Max Scheler, Dan Zahavi, Shaun Gallagher.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Wittgensteinian criterial view","Enactivism"],"thinkers":["scheler"],"position":"direct_perception_of_other_minds","tags":["social perception","other minds","empathy","phenomenology","direct social perception"]},{"term":"Informational semantics","def":"A thought is about horses because it is reliably caused by horses, carrying information about them.","more":"How can a thought be about something, a horse, a distant star or next Tuesday? Philosophers call this “aboutness” intentionality. Many cognitive scientists hold the representational theory of mind: thinking is the manipulation of inner representations, a bit like sentences in the head (Jerry Fodor’s “language of thought”). Informational semantics adds an account of what makes a representation about what it is about: a brain state represents horses because it is reliably caused by horses, so it carries information about them, the way smoke carries information about fire. Also called causal theory of content. Held by Fred Dretske, Jerry Fodor.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Teleosemantics","Functionalism","Representationalism (about experience)"],"thinkers":[],"position":"informational_semantics","tags":["causal theory of content","dretske","fodor","asymmetric dependence","mental content","intentionality"]},{"term":"Teleosemantics","def":"What a mental state represents is fixed by what it was selected, by evolution or learning, to do.","more":"Teleosemantics says that what a mental state represents is fixed by its biological function: what it was selected for, by evolution or by learning. ) A frog’s fly-detector represents flies, not “small dark moving dots”, because detecting flies is what it was selected to do: that is how it helped the frog’s ancestors survive. This makes room for error, which simple causal theories struggle with: when the frog snaps at a pellet, its detector is malfunctioning, misrepresenting the pellet as a fly. Ruth Millikan and David Papineau developed the view in the 1980s, and it is one of the main naturalistic theories of content. Also called teleological theory of content. Held by Ruth Millikan, David Papineau, Karen Neander.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Informational semantics","Physicalism"],"thinkers":["papineau"],"position":"teleosemantics","tags":["teleosemantic theory","millikan","biosemantics","teleological functions","mental representation","intentionality","teleological theory of content"]},{"term":"Interpretivism","def":"Having beliefs and desires is a matter of being best interpreted as having them.","more":"Interpretivism says that having beliefs and desires is a matter of being best interpreted as having them. Daniel Dennett’s “intentional stance” is the strategy of predicting a system’s behaviour by treating it as a rational agent with beliefs and desires. You do this with people, with dogs, and even with a chess computer (“it wants to take my queen”). For Dennett, any system whose behaviour is reliably and richly predictable from this stance really does have beliefs; there need be no further inner fact, such as a sentence in the head. Also called the intentional stance. Held by Daniel Dennett, Donald Davidson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Wittgensteinian criterial view"],"thinkers":["dennett"],"position":"interpretivism","tags":["intentional stance","dennett","davidson","radical interpretation","belief","intentionality","the intentional stance"]},{"term":"Phenomenal intentionality","def":"The most basic aboutness comes from conscious experience itself.","more":"The phenomenal intentionality theory says that the most basic kind of aboutness comes from conscious experience itself. When you see a red apple, your experience presents redness and roundness simply by feeling the way it does; when you understand a sentence, there is something it is like to grasp its meaning. Other kinds of representation, such as unconscious brain states or words on a page, are about things only in a derived way, through their links to conscious minds. Defenders include Terence Horgan and John Tienson, Uriah Kriegel, Galen Strawson and Angela Mendelovici. Held by Terence Horgan, Uriah Kriegel, Galen Strawson, Angela Mendelovici.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Non-reductivism about consciousness","Qualia realism"],"thinkers":["gstrawson"],"position":"phenomenal_intentionality","tags":["intentionality","phenomenology","aboutness","consciousness","horgan","kriegel"]},{"term":"Representationalism (about experience)","def":"What an experience feels like is fixed by how it represents the world as being.","more":"Representationalism about experience says that what an experience is like is fixed by what it represents, how it presents the world as being. The redness in your experience of a tomato is not a special inner “paint” on your mind; it is the redness your experience attributes to the tomato. Gilbert Harman pointed to the “transparency” of experience: try to focus on the qualities of your experience itself, and you find yourself attending only to the things experienced, the tomato’s colour and shape. If so, there are no extra qualities of experience over and above what it represents. Also called intentionalism. Held by Gilbert Harman, Michael Tye, Fred Dretske.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Informational semantics","Physicalism","Functionalism"],"thinkers":["harman"],"position":"representationalism_about_consciousness","tags":["intentionalism","representationalism","transparency of experience","tye","qualia"]},{"term":"Qualia realism","def":"Experiences have intrinsic felt qualities that go beyond what they represent.","more":"Qualia realism, in the sense used here, says that experiences have qualia: intrinsic felt qualities that go beyond what the experience represents. Two people could represent the world in the same way and yet have experiences that feel different, or the same feel could carry different content. Ned Block calls this felt side of experience “mental paint”, by analogy with the paint on a canvas that depicts a scene: the scene is what is represented, the paint is how it is done. Evidence he points to includes blurry vision (seeing something blurrily differs from seeing it as blurry), afterimages, and moods with no clear object. Also called phenomenism, non-representational qualia. Held by Ned Block, Christopher Peacocke.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Non-reductivism about consciousness","Phenomenal intentionality"],"thinkers":["block"],"position":"qualia_realism","tags":["qualia","subjective experience","what it's like","felt quality","inverted spectrum","consciousness","phenomenism","non-representational qualia"]},{"term":"Extended mind thesis","def":"The mind can extend beyond the skull into tools such as notebooks and phones.","more":"The extended mind thesis, proposed by Andy Clark and David Chalmers in 1998, says that the mind can extend beyond the skull and skin into tools and the environment. Their example: Inga remembers that the museum is on 53rd Street and walks there. Otto, who has Alzheimer’s disease, always carries a notebook; he looks up the address and walks there. Clark and Chalmers argue that the notebook plays the same role for Otto that biological memory plays for Inga, so it is literally part of his memory: Otto believed the museum was on 53rd Street even before he looked. Also called extended cognition. Held by Andy Clark, David Chalmers, Mark Rowlands.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Enactivism"],"thinkers":["chalmers","rowlands"],"position":"extended_mind_thesis","tags":["extended mind","clark and chalmers","otto's notebook","extended cognition","4e cognition"]},{"term":"Embedded cognition","def":"Thinking leans heavily on tools and surroundings, but it still happens inside the organism.","more":"Embedded cognition says that thinking relies heavily on the body and environment, on notebooks, diagrams, fingers for counting and phones, but that the thinking itself still happens inside the organism, typically in the brain. Tools are crucial inputs and aids, not parts of the mind. Robert Rupert called this the hypothesis of embedded cognition, and Fred Adams and Ken Aizawa argue for “the bounds of cognition”: cognitive processes have distinctive features, such as working with representations that have meaning in their own right, which notebooks lack. On this view, Otto’s notebook is like a reference book he consults; it helps him remember, but it no more remembers than a library thinks. Also called bounded cognition, hypothesis of embedded cognition. Held by Robert Rupert, Fred Adams, Ken Aizawa.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"embedded_cognition","tags":["embedded mind","situated cognition","4e cognition","scaffolding","bounded cognition","hypothesis of embedded cognition"]},{"term":"Enactivism","def":"The mind is not inside the head but is the skilful activity of a living organism engaging its world.","more":"Enactivism says that minds are not things inside heads but activities: the ongoing, skilful interaction of a living organism with its environment. Perceiving, on this view, is something you do, like exploring a shape with your hands, not the brain building an internal picture. Alva Noë argues that seeing depends on practical knowledge of how appearances change as you move. Francisco Varela, Evan Thompson and Eleanor Rosch launched the approach in The Embodied Mind (1991), drawing on biology, phenomenology and Buddhist philosophy, and Thompson later argued that mind is continuous with life itself. Also called enactive cognition. Held by Francisco Varela, Evan Thompson, Alva Noë.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Extended mind thesis","Direct perception of other minds","Robot reply"],"thinkers":[],"position":"enactivism","tags":["enaction","varela","thompson","embodied cognition","sensorimotor","4e cognition","enactive cognition"]},{"term":"Anomalous monism","def":"Every mental event is a physical event, but there are no strict laws linking mental kinds to physical kinds.","more":"Anomalous monism is Donald Davidson’s version of physicalism, set out in “Mental Events” (1970). It is “monism” because each particular mental event, such as your sudden wish for tea, is one and the same as some physical event in your brain. It is “anomalous” (lawless) because there are no strict laws connecting mental kinds with physical kinds: nothing of the form “whenever someone wishes for tea, brain state B occurs”. Davidson argued that mental descriptions answer to the logic of reasons and interpretation, while physical descriptions answer to the laws of physics, and the two ways of carving up events never line up neatly. Also called Token physicalism (Davidson’s version). Held by Donald Davidson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Physicalism","Functionalism","Interpretivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"anomalous_monism","tags":["davidson","token identity","mental events","anomalism of the mental","supervenience","physicalism","token physicalism (davidson’s version)"]},{"term":"Russellian monism","def":"Physics describes only what matter does; consciousness, or something close to it, is what matter is in itself.","more":"Russellian monism starts from an observation made by Bertrand Russell in The Analysis of Matter (1927) and by Arthur Eddington: physics tells us only about structure and behaviour, such as mass being a disposition to resist acceleration. It never says what the things that have this structure are like in themselves. Russellian monists propose that these hidden “intrinsic natures” are experiences, or something that gives rise to experience. The physical world is then all there is, but it is not what we thought: its inner side is what shows up in consciousness. Also called Type-F monism. Held by Bertrand Russell, Galen Strawson, Philip Goff, Arthur Eddington.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Panpsychism","Neutral monism","Panprotopsychism"],"thinkers":["russell","gstrawson","goff"],"position":"russellian_monism","tags":["russell","panpsychism","panprotopsychism","intrinsic nature of matter","structuralism","consciousness","type-f monism"]},{"term":"Constitutive micropsychism","def":"The smallest bits of matter have simple experiences, and your experience is made up of theirs.","more":"Constitutive micropsychism is the most common form of panpsychism today. “Micro” means it starts at the smallest scale: fundamental particles, or whatever physics finally says is basic, have very simple experiences. “Constitutive” means that bigger experiences, like yours as you read this, are made up of those tiny ones, roughly as a wall is made up of bricks. Nothing wholly new appears when the right parts come together: your experience just is a very complex arrangement of micro-experiences. Held by Galen Strawson, Philip Goff.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Panpsychism","Russellian monism"],"thinkers":["gstrawson","goff"],"position":"constitutive_micropsychism","tags":["micropsychism","panpsychism","combination problem","goff","strawson","consciousness"]},{"term":"Cosmopsychism","def":"The universe as a whole is the basic conscious subject, and smaller minds are aspects or parts of it.","more":"Cosmopsychism turns panpsychism upside down. Instead of starting with tiny experiences in particles and building up, it starts with the whole cosmos, which it takes to be the one fundamental conscious subject. Animal and human minds are then derived from it, as aspects or parts of the cosmic experience. Philip Goff defends a version he calls priority cosmopsychism, drawing on Jonathan Schaffer’s view that the whole universe is more basic than its parts. Held by Philip Goff, Itay Shani, Yujin Nagasawa.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Panpsychism","Absolute idealism","Priority monism"],"thinkers":["goff"],"position":"cosmopsychism","tags":["cosmic consciousness","panpsychism","priority monism","goff","universal mind","consciousness"]},{"term":"Emergent panpsychism","def":"Tiny experiences exist everywhere, but a new, unified subject emerges when they come together in a brain.","more":"Emergent panpsychism accepts that basic physical things have simple experiences, but denies that your mind is just those experiences stacked together. When micro-experiences are organised in the right way, as in a brain, a genuinely new subject comes into being with its own unified point of view. This is “emergence” of a strong kind: the whole has features that are not simply the sum of its parts. Godehard Brüntrup has developed such a view, and others have explored ways in which the parts might fuse into a new whole. Held by Godehard Brüntrup.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Panpsychism","Emergentism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"emergent_panpsychism","tags":["panpsychism","emergence","combination problem","consciousness"]},{"term":"Panprotopsychism","def":"Basic matter is not conscious, but it has special properties that make up consciousness when combined.","more":"Panprotopsychism is a close cousin of panpsychism. It agrees that ordinary physics leaves out the intrinsic nature of matter, and that this nature is what explains consciousness. But it denies that particles have experiences. Instead, they have “protophenomenal” properties: not experiences themselves, but the ingredients from which experiences are built, as hydrogen and oxygen are not wet but make up water. Also called Panqualityism (Coleman’s version). Held by David Chalmers, Sam Coleman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Russellian monism","Panpsychism","Neutral monism"],"thinkers":["chalmers"],"position":"panprotopsychism","tags":["protophenomenal properties","panpsychism","russellian monism","chalmers","consciousness","panqualityism (coleman’s version)"]},{"term":"Thomistic hylomorphism","def":"The soul is the form of the body, yet because it can think, it can survive the body’s death.","more":"Thomistic hylomorphism is Thomas Aquinas’s development of Aristotle’s view, written up in the 13th century, chiefly in the Summa Theologiae. Like Aristotle, Aquinas held that the soul is the form of the living body and that a human being is one substance, not a soul trapped in a body. But he argued that the human soul is special. Thinking, especially grasping universal ideas such as “triangle” or “justice”, is not the work of any bodily organ. Held by Thomas Aquinas, Eleonore Stump, Edward Feser, David Oderberg.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Hylomorphism (about the mind)","Thomistic natural law","Immortal soul"],"thinkers":["aquinas","stump"],"position":"thomistic_hylomorphism","tags":["aquinas","thomism","soul as form of the body","hylomorphism","aristotle","immortal soul"]},{"term":"Naturalistic hylomorphism","def":"The soul is the organising structure of a living body, and it ends when that body dies.","more":"Naturalistic hylomorphism keeps Aristotle’s idea that the soul is the form of a living body, but drops any claim that part of the soul survives death. A human being is a living organism whose matter is organised to perceive, feel and think, just as an eye is organised to see. When the organism dies, its form, and so its soul, ceases. Recent defenders, such as William Jaworski, argue that this “structure” is a real part of nature that science already relies on, and that it lets us avoid both dualism and a flat physicalism that treats you as nothing but particles. Held by William Jaworski.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Hylomorphism (about the mind)","Enactivism","Emergentism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"naturalistic_hylomorphism","tags":["hylomorphism","aristotle","soul as form","neo-aristotelian","form and matter"]},{"term":"Berkeleian idealism","def":"To exist is to be perceived (or to perceive), and God perceives everything all the time.","more":"Berkeleian idealism, which Berkeley himself called immaterialism, is the view George Berkeley set out in his Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) and Three Dialogues (1713). Physical objects are nothing but collections of ideas, such as colours, shapes and textures, perceived by minds. His slogan was esse est percipi: to be is to be perceived. There is no matter lying behind our ideas, because we could never have any idea of it. Also called Immaterialism, Subjective idealism. Held by George Berkeley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Idealism","Phenomenalism"],"thinkers":["berkeley"],"position":"berkeleian_idealism","tags":["berkeley","subjective idealism","immaterialism","esse est percipi","to be is to be perceived","idealism"]},{"term":"Absolute idealism","def":"Reality is one all-embracing mind or experience, and individual minds are fragments or aspects of it.","more":"Absolute idealism holds that reality as a whole is a single mental or spiritual unity, often called the Absolute. Individual people, and the objects they perceive, are partial aspects of that one reality, not independent things. The view grew out of German philosophy after Kant, especially G. W. Also called Cosmic idealism (in recent versions). Held by G. W. F. Hegel, F. H. Bradley, Josiah Royce, Bernardo Kastrup.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Idealism","Cosmopsychism","Priority monism"],"thinkers":["hegel","bradley"],"position":"absolute_idealism","tags":["hegel","bradley","the absolute","absolute spirit","monistic idealism","idealism","cosmic idealism (in recent versions)"]},{"term":"Pluralistic idealism","def":"Reality is made of many minds, and what we call matter is how they appear.","more":"Pluralistic idealism holds that the world is made up of many mental beings, each with its own point of view, and nothing else at bottom. Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz’s Monadology (1714) is the classic version: reality is made of countless “monads”, simple soul-like substances, each perceiving the universe from its own angle, more or less clearly. Bodies are the way collections of monads appear. J. Also called Monadology (Leibniz’s version), Personal idealism. Held by Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, J. M. E. McTaggart.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Idealism","Panpsychism","Parallelism","Unreality of time"],"thinkers":["leibniz","mctaggart"],"position":"pluralistic_idealism","tags":["leibniz","monads","panpsychism","personal idealism","idealism","monadology (leibniz’s version)"]},{"term":"Classical neutral monism","def":"The world is made of neutral events, like sensations without a subject, which make up both minds and bodies.","more":"Classical neutral monism is the version of neutral monism held by Ernst Mach, William James and the later Bertrand Russell. All three thought that the world is built from simple events or “elements”, such as a patch of colour, a sound or a feeling of warmth, which are in themselves neither mental nor physical. Arranged one way, by their links to a body and a memory, these elements make up a mind; arranged another way, by the laws of physics, the very same elements make up a physical thing. ” (1904), James gave the example of a room: the same experience of the room belongs both to your personal history and to the history of the house. Held by Ernst Mach, William James, Bertrand Russell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Neutral monism","Phenomenalism","Russellian monism"],"thinkers":["mach","james","russell"],"position":"classical_neutral_monism","tags":["neutral monism","mach","russell","william james","neutral stuff","monism"]},{"term":"Dual-aspect monism","def":"There is one kind of reality with two sides: seen from outside it is physical, from inside it is mental.","more":"Dual-aspect monism says there is one underlying reality that shows itself in two ways. Baruch Spinoza’s Ethics (1677) is the classic statement: there is one substance, God or Nature, which can be grasped under the attribute of thought or under the attribute of extension (being spread out in space). Your mind and your body are the same thing understood in two ways, so they never need to interact. Gustav Fechner, a founder of experimental psychology, held a similar view in the 19th century, and Thomas Nagel has speculated that something like it may be true. Also called Double-aspect theory. Held by Baruch Spinoza, Gustav Fechner, Thomas Nagel.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Neutral monism","Russellian monism"],"thinkers":["spinoza","nagel"],"position":"dual_aspect_monism","tags":["double-aspect theory","spinoza","neutral monism","mind and matter","monism"]},{"term":"Global workspace theory","def":"A state is conscious when it is broadcast across the brain, so that many systems can use it at once.","more":"Global workspace theory says that the brain contains many specialised processes working in parallel and out of awareness, plus a limited “workspace” whose contents are broadcast to all of them. A piece of information becomes conscious when it wins the competition to enter the workspace and becomes available for speech, memory, planning and control. The psychologist Bernard Baars proposed the idea in 1988, comparing it to a spotlight on a theatre stage. Stanislas Dehaene and colleagues developed a neural version, the “global neuronal workspace”, in which conscious perception shows up as a sudden, brain-wide “ignition” of activity. Also called Global neuronal workspace theory. Held by Bernard Baars, Stanislas Dehaene, Daniel Dennett.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Computational functionalism"],"thinkers":["dennett"],"position":"global_workspace_theory","tags":["gwt","baars","dehaene","global neuronal workspace","broadcast","consciousness","global neuronal workspace theory"]},{"term":"Integrated information theory","def":"Consciousness is integrated information: a system is conscious to the degree its parts form one irreducible causal whole.","more":"Integrated information theory (IIT) was proposed by the neuroscientist Giulio Tononi in 2004 and developed with Christof Koch and others. It starts from features of experience that it takes as self-evident, such as that each experience is unified and specific, and asks what a physical system must be like to have them. Its answer is that consciousness is integrated information, measured by a quantity called Φ (phi): roughly, how much a system’s causal structure as a whole exceeds that of its parts taken separately. A system with high Φ is conscious; one with zero Φ is not. Also called IIT. Held by Giulio Tononi, Christof Koch.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Panpsychism","Biological naturalism"],"thinkers":["koch"],"position":"integrated_information_theory","tags":["iit","tononi","phi","integrated information","consciousness"]},{"term":"Recurrent processing theory","def":"A perception is conscious when signals loop back within the sensory areas, even before the rest of the brain uses them.","more":"Recurrent processing theory was developed by the Dutch neuroscientist Victor Lamme. When you see something, signals first sweep forward from the eyes through the visual areas of the brain. Lamme argued that this fast forward sweep is unconscious. Experience arises when later areas send signals back to earlier ones, so that activity loops (recurs) within the visual system and binds features such as colour and shape together. Also called Local recurrence theory. Held by Victor Lamme, Ned Block.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Qualia realism","Identity theory"],"thinkers":["block"],"position":"recurrent_processing_theory","tags":["rpt","lamme","recurrent processing","feedback loops","visual consciousness","consciousness","local recurrence theory"]},{"term":"Analytic functionalism","def":"Mental states are whatever plays the roles that everyday talk about the mind assigns them.","more":"Analytic functionalism, also called commonsense functionalism, holds that the meaning of words like “pain” and “belief” is fixed by the roles that ordinary people take these states to play. We all know, roughly, that pain is caused by injury, makes you want it to stop and makes you wince. Gather all such platitudes together and you have a definition: pain is whatever state plays that role. Science then discovers what actually plays it, in humans a certain brain process. Also called Commonsense functionalism. Held by David Lewis, D. M. Armstrong.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Type-A physicalism"],"thinkers":["lewis","armstrong"],"position":"analytic_functionalism","tags":["common-sense functionalism","functionalism","folk psychology","lewis","armstrong","functional role","commonsense functionalism"]},{"term":"Psychofunctionalism","def":"Mental states are defined by the roles the best scientific psychology finds them playing.","more":"Psychofunctionalism agrees with other functionalists that a mental state is defined by its causal role, but says the roles should come from the best scientific psychology, not from everyday talk. Ordinary people know little about memory consolidation or attention bottlenecks, yet cognitive science may find that these are exactly what makes a state a memory or a perception. The name was coined by Ned Block in 1978, and the view fits the practice of cognitive scientists such as Jerry Fodor and Zenon Pylyshyn, who modelled the mind as an information-processing system. Because the roles are discovered empirically rather than built into our concepts, psychofunctionalism usually allows that the identity of mental states with functional states is discovered, not known by reasoning alone. Held by Jerry Fodor, Zenon Pylyshyn.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Computational functionalism","Type-B physicalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"psychofunctionalism","tags":["scientific functionalism","functionalism","cognitive science","fodor","functional role"]},{"term":"Machine functionalism","def":"To have a mind is to run the right program: mental states are states of a computing machine.","more":"Machine functionalism was the first form of functionalism, proposed by Hilary Putnam in the 1960s. It compares a mind to a Turing machine, an abstract computer defined by a table that says, for each internal state and input, what output to give and which state to move to next. A mental state such as pain is one of the states in such a machine table, defined entirely by its links to inputs, outputs and other states. Any system that realises the same table, whether made of neurons, silicon or something else, has the same mental states. Held by Hilary Putnam, Alan Turing.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Functionalism","Computational functionalism"],"thinkers":["putnam","turing"],"position":"machine_functionalism","tags":["turing machine functionalism","functionalism","putnam","computational theory of mind","computationalism"]},{"term":"Emergent dualism","def":"The soul is a real non-physical self, but it grows out of the brain as the brain develops.","more":"Emergent dualism is a form of substance dualism defended by William Hasker in The Emergent Self (1999). It agrees with traditional dualists that you are a single, unified subject, not just a collection of brain processes, and that this subject is not physical. But it denies that God creates each soul separately and puts it into a body. Instead, when a nervous system becomes complex enough, it gives rise to a new kind of entity, a soul or “emergent self”, much as a magnet gives rise to a magnetic field. Held by William Hasker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Substance dualism","Emergentism","Interactionism"],"thinkers":["hasker"],"position":"emergent_dualism","tags":["hasker","emergent soul","dualism","emergence","substance dualism"]},{"term":"Emergentism","def":"At higher levels of complexity, genuinely new properties and powers appear that could not be predicted, even in principle, from the parts.","more":"Emergentism holds that when matter is organised in complex ways, genuinely new properties and causal powers emerge, which could not be predicted even in principle from complete knowledge of the parts and their laws. The British emergentists, including Samuel Alexander, C. Lloyd Morgan and C. D. Also called Strong emergence, British emergentism. Held by C. D. Broad, Samuel Alexander, C. Lloyd Morgan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Mind","related":["Autonomy of the special sciences","Scientific pluralism"],"thinkers":["broad"],"position":"emergentism","tags":["emergence","strong emergence","emergent properties","holism","levels of reality","downward causation","british emergentism"]},{"term":"Agnosticism about free will","def":"Free will needs an open future, and we don’t yet know whether we have one.","more":"Agnosticism about free will holds that the question is still open. In the version here, the agnostic agrees with incompatibilists that free will would require some of our choices not to be determined. But whether our brains ever work that way is an empirical question (one for observation and experiment) that current physics and neuroscience have not answered. So we should suspend judgment. Also called Agnostic incompatibilism. Held by Mark Balaguer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Agnosticism about determinism","Incompatibilism"],"thinkers":["balaguer"],"position":"free_will_agnosticism","tags":["free will","open question","determinism","indeterminism","agnostic incompatibilism"]},{"term":"Causal determinism","def":"The state of the world at any moment, plus the laws of nature, fixes everything that happens afterwards.","more":"Causal determinism is the claim that everything that happens is fixed by what came before together with the laws of nature. Given the exact state of the universe a million years ago, only one future was possible, including every word you have ever said. Pierre-Simon Laplace pictured an intellect that knew the position of every particle and every law; for it, he said, the future would be as clear as the past. Determinism is a claim about how the world is, not about whether we can predict it: a world could be determined yet far too complex to forecast. Also called Determinism. Held by Pierre-Simon Laplace, Baron d'Holbach, Baruch Spinoza, Albert Einstein.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Hard determinism","Near-determinism"],"thinkers":["holbach","spinoza","einstein"],"position":"causal_determinism","tags":["determinism","laws of nature","hard determinism","causation","fate","predictability"]},{"term":"Near-determinism","def":"There may be chance among tiny particles, but for anything as large as a brain, the world is as good as determined.","more":"Near-determinism accepts that quantum physics may involve real chance at the smallest scales, but holds that this chance averages out before it reaches the level of cells, brains and decisions. A brain cell is made of trillions of particles, and the random behaviour of any one of them is swamped by the rest, just as the wobbles of individual gas molecules don’t make a balloon unpredictable. So, for practical and philosophical purposes, our choices are as fixed by prior causes as they would be in a fully deterministic world. Ted Honderich defended a view of this kind. Held by Ted Honderich.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Causal determinism","Hard determinism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"near_determinism","tags":["determinism","quantum indeterminacy","adequate determinism","causation"]},{"term":"Agnosticism about determinism","def":"We don’t know whether the world, or our brains, are determined, and current science can’t yet tell us.","more":"Agnosticism about determinism holds that we lack good enough evidence to say whether determinism is true. Physics does not settle it: quantum mechanics can be read either way, and some of its interpretations are deterministic while others are not. Even within classical physics, the philosopher of science John Earman showed that determinism can fail in surprising cases, and is harder to define and test than it looks. And even if some physical events are undetermined, we don’t know whether any of that chance reaches our decisions. Held by John Earman, Mark Balaguer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Agnosticism about free will"],"thinkers":["balaguer"],"position":"agnosticism_about_determinism","tags":["determinism","indeterminism","open question","quantum mechanics"]},{"term":"Incompatibilism","def":"Free will and determinism cannot both be true: if everything is determined, no one is free.","more":"Incompatibilism is the thesis that free will is incompatible with determinism. If the past and the laws of nature fixed every choice you make, then you would not be free or truly responsible. On its own, incompatibilism says nothing about whether we are free. Libertarians accept it and conclude that determinism is false; hard determinists accept it and conclude that we are not free; hard incompatibilists accept it and add that indeterminism wouldn’t help either. Held by Peter van Inwagen, Robert Kane, Derk Pereboom, Carl Ginet.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Libertarianism (about free will)","Hard incompatibilism","Hard determinism"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen","kane","pereboom","ginet"],"position":"incompatibilism","tags":["consequence argument","van inwagen","hard determinism","libertarianism","free will"]},{"term":"Classical compatibilism","def":"You act freely when you do what you want and nothing stops or forces you.","more":"Classical compatibilism is the oldest and simplest form of compatibilism, found in Thomas Hobbes and David Hume and later in G. E. Moore and A. J. Also called Humean compatibilism, Conditional analysis. Held by Thomas Hobbes, David Hume, A. J. Ayer, G. E. Moore.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism","New dispositionalism"],"thinkers":["hobbes","hume","ayer","moore","schlick"],"position":"classical_compatibilism","tags":["compatibilism","soft determinism","hobbes","hume","freedom of the will","acting on desires","humean compatibilism","conditional analysis"]},{"term":"Hierarchical compatibilism","def":"You act freely when the desire that moves you is one you endorse, a desire you want to have.","more":"Hierarchical compatibilism was set out by Harry Frankfurt in 1971. He noticed that humans don’t just have desires (first-order desires, like wanting a cigarette); we also have desires about our desires (second-order desires, like wishing we didn’t want cigarettes). A person acts freely when the desire that actually moves them is one they want to move them. An unwilling addict acts unfreely, because the craving wins against what he wants to want; a willing addict who embraces the habit acts freely, even though he couldn’t resist. Also called Mesh theory, Real self view. Held by Harry Frankfurt, Gary Watson, Gerald Dworkin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism","Ownership view"],"thinkers":["frankfurt","watson"],"position":"hierarchical_compatibilism","tags":["frankfurt","second-order desires","identification","hierarchical model","free will","compatibilism","mesh theory","real self view"]},{"term":"Reasons-responsiveness view","def":"You act freely when your choice comes from your own reasoning and that reasoning would change course if there were good reason to.","more":"The reasons-responsiveness view, developed most fully by John Martin Fischer and Mark Ravizza, says a person controls their action when it comes from a way of reasoning that would have led elsewhere if the reasons had been different. Someone with a compulsion would take the drug however strong the reasons against it; an ordinary person would stop if they learned it was poisoned. Fischer and Ravizza add that the reasoning must be the agent’s own: they must have come to see themselves as responsible for it over time. Susan Wolf’s related “reason view” stresses the ability to recognise what is right and good. Also called Reasons-responsive compatibilism. Held by John Martin Fischer, Mark Ravizza.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Semicompatibilism","Source view","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["fischer"],"position":"reasons_responsiveness_view","tags":["fischer and ravizza","guidance control","reasons-responsiveness","compatibilism","moral responsibility","reasons-responsive compatibilism"]},{"term":"Reactive attitudes view","def":"Being responsible means being a fitting target of feelings like resentment, gratitude and forgiveness, and no theory could talk us out of these.","more":"In his 1962 essay “Freedom and Resentment”, P. F. Strawson changed the debate. Instead of asking first whether we have some metaphysical freedom, he looked at our reactive attitudes: the feelings, like resentment, gratitude, indignation and forgiveness, that we have toward people who treat us well or badly. Also called Strawsonian compatibilism. Held by P. F. Strawson, R. Jay Wallace, Michael McKenna.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism","Ownership view"],"thinkers":["pfstrawson","wallace"],"position":"reactive_attitudes_view","tags":["strawson","freedom and resentment","reactive attitudes","resentment","moral responsibility","blame","strawsonian compatibilism"]},{"term":"New dispositionalism","def":"Being able to do otherwise means having the right capacities, and determined people can have them.","more":"New dispositionalism is a recent compatibilist account of the ability to do otherwise, defended by Kadri Vihvelin, Michael Fara and Michael Smith. A disposition is a tendency that something has even when it isn’t showing it: glass is fragile even when not breaking, sugar soluble even when dry. New dispositionalists say that being able to choose differently is a bundle of such dispositions: you have the capacity to see reasons and act on them, and nothing is blocking that capacity. A determined person can have these capacities, just as a determined glass can be fragile. Held by Kadri Vihvelin, Michael Fara, Michael Smith.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Classical compatibilism","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"new_dispositionalism","tags":["dispositional compatibilism","vihvelin","fara","ability to do otherwise","capacities","compatibilism"]},{"term":"Local miracle compatibilism","def":"Under determinism you can still act otherwise; if you had, the laws would have been slightly different, but you wouldn’t have broken them.","more":"”. Suppose determinism is true and you raised your hand. Could you have kept it down? Lewis says yes. Held by David Lewis.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism","Classical compatibilism"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"position":"local_miracle_compatibilism","tags":["lewis","local miracle","ability to do otherwise","compatibilism","consequence argument"]},{"term":"Semicompatibilism","def":"Moral responsibility fits with determinism, even if the freedom to do otherwise does not.","more":"Semicompatibilism, a term from John Martin Fischer, splits two questions. Is determinism compatible with being able to do otherwise? Fischer grants that it may not be; the consequence argument might work. Is determinism compatible with moral responsibility? Held by John Martin Fischer, Mark Ravizza.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Source view","Reasons-responsiveness view","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["fischer"],"position":"semicompatibilism","tags":["fischer","moral responsibility","compatibilism","frankfurt cases","guidance control"]},{"term":"Event-causal libertarianism","def":"Free choices are caused by our reasons and efforts, but not in a way that fixes the outcome in advance.","more":"Event-causal libertarianism says that free choices are caused by events in the agent, such as their beliefs, desires and efforts, but undeterminedly: the causes make each option possible without fixing which one happens. Robert Kane’s version is the best known. In hard moral or practical choices, he argues, we are torn, and the brain may be in a state where chance plays a role. The agent makes efforts in both directions, and whichever wins, the choice is her own, made for her reasons and backed by her effort. Held by Robert Kane, Laura Ekstrom.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Libertarianism (about free will)","Self-forming actions","Indeterminism (physical)"],"thinkers":["kane"],"position":"event_causal_libertarianism","tags":["kane","event-causal","indeterminism","libertarian free will","free will"]},{"term":"Agent-causal libertarianism","def":"In free choices, the person, not a chain of events, is the cause.","more":"Agent-causal libertarianism says that free actions are caused by the agent herself, as a persisting being, rather than by events such as desires or brain states. Normal causation is between events: a spark causes a fire. Agent causation is a different kind: you cause your decision, and nothing causes you to cause it. Thomas Reid defended this in the eighteenth century, and Roderick Chisholm argued in 1964 that in free action each of us is a “prime mover unmoved”. Also called Agent causation. Held by Thomas Reid, Roderick Chisholm, Timothy O’Connor.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Libertarianism (about free will)"],"thinkers":["reid","chisholm"],"position":"agent_causal_libertarianism","tags":["agent causation","reid","chisholm","o'connor","libertarian free will","free will"]},{"term":"Non-causal libertarianism","def":"Free choices aren’t caused at all; they are basic acts of the agent, explained by reasons without being caused by them.","more":"Non-causal libertarianism holds that a free decision is not caused by anything, neither by prior events nor by the agent as a cause. Instead, a decision is a basic mental act that the agent simply performs. It is still not random, because it is done for reasons: you chose the train because it was cheaper. On this view, reasons explain actions without causing them, in the way purposes explain behaviour. Held by Carl Ginet, Hugh McCann.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Libertarianism (about free will)"],"thinkers":["ginet"],"position":"non_causal_libertarianism","tags":["ginet","volition","non-causal agency","libertarian free will","free will"]},{"term":"Source view","def":"What makes you responsible is that your action really came from you, not that you could have done something else.","more":"The source view says that responsibility depends on the actual history of an action: whether it came from the agent in the right way. Whether the agent had alternatives is not what matters. The main support is Frankfurt cases, introduced by Harry Frankfurt in 1969: a hidden device would have forced your choice, but never needed to act, so you couldn’t have done otherwise, yet you chose on your own and seem responsible. This shows the principle of alternative possibilities (that responsibility requires being able to do otherwise) is false. Also called Source incompatibilism (one version), Sourcehood view. Held by Harry Frankfurt, John Martin Fischer, Derk Pereboom, Daniel Dennett.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Semicompatibilism","Reasons-responsiveness view"],"thinkers":["frankfurt","fischer","pereboom","dennett"],"position":"source_view","tags":["sourcehood","source incompatibilism","ultimate source","moral responsibility","free will","source incompatibilism (one version)","sourcehood view"]},{"term":"Leeway view","def":"You are morally responsible for what you did only if you could have done otherwise.","more":"The leeway view says that free will and moral responsibility require alternatives: genuinely open options that the agent could have taken. Its central principle is the principle of alternative possibilities (PAP): a person is morally responsible for what they did only if they could have done otherwise. The idea is intuitive: we don’t blame someone who had no choice. Leeway theorists are often libertarians, since determinism seems to remove alternatives, as the consequence argument tries to show. Also called Principle of alternative possibilities, PAP. Held by Peter van Inwagen, Carl Ginet.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Libertarianism (about free will)","Dilemma defence","Flicker of freedom strategy","Incompatibilism"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen","ginet"],"position":"leeway_view","tags":["alternative possibilities","principle of alternate possibilities","pap","could have done otherwise","moral responsibility","principle of alternative possibilities"]},{"term":"Dilemma defence","def":"Frankfurt cases don’t work: either they quietly assume determinism, or the person could still have chosen otherwise.","more":"The dilemma defence is a reply to Frankfurt cases, developed by David Widerker, Robert Kane and Carl Ginet in the 1990s. In a Frankfurt case, a device stands ready to force your choice if you were about to choose differently. To know when to act, the device must detect some prior sign of what you will choose, such as a blush or a brain pattern. Now there is a dilemma. Held by David Widerker, Robert Kane, Carl Ginet.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Leeway view","Libertarianism (about free will)"],"thinkers":["widerker","kane","ginet"],"position":"dilemma_defence","tags":["frankfurt cases","widerker","kane","alternative possibilities","moral responsibility"]},{"term":"Flicker of freedom strategy","def":"Even in Frankfurt cases you had a small alternative, and that is enough for responsibility.","more":"The flicker of freedom strategy is a reply to Frankfurt cases. Even when a device stands ready to force your choice, you still have some alternative: you could have begun to choose differently, or shown the sign that triggers the device. In that case you would have been forced, and would not have been responsible. So there is a small “flicker” of freedom, and it may be where responsibility lives: you are responsible for choosing on your own, which was avoidable, even if the final outcome wasn’t. Held by Peter van Inwagen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Leeway view"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen"],"position":"flicker_of_freedom_strategy","tags":["frankfurt cases","alternative possibilities","flicker","moral responsibility"]},{"term":"Ownership view","def":"You are responsible for who you are if your values are truly yours now, however you came to have them.","more":"The ownership view answers worries about our origins by focusing on the present. You didn’t choose your genes or upbringing, but that doesn’t matter: what counts is whether the values and desires you now act on are really yours. Harry Frankfurt argued that a person owns a desire when they identify with it wholeheartedly, standing behind it without inner conflict. John Martin Fischer and Mark Ravizza add a historical twist: you own your way of reasoning when you have come to see yourself as an agent whose choices have effects and who is a fair target of praise and blame. Also called Identification view. Held by Harry Frankfurt, John Martin Fischer, Mark Ravizza.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Hierarchical compatibilism","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["frankfurt","fischer"],"position":"ownership_view","tags":["ownership","taking responsibility","fischer and ravizza","moral responsibility","character","identification view"]},{"term":"Self-forming actions","def":"We become authors of our own characters through hard choices whose outcome was genuinely open.","more":"Self-forming actions are at the centre of Robert Kane’s libertarian theory. Kane agrees with Galen Strawson that true responsibility requires ultimate responsibility: being in part the source of the character you act from. He denies that this is impossible. At some moments in life, we face real conflict, such as between ambition and conscience, and our brain may be in an undetermined state. Also called Ultimate responsibility through self-formation. Held by Robert Kane.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Event-causal libertarianism","Libertarianism (about free will)"],"thinkers":["kane"],"position":"self_forming_actions","tags":["sfas","kane","ultimate responsibility","character formation","libertarian free will","ultimate responsibility through self-formation"]},{"term":"Impossibility of ultimate responsibility","def":"No one can make themselves, so no one can be ultimately responsible for who they are or what they do.","more":"Galen Strawson’s Basic Argument aims to show that ultimate moral responsibility is impossible, whether determinism is true or not. You do what you do because of how you are (your character, values and mental state). To be truly responsible for what you do, you would have to be truly responsible for how you are. But to have made yourself a certain way, you must already have been some way, with values that guided that making, and you would need to be responsible for those too, and so on forever. Also called The Basic Argument. Held by Galen Strawson, Friedrich Nietzsche.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Hard incompatibilism","Hard determinism"],"thinkers":["gstrawson","nietzsche"],"position":"impossibility_of_ultimate_responsibility","tags":["basic argument","galen strawson","ultimate responsibility","causa sui","free will skepticism","the basic argument"]},{"term":"Forward-looking responsibility","def":"Praise and blame are justified by their good effects on future behaviour, not by what someone ultimately deserves.","more":"Forward-looking responsibility says that holding people responsible is justified by its results. Praise, blame and punishment are tools for shaping behaviour: they teach, deter, and encourage people to grow. ” Moritz Schlick argued in 1930 that the responsible person is the one on whom blame or reward will have an effect; J. J. Also called Consequentialist account of responsibility. Held by Moritz Schlick, J. J. C. Smart, Manuel Vargas.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Revisionism about free will","Forward-looking blame"],"thinkers":["schlick","smart"],"position":"forward_looking_responsibility","tags":["forward-looking","consequentialism","instrumentalism about blame","moral responsibility","praise and blame","consequentialist account of responsibility"]},{"term":"Historical compatibilism","def":"How you came to have your values matters: manipulation undermines responsibility, but ordinary causes don’t.","more":"Historical compatibilism says that whether an agent is free or responsible depends partly on their history: on how they came to have the desires and values they act on. Two people identical in their present psychology might differ in responsibility if one was manipulated into that state and the other got there by normal development. Alfred Mele defended this in Autonomous Agents (1995), and Fischer and Ravizza’s requirement that the agent take ownership of their reasoning is also historical. This lets compatibilists answer manipulation arguments with a “soft line”: in the cases where scientists program Professor Plum, his values bypassed his own capacity to evaluate and revise them, so he isn’t responsible. Also called Soft-line reply to manipulation. Held by Alfred Mele, John Martin Fischer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism","Ownership view"],"thinkers":["fischer"],"position":"historical_compatibilism","tags":["manipulation argument","mele","history-sensitive compatibilism","compatibilism","moral responsibility","soft-line reply to manipulation"]},{"term":"Hard-line reply to manipulation","def":"If a manipulated person reasons, cares and chooses like anyone else, they are responsible, however they came to be that way.","more":"The hard-line reply answers manipulation arguments by accepting that even the manipulated agent is responsible. In Pereboom’s case 1, scientists program Professor Plum’s reasoning moment by moment, yet he still weighs reasons, cares about what he does, and acts from his own settled values. Michael McKenna argued that once we really imagine Plum as a full, normal agent, our sense that he isn’t responsible weakens. Our first reaction is driven by the creepy picture of scientists at the controls, not by anything missing in Plum himself. Held by Michael McKenna.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"hard_line_reply","tags":["manipulation argument","mckenna","compatibilism","moral responsibility","four-case argument"]},{"term":"Acceptance of moral luck","def":"How things turn out, even when beyond our control, can affect what we are responsible and blameworthy for.","more":"Moral luck happens when what a person is blamed or praised for depends on factors outside their control. Thomas Nagel and Bernard Williams named and analysed the problem in 1976. Nagel listed kinds of luck: in how things turn out (the drunk driver who hits a child), in circumstances (the person who never faced a test of courage), and in the character you happened to develop. Accepting moral luck means holding that such luck really does affect what people answer for. Held by Bernard Williams, Margaret Urban Walker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Reactive attitudes view"],"thinkers":["williams"],"position":"acceptance_of_moral_luck","tags":["moral luck","williams","nagel","resultant luck","gauguin","responsibility"]},{"term":"Denial of moral luck","def":"We are blameworthy only for what was in our control, so luck in outcomes doesn’t change how much we deserve blame.","more":"Deniers of moral luck hold to the control principle: people are morally assessable only for what is within their control. The two drunk drivers made the same choice with the same risk, so they are equally blameworthy; the killer’s worse outcome is a tragedy, but not extra guilt. The idea has roots in Immanuel Kant, who held that a good will is good in itself, whatever it manages to achieve. Michael J. Also called Control principle. Held by Immanuel Kant, Michael J. Zimmerman, Norvin Richards.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Libertarianism (about free will)"],"thinkers":["kant"],"position":"denial_of_moral_luck","tags":["moral luck","control principle","kant","blame","responsibility"]},{"term":"Hard luck view","def":"Luck reaches so deep into who we are and what we choose that no one is truly morally responsible.","more":"The hard luck view, defended by Neil Levy in his book Hard Luck (2011), turns moral luck into an argument for skepticism about responsibility. Levy distinguishes present luck (chance in the moment of choice, such as which considerations happen to occur to you) and constitutive luck (the luck of having the character, abilities and values you have). Libertarian accounts face present luck, since undetermined choices seem chancy. Compatibilist accounts face constitutive luck, since our characters come from factors we didn’t control. Held by Neil Levy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Hard incompatibilism","Acceptance of moral luck"],"thinkers":["levy"],"position":"hard_luck_view","tags":["moral luck","levy","constitutive luck","free will skepticism","responsibility"]},{"term":"Quarantine model","def":"Without free will, we may restrain dangerous people only as we quarantine carriers of disease: to protect others, with as little harm as possible.","more":"The quarantine model is a proposal by Derk Pereboom, developed further by Gregg Caruso as the “public health-quarantine model”. If no one deserves punishment, can society still lock up dangerous people? Yes, the model says, in the same way it can quarantine someone with a deadly infectious disease. The carrier hasn’t done anything blameworthy, yet we may restrict their freedom to protect others. Also called Public health-quarantine model. Held by Derk Pereboom, Gregg Caruso.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Hard incompatibilism","Forward-looking blame"],"thinkers":["pereboom","caruso"],"position":"quarantine_model","tags":["pereboom","public health quarantine","free will skepticism","punishment","incapacitation","public health-quarantine model"]},{"term":"Forward-looking blame","def":"Even without free will, we can hold wrongdoers to account by asking them to explain, repair and reform.","more":"Forward-looking blame is the part of free will skepticism that answers the worry that, without desert, we would have to stop holding anyone to account. Derk Pereboom argues that blame can rest on three forward-looking goals: protecting potential victims, restoring damaged relationships, and helping the wrongdoer become a better person. ” and expect an answer; we can still express moral protest at what they did. What goes is the idea that the wrongdoer deserves to suffer just because of what they did. Also called Answerability without desert. Held by Derk Pereboom.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Quarantine model","Hard incompatibilism","Forward-looking responsibility"],"thinkers":["pereboom"],"position":"forward_looking_blame","tags":["forward-looking","pereboom","free will skepticism","blame","moral responsibility","answerability without desert"]},{"term":"Responsibility abolitionism","def":"We should drop the whole idea of moral responsibility and treat wrongdoing as a problem to understand and fix.","more":"Responsibility abolitionism holds that moral responsibility does not exist and that we would be better off abandoning the concept completely, not just its harshest parts. Bruce Waller argued in Against Moral Responsibility (2011) that the system of moral responsibility makes us focus on blaming individuals rather than understanding the causes of their behaviour. A skilled engineer who finds a bridge failing asks why, not whom to blame; Waller suggests we should approach wrongdoing in the same way. Abolitionism goes further than Pereboom’s view, which keeps forward-looking blame. Also called Moral responsibility skepticism (abolitionist). Held by Bruce Waller.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Hard incompatibilism","Quarantine model"],"thinkers":["waller"],"position":"responsibility_abolitionism","tags":["abolitionism","free will skepticism","moral responsibility skepticism","eliminativism","blame","moral responsibility skepticism (abolitionist)"]},{"term":"Optimistic free will skepticism","def":"We don’t have the free will needed for deserved blame, and recognising this would make life kinder, not worse.","more":"Optimistic free will skepticism holds that no one has free will in the sense needed for basic desert, and that accepting this would be good for us. Derk Pereboom argues that what really matters survives: we can still love, keep promises, seek meaning, and call acts right or wrong. What goes is the belief that wrongdoers deserve to suffer, along with resentment, harsh self-blame and much of our punitive culture. Pereboom suggests replacing anger with feelings like sorrow, disappointment and moral concern, and Spinoza had argued centuries earlier that understanding the necessity of things brings peace of mind. Also called Optimistic skepticism, Disillusionism. Held by Derk Pereboom, Gregg Caruso, Baruch Spinoza.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Hard incompatibilism","Quarantine model","Forward-looking blame"],"thinkers":["pereboom","caruso","spinoza"],"position":"optimistic_free_will_skepticism","tags":["pereboom","free will skepticism","optimism","living without free will","hard incompatibilism","optimistic skepticism","disillusionism"]},{"term":"Revisionism about free will","def":"Our everyday idea of free will is partly mistaken, so we should revise it while keeping the practices of responsibility that work.","more":"Revisionism, defended by Manuel Vargas, takes a middle road. Vargas argues that our ordinary idea of free will includes libertarian elements: many people think free choices must be undetermined. On that point he thinks ordinary thinking is probably wrong. But rather than throw out the concept, as skeptics do, he proposes revising it, the way we kept the word “atom” after learning atoms can be split. Held by Manuel Vargas.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism","Forward-looking responsibility","Agency cultivation model"],"thinkers":[],"position":"free_will_revisionism","tags":["revisionism","vargas","free will","moral responsibility","conceptual revision"]},{"term":"Free will skepticism","def":"No one has the kind of free will that would make people truly deserve praise or blame.","more":"Free will skepticism is the family of views that deny we have free will in the sense that matters most: the control needed for basic desert, which means deserving praise or blame just because of what we did, not because blaming does any good. Skeptics reach this conclusion by different routes. Hard determinists say everything we do is fixed by earlier causes. Hard incompatibilists, such as Derk Pereboom, add that chance would not help either. Also called Skepticism about free will. Held by Derk Pereboom, Gregg Caruso, Neil Levy, Galen Strawson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Hard incompatibilism","Hard determinism","Optimistic free will skepticism","Illusionism (about free will)"],"thinkers":["pereboom","caruso","levy","gstrawson","holbach"],"position":"free_will_skepticism","tags":["hard determinism","hard incompatibilism","pereboom","illusion of free will","no free will","determinism","skepticism about free will"]},{"term":"Fundamental dualism","def":"Compatibilists and hard determinists are each partly right, and morality needs both points of view.","more":"Fundamental dualism is Saul Smilansky’s view, defended in Free Will and Illusion (2000). He agrees with skeptics that libertarian free will does not exist, so no one is ultimately the source of who they are. From that ultimate point of view, punishing even the guilty involves a deep injustice, since they are in the end victims of their circumstances. But he also agrees with compatibilists that the difference between acting freely and acting under compulsion is real and matters: it is the basis of respect, of fair treatment and of a sense of desert. Also called Smilansky’s dualism. Held by Saul Smilansky.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Illusionism (about free will)","Compatibilism","Free will skepticism"],"thinkers":["smilansky"],"position":"fundamental_dualism","tags":["smilansky","illusionism","compatibilism","hard determinism","free will","smilansky’s dualism"]},{"term":"Compatibilist libertarianism","def":"Even if physics is deterministic, a person’s choices can be genuinely open at the level of psychology, so we can do otherwise.","more":"Compatibilist libertarianism is Christian List’s view, set out in Why Free Will Is Real (2019). List agrees with libertarians that free will requires real alternative possibilities, not just doing what you want. But he denies that this requires the laws of physics to be indeterministic. A person is described at the level of psychology, as an agent with beliefs, desires and options, and many different physical states could realise the very same psychological state. Also called Level-relative libertarianism. Held by Christian List.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Libertarianism (about free will)","Compatibilism","New dispositionalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"compatibilist_libertarianism","tags":["libertarianism","compatibilism","list","higher-level indeterminism","free will","level-relative libertarianism"]},{"term":"Agnostic autonomism","def":"We do have free will, but it remains an open question whether free will requires indeterminism.","more":"Agnostic autonomism is Alfred Mele’s position, developed in Autonomous Agents (1995) and Free Will and Luck (2006). Mele believes that there are free and responsible agents, but he does not claim to know whether compatibilism or incompatibilism is true. So he builds the best account he can of each: a compatibilist account of autonomous agency, and a “modest libertarian” account on which chance enters early in deliberation, in which considerations come to mind, rather than at the moment of choice. Whichever turns out to be correct, he argues, some people meet its conditions. Held by Alfred Mele.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism","Event-causal libertarianism","Agnosticism about free will"],"thinkers":[],"position":"agnostic_autonomism","tags":["mele","autonomism","agnosticism","free will"]},{"term":"Basic desert","def":"Wrongdoers can deserve blame simply because of what they knowingly and freely did.","more":"Basic desert is the idea that a person can deserve blame or praise just because of what they did, given that they understood what they were doing, and not because blaming them will do any good or because we have agreed on a practice. The phrase was made central by Derk Pereboom, who denies that anyone has it, but most libertarians and many compatibilists, such as John Martin Fischer and Michael McKenna, hold that people sometimes deserve blame in this sense. The dispute between them is over what kind of freedom basic desert needs. Basic desert of blame is not the same as retributivism about punishment: someone may think a wrongdoer deserves resentment and reproach without thinking the state may make them suffer. Also called Desert-based responsibility. Held by John Martin Fischer, Michael McKenna, Robert Kane, Michael S. Moore.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Retributivism","Libertarianism (about free will)","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["fischer","kane","michael_moore"],"position":"basic_desert","tags":["desert","retributivism","blameworthiness","moral responsibility","desert-based responsibility"]},{"term":"Pluralism about responsibility","def":"“Responsible” covers several different relations, each with its own conditions.","more":"Pluralism about responsibility holds that there is no single thing called moral responsibility. Gary Watson, in “Two Faces of Responsibility” (1996), distinguished attributability, when an act really expresses who you are and what you value, from accountability, when others may hold you to a demand and blame or sanction you. David Shoemaker, in Responsibility from the Margins (2015), argued for a third face, answerability: being open to being asked for your reasons. The conditions differ. Also called Attributability, answerability and accountability. Held by Gary Watson, David Shoemaker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Reactive attitudes view","Forward-looking blame"],"thinkers":["watson"],"position":"responsibility_pluralism","tags":["attributability","answerability","accountability","moral responsibility","shoemaker","attributability, answerability and accountability"]},{"term":"Consequentialist theory of responsibility","def":"Praise and blame are justified when, and because, they influence behaviour for the better.","more":"The consequentialist theory of responsibility treats praise and blame as tools. Moritz Schlick argued in Problems of Ethics (1930) that asking who is responsible for an act is asking where reward or punishment should be applied to change future behaviour. J. J. Also called Social-influence view of blame. Held by Moritz Schlick, J. J. C. Smart, P. H. Nowell-Smith.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Forward-looking responsibility","Classical compatibilism"],"thinkers":["schlick","smart"],"position":"consequentialist_theory_of_responsibility","tags":["consequentialism","schlick","smart","moral responsibility","praise and blame","social-influence view of blame"]},{"term":"Agency cultivation model","def":"Our practice of praise and blame is justified because it builds people who notice and act on moral reasons.","more":"The agency cultivation model is Manuel Vargas’s account of why responsibility practices are justified, set out in Building Better Beings (2013). Vargas is a revisionist: he thinks our ordinary idea of free will contains libertarian elements that are probably false, but that the practice of praise and blame should be kept. It is justified, he argues, not by any deep fact of desert and not by the effects of each separate act of blame, but because the practice as a whole fosters a valuable kind of agency: being able to recognise moral considerations and to govern yourself by them. Within the practice, ordinary norms decide who is blameworthy and how much. Held by Manuel Vargas.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Revisionism about free will","Forward-looking responsibility"],"thinkers":[],"position":"agency_cultivation_model","tags":["vargas","moral responsibility","agency","revisionism","praise and blame"]},{"term":"The Reason View","def":"You are free and responsible when you are able to see what is right and good and to act on it.","more":"The Reason View is Susan Wolf’s account, set out in Freedom Within Reason (1990). Freedom, she argues, is the ability to act in accordance with the True and the Good: to recognise good reasons and be moved by them. This makes freedom asymmetric. Someone who does the right thing for the right reasons is praiseworthy even if she could not have done otherwise, because her goodness is exactly what freedom is for. Also called Wolf’s Reason View. Held by Susan Wolf, Dana Kay Nelkin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Compatibilism","Reasons-responsiveness view"],"thinkers":["wolf"],"position":"reason_view","tags":["susan wolf","freedom within reason","reason","the true and the good","moral responsibility","wolf’s reason view"]},{"term":"Scientific free will skepticism","def":"Brain science shows that our conscious choices do not cause our actions in the way free will seems to need.","more":"Scientific free will skepticism rests the case against free will on psychology and neuroscience rather than on abstract arguments. In 1983 Benjamin Libet found that brain activity building up to a simple movement began before people reported deciding to move. The psychologist Daniel Wegner, in The Illusion of Conscious Will (2002), argued more broadly that the feeling of willing an act is the mind’s after-the-fact interpretation, not a perception of the act’s real cause. Sam Harris popularised the view in Free Will (2012): thoughts and intentions simply appear in consciousness, produced by causes we never see. Also called Neuroscientific skepticism about free will. Held by Daniel Wegner, Sam Harris.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Free will skepticism","Hard determinism"],"thinkers":["wegner","harris"],"position":"scientific_free_will_skepticism","tags":["libet","neuroscience","wegner","illusion of conscious will","free will skepticism","neuroscientific skepticism about free will"]},{"term":"Free will as an open scientific question","def":"Whether we have libertarian free will depends on facts about the brain that no one yet knows.","more":"This form of agnosticism, defended by Mark Balaguer in Free Will as an Open Scientific Problem (2010), says the question of libertarian free will comes down to brain science. Balaguer focuses on “torn decisions”, choices where we have reasons on both sides and feel we could go either way. If some of these decisions are undetermined at the moment of choice, he argues, then we have libertarian free will, and the familiar objection that undetermined choices would be mere luck fails. Whether they are undetermined is an empirical question that neither physics nor neuroscience has answered. Also called Balaguer’s agnosticism. Held by Mark Balaguer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Agnosticism about free will","Event-causal libertarianism","Agnosticism about determinism"],"thinkers":["balaguer"],"position":"empirical_free_will_agnosticism","tags":["free will","science","neuroscience","quantum indeterminacy","libertarian free will","balaguer’s agnosticism"]},{"term":"Mysterianism about free will","def":"Free will may be real, but how it could work is a mystery that human minds may never solve.","more":"Mysterianism about free will holds that the problem may be beyond us. Colin McGinn, in Problems in Philosophy (1993), argued that some philosophical problems persist because human minds are not built to solve them, much as a dog cannot grasp calculus. Free will, he suggested, is one: we can conceive of choices only as either determined or random, and neither captures freedom, yet the true nature of choice may lie outside both concepts. Peter van Inwagen reached a related verdict in “Free Will Remains a Mystery” (2000). Held by Colin McGinn, Peter van Inwagen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Free will","related":["Agnosticism about free will","Libertarianism (about free will)"],"thinkers":["mcginn","vaninwagen"],"position":"free_will_mysterianism","tags":["mysterianism","van inwagen","mystery","free will"]},{"term":"Reductionism about personal identity","def":"Being the same person is nothing over and above facts about bodies, brains and mental links, and sometimes there is no answer.","more":"Reductionism, defended most famously by Derek Parfit, says that personal identity “reduces” to more basic facts: facts about a brain and body, and about memories, intentions and character linked to one another over time. There is no extra, all-or-nothing fact about being the same person, such as a soul being present. ”. That question becomes empty, like asking whether a club that dissolved and re-formed is “really” the same club. Also called Parfit’s view, Complex view. Held by Derek Parfit, Mark Siderits.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Psychological continuity","Identity is not what matters","Buddhist no-self","Bundle theory"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"position":"reductionism_about_personal_identity","tags":["parfit","reductionism","psychological continuity","personal identity","reasons and persons","parfit’s view","complex view"]},{"term":"Soul view","def":"You are, or essentially have, a non-physical soul, and you are the same person exactly when you have the same soul.","more":"The soul view says that your identity over time is fixed by a soul: a non-physical thing that does your thinking and feeling and is not made of parts. Your body changes, your memories fade, but as long as the same soul continues, you continue. Most versions are forms of substance dualism (the view that minds and bodies are two different kinds of thing), as in Plato, René Descartes and, today, Richard Swinburne. Thomists instead treat the soul as the form of a living human body, and many Hindu traditions speak of the ātman, a self of pure awareness beneath memory and character. Also called Cartesian view of the self. Held by Plato, René Descartes, Richard Swinburne, Joseph Butler.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Substantial self","Simple view of personal identity"],"thinkers":["plato","descartes","swinburne"],"position":"soul_view","tags":["soul","immaterial self","substance dualism","personal identity","afterlife","cartesian view of the self"]},{"term":"Brain view","def":"You are, or go wherever, your brain goes, since the brain is the organ of your mind.","more":"The brain view says that what keeps you the same over time is the continued existence and functioning of your brain, especially the parts that make consciousness possible. If your brain were moved into another body, you would go with it; if your brain were destroyed and perfectly copied, the copy would not be you. This sits between two rivals. Like the psychological view, it puts the mind at the centre and says you go with your brain in a transplant. Also called Embodied mind account. Held by Jeff McMahan, Thomas Nagel.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Animalism","Psychological continuity","Narrow psychological criterion"],"thinkers":["mcmahan","nagel"],"position":"brain_view","tags":["brain","transplant","personal identity","animalism","neuroscience","embodied mind account"]},{"term":"Narrative self view","def":"You are constituted by the life story through which you understand your past, present and future.","more":"The narrative view says that a person is not just a body or a chain of memories but someone who lives a life that hangs together as a story. On Marya Schechtman’s “narrative self-constitution” view, what makes an experience or action yours is that it fits into the self-understanding you use to make sense of your life. ”. Unlike the psychological view, which counts any mental link, the narrative view asks how the pieces are organised and interpreted. Also called Narrative self-constitution view. Held by Marya Schechtman, Alasdair MacIntyre, Paul Ricoeur.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Psychological continuity","Self as a centre of narrative gravity","Person life view"],"thinkers":["schechtman","macintyre"],"position":"narrative_self_view","tags":["narrative identity","life story","schechtman","macintyre","ricoeur","self","narrative self-constitution view"]},{"term":"Substantial self","def":"There really is a lasting “I”: a single subject that has all your experiences and is not just a collection of them.","more":"The substantial self view says that there is a real self: one subject that has your thoughts, feelings and perceptions, and that stays the same while they come and go. A “substance” here means a thing that exists in its own right and has properties, rather than being a property or a collection. Many substantial-self theorists, like René Descartes, think this self is a non-physical soul; others, like E. J. Also called Simple self, Ego theory. Held by René Descartes, Thomas Reid, Richard Swinburne, E. J. Lowe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Soul view","Simple view of personal identity"],"thinkers":["descartes","reid","swinburne"],"position":"substantial_self","tags":["cartesian self","ego","subject","soul","personal identity","simple self","ego theory"]},{"term":"Buddhist no-self","def":"There is no lasting self; “I” is a convenient name for a changing stream of body and mind, and clinging to a self causes suffering.","more":"The Buddhist doctrine of no-self (anattā in Pali, anātman in Sanskrit) says that when you examine what a person is made of, you find only five changing groups of events, called aggregates: body, feeling, perception, mental formations (such as habits and intentions) and consciousness. None of these is a permanent self, and there is nothing else beyond them. “I” and names like “Nāgasena” are conventional labels, useful for everyday life, like the word “chariot” for an arrangement of parts. Many Buddhist schools say persons are conventionally real but not ultimately real. Also called Anattā, Anātman, Buddhist reductionism. Held by The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama), Nāgasena, Vasubandhu, Nagarjuna.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Bundle theory","Reductionism about personal identity","Self-model theory"],"thinkers":["buddha","vasubandhu","nagarjuna"],"position":"buddhist_no_self","tags":["anatta","anatman","no-self","buddhism","five aggregates","skandhas","non-self","anattā","anātman","buddhist reductionism"]},{"term":"Minimal self","def":"There is a real but thin self: the built-in sense that your experiences are given to you, from your own point of view.","more":"The minimal self view, developed by phenomenologists (philosophers who study experience as it is lived) such as Dan Zahavi and Shaun Gallagher, says that every conscious experience has a “for-me-ness”: it feels like it is happening to you, from the inside, even before you think about yourself. When you see a tree, you don’t need to infer that you are the one seeing it. This first-person character is the minimal self. It is thinner than a soul or a substance, since it is not a hidden thing behind experience but a feature of experience itself. Also called Experiential self. Held by Dan Zahavi, Shaun Gallagher.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Substantial self","Narrative self view"],"thinkers":[],"position":"minimal_self","tags":["pre-reflective self-awareness","zahavi","phenomenology","mineness","self","experiential self"]},{"term":"Self-model theory","def":"The self is a picture the brain makes of the organism, and we cannot see that it is only a picture.","more":"Self-model theory, developed by Thomas Metzinger, says that nobody has ever been or had a self in the sense of a lasting inner thing. What exists is a self-model: a representation (an inner map) the brain builds of the whole organism, its body, feelings and thoughts. Because this model is “transparent,” we do not experience it as a model; we look straight through it, the way you see the world through a clean window without noticing the glass. So it seems that there is a self in here, when really there is a brain running a very good simulation of one. Also called Self-model illusion. Held by Thomas Metzinger.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Bundle theory","Buddhist no-self","Self as a centre of narrative gravity"],"thinkers":["metzinger"],"position":"self_model_theory","tags":["metzinger","self-model","being no one","phenomenal self-model","self as illusion","self-model illusion"]},{"term":"Simple view of personal identity","def":"Being the same person is a basic, all-or-nothing fact that cannot be reduced to facts about bodies, brains or memories.","more":"The simple view (also called non-reductionism) says that personal identity is a further fact: something over and above physical and psychological continuity. Memories and bodily continuity are evidence of who someone is, but they are not what identity consists in. ”, even if we cannot find it out. Joseph Butler and Thomas Reid held versions of it, often tied to a belief in a soul; Roderick Chisholm and Richard Swinburne defend it in the 20th century; Trenton Merricks has argued for it without relying on souls. Also called Non-reductionism, Further fact view. Held by Joseph Butler, Thomas Reid, Roderick Chisholm, Richard Swinburne.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Soul view","Substantial self","Identity matters"],"thinkers":["reid","chisholm","swinburne"],"position":"simple_view_of_personal_identity","tags":["simple view","non-reductionism","soul","chisholm","swinburne","personal identity","further fact view"]},{"term":"Non-branching view","def":"You survive only if exactly one future person is continuous with you; if you split into two, neither is you.","more":"The non-branching view is a version of the psychological continuity view designed to handle fission (splitting). Psychological continuity is normally one-to-one: one past you, one future you. But imagine a teleporter that makes two perfect copies, or a surgeon who puts each half of your brain into a new body. Both resulting people are psychologically continuous with you. Also called No-branching psychological continuity, Closest continuer theory (in tie cases). Held by Sydney Shoemaker, Robert Nozick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Psychological continuity","Identity is not what matters"],"thinkers":["shoemaker","nozick"],"position":"non_branching_view","tags":["non-branching","fission","duplication","psychological continuity","personal identity","no-branching psychological continuity","closest continuer theory (in tie cases)"]},{"term":"Multiple occupancy view","def":"In a split, there were two people all along, sharing one body and one past until they went separate ways.","more":"The multiple occupancy view, defended by David Lewis, offers a clever answer to fission. Lewis thought of a person as stretched out through time, made up of “temporal parts” (person-stages at each moment), much as a road is made of stretches. In an ordinary life, one person is made of all those stages. But if you split into Lefty and Righty next year, then two overlapping people exist: one made of your stages plus Lefty’s, another made of your stages plus Righty’s. Also called Lewis’s view of fission. Held by David Lewis.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Psychological continuity","Identity matters"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"position":"multiple_occupancy_view","tags":["multiple occupancy","fission","lewis","personal identity","split brain","lewis’s view of fission"]},{"term":"Identity is not what matters","def":"What really matters in survival is that your memories, character and plans carry on, whether or not the future person is strictly you.","more":"This is Derek Parfit’s most famous claim. Consider fission: your brain halves go into two bodies and two people wake up, each with your memories and plans. By the non-branching view, neither is you, so you do not survive. But is this as bad as ordinary death? Also called Parfit’s view of what matters, Relation R view. Held by Derek Parfit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Reductionism about personal identity","Non-branching view","Discounting by connectedness","The Extreme Claim"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"position":"identity_is_not_what_matters","tags":["parfit","what matters","relation r","psychological continuity","survival","fission","parfit’s view of what matters","relation r view"]},{"term":"Identity matters","def":"What you care about in survival is that you yourself continue; a perfect continuer who is not you is not good enough.","more":"This view keeps the common-sense idea that what matters in survival is identity: that the very same person, you, goes on. It can be held in different ways. Soul theorists and simple view theorists think identity is an all-or-nothing further fact, and naturally the most important one. David Lewis, who agreed that psychological continuity is the core, argued that identity and continuity never really come apart, since in fission there are two persons who each survive. Also called Identity-based concern. Held by David Lewis, Richard Swinburne, Mark Johnston.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Simple view of personal identity","Multiple occupancy view","Soul view"],"thinkers":["lewis","swinburne"],"position":"identity_matters_view","tags":["personal identity","survival","what matters","self-interest","parfit","identity-based concern"]},{"term":"Eliminativism about persons","def":"Strictly speaking, there are no persons or selves at all, since nothing could have the sharp boundaries such things would need.","more":"Eliminativism about persons says that persons, including you, do not really exist. ” His reasoning uses the sorites paradox (the puzzle of the heap): removing one grain of sand never turns a heap into a non-heap, yet removing all grains leaves nothing. In the same way, removing a single cell from you could not make you stop existing, but if that is so, step by step you could be reduced to nothing while still existing, which is absurd. Unger concluded that our concept of a person is incoherent, so nothing falls under it. Also called Nihilism about persons. Held by Peter Unger.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Buddhist no-self","Reductionism about personal identity"],"thinkers":["unger"],"position":"eliminativism_about_persons","tags":["eliminativism","nihilism about persons","no persons","personal identity","no-self"]},{"term":"Organicism","def":"Chariots and tables do not really exist as single things, but living organisms do, and you are one of them.","more":"Organicism is Peter van Inwagen’s answer to the question of when many small things make up one bigger thing. He argued that particles make up a single object only when their activity constitutes a life. ” But living organisms, including you, really exist, because the particles in you are caught up in the self-maintaining process of a single life. Applied to Nāgasena’s chariot, organicism agrees with the Buddhist that the chariot is not a real extra thing, but it rejects the step to persons: you are not like a chariot, because you are alive. Also called Van Inwagen’s view. Held by Peter van Inwagen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Animalism","Hylomorphism","Eliminativism about ordinary objects"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen"],"position":"organicism","tags":["van inwagen","mereological nihilism","composition","special composition question","material objects","van inwagen’s view"]},{"term":"Discounting by connectedness","def":"It can be rational to care less about a far-future self who will share little with you, because less of what matters connects you.","more":"This view grows out of Derek Parfit’s reductionism. If personal identity is not a deep further fact, and what matters is psychological connectedness (shared memories, intentions, values), then how much you should care about a future self may depend on how connected you will be. Next year’s you shares almost everything with you. You at 85 may share far less. Also called Parfit’s moderate claim about prudence. Held by Derek Parfit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Identity is not what matters","Reductionism about personal identity"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"position":"connectedness_discounting","tags":["parfit","future self","temporal discounting","prudence","psychological connectedness","parfit’s moderate claim about prudence"]},{"term":"Self as a centre of narrative gravity","def":"The self is a useful fiction: the main character in the story your brain tells about you.","more":"Daniel Dennett argued that the self is like a centre of gravity in physics. A centre of gravity is not a real lump of matter; it is an abstract point that makes predicting an object’s behaviour easy, yet it is objective and useful. In the same way, your brain constantly produces stories about what “I” did and want. ” It is not a thing in the brain, but it is not nothing either. Also called Centre of narrative gravity, Dennett’s view of the self. Held by Daniel Dennett.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Narrative self view","Self-model theory","Bundle theory"],"thinkers":["dennett"],"position":"narrative_fiction_view","tags":["dennett","narrative gravity","useful fiction","narrative self","self as illusion","centre of narrative gravity","dennett’s view of the self"]},{"term":"Anti-narrativism","def":"You need not see your life as a story, and many people who do not are living perfectly well.","more":"Anti-narrativism is Galen Strawson’s attack on the popular idea that people do and should experience their lives as a story. He distinguishes “Diachronic” people, who naturally feel the self that existed long ago is the same self as now, from “Episodic” people, who do not feel that their present self was there in the distant past. Strawson says he is Episodic himself: he knows his past happened to the same human being, but it does not feel like it happened to him. Episodic people can have rich moral lives, keep promises and form close friendships. Also called Episodic view. Held by Galen Strawson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Minimal self"],"thinkers":["gstrawson"],"position":"anti_narrativism","tags":["galen strawson","against narrativity","episodic self","diachronic self","narrative self","episodic view"]},{"term":"Constitution view","def":"The statue and the clay are two different things in the same place: the clay constitutes the statue without being it.","more":"The constitution view says that the statue and the lump of clay are two distinct objects that share the same place and matter. They must be different because they differ in their properties: the lump existed before the sculptor shaped it and would survive being squashed, but the statue would not. By Leibniz’s law (if two things are identical, they have all the same properties), different properties mean different things. The clay constitutes the statue: it makes it up, without being identical to it. Also called Constitution is not identity, Pluralism about coinciding objects. Held by David Wiggins, Lynne Rudder Baker, Kit Fine.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Restricted composition (common-sense)","Hylomorphism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"constitution_view","tags":["constitution","baker","statue and clay","coincidence","material constitution","constitution is not identity","pluralism about coinciding objects"]},{"term":"Endurantism","def":"Things are wholly present at each moment they exist, and last by being the very same whole thing at different times.","more":"Endurantism says that an object such as you persists through time by being wholly present at every moment of its existence. The you that woke up this morning is entirely the same thing as the you reading now, not just one part of a longer thing. On this view objects do not have temporal parts (parts spread out in time, like slices of a loaf); they have only spatial parts, such as hands and feet. This is close to how most people naturally think about things. Also called Three-dimensionalism. Held by Peter van Inwagen, Roderick Chisholm, David Wiggins, E. J. Lowe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Presentism","A-theory of time"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen","chisholm"],"position":"endurantism","tags":["enduring","three-dimensionalism","persistence","wholly present","identity over time"]},{"term":"Perdurantism","def":"Things last by having different temporal parts at different times, like a worm stretched through time.","more":"Perdurantism, also called four-dimensionalism or the worm view, says that objects are extended in time as well as in space. Just as your body has spatial parts (left hand, right hand), your life has temporal parts: you-today, you-last-year, you-as-a-baby. You are the whole ‘space-time worm’ made of all of them, and you persist by having a different part at each time, as a road stretches across a country by having a different stretch in each town. Change is just having temporal parts that differ, like a road that is bumpy in one town and smooth in the next. Also called Four-dimensionalism, Worm theory. Held by David Lewis, Theodore Sider, W. V. O. Quine, Mark Heller.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Eternalism","B-theory of time","Mereological universalism"],"thinkers":["lewis","sider","quine"],"position":"perdurantism","tags":["temporal parts","four-dimensionalism","persistence","lewis","space-time worms","identity over time","worm theory"]},{"term":"Stage theory","def":"You are a momentary stage; you persist by having earlier and later stages that count as you.","more":"Stage theory agrees with perdurantism that the world contains momentary slices of objects, but it says that ordinary things like you are the slices themselves, not the long worm. At each moment, ‘you’ refers to the stage that exists then. When we say ‘you were once a baby’, this is true because an earlier stage stands in the right relations of continuity to you: it is your temporal counterpart. So persisting is a matter of being linked to other stages, not of being one stretched-out thing. Also called Exdurantism. Held by Theodore Sider, Katherine Hawley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Perdurantism","Eternalism"],"thinkers":["sider"],"position":"stage_theory","tags":["stage view","sider","hawley","temporal counterparts","exdurantism","persistence"]},{"term":"No-self view","def":"Strictly, no single self lasts through time: there is a stream of bodily and mental events, and “I” is a label for it.","more":"The no-self view denies that there is any lasting self: no single thing that stays the same through your life and has all your experiences. What there is, on this view, is a stream of physical and mental events, causally linked, each depending on the ones before. We call the stream “I” for convenience, as we call a collection of parts a “chariot”. The view has two main sources. Also called Anattā, No-self. Held by David Hume, Thomas Metzinger, Mark Siderits.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Buddhist no-self","Bundle theory","Self-model theory","Eliminativism about persons"],"thinkers":["hume","metzinger"],"position":"no_self_view","tags":["no-self","bundle theory","hume","buddhism","stream of consciousness","self","anattā"]},{"term":"Memory theory","def":"You are the same person as someone in the past exactly when you can remember, from the inside, what they did or felt.","more":"The memory theory is the simplest psychological view of personal identity. It is usually traced to John Locke, who wrote in the second edition of his Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1694) that a person reaches as far back as their consciousness can extend to past actions and thoughts. On a strict reading, you are the same person as someone who existed earlier if, and only if, you can remember their experiences or actions from the inside, as your own. The theory explains why we care about memory in questions of identity and why Locke thought a prince’s memories, put into a cobbler’s body, would bring the prince with them. Also called Lockean memory criterion. Held by John Locke.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Psychological continuity","Narrow psychological criterion","Wide psychological criterion"],"thinkers":["locke"],"position":"memory_theory_of_personal_identity","tags":["memory criterion","locke","psychological continuity","personal identity","memory","lockean memory criterion"]},{"term":"Narrow psychological criterion","def":"You are the same person if overlapping chains of memory, character and intention link you, and they are carried by the same brain.","more":"The narrow psychological criterion is a version of the psychological continuity view, named by Derek Parfit in Reasons and Persons (1984). Like other psychological views, it says you persist through overlapping chains of psychological connections: memories of past experiences, intentions carried out later, lasting beliefs, desires and character traits. But it adds a requirement about the cause. The chain must have its normal cause, the continued existence and working of the same brain, or at least enough of it.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Psychological continuity","Brain view","Wide psychological criterion"],"thinkers":[],"position":"narrow_psychological_criterion","tags":["psychological continuity","psychological criterion","parfit","personal identity"]},{"term":"Wide psychological criterion","def":"You are the same person if overlapping chains of memory, character and intention link you, whatever reliably causes them.","more":"The wide psychological criterion is the most liberal mainstream version of the psychological continuity view. It says you persist as long as there are overlapping chains of psychological connections, such as memories, intentions carried out, and lasting character, between you now and a later person, provided they have some reliable cause. The cause need not be your brain. If a teletransporter scans you, destroys your body and builds an exact copy on Mars, the person on Mars is you, since the scanner reliably carries your psychology across. Held by Derek Parfit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Psychological continuity","Reductionism about personal identity","Identity is not what matters"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"position":"wide_psychological_criterion","tags":["psychological continuity","psychological criterion","parfit","personal identity"]},{"term":"Embodied mind account","def":"You are an embodied mind: you last as long as the parts of your brain that make consciousness possible go on working.","more":"The embodied mind account is a version of the brain view defended by Jeff McMahan in The Ethics of Killing (2002). It says that you are not essentially a whole human organism, nor a bundle of memories, but an embodied mind: the parts of your brain, chiefly the cerebral hemispheres, that generate consciousness and mental activity. You persist as long as those parts survive and keep their capacity to support a mental life, even if your memories and character are lost. So you would go with your cerebrum if it were transplanted, and you would cease to exist if your cerebrum died, even if your body went on living in a permanent vegetative state. Held by Jeff McMahan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Brain view","Psychological continuity"],"thinkers":["mcmahan"],"position":"embodied_mind_account","tags":["embodied mind","mcmahan","personal identity","brain","self"]},{"term":"Bodily criterion","def":"You are the same person as long as you have the same human body, and a body can outlast its life as a corpse.","more":"The bodily criterion says that personal identity follows the body. You at 70 are the same person as you at 7 because you have one continuing human body, however much its cells and its mind have changed. It differs from animalism in emphasis: animalism says you are a living organism, which stops existing when its life ends, while the bodily criterion says you are your body, which can go on existing for a while after death. Bernard Williams’s papers, especially “The Self and the Future” (1970), pushed many readers toward a bodily view by showing how fragile our intuitions about mind-swaps are. Also called Somatic criterion, Bodily continuity view. Held by Judith Jarvis Thomson, Bernard Williams, Fred Feldman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Animalism","Brain view"],"thinkers":["thomson","williams"],"position":"bodily_criterion","tags":["bodily continuity","physical criterion","animalism","personal identity","body","somatic criterion","bodily continuity view"]},{"term":"Person life view","def":"A person is the subject of a person-life: a life lived within the social practices of persons, which others help to shape.","more":"The person life view is Marya Schechtman’s later account of personal identity, set out in Staying Alive (2014). Her earlier narrative self-constitution view said a person is made by their own self-told story. The person life view widens this. A person is the one who lives a person-life: a life that combines biological functioning, psychological capacities and a place in the social world of persons, with its practices of naming, responsibility, care and recognition. Held by Marya Schechtman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Narrative self view","Animalism"],"thinkers":["schechtman"],"position":"person_life_view","tags":["schechtman","person life","personal identity","narrative self"]},{"term":"The Extreme Claim","def":"Neither identity nor mental continuity gives you a special reason to care about your own future.","more":"The Extreme Claim is a view Derek Parfit described in Reasons and Persons (1984). Most people think they have a special reason to care about their own future, a reason they do not have to care about other people’s futures. Non-reductionists ground this in a deep further fact: the future person will be me. Parfit argued that there is no such further fact: personal identity consists only in physical and mental continuity.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Identity is not what matters","Reductionism about personal identity","Buddhist no-self"],"thinkers":[],"position":"extreme_claim","tags":["parfit","extreme claim","self-interest","future self","personal identity"]},{"term":"Closest continuer theory","def":"A later person is you if they continue you closely enough and nobody else continues you as closely or more so.","more":"The closest continuer theory was proposed by Robert Nozick in Philosophical Explanations (1981). It says that whether a later thing is the same as an earlier one depends on whether it is the closest continuer of the earlier thing: it must grow out of it, resemble it closely enough, and be closer to it than any rival. Applied to persons, a later person is you if they are psychologically and physically continuous with you, close enough to count, and no other candidate is as close. If two continuers are tied, as in fission, neither is you. Held by Robert Nozick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Self and identity","related":["Non-branching view","Psychological continuity"],"thinkers":["nozick"],"position":"closest_continuer_theory","tags":["nozick","closest continuer","fission","personal identity"]},{"term":"Kantian synthesis (transcendental idealism)","def":"Knowledge comes from experience shaped by built-in forms of the mind.","more":"Immanuel Kant tried to end the fight between rationalists and empiricists. He agreed with empiricists that all knowledge begins with experience, but argued that not all of it comes from experience. The mind brings its own framework: space, time, and concepts like cause and substance. Every experience we can have is already organised by this framework, like light passing through a lens we cannot take off. Also called Kant’s synthesis, critical philosophy. Held by Immanuel Kant.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Synthetic a priori knowledge","Rationalism","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["kant"],"position":"transcendental_idealism","tags":["kant","transcendental idealism","categories","synthesis","phenomena and noumena","critique of pure reason","kant’s synthesis","critical philosophy"]},{"term":"Naturalized epistemology","def":"The study of knowledge should become part of science, especially psychology.","more":"Naturalized epistemology says we should study knowledge the way we study anything else in nature: with science. W. V. O. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Hilary Kornblith, Alvin Goldman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","Reliabilism","Epistemic externalism","Confirmation holism"],"thinkers":["quine","goldman"],"position":"naturalized_epistemology","tags":["quine","psychology","cognitive science","naturalism","epistemology"]},{"term":"Synthetic a priori knowledge","def":"Reason alone can tell us real facts about the world, not just about word meanings.","more":"This view says some of what we know a priori is ‘synthetic’. A priori knowledge is knowledge we can have without relying on experience for the justification. A truth is ‘analytic’ if it is true just by the meanings of its words (“all bachelors are unmarried”), and ‘synthetic’ if it says something more. The view holds that some synthetic truths can be known a priori: for example, that nothing can be red and green all over at the same time, that if A is taller than B and B taller than C then A is taller than C, or that a straight line is the shortest path. Also called substantive a priori knowledge. Held by Immanuel Kant, Laurence BonJour, Michael Huemer, George Bealer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Rationalism","Kantian synthesis (transcendental idealism)"],"thinkers":["kant","bonjour","huemer"],"position":"synthetic_a_priori_knowledge","tags":["synthetic a priori","kant","a priori","rationalism","mathematics","geometry","substantive a priori knowledge"]},{"term":"Logical empiricism","def":"The only a priori truths are true by definition, so they tell us nothing about the world.","more":"Logical empiricism (also called logical positivism) was a movement of the 1920s–1950s, centred on the Vienna Circle. It tried to make empiricism consistent with the certainty of logic and maths. Its answer was that a priori truths exist, but all of them are analytic: true just because of what the words or symbols mean, like “all triangles have three sides”. Such truths are known without experience precisely because they say nothing about the world. Also called logical positivism. Held by A. J. Ayer, Rudolf Carnap, Moritz Schlick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism"],"thinkers":["ayer","carnap","schlick"],"position":"logical_empiricism","tags":["logical positivism","vienna circle","verificationism","carnap","analytic truths","empiricism"]},{"term":"Confirmation holism","def":"No belief is immune to revision by experience, not even logic or maths.","more":"Confirmation holism, defended by W. V. O. Quine, says that our beliefs face experience not one by one but as a whole web. Also called Quinean holism, rejection of the a priori. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Michael Devitt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","Naturalized epistemology","Coherentism"],"thinkers":["quine"],"position":"confirmation_holism","tags":["holism","quine","duhem-quine thesis","web of belief","two dogmas","revisability","quinean holism","rejection of the a priori"]},{"term":"Bayesian epistemology","def":"Beliefs come in degrees, and evidence should change them according to the rules of probability.","more":"Bayesian epistemology says that belief is not all-or-nothing but comes in degrees of confidence, which should behave like probabilities. When you get new evidence, you should update your confidence by a rule from probability theory called Bayes’ theorem (named after Thomas Bayes): roughly, a theory gains support when it made the evidence more likely than its rivals did. Applied to induction, Bayesians say we do not need to prove that the future will resemble the past. Instead, if you start with reasonable confidence in a range of theories, repeated observations will rationally shift your confidence, and different people starting with different confidence will tend to converge as evidence piles up. Also called Bayesianism. Held by Frank Ramsey, Bruno de Finetti, Richard Jeffrey, Colin Howson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Pragmatic vindication of induction"],"thinkers":[],"position":"bayesian_epistemology","tags":["bayesianism","bayes theorem","credence","probability","conditionalisation","degrees of belief"]},{"term":"Falsificationism","def":"A theory is scientific only if some possible observation could show it false, and science advances by trying hard to refute its theories.","more":"Falsificationism is Karl Popper’s philosophy of science. It starts from a logical asymmetry: no number of white swans proves that all swans are white, but one black swan proves that they are not. So theories can never be proved, only refuted. Science, for Popper, is a cycle of bold conjectures and attempted refutations. Also called Critical rationalism. Held by Karl Popper, David W. Miller.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Lakatos’s methodology of research programmes","Error statistics","Truthlikeness view of progress","Inductive skepticism"],"thinkers":["popper"],"position":"falsificationism","tags":["popper","falsifiability","demarcation problem","conjectures and refutations","refutation","pseudoscience","critical rationalism"]},{"term":"Pragmatic vindication of induction","def":"We can’t prove induction will work, but if any method works, induction will.","more":"This view, developed by Hans Reichenbach and later Wesley Salmon, gives up trying to prove that induction is reliable. Instead it argues that using induction is the best bet. Either nature has regularities we can find, or it doesn’t. If it doesn’t, no method of prediction will succeed consistently, so we lose nothing by using induction. Held by Hans Reichenbach, Wesley Salmon.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Bayesian epistemology","Pragmatism"],"thinkers":["salmon"],"position":"pragmatic_vindication_of_induction","tags":["reichenbach","induction","problem of induction","pragmatic justification","straight rule"]},{"term":"Dissolving the problem of induction","def":"Asking whether induction is reasonable is like asking whether the law is legal: reasoning inductively is part of what ‘reasonable’ means.","more":"This view says the problem of induction is not solved but dissolved. P. F. Strawson argued in 1952 that to call a belief ‘reasonable’ just is, in part, to say it is backed by good inductive evidence. Also called ordinary-language dissolution, Strawson’s dissolution. Held by P. F. Strawson, Paul Edwards.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Natural belief"],"thinkers":["pfstrawson"],"position":"analytic_dissolution_of_induction","tags":["strawson","induction","problem of induction","ordinary language","hume","ordinary-language dissolution","strawson’s dissolution"]},{"term":"Inductive skepticism","def":"There is no good reason to think the future will resemble the past; we just expect it out of habit.","more":"Inductive skepticism says that inductive reasoning, from observed cases to unobserved ones, cannot be rationally justified. The classic argument is from David Hume. Any reason to trust induction must be either a matter of pure logic or of experience. Logic can’t do it: it is perfectly consistent to imagine the sun not rising tomorrow. Held by David Hume, Sextus Empiricus.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Natural belief","Radical skepticism"],"thinkers":["hume","sextus"],"position":"inductive_skepticism","tags":["hume","problem of induction","skepticism","uniformity of nature","induction"]},{"term":"Indirect realism","def":"We perceive the world through inner representations that the world causes in us.","more":"Indirect realism says that a physical world exists independently of us, but we are only directly aware of mental representations of it, often called ideas or sense data. Light bounces off a tree, hits your eye, sets off signals in your brain, and the result is an inner image of a tree. You know about the tree by way of this image. John Locke held a version: our ideas of shape and size resemble real features of objects, but colours and tastes are produced in us and don’t resemble anything in the object. Also called representative realism, representationalism about perception. Held by John Locke, René Descartes, Bertrand Russell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism"],"thinkers":["locke","descartes","russell"],"position":"indirect_realism","tags":["representative realism","sense data","locke","veil of perception","perception","representationalism about perception"]},{"term":"Phenomenalism","def":"Talk of physical objects is really talk about the experiences we have or would have.","more":"Phenomenalism says that physical objects are nothing over and above actual and possible experiences. To say there is a table in the next room is to say that if you went in and looked, you would have table-like experiences. John Stuart Mill described matter as the ‘permanent possibility of sensation’. The view grew out of empiricism: if all we ever meet in experience are sensations, then claims about objects must be translatable into claims about sensations. Held by John Stuart Mill, A. J. Ayer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","Logical empiricism"],"thinkers":["mill","ayer"],"position":"phenomenalism","tags":["mill","permanent possibilities of sensation","ayer","sense data","idealism"]},{"term":"Fallibilist externalism (about skepticism)","def":"You can know you have hands without being able to prove you’re not a brain in a vat.","more":"This response to radical skepticism says that knowing does not require being able to rule out every wild possibility. ‘Fallibilism’ means knowledge doesn’t require certainty: you can know something even though it is possible you are wrong. ‘Externalism’ means what makes a belief knowledge can include facts you can’t check from the inside, such as whether your senses actually work reliably. If you are in fact a normal person with working eyes, your belief that you have hands comes from a reliable process, and so it is knowledge, even though a brain in a vat would feel exactly the same. Also called externalist anti-skepticism. Held by Alvin Goldman, Ernest Sosa, John Greco.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemic externalism","Reliabilism","Virtue epistemology","Epistemic closure"],"thinkers":["goldman","sosa"],"position":"fallibilist_externalism","tags":["externalism","fallibilism","brain in a vat","skepticism","knowledge","externalist anti-skepticism"]},{"term":"Mooreanism (common sense)","def":"I’m more certain I have hands than I could ever be of any premise in a skeptical argument.","more":"Mooreanism takes its name from G. E. ” His point was not that this proves anything to a determined skeptic. It was that some ordinary beliefs are more certain than any philosophical premise that could be used to attack them. Also called Moorean response, common-sense philosophy. Held by G. E. Moore, Thomas Reid, James Pryor.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Direct realism","Epistemic closure","Fallibilist externalism (about skepticism)"],"thinkers":["moore","reid"],"position":"mooreanism","tags":["moore","common sense","here is one hand","proof of an external world","skepticism","moorean response","common-sense philosophy"]},{"term":"Epistemic contextualism","def":"Whether it’s true to say “I know” depends on how high the standards are in the conversation.","more":"Contextualism says that the word ‘know’ is like ‘tall’ or ‘flat’: what it takes to count as knowing shifts with the context of the conversation. ” When a philosopher raises the brain-in-a-vat possibility, the standards rise, and now “I know I have hands” becomes false, because you can’t rule out that possibility. Both the everyday speaker and the skeptic speak truly, each in their own context. This explains why skeptical arguments feel compelling in the seminar but vanish when you leave it. Also called contextualism about knowledge. Held by Keith DeRose, David Lewis, Stewart Cohen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemic closure"],"thinkers":["derose","lewis"],"position":"epistemic_contextualism","tags":["contextualism","stakes","knowledge attributions","dretske","lewis","derose","skepticism","contextualism about knowledge"]},{"term":"Hinge epistemology","def":"Some certainties are not known or doubted at all; they are the hinges on which knowing and doubting turn.","more":"Hinge epistemology comes from Ludwig Wittgenstein’s last notes, published as On Certainty (1969). He argued that some beliefs, like “I have hands” or “the Earth has existed for many years”, are not conclusions we reached from evidence. They are the background that makes all evidence-weighing possible, like the hinges a door must stay fixed on in order to turn. Doubting them isn’t a sharper form of inquiry; it would remove the framework that makes inquiry meaningful. Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein, Crispin Wright, Duncan Pritchard, Annalisa Coliva.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Mooreanism (common sense)","Pragmatism"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein","crispin_wright"],"position":"hinge_epistemology","tags":["wittgenstein","on certainty","hinges","hinge propositions","certainty","skepticism"]},{"term":"Semantic externalism (Putnam’s reply)","def":"A brain in a vat couldn’t even think “I am a brain in a vat”, so the thought refutes itself.","more":"Semantic externalism is a view about meaning: what your words refer to depends partly on your causal links to the world, not only on what is in your head. Hilary Putnam used it in 1981 to argue against the brain-in-a-vat scenario. A brain that had always been in a vat has never been causally connected to real brains or vats. So when it thinks “brain” or “vat”, its words refer to something else, perhaps the computer signals that produce its images of brains and vats. Also called Putnam’s brain-in-a-vat argument. Held by Hilary Putnam.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Fallibilist externalism (about skepticism)"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"semantic_externalism","tags":["putnam","brain in a vat","semantic externalism","content externalism","reference","skepticism","putnam’s brain-in-a-vat argument"]},{"term":"Radical skepticism","def":"Since you can’t rule out being a brain in a vat, you don’t know you have hands.","more":"Radical skepticism says we know little or nothing about the external world. The modern argument is simple. If you know you have hands, you can know you’re not a handless brain in a vat being fed perfect fake experiences. But you can’t know that: everything you experience would be the same either way. Also called global skepticism, Cartesian skepticism. Held by Peter Unger.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Pyrrhonism","Academic skepticism","Inductive skepticism","Epistemic closure"],"thinkers":[],"position":"radical_skepticism","tags":["global skepticism","brain in a vat","evil demon","cartesian skepticism","doubt","skepticism"]},{"term":"Academic skepticism","def":"Nothing can be known for certain, but some views are more convincing than others, and we can act on those.","more":"Academic skepticism developed in Plato’s Academy in the third and second centuries BCE, under Arcesilaus and later Carneades, and is known mainly through Cicero’s Academica. Its central target was the Stoic claim that some impressions are so clear they cannot be false. The Academics argued that for any true impression, a false one could be exactly like it (twins, dreams, wax replicas), so no impression guarantees truth. Hence nothing can be known with certainty. Held by Carneades, Marcus Tullius Cicero, Arcesilaus.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Radical skepticism","Pyrrhonism"],"thinkers":["carneades","cicero"],"position":"academic_skepticism","tags":["carneades","arcesilaus","academy","probabilism","ancient skepticism","skepticism"]},{"term":"Natural belief","def":"Skeptical arguments may be unanswerable, but human nature makes us believe anyway, and that is fine.","more":"Natural belief is David Hume’s response to skepticism, later developed by P. F. Strawson. Hume found skeptical arguments, about the senses and about induction, hard to refute. Also called skeptical naturalism, Humean naturalism. Held by David Hume, P. F. Strawson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Hinge epistemology","Dissolving the problem of induction"],"thinkers":["hume","pfstrawson"],"position":"natural_belief","tags":["hume","naturalism","skepticism","custom","human nature","skeptical naturalism","humean naturalism"]},{"term":"Defeasibility theory","def":"Knowledge is justified true belief that no further truth would undermine.","more":"Defeasibility theory is one of the main attempts to repair the justified true belief (JTB) analysis after Gettier. It adds a fourth condition: there must be no ‘defeater’. A defeater is a true fact that, if you learned it, would take away your justification. In the stopped clock case, the fact that the clock has stopped is a defeater: if you knew it, you would no longer be justified in believing it is 2:00. Also called no-defeater analysis, JTB plus no defeaters. Held by Keith Lehrer, Peter Klein, John Pollock.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Justified true belief (JTB)","Epistemic internalism"],"thinkers":["lehrer"],"position":"defeasibility_theory","tags":["defeasibility","gettier problem","defeaters","knowledge","justified true belief","no-defeater analysis","jtb plus no defeaters"]},{"term":"Reliabilism","def":"A belief is justified, and can be knowledge, when it comes from a process that usually gets things right.","more":"Reliabilism says that what makes a belief justified is not the reasons you can cite, but the process that produced it. If a belief comes from a reliable process, one that tends to produce true beliefs, like normal vision in good light or careful memory, it is justified. Wishful thinking and guesswork are unreliable, so beliefs from them are not. Alvin Goldman gave the classic version in 1979; earlier, Frank Ramsey and D. Also called process reliabilism. Held by Alvin Goldman, D. M. Armstrong, Frank Ramsey.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemic externalism","Virtue epistemology","Naturalized epistemology","Sensitivity (truth-tracking) theory"],"thinkers":["goldman","armstrong"],"position":"reliabilism","tags":["process reliabilism","goldman","reliable process","externalism","justification","knowledge"]},{"term":"Knowledge-first epistemology","def":"Knowledge can’t be broken down into belief plus extras; it is basic, and explains everything else.","more":"Knowledge-first epistemology, developed by Timothy Williamson, reverses the usual order of explanation. After decades of failed attempts to define knowledge as belief plus truth plus something else, Williamson argued in Knowledge and Its Limits (2000) that the project was misguided. Knowledge is a basic mental state, like seeing that something is the case, and cannot be split into an inner belief part and an outer truth part. Instead of explaining knowledge with belief and justification, we should explain those with knowledge. Held by Timothy Williamson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemic externalism","Fallibilist externalism (about skepticism)","Epistemic closure"],"thinkers":["williamson"],"position":"knowledge_first_epistemology","tags":["williamson","knowledge first","e=k","knowledge","evidence"]},{"term":"Sensitivity (truth-tracking) theory","def":"You know only if your belief tracks the truth: had it been false, you would not have believed it.","more":"The truth-tracking theory, set out by Robert Nozick in Philosophical Explanations (1981), says knowledge needs more than a true, well-supported belief. Your belief must be sensitive to the truth. If what you believe were false, you would not believe it, and if it were true in slightly different circumstances, you would still believe it. Reading a stopped clock fails: had the time been different, you would still believe it was 2:00, because the clock shows 2:00 whatever the time. Also called Sensitivity theory, Tracking theory. Held by Robert Nozick, Fred Dretske.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Reliabilism","Closure denial","Epistemic externalism"],"thinkers":["nozick"],"position":"truth_tracking_theory","tags":["sensitivity","nozick","truth-tracking","tracking theory","safety","knowledge","sensitivity theory"]},{"term":"Epistemic closure","def":"If you know something, and correctly deduce something else from it, you can know that too.","more":"The closure principle says knowledge is ‘closed’ under known logical consequence: if you know P, and you know that P implies Q, and you believe Q on that basis, you know Q. It looks almost obvious. It is how deduction extends our knowledge: you know the meeting is on Tuesday and that Tuesday is the 5th, so you know the meeting is on the 5th. But closure also powers the skeptic’s argument. Also called the closure principle. Held by Timothy Williamson, John Hawthorne, G. E. Moore.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Mooreanism (common sense)","Fallibilist externalism (about skepticism)","Epistemic contextualism"],"thinkers":["williamson","moore"],"position":"epistemic_closure","tags":["closure principle","deductive closure","skepticism","knowledge","entailment","the closure principle"]},{"term":"Closure denial","def":"You can know you’re looking at a zebra without knowing it isn’t a cleverly painted mule.","more":"Closure denial says knowledge is not always passed along by deduction. Fred Dretske argued in 1970 that some ‘heavyweight’ consequences of what we know are beyond our knowledge. At the zoo, you know the animal is a zebra, which implies it is not a mule cleverly painted to look like a zebra. But your evidence (it looks like a zebra) does nothing to rule out the painted mule. Also called Dretske-Nozick view, anti-closure. Held by Fred Dretske, Robert Nozick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Sensitivity (truth-tracking) theory","Epistemic externalism"],"thinkers":["nozick"],"position":"closure_denial","tags":["denial of closure","dretske","nozick","zebra case","skepticism","knowledge","dretske-nozick view","anti-closure"]},{"term":"Epistemic internalism","def":"What justifies your belief must be something you can, in principle, become aware of by reflection.","more":"Internalism says that whether a belief is justified depends only on factors ‘internal’ to the believer. Access internalists, like Roderick Chisholm and Laurence BonJour (in his internalist phase), say the justifiers must be things you can become aware of just by reflecting. Mentalists, like Earl Conee and Richard Feldman, say justification depends only on your mental states, such as your experiences and other beliefs. Either way, two people with exactly the same inner life are equally justified, whatever the outside world is like. Also called internalism about justification, access internalism, mentalism. Held by Roderick Chisholm, Richard Feldman, Earl Conee, René Descartes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Evidentialism","Foundationalism","Justified true belief (JTB)"],"thinkers":["chisholm","feldman_r","descartes"],"position":"epistemic_internalism","tags":["internalism","access internalism","justification","bonjour","evidentialism","internalism about justification","mentalism"]},{"term":"Epistemic externalism","def":"Your belief can be justified by facts you can’t see from the inside, such as whether your senses actually work.","more":"Externalism says that what makes a belief justified (or what makes it knowledge) can include factors outside the believer’s awareness. The most common example is reliability: a belief from a process that in fact reliably produces truths is justified, whether or not you know that the process is reliable. Other externalist ideas include proper function (Alvin Plantinga: faculties working as designed in the right environment), tracking the truth (Robert Nozick), and the exercise of virtues (Ernest Sosa). Externalists think internalism wrongly turns knowing into a reflective exercise in giving reasons, something only philosophers do well. Also called externalism about justification. Held by Alvin Goldman, D. M. Armstrong, Alvin Plantinga, Fred Dretske.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Reliabilism","Virtue epistemology","Fallibilist externalism (about skepticism)","Naturalized epistemology"],"thinkers":["goldman","armstrong","plantinga"],"position":"epistemic_externalism","tags":["externalism","reliabilism","justification","goldman","knowledge","externalism about justification"]},{"term":"Internalist externalism","def":"The grounds of your belief must be accessible to you, but whether they are good grounds depends on the world.","more":"Internalist externalism is a middle position defended by William Alston in a 1988 paper of that name. It splits justification into two parts. First, the ground of a belief (the experience or other belief it is based on) must be something accessible to you: you can tell what you are believing on the basis of. This is the internalist part. Also called hybrid view of justification. Held by William Alston.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemic externalism","Epistemic internalism","Foundationalism"],"thinkers":["alston"],"position":"internalist_externalism","tags":["internalism","externalism","justification","alston","hybrid view of justification"]},{"term":"Infinitism","def":"The chain of reasons never has to end; justification comes from having more reasons available.","more":"Infinitism takes the option that both foundationalists and coherentists reject in the regress problem: the chain of reasons goes on forever without repeating. Its main modern defender, Peter Klein, argues that this is not as bad as it sounds. You don’t need to have actually thought through infinitely many reasons. You need only have reasons available: for any reason you give, you could, if challenged, give another one that supports it. Held by Peter Klein, Scott Aikin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":[],"thinkers":[],"position":"infinitism","tags":["infinite regress","regress problem","klein","justification","foundationalism","coherentism"]},{"term":"Foundherentism","def":"Justification is like a crossword: experience gives clues, and answers support each other.","more":"Foundherentism is Susan Haack’s blend of foundationalism and coherentism, set out in Evidence and Inquiry (1993). She compares justification to solving a crossword. The clues are like experience: they give independent support to particular entries. The intersecting entries are like other beliefs: they support one another by fitting together. Held by Susan Haack.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Coherentism","Foundationalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"foundherentism","tags":["haack","foundationalism","coherentism","crossword analogy","justification"]},{"term":"The will to believe","def":"When evidence can’t settle a vital question, you may let your hopes and needs decide.","more":"The will to believe is William James’s answer to Clifford, set out in an 1896 lecture of that name. James agreed that, normally, we should follow the evidence. But he argued that some choices are ‘genuine options’: the alternatives are live (both seem possible to you), forced (you can’t avoid choosing, since not deciding is itself a choice), and momentous (much is at stake, and the chance may not return). When such a question cannot be settled by evidence, James said, our passions may and must decide. Also called Jamesian permissivism. Held by William James.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Pragmatism","Pragmatism about belief","Pascal’s wager","Fideism"],"thinkers":["james"],"position":"will_to_believe","tags":["william james","will to believe","faith","pascal's wager","belief","religious belief","jamesian permissivism"]},{"term":"Pragmatism about belief","def":"The good consequences of believing something can be a genuine reason to believe it.","more":"Pragmatism about belief says that practical considerations, like how believing something would affect your happiness, health or relationships, can be real reasons for belief, not only evidence. It is broader than James’s will to believe, which applies only when evidence is silent. Blaise Pascal’s famous wager is an early example: if God exists, believing gains you infinite reward, and if not, you lose little, so you have reason to believe (and to take steps to make yourself believe). Modern defenders like Susanna Rinard argue that beliefs should be judged by the same standards as actions: if believing something would make your life go much better, that counts in its favour. Also called pragmatic reasons for belief, doxastic pragmatism. Held by Blaise Pascal, Susanna Rinard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["The will to believe","Pragmatism"],"thinkers":["pascal"],"position":"pragmatism_about_belief","tags":["pragmatic reasons","james","belief","practical reasons","pascal's wager","doxastic voluntarism","pragmatic reasons for belief","doxastic pragmatism"]},{"term":"Conciliationism","def":"When an equally smart, equally informed peer disagrees with you, you should become less confident.","more":"Conciliationism is a view about peer disagreement. An epistemic peer is someone as intelligent, careful and well informed as you on the question. Conciliationists like David Christensen, Adam Elga and Richard Feldman argue that if you learn that a peer reached the opposite conclusion from the same evidence, you should move your confidence toward theirs, giving their view as much weight as your own. Christensen’s example: two friends split a restaurant bill; one works out $43 each, the other $45. Also called equal weight view. Held by David Christensen, Adam Elga, Richard Feldman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["feldman_r"],"position":"conciliationism","tags":["conciliatory view","equal weight view","peer disagreement","christensen","elga","disagreement"]},{"term":"Steadfastness","def":"If you’ve reasoned well, a peer’s disagreement doesn’t oblige you to give up your view.","more":"Steadfastness says that you can sometimes rationally keep your belief even after learning that an equally smart and well-informed peer disagrees. The idea is that what matters is the evidence and whether you assessed it correctly. If your peer has made a mistake, then the fact that they disagree doesn’t change what the evidence supports. Thomas Kelly, in his early work, called this the ‘right reasons’ view. Also called steadfast view, right reasons view. Held by Alvin Plantinga, Peter van Inwagen, Thomas Kelly.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":[],"thinkers":["plantinga","vaninwagen"],"position":"steadfastness","tags":["steadfast view","peer disagreement","kelly","disagreement","right reasons","right reasons view"]},{"term":"Total evidence view","def":"Weigh the disagreement as one more piece of evidence, alongside the original evidence itself.","more":"The total evidence view, defended by Thomas Kelly in 2010, takes a middle way between conciliationism and steadfastness. When you learn a peer disagrees, you should respond to your total evidence: both the ‘first-order’ evidence about the question itself, and the ‘higher-order’ evidence that a capable person read it differently. Neither side automatically wins. If the original evidence strongly supports one answer, and you assessed it correctly, the disagreement should shift you only a little. Held by Thomas Kelly.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Steadfastness","Conciliationism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"total_evidence_view","tags":["total evidence","kelly","peer disagreement","disagreement","evidence"]},{"term":"Anti-reductionism about testimony","def":"Trusting what people tell you is a basic source of knowledge, like perception or memory.","more":"Anti-reductionism says testimony, what we learn from what others tell us, is a basic source of justification, on a par with perception, memory and reasoning. You are entitled to believe what you are told unless you have positive reasons for doubt. You don’t need to first check that your informant is reliable. Thomas Reid argued that humans have a natural tendency to tell the truth and a matching tendency to believe what they are told. Also called credulism, the default view of testimony. Held by Thomas Reid, C. A. J. Coady, Tyler Burge.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Mooreanism (common sense)","Dualism about testimony"],"thinkers":["reid"],"position":"anti_reductionism_about_testimony","tags":["testimony","trust","reid","burge","coady","default entitlement","credulism","the default view of testimony"]},{"term":"Reductionism about testimony","def":"You should believe what someone tells you only when your own evidence shows they’re reliable.","more":"Reductionism says that testimony is not a basic source of justification. A belief based on what someone tells you is justified only if you have independent reasons, from your own perception, memory and inference, to think the speaker is reliable on that topic. Testimony’s authority ‘reduces’ to these other sources. David Hume is often read as a reductionist: he said we trust testimony because experience has shown a general match between what people say and what is true, though this reading is contested. Held by David Hume, Elizabeth Fricker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Empiricism","Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["hume"],"position":"reductionism_about_testimony","tags":["testimony","hume","reductionism","trust","evidence"]},{"term":"Dualism about testimony","def":"Learning from testimony needs both a reliable speaker and some positive reason to trust them.","more":"Dualism about testimony, defended by Jennifer Lackey in Learning from Words (2008), says that both reductionists and anti-reductionists get half of the story right. For you to acquire justified belief or knowledge from what someone tells you, two things are needed. On the speaker’s side, their statement must be reliable: likely to be true. On the hearer’s side, you must have some positive reasons for accepting it (not just an absence of doubt), though these reasons need not be enough on their own to justify the belief. Also called hybrid view of testimony. Held by Jennifer Lackey.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Anti-reductionism about testimony","Reductionism about testimony"],"thinkers":[],"position":"testimonial_dualism","tags":["testimony","lackey","dualism","reliability","trust","hybrid view of testimony"]},{"term":"Infallibilism","def":"To know something, your grounds must make it impossible for you to be wrong.","more":"Infallibilism says that knowledge needs a guarantee. Good evidence is not enough: if your reasons leave any chance, however small, that your belief is false, then you do not know, you only believe with good reason. This is an old and natural idea. In Plato’s Republic, Socrates contrasts knowledge, which cannot err, with mere opinion, and Descartes set out to find beliefs so certain that not even an all-powerful deceiver could make them false. Also called Certainty view of knowledge. Held by René Descartes, Plato, Peter Unger, John McDowell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Radical skepticism","Foundationalism","Epistemic internalism"],"thinkers":["descartes","plato","unger"],"position":"infallibilism","tags":["certainty","infallible knowledge","skepticism","fallibilism","knowledge","certainty view of knowledge"]},{"term":"Cartesian infallibilism","def":"Real knowledge rests on certainties that no doubt can touch, such as “I think, so I exist”.","more":"Cartesian infallibilism is René Descartes’s way of making knowledge secure. In the Meditations (1641) he decides to doubt everything that can possibly be doubted: his senses (which sometimes deceive), his waking life (which might be a dream), even simple arithmetic (an all-powerful deceiver might trick him). One thing survives: while he is doubting, he must exist, since he is thinking. From this first certainty he tries to rebuild knowledge, using the rule that whatever he perceives “clearly and distinctly” is true, and arguing that a perfect God would not let such perceptions mislead him. Also called Cartesian foundationalism. Held by René Descartes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Infallibilism","Rationalism","Foundationalism"],"thinkers":["descartes"],"position":"cartesian_infallibilism","tags":["descartes","certainty","cogito","foundationalism","indubitability","rationalism","cartesian foundationalism"]},{"term":"Epistemological disjunctivism","def":"When you really see that something is so, your reason for believing it is the seeing itself, which cannot mislead you.","more":"Epistemological disjunctivism, defended by John McDowell and later by Duncan Pritchard, rejects a picture shared by skeptics and many of their opponents: that when you see a cup and when you merely seem to see one (in a perfect hallucination), you have exactly the same evidence. On the disjunctivist view, the two cases differ in what you have. In the good case your reason is that you see that there is a cup, and seeing that something is so cannot happen unless it is so. In the bad case you only seem to see it. Held by John McDowell, Duncan Pritchard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Infallibilism","Direct realism","Epistemic internalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"epistemological_disjunctivism","tags":["disjunctivism","mcdowell","pritchard","factive reasons","perception"]},{"term":"Access internalism","def":"A belief is justified only by reasons you could become aware of just by reflecting.","more":"Access internalism is the classic form of internalism about justification. It says that whatever makes your belief justified must be something you can get at from the inside, by thinking it over, without investigating the world. If you believe it will rain because of the dark clouds you see, the seeing and the inference are things you can reflect on. If a belief is supported only by facts you have no access to, such as the hidden reliability of your brain, it is not justified for you. Held by Roderick Chisholm, Laurence BonJour, Richard Fumerton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemic internalism","Foundationalism","Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["chisholm","bonjour"],"position":"access_internalism","tags":["internalism","justification","reflective access"]},{"term":"Phenomenal conservatism","def":"If something seems true to you, you are justified in believing it, unless you have reason to doubt it.","more":"Phenomenal conservatism, defended by Michael Huemer, says that appearances are innocent until proven guilty. If it seems to you that there is a tree ahead, or that torturing for fun is wrong, or that 2 + 2 = 4, then you have some justification for believing it, as long as nothing defeats that seeming. “Seemings” here are the way things strike you: perceptual, remembered, intellectual or moral. The view is internalist, since seemings are states of your own mind, and conservative, since it lets you keep what seems true without first proving it. Also called Dogmatism (in Pryor’s sense). Held by Michael Huemer, James Pryor, Chris Tucker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Epistemic internalism","Foundationalism","Perceptual dogmatism","Mooreanism (common sense)"],"thinkers":["huemer"],"position":"phenomenal_conservatism","tags":["huemer","seemings","appearances","justification","dogmatism","dogmatism (in pryor’s sense)"]},{"term":"Process reliabilism","def":"A belief is justified when the mental process that produced it usually produces true beliefs.","more":"” (1979). It looks at how a belief came about. Vision in good light, careful memory and sound reasoning tend to produce true beliefs, so beliefs formed by them are justified; wishful thinking, guessing and hasty generalisation tend to produce false ones, so beliefs formed by them are not. You do not need to know that your process is reliable, or even be able to describe it. Held by Alvin Goldman, D. M. Armstrong, Frank Ramsey.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Reliabilism","Epistemic externalism","Naturalized epistemology"],"thinkers":["goldman","armstrong"],"position":"process_reliabilism","tags":["reliabilism","goldman","reliable process","externalism","justification"]},{"term":"Safety theory of knowledge","def":"You know only if your belief could not easily have been false.","more":"The safety theory says that knowledge requires a belief that is safe from error: in situations very like the actual one, if you formed the belief in the same way, it would still be true. It was developed by Ernest Sosa (1999), Timothy Williamson (2000) and Duncan Pritchard (2005). Gettier cases fail the test. If you glance at a stopped clock that happens to show the right time, then in a nearby situation where you looked a minute later you would believe falsely, so your true belief is lucky, not safe. Also called Safety condition. Held by Ernest Sosa, Timothy Williamson, Duncan Pritchard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Reliabilism","Anti-luck virtue epistemology","Epistemic closure"],"thinkers":["sosa","williamson"],"position":"safety_theory","tags":["safety","safe belief","sosa","williamson","pritchard","modal condition","safety condition"]},{"term":"Proper functionalism","def":"A belief is warranted when it comes from faculties working properly, as designed, in the right environment, aimed at truth.","more":"Proper functionalism is Alvin Plantinga’s theory of warrant, the property that turns true belief into knowledge, set out in Warrant and Proper Function (1993). A belief has warrant when it is produced by cognitive faculties that are working properly, in an environment they were designed for, according to a design plan aimed at truth, and when that design plan is a good one, so that such beliefs are very likely true. Your eyes working normally in daylight on Earth give warranted beliefs; the same eyes in a world of trick mirrors, or damaged by a brain lesion, do not. Plantinga thinks the design can come from God or, for naturalists, perhaps from evolution, though he argues that evolution alone gives no reason to trust our faculties. Held by Alvin Plantinga, Michael Bergmann.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Reliabilism","Epistemic externalism","Reformed epistemology"],"thinkers":["plantinga"],"position":"proper_functionalism","tags":["proper function","plantinga","warrant","design plan","reformed epistemology","externalism"]},{"term":"Virtue reliabilism","def":"Knowledge is belief that is true because of a reliable ability of the believer, such as good eyesight or memory.","more":"Virtue reliabilism treats intellectual virtues as reliable faculties: perception, memory, reasoning and so on. Ernest Sosa’s version compares a belief to an archer’s shot. A shot can be accurate (it hits), adroit (it shows skill) and apt (accurate because adroit). Knowledge is apt belief: true because of the believer’s competence, not just true and competently formed. Held by Ernest Sosa, John Greco.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Virtue epistemology","Reliabilism","Anti-luck virtue epistemology"],"thinkers":["sosa"],"position":"virtue_reliabilism","tags":["virtue epistemology","sosa","greco","intellectual virtue","reliabilism","apt belief"]},{"term":"Virtue responsibilism","def":"Knowledge and good belief come from intellectual character: traits like open-mindedness, care and intellectual courage.","more":"Virtue responsibilism models epistemology on Aristotle’s ethics of character. Intellectual virtues are not natural faculties like eyesight but acquired traits of character: open-mindedness, intellectual humility, thoroughness, honesty, curiosity and the courage to follow an argument. Linda Zagzebski, in Virtues of the Mind (1996), defined knowledge as belief arising from acts of intellectual virtue, where an act of virtue involves both the right motivation (a love of truth) and success because of it. Lorraine Code and James Montmarquet stressed that we are responsible for how we inquire, and that a good knower is a good kind of person. Held by Linda Zagzebski, Lorraine Code, James Montmarquet.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Virtue epistemology","Virtue ethics"],"thinkers":["zagzebski"],"position":"virtue_responsibilism","tags":["virtue epistemology","zagzebski","intellectual virtue","intellectual character","open-mindedness","responsibility"]},{"term":"Anti-luck virtue epistemology","def":"Knowledge needs two things: true belief that comes from your ability, and true belief that is safe from luck.","more":"Anti-luck virtue epistemology is Duncan Pritchard’s attempt to combine two ideas that each seem to capture part of knowledge. The first is the virtue idea: knowledge comes from your own cognitive abilities. The second is the anti-luck idea: knowledge cannot be true merely by luck, so your belief must be safe, not easily false. Pritchard argued that neither idea works alone. Held by Duncan Pritchard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Virtue epistemology","Safety theory of knowledge","Virtue reliabilism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"anti_luck_virtue_epistemology","tags":["virtue epistemology","pritchard","epistemic luck","safety","gettier problem"]},{"term":"Evidence as knowledge (E = K)","def":"Your evidence is exactly what you know, and a belief is justified when that evidence supports it.","more":"E = K is Timothy Williamson’s thesis, defended in Knowledge and its Limits (2000), that your evidence consists of all and only the things you know. If you know that the road is wet, that fact is part of your evidence; if you merely believe something, it is not. This reverses the traditional order, in which evidence was used to explain knowledge. Justified belief can then be explained in terms of knowledge: a belief is justified to the degree that your evidence, what you know, makes it probable. Held by Timothy Williamson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Knowledge-first epistemology","Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["williamson"],"position":"e_equals_k","tags":["e=k","williamson","evidence","knowledge-first epistemology","knowledge"]},{"term":"Justification as knowledge (J = K)","def":"A belief is justified only if it is knowledge; anything less is at best excused.","more":"The view that justification is knowledge was defended by Jonathan Sutton in Without Justification (2007) and, in related forms, by Clayton Littlejohn and others. It says that there is no lesser state of “justified but not knowledge”. Belief aims at knowledge, and a belief that falls short, whether it is false or Gettiered, has failed by the standard that governs belief. That does not make the believer foolish or blameworthy. Held by Jonathan Sutton, Clayton Littlejohn.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Knowledge-first epistemology","Evidence as knowledge (E = K)"],"thinkers":[],"position":"j_equals_k","tags":["j=k","sutton","justification","knowledge-first epistemology","knowledge"]},{"term":"Justification as would-be knowledge","def":"A belief is justified if it would have been knowledge had things been normal, even if they were not.","more":"This knowledge-first view, defended by Alexander Bird in “Justified Judging” (2007) and in a different version by Jonathan Ichikawa, explains justification as would-be or potential knowledge. Your belief is justified if, in a normal situation where you had the same mental states and formed the belief in the same way, it would have been knowledge. A victim of a deceiving demon forms beliefs exactly as you do; in a normal world those beliefs would be knowledge, so they are justified, even though they are false. This keeps knowledge first, since justification is defined in terms of knowledge rather than the reverse, while agreeing with internalists that the demon victim is justified. Held by Alexander Bird, Jonathan Ichikawa.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Knowledge-first epistemology","Epistemic internalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"justification_as_potential_knowledge","tags":["bird","justification","knowledge-first epistemology","knowledge"]},{"term":"Warranted assertibility (Dewey)","def":"Inquiry aims to turn a troubled situation into a settled one; its results are warranted, not final.","more":"John Dewey’s theory of inquiry, laid out in Logic: The Theory of Inquiry (1938), treats knowing as a kind of problem-solving. Inquiry starts when a situation is unsettled: something has gone wrong, a habit no longer works. It ends when the situation is transformed into a settled one, through hypotheses, experiments and checking. Dewey preferred to call the result “warranted assertibility” rather than knowledge or truth, to stress that it is earned by inquiry and always open to revision. Also called Instrumentalism (Dewey’s sense). Held by John Dewey.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Pragmatism","Naturalized epistemology"],"thinkers":["dewey"],"position":"warranted_assertibility_theory","tags":["dewey","warranted assertibility","inquiry","pragmatism","truth","instrumentalism (dewey’s sense)"]},{"term":"Neopragmatism (Rorty)","def":"Justification is a social practice: a belief is justified when it can be defended to the relevant community.","more":"Richard Rorty’s neopragmatism, set out in Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature (1979), rejects the idea that the mind mirrors nature and that epistemology could find foundations to check the mirror. Rorty proposed what he called epistemological behaviourism: to explain why a belief is justified, we look at how people actually justify claims to one another, not at a special relation between mind and world. Knowledge is a matter of conversation and social practice. There is no neutral standpoint outside all practices from which to judge them, so the goal of inquiry is better agreement and more useful ways of speaking, not correspondence with reality. Also called Epistemological behaviourism. Held by Richard Rorty.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Pragmatism","Coherentism"],"thinkers":["rorty"],"position":"neopragmatism","tags":["rorty","neopragmatism","pragmatism","justification","solidarity","anti-representationalism","epistemological behaviourism"]},{"term":"Perceptual dogmatism","def":"Seeming to see your hands justifies you in believing you have hands, without first proving you are not deceived.","more":"Perceptual dogmatism is James Pryor’s answer to the skeptic in “The Skeptic and the Dogmatist” (2000). The name is not an insult: it means that perceptual experience gives you immediate justification, justification that does not depend on any further belief. When you seem to see hands, you are thereby justified in believing you have hands, unless you have reason to doubt it. You do not first need to know that you are not dreaming or deceived. Also called Dogmatism (Pryor). Held by James Pryor, Michael Huemer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Mooreanism (common sense)","Phenomenal conservatism","Foundationalism"],"thinkers":["huemer"],"position":"perceptual_dogmatism","tags":["dogmatism","pryor","perceptual justification","phenomenal conservatism","perception","dogmatism (pryor)"]},{"term":"Common-sense particularism","def":"We know particular things, like that we have hands, better than we could ever know the general principles a skeptic uses.","more":"Common-sense particularism starts from cases rather than criteria. Roderick Chisholm, in The Problem of the Criterion (1973), framed an old puzzle: to know which beliefs are knowledge you need a criterion, but to know which criterion is right you need to know which beliefs are knowledge. Methodists start with a criterion; particularists, like Chisholm, start with particular things they know, and work out criteria to fit them. G. Also called Particularism (Chisholm), Common-sense philosophy. Held by G. E. Moore, Roderick Chisholm, Thomas Reid, William Lycan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Mooreanism (common sense)","Foundationalism"],"thinkers":["moore","chisholm","reid"],"position":"common_sense_particularism","tags":["particularism","chisholm","moore","common sense","problem of the criterion","particularism (chisholm)","common-sense philosophy"]},{"term":"Entitlement to trust (Wright)","def":"We have a rational right to trust our basic certainties without evidence, because no inquiry could start otherwise.","more":")” (2004), is a form of hinge epistemology. Wright agreed with the skeptic that we have no evidence that we are not brains in vats, and that Moore’s proof fails to transmit warrant: you cannot use “here is a hand” to gain grounds for “the external world exists”, since you trusted the second in accepting the first. But he argued that we are nonetheless entitled to trust such hinge propositions. An entitlement is a warrant you have without doing any work for it. Held by Crispin Wright.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Hinge epistemology","Pragmatic vindication of induction"],"thinkers":["crispin_wright"],"position":"epistemic_entitlement","tags":["wright","entitlement","hinge epistemology","cornerstones","trust"]},{"term":"Hinge certainties as non-beliefs","def":"Our deepest certainties, like “I have hands”, are not beliefs at all, so they are not the kind of thing that needs evidence.","more":"This reading of Wittgenstein’s On Certainty (1969), defended by Danièle Moyal-Sharrock and in a related form by Duncan Pritchard, holds that hinge certainties are not beliefs or propositions we have found out. They are more like ways of acting: the unquestioned background that shows itself in how we live, rather than something we assert. Moyal-Sharrock speaks of an animal or “primitive” certainty. Since hinges are not beliefs, the skeptic’s demand for evidence for them is confused, like asking for evidence that a ruler is the right length when it is what we measure with. Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein, Danièle Moyal-Sharrock, Duncan Pritchard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Hinge epistemology","Natural belief"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"position":"nonbelief_hinge_epistemology","tags":["wittgenstein","moyal-sharrock","hinges","on certainty","certainty","hinge epistemology"]},{"term":"Pragmatic encroachment","def":"Whether you know something can depend on what is at stake for you if you are wrong.","more":"Pragmatic encroachment, also called interest-relative invariantism, says that practical stakes affect knowledge itself. Two people with the same evidence can differ in whether they know, if one has much more riding on being right. If Hannah has a cheque to deposit and nothing hangs on it, her memory that the bank was open on a recent Saturday gives her knowledge that it is open this Saturday; if a missed deposit would ruin her, the same memory is not enough. Jason Stanley defended the view in Knowledge and Practical Interests (2005), and Jeremy Fantl and Matthew McGrath in Knowledge in an Uncertain World (2009). Also called Interest-relative invariantism, Subject-sensitive invariantism. Held by Jason Stanley, Jeremy Fantl, Matthew McGrath, John Hawthorne.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Pragmatism","Knowledge-first epistemology"],"thinkers":[],"position":"interest_relative_invariantism","tags":["encroachment","stakes","fantl","mcgrath","stanley","knowledge and action","interest-relative invariantism","subject-sensitive invariantism"]},{"term":"Pragmatist theory of truth","def":"True beliefs are the ones that work and would keep working as inquiry goes on.","more":"The pragmatist theory links truth to inquiry and action. Charles Sanders Peirce said the truth is the opinion that everyone who investigated would eventually agree on, if inquiry went on long enough. William James put more stress on usefulness: a true belief is one that “works”, that guides us successfully through experience. John Dewey spoke of “warranted assertibility”, what careful inquiry entitles us to assert. Held by Charles Sanders Peirce, William James, John Dewey, Cheryl Misak.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Knowledge","related":["Coherence theory of truth"],"thinkers":["peirce","james","dewey"],"position":"pragmatist_theory_of_truth","tags":["pragmatism","james","peirce","dewey","truth","usefulness"]},{"term":"Structural realism","def":"Science gets the mathematical structure of the world right, even when it is wrong about what things are.","more":"Structural realism was revived by John Worrall in 1989, drawing on Henri Poincaré, as “the best of both worlds”. It accepts the pessimists’ point that theories about what things are, such as the idea that light is a wave in an ether, have often been thrown out. But it notices that the equations often survive. Augustin Fresnel’s equations for how light reflects and refracts were worked out using the ether theory, yet they reappear unchanged in James Clerk Maxwell’s later theory, in which light is a wave in an electromagnetic field. Held by John Worrall, James Ladyman, Don Ross, Steven French.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Selective realism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["worrall"],"position":"structural_realism","tags":["worrall","structure","scientific realism","pessimistic induction"]},{"term":"Selective realism","def":"We should believe the parts of a theory that actually did the work in its successes, and not the rest.","more":"Selective realism is a realist response to the history of failed theories. Its key idea is that a successful theory is a mixture: some of its parts actually produced its predictions, while others were idle decoration. When old theories were overturned, the idle parts went, but the working parts usually survived. Stathis Psillos called this strategy “divide et impera” (Latin for divide and conquer), and Philip Kitcher distinguished a theory’s “working posits” from its merely “presuppositional posits”. Also called Divide-and-conquer realism, Deployment realism. Held by Stathis Psillos, Philip Kitcher.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Structural realism","Entity realism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["kitcher"],"position":"selective_realism","tags":["divide and conquer","kitcher","psillos","scientific realism","divide-and-conquer realism","deployment realism"]},{"term":"Entity realism","def":"If we can reliably use something as a tool to change other things, it really exists, whatever our theories say about it.","more":"Entity realism moves the focus from theories to experiments. Ian Hacking noticed that physicists in a laboratory do not just theorise about electrons: they spray them onto things, aim them and use them to investigate other, less understood things. When we can manipulate an entity reliably to produce new effects, we have the best possible reason to believe that it exists. But entity realists do not commit themselves to the theories describing such entities, which keep changing: you can be sure electrons exist while doubting the latest theory of what they are. Also called Experimental realism. Held by Ian Hacking, Nancy Cartwright.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Selective realism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["hacking"],"position":"entity_realism","tags":["hacking","cartwright","experimentation","scientific realism","experimental realism"]},{"term":"Convergent realism","def":"Successive theories get closer to the truth, and today’s mature theories are already close.","more":"Convergent realism is the name Larry Laudan gave in 1981 to the classic realist picture defended by Hilary Putnam and Richard Boyd in the 1970s. On this view, the mature theories of a science are approximately true, and later theories keep what was right in earlier ones while correcting their errors, so that the whole sequence converges on the truth. Newton’s theory of gravity was not simply thrown away when Einstein’s arrived: it comes out as an excellent approximation whenever speeds are low and gravity is weak. Convergent realists answer the pessimistic induction by saying that most of the failed theories it cites were immature or made no surprising predictions. Held by Hilary Putnam, Richard Boyd, Ilkka Niiniluoto.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific realism","Truthlikeness view of progress"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"convergent_realism","tags":["approximate truth","scientific progress","truthlikeness","scientific realism"]},{"term":"Scientific anti-realism","def":"We are not entitled to believe that our theories are true about the unobservable world.","more":"Scientific anti-realism is the family of views that deny what scientific realism asserts: that we should believe our best theories are approximately true about unobservable things. Anti-realists usually accept that science is hugely successful and deserves trust about what we can observe. Their doubt concerns the step from success to truth about a hidden world. They point to the history of science, where successful theories of the ether, caloric fluid and phlogiston were later abandoned, and to underdetermination, the fact that rival theories can fit the same evidence. Held by Bas van Fraassen, Ernst Mach, Kyle Stanford.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Constructive empiricism","Instrumentalism"],"thinkers":["van_fraassen","mach"],"position":"scientific_anti_realism","tags":["antirealism","instrumentalism","empiricism","underdetermination","pessimistic induction","scientific realism"]},{"term":"Constructive empiricism","def":"Science aims only to get the observable world right; we can accept a theory without believing what it says about unobservable things.","more":"Constructive empiricism was set out by Bas van Fraassen in The Scientific Image (1980). It says that the aim of science is not truth but empirical adequacy. A theory is empirically adequate if everything it says about observable things (things a human could perceive unaided, in the right conditions) is true. Accepting a theory, on this view, means believing that it is empirically adequate and working with it, not believing that its unobservable entities exist. Held by Bas van Fraassen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific anti-realism","Instrumentalism","Pragmatic theory of explanation"],"thinkers":["van_fraassen"],"position":"constructive_empiricism","tags":["van fraassen","empirical adequacy","observables","anti-realism"]},{"term":"Social constructivism about science","def":"What scientists come to accept is shaped by social factors such as interests, negotiation and authority, not by nature alone.","more":"Social constructivism holds that scientific knowledge is, at least in large part, a social product. Its best-known forms grew out of the sociology of science in the 1970s. David Bloor’s Strong Programme said that true and false scientific beliefs should be explained in the same way, by their social causes, instead of explaining true beliefs by “the evidence” and only false ones by bias. Bruno Latour and Steve Woolgar studied a laboratory the way anthropologists study a village and described how facts are built through instruments, papers and persuasion. Also called Sociology of scientific knowledge, Strong Programme. Held by David Bloor, Barry Barnes, Bruno Latour, Steve Woolgar.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Kuhn’s paradigm view","Contextual empiricism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"social_constructivism","tags":["social constructionism","sociology of science","sssk","construction","relativism","sociology of scientific knowledge","strong programme"]},{"term":"Conventionalism about theory choice","def":"When rival theories fit all the evidence equally well, choosing between them is a matter of convenient convention, not fact.","more":"Conventionalism, associated with the French mathematician Henri Poincaré, says that some parts of science are not forced on us by the world but chosen as conventions because they are simple and convenient. Poincaré’s famous case was geometry. He argued that experiments could never settle whether physical space obeys Euclid’s geometry, because any result could be accommodated by changing our assumptions about how measuring rods and light rays behave. We pick the simplest overall package. Held by Henri Poincaré, Hans Reichenbach.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Constructive empiricism","Instrumentalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"scientific_conventionalism","tags":["conventionalism","poincare","duhem","simplicity","underdetermination","theory choice"]},{"term":"Truthlikeness view of progress","def":"Science progresses when its theories get closer to the truth, even if each of them is false.","more":"The truthlikeness view says scientific progress is a matter of getting closer to the truth. Karl Popper introduced the idea of verisimilitude, or truthlikeness, in Conjectures and Refutations (1963). His insight was that even if every theory we have is strictly false, one false theory can be closer to the truth than another. Einstein’s physics may be wrong somewhere, but it is presumably closer to the truth than Newton’s, and that is why replacing Newton was progress. Also called Verisimilitude view of progress, Semantic view of progress. Held by Karl Popper, Ilkka Niiniluoto, Graham Oddie.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Convergent realism","Falsificationism"],"thinkers":["popper"],"position":"truthlikeness_view_of_progress","tags":["truthlikeness","verisimilitude","popper","niiniluoto","scientific progress","verisimilitude view of progress","semantic view of progress"]},{"term":"Problem-solving view of progress","def":"Science progresses by solving more problems, and more important ones, whether or not it gets closer to the truth.","more":"The problem-solving view says that science progresses when it becomes better at solving problems. Thomas Kuhn, in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962), argued that we may have to give up the idea that changes of theory carry science ever closer to the truth. Progress is real, but it consists in an increasing ability to solve puzzles. Larry Laudan developed this in Progress and Its Problems (1977): a theory is progressive if it solves more empirical problems (explaining observations) and conceptual problems (removing inconsistencies) than its rivals. Held by Thomas Kuhn, Larry Laudan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Kuhn’s paradigm view","Instrumentalism"],"thinkers":["kuhn"],"position":"problem_solving_view_of_progress","tags":["laudan","problem solving","scientific progress","research traditions"]},{"term":"Epistemic view of progress","def":"Science progresses when it adds to our knowledge, not just to our true beliefs or solved problems.","more":"” (2007), says that science progresses when scientific knowledge accumulates. Knowledge is more than true belief: it must be properly grounded, not a lucky guess. So a true hypothesis reached by a careless method is not yet progress; it becomes progress when good evidence turns it into knowledge. This sets the view apart from the truthlikeness view, for which any move toward the truth counts, however it was reached, and from the problem-solving view, for which truth does not matter. Held by Alexander Bird.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific realism","Truthlikeness view of progress"],"thinkers":[],"position":"epistemic_view_of_progress","tags":["bird","knowledge accumulation","scientific progress","epistemic"]},{"term":"Kuhn’s paradigm view","def":"Science works within shared frameworks, called paradigms, that guide normal puzzle-solving until crises lead to revolutions.","more":"In The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962), Thomas Kuhn argued that mature science alternates between two modes. In normal science, a community shares a paradigm: a model achievement, such as Newton’s mechanics, together with the methods, standards and assumptions that come with it. Scientists then solve puzzles the paradigm defines, rather than testing its foundations. Anomalies (results that do not fit) pile up until a crisis, and a revolution replaces the old paradigm with a new one, as when Lavoisier’s oxygen chemistry replaced phlogiston theory. Also called Paradigm theory of science. Held by Thomas Kuhn.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Problem-solving view of progress","Social constructivism about science","Lakatos’s methodology of research programmes"],"thinkers":["kuhn"],"position":"kuhnian_paradigm_view","tags":["kuhn","paradigm","paradigm shift","normal science","scientific revolutions","incommensurability","paradigm theory of science"]},{"term":"Lakatos’s methodology of research programmes","def":"Science should be judged by research programmes over time: a programme is scientific and healthy while it keeps predicting new facts.","more":"Imre Lakatos tried to combine Popper’s concern with rational standards and Kuhn’s respect for history. His unit of assessment is not a single theory but a research programme: a series of theories sharing a hard core of basic assumptions, protected by a belt of auxiliary hypotheses that can be adjusted when problems arise. Newton’s three laws and law of gravity formed the hard core of one famous programme. A programme is progressive if its adjustments lead to novel predictions that are then confirmed, and degenerating if it only patches problems after the fact. Also called Methodology of scientific research programmes. Held by Imre Lakatos.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Falsificationism","Kuhn’s paradigm view","Predictivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"methodology_of_scientific_research_programmes","tags":["lakatos","research programmes","hard core","protective belt","progressive programme","degenerating programme","methodology of scientific research programmes"]},{"term":"Epistemological anarchism","def":"There is no single scientific method: every rule has been broken by good science, so no method should be imposed.","more":"Epistemological anarchism is Paul Feyerabend’s view, set out in Against Method (1975). Feyerabend argued that history shows great scientific advances being made by breaking whatever methodological rule philosophers proposed. His main case was Galileo, who, on Feyerabend’s reading, won acceptance for a moving Earth partly through clever rhetoric and bold assumptions rather than by following accepted standards of evidence (this reading is disputed by historians). The only principle that never hinders progress, he concluded with deliberate provocation, is that anything goes. Also called Methodological anarchism. Held by Paul Feyerabend.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Kuhn’s paradigm view","Scientific pluralism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"epistemological_anarchism","tags":["feyerabend","anything goes","against method","methodological anarchism","pluralism"]},{"term":"Multi-criteria demarcation","def":"Science has no single defining feature; it is marked by a cluster of features that real sciences have far more of than pseudosciences.","more":"In 1983 Larry Laudan argued that every attempt to find a single criterion separating science from pseudoscience had failed, and declared the demarcation problem dead. The multi-criteria view agrees that no single test works, but refuses to give up. Massimo Pigliucci drew on Ludwig Wittgenstein’s idea of family resemblance: members of a family resemble one another in overlapping ways, though no one feature is shared by all. Sciences likewise share a cluster of features, including testable claims, a track record of empirical success, open criticism, links to other sciences, and growing understanding. Also called Family-resemblance view of science, Cluster view of science. Held by Massimo Pigliucci, Paul Thagard.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific pluralism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"multi_criteria_demarcation","tags":["demarcation problem","pseudoscience","science vs pseudoscience","cluster concept","family-resemblance view of science","cluster view of science"]},{"term":"Hypothetico-deductivism","def":"A theory is confirmed when predictions logically deduced from it come true, and refuted when they fail.","more":"Hypothetico-deductivism, often called the hypothetico-deductive method, describes confirmation in three steps: propose a hypothesis, deduce an observable prediction from it (with the help of other assumptions), and test the prediction. If it comes true, the hypothesis is confirmed; if it fails, something in the package is false. Carl Hempel’s textbook example is Ignaz Semmelweis in 1840s Vienna, who tested several hypotheses about why so many mothers on one ward were dying of childbed fever. When he had doctors wash their hands in chlorinated lime, deaths fell, confirming his hypothesis about contamination. Also called Hypothetico-deductive method. Held by William Whewell, Christiaan Huygens.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Falsificationism","Predictivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"hypothetico_deductivism","tags":["h-d method","hypothetico-deductive method","scientific method","confirmation","hempel"]},{"term":"Error statistics","def":"Evidence supports a claim when the claim has passed a severe test, one that would very probably have exposed it if it were false.","more":"Error statistics is the philosophy of evidence developed by Deborah Mayo from the statistical methods of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson. Its central idea is severity. A claim passes a severe test when the test would very probably have given a worse result if the claim were false. Probabilities here describe testing methods, how often they would lead us into error, rather than how likely a hypothesis is. Also called Severe testing. Held by Deborah Mayo, Aris Spanos.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Falsificationism","Predictivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"error_statistics","tags":["mayo","severe testing","severity","frequentism","statistical inference"]},{"term":"The value-free ideal","def":"Moral, social and political values should play no part in judging whether scientific claims are well supported, though they may guide what we study.","more":"The value-free ideal separates different stages of science. Values may properly guide which questions get studied, what is funded and how results are used. But when it comes to judging whether a claim is supported by the evidence, only epistemic values (those concerned with truth, such as accuracy, consistency and simplicity) should count. The sociologist Max Weber argued in “Science as a Vocation” (1919) that a teacher in the lecture hall should present facts and not take sides on political questions. Held by Max Weber, Richard Jeffrey, Gregor Betz, Hugh Lacey.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Bayesianism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"value_free_ideal","tags":["value-free science","objectivity","neutrality","values in science","fact-value distinction"]},{"term":"Inductive risk view","def":"Because accepting a claim always risks error, scientists must use values to decide how much evidence is enough.","more":"The inductive risk view holds that moral and social values have a legitimate role inside science, not just around it. Inductive risk is the chance of being wrong when you accept or reject a claim on incomplete evidence, and evidence is always incomplete. How much evidence is enough depends on what is at stake. Wrongly declaring a safe chemical dangerous costs jobs; wrongly declaring a dangerous chemical safe costs lives. Also called Argument from inductive risk. Held by Richard Rudner, Heather Douglas, C. West Churchman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Contextual empiricism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"inductive_risk_view","tags":["inductive risk","rudner","douglas","values in science","error","argument from inductive risk"]},{"term":"Contextual empiricism","def":"Values enter science through the background assumptions needed to link evidence to theory, so objectivity comes from communities that criticise one another.","more":"Contextual empiricism is Helen Longino’s account of science, set out in Science as Social Knowledge (1990). It starts from a point about evidence: a piece of data, like a fossil or a brain scan, supports a hypothesis only given background assumptions about how the data connect to the hypothesis. Those assumptions cannot all be checked at once, and they can carry the values of the people who hold them. So no individual scientist can be value-free. Also called Feminist contextual empiricism. Held by Helen Longino.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Inductive risk view","Social constructivism about science"],"thinkers":[],"position":"contextual_empiricism","tags":["longino","background assumptions","values in science","social epistemology","feminist contextual empiricism"]},{"term":"Predictivism","def":"A theory earns more support by successfully predicting a fact than by being fitted to facts already known.","more":"Predictivism holds that successful prediction carries special weight. When Dmitri Mendeleev published his periodic table, he left gaps and predicted the properties of undiscovered elements. When gallium was found in 1875 with roughly the properties he had predicted, chemists were far more impressed than they had been by the table’s fit with the elements already known. William Whewell, Karl Popper and Imre Lakatos all gave novel predictions a central place in judging theories. Held by William Whewell, Karl Popper, Imre Lakatos, John Worrall.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Lakatos’s methodology of research programmes","Falsificationism"],"thinkers":["popper","worrall"],"position":"predictivism","tags":["prediction","novel prediction","accommodation","confirmation"]},{"term":"Weak predictivism","def":"Prediction matters only because it is evidence of something else, such as a theory that is not overfitted or a scientist with good judgment.","more":"Weak predictivism agrees that predictions often deserve extra credit, but denies that there is anything special about prediction in itself. A successful prediction matters because it is evidence of some other feature that really bears on truth. Christopher Hitchcock and Elliott Sober (2004) pointed to overfitting: a theory built to fit known data closely may be fitting random noise as well as the real pattern, and successful prediction is good evidence that this has not happened. Eric Barnes (2008) argued that a successful prediction is also evidence that the predictor’s background beliefs are reliable. Held by Christopher Hitchcock, Elliott Sober, Eric Christian Barnes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Predictivism","Bayesianism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"weak_predictivism","tags":["prediction","accommodation","confirmation","predictivism"]},{"term":"Anti-predictivism","def":"Evidence supports a theory equally whether it was predicted in advance or already known; only the fit between theory and evidence matters.","more":"Anti-predictivism holds that the support a theory gets from evidence depends only on the evidence and the theory, not on when the evidence was found or whether it was used to build the theory. John Stuart Mill argued against William Whewell that fulfilled predictions are impressive to the public but add nothing to the logical case for a theory. John Maynard Keynes took a similar line. Many Bayesians agree, since on their account confirmation depends on probabilities, not on history. Held by John Stuart Mill, John Maynard Keynes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Bayesianism"],"thinkers":["mill"],"position":"anti_predictivism","tags":["accommodation","prediction","confirmation"]},{"term":"Humean best-system account of laws","def":"Laws of nature are just the patterns in what happens, summed up in the simplest and most informative way.","more":"Humeans about laws deny that anything forces the world to follow laws. The world is a vast arrangement of particular events, and laws describe its patterns rather than governing them. The view is named after David Hume, whose account of causation inspired it, though whether Hume himself held it is contested. A simple version says a law is any true universal generalisation, but that counts accidents as laws: it may be true that no gold sphere is a mile wide, but it is no law. Also called Mill-Ramsey-Lewis account, Humeanism about laws, Regularity theory of laws. Held by David Lewis, Barry Loewer, Helen Beebee.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Anti-predictivism"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"position":"best_system_account","tags":["best system","lewis","mill-ramsey-lewis","regularity theory","humeanism","laws of nature","mill-ramsey-lewis account","humeanism about laws","regularity theory of laws"]},{"term":"Necessitarian view of laws","def":"Laws are real relations of necessity between properties, which make events follow the patterns they do.","more":"The necessitarian view was proposed independently in 1977 by Fred Dretske and Michael Tooley, and developed most fully by D. M. Armstrong in What Is a Law of Nature? (1983). Also called Dretske-Tooley-Armstrong view, Nomic necessitation view. Held by D. M. Armstrong, Michael Tooley, Fred Dretske.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Primitivism about laws"],"thinkers":["armstrong","tooley"],"position":"necessitarianism_about_laws","tags":["necessitarianism","armstrong","dretske","tooley","nomic necessity","laws of nature","dretske-tooley-armstrong view","nomic necessitation view"]},{"term":"Dispositional essentialism","def":"Laws flow from the essential powers of things: a property just is a power to behave in certain ways, so the laws could not have been different.","more":"Dispositional essentialism says that the basic properties of nature are powers, or dispositions. To have negative charge just is to be disposed to repel other negative charges and attract positive ones; there is no hidden inner nature of charge beyond what it does. Laws of nature describe how these powers show themselves. Since a property’s powers make it the property it is, the laws are necessary: in any world where there is charge, charge behaves as it does here. Also called Powers view of laws, Scientific essentialism. Held by Brian Ellis, Alexander Bird, Sydney Shoemaker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Necessitarian view of laws","Powers theory of causation","Essentialism about modality"],"thinkers":["shoemaker"],"position":"dispositional_essentialism","tags":["powers","bird","ellis","dispositions","laws of nature","powers view of laws","scientific essentialism"]},{"term":"Primitivism about laws","def":"Laws of nature are basic: they govern the world and cannot be reduced to anything else.","more":"Primitivism about laws says that laws of nature are fundamental. They are not summaries of events (as Humeans say), nor relations between universals, nor consequences of powers; they are basic ingredients of reality that govern how the world develops. Tim Maudlin argued that physics itself treats laws this way: a law of motion takes the state of the world at one time and ‘produces’ its later states, and this production cannot be analysed further. John Carroll defended a related view on which lawhood cannot be reduced to non-lawful facts. Also called Governing primitivism. Held by Tim Maudlin, John W. Carroll.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Necessitation view of laws","Necessitarian view of laws"],"thinkers":[],"position":"primitivism_about_laws","tags":["primitivism","maudlin","governing laws","laws of nature","governing primitivism"]},{"term":"Anti-realism about laws","def":"There are, strictly speaking, no laws of nature: what we call laws are idealised models or summaries, not features of the world.","more":"Anti-realism about laws denies that the world contains laws of nature in the sense philosophers argue about. Its defenders reach this conclusion by different routes. Bas van Fraassen, in Laws and Symmetry (1989), argued that every philosophical account of laws fails and that science can do without them, working instead with models and symmetries. Nancy Cartwright, in How the Laws of Physics Lie (1983), argued that fundamental laws are true only in idealised conditions that never occur, so they are false of the messy real world. Also called Lawlessness, Eliminativism about laws. Held by Bas van Fraassen, Nancy Cartwright, Stephen Mumford.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific pluralism","Constructive empiricism"],"thinkers":["van_fraassen","mumford"],"position":"anti_realism_about_laws","tags":["van fraassen","cartwright","how the laws of physics lie","laws of nature","lawlessness","eliminativism about laws"]},{"term":"Autonomy of the special sciences","def":"Everything is physical, but biology, psychology and economics have their own kinds and laws that physics cannot replace.","more":"The autonomy view accepts that everything is made of physical stuff, but denies that higher-level sciences can be reduced to physics. Jerry Fodor called sciences other than basic physics the special sciences, and argued in 1974 that their kinds are multiply realisable: the same higher-level kind can be made of physically very different things. Money can be coins, shells, paper or electronic records, and nothing physical unites them. So a law about money, such as the tendency of bad money to drive out good, cannot be written in the language of physics. Also called Non-reductive physicalism, Anti-reductionism. Held by Jerry Fodor, Hilary Putnam, Philip Kitcher.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific pluralism","Unificationist account of explanation"],"thinkers":["putnam","kitcher"],"position":"autonomy_of_special_sciences","tags":["special sciences","fodor","multiple realisability","non-reductive physicalism","levels","anti-reductionism"]},{"term":"Scientific pluralism","def":"The world is too varied for one unified science: many sciences and models, not reducible to one another, each capture part of it.","more":"Scientific pluralism rejects the picture of the sciences as a single ladder with physics at the bottom. John Dupré, in The Disorder of Things (1993), defended what he called promiscuous realism: there are many equally real ways of dividing the world into kinds, depending on our purposes. A cook and a botanist classify plants differently, and neither is simply wrong. Nancy Cartwright, in The Dappled World (1999), argued that laws hold only in patches, in carefully arranged settings, and that the world is not governed by one seamless system of laws. Also called Disunity of science, Promiscuous realism. Held by John Dupré, Nancy Cartwright.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Anti-realism about laws","Autonomy of the special sciences","Multi-criteria demarcation"],"thinkers":[],"position":"scientific_pluralism","tags":["pluralism","disunity of science","dupre","cartwright","dappled world","unity of science","promiscuous realism"]},{"term":"Deductive-nomological model","def":"To explain something is to show that it was to be expected, by deducing it from laws of nature plus the circumstances.","more":"The deductive-nomological (DN) model was set out by Carl Hempel and Paul Oppenheim in 1948. “Nomological” comes from the Greek nomos, law. On this model, an explanation is a logical argument. Its premises include at least one law of nature and statements of the particular circumstances, and its conclusion is the event to be explained. Also called Covering-law model. Held by Carl Hempel, Paul Oppenheim.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Reductionism","Unificationist account of explanation"],"thinkers":[],"position":"deductive_nomological_model","tags":["d-n model","covering law model","hempel","scientific explanation","laws","covering-law model"]},{"term":"Causal-mechanical model","def":"To explain something is to show the causes and mechanisms that produced it.","more":"The causal-mechanical model says that explaining an event means showing how it fits into the causal structure of the world: which causes produced it, and by what mechanism. Wesley Salmon developed the view in Scientific Explanation and the Causal Structure of the World (1984), describing causal processes, which can carry a change from one place to another, and causal interactions, where processes meet and change each other. This handles the flagpole at once: the pole blocks sunlight and so causes the shadow, while the shadow causes nothing about the pole. Later “new mechanists” such as Peter Machamer, Lindley Darden and Carl Craver argued that explanation in biology and neuroscience describes mechanisms, organised parts and activities producing a phenomenon, such as how a nerve cell fires. Also called Causal account of explanation, Mechanistic explanation. Held by Wesley Salmon, Carl Craver, James Woodward.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Autonomy of the special sciences"],"thinkers":["salmon","woodward"],"position":"causal_mechanical_model","tags":["causal mechanical","salmon","mechanism","scientific explanation","causation","causal account of explanation","mechanistic explanation"]},{"term":"Unificationist account of explanation","def":"To explain something is to fit it into a pattern that covers many different phenomena using a few basic ideas.","more":"Unificationism says that science explains by unifying: by showing that many apparently different phenomena are instances of a few basic patterns. Michael Friedman proposed the idea in 1974, and Philip Kitcher developed it. Newton explained falling apples, the tides and the orbits of the planets with one law of gravity; Darwin explained the fossil record, the geography of species and the anatomy of living things with natural selection and common descent. For Kitcher, the best set of explanations is the one that derives the most facts using the fewest and most stringent argument patterns. Also called Unificationism. Held by Michael Friedman, Philip Kitcher.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Inference to the best explanation","Deductive-nomological model"],"thinkers":["kitcher"],"position":"explanatory_unificationism","tags":["unification","kitcher","friedman","scientific explanation","unificationism"]},{"term":"Pragmatic theory of explanation","def":"An explanation is an answer to a why-question, and what counts as a good one depends on who is asking and why.","more":"The pragmatic theory, defended by Bas van Fraassen in The Scientific Image (1980) and by Peter Achinstein, says that explanation is not a fixed relation between a theory and a fact but an answer to a question asked in a context. Every why-question has a topic, a contrast and a standard of relevance. ” asks something different depending on whether the contrast is Eve, or the pear, or merely looking at the apple. So what counts as a good explanation depends on interests. Held by Bas van Fraassen, Peter Achinstein.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Constructive empiricism"],"thinkers":["van_fraassen"],"position":"pragmatic_theory_of_explanation","tags":["van fraassen","why-questions","pragmatics of explanation","contrastive explanation","scientific explanation"]},{"term":"Copenhagen interpretation","def":"Quantum mechanics is a tool for predicting the results of measurements; asking what happens between measurements has no clear answer.","more":"The Copenhagen interpretation is associated with Niels Bohr and Werner Heisenberg, who shaped quantum theory in the 1920s. It is less a single doctrine than a family of attitudes, and historians note that Bohr and Heisenberg disagreed on important points. The shared core is this. The wave function, the mathematical object of quantum theory, tells us the probabilities of measurement results. Also called Orthodox interpretation. Held by Niels Bohr, Werner Heisenberg.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Instrumentalism","QBism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"copenhagen_interpretation","tags":["copenhagen","bohr","heisenberg","wave function collapse","quantum mechanics","measurement problem","orthodox interpretation"]},{"term":"Many-worlds interpretation","def":"The wave function never collapses: every possible outcome of a measurement happens, each in its own branch of reality.","more":"The many-worlds interpretation began with Hugh Everett III’s 1957 thesis. Everett proposed taking the basic equation of quantum mechanics, the Schrödinger equation, as the whole story: the wave function never collapses. When you measure a particle that is in a superposition (a combination of possibilities) of two states, you and your instrument become part of the superposition too. The result is two branches, in each of which you see one definite outcome. Also called Everett interpretation, Everettian quantum mechanics. Held by Hugh Everett III, Bryce DeWitt, David Deutsch, David Wallace.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"many_worlds_interpretation","tags":["everett","many worlds","mwi","multiverse","quantum mechanics","branching","everett interpretation","everettian quantum mechanics"]},{"term":"Pilot-wave theory","def":"Particles always have definite positions and are guided by the wave function; quantum randomness comes from our ignorance of the exact starting positions.","more":"Pilot-wave theory was first proposed by Louis de Broglie in 1927 and rediscovered and developed by David Bohm in 1952, which is why it is also called de Broglie-Bohm theory or Bohmian mechanics. It says that a quantum system has two parts: particles, which always have definite positions, and the wave function, which guides them like a pilot. In the famous two-slit experiment, each particle goes through one slit only, while its guiding wave passes through both and steers the particle into the striped pattern on the screen. The theory is deterministic. Also called de Broglie-Bohm theory, Bohmian mechanics. Held by Louis de Broglie, David Bohm, Sheldon Goldstein, Detlef Dürr.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"pilot_wave_theory","tags":["de broglie-bohm","bohmian mechanics","pilot wave","hidden variables","quantum mechanics","de broglie-bohm theory"]},{"term":"Objective collapse theories","def":"Superpositions really collapse, through a random physical process that is very rare for single particles but almost instant for large objects.","more":"Objective collapse theories change quantum mechanics itself. They add a new law saying that wave functions spontaneously collapse to definite states at random moments. In the best-known version, proposed by GianCarlo Ghirardi, Alberto Rimini and Tullio Weber in 1986 (GRW), a single particle collapses only very rarely, on average about once in a hundred million years. But a measuring device or a cat contains so many particles that one of them collapses almost immediately, and because the particles are linked, the whole object then settles into one definite state. Also called Spontaneous collapse theories, GRW theory, Dynamical reduction models. Held by GianCarlo Ghirardi, Alberto Rimini, Tullio Weber, Philip Pearle.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["penrose"],"position":"objective_collapse_theory","tags":["grw","spontaneous collapse","collapse theory","penrose","quantum mechanics","measurement problem","spontaneous collapse theories","grw theory","dynamical reduction models"]},{"term":"QBism","def":"The wave function describes an agent’s personal expectations about the results of their own actions on the world, not the world itself.","more":"QBism (originally short for Quantum Bayesianism) was developed by Christopher Fuchs, Rüdiger Schack and Carlton Caves, and later championed by N. David Mermin. It takes the probabilities of quantum mechanics to be Bayesian probabilities: an agent’s personal degrees of belief. The wave function is therefore not a physical object but an agent’s tool for setting expectations about what they will experience when they act on the world, which is what a measurement is. Also called Quantum Bayesianism. Held by Christopher Fuchs, Rüdiger Schack, N. David Mermin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Bayesianism","Copenhagen interpretation"],"thinkers":[],"position":"qbism","tags":["quantum bayesianism","fuchs","subjective probability","quantum mechanics"]},{"term":"Scientific perspectivism","def":"Science tells us about the real world, but always from a particular point of view.","more":"Scientific perspectivism says that every scientific claim is made from a perspective: a set of instruments, models, questions and concepts. A thermal camera and the human eye show the same scene differently, and both show something real. Likewise a fluid model of water and a molecular model of water each get real features right, for different purposes. Perspectivists reject the realist dream of a single “view from nowhere” that describes the world exactly as it is, but they also reject the anti-realist claim that science tells us nothing about unseen reality. Also called Perspectivism. Held by Ronald Giere, Michela Massimi, Nancy Cartwright, Hilary Putnam.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific pluralism","Pragmatism","Entity realism"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"scientific_perspectivism","tags":["perspectivism","giere","massimi","perspective","scientific realism"]},{"term":"Semirealism","def":"Believe in the things science detects through their effects, and in the causal structure they produce, but not in further speculation.","more":"Semirealism is Anjan Chakravartty’s attempt to combine the strengths of entity realism and structural realism. Entity realists believe in things we can manipulate; structural realists believe in the mathematical relations that survive theory change. Chakravartty argued that each needs the other. To manipulate electrons we must know some of their properties, and those properties are what produce the relations described by equations. Held by Anjan Chakravartty.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Entity realism","Structural realism","Selective realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"semirealism","tags":["chakravartty","detection properties","scientific realism"]},{"term":"Epistemic structural realism","def":"Things have hidden natures, but science can know only how they are related, not what they are.","more":"Epistemic structural realism is the version of structural realism set out by John Worrall in 1989, with roots in Henri Poincaré and Bertrand Russell. It says the world contains objects with their own intrinsic natures, but science can know only the structure: the mathematical relations among them. When the ether theory of light was replaced by Maxwell’s field theory, the picture of what light is changed, but Fresnel’s equations survived. That shows, Worrall argued, that science reliably captures structure and not nature. Held by John Worrall, Elie Zahar, Bertrand Russell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Structural realism","Selective realism"],"thinkers":["worrall","russell"],"position":"epistemic_structural_realism","tags":["worrall","structure","structural realism"]},{"term":"Ontic structural realism","def":"Structure is not just all we can know, it is all there is: things are positions in a network of relations.","more":"Ontic structural realism, defended by James Ladyman and Steven French, takes structural realism a step further. Epistemic structural realists say there are hidden natures we cannot know. Ontic structural realists say there is nothing hidden: reality at its most basic is structure. Objects exist, but only as places in a pattern of relations, and they have no identity apart from it. Held by James Ladyman, Steven French, Don Ross.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Structural realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"ontic_structural_realism","tags":["ladyman","french","structure","structural realism"]},{"term":"Inference to the most likely cause","def":"Believe in the causes that explain the effects you see, but not in the high-level laws that summarise them.","more":"Inference to the most likely cause is Nancy Cartwright’s version of entity realism. In How the Laws of Physics Lie (1983) she distinguished two kinds of explanation. Fitting phenomena under general theoretical laws gives no reason to think the laws are true; in fact the most general laws are usually false of real situations, true only of idealised ones. But explaining an effect by citing its cause is different: if you accept the causal explanation, you are committed to the cause existing. Held by Nancy Cartwright.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Entity realism","Anti-realism about laws","Scientific pluralism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"inference_to_the_most_likely_cause","tags":["cartwright","causal inference","entity realism","ibe"]},{"term":"Robustness reasoning","def":"An unseen thing is real when many independent methods, relying on different assumptions, detect it in the same way.","more":"Robustness reasoning says the best evidence for an unseen entity is agreement between independent ways of detecting it. If two microscopes based on entirely different physical principles show the same tiny structure, it would be an incredible coincidence for that structure to be an artefact of both. The classic case is the atom. Around 1908–1913 Jean Perrin calculated Avogadro’s number (the number of molecules in a standard amount of a substance) by many different methods, from Brownian motion to radioactivity and the blue of the sky, and they agreed closely. Held by Wesley Salmon, William Wimsatt, Ian Hacking.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Entity realism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["salmon","hacking"],"position":"robustness_reasoning","tags":["robustness","triangulation","wimsatt","multiple determination","scientific realism"]},{"term":"The empirical stance","def":"Empiricism is a stance, a set of attitudes and values, and rationality permits but does not require belief in unseen things.","more":"The empirical stance is Bas van Fraassen’s later development of his empiricism. In The Empirical Stance (2002) he argued that empiricism should not be a doctrine, such as “all knowledge comes from experience”, because such a doctrine undermines itself: it is not known from experience. Empiricism is instead a stance: a commitment to experience as the touchstone, a suspicion of metaphysical explanation, and an openness to change. This fits his earlier “voluntarist” view of rational belief in Laws and Symmetry (1989): rationality is a matter of permission, not compulsion. Held by Bas van Fraassen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Constructive empiricism","Pragmatism"],"thinkers":["van_fraassen"],"position":"empirical_stance","tags":["van fraassen","empiricism","stance"]},{"term":"Semantic instrumentalism","def":"Statements about unseen things are not true or false: they are calculating devices for getting from observations to predictions.","more":"Semantic instrumentalism is the classic, strong form of instrumentalism. It says that the theoretical parts of science, the talk of atoms, fields and forces, do not describe anything at all. They are rules of inference, like a slide rule or a map’s grid lines: devices that let us move from some observations to predictions about others. So it makes no sense to ask whether electrons “really” exist, any more than to ask whether lines of latitude are painted on the Earth. Held by Ernst Mach, Andreas Osiander.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Instrumentalism","Logical empiricism"],"thinkers":["mach"],"position":"semantic_instrumentalism","tags":["instrumentalism","theoretical terms","anti-realism"]},{"term":"Scientific fictionalism","def":"Theories about unseen things are useful fictions: meaningful, probably false, but valuable to reason with “as if” they were true.","more":"Scientific fictionalism treats theoretical talk the way we treat a good story or an idealised model. Statements about electrons, frictionless planes or perfectly rational economic agents are meaningful and literally true or false, but we need not believe them. We can reason as if they were true because doing so is useful. The idea goes back to Hans Vaihinger’s The Philosophy of “As If” (1911), which argued that much of science, mathematics and ethics consists of deliberate fictions known to be false. Held by Hans Vaihinger.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Instrumentalism","Constructive empiricism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"scientific_fictionalism","tags":["fictionalism","models as fictions","vaihinger","instrumentalism","anti-realism"]},{"term":"Epistemic instrumentalism","def":"Theories really describe unseen things, but history shows we cannot trust them as true, only as reliable tools.","more":"Epistemic instrumentalism, defended by Kyle Stanford in Exceeding Our Grasp (2006), accepts that theories make genuine claims about unobservable reality, but argues that we are not in a position to believe them. Its key argument is the “problem of unconceived alternatives”. Looking at the history of theories of heredity, Stanford found that scientists repeatedly failed to imagine serious rival theories that later replaced theirs, even though those rivals fitted the evidence as well. If this is a recurring pattern, our current theories are probably not the last word either. Held by Kyle Stanford.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Instrumentalism","Scientific anti-realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"epistemic_instrumentalism","tags":["instrumentalism","stanford","unconceived alternatives","anti-realism"]},{"term":"Perspectival realism","def":"Each scientific perspective reveals real features of one world, as different instruments show different real aspects of a scene.","more":"Perspectival realism is the most realist form of perspectivism. It holds that scientific knowledge is always produced from within a perspective, a historically situated practice with its own models, instruments and standards, but that these perspectives reveal real features of a single world. Ronald Giere compared science to colour vision: colours depend on our visual systems, yet colour vision tells us something true about surfaces. Michela Massimi, in Perspectival Realism (2022), argued that the many perspectives in science work together, like an inter-connected map, to tell us about the modal features of the world: what is possible and what is not. Held by Ronald Giere, Michela Massimi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific perspectivism","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"perspectival_realism","tags":["massimi","perspectivism","scientific realism"]},{"term":"Internal realism","def":"There are real facts, but what counts as an object and a fact depends on the concepts we use to describe the world.","more":"Internal realism was Hilary Putnam’s view in Reason, Truth and History (1981). He rejected “metaphysical realism”, the idea of one fixed totality of mind-independent objects and one true description of them from a God’s-eye view. Instead, what objects there are makes sense only within a conceptual scheme. In a later example (1987), he asked how many objects are in a room containing three marbles: three, or seven if you count every combination of marbles as an object. Held by Hilary Putnam.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Scientific perspectivism","Pragmatist theory of truth"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"internal_realism","tags":["putnam","conceptual relativity","pragmatic realism","anti-realism"]},{"term":"The strong programme","def":"The beliefs that scientists accept, true or false, should be explained by the same kinds of social causes.","more":"The strong programme in the sociology of scientific knowledge was developed in Edinburgh in the 1970s by David Bloor and Barry Barnes. Earlier sociologists explained only failures socially: true theories were accepted because of the evidence, while false ones needed social explanation. The strong programme insisted on four principles. Sociology of knowledge should be causal, explaining why beliefs are held; impartial between truth and falsity; symmetrical, using the same types of cause for both; and reflexive, applying to itself. Held by David Bloor, Barry Barnes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Social constructivism about science"],"thinkers":[],"position":"strong_programme","tags":["strong programme","bloor","barnes","symmetry principle","edinburgh school","sociology of science"]},{"term":"Actor-network theory","def":"Scientific facts are built and held in place by networks of people, instruments, organisms and texts.","more":"Actor-network theory was developed from the 1980s by Bruno Latour, Michel Callon and John Law. It began with studies of laboratories, such as Latour and Steve Woolgar’s Laboratory Life (1979), which followed how a claim about a hormone moved from a tentative statement in a lab notebook to an accepted fact. Actor-network theory treats everything that makes a difference to such a process as an “actor”: scientists, but also microbes, instruments, funding bodies and papers. Facts are stable outcomes of these networks. Held by Bruno Latour, Michel Callon, John Law.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Social constructivism about science"],"thinkers":[],"position":"actor_network_theory","tags":["ant","latour","callon","actants","networks","science studies"]},{"term":"Standpoint epistemology","def":"What science asks and notices depends on the social position of those doing it, and drawing on marginalised standpoints makes it more objective.","more":"Standpoint epistemology holds that knowledge is shaped by the social position, or standpoint, of the knower. Developed by feminist thinkers such as the political theorist Nancy Hartsock and the philosophers of science Sandra Harding and Donna Haraway, it argues that when science was done almost entirely by one social group, its questions, assumptions and blind spots reflected that group. Examples often cited include medical research that long studied mainly men, and theories of animal behaviour that cast males as active and females as passive. Starting inquiry from the lives of those outside the dominant group can reveal what was overlooked. Held by Sandra Harding, Donna Haraway, Nancy Hartsock.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Contextual empiricism","Social constructivism about science"],"thinkers":[],"position":"standpoint_epistemology","tags":["standpoint theory","harding","feminist epistemology","situated knowledge","social epistemology"]},{"term":"Subjective Bayesianism","def":"Any starting probabilities are rational as long as they are consistent, and evidence will bring reasonable people together.","more":"Subjective Bayesianism says that probabilities are degrees of belief, and the only constraints on your starting beliefs (your “priors”) are that they obey the rules of probability. Two scientists may begin with very different confidence in a hypothesis and both be rational. What makes the view workable is that, as evidence accumulates and both update by Bayes’s rule, their opinions tend to converge: the evidence “washes out” the priors. Bruno de Finetti and Frank Ramsey laid its foundations, and Colin Howson and Peter Urbach defended it as an account of scientific reasoning. Held by Bruno de Finetti, Frank Ramsey, Colin Howson, Peter Urbach.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Bayesianism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"subjective_bayesianism","tags":["de finetti","personal probability","priors","coherence","bayesianism"]},{"term":"Objective Bayesianism","def":"Starting probabilities are fixed by rational principles, such as spreading belief as evenly as the evidence allows.","more":"Objective Bayesianism agrees that confirmation works by the rules of probability, but denies that any consistent starting point is as good as any other. Rational priors must also respect principles such as calibration (matching known frequencies) and equivocation (spreading belief as evenly as the evidence allows, so as not to assume more than you know). The physicist E. T. Held by E. T. Jaynes, Jon Williamson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Science","related":["Bayesianism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"objective_bayesianism","tags":["maximum entropy","principle of indifference","jaynes","priors","bayesianism"]},{"term":"Priority pluralism","def":"The most basic things are many small parts, and wholes depend on them.","more":"Priority pluralism says that the fundamental things are many: the smallest parts of the world, such as particles or points of a physical field. Bigger things, like molecules, tables and planets, exist, but they depend on their parts: the table is the way it is because its parts are the way they are, not the other way round. Philosophers call this kind of dependence grounding: the parts ground the whole. This is the default picture in much of science, which explains big things by their small parts, and it goes back to the ancient atomists. Also called Atomism about fundamentality. Held by Democritus, David Lewis, Theodore Sider.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Mereological universalism"],"thinkers":["democritus","lewis","sider"],"position":"priority_pluralism","tags":["pluralism","atomism","grounding","fundamentality","parts and wholes","atomism about fundamentality"]},{"term":"Priority monism","def":"The whole universe is the one basic thing, and its parts depend on it.","more":"Priority monism says that the cosmos as a whole is the one fundamental thing, and everything else, including particles, people and planets, exists as a part of it and depends on it. This is not the extreme claim (made by Parmenides, on a common reading) that only one thing exists at all; priority monism agrees that there are many things. Its claim is that the whole comes first in the order of dependence, the way a cake is prior to the slices you cut from it. Jonathan Schaffer revived the view in 2010, arguing that it fits quantum physics better than its rival; some scholars read Spinoza and the British idealists as earlier monists, though those readings are debated. Held by Jonathan Schaffer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Idealism"],"thinkers":["schaffer"],"position":"priority_monism","tags":["schaffer","monism","the one","grounding","fundamentality","holism"]},{"term":"Metaphysical infinitism","def":"There may be no bottom level: everything could depend on something deeper, forever.","more":"Metaphysical infinitism says that reality may have no fundamental level. Molecules depend on atoms, atoms on smaller particles, and perhaps every level depends on a level below it without end, like the old story of a world resting on a turtle that stands on another turtle, ‘turtles all the way down’. Most philosophers assume there must be a foundation, something basic on which everything else rests. Infinitists challenge that assumption. Also called No fundamental level. Held by Ross Cameron, Matteo Morganti.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":[],"thinkers":["cameron_r"],"position":"metaphysical_infinitism","tags":["infinite descent","no fundamental level","gunk","grounding","fundamentality"]},{"term":"Brute facts view","def":"Some facts have no explanation at all; they are just the way things are.","more":"The brute facts view says that explanation must stop somewhere, and where it stops there are brute facts: facts with no explanation, not even one we have yet to find. Perhaps the most basic laws of physics, or the fact that there is a universe at all, are like this. Bertrand Russell put the attitude memorably in his 1948 radio debate with Frederick Copleston, saying in effect that the universe is just there, and that is all. The claim is not a shrug about what science has yet to learn; it is that some facts have no explanation to be found. Also called Rejection of the PSR. Held by Bertrand Russell, David Hume.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Regularity theory of causation"],"thinkers":["russell","hume"],"position":"brute_facts_view","tags":["brute fact","unexplained facts","principle of sufficient reason","explanation","contingency","rejection of the psr"]},{"term":"Growing block theory","def":"The past and present are real, the future is not; reality grows as time passes.","more":"The growing block theory says that past and present things exist, but future things do not yet. Reality is like a block that keeps being added to: each new moment is a fresh slice laid on top, and the newest slice is the present. Your tenth birthday is still part of reality; your thirtieth is not anywhere yet. C. Also called Growing universe theory. Held by C. D. Broad, Michael Tooley, Peter Forrest.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["A-theory of time","Presentism","Eternalism"],"thinkers":["broad","tooley"],"position":"growing_block","tags":["growing block","tooley","broad","passage of time","open future","philosophy of time","growing universe theory"]},{"term":"Moving spotlight theory","def":"All times exist, but one of them is objectively present, and the present moves.","more":"The moving spotlight theory says that all times are real, as eternalists claim, but one time has a special feature: it is objectively present, and that feature moves along from earlier times to later ones, like a spotlight sweeping along a row of houses. It combines the eternalist’s real past and future with a real flow of time. Ross Cameron defended a version in his 2015 book The Moving Spotlight. It is an A-theory of time, since it treats presentness as a real, changing feature of reality, not just a matter of perspective. Held by Ross Cameron.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Eternalism","A-theory of time"],"thinkers":["cameron_r"],"position":"moving_spotlight","tags":["moving spotlight","a-theory","eternalism","passage of time","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Unreality of time","def":"Time is not part of reality as it is in itself; it is an appearance.","more":"This view says that time, with its past, present and future, is not a feature of reality itself. The most famous argument is J. M. E. Also called Temporal anti-realism. Held by J. M. E. McTaggart, Parmenides, F. H. Bradley, Julian Barbour.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":[],"thinkers":["mctaggart","parmenides","bradley"],"position":"unreality_of_time","tags":["mctaggart","idealism","illusion of time","philosophy of time","temporal anti-realism"]},{"term":"A-theory of time","def":"There is an objective present, and time really passes.","more":"The A-theory says that the difference between past, present and future is a real, objective feature of the world, not just a matter of perspective. The name comes from J. M. E. Also called Tensed theory of time, Dynamic theory of time. Held by Arthur Prior, C. D. Broad, William Lane Craig, Ned Markosian.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Presentism","Growing block theory","Moving spotlight theory"],"thinkers":["prior","broad","craig","markosian"],"position":"a_theory_of_time","tags":["a-theory","tensed theory","presentism","passage of time","now","mctaggart","tensed theory of time","dynamic theory of time"]},{"term":"B-theory of time","def":"There is no objective now; time is just events ordered as earlier and later.","more":"The B-theory says that the basic facts about time are facts about which events come earlier or later than others, what McTaggart called the ‘B-series’. These facts never change: the Battle of Hastings is always earlier than the Moon landing. Words like ‘now’, ‘past’ and ‘future’ are like ‘here’ and ‘there’: they describe things from wherever the speaker happens to be. There is no single objective present and no real flow of time. Also called Tenseless theory of time, Static theory of time. Held by Bertrand Russell, J. J. C. Smart, D. H. Mellor, Theodore Sider.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Eternalism","Perdurantism"],"thinkers":["russell","smart","sider"],"position":"b_theory_of_time","tags":["b-theory","tenseless theory","eternalism","block universe","four-dimensionalism","mctaggart","tenseless theory of time","static theory of time"]},{"term":"Counterfactual theory of causation","def":"A caused B when, had A not happened, B would not have happened.","more":"The counterfactual theory says that causation is a kind of dependence. ’ On this view the flip caused the light because the light depended on it in this way. David Lewis gave the classic version in 1973, and later work by James Woodward and the computer scientist Judea Pearl tied it to interventions: X causes Y if changing X, and nothing else, would change Y. The view is Humean in spirit, since it needs no hidden power or force, but it says more than the regularity theory, which counts only patterns of what actually happens. Also called Interventionism (a close relative). Held by David Lewis, James Woodward, Judea Pearl.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Regularity theory of causation","Best system account of laws"],"thinkers":["lewis","woodward"],"position":"counterfactual_theory_of_causation","tags":["counterfactual causation","lewis","counterfactual dependence","causation","difference-making","interventionism (a close relative)"]},{"term":"Regularity theory of causation","def":"Causing is just a regular pattern: things like A are always followed by things like B.","more":"The regularity theory says that when A caused B, the fact behind it is a pattern: events like A are always (or, in later versions, reliably) followed by events like B. There is no hidden glue or force joining cause and effect. David Hume argued that when we look closely at any case of causing, such as one billiard ball striking another, we observe one event, then another, and nothing more; the idea of a necessary connection comes from our habit of expecting the second after the first. ) J. Also called Humean theory of causation. Held by David Hume, John Stuart Mill, J. L. Mackie.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Best system account of laws","Counterfactual theory of causation","Brute facts view"],"thinkers":["hume","mill","mackie"],"position":"regularity_theory_of_causation","tags":["regularity theory","hume","constant conjunction","causation","humeanism","humean theory of causation"]},{"term":"Powers theory of causation","def":"Things have real powers, and causing is those powers at work.","more":"The powers theory says that objects have genuine powers, or dispositions: tendencies to produce effects in certain conditions. Salt is soluble, glass is fragile, a magnet has a pull. These are real features of the things, not just patterns in how they happen to behave. Causation is the exercise of powers, usually several working together: salt dissolves because its solubility and the water’s power to dissolve it meet. Also called Dispositionalism about causation. Held by Aristotle, Stephen Mumford, Nancy Cartwright, Rani Lill Anjum.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Dispositional essentialism","Hylomorphism"],"thinkers":["aristotle","mumford"],"position":"causal_powers_theory","tags":["powers","causal powers","dispositions","mumford","anjum","causation","dispositionalism about causation"]},{"term":"Process theory of causation","def":"Causing is passing something real, such as energy or momentum, from cause to effect.","more":"The process theory, also called the conserved quantity or transference theory, says that causation is a physical connection. A causal process is something like a moving billiard ball that carries a conserved quantity: one that physics says is never created or destroyed overall, such as energy, momentum or electric charge. Causation happens when processes meet and exchange such a quantity, as when the cue ball hands its momentum to the eight ball. Wesley Salmon and Phil Dowe developed the view, which ties causation directly to physics. Also called Conserved quantity theory, Transference theory. Held by Wesley Salmon, Phil Dowe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Physicalism","Powers theory of causation"],"thinkers":["salmon","dowe"],"position":"process_theory_of_causation","tags":["process theory","salmon","dowe","conserved quantity","causation","transference","conserved quantity theory","transference theory"]},{"term":"Causal primitivism","def":"Causation is basic: it cannot be explained in terms of anything simpler.","more":"Causal primitivism says that causation cannot be analysed: no definition of ‘A caused B’ in terms of patterns, counterfactuals, energy transfer or anything else captures it exactly. Causation is one of the basic features of reality, and we grasp it directly, for example when we push or are pushed. Elizabeth Anscombe argued that we learn causal concepts through many specific words, like ‘push’, ‘scrape’, ‘burn’ and ‘knock over’, and that there is no need for a general formula behind them. Michael Tooley argued that causal facts are not fixed by non-causal facts: two worlds could match in all their patterns yet differ in what caused what. Held by G. E. M. Anscombe, Michael Tooley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Powers theory of causation"],"thinkers":["anscombe","tooley"],"position":"causal_primitivism","tags":["primitivism","causation","anscombe","tooley","non-reductive"]},{"term":"Causal eliminativism","def":"There is no causation in basic reality; ‘cause’ is a useful everyday idea, not a feature of physics.","more":"Causal eliminativism says that causation is not part of the basic structure of the world. Bertrand Russell argued in 1913 that the word ‘cause’ plays no role in advanced physics, which describes the world with equations relating quantities at different times, with nothing marked as the cause and nothing as the effect. He called the law of causality ‘a relic of a bygone age’. Modern versions, often called neo-Russellian, accept that causal talk is valuable in everyday life and in sciences like medicine, because it guides action, but deny that it describes anything in fundamental physics. Also called Neo-Russellianism about causation. Held by Bertrand Russell, John D. Norton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Best system account of laws"],"thinkers":["russell"],"position":"causal_eliminativism","tags":["eliminativism","russell","causal anti-realism","causation","neo-russellianism about causation"]},{"term":"Best system account of laws","def":"Laws of nature are the simplest, most informative summaries of what actually happens.","more":"The best system account says that laws of nature do not govern or push the world around; they describe it. Imagine a list of every particular fact about the universe: every particle, everywhere, at every moment. Many systems of true general statements could summarise these facts. The best system is the one with the best balance of simplicity and strength (how much it tells you), and its general statements are the laws. Also called Humeanism about laws, Mill-Ramsey-Lewis view. Held by David Lewis, John Stuart Mill, Frank Ramsey, Barry Loewer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Regularity theory of causation","Counterfactual theory of causation"],"thinkers":["lewis","mill"],"position":"best_system_account_of_laws","tags":["best system account","lewis","humean laws","mill-ramsey-lewis","laws of nature","humeanism about laws","mill-ramsey-lewis view"]},{"term":"Necessitation view of laws","def":"Laws are real relations between properties that make things happen as they do.","more":"The necessitation view says that a law of nature is more than a pattern. When it is a law that F things are G (for instance, that masses attract), there is a real relation of necessitation between the property F and the property G, and this relation produces and explains the pattern. D. M. Also called Armstrong-Dretske-Tooley view, Governing view of laws. Held by D. M. Armstrong, Michael Tooley, Fred Dretske.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Realism about universals","Causal primitivism"],"thinkers":["armstrong","tooley"],"position":"necessitation_view_of_laws","tags":["necessitation","armstrong","dretske","tooley","nomic necessitation","laws of nature","armstrong-dretske-tooley view","governing view of laws"]},{"term":"Realism about universals","def":"Properties like redness are real things that many objects can share at once.","more":"Realism about universals says that when two tomatoes are both red, there is literally one thing they have in common: the universal redness, which is wholly present in each. A universal is a property or relation that can be in many places at once, unlike a particular such as a single tomato. Plato placed universals (his ‘Forms’) outside space and time. Aristotle held, on the usual reading, that they exist only in the things that have them. Held by Plato, Aristotle, Bertrand Russell, D. M. Armstrong.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Necessitation view of laws"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle","russell","armstrong"],"position":"realism_about_universals","tags":["universals","platonism","realism","armstrong","problem of universals","properties"]},{"term":"Trope theory","def":"Properties are real, but each is a particular: this tomato’s redness and that one’s are two exactly similar items.","more":"Trope theory says that properties exist, but they are particulars, not universals. A trope is a particular instance of a property: the specific redness of this tomato, which exists only where this tomato is. Another tomato has its own redness trope, which exactly resembles the first but is a different item. Two things ‘share’ a property when they have exactly resembling tropes. Also called Tropism, Theory of abstract particulars. Held by Donald C. Williams, Keith Campbell, Douglas Ehring.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Bundle theory"],"thinkers":[],"position":"trope_theory","tags":["tropes","particularized properties","abstract particulars","williams","properties","tropism","theory of abstract particulars"]},{"term":"Nominalism about universals","def":"There are only particular things; there are no properties over and above them.","more":"Nominalism, in this debate, says that there are no universals and no tropes either: only particular things. Two tomatoes are both red, but there is no further entity, redness, that they share or that each contains. Nominalists explain resemblance in different ways. Predicate nominalism says things count as red because the word ‘red’ applies to them; class nominalism says being red is belonging to the class of red things; resemblance nominalism says red things are red because they resemble one another in the right way. Also called Nominalism. Held by William of Ockham, W. V. O. Quine, Nelson Goodman, Gonzalo Rodriguez-Pereyra.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":[],"thinkers":["ockham","quine","goodman"],"position":"nominalism_about_universals","tags":["nominalism","problem of universals","particulars","ockham","resemblance nominalism","properties"]},{"term":"Substratum theory","def":"Each object has a bare core, a substratum, that has its properties but is not itself a property.","more":"Substratum theory says that an object is made up of its properties plus something that has them: a substratum, or ‘bare particular’, a core that in itself has no qualities but is what the qualities belong to. The apple’s redness and roundness belong to one thing because both are attached to the same substratum. John Locke described substance as something we know not what that supports qualities, though how far he endorsed the idea is debated. In the 20th century Gustav Bergmann defended bare particulars, and Theodore Sider has argued that they are less strange than they sound. Also called Bare particular theory. Held by John Locke, Gustav Bergmann, Theodore Sider.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":[],"thinkers":["locke","sider"],"position":"substratum_theory","tags":["substratum","bare particulars","locke","substance","bundle theory","bare particular theory"]},{"term":"Restricted composition (common-sense)","def":"Parts make a whole only sometimes, roughly in the cases common sense recognises: chairs, cats and mountains, but not random collections.","more":"This view says that composition (several things together making up one more thing) happens sometimes but not always, and that the cases where it happens are roughly the ones we recognise in ordinary life. The bricks of a house make up a house; the cells of a cat make up a cat; but your left shoe and the Eiffel Tower make up nothing. Defenders offer different rules: the parts must be stuck together, work together, or fall under a kind of thing we have a concept for. Ned Markosian has argued that there may be no simple rule at all, and Daniel Korman has defended common-sense objects against the main arguments that they are arbitrary. Also called Conservatism about composition, Common-sense ontology. Held by Daniel Z. Korman, Ned Markosian.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Hylomorphism"],"thinkers":["korman","markosian"],"position":"restricted_composition","tags":["restricted composition","special composition question","van inwagen","composition","mereology","conservatism about composition","common-sense ontology"]},{"term":"Mereological universalism","def":"Any things whatever make up a whole: even your nose and the Moon.","more":"Mereological universalism (mereology is the study of parts and wholes) says that whenever there are some things, however scattered or unrelated, there is something they make up together, called their fusion or sum. So there is an object made of your left shoe and the Eiffel Tower, and one made of your nose and the Moon. Most of these objects are uninteresting, and ordinary language does not bother to name them, but they exist. David Lewis and Theodore Sider defended the view, partly through the argument from vagueness, and W. Also called Unrestricted composition. Held by David Lewis, Theodore Sider, W. V. O. Quine.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Perdurantism","Priority pluralism"],"thinkers":["lewis","sider","quine"],"position":"mereological_universalism","tags":["universalism","unrestricted composition","mereology","mereological sums","composition"]},{"term":"Mereological nihilism","def":"Parts never make a whole: there are only simple particles arranged in various ways.","more":"Mereological nihilism says that composition never happens. There are simples (things with no parts, such as perhaps fundamental particles), but no composite objects: no tables, rocks or trees, and on the strictest versions no people either. When we say ‘there is a table’, what is strictly true is that there are simples arranged table-wise. Nihilists usually add that ordinary talk is fine for everyday purposes: it is only strictly false, or true in a looser sense. Also called Compositional nihilism. Held by Peter Unger, Cian Dorr.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Eliminativism about ordinary objects","Organicism"],"thinkers":["unger"],"position":"mereological_nihilism","tags":["nihilism","simples","composition","mereology","compositional nihilism"]},{"term":"Identity view (statue = clay)","def":"The statue just is the clay; ‘statue’ and ‘lump’ are two ways of describing one object.","more":"The identity view says there is only one object in the sculptor’s hands: the statue is the clay. How then can the ‘lump’ survive squashing while the ‘statue’ does not? The answer is that claims about what could happen to a thing depend on how we describe it. Allan Gibbard imagined a sculptor who makes a statue’s two halves and joins them, creating statue and lump at the same moment, then destroys both together; he argued that in such a case they are simply identical, and the two names bring different standards for survival. Also called Contingent identity, Monism about material constitution. Held by Allan Gibbard, David Lewis, Harold Noonan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Perdurantism","Modal realism"],"thinkers":["gibbard","lewis"],"position":"constitution_as_identity","tags":["identity","statue and clay","constitution","material constitution","coincidence","contingent identity","monism about material constitution"]},{"term":"Eliminativism about ordinary objects","def":"Strictly, neither the statue nor the lump exists; there are only particles arranged statue-wise.","more":"Eliminativism solves the puzzle by denying that the troublesome objects exist. If there were both a statue and a lump of clay, we would face a hard choice between two things in one place and one thing with conflicting properties. So, eliminativists say, strictly there are neither statues nor lumps: there are only particles (or simples) arranged statue-wise. When the sculptor squashes the clay, the particles are rearranged; nothing is destroyed. Also called Eliminativism (about statues and lumps). Held by Peter van Inwagen, Trenton Merricks, Peter Unger.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Mereological nihilism","Organicism"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen","unger"],"position":"eliminativism_about_ordinary_objects","tags":["eliminativism","ordinary objects","mereological nihilism","material objects","eliminativism (about statues and lumps)"]},{"term":"Ersatzism","def":"Possible worlds exist, but as abstract objects: complete ways the world could have been, not other universes.","more":"Ersatzism says that possible worlds exist, but they are abstract objects, such as complete, consistent descriptions of how everything might be, or complete ways things could be. ‘Ersatz’ means substitute: these worlds are stand-ins, not concrete universes like ours. ‘There could have been talking donkeys’ is true because some consistent complete description includes talking donkeys, not because talking donkeys exist somewhere. Ersatzists are usually actualists: they hold that everything that exists is actual. Also called Actualism about possible worlds, Abstractionism about worlds. Held by Alvin Plantinga, Robert Merrihew Adams, Robert Stalnaker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Modal primitivism"],"thinkers":["plantinga","adams_rm"],"position":"ersatzism","tags":["abstract worlds","possible worlds","actualism","modal realism","actualism about possible worlds","abstractionism about worlds"]},{"term":"Modal primitivism","def":"Possibility and necessity are basic; they do not need possible worlds to explain them.","more":"Modal primitivism says that facts about what could be or must be are basic features of reality. ‘It is possible that there are talking donkeys’ is not made true by anything else, such as other universes or abstract descriptions; ‘possibly’ is a basic notion we understand without analysis. Possible worlds can still be a handy tool, but they are not what possibility consists in. Arthur Prior treated modal words like ‘possibly’ and ‘necessarily’ as basic, just as he treated tense words like ‘will’ and ‘was’ as basic. Also called Modalism. Held by Arthur Prior.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Ersatzism"],"thinkers":["prior"],"position":"modal_primitivism","tags":["primitivism","possibility","necessity","modality","modal","modalism"]},{"term":"Essentialism about modality","def":"What must be so comes from what things essentially are.","more":"This view says that necessity is grounded in essence: what it is to be a certain thing. It is necessary that water is H2O, or that Socrates is human, because being H2O is part of what water is, and being human is part of what Socrates is. Kit Fine argued in 1994 that essence is the more basic notion. Socrates necessarily belongs to the set whose only member is Socrates, yet it is no part of what Socrates is to belong to a set; so essence cannot be defined in terms of necessity, while necessity can be explained by essence. Also called Essence-based modality. Held by Kit Fine, E. J. Lowe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Dispositional essentialism","Hylomorphism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"essentialist_theory_of_modality","tags":["essentialism","fine","necessity","essence","modality","de re modality","essence-based modality"]},{"term":"Modal fictionalism","def":"Talk of possible worlds is a useful story; it is true ‘according to the fiction’, not literally.","more":"Modal fictionalism, proposed by Gideon Rosen in 1990, treats possible worlds the way we treat characters in a novel. Sherlock Holmes does not exist, but according to the stories he lives on Baker Street. Likewise, other possible worlds do not exist, but claims about them can be true according to the fiction of possible worlds (for example, the story told by Lewis’s modal realism). ‘There could have been talking donkeys’ means roughly: according to the possible-worlds story, there is a world with talking donkeys. Held by Gideon Rosen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":[],"thinkers":[],"position":"modal_fictionalism","tags":["rosen","possible worlds","fiction","modality"]},{"term":"Robust metaontological realism","def":"Questions like ‘Do tables exist?’ have deep, objective answers, and we should try to find them.","more":"’ real disagreements with objective answers? Robust realism says yes. There is one best, most basic meaning of ‘exists’ (or ‘there is’), and the world settles which claims using it are true. W. Also called Heavyweight ontology, Neo-Quinean metaontology. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Peter van Inwagen, Theodore Sider.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":[],"thinkers":["quine","vaninwagen","sider"],"position":"metaontological_realism","tags":["robust realism","metaontology","ontology","existence","sider","heavyweight ontology","neo-quinean metaontology"]},{"term":"Carnapian deflationism","def":"Existence questions are either trivial within a language or practical choices about which language to use.","more":"Rudolf Carnap argued in 1950 that existence questions come in two kinds. Internal questions are asked within a linguistic framework: a system of words with rules for using them, such as the language of numbers. ’ is internal, and easily answered by the rules. ’ Carnap held that these have no factual answer; they are disguised practical questions about whether a framework is useful to adopt. Also called Carnap’s internal/external distinction. Held by Rudolf Carnap, Huw Price.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Quantifier variance","Easy ontology"],"thinkers":["carnap"],"position":"carnapian_deflationism","tags":["carnap","deflationism","internal and external questions","frameworks","metaontology","carnap’s internal/external distinction"]},{"term":"Quantifier variance","def":"There are many equally good meanings of ‘there is’, so many ontological disputes are verbal.","more":"Quantifier variance says that words like ‘there is’, ‘exists’ and ‘something’ (what logicians call the quantifier) can be given different meanings, none of which is uniquely correct. A universalist and a nihilist about composition may both be speaking truly, each in their own language: in one, ‘there is an object made of my nose and the Moon’ is true; in the other, false; and both describe the same world. Hilary Putnam suggested the idea in the 1980s, and Eli Hirsch developed it, arguing that many disputes about composition and persistence are merely verbal, and that ordinary English is usually the best language for everyday purposes. The view rejects robust realism’s single privileged ‘exists’, while saying more than Carnap about why the disputants talk past each other. Held by Eli Hirsch, Hilary Putnam.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Carnapian deflationism","Easy ontology"],"thinkers":["hirsch","putnam"],"position":"quantifier_variance","tags":["hirsch","putnam","verbal disputes","metaontology"]},{"term":"Easy ontology","def":"Existence questions are easy: trivial steps from ordinary truths answer them.","more":"Easy ontology, developed by Amie Thomasson, says that most existence questions are not deep at all. From an obvious truth like ‘the cups are on the table’, a trivial step gets you ‘there is a table’. From ‘the shirt is red’ you get ‘the shirt has the property of redness’, so there are properties. From ‘there are five cups’ you get ‘the number of cups is five’, so there are numbers. Also called Neo-Carnapian ontology. Held by Amie Thomasson, Crispin Wright, Bob Hale.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Carnapian deflationism","Quantifier variance"],"thinkers":["crispin_wright"],"position":"easy_ontology","tags":["thomasson","deflationism","existence","metaontology","neo-carnapian ontology"]},{"term":"Neo-Aristotelian metaphysics","def":"Whether numbers or tables exist is easy; the deep question is what is fundamental and what depends on what.","more":"Neo-Aristotelian metaphysics shifts the focus of ontology. Quine asked what exists, and treated that as the hard question. Neo-Aristotelians such as Jonathan Schaffer and Kit Fine think existence questions are mostly easy: of course there are numbers, tables, holes and people. The real work is to say how these things are ordered: which are fundamental, and which exist only because of, or “in virtue of”, others. Also called Grounding metaphysics. Held by Jonathan Schaffer, Kit Fine, Aristotle, E. J. Lowe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Priority monism","Priority pluralism","Hylomorphism"],"thinkers":["schaffer","aristotle"],"position":"neo_aristotelian_metaphysics","tags":["neo-aristotelian","grounding","schaffer","fundamentality","metaphysics","grounding metaphysics"]},{"term":"Ontological deflationism","def":"Disputes about what “really” exists are not deep: once the facts are agreed, the rest is a choice of words.","more":"Ontological deflationism says that many famous debates in metaphysics are not about the world at all. Suppose two philosophers agree on where every particle is and how they are arranged, but one says there is a table and the other says there are only particles arranged table-wise. Deflationists hold that nothing in the world decides between them. ” asked outside any way of talking is a practical question: which framework of words is most useful? Also called Metaontological deflationism, Dismissivism. Held by Rudolf Carnap, Eli Hirsch, Hilary Putnam.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Carnapian deflationism","Quantifier variance","Easy ontology"],"thinkers":["carnap","hirsch","putnam"],"position":"ontological_deflationism","tags":["deflationism","verbal disputes","existence","metaontology","metaontological deflationism","dismissivism"]},{"term":"Quinean ontology","def":"Believe in exactly what your best scientific theories need to say exists.","more":"Quinean ontology follows W. V. O. Quine’s method. Also called Neo-Quinean ontology. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Hilary Putnam.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Robust metaontological realism","Platonism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["quine","putnam"],"position":"quinean_ontology","tags":["quine","on what there is","ontological commitment","quine's criterion","existence","neo-quinean ontology"]},{"term":"Common-sense ontology","def":"Ordinary beliefs about what exists, such as that there are tables and people, outweigh clever arguments against them.","more":"Common-sense ontology treats our ordinary beliefs about what there is as strong evidence. You are more certain that there are chairs, cats and people than you are of any premise in an argument that there are none. So when an argument concludes that there are no tables, or that your shoe and the Eiffel Tower make up an object, something has gone wrong with the argument. The approach goes back to G. Also called Moorean ontology, Conservatism about objects. Held by G. E. Moore, Daniel Z. Korman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Restricted composition (common-sense)","Common-sense particularism"],"thinkers":["moore","korman"],"position":"commonsense_ontology","tags":["common sense","ordinary objects","existence","ontology","moorean ontology","conservatism about objects"]},{"term":"Ontological austerity","def":"Accept only what the simplest theory that explains everything requires, even if common sense must give way.","more":"Ontological austerity puts simplicity first. Following the principle known as Ockham’s razor, it says we should not multiply entities beyond necessity: if everything can be explained with fewer kinds of thing, believe only in those. W. V. Also called Ockhamism in ontology, Desert-landscape ontology. Held by Theodore Sider, W. V. O. Quine, Cian Dorr.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Mereological nihilism","Nominalism about universals","Robust metaontological realism"],"thinkers":["sider","quine"],"position":"ontological_austerity","tags":["ockham's razor","parsimony","simplicity","austerity","ontology","ockhamism in ontology","desert-landscape ontology"]},{"term":"Epistemic dismissivism","def":"Many existence disputes are real, but we have no way of finding out who is right.","more":"Epistemic dismissivism, which Karen Bennett calls “epistemicism”, is a middle path. Unlike deflationists, it holds that disputes such as whether the statue and the clay are two things, or whether any two objects make up a third, are about real facts. But it holds that for many of these disputes we are in no position to know the answer. Bennett argued that in some debates the rival sides are evenly matched: each view that posits fewer objects has to say things that sound just as odd as the view that posits more, and the arguments on each side can be translated into the other’s terms. Also called Epistemicism about ontology. Held by Karen Bennett.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Robust metaontological realism","Ontological deflationism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"epistemic_dismissivism","tags":["bennett","dismissivism","metaontology","epistemic humility","existence","epistemicism about ontology"]},{"term":"Neo-Fregeanism","def":"Numbers exist because principles like “the number of Fs equals the number of Gs if and only if they match one to one” define them.","more":"Neo-Fregeanism, defended by Crispin Wright and Bob Hale, revives Gottlob Frege’s idea that numbers are objects we can know about through logic and definitions. Its key tool is an “abstraction principle”. Hume’s principle says that the number of Fs is the same as the number of Gs if and only if the Fs and the Gs can be paired off one to one. If there are as many knives as plates on a table, then by this principle the number of knives is the number of plates, so there is something, a number, that both have. Also called Neo-logicism, Abstractionism. Held by Crispin Wright, Bob Hale, Gottlob Frege.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Easy ontology","Platonism about mathematics","Logicism"],"thinkers":["crispin_wright","frege"],"position":"neo_fregeanism","tags":["hale","wright","hume's principle","abstraction","platonism","numbers","neo-logicism","abstractionism"]},{"term":"Pleonastic entities","def":"Properties, propositions and similar things exist, but they are “thin”: they have no nature beyond what our ways of talking give them.","more":"Stephen Schiffer introduced the idea of pleonastic entities, from a Greek word meaning “redundant”. Some ways of talking let us move from a statement with no reference to a thing to one that refers to it, adding nothing: from “Lassie is a dog” to “Lassie has the property of being a dog”, or from “snow is white” to “that snow is white is true”. Schiffer called these “something-from-nothing transformations”. The entities they introduce, such as properties, propositions and fictional characters, really exist, but there is nothing more to them than what the transformations settle. Also called Lightweight properties. Held by Stephen Schiffer, Amie Thomasson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Easy ontology","Nominalism about universals"],"thinkers":[],"position":"pleonastic_entities","tags":["pleonastic","schiffer","easy ontology","properties","propositions","lightweight properties"]},{"term":"Philosophical quietism","def":"Questions like “Do tables really exist?” come from misunderstandings of how our words work, and should be dissolved, not answered.","more":"Quietism in metaphysics is the view that the big questions of ontology arise from confusions about language, and that the right response is to show how the confusion arises rather than to give an answer. It is most associated with Ludwig Wittgenstein’s later work. In Philosophical Investigations (1953) he wrote that philosophical problems arise when language “goes on holiday”: we take a word like “exists” or “object” out of the everyday activities that give it meaning and ask it questions in a vacuum. The quietist does not claim that tables are unreal, nor that “there are tables” is true in one framework and false in another. Also called Wittgensteinian quietism. Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Carnapian deflationism","Ontological deflationism"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"position":"philosophical_quietism","tags":["quietism","wittgenstein","therapeutic philosophy","dissolving problems","metaphilosophy","wittgensteinian quietism"]},{"term":"Deflationism about time","def":"The debate between presentists and eternalists is not a real disagreement about the world.","more":"Deflationism about time says that the famous debate over whether only the present exists, or all times, is empty or merely verbal. Ask a presentist and an eternalist whether dinosaurs exist. If “exist” means “exist now”, both say no. If it means “exist at some time or other”, both say yes. Held by Ulrich Meyer, Yuval Dolev.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Ontological deflationism","Eternalism","Presentism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"deflationism_about_time","tags":["deflationism","presentism","eternalism","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Primitive direction of time","def":"Time has a basic, built-in direction from past to future, and that is what its passing is.","more":"This view, defended by Tim Maudlin in The Metaphysics Within Physics (2007), accepts that all times exist, but denies that time is just like space. Space has no preferred direction: left to right is no different from right to left. Time, Maudlin argues, has an intrinsic direction, from earlier to later, that is part of the basic structure of the world rather than something that emerges from the way entropy (disorder) increases. The laws of physics produce later states from earlier ones, not the reverse. Held by Tim Maudlin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Eternalism","Primitivism about laws"],"thinkers":[],"position":"primitive_direction_of_time","tags":["direction of time","arrow of time","maudlin","time asymmetry","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Lucretianism","def":"The present world has past-tensed properties, such as having once contained Caesar crossing the Rubicon, and these make past truths true.","more":"Lucretianism is a presentist answer to the question of what makes truths about the past true. John Bigelow (in “Presentism and Properties”, 1996) took the idea from the Roman poet Lucretius, who said that past events are “accidents” of the lands and peoples where they happened. On this view, although Caesar no longer exists, the world as it is now has the property of having once contained Caesar crossing the Rubicon. That present property is what makes “Caesar crossed the Rubicon” true. Held by John Bigelow.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Presentism","Ersatz presentism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"lucretianism","tags":["presentism","truthmakers","past","bigelow","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Ersatz presentism","def":"Past times are represented by abstract, presently existing descriptions of how things were, which make past truths true.","more":"Ersatz presentism borrows an idea from the metaphysics of possibility. Many philosophers think possible worlds are not real universes but abstract descriptions of ways things could be. Ersatz presentists, such as Craig Bourne and Thomas Crisp, treat past times the same way: an “ersatz time” is an abstract object, such as a maximal set of propositions, that describes how the whole world was at some moment. Abstract objects exist now, so the presentist can accept them. Held by Craig Bourne, Thomas Crisp.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Presentism","Ersatzism","Lucretianism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"ersatz_presentism","tags":["presentism","abstract times","truthmakers","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Presentism without truthmakers","def":"Truths about the past need nothing that exists now to make them true; they are true because of how things were.","more":"Some presentists reject the demand for “truthmakers”, things whose existence guarantees that a truth is true. Trenton Merricks argued in Truth and Ontology (2007) that the demand fails in many places, such as for negative truths (“there are no unicorns”) and for truths about what would have happened. For the past, he argues, “Caesar crossed the Rubicon” is true simply because Caesar did cross the Rubicon. Nothing present, whether a property or an abstract description, is needed to make it true. Held by Trenton Merricks, Arthur Prior.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Presentism"],"thinkers":["prior"],"position":"presentism_without_truthmakers","tags":["presentism","truthmaker objection","truthmakers","past","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Anti-realism about the past","def":"A statement about the past is true only if there is, or could be, present evidence for it.","more":"Anti-realism about the past holds that the truth of past-tense statements depends on what evidence for them exists or could exist now. If every trace of some event has vanished, so that nothing anyone could ever find would show whether it happened, then the statement that it happened is neither true nor false. Michael Dummett set out the view, as a consequence of his general anti-realism about meaning, in “The Reality of the Past” (1969). He argued that we learn the meaning of past-tense sentences by learning what counts as evidence for them, so their meaning cannot outrun all possible evidence. Held by Michael Dummett.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Presentism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"anti_realism_about_the_past","tags":["anti-realism","dummett","past","verificationism","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Dead-past growing block","def":"The past is still real, but only the present edge of the growing block is alive with consciousness.","more":"The dead-past view is a version of the growing block theory, on which the past and present exist but the future does not yet. It answers a sharp objection. ” (2004), how anyone in a growing block could know they are at its present edge. The block contains countless past people, all thinking “this is now”, and all wrong, since they are not at the edge. Held by Peter Forrest.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Growing block theory"],"thinkers":[],"position":"dead_past_view","tags":["growing block","dead past","present","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Eleatic monism","def":"Reality is one, unchanging and timeless; change, motion and time are illusions of the senses.","more":"Eleatic monism takes its name from Elea, the Greek city in southern Italy where Parmenides taught early in the fifth century BC. In his poem, a goddess shows him the “Way of Truth”: what is cannot come from what is not, nor pass into it, so what is cannot be born, perish or change. Reality is one, whole and unmoving, and the many changing things we perceive belong to the “Way of Opinion”, a mere appearance. His follower Zeno of Elea devised his famous paradoxes, such as the arrow and Achilles and the tortoise, to show that belief in motion and plurality leads to absurdity; Melissus of Samos defended a similar view. Also called Parmenidean monism. Held by Parmenides, Zeno of Elea, Melissus of Samos.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Unreality of time"],"thinkers":["parmenides","zeno_elea"],"position":"eleatic_monism","tags":["parmenides","zeno","eleatics","the one","monism","illusion of change","parmenidean monism"]},{"term":"Platonism about universals","def":"Properties exist outside space and time, and can exist even if nothing has them.","more":"Platonism about universals holds that properties such as redness, circularity or justice are real entities that exist independently of the things that have them. They are not located anywhere, they do not change, and they would exist even if nothing in the world were red or circular. Particular things “participate in” or “instantiate” them. The view descends from Plato’s theory of Forms: in the Symposium, the climb of love leads from beautiful bodies to Beauty itself, which is not in any body. Also called Transcendent realism, Ante rem realism. Held by Plato, Bertrand Russell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Realism about universals","Platonism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["plato","russell"],"position":"platonism_about_universals","tags":["platonism","plato","forms","theory of forms","universals","abstract objects","transcendent realism","ante rem realism"]},{"term":"Aristotelian realism about universals","def":"Properties are real and shared, but exist only in the things that have them.","more":"Aristotelian realism, also called immanent realism, agrees with Platonism that two red tomatoes literally share one property, redness. But it holds that redness exists only in red things: it is wholly present in each tomato, and if nothing were red, there would be no redness at all. Aristotle argued against Plato that forms do not exist apart from things. In modern times D. Also called Immanent realism, In re realism. Held by D. M. Armstrong, Aristotle.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Realism about universals","Necessitation view of laws"],"thinkers":["armstrong","aristotle"],"position":"aristotelian_realism_about_universals","tags":["aristotle","immanent realism","armstrong","universals","in rebus","properties","in re realism"]},{"term":"Resemblance nominalism","def":"Red things are red because they resemble one another, and resemblance is a basic fact needing no shared property.","more":"Resemblance nominalism explains what things have in common without universals. A tomato is red, on this view, not because it has a shared entity, redness, but because it resembles other red things, such as fire engines and ripe cherries, in the right way. Resemblance between particular things is taken as basic: it is not explained by anything further. The view has a long history, and its best-known modern defence is Gonzalo Rodriguez-Pereyra’s Resemblance Nominalism (2002), which replies in detail to the classic objections raised by Bertrand Russell and Nelson Goodman. Held by Gonzalo Rodriguez-Pereyra, H. H. Price.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Nominalism about universals","Trope theory"],"thinkers":[],"position":"resemblance_nominalism","tags":["rodriguez-pereyra","resemblance","nominalism","universals"]},{"term":"Ostrich nominalism","def":"That a tomato is red needs no explanation in terms of properties or resemblance: it is just red.","more":"Ostrich nominalism is a name D. M. Armstrong gave, in Universals and Scientific Realism (1978), to W. V. Also called Austere nominalism. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Michael Devitt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Nominalism about universals","Quinean ontology"],"thinkers":["quine"],"position":"ostrich_nominalism","tags":["quine","devitt","nominalism","universals","austere nominalism"]},{"term":"Necessitarianism","def":"Everything that is the case had to be the case: nothing could have been otherwise.","more":"Necessitarianism holds that there is no real contingency: every truth is necessary. Things could not have happened in any other way. The most famous necessitarian is Baruch Spinoza, who wrote in his Ethics (1677) that in nature nothing is contingent, and that things could not have been produced by God in any other way or order. Our belief that things could have gone differently, on this view, comes from ignorance of their causes. Also called Spinozism about modality. Held by Baruch Spinoza, Michael Della Rocca.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Metaphysics","related":["Principle of sufficient reason"],"thinkers":["spinoza"],"position":"necessitarianism","tags":["spinoza","modal collapse","necessity","fatalism","spinozism about modality"]},{"term":"Platonism about mathematics","def":"Numbers and other mathematical objects really exist, outside space and time, and mathematicians discover truths about them.","more":"Platonism says that mathematics is about abstract objects: things like numbers, sets and functions that exist but are not anywhere in space, do not change, and cause nothing. The number 7 is not a mark on paper or an idea in your head; it would exist even if no one had ever counted. On this view mathematicians are explorers, not inventors. When Euclid proved that there are infinitely many primes, he found out something that was already true. Also called Mathematical realism, Mathematical platonism. Held by Plato, Gottlob Frege, Kurt Gödel, Alonzo Church.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Object platonism","Ante rem structuralism","Mathematical intuition","Indispensability argument"],"thinkers":["plato","frege","godel"],"position":"platonism_about_mathematics","tags":["mathematical platonism","abstract objects","frege","godel","numbers","realism","mathematical realism"]},{"term":"Intuitionism","def":"Mathematics is a creation of the human mind: a mathematical object exists only if it can be constructed, and a statement is true only if it can be proved.","more":"Intuitionism, founded by the Dutch mathematician L. E. J. Brouwer, says that mathematics is a mental activity of building, or constructing, objects step by step. Also called Mathematical constructivism. Held by L. E. J. Brouwer, Michael Dummett, Arend Heyting.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Intuitionistic logic"],"thinkers":["brouwer","dummett"],"position":"mathematical_intuitionism","tags":["brouwer","constructivism","heyting","mental construction","philosophy of mathematics","mathematical constructivism"]},{"term":"Logicism","def":"Mathematics, or at least arithmetic, is really logic: its truths follow from logical laws plus definitions.","more":"Logicism is the claim that mathematics can be reduced to logic. Gottlob Frege tried to define numbers using only logical ideas: roughly, the number 2 is what all pairs have in common, defined through one-to-one matching between collections. With such definitions in place, the truths of arithmetic would follow from logical laws alone, so knowing maths would be as secure as knowing logic. Frege’s system turned out to be inconsistent: in 1902 Bertrand Russell wrote to Frege pointing out a contradiction in it (Russell’s paradox). Also called Neo-logicism (modern version). Held by Gottlob Frege, Bertrand Russell, Crispin Wright, Bob Hale.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Platonism about mathematics","Conventionalism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["frege","russell","crispin_wright"],"position":"logicism","tags":["frege","russell","principia mathematica","foundations of mathematics","neo-logicism (modern version)"]},{"term":"Aristotelian realism about mathematics","def":"Mathematics studies real properties of physical things, such as quantity, ratio and symmetry.","more":"Aristotelian realism agrees with platonism that mathematics is about something real, but denies that it is a separate realm of abstract objects. Instead, maths studies properties and relations that physical things actually have. A heap of three apples really has a numerical property; a plate really is round; a snowflake really has six-fold symmetry. Mathematicians abstract these features (they consider them apart from the stuff that has them) and study them in general. Also called Aristotelianism about mathematics. Held by Aristotle, James Franklin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Empiricism about mathematics","Modal structuralism"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"position":"aristotelian_realism_about_mathematics","tags":["aristotelian realism","franklin","quantity","philosophy of mathematics","realism","aristotelianism about mathematics"]},{"term":"Fictionalism about mathematics","def":"There are no numbers, so mathematics is strictly false, but it is a useful story, true “according to” the story of maths.","more":"Fictionalism takes mathematical statements at face value: “there is a prime between 10 and 15” really does claim that a certain object exists. But fictionalists think there are no abstract objects, so they conclude that such statements are, strictly speaking, not true. They are true only within the story of mathematics, the way “Sherlock Holmes lived on Baker Street” is true in Conan Doyle’s stories. Why do science and engineering use maths so successfully, then? Also called Mathematical nominalism (one form). Held by Hartry Field, Mary Leng.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Formalism"],"thinkers":["field","leng"],"position":"mathematical_fictionalism","tags":["mathematical fictionalism","field","nominalism","numbers","philosophy of mathematics","mathematical nominalism (one form)"]},{"term":"Mathematical intuition","def":"We grasp basic mathematical truths directly, by a kind of rational insight.","more":"On this view, we know the starting points of mathematics, its axioms, because we can simply see that they are true when we understand them, much as we see colours with our eyes. This “seeing” is rational intuition: a non-sensory awareness of what must be the case. Kurt Gödel was its most famous defender. In 1964 he wrote that the axioms of set theory “force themselves upon us as being true” and compared this to sense perception. Also called Rational intuition. Held by Kurt Gödel, Laurence BonJour.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Platonism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["godel","bonjour"],"position":"mathematical_intuition","tags":["godel","rational insight","a priori","intuition","rational intuition"]},{"term":"Indispensability argument","def":"We should believe in mathematical objects because our best scientific theories cannot do without them.","more":"The indispensability argument, associated with W. V. O. Quine and Hilary Putnam, says we know mathematics in the same way we know about electrons. Also called Quine-Putnam indispensability argument. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Hilary Putnam, Mark Colyvan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Platonism about mathematics","Quine’s rejection of analyticity"],"thinkers":["quine","putnam"],"position":"indispensability_argument","tags":["quine-putnam","indispensability","mathematical platonism","quine","putnam","numbers","quine-putnam indispensability argument"]},{"term":"Conventionalism about mathematics","def":"Mathematical truths are true because of the meanings we have given to mathematical words.","more":"Conventionalism says that mathematical truths are analytic: true purely in virtue of meaning, like “all bachelors are unmarried”. We fix the meanings of “2”, “+” and “=” by adopting rules, and “2 + 2 = 4” follows from those rules. That is why it can be known without looking at the world and why no experience could ever refute it. The logical positivists, especially A. Also called Linguistic conventionalism. Held by A. J. Ayer, Rudolf Carnap.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["The analytic/synthetic distinction","Logicism","Formalism"],"thinkers":["ayer","carnap"],"position":"mathematical_conventionalism","tags":["conventionalism","analytic truth","carnap","truth by convention","philosophy of mathematics","linguistic conventionalism"]},{"term":"Empiricism about mathematics","def":"We learn mathematical truths from experience, as very well-confirmed generalisations about the world.","more":"Mathematical empiricism says that our knowledge of arithmetic and geometry ultimately comes from experience, like our knowledge of physics, only better confirmed. John Stuart Mill argued that we learn “2 + 1 = 3” by observing that whenever two things and one thing are put together they make three. These generalisations have been confirmed so often that we cannot imagine them failing, which makes them feel necessary. Philip Kitcher later gave a modern version: mathematical knowledge began with simple manipulations of physical objects and has been passed down and extended through a long tradition. Also called Millian empiricism. Held by John Stuart Mill, Philip Kitcher.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Aristotelian realism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["mill","kitcher"],"position":"mathematical_empiricism","tags":["mill","empiricism","mathematical empiricism","induction","philosophy of mathematics","millian empiricism"]},{"term":"Ante rem structuralism","def":"Mathematics is about structures, and numbers are simply positions in those structures.","more":"Structuralism says that mathematics studies structures, not individual objects. The number 2 has no inner nature: all there is to being 2 is coming after 1, before 3, and so on. It is a place in the natural-number structure, the way “vice-president” is an office in an organisation, whoever fills it. “Ante rem” means “before the things”: on this version, defended by Stewart Shapiro, structures exist in their own right, whether or not anything physical has that structure. Also called Non-eliminative structuralism. Held by Stewart Shapiro, Michael Resnik.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Platonism about mathematics","Modal structuralism"],"thinkers":["shapiro"],"position":"ante_rem_structuralism","tags":["shapiro","structuralism","positions in structures","philosophy of mathematics","non-eliminative structuralism"]},{"term":"Object platonism","def":"Numbers are individual abstract objects, each with its own identity, not mere positions in a pattern.","more":"Object platonism is the traditional form of mathematical platonism. Each number is a particular abstract object. The number 2 is a definite thing, and statements about it are about that thing, just as statements about Mars are about a particular planet. Frege held a view like this: he argued that numbers are “self-subsistent objects”, picked out by expressions such as “the number of Fs”. Held by Gottlob Frege, Crispin Wright, Bob Hale.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Platonism about mathematics","Logicism"],"thinkers":["frege","crispin_wright"],"position":"object_platonism","tags":["platonism","abstract objects","numbers","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Modal structuralism","def":"Maths is about what would be true in any possible system with a given structure, without assuming that structures exist.","more":"Modal structuralism agrees that mathematics is about structure, but denies that structures or numbers exist as objects. Instead, a statement like “2 + 3 = 5” is understood as saying: in any possible system with the structure of the natural numbers, the thing in the “2” position plus the thing in the “3” position gives the thing in the “5” position. The word “modal” refers to possibility: mathematics talks about what is possible and necessary, not about what actually exists. Hilary Putnam suggested this in 1967, and Geoffrey Hellman developed it fully in 1989. Also called Eliminative structuralism. Held by Hilary Putnam, Geoffrey Hellman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Ante rem structuralism","Aristotelian realism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"modal_structuralism","tags":["hellman","structuralism","possibility","philosophy of mathematics","eliminative structuralism"]},{"term":"Deflationism about truth","def":"There is no deep nature of truth: saying “it is true that snow is white” says no more than “snow is white”.","more":"Deflationism says that the word “true” does not name a substantial property that needs a theory. Saying “‘snow is white’ is true” comes to the same as saying “snow is white”. The truth predicate is a useful logical device, mainly for agreeing (“that’s true”) and for generalising over things we cannot list (“everything the witness said was true”). Frank Ramsey gave an early version; Quine described truth as “disquotation” (removing the quotation marks); Paul Horwich’s “minimalism” says that all there is to truth is captured by the instances of the scheme “the proposition that p is true if and only if p”. Also called Minimalism about truth, Disquotationalism. Held by Frank Ramsey, W. V. O. Quine, Paul Horwich, Hartry Field.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Use theory of meaning","Inferentialism"],"thinkers":["quine","field"],"position":"deflationism_about_truth","tags":["deflationary theory of truth","minimalism","redundancy theory","disquotationalism","horwich","truth","minimalism about truth"]},{"term":"Truth pluralism","def":"There is more than one way of being true: what makes a sentence true depends on what it is about.","more":"Truth pluralism says that truth may consist in different things in different areas of discourse. For claims about physical objects, truth may be correspondence with the world. For claims in mathematics, ethics or comedy, it may be something else, such as provability, coherence or what Crispin Wright called “superassertibility” (being justified and staying justified however much more we learned). There is still a single concept of truth, defined by its general features (true statements are correct to assert, truth is what belief aims at), but different properties play that role in different areas. Also called Alethic pluralism. Held by Michael P. Lynch, Crispin Wright.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Logical pluralism","Correspondence theory of truth","Coherence theory of truth"],"thinkers":["lynch","crispin_wright"],"position":"truth_pluralism","tags":["alethic pluralism","lynch","wright","truth","pluralism"]},{"term":"Classical logic","def":"The correct logic is classical logic, in which every statement is true or false and no contradiction is ever true.","more":"Classical logic is the standard logic taught in universities and used in most of mathematics. It was developed by Gottlob Frege, Bertrand Russell and others around 1900. Two of its laws are especially important. The law of excluded middle says that for any statement, either it or its denial is true. Also called Logical monism (classical). Held by Gottlob Frege, W. V. O. Quine, Timothy Williamson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Epistemicism","Tarskian hierarchy"],"thinkers":["frege","quine","williamson"],"position":"classical_logic","tags":["bivalence","excluded middle","boolean logic","first-order logic","logic","logical monism (classical)"]},{"term":"Intuitionistic logic","def":"The correct logic drops the law that every statement is true or false, and requires proof of what we assert.","more":"Intuitionistic logic was developed by Arend Heyting in 1930 from Brouwer’s philosophy of mathematics. Its key difference from classical logic is that it does not accept the law of excluded middle (“either p or not-p”) in general. In intuitionistic logic, to assert “p or q” you must be able to prove p or prove q, and to assert that something exists you must be able to show how to find it. Proving that the denial of p leads to contradiction does not prove p. Held by L. E. J. Brouwer, Michael Dummett, Arend Heyting.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Intuitionism","Inferentialism"],"thinkers":["brouwer","dummett"],"position":"intuitionistic_logic","tags":["constructive logic","excluded middle","brouwer","heyting","logic"]},{"term":"Paraconsistent logic","def":"The correct logic does not let a contradiction prove everything.","more":"In classical logic, from a contradiction anything follows: if you accept both “p” and “not-p”, you can prove “the moon is cheese”. This is called explosion. A paraconsistent logic is any logic in which explosion fails, so a contradiction stays contained and does not wreck the whole system. Paraconsistent logics were developed by Newton da Costa in Brazil, and by Richard Routley (later Sylvan), Graham Priest and others in Australia. Held by Graham Priest, Newton da Costa, Richard Routley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Dialetheism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"paraconsistent_logic","tags":["paraconsistency","explosion","priest","dialetheism","contradiction","logic"]},{"term":"Logical pluralism","def":"There is more than one correct logic.","more":"” has more than one right answer. JC Beall and Greg Restall argued that validity means truth-preservation in all “cases”, but that there are several legitimate ways of understanding a case: complete and consistent possible worlds (giving classical logic), constructions that may be incomplete (giving intuitionistic logic), or situations that may be incomplete or inconsistent (giving relevant logic). Each gives a genuine and correct notion of validity. Rudolf Carnap held an earlier version through his “principle of tolerance”: we are free to choose any logic, provided we state its rules clearly. Held by JC Beall, Greg Restall, Rudolf Carnap.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Truth pluralism"],"thinkers":["carnap"],"position":"logical_pluralism","tags":["beall","restall","pluralism","logic"]},{"term":"Tarskian hierarchy","def":"Truth for a language must be defined in a higher “metalanguage”, so no sentence can say of itself that it is false.","more":"Alfred Tarski showed in 1933 that a language containing its own truth predicate, plus ordinary logic, will produce the liar paradox. His solution was to divide language into levels. An “object language” talks about the world. A “metalanguage” talks about the object language and contains the predicate “true in the object language”. Also called Hierarchy of languages. Held by Alfred Tarski, Bertrand Russell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Classical logic"],"thinkers":["russell"],"position":"tarskian_hierarchy","tags":["tarski","object language","metalanguage","liar paradox","truth","hierarchy of languages"]},{"term":"Gap theory","def":"The liar sentence is neither true nor false: it falls into a truth-value gap.","more":"Gap theories say that the liar sentence, “this sentence is false”, has no truth value: it is neither true nor false. The reasoning that leads to paradox assumes it must be one or the other. Saul Kripke’s 1975 theory is the best-known version. He imagined building up the truth predicate in stages. Also called Kripke’s fixed-point theory (main version). Held by Saul Kripke, Tim Maudlin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Supervaluationism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"truth_value_gap_theory","tags":["truth-value gaps","kripke","liar paradox","gaps","truth","kripke’s fixed-point theory (main version)"]},{"term":"Dialetheism","def":"Some contradictions are true: the liar sentence is both true and false.","more":"Dialetheism (from Greek for “two-way truth”) says that some statements are both true and false. A true contradiction is called a dialetheia. Graham Priest, its leading defender, argues that the liar sentence is the clearest example: the reasoning that it is true if false and false if true is sound, and the conclusion is simply that it is both. To keep this from leading to absurdity, dialetheists use a paraconsistent logic, in which a contradiction does not imply everything. Held by Graham Priest, JC Beall.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Paraconsistent logic"],"thinkers":[],"position":"dialetheism","tags":["priest","true contradictions","liar paradox","paraconsistency","contradiction"]},{"term":"Revision theory of truth","def":"The liar shows that truth is a circular concept, settled by repeatedly revising guesses, and the liar never settles.","more":"The revision theory, developed by Anil Gupta, Hans Herzberger and Nuel Belnap, says that “true” is a circular concept: what counts as true depends partly on what counts as true. Such concepts can be understood through a process of revision. Start with a guess about which sentences are true. Use it to work out a new list, then use that list to work out another, and so on. Held by Anil Gupta, Nuel Belnap, Hans Herzberger.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Classical logic"],"thinkers":[],"position":"revision_theory_of_truth","tags":["revision theory","gupta","belnap","liar paradox","truth"]},{"term":"Degree theory","def":"Truth comes in degrees: “this is a heap” can be true to degree 0.7.","more":"Degree theories say that vague statements can be partly true. Instead of only true (1) and false (0), a statement can have any degree of truth in between. As grains of sand are added, “this is a heap” becomes gradually truer, from 0 to 1, with no sharp jump. The sorites premise “if n grains are not a heap, then n + 1 grains are not a heap” is very nearly true for each n, but not perfectly true, and tiny losses of truth add up over many steps. Also called Fuzzy logic approach. Held by Nicholas J. J. Smith, Kenton Machina, Lotfi Zadeh.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Logical pluralism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"degree_theory_of_vagueness","tags":["degrees of truth","fuzzy logic","vagueness","sorites paradox","heap","fuzzy logic approach"]},{"term":"Contextualism about vagueness","def":"Where the boundary of a vague word falls shifts with context, so we never find it where we look.","more":"Contextualist theories say that vague words are sensitive to context, and the context shifts as we consider cases. When you compare two neighbouring heaps of sand, one grain apart, you treat them alike, because they look the same; so the boundary is never located between the two cases you are attending to. It is always somewhere else. Delia Graff Fara argued that “heap” means something like “significantly larger than normal for the current purposes”, and that whatever pair of cases we focus on, the standard shifts so that they come out the same. Held by Delia Graff Fara, Diana Raffman, Stewart Shapiro.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Supervaluationism"],"thinkers":["shapiro"],"position":"contextualism_about_vagueness","tags":["contextualism","vagueness","sorites paradox","shapiro","raffman"]},{"term":"Nihilism about vagueness","def":"The sorites shows that vague words like “heap” apply to nothing at all; strictly, there are no heaps.","more":"Nihilism about vagueness accepts the sorites argument as sound and draws its conclusion. If no single grain can turn a non-heap into a heap, and one grain is not a heap, then there are no heaps at all. Since almost all ordinary words, like “table”, “person” and “bald”, are vague, Peter Unger concluded in 1979 that there are no tables, no stones, and in a sense no people. Samuel Wheeler argued for related conclusions. Also called Sorites nihilism. Held by Peter Unger, Samuel Wheeler.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":[],"thinkers":["unger"],"position":"nihilism_about_vagueness","tags":["nihilism","vagueness","sorites paradox","unger","heap","sorites nihilism"]},{"term":"Truth-conditional semantics","def":"To know the meaning of a sentence is to know under what conditions it would be true.","more":"Truth-conditional semantics says that the meaning of a sentence is given by its truth conditions: the way the world would have to be for it to be true. To understand “the cat is on the mat” is to know that it is true if and only if a certain cat is on a certain mat. The meanings of words are their contributions to the truth conditions of sentences they appear in: names pick out objects, predicates pick out properties, and so on. The idea goes back to Frege and the early Wittgenstein, who wrote in the Tractatus that to understand a proposition is to know what is the case if it is true. Held by Gottlob Frege, Donald Davidson, David Lewis.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Correspondence theory of truth"],"thinkers":["frege","lewis"],"position":"truth_conditional_semantics","tags":["truth conditions","davidson","frege","semantics","meaning","formal semantics"]},{"term":"Use theory of meaning","def":"The meaning of a word is its use in the language.","more":"The use theory says that what a word means is a matter of how it is used: the practices, situations and purposes in which speakers employ it. The later Wittgenstein wrote that for a large class of cases the meaning of a word is its use in the language. He compared language to a collection of “language games”: giving orders, telling jokes, describing, praying, each with its own rules. Words are like tools, with many different jobs, not just labels for things. Also called Meaning as use. Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein, Paul Horwich.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Inferentialism","Deflationism about truth","Impossibility of a private language"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"position":"use_theory_of_meaning","tags":["meaning as use","wittgenstein","language games","use","ordinary language","meaning"]},{"term":"Intention-based semantics","def":"Meaning comes from what speakers intend to communicate; word meaning rests on those intentions.","more":"Intention-based semantics, from Paul Grice, explains meaning by starting with speakers rather than with words. For a speaker to mean something by an utterance is, roughly, to intend to produce a belief in a listener by getting the listener to recognise that very intention. If I wave a key at you, I mean “let’s go” if I intend you to think it is time to go by recognising that I want you to think so. Word and sentence meanings are then explained as stable conventions built up from such communicative intentions. Also called Gricean semantics. Held by Paul Grice, Stephen Schiffer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Truth-conditional semantics"],"thinkers":[],"position":"intention_based_semantics","tags":["grice","speaker meaning","intentionalism","pragmatics","meaning","gricean semantics"]},{"term":"Inferentialism","def":"The meaning of a word is its role in reasoning: what follows from using it, and what it follows from.","more":"Inferentialism says that what a word means is fixed by its role in inference, that is, by which claims it lets you draw from it and which entitle you to it. To understand “red” is to know, among other things, that from “this is red” you may infer “this is coloured”, and that you may not also say “this is green all over”. To understand “and” is to know that from “p and q” you may infer p. Wilfrid Sellars started the view, and Robert Brandom developed it at length in Making It Explicit. Also called Inferential role semantics. Held by Robert Brandom, Wilfrid Sellars.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Use theory of meaning","Intuitionistic logic","Deflationism about truth"],"thinkers":[],"position":"inferentialism","tags":["brandom","inferential role","meaning","conceptual role","inferential role semantics"]},{"term":"Descriptivism about names","def":"A name refers to whoever fits the descriptions that speakers associate with it.","more":"Descriptivism says that a proper name works like a hidden description. “Aristotle” means something like “the teacher of Alexander the Great who wrote the Nicomachean Ethics”, and it refers to whoever fits that description. Frege held that names have a “sense”, a way of presenting their bearer, and that this explains why “Hesperus is Phosphorus” (two names for Venus) can be informative while “Hesperus is Hesperus” is not. Russell held that ordinary names are “disguised definite descriptions”. Also called Description theory of names, Frege-Russell view. Held by Gottlob Frege, Bertrand Russell, John Searle.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Causal descriptivism","Truth-conditional semantics"],"thinkers":["frege","russell","searle"],"position":"descriptivist_theory_of_names","tags":["descriptivism","frege","russell","description theory of names","proper names","reference","frege-russell view"]},{"term":"Causal-historical theory of reference","def":"A name refers to whoever it was first given to, passed down through a chain of people using it.","more":"The causal-historical theory, set out by Saul Kripke in lectures in 1970 (published in 1972, and as the book Naming and Necessity in 1980), says that names refer not through descriptions but through history. A name is introduced in an initial “baptism”: someone points at a baby and says “let’s call her Ada”. The name is then passed from speaker to speaker, each intending to use it as the person they learned it from did. Your use of “Aristotle” refers to Aristotle because it stands at the end of a long chain going back to him, even if everything you believe about him is false. Also called Causal theory of names, Kripke’s picture. Held by Saul Kripke, Hilary Putnam, Michael Devitt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Natural kind realism"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"causal_historical_theory_of_reference","tags":["causal theory of reference","kripke","naming and necessity","baptism","rigid designation","reference","causal theory of names","kripke’s picture"]},{"term":"Causal descriptivism","def":"Names refer through descriptions, but those descriptions mention the causal chain itself.","more":"Causal descriptivism tries to combine the strengths of both main theories. It agrees with Kripke that reference depends on causal chains of communication, and agrees with Frege that names are associated with descriptions. The trick is that the relevant description is itself causal: “Aristotle” refers to “the person who stands at the start of the chain of uses that led to my use of ‘Aristotle’”. David Lewis and Frank Jackson defended versions, and Frederick Kroon gave the name. Held by David Lewis, Frank Jackson, Frederick Kroon.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Descriptivism about names","Causal-historical theory of reference"],"thinkers":["lewis","jackson"],"position":"causal_descriptivism","tags":["jackson","lewis","reference","descriptivism"]},{"term":"Impossibility of a private language","def":"A language that only one person could ever understand, about their own private sensations, is impossible.","more":"In Philosophical Investigations (1953), Wittgenstein argued that there could not be a “private language”: a language whose words refer to the speaker’s own private sensations, so that no one else could understand it. In his diary example, someone writes “S” whenever a certain sensation occurs. But what makes a later use of “S” correct? Only that it seems right to them. Also called Private language argument (standard reading). Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein, Norman Malcolm, P. M. S. Hacker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Use theory of meaning","Communitarian view of meaning"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"position":"impossibility_of_private_language","tags":["private language argument","wittgenstein","beetle in a box","rule-following","language","private language argument (standard reading)"]},{"term":"Communitarian view of meaning","def":"Meaning and rule-following only exist within a community whose members can check each other.","more":"The communitarian view goes further than the standard reading of Wittgenstein. It says that following a rule, and so meaning anything, requires an actual community of speakers. Saul Kripke presented this reading in Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language (1982), explaining Wittgenstein’s argument as it struck him without claiming that it was Wittgenstein’s own view or fully endorsing it. Kripke’s sceptic asks: what fact about you makes it true that by “plus” you meant addition and not some odd function that agrees with addition for all the sums you have done so far? Also called Kripkenstein’s sceptical solution, Community view. Held by Crispin Wright.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Impossibility of a private language","Use theory of meaning"],"thinkers":["crispin_wright"],"position":"communitarian_view_of_meaning","tags":["community view","kripke","wittgenstein","rule-following","meaning","language","kripkenstein’s sceptical solution"]},{"term":"Possibility of a private language","def":"A person could have words for their own private sensations; Wittgenstein’s argument fails.","more":"This position denies that Wittgenstein showed a private language to be impossible. A. J. Ayer argued in 1954 that a solitary person could perfectly well name a private sensation and recognise it again, using memory, just as we rely on memory everywhere. Held by A. J. Ayer, Simon Blackburn.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Descriptivism about names"],"thinkers":["ayer","blackburn"],"position":"possibility_of_private_language","tags":["private language","private language argument","wittgenstein","sensations","language"]},{"term":"The analytic/synthetic distinction","def":"Some statements are true just because of what their words mean (analytic); others depend on the facts (synthetic).","more":"This position defends the traditional distinction. An analytic statement, like “all bachelors are unmarried”, is true in virtue of meaning: once you understand the words you can see it must be true. A synthetic statement, like “all bachelors are untidy”, depends on how the world is and must be checked by experience. Kant drew a version of this distinction, and the logical positivists made it central. Also called Defence of analyticity. Held by Rudolf Carnap, A. J. Ayer, Paul Grice, P. F. Strawson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Conventionalism about mathematics","Epistemic analyticity"],"thinkers":["carnap","ayer","pfstrawson","chalmers"],"position":"analytic_synthetic_distinction","tags":["analytic-synthetic distinction","analytic truth","synthetic truth","kant","meaning","necessity","defence of analyticity"]},{"term":"Quine’s rejection of analyticity","def":"There is no sharp line between truths of meaning and truths of fact; every belief can in principle be revised.","more":"In “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951), W. V. O. Quine argued that the analytic/synthetic distinction cannot be drawn in a clear, non-circular way. Also called Confirmation holism, Two Dogmas view. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Gilbert Harman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Indispensability argument","Empiricism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["quine","harman"],"position":"rejection_of_analyticity","tags":["two dogmas","quine","analyticity","holism","web of belief","confirmation holism","two dogmas view"]},{"term":"Epistemic analyticity","def":"No sentence is made true by meaning alone, but some can be known just by understanding them.","more":"This middle position comes from Paul Boghossian’s “Analyticity Reconsidered” (1996). Boghossian separated two ideas of analyticity. “Metaphysical” analyticity says a sentence is made true by its meaning, so the world plays no part. He agreed with Quine that this is confused: “all bachelors are unmarried” is true because all bachelors are, in fact, unmarried; meaning only fixes what the sentence says. Also called Moderate analyticity. Held by Paul Boghossian.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["The analytic/synthetic distinction","Inferentialism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"epistemic_analyticity","tags":["boghossian","analyticity","a priori","meaning","moderate analyticity"]},{"term":"Natural kind realism","def":"Nature comes divided into real kinds, like gold, water and tigers, which our best categories discover.","more":"Natural kind realism says that some of our categories “carve nature at its joints”, to borrow Plato’s image. Gold, water, electrons and tigers are not just groups we find handy; they are real kinds, grouped by nature itself. Kripke and Putnam argued that such kinds have hidden essences: water is H₂O, and gold is the element with atomic number 79. We name a kind by pointing to examples, and science discovers what they share. Also called Essentialism about kinds. Held by Saul Kripke, Hilary Putnam, Brian Ellis, Richard Boyd.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Causal-historical theory of reference"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"natural_kind_realism","tags":["natural kinds","realism","essentialism","kripke","putnam","carving nature at its joints","essentialism about kinds"]},{"term":"Pragmatism about kinds","def":"Nature contains many real similarities, but which groupings count as kinds depends on our purposes.","more":"Pragmatism about kinds takes a middle path. The world is not a formless blob: there are real similarities and causal patterns. But there are many ways of grouping things that track real patterns, and which ones we use depends on our purposes. A cook, a botanist and a farmer may all classify plants differently, and each classification can be correct for its job. Also called Promiscuous realism, Pluralism about kinds. Held by John Dupré, P. D. Magnus.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Natural kind realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"pragmatism_about_kinds","tags":["natural kinds","pragmatism","dupre","classification","kinds","promiscuous realism","pluralism about kinds"]},{"term":"Conventionalism about kinds","def":"Kinds are drawn by us: our categories reflect human choices and language more than nature’s divisions.","more":"Conventionalism says that the boundaries of our categories are set by human decisions and conventions. Nature contains things with many properties, but which of them we use to sort things into kinds is up to us. John Locke argued that the “sorts” we name are “the workmanship of the understanding”: we form abstract ideas, which he called “nominal essences”, and group things by them. Even if things have hidden “real essences”, those are not what our kind words track. Also called Conventionalism about classification. Held by John Locke, Nelson Goodman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Nominalism about kinds","Pragmatism about kinds"],"thinkers":["locke","goodman"],"position":"conventionalism_about_kinds","tags":["conventionalism","natural kinds","classification","social construction","kinds","conventionalism about classification"]},{"term":"Nominalism about kinds","def":"Only individual things exist; a kind is nothing over and above the particular things we group under a name.","more":"Nominalism (from Latin nomen, “name”) says that there are no universals or kinds existing in their own right. There is this tiger and that tiger, but no extra entity, “tigerhood” or “the kind tiger”, that they share. A general word like “tiger” applies to many particulars, but that is a fact about words and the particular things, not about an extra object. William of Ockham was a famous medieval nominalist, and Hobbes held that nothing in the world is universal but names. Also called Nominalism about universals. Held by William of Ockham, Thomas Hobbes, Gonzalo Rodriguez-Pereyra.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Conventionalism about kinds"],"thinkers":["ockham","hobbes"],"position":"nominalism_about_kinds","tags":["nominalism","natural kinds","classification","kinds","nominalism about universals"]},{"term":"Weak linguistic relativity","def":"The language you speak influences how you think, perceive and remember, without limiting what you can think.","more":"Weak linguistic relativity says that language shapes thought in measurable but limited ways. Speakers of different languages tend to attend to, remember and categorise things somewhat differently, because their languages make some distinctions habitual. Speakers of Guugu Yimithirr, an Australian language that describes locations with compass directions rather than “left” and “right”, keep track of north and south remarkably well, as Stephen Levinson’s team found. Research on colour terms suggests that having separate words for light and dark blue, as Russian does, can speed up telling the shades apart. Also called Weak Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. Held by John A. Lucy, Lera Boroditsky, Stephen C. Levinson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Strong linguistic relativity"],"thinkers":[],"position":"weak_linguistic_relativity","tags":["sapir-whorf","whorfian hypothesis","linguistic relativity","language and thought","weak sapir-whorf hypothesis"]},{"term":"Linguistic universalism","def":"Human thought is basically the same everywhere; languages differ on the surface but do not shape how we think.","more":"Universalism says that all humans share the same basic concepts and ways of thinking, rooted in a common biology. Languages differ in words and grammar, but these differences are mainly in how thoughts are expressed, not in the thoughts themselves. Noam Chomsky argued that all human languages share a deep “universal grammar”, an innate capacity for language. Steven Pinker argued in The Language Instinct that we think in a “language of thought” (“mentalese”) that is the same for everyone, and that the strong Sapir-Whorf hypothesis is wrong. Also called Universalism about language and thought. Held by Noam Chomsky, Steven Pinker, Brent Berlin, Paul Kay.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":[],"thinkers":["chomsky","pinker"],"position":"linguistic_universalism","tags":["universal grammar","chomsky","language of thought","pinker","language and thought","universalism about language and thought"]},{"term":"Strong linguistic relativity","def":"Your language determines how you think: speakers of very different languages live in different mental worlds.","more":"Strong linguistic relativity, also called linguistic determinism, says that the language you speak sets the limits of the concepts you can form and fixes your view of the world. It is associated with the linguist Edward Sapir and his student Benjamin Lee Whorf, so it is often called the “Sapir-Whorf hypothesis”. Whorf argued, for example, that the Hopi language lacks tense in the European sense and that its speakers therefore understand time differently. Scholars disagree about whether Whorf himself held the strong version or something closer to weak relativity, so the label is partly a later construction. Also called Linguistic determinism, Strong Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. Held by Benjamin Lee Whorf.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Weak linguistic relativity"],"thinkers":[],"position":"strong_linguistic_relativity","tags":["linguistic determinism","sapir-whorf","whorf","linguistic relativity","language and thought","strong sapir-whorf hypothesis"]},{"term":"Verificationist theory of meaning","def":"To know what a sentence means is to know what would show it to be true.","more":"The verificationist theory ties meaning to evidence. A sentence means what it does because of what would count as establishing it: an observation, a test, a proof. To understand “the kettle is boiling” is to know how to check it. The logical positivists of the Vienna Circle (Moritz Schlick, Rudolf Carnap, A. Also called Verificationism, Assertibility-conditional semantics. Held by Moritz Schlick, Rudolf Carnap, A. J. Ayer, Michael Dummett.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Semantic anti-realism (Dummett)","Logical empiricism","Intuitionistic logic"],"thinkers":["schlick","carnap","ayer","dummett","crispin_wright"],"position":"verificationist_theory_of_meaning","tags":["verificationism","verification principle","logical positivism","ayer","meaning","assertibility-conditional semantics"]},{"term":"Meaning skepticism","def":"There is no fact of the matter about what our words mean, beyond what speakers say and do.","more":"Meaning skepticism holds that the facts about how people speak and behave do not settle what their words mean, and that there is nothing else to settle it. W. V. O. Also called Semantic skepticism, Meaning indeterminacy. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Saul Kripke.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Indeterminacy of translation (Quine)","Kripke’s skeptical solution","Quine’s rejection of analyticity"],"thinkers":["quine"],"position":"meaning_skepticism","tags":["kripkenstein","kripke","rule-following","indeterminacy","meaning","semantic skepticism","meaning indeterminacy"]},{"term":"Fregean semantics (sense and reference)","def":"Words have a sense, a way of presenting what they stand for, as well as a reference.","more":"Gottlob Frege, in “On Sense and Reference” (1892), argued that the meaning of an expression has two parts. Its reference (Bedeutung) is what it stands for: the planet Venus, for “the morning star”. Its sense (Sinn) is the way it presents that thing: as the bright body seen in the morning. “The morning star” and “the evening star” share a reference but differ in sense, which is why “the morning star is the evening star” can be news. Also called Fregeanism, Sense and reference. Held by Gottlob Frege, Michael Dummett, Gareth Evans, John McDowell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Truth-conditional semantics","Descriptivism about names"],"thinkers":["frege","dummett","chalmers"],"position":"fregean_semantics","tags":["frege","sense and reference","sinn","bedeutung","morning star evening star","meaning","fregeanism"]},{"term":"Davidsonian semantics","def":"A theory of meaning for a language is a theory of truth for it that an interpreter could use.","more":"Donald Davidson, in “Truth and Meaning” (1967), proposed that we can do without meanings as entities altogether. Instead, a theory of meaning for a language should be a theory of truth in Alfred Tarski’s style: a finite set of rules about words that entails, for every sentence, a statement such as “‘Schnee ist weiss’ is true if and only if snow is white”. Such a theory shows how the truth conditions of infinitely many sentences depend on a finite vocabulary. What makes the theory correct for a community is that an interpreter using it could make the best sense of what its speakers say, treating them as mostly rational and mostly right about the world (the principle of charity). Held by Donald Davidson, John McDowell, Ernest Lepore.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Truth-conditional semantics","Indeterminacy of translation (Quine)"],"thinkers":[],"position":"davidsonian_semantics","tags":["davidson","truth-conditional semantics","radical interpretation","tarski","meaning"]},{"term":"Russellian semantics (direct reference)","def":"A sentence expresses a structured proposition made of the very objects and properties it is about.","more":"On Russellian semantics, the proposition expressed by “Venus is bright” contains Venus itself, the planet, together with the property of being bright. Names and indexicals such as “I” and “here” contribute their referents directly, with no intervening sense: this is the direct reference theory. Bertrand Russell held this for a few “logically proper names”; David Kaplan, Nathan Salmon and Scott Soames extended it to ordinary names. Because the planet contributes itself whichever name you use, “Hesperus is Phosphorus” and “Hesperus is Hesperus” express the same proposition. Also called Direct reference theory, Millianism, Structured propositions. Held by Bertrand Russell, David Kaplan, Nathan Salmon, Scott Soames.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Truth-conditional semantics","Causal-historical theory of reference"],"thinkers":["russell"],"position":"russellian_semantics","tags":["russell","direct reference","millianism","structured propositions","singular propositions","reference","direct reference theory"]},{"term":"Possible-worlds semantics","def":"The meaning of a sentence is the set of possible situations in which it would be true.","more":"Possible-worlds semantics takes the truth-conditional idea literally. Think of all the ways the world could have been, called possible worlds. The meaning (intension) of a sentence is a function from each world to true or false, or simply the set of worlds where it is true. The meaning of a predicate like “red” picks out the red things in each world. Also called Intensional semantics, Montague semantics. Held by Rudolf Carnap, David Lewis, Richard Montague, Robert Stalnaker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Truth-conditional semantics"],"thinkers":["carnap","lewis"],"position":"possible_worlds_semantics","tags":["possible worlds semantics","intensional semantics","montague","lewis","stalnaker","meaning","montague semantics"]},{"term":"Wittgensteinian quietism about meaning","def":"Meaning is shown in our many language games, and needs description, not a theory.","more":"The later Wittgenstein held that philosophy should not build theories of meaning at all. In the Philosophical Investigations (1953) he described language as a motley of “language games”: giving orders, describing, joking, praying, greeting. Their meanings lie open to view in how we use words in the stream of life, which he called a “form of life”. Philosophical puzzles about meaning arise when we look for something hidden, such as a mental image, an abstract object or a rule that interprets itself, behind ordinary use. Also called Therapeutic Wittgensteinianism. Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein, John McDowell, P. M. S. Hacker.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Use theory of meaning","Impossibility of a private language"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"position":"quietism_about_meaning","tags":["wittgenstein","quietism","language games","meaning as use","meaning","therapeutic wittgensteinianism"]},{"term":"Regularity use theory (Horwich)","def":"Each word’s meaning is the basic regularity in how speakers accept sentences containing it.","more":"Paul Horwich turned Wittgenstein’s slogan that meaning is use into a systematic theory, in Meaning (1998). For each word there is a basic regularity in its use that explains all the rest. For “red”, it is roughly the tendency to accept “that is red” when clearly seeing something red; for “and”, the tendency to accept “p and q” just when accepting both p and q. A word’s meaning simply is its having that basic acceptance property. Held by Paul Horwich.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Use theory of meaning","Deflationism about truth"],"thinkers":[],"position":"regularity_use_theory","tags":["horwich","use theory","meaning as use","meaning"]},{"term":"Speech act theory","def":"To understand an utterance is to understand what act the speaker performs with it.","more":"Speech act theory holds that speaking is doing things. J. L. Austin, in How to Do Things with Words (lectures of 1955, published 1962), noticed that “I promise to come”, “I name this ship the Queen Elizabeth” and “I do” (at a wedding) are not descriptions but actions: saying them, in the right setting, makes the promise, the naming, the marriage. Held by J. L. Austin, John Searle, P. F. Strawson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Use theory of meaning","Intention-based semantics"],"thinkers":["searle"],"position":"speech_act_theory","tags":["speech acts","austin","searle","illocution","performatives","how to do things with words"]},{"term":"Normative inferentialism (Brandom)","def":"Meaning is a word’s role in the social practice of giving and asking for reasons.","more":"Robert Brandom, building on Wilfrid Sellars, holds that to say something is to take on a commitment that others can challenge and that you may be asked to justify. In Making It Explicit (1994) he describes conversation as “deontic scorekeeping”: each speaker keeps track of which claims others are committed to and entitled to, and what follows from them. The meaning of a sentence is its place in this web of reasons: what commits you to it, what it commits you to, and what it rules out. The view is normative because it is about what you ought to accept, not what you happen to infer, and social because the norms are kept by a community. Held by Robert Brandom, Wilfrid Sellars.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Inferentialism","Deflationism about truth"],"thinkers":[],"position":"normative_inferentialism","tags":["brandom","inferentialism","giving and asking for reasons","normative pragmatics","meaning"]},{"term":"Proof-theoretic semantics","def":"The meaning of a logical word is fixed by the rules for proving and using sentences containing it.","more":"Proof-theoretic semantics applies the inferential idea with care, starting from logic. Gerhard Gentzen remarked in the 1930s that the rules for introducing a connective such as “and” into a proof can be seen as defining it. Dag Prawitz and Michael Dummett developed this: the meaning of “and” is given by its rules (from A and B, infer “A and B”; from “A and B”, infer either), and other rules must be in “harmony” with these, neither drawing out more nor less than the introduction rules put in. Harmony blocks bogus connectives like Arthur Prior’s “tonk”. Held by Michael Dummett, Dag Prawitz, Gerhard Gentzen, Neil Tennant.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Inferentialism","Intuitionistic logic","Semantic anti-realism (Dummett)"],"thinkers":["dummett"],"position":"proof_theoretic_semantics","tags":["gentzen","dummett","prawitz","inferentialism","logic"]},{"term":"Conceptual role semantics","def":"A word or concept means what it does because of its role in thought, perception and action.","more":"Conceptual role semantics holds that the content of a concept is fixed by its causal role in a thinker’s mental life: what perceptions lead to applying it, what inferences it figures in, and what actions it prompts. Gilbert Harman, Ned Block and Hartry Field developed it, partly inspired by functionalism in the philosophy of mind. Unlike Brandom’s view, these roles are not social norms but psychological patterns. Many versions are “two-factor”: the inner role fixes a narrow aspect of content that two people can share, while relations to the environment fix what the concept refers to, so that Twin Earth cases can be handled. Also called Inferential role semantics. Held by Gilbert Harman, Ned Block, Hartry Field.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Inferentialism","Two-factor semantics (narrow and wide content)"],"thinkers":["harman","block","field"],"position":"conceptual_role_semantics","tags":["inferential role","functional role","harman","block","meaning","inferential role semantics"]},{"term":"Conventionalism about linguistic meaning (Lewis)","def":"A language is ours because we share conventions of truthfulness and trust in it.","more":"David Lewis, in Convention (1969), explained how conventions can arise without anyone agreeing to them: a convention is a regularity everyone follows because everyone expects the others to, and each prefers to conform if the others do. Driving on the left is a convention. In “Languages and Language” (1975) he applied this to meaning. A language is an abstract pairing of sentences with meanings. Held by David Lewis, Stephen Schiffer, Jonathan Bennett.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Intention-based semantics","Truth-conditional semantics"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"position":"conventionalism_about_linguistic_meaning","tags":["lewis","convention","conventions of language","truthfulness","meaning"]},{"term":"Semantic anti-realism (Dummett)","def":"Understanding consists in recognising what establishes a sentence, so not every sentence need be true or false.","more":"Michael Dummett argued that the deepest disputes about reality, over the past, mathematics or other minds, are really disputes about meaning. A realist holds that sentences have truth conditions that may hold or fail beyond all possible evidence, so every sentence is determinately true or false (the principle of bivalence). The anti-realist replies that understanding must be shown in what we can do, so meaning must be tied to the conditions we can recognise as establishing a sentence. For sentences we have no way of deciding, such as “Jones, who never faced danger, was brave”, there may be no fact making them true or false. Also called Anti-realism (Dummett). Held by Michael Dummett, Crispin Wright, Neil Tennant.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Verificationist theory of meaning","Intuitionistic logic","Intuitionism"],"thinkers":["dummett","crispin_wright"],"position":"semantic_anti_realism","tags":["dummett","anti-realism","semantic anti-realism","verificationism","bivalence","meaning","anti-realism (dummett)"]},{"term":"Indeterminacy of translation (Quine)","def":"Rival translations can fit all the evidence equally well, and there is no fact about which is right.","more":"In Word and Object (1960), W. V. O. Quine imagined a linguist translating an unknown language from scratch, with only speakers’ behaviour to go on. Held by W. V. O. Quine, Donald Davidson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Meaning skepticism","Quine’s rejection of analyticity"],"thinkers":["quine"],"position":"indeterminacy_of_translation","tags":["quine","indeterminacy of translation","radical translation","gavagai","word and object","meaning"]},{"term":"Kripke’s skeptical solution","def":"No fact makes it true that you mean addition, but meaning talk is justified by its role in a community.","more":"In Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language (1982), Saul Kripke presented an argument he attributed to Wittgenstein, sometimes called “Kripkenstein”. Nothing in your past behaviour, your mind or your dispositions makes it a fact that by “plus” you meant addition rather than a rival function, “quus”. Kripke then offered a “skeptical solution”, modelled on Hume’s response to skepticism about causation. We do not need meaning facts. Also called Kripkenstein. Held by Saul Kripke.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Meaning skepticism","Communitarian view of meaning"],"thinkers":[],"position":"kripkean_skeptical_solution","tags":["kripke","kripkenstein","skeptical solution","rule-following","wittgenstein","meaning"]},{"term":"Mathematical structuralism","def":"Mathematics studies structures; numbers are just places in a pattern, not objects with an inner nature.","more":"Structuralism says that mathematics is the study of structures, and that mathematical objects are nothing more than positions in them. The number 2 has no inner nature: all there is to it is being the successor of 1 and the predecessor of 3 in the natural number structure, just as all there is to being the shortstop is playing that role in a baseball team. Richard Dedekind (1888) took numbers to be what any “simply infinite system” has in common. Paul Benacerraf (1965) gave the modern argument, and Stewart Shapiro, Michael Resnik and Geoffrey Hellman developed rival versions. Held by Stewart Shapiro, Michael Resnik, Geoffrey Hellman, Paul Benacerraf.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Ante rem structuralism","Modal structuralism"],"thinkers":["shapiro"],"position":"mathematical_structuralism","tags":["structuralism","shapiro","resnik","benacerraf","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Eliminative structuralism","def":"Talk of numbers is really general talk about any system with the right structure.","more":"Eliminative (or “in re”) structuralism agrees that arithmetic is about structure, but denies that structures or their places exist as extra objects. A statement like “2 + 3 = 5” is shorthand for a general claim: in any system of objects arranged like the natural numbers (a first element, each followed by a next, and so on), the item in the “2” position combined in the right way with the “3” item gives the “5” item. Mathematics is then about all the real systems that have a given structure. The view avoids abstract objects, but faces a problem: if there are not infinitely many things in reality, there may be no system with the structure of the natural numbers, and every arithmetical claim would come out trivially true. Also called In re structuralism. Held by Paul Benacerraf.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Mathematical structuralism","Modal structuralism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"eliminative_structuralism","tags":["in re structuralism","structuralism","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"The set-theoretic multiverse","def":"There are many equally real universes of sets, and questions like the continuum hypothesis have different answers in each.","more":"Set theory is the usual foundation of mathematics. The continuum hypothesis (CH) says there is no size of infinity between that of the whole numbers and that of the real numbers. Kurt Gödel (1940) and Paul Cohen (1963) showed that the standard axioms can neither prove nor disprove it. A traditional platonist, like Gödel, thinks there is one true universe of sets, so CH is true or false, and we must find new axioms to decide it. Held by Joel David Hamkins.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Platonism about mathematics","Full-blooded (plenitudinous) platonism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"set_theoretic_multiverse","tags":["set-theoretic multiverse","hamkins","set theory","continuum hypothesis","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Full-blooded (plenitudinous) platonism","def":"Every consistent mathematical theory truly describes some abstract objects.","more":"Full-blooded platonism, defended by Mark Balaguer in Platonism and Anti-Platonism in Mathematics (1998), says that all the mathematical objects that possibly could exist do exist. So any consistent theory is true of some part of the mathematical realm. This is meant to answer the challenge of how we could know about abstract objects with which we have no contact. If every consistent theory describes something, then all we need in order to get mathematics right is to make sure our theories are consistent, and we can know that without any contact with the objects. Also called Full-blooded platonism, Plenitudinous platonism. Held by Mark Balaguer, Bernard Linsky, Edward Zalta.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Platonism about mathematics","The set-theoretic multiverse"],"thinkers":["balaguer"],"position":"plenitudinous_platonism","tags":["plenitudinous platonism","balaguer","full-blooded platonism","platonism","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Game formalism","def":"Mathematics is a game played with symbols, and its theorems are just legal positions.","more":"Game formalism is the strictest form of formalism. It compares mathematics to chess. Chess pieces mean nothing; there are only the rules for moving them. Likewise, the numerals and symbols of arithmetic stand for nothing, and a theorem is a string of symbols reachable from the starting strings by the rules. Held by Johannes Thomae, Eduard Heine, Haskell Curry.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Formalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"game_formalism","tags":["formalism","symbols","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Hilbert’s finitism","def":"Finite arithmetic is meaningful; talk of the infinite is an ideal tool to be proved safe.","more":"David Hilbert, in the 1920s, proposed a programme to secure mathematics after the paradoxes of set theory. He split mathematics into two parts. Finitary mathematics, about concrete strings of strokes and finite calculations, is meaningful and certain. Ideal mathematics, about infinite sets and the like, is not directly meaningful, but it is a powerful device for proving finitary truths, as imaginary numbers are a device for solving real equations. Also called Hilbert’s programme. Held by David Hilbert, Paul Bernays.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Formalism"],"thinkers":["hilbert"],"position":"hilbertian_finitism","tags":["finitism","hilbert","hilbert's program","formalism","infinity","philosophy of mathematics","hilbert’s programme"]},{"term":"Deductivism (if-thenism)","def":"A theorem says only that its conclusion follows from the axioms.","more":"Deductivism holds that a mathematical theorem is really a conditional: if the axioms hold, then so does the theorem. “Every triangle’s angles sum to two right angles” says that in any system satisfying Euclid’s axioms, this follows. Mathematics then makes no claim about whether anything satisfies the axioms, so it needs no abstract objects and is certain as far as logic is. Bertrand Russell put the idea at the start of The Principles of Mathematics (1903), defining pure mathematics as the class of propositions of the form “p implies q”. Also called If-thenism. Held by Bertrand Russell, Hilary Putnam.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Formalism","Logicism"],"thinkers":["russell","putnam"],"position":"deductivism","tags":["deductivism","if-thenism","russell","formalism","axioms","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Brouwer’s intuitionism","def":"Mathematics is the mind’s construction, prior to language, grounded in our intuition of time.","more":"L. E. J. Brouwer, in his dissertation (1907) and later work, held that mathematics is a free activity of the mind, independent of language and logic. Held by L. E. J. Brouwer, Arend Heyting.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Intuitionism","Intuitionistic logic"],"thinkers":["brouwer"],"position":"brouwerian_intuitionism","tags":["brouwer","intuitionism","constructivism","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Neo-logicism","def":"Arithmetic follows from logic plus a principle that simply explains what numbers are.","more":"Frege tried to derive arithmetic from logic, but his system contained a contradiction, found by Russell in 1902. Neo-logicists, led by Crispin Wright and Bob Hale, revived the project. They noticed that Frege had used his faulty law only to derive Hume’s Principle: the number of Fs equals the number of Gs just when the Fs and the Gs can be paired off one to one. From Hume’s Principle in second-order logic, all the basic laws of arithmetic follow, a result now called Frege’s theorem. Also called Abstractionism. Held by Crispin Wright, Bob Hale.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Logicism","Platonism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["crispin_wright"],"position":"neo_logicism","tags":["neologicism","neo-fregeanism","hale","wright","hume's principle","logicism","abstractionism"]},{"term":"Field’s nominalism (hard-road fictionalism)","def":"Science can be rewritten without numbers, so mathematics need not be true to be useful.","more":"The strongest argument for believing in numbers is that science cannot do without them: physics states its laws in mathematical terms. Hartry Field, in Science Without Numbers (1980), took this on directly. He rewrote Newtonian gravitational theory without mentioning any numbers, using only relations among space-time points (such as one point being between two others). He then argued that mathematics is “conservative”: adding it to a nominalist theory never lets you derive new claims about the physical world that you could not derive without it. Held by Hartry Field.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Fictionalism about mathematics"],"thinkers":["field"],"position":"hard_road_fictionalism","tags":["field","science without numbers","nominalism","fictionalism","indispensability argument"]},{"term":"Easy-road fictionalism","def":"Mathematics is indispensable to science, yet still not literally true.","more":"Easy-road fictionalists agree that we may never be able to rewrite science without mathematics. But they deny that this forces us to believe in numbers. Scientists use mathematics to represent physical things, and a representation can be useful without being literally true, as a map’s grid lines help you find your way though they are not on the ground. Joseph Melia argued that scientists can use mathematics and then “take back” its existence claims. Held by Mary Leng, Mark Balaguer, Joseph Melia, Jody Azzouni.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Fictionalism about mathematics","Figuralism"],"thinkers":["leng","balaguer"],"position":"easy_road_fictionalism","tags":["yablo","azzouni","fictionalism","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Figuralism","def":"Mathematical talk is a kind of figurative speech, true in what it really conveys.","more":"4 children” or “there’s a lot of sadness in him”. No one thinks there is an average family or an amount of sadness, yet what we say is perfectly correct in what it conveys. On Yablo’s figuralism, saying “the number of planets is eight” really conveys that there are eight planets, and it uses numbers as a convenient way of putting it. Mathematics is a game of make-believe whose rules make it a very efficient medium for describing the concrete world. Held by Stephen Yablo.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Fictionalism about mathematics","Easy-road fictionalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"figuralism","tags":["yablo","figurative language","fictionalism","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Natural kind externalism","def":"What words like “water” mean depends on the actual nature of the stuff around us, not only on what is in our heads.","more":"Hilary Putnam, in “The Meaning of ‘Meaning’” (1975), imagined Twin Earth, a planet exactly like Earth except that the clear drinkable liquid in its rivers is not H₂O but a different compound, XYZ. In 1750, before chemistry, Oscar on Earth and his molecule-for-molecule duplicate on Twin Earth are in the same inner state when they say “water”. Yet Oscar’s word refers to H₂O and his twin’s to XYZ. So what a word refers to, and hence its meaning, is not fixed by what is in the speaker’s head. Also called Semantic externalism (Twin Earth). Held by Hilary Putnam, Saul Kripke.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Social externalism","Causal-historical theory of reference","Natural kind realism"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"natural_kind_externalism","tags":["putnam","twin earth","meaning ain't in the head","semantic externalism","semantic externalism (twin earth)"]},{"term":"Social externalism","def":"What you mean and think can depend on how your language community uses words, even ones you misunderstand.","more":"Tyler Burge, in “Individualism and the Mental” (1979), described a patient who believes he has arthritis in his thigh. Arthritis is in fact a disease of the joints, so his belief is false. Now imagine a community where doctors use “arthritis” for a wider range of rheumatic ailments, including ones in the thigh. A patient there, with exactly the same body, history and inner states, would have a true belief about a different condition. Also called Anti-individualism. Held by Tyler Burge, Hilary Putnam.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Natural kind externalism","Communitarian view of meaning"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"position":"social_externalism","tags":["burge","arthritis","division of linguistic labour","semantic externalism","anti-individualism"]},{"term":"Two-factor semantics (narrow and wide content)","def":"Meaning has an inner part fixed by the head and an outer part fixed by the world.","more":"Two-factor views accept Twin Earth but insist that something is still shared. Oscar and his twin mean different stuff by “water”, but they think about it in the same way: both pick out “the clear, drinkable liquid around here”. On these views, meaning has two parts. Narrow content depends only on what is in the head and explains how people reason and act. Also called Narrow content view, Two-dimensional semantics. Held by Ned Block, David Chalmers, Frank Jackson, Hartry Field.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Conceptual role semantics","Fregean semantics (sense and reference)"],"thinkers":["block","chalmers","jackson","field"],"position":"two_factor_semantics","tags":["two-factor theory","narrow content","wide content","two-dimensionalism","mental content","narrow content view","two-dimensional semantics"]},{"term":"Semantic internalism","def":"What a speaker means is fixed by what is in her head; the environment fixes only what her words happen to apply to.","more":"Semantic internalism holds that meaning, at least the meaning that matters for understanding and psychology, is determined by the speaker’s internal states. Noam Chomsky argues that the scientific study of language is the study of an individual’s internal language faculty, and that reference to things in the world is a matter of how people use words, not a fixed relation that semantics must describe. John Searle argued that the content of Oscar’s concept includes something like “the stuff that causes these very experiences in me”, so the twins share an inner meaning that applies to different stuff because of their different surroundings. Older descriptivists simply held that in 1750 “water” meant any clear drinkable liquid, so XYZ would count as water too. Also called Individualism about meaning. Held by Noam Chomsky, John Searle, Gabriel Segal.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Descriptivism about names","Two-factor semantics (narrow and wide content)"],"thinkers":["chomsky","searle"],"position":"semantic_internalism","tags":["narrow content","individualism","meaning","mental content","individualism about meaning"]},{"term":"Identity theory of truth","def":"A true thought does not match a fact; it is a fact.","more":"The identity theory says that when a thought or proposition is true, it is identical to a fact, rather than corresponding to one. The correspondence theory pictures truth as a relation between two different things, a sentence or thought on one side and a fact in the world on the other. Identity theorists think this opens a gap that can never be closed. On their view, what you think when you think truly (that snow is white) just is part of how the world is. Held by John McDowell, Jennifer Hornsby, G. E. Moore.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Correspondence theory of truth","Deflationism about truth"],"thinkers":["moore"],"position":"identity_theory_of_truth","tags":["identity theory","mcdowell","hornsby","facts","truth"]},{"term":"Russell’s theory of descriptions","def":"“The so-and-so” is not a name but a hidden claim that exactly one thing is so-and-so.","more":"In “On Denoting” (1905), Bertrand Russell analysed sentences containing definite descriptions like “the present King of France”. “The present King of France is bald” does not refer to a king and say he is bald. It says three things together: there is at least one present King of France, there is at most one, and whoever it is, is bald. Since the first part is false, the whole sentence is simply false, with no need for a non-existent king. Held by Bertrand Russell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Language","related":["Descriptivism about names"],"thinkers":["russell"],"position":"theory_of_descriptions","tags":["theory of descriptions","russell","on denoting","definite descriptions","king of france"]},{"term":"Non-theistic religion","def":"There is something sacred and ultimate, but it is not a God who thinks and chooses.","more":"Non-theistic religion holds that reality has a sacred depth, but that this ultimate is not a personal creator. Many Buddhist traditions teach that there is no creator God, yet there is a real path that ends suffering and rebirth (nirvana). Jainism teaches that souls are eternal and can free themselves without any creator’s help. Daoism speaks of the Dao, “the Way”, a nameless source and pattern of all things with no plan or will. Also called Non-theism. Held by The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama), Nagarjuna, Dharmakirti, Mahavira.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Rebirth","Return to the whole"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna","dharmakirti","mahavira","laozi"],"position":"non_theistic_religion","tags":["religion without god","religious naturalism","buddhism","the sacred","non-theism"]},{"term":"Theological noncognitivism","def":"“God exists” does not make a clear claim, so it is neither true nor false.","more":"Theological noncognitivism holds that sentences like “God exists” lack clear factual meaning. The idea grew out of logical positivism, the twentieth-century movement holding that a statement is meaningful only if some possible experience could count for or against it. A. J. Also called Ignosticism. Held by A. J. Ayer, Antony Flew.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Agnosticism"],"thinkers":["ayer","flew"],"position":"theological_noncognitivism","tags":["noncognitivism","verificationism","ayer","meaningless","religious language","ignosticism"]},{"term":"Classical theism","def":"God is one, perfect, unchanging, outside time, all-knowing, all-powerful and wholly good.","more":"Classical theism is the traditional picture of God shared by major thinkers in Judaism (Maimonides), Christianity (Augustine, Anselm, Aquinas) and Islam (Avicenna, al-Ghazali). God is the perfect being: the uncreated source of everything, who knows all things, including every future free choice, can do anything logically possible, and is perfectly good. Many classical theists add stronger claims: God is simple (not made of parts), unchanging (immutable), outside time (eternal), and not affected by creatures (impassible). On this view God is not a very large person among other things, but being itself, the reason anything exists. Also called Perfect being theism. Held by Thomas Aquinas, Anselm of Canterbury, Augustine of Hippo, Maimonides.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Divine timelessness","Natural law theory","Cosmological argument"],"thinkers":["aquinas","anselm","augustine","maimonides","davies_b"],"position":"classical_theism","tags":["god","divine simplicity","omniscience","omnipotence","aquinas","perfect being theism"]},{"term":"Open theism","def":"God is loving and responsive but does not know in advance what free creatures will choose.","more":"Open theism holds that the future is partly open, even to God. God knows everything that can be known, but future free choices are not yet there to be known, so God learns them as they happen. God is also affected by what we do: God responds to prayer, takes risks, and can grieve or change plans. Open theists, such as Clark Pinnock, William Hasker and John Sanders, see this as the most faithful reading of the many biblical stories in which God tests, regrets or relents, and as the best way to make human freedom real. Also called Openness theology, Free will theism. Held by Clark Pinnock, William Hasker, Richard Swinburne, John Sanders.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Free will defence","Theism"],"thinkers":["pinnock","hasker","swinburne"],"position":"open_theism","tags":["openness theology","open future","divine foreknowledge","god","free will theism"]},{"term":"Panentheism","def":"The world is in God, but God is more than the world.","more":"Panentheism (Greek for “all in God”) holds that the universe exists within God, yet God is greater than the universe. A common picture: the world is to God roughly as your body is to you. Your body is part of you, and what happens to it affects you, but you are more than your body. The term was coined by the German philosopher Karl Krause in the early nineteenth century. Held by Karl Christian Friedrich Krause, Charles Hartshorne, Alfred North Whitehead.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Process theism","Pantheism"],"thinkers":["krause","hartshorne","whitehead"],"position":"panentheism","tags":["god","pantheism","process theology","world in god"]},{"term":"Polytheism","def":"There are many gods, each with their own powers and character.","more":"Polytheism holds that there are many divine beings. It was the religion of ancient Egypt, Greece, Rome and the Norse world, and lives on in Shinto, many African and Indigenous traditions, and modern revivals. Whether Hinduism is polytheistic is debated: many Hindus worship many deities while holding they are forms of one ultimate reality. Philosophical polytheists such as the Neoplatonist Proclus held that many gods proceed from a single first principle, while still deserving worship in their own right. Held by Proclus, Julian (Roman emperor).","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"polytheism","tags":["many gods","gods","paganism","hinduism","pantheon"]},{"term":"Moral argument","def":"Real right and wrong, and our duty to follow them, are best explained by God.","more":"The moral argument says that the existence of objective morality points to God. Most people believe that some things, like torturing a child for fun, are really wrong, not just disliked. The argument asks what could make that true. A popular version, defended by William Lane Craig, runs: if God does not exist, objective moral values and duties do not exist; they do exist; so God exists. Held by William Lane Craig, Robert Merrihew Adams, Immanuel Kant, C. S. Lewis.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Modified divine command theory","Natural law theory"],"thinkers":["craig","adams_rm","kant"],"position":"moral_argument","tags":["god","divine command","objective morality","kant","existence of god"]},{"term":"Argument from religious experience","def":"Many people experience God directly, and experiences are normally good evidence.","more":"This argument holds that experiences of God or the sacred give real evidence that God exists. Millions of people across cultures report sensing God’s presence, in prayer, worship, nature or moments of crisis. Richard Swinburne’s principle of credulity says that if it seems to you that something is present, you should believe it is, unless you have special reason to doubt it; we rely on this for all ordinary perception. William Alston argued that Christian experience is a practice of forming beliefs much like sense perception: socially established, self-correcting and reliable as far as anyone can tell. Held by Richard Swinburne, William Alston, Caroline Franks Davis.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Reformed epistemology","Cumulative case theism"],"thinkers":["swinburne","alston"],"position":"argument_from_religious_experience","tags":["religious experience","mysticism","alston","swinburne","existence of god"]},{"term":"No successful argument for God","def":"None of the arguments for God’s existence succeeds; each has a decisive flaw.","more":"This position holds that every standard argument for God fails. The cosmological argument assumes the universe needs a cause without showing that God does not; the design argument has rival explanations in natural selection and, perhaps, a multiverse; the ontological argument tries to define something into existence; the moral argument assumes morality needs God; and religious experience conflicts across religions and has natural explanations. Holders include atheists such as J. L. Held by J. L. Mackie, Graham Oppy, Bertrand Russell, David Hume.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Agnosticism","Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["mackie","russell","hume"],"position":"no_good_argument_for_god","tags":["atheism","agnosticism","existence of god","arguments for god","natural theology"]},{"term":"Logical argument from evil","def":"The existence of evil is logically incompatible with an all-powerful, all-good God.","more":"The logical argument from evil claims that God and evil cannot both exist. An ancient version, attributed to Epicurus by later writers, asks whether God is willing but unable to prevent evil, able but unwilling, or both. In its classic modern form, J. L. Also called Deductive problem of evil. Held by J. L. Mackie, H. J. McCloskey.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism from evil","Evidential argument from evil"],"thinkers":["mackie"],"position":"logical_argument_from_evil","tags":["problem of evil","logical problem of evil","mackie","epicurus","theodicy","deductive problem of evil"]},{"term":"Evidential argument from evil","def":"The amount and kinds of suffering make it unlikely that an all-good, all-powerful God exists.","more":"The evidential argument from evil does not claim that God and evil are strictly incompatible. Instead it claims that the suffering we observe is strong evidence against God. William Rowe’s famous example: a fawn is trapped in a forest fire, badly burned, and dies slowly, with no human ever knowing. It is hard to see any greater good this serves. Also called Probabilistic argument from evil, Inductive argument from evil. Held by William Rowe, Paul Draper.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism from evil","Logical argument from evil"],"thinkers":["rowe","draper"],"position":"evidential_argument_from_evil","tags":["problem of evil","evidential problem of evil","rowe","gratuitous suffering","theodicy","probabilistic argument from evil","inductive argument from evil"]},{"term":"Cumulative case theism","def":"Suffering counts against God, but the total evidence for God is stronger.","more":"Cumulative case theism treats belief in God like a verdict reached by weighing all the evidence together. It grants that suffering is real evidence against God; it does not pretend the problem of evil is easy. But it holds that other evidence, such as the existence of a universe, its orderly laws, the fine-tuning of physics for life, consciousness, moral awareness and religious experience, together outweighs it. Basil Mitchell compared the case for God to the judgement of a historian or a detective: no single clue settles it, but the pieces fit together into the best overall explanation. Held by Richard Swinburne, William Lane Craig, Basil Mitchell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Design argument","Cosmological argument","Argument from religious experience"],"thinkers":["swinburne","craig"],"position":"cumulative_case_theism","tags":["cumulative case","swinburne","natural theology","problem of evil","existence of god"]},{"term":"Process theism","def":"God is real and good but cannot simply override the world, so suffering does not count against God.","more":"Process theism holds that God does not have the power to control events single-handedly. Drawing on the philosophy of Alfred North Whitehead, it pictures reality as a web of events, each with some degree of self-determination. God acts by persuasion, offering each event its best possibility, not by force. God is also affected by everything that happens, feeling the world’s joys and sufferings. Also called Process theology. Held by Alfred North Whitehead, Charles Hartshorne, David Ray Griffin, Harold Kushner.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Panentheism","Open theism"],"thinkers":["whitehead","hartshorne"],"position":"process_theism","tags":["process theology","whitehead","hartshorne","finite god","problem of evil"]},{"term":"Free will defence","def":"God allows evil because a world of truly free beings, who can choose wrongly, is worth the risk.","more":"The free will defence explains moral evil, the wrong that people do, by appeal to the value of free will. Augustine argued that evil comes from the misuse of free will by creatures, not from God. In its modern form, Alvin Plantinga argued that a world containing free creatures who sometimes do good is more valuable than a world of perfectly obedient robots. But God cannot make free creatures and also guarantee they always choose good, since then they would not be free. Held by Alvin Plantinga, Augustine of Hippo, William Hasker, Peter van Inwagen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theodicy","Open theism","Molinism"],"thinkers":["plantinga","augustine","hasker","vaninwagen"],"position":"free_will_defence","tags":["free will defense","plantinga","problem of evil","theodicy","free will"]},{"term":"Soul-making theodicy","def":"Suffering is part of a world designed for people to grow into goodness, and that growth is worth the cost.","more":"The soul-making theodicy, developed by John Hick, holds that God’s aim was not a pleasure palace but an environment where people could develop into mature, morally good beings. Hick drew on the early Christian writer Irenaeus, who suggested humans were created immature and meant to grow into God’s likeness; how closely Hick’s view matches Irenaeus is debated. The phrase “vale of soul-making” comes from a letter by the poet John Keats. On this view, a world without danger, pain or need would leave no room for courage, compassion, perseverance or self-sacrifice. Also called Irenaean theodicy, Greater-good theodicy. Held by John Hick, Richard Swinburne.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theodicy","Free will defence","Epistemic distance"],"thinkers":["hick","swinburne"],"position":"soul_making_theodicy","tags":["soul-making","irenaean theodicy","hick","irenaeus","problem of evil","theodicy","greater-good theodicy"]},{"term":"Skeptical theism","def":"If God exists, we should not expect to understand God’s reasons, so unexplained suffering is not evidence against God.","more":"Skeptical theism combines belief in God with skepticism (doubt) about our ability to know God’s purposes. It replies to the evidential argument from evil: from “I can’t see any good reason for this suffering” it does not follow that “there is probably no good reason”. Stephen Wykstra argued that we may infer “there is none” from “I see none” only when we would expect to see it if it were there. Since God’s knowledge and purposes would vastly exceed ours, we should not expect to see God’s reasons. Held by William Alston, Stephen Wykstra, Michael Bergmann.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism","Free will defence"],"thinkers":["alston"],"position":"skeptical_theism","tags":["wykstra","problem of evil","divine reasons","noseeum"]},{"term":"Karma theodicy","def":"Suffering is the result of each being’s own past actions, in this life or earlier ones, so it is not unjust.","more":"The karma theodicy, found in Hindu, Jain and Buddhist thought, explains suffering through karma: the principle that intentional actions have consequences that return to the one who acted, often across many lives. A child born into hardship is reaping the results of deeds in a previous life; nothing happens to anyone that is wholly undeserved. Theistic versions, such as that of the Hindu philosopher Shankara in his commentary on the Brahma Sutras, argue that God is not unfair or cruel in allowing inequality, because God apportions results according to each soul’s karma, like rain that falls equally while different seeds grow differently. Since the cycle of rebirth has no beginning, there is no first undeserved suffering. Also called Karma and rebirth explanation of suffering. Held by Adi Shankara, Mahavira, Ramanuja.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Rebirth","Non-theistic religion"],"thinkers":["shankara","mahavira","ramanuja"],"position":"karma_theodicy","tags":["karma","rebirth","hinduism","buddhism","problem of evil","theodicy","karma and rebirth explanation of suffering"]},{"term":"Atheism from evil","def":"No explanation reconciles the world’s suffering with a good God, so there is no such God.","more":"Atheism from evil is the view that suffering is the decisive reason to reject God. Its holders have considered the free will defence, soul-making, skeptical theism and the rest, and found them inadequate to the scale and kinds of suffering in the world: children dying of disease, animals suffering for millions of years before humans, horrors that crush rather than build character. They conclude that no all-powerful, all-good God exists. The view need not rely on a strict proof (the logical argument) or only on probabilities (the evidential argument); it can rest on the judgement that no answer offered so far works. Held by J. L. Mackie, William Rowe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Logical argument from evil","Evidential argument from evil"],"thinkers":["mackie","rowe"],"position":"atheism_from_evil","tags":["atheism","problem of evil","argument from evil","suffering"]},{"term":"Reformed epistemology","def":"Belief in God can be rational without arguments, as a basic belief like trust in memory or perception.","more":"Reformed epistemology, named after its roots in the Reformed (Calvinist) Christian tradition, holds that belief in God can be “properly basic”: reasonable without being inferred from other beliefs. Alvin Plantinga and Nicholas Wolterstorff point out that many of our most secure beliefs are basic in this way. You believe you had breakfast this morning because you remember it, not because of an argument. Plantinga argued that belief in God can arise in the same direct way, for example when looking at the stars or feeling guilty or forgiven, through what John Calvin called a “sense of divinity”. Also called Proper basicality. Held by Alvin Plantinga, Nicholas Wolterstorff, William Alston.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Argument from religious experience","Exclusivism"],"thinkers":["plantinga","wolterstorff","alston"],"position":"reformed_epistemology","tags":["plantinga","properly basic belief","wolterstorff","alston","faith","proper basicality"]},{"term":"Exclusivism","def":"One religion is true, and where others contradict it they are mistaken.","more":"Religious exclusivism holds that one religion’s central claims are true, and that where other religions contradict them, those religions are false. Often it adds that salvation or liberation is found only through that one religion. Many traditional forms of Christianity, Islam and some Hindu schools have held exclusivist views. Alvin Plantinga has defended the epistemic version: it is not arrogant or irrational to believe that one’s own religion is true and others mistaken, any more than it is arrogant to hold any belief that others reject. Also called Religious exclusivism, Particularism. Held by Alvin Plantinga, Harold Netland.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Reformed epistemology"],"thinkers":["plantinga"],"position":"exclusivism","tags":["religious exclusivism","one true religion","salvation","religious diversity","particularism"]},{"term":"Inclusivism","def":"One religion is truest, but others contain real truth, and their followers can reach salvation through it.","more":"Inclusivism holds that one religion has the fullest truth, but that other religions also contain real truth and goodness, and that their sincere followers can be saved or liberated. The best-known Christian inclusivist is Karl Rahner, who suggested that people of other faiths who live by grace may be “anonymous Christians”, saved through Christ without knowing it. The Second Vatican Council’s declaration Nostra Aetate (1965) said the Catholic Church rejects nothing that is true and holy in other religions. Inclusivist ideas are also found in other traditions; some Hindu thinkers, for instance, see other religions as partial paths to the same goal that Vedanta most fully describes. Also called Religious inclusivism. Held by Karl Rahner, Second Vatican Council.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Religious pluralism"],"thinkers":["rahner"],"position":"inclusivism","tags":["religious inclusivism","rahner","anonymous christians","religious diversity"]},{"term":"Religious pluralism","def":"The great religions are different but equally valid human responses to one ultimate reality.","more":"Religious pluralism, in the form developed by John Hick, holds that the world’s major religions are different culturally shaped responses to the same ultimate reality, which Hick called “the Real”. The Real in itself is beyond human concepts. Christians experience it as the Holy Trinity, Muslims as Allah, Hindus as Brahman or Krishna, Buddhists as nirvana or emptiness. Each tradition is a valid path, and Hick’s test was moral and spiritual: all the great faiths seem roughly equally good at transforming people from self-centredness to reality-centredness. Also called Pluralism (Hick). Held by John Hick, Swami Vivekananda.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Inclusivism","Epistemic distance"],"thinkers":["hick","vivekananda"],"position":"religious_pluralism","tags":["hick","the real","religious diversity","many paths","pluralism (hick)"]},{"term":"Naturalism about religion","def":"No religion is true in its claims about gods or the supernatural, whatever wisdom it contains.","more":"Naturalism about religion holds that religions’ supernatural claims, about gods, spirits, souls, miracles or karma, are false, because the natural world studied by science is all there is. Religions are then explained as human creations: products of psychology, culture and history. Sigmund Freud saw religion as wish fulfilment, an expression of the longing for a protective father. Daniel Dennett and others have offered evolutionary explanations, suggesting religious ideas spread because they exploit features of human minds, such as a tendency to see agents behind events. Held by Sigmund Freud, Daniel Dennett, Bertrand Russell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Humean skepticism about miracles"],"thinkers":["freud","dennett","russell"],"position":"naturalism_about_religion","tags":["naturalism","atheism","cognitive science of religion","evolution of religion","religion"]},{"term":"Theological determinism","def":"God determines everything that happens, including our choices, and this is compatible with our being responsible.","more":"Theological determinism holds that God ordains everything that happens, including every human choice, and that God knows the future because God has decided it. This is the view of John Calvin and Jonathan Edwards, and of many in the Reformed tradition; the Ash’ari school of Islamic theology also held that God creates all acts. To keep human responsibility, theological determinists are usually compatibilists: they hold that a choice is free if it flows from your own desires and is not forced on you, even if those desires were ultimately determined. Edwards argued that we always act on our strongest motive, and that this is what freedom means. Also called Calvinism (on providence), Theological compatibilism. Held by John Calvin, Jonathan Edwards, Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism","Theological voluntarism"],"thinkers":["ashari"],"position":"theological_determinism","tags":["predestination","calvinism","divine sovereignty","providence","calvinism (on providence)","theological compatibilism"]},{"term":"Divine timelessness","def":"God exists outside time, seeing all of history at once, so knowing our choices does not mean knowing them in advance.","more":"The timeless solution to the foreknowledge problem says that God is eternal in the sense of being outside time altogether. God does not foresee tomorrow; God sees all times in a single eternal present. Boethius, writing while awaiting execution around 524, argued that God’s knowledge of our future acts is like our knowledge of something we watch happening now: seeing someone walk does not make them walk. Augustine and Aquinas held similar views, and Eleonore Stump and Norman Kretzmann defended it in detail in the twentieth century. Also called Boethian solution, Divine eternity. Held by Boethius, Thomas Aquinas, Augustine of Hippo, Eleonore Stump.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism"],"thinkers":["boethius","aquinas","augustine","stump"],"position":"divine_timelessness","tags":["timelessness","eternity","boethius","god outside time","divine foreknowledge","boethian solution","divine eternity"]},{"term":"Molinism","def":"God knows what every possible free creature would freely do in any situation, and uses this to plan history.","more":"Molinism, named after the sixteenth-century Jesuit Luis de Molina, claims that God has “middle knowledge”: knowledge of what any free creature would freely do in any possible circumstances. These truths are called counterfactuals of freedom (for example, “If Peter were asked about Jesus in the courtyard, he would freely deny him”). Using this knowledge, God chooses which circumstances to create, and so knows the whole future, without determining any free choice. God can thus guide history precisely while leaving creatures genuinely free. Also called Middle knowledge. Held by Luis de Molina, William Lane Craig, Thomas Flint, Alvin Plantinga.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Free will defence","Classical theism"],"thinkers":["craig","plantinga"],"position":"molinism","tags":["molina","middle knowledge","divine foreknowledge","free will"]},{"term":"Ockhamism","def":"God’s past knowledge of your future choice depends on what you will freely do, so it does not fix your choice.","more":"Ockhamism, named after William of Ockham, solves the foreknowledge problem by questioning what counts as a fixed fact about the past. The argument that foreknowledge destroys freedom says: God’s belief yesterday that you will lie tomorrow is a past fact, and the past cannot be changed, so you cannot do otherwise. Ockhamists reply that not every fact about the past is a “hard” fact, fully settled. “God believed yesterday that you will lie tomorrow” is a “soft” fact: it is partly about tomorrow, and its truth depends on what you will freely do. Also called Ockhamist solution. Held by William of Ockham, Marilyn McCord Adams, Alvin Plantinga.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism","Molinism"],"thinkers":["ockham","adams_mm","plantinga"],"position":"ockhamism","tags":["ockham","divine foreknowledge","soft facts","free will","ockhamist solution"]},{"term":"Hiddenness argument","def":"A perfectly loving God would make itself known to anyone open to it; since many sincere seekers find nothing, there is no such God.","more":"The hiddenness argument, developed by J. L. Schellenberg in 1993, is a distinct argument against God that does not rely on suffering. It starts from what perfect love involves. Also called Argument from divine hiddenness, Argument from nonbelief. Held by J. L. Schellenberg, Theodore Drange.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Evidential argument from evil"],"thinkers":["schellenberg"],"position":"hiddenness_argument","tags":["divine hiddenness","schellenberg","hiddenness","nonbelief","existence of god","argument from divine hiddenness","argument from nonbelief"]},{"term":"Epistemic distance","def":"God stays partly hidden so that people can freely come to faith and goodness, rather than being overwhelmed.","more":"The epistemic distance view holds that God deliberately leaves the world ambiguous about God’s existence, to protect human freedom. John Hick coined the phrase “epistemic distance”, meaning a distance in knowledge rather than space. If God’s presence were as obvious as the sun, Hick argued, we could not freely choose to love God or do good for its own sake; we would be compelled by the overwhelming presence of an all-powerful being, the way anyone would behave well with a police officer standing beside them. Michael Murray argued similarly that obvious divine presence would make threats and rewards so vivid that moral choice would become coercion. Held by John Hick, Richard Swinburne, Michael Murray.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Soul-making theodicy","Free will defence"],"thinkers":["hick","swinburne"],"position":"epistemic_distance","tags":["hick","divine hiddenness","soul-making","free will"]},{"term":"The hidden God (deus absconditus)","def":"God is hidden from those who do not truly want God, and found by those who seek with the whole heart.","more":"This view, named from the Latin for “the hidden God” (from Isaiah 45:15 in the Latin Bible), holds that God’s hiddenness is a feature of how God relates to human hearts. Blaise Pascal argued that God gives enough light for those who sincerely desire to see, and enough obscurity to leave those who do not desire it in their blindness. The evidence is real but not overwhelming, so that what we find depends partly on what we want. Paul Moser developed the view in The Elusive God (2008): God offers evidence of a special kind, not to satisfy idle curiosity, but to call people into a transforming relationship, and that evidence becomes available when one is willing to be changed. Also called Pascalian hiddenness, Divine elusiveness. Held by Blaise Pascal, Paul Moser.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Epistemic distance","Fideism"],"thinkers":["pascal"],"position":"deus_absconditus","tags":["deus absconditus","hidden god","pascal","divine hiddenness","isaiah","pascalian hiddenness","divine elusiveness"]},{"term":"Humean skepticism about miracles","def":"It is never reasonable to believe a report of a miracle, because the evidence against it will always be stronger.","more":"In his essay “Of Miracles” (1748), David Hume argued that we can never be justified in believing a report that a miracle occurred. A miracle is a violation of a law of nature, and our belief that something is a law of nature rests on the most uniform experience possible: dead people stay dead, every time. Against this, the evidence for a miracle is only testimony, and testimony is often mistaken, exaggerated or invented, especially where religion and wonder are involved. A wise person proportions belief to the evidence, and asks which would be more surprising: that the witness is wrong, or that the miracle happened. Also called Hume on miracles. Held by David Hume, J. L. Mackie, Antony Flew.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Evidentialism","Naturalism about religion","Deism"],"thinkers":["hume","mackie","flew"],"position":"humean_skepticism_about_miracles","tags":["hume","miracles","of miracles","testimony","laws of nature","skepticism","hume on miracles"]},{"term":"Credibility of miracles","def":"Miracles are possible if God exists, and good enough evidence could make it reasonable to believe in one.","more":"This position holds that it can be reasonable to believe that a miracle has occurred, given sufficient evidence. If God exists and created the laws of nature, God can act in ways that go beyond them, just as an author can write events into a story. The probability of a miracle therefore depends on how likely it is that God exists and would act in that way. Richard Swinburne argues that testimony, physical traces and the religious significance of an event can together make a miracle claim credible. Held by Richard Swinburne, Richard Price, C. S. Lewis.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism","Cumulative case theism"],"thinkers":["swinburne"],"position":"credibility_of_miracles","tags":["miracles","credibility","swinburne","divine action","testimony"]},{"term":"Impossibility of miracles","def":"Miracles cannot happen, because nature’s laws are necessary and cannot be broken, even by God.","more":"This view holds that miracles, understood as breaks in the laws of nature, are impossible, not merely unlikely. Its classic defender is Baruch Spinoza. In his Theological-Political Treatise (1670) he argued that the laws of nature follow from the necessity of God’s own nature (which he identified with nature itself). A breach of those laws would mean God acting against God’s own nature, which is absurd. Held by Baruch Spinoza.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Pantheism","Naturalism about religion"],"thinkers":["spinoza"],"position":"impossibility_of_miracles","tags":["miracles","spinoza","laws of nature","naturalism","necessitarianism"]},{"term":"Many-gods objection","def":"Pascal’s wager fails because there are many possible gods to bet on, and some might punish the wrong choice.","more":"The many-gods objection holds that Pascal’s wager cannot tell us what to believe, because it applies equally to countless possible gods. Pascal set up the wager as a choice between the Christian God and no God. But there are many religions, each offering rewards to believers, and one can imagine endless other possible gods, including one who rewards only atheists for their honesty. Since each of these has some non-zero probability, each yields an infinite expected value, and they cancel out. Held by Denis Diderot, J. L. Mackie.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Argument from religious experience"],"thinkers":["mackie"],"position":"many_gods_objection","tags":["many gods","pascal's wager","wager","objection"]},{"term":"Doxastic involuntarism","def":"You cannot simply choose to believe something because it would pay, so the wager cannot produce real belief.","more":"Doxastic involuntarism (from the Greek doxa, “belief”) is the view that belief is not under our direct voluntary control. You cannot believe it is raining just because someone offers you money to believe it; belief responds to how things seem to you, not to what you want. Bernard Williams argued that if you could decide to believe something regardless of its truth, you could not see it as a belief at all, since believing is regarding as true. Applied to Pascal’s wager, this means the wager cannot directly make anyone believe in God. Also called Involuntarism objection. Held by Bernard Williams.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["williams"],"position":"doxastic_involuntarism","tags":["involuntarism","belief","doxastic voluntarism","will to believe","pascal's wager","involuntarism objection"]},{"term":"Annihilation","def":"Death is the end of you; you simply stop existing.","more":"The annihilation view holds that when you die, you cease to exist. There is no soul that survives, no rebirth, no resurrection. Your experiences end, as they did not exist before you were born. Many naturalists hold this view, since they think the mind depends entirely on the brain. Also called Extinction view. Held by Epicurus, Lucretius, Bertrand Russell, David Hume.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Naturalism about religion"],"thinkers":["epicurus","lucretius","russell","hume"],"position":"annihilation","tags":["death","oblivion","no afterlife","mortality","nonexistence","extinction view"]},{"term":"Immortal soul","def":"Your soul, the real you, survives the death of your body and lives on.","more":"The immortal soul view holds that each person has, or is, a soul, a non-physical self that thinks and feels, and that it continues to exist after the body dies. Plato’s Phaedo gives the classic philosophical arguments: the soul is simple and so cannot fall apart, and it grasps eternal truths and so is akin to them. Descartes argued that the mind is a thinking thing distinct from the extended body, so it need not perish with it. Many religious traditions, including much popular Christian, Muslim and Jewish belief, as well as many Hindu and African traditions, hold that the soul survives death to face judgement or to join the ancestors. Held by Plato, René Descartes, Avicenna (Ibn Sina), Richard Swinburne.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism","Resurrection"],"thinkers":["plato","descartes","avicenna","swinburne","mbiti"],"position":"immortal_soul","tags":["immortality","soul","afterlife","life after death","plato","dualism"]},{"term":"Resurrection","def":"After death you will be raised again, body and soul, as a whole person.","more":"The resurrection view holds that the dead will be raised to new bodily life, usually at the end of history. It is central to traditional Christianity, Islam and much of Judaism. Unlike the immortal soul view, it treats the body as part of what a person is, so that real survival requires a renewed body. Many holders, like Thomas Aquinas, think the soul continues between death and resurrection, but in an incomplete state; others, called Christian materialists, such as Peter van Inwagen, hold that humans are wholly physical and that God will bring them back as the same persons. Also called Bodily resurrection. Held by Thomas Aquinas, Peter van Inwagen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism","Immortal soul","Credibility of miracles"],"thinkers":["aquinas","vaninwagen"],"position":"resurrection","tags":["bodily resurrection","afterlife","christianity","life after death"]},{"term":"Rebirth","def":"After death you are reborn into another life, again and again, until you are freed from the cycle.","more":"The rebirth view (also called reincarnation, transmigration or samsara) holds that death leads to a new birth, as a human, an animal, or another kind of being, depending on karma, the moral weight of one’s actions. The cycle can continue indefinitely, and the religious goal is to escape it: moksha in Hinduism and Jainism, nirvana in Buddhism. Hindu and Jain traditions usually hold that an enduring soul (atman or jiva) passes from life to life. Buddhism teaches rebirth without a permanent self: what continues is a stream of causes, like a flame passed from one candle to another. Also called Reincarnation, Samsara, Transmigration. Held by The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama), Mahavira, Pythagoras, Vasubandhu.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Karma theodicy","Non-theistic religion"],"thinkers":["buddha","mahavira","pythagoras","vasubandhu"],"position":"rebirth","tags":["reincarnation","samsara","transmigration","karma","afterlife"]},{"term":"Return to the whole","def":"At death the separate self dissolves back into the ultimate reality it was always part of.","more":"This view holds that death, or final liberation, ends the separate self by returning it to the whole from which it came. In Advaita Vedanta, the non-dualist school of Hinduism taught by Shankara, the individual self (atman) is in truth identical with ultimate reality (Brahman); liberation is the realisation of this identity, when the illusion of separateness ends, like a drop of water rejoining the ocean. Some Stoics held that at death the soul is reabsorbed into the rational fire or reason that runs through the cosmos; Marcus Aurelius considered this among the possibilities. Daoist texts such as the Zhuangzi describe death as a transformation within the endless changes of the Dao. Also called Absorption, Merging with the Absolute. Held by Adi Shankara, Marcus Aurelius, Zhuangzi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Non-theistic religion","Pantheism"],"thinkers":["shankara","marcus_aurelius","zhuangzi"],"position":"return_to_the_whole","tags":["union with god","nirvana","moksha","brahman","afterlife","absorption","merging with the absolute"]},{"term":"Agnosticism about the afterlife","def":"No one can know what happens after death, and I do not lean either way.","more":"Agnosticism about the afterlife holds that we lack the knowledge to say what, if anything, happens after death. The evidence that the mind depends on the brain suggests that death is the end; yet many hold that the mind may not be fully explained by the brain, and religious traditions offer accounts that cannot be checked from this side. Agnostics conclude that the honest response is to suspend judgment. Plato’s Socrates, at his trial in the Apology, said that death is either like a dreamless sleep or a journey to another place, and that neither need be feared; whether he fully held agnosticism is debated, since in the Phaedo he argues for the soul’s immortality. Held by Socrates.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Agnosticism"],"thinkers":["socrates"],"position":"agnosticism_about_the_afterlife","tags":["afterlife","agnosticism","death","life after death","uncertainty"]},{"term":"Presumption of atheism","def":"Without good evidence that God exists, the reasonable default is not to believe, just as with any other unsupported claim.","more":"The presumption of atheism says that the burden of proof lies on the person who claims God exists. Antony Flew’s essay “The Presumption of Atheism” (1976) compared it to the presumption of innocence in a court: we start from “no God” not because it has been proved, but because the believer is the one making the positive claim. Bertrand Russell made a similar point with his teapot orbiting the Sun: no one is obliged to believe in it just because it cannot be disproved. On this view, atheism is mainly the absence of belief that follows when the case for God fails, not a rival grand theory. Also called Negative atheism, Atheism by default. Held by Antony Flew, Bertrand Russell, George H. Smith.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Agnostic atheism","Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["flew","russell"],"position":"presumption_of_atheism","tags":["burden of proof","default atheism","antony flew","lack of evidence","negative atheism","atheism by default"]},{"term":"Naturalistic atheism","def":"There is no God because nature, studied by science, is all there is, and it needs nothing beyond itself to explain it.","more":"Naturalistic atheism grounds disbelief in a picture of the world: everything that exists is part of one natural order of matter, energy and the laws they obey, and that order needs no creator or designer. The famous story has Laplace telling Napoleon that his account of the heavens had no need of the God hypothesis. Naturalistic atheists argue that every time science has explained something once credited to gods, such as lightning, disease or the variety of living things, the explanation has been natural. Graham Oppy argues that naturalism is simpler than theism and explains everything theism explains at least as well. Also called Positive atheism. Held by Graham Oppy, J. L. Mackie, Sean Carroll.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Naturalism about religion"],"thinkers":["mackie"],"position":"naturalistic_atheism","tags":["scientific atheism","naturalism","materialism","no god","science and religion","positive atheism"]},{"term":"Atheism from incoherence","def":"There is no God because the very idea of God is inconsistent: some of the properties God is said to have rule each other out.","more":"This view argues that God, as usually described, could not exist, as a square circle could not. Its arguments target the divine attributes. Can an all-powerful being make a stone it cannot lift? Can a being who knows everything know what it is like to learn, or to be wrong? Held by Michael Martin, Theodore Drange.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism"],"thinkers":["martin_m"],"position":"atheism_from_incoherence","tags":["incoherence of god","logical atheism","omnipotence paradox","divine attributes","impossible god"]},{"term":"Evidential agnosticism","def":"We cannot yet tell whether God exists, because the evidence and arguments on each side are evenly balanced or too weak to settle it.","more":"Evidential agnosticism suspends judgement about God because of the evidence as it stands, not because the question is beyond human reach in principle. Some evidential agnostics think the arguments on both sides are strong and roughly cancel out; others think they are all weak. Either way, belief and disbelief both go beyond what the evidence supports. T. Also called Empirical agnosticism, Temporary agnosticism. Held by Thomas Henry Huxley, Anthony Kenny.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Agnosticism","Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["huxley","kenny"],"position":"evidential_agnosticism","tags":["weak agnosticism","temporary agnosticism","suspending judgment","undecided about god","evidence for god","empirical agnosticism"]},{"term":"Principled agnosticism","def":"Whether God exists is beyond what human minds could ever know, whatever evidence comes in.","more":"Principled agnosticism, sometimes called strong or permanent agnosticism, holds that the question of God lies beyond the reach of human knowledge for good. Protagoras is reported to have said that he could not know whether the gods exist, because the subject is obscure and human life is short. Immanuel Kant argued in the Critique of Pure Reason (1781) that reason, which works by applying concepts to possible experience, cannot prove or disprove a being beyond all experience; every such proof falls into illusion. Unlike the evidential agnostic, the principled agnostic does not expect better arguments to help. Also called Strong agnosticism, Permanent agnosticism. Held by Protagoras, Immanuel Kant, Thomas Henry Huxley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Agnosticism"],"thinkers":["protagoras","kant","huxley"],"position":"principled_agnosticism","tags":["strong agnosticism","permanent agnosticism","unknowable god","huxley","limits of knowledge"]},{"term":"Apatheism","def":"Whether God exists may be unknowable, and in any case it matters little for how to live, so the question can be set aside.","more":"Apatheism (from “apathy” and “theism”) treats the question of God as unimportant to practical life. An apatheist may be unsure whether God exists, but does not think the answer would change much about how to live well or treat others. The attitude has an ancient relative in the Buddha’s refusal to answer certain metaphysical questions, such as whether the world is eternal: in the parable of the poisoned arrow, a wounded man who insists on knowing who shot him before accepting treatment will die first. The journalist Jonathan Rauch popularised the word in a 2003 essay, praising a relaxed indifference to religious questions as a civic virtue. Also called Pragmatic agnosticism. Held by The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama), Jonathan Rauch.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Agnosticism"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"position":"apatheism","tags":["apathy about god","pragmatic atheism","indifference to religion","practical atheism","religious indifference","pragmatic agnosticism"]},{"term":"Advaita Vedānta","def":"There is one ultimate reality, Brahman, beyond all qualities, and your deepest self (ātman) is identical with it.","more":"Advaita Vedānta (“non-dual Vedānta”) is a Hindu school of thought, given its classic form by Ādi Śaṅkara. It holds that the only ultimate reality is Brahman: pure being, consciousness and bliss, without parts or personal qualities. The world of many separate things is not simply unreal, but it is an appearance (māyā) that hides the one reality, as a rope in dim light may look like a snake. Your true self, ātman, is not your body or personality but Brahman itself, as the Upanishadic teaching “tat tvam asi” (“you are that”) declares. Also called Advaita, Non-dualism. Held by Adi Shankara, Gauḍapāda.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Non-theistic religion","Return to the whole","Pantheism"],"thinkers":["shankara"],"position":"advaita_vedanta","tags":["advaita","vedanta","nondualism","non-dualism","brahman","atman","shankara","maya"]},{"term":"Buddhist non-theism","def":"There is no creator God and no lasting self, but there is a real path that ends suffering and rebirth.","more":"Buddhist non-theism is the view, shared by most Buddhist traditions, that the world was not made by a creator God. Things arise from causes and conditions in a beginningless web, governed by karma, the moral law of action and result. What matters is release from suffering, nirvana, reached by understanding that all things are impermanent and without a lasting self, and by living the eightfold path. Gods and spirits may exist, but as beings inside the cycle of rebirth, not as the ultimate reality. Held by The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama), Nagarjuna, Dharmakirti.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Non-theistic religion","Buddhist no-self","Karma theodicy","Rebirth"],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna","dharmakirti"],"position":"buddhist_nontheism","tags":["buddhism","anatta","no-self","nontheism","four noble truths","nirvana","no creator"]},{"term":"Daoism","def":"The ultimate is the Dao, the nameless Way: a source and pattern all things follow naturally, with no plan or will.","more":"Daoism takes its name from the Dao, “the Way”. The Daodejing, traditionally credited to Laozi, opens by saying that the Dao that can be spoken of is not the constant Dao. It is the nameless source from which all things arise and the pattern by which they move, like water flowing to low places, without striving or intention. It is not a creator with a plan, and it does not reward or punish. Held by Laozi, Zhuangzi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Non-theistic religion","Pantheism"],"thinkers":["laozi","zhuangzi"],"position":"daoism","tags":["taoism","dao","tao","the way","laozi","wu wei","tao te ching","daodejing"]},{"term":"Confucian Heaven","def":"Heaven (tian) is the source of moral order in the world, worthy of reverence, but on many readings it is not a person who listens and decides.","more":"In Confucian thought, Heaven (tian) is the highest reality: the source of the moral order and of human nature, something the sage reveres and seeks to understand. Whether Heaven is personal is contested. In the Analects, Confucius speaks as if Heaven knows him and has given him a mission, yet he also says Heaven does not speak, and the four seasons simply proceed. Mencius tied Heaven to the goodness within human nature. Also called Tian, Confucian religiousness. Held by Confucius, Xunzi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Non-theistic religion"],"thinkers":["confucius","xunzi"],"position":"confucian_heaven","tags":["tian","heaven","mandate of heaven","confucianism","tianming","moral order","confucian religiousness"]},{"term":"Jain non-theism","def":"There is no creator, but every soul is eternal and can free itself from karma and rebirth by its own effort.","more":"Jainism, taught in its present form by Mahāvīra in ancient India, holds that the universe was never created and will never end. It contains countless eternal souls (jīva), each bound to matter by karma, which Jains understand as a fine physical substance that sticks to the soul through harmful action. Through non-violence (ahiṃsā), truthfulness, non-attachment and strict discipline, a soul can shed its karma and reach liberation, rising to the top of the universe in perfect knowledge and bliss. Liberated souls (siddhas) and the great teachers (tīrthaṅkaras) are revered, but they do not create or govern the world. Also called Jainism. Held by Mahavira, Umasvati.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Non-theistic religion","Karma theodicy","Rebirth"],"thinkers":["mahavira","umasvati"],"position":"jainism","tags":["jainism","nontheism","karma","ahimsa","moksha","liberation","no creator"]},{"term":"Verificationist noncognitivism","def":"Statements about God are literally meaningless, because no possible experience could show them true or false.","more":"This is the version of theological noncognitivism defended by the logical positivists. They held a verification principle: a statement about the world has factual meaning only if some possible observation would count toward its truth or falsity. A. J. Held by A. J. Ayer, Rudolf Carnap.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theological noncognitivism"],"thinkers":["ayer","carnap"],"position":"verificationist_noncognitivism","tags":["logical positivism","meaninglessness of god talk","verification principle","ayer","religious language"]},{"term":"Religious expressivism","def":"Religious statements do not describe facts; they express a commitment to a way of life, backed by stories that inspire it.","more":"Religious expressivism holds that religious language works more like moral language than like science. R. B. Braithwaite, in “An Empiricist’s View of the Nature of Religious Belief” (1955), argued that a Christian’s assertions mainly express an intention to live a life of love (agape), supported by stories that need not be believed as literal history. Also called Religious non-realism. Held by R. B. Braithwaite, Don Cupitt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theological noncognitivism","Wittgensteinian view of religion"],"thinkers":[],"position":"religious_expressivism","tags":["noncognitivism","religious language","way of life","braithwaite","expressivism about religion","religious non-realism"]},{"term":"Wittgensteinian view of religion","def":"Religious belief is not a hypothesis about the world but a way of seeing and living, with its own grammar and standards.","more":"This view draws on Ludwig Wittgenstein’s remarks on religion, recorded by students in lectures around 1938 and published in 1966. Wittgenstein said that someone who believes in a Last Judgement and someone who does not are not contradicting each other, as two forecasters disagree about tomorrow’s rain; the believer has a picture that guides their whole life. D. Z. Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein, D. Z. Phillips, Norman Malcolm.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theological noncognitivism","Fideism","Religious expressivism"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein","phillips_dz"],"position":"wittgensteinian_fideism","tags":["wittgenstein","fideism","form of life","language games","d. z. phillips","religious language"]},{"term":"Agnostic theism","def":"I believe there is a God, but I do not claim to know it.","more":"Agnostic theism separates belief from knowledge. The agnostic theist believes in God, often with deep commitment, but holds that this belief falls short of knowledge: it rests on trust, hope or a balance of reasons that could be mistaken. Immanuel Kant wrote that he had to deny knowledge in order to make room for faith; on his view, God cannot be known by theoretical reason, but belief in God is required by morality. Many religious traditions also stress that God surpasses human understanding. Held by Immanuel Kant, Leslie Weatherhead.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Theism","Agnosticism","The will to believe"],"thinkers":["kant"],"position":"agnostic_theism","tags":["fideism","faith without knowledge","belief without certainty","agnosticism","theism"]},{"term":"Agnostic atheism","def":"I do not believe there is a God, but I do not claim to know there is none.","more":"Agnostic atheism, like agnostic theism, separates belief from knowledge. The agnostic atheist lacks belief in God, or thinks God very unlikely, but does not claim certainty that no God exists. George H. Smith used the term in Atheism: The Case Against God (1974), and many atheists describe themselves this way. Held by George H. Smith, Richard Dawkins.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Presumption of atheism","Agnosticism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"agnostic_atheism","tags":["weak atheism","negative atheism","lack of belief","atheism","agnosticism"]},{"term":"Religious naturalism","def":"There is no God or supernatural realm, but nature itself can be the object of reverence, gratitude and religious practice.","more":"Religious naturalism agrees with atheists that there is no God and nothing beyond nature, but holds that the natural world deserves the attitudes religion has traditionally directed elsewhere: awe, reverence, gratitude, humility, and a sense of belonging. The biologist Ursula Goodenough, in The Sacred Depths of Nature (1998), drew such responses from the scientific story of the universe and of life. Ronald Dworkin, in Religion without God (2013), argued that a religious attitude, the conviction that life has objective meaning and nature is wondrous, does not require belief in God. Albert Einstein spoke of a “cosmic religious feeling”. Held by Ursula Goodenough, Ronald Dworkin, Albert Einstein.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Atheism","Pantheism","Non-theistic religion"],"thinkers":["dworkin","einstein"],"position":"religious_naturalism","tags":["spiritual naturalism","nature worship","reverence for nature","pantheism","religious humanism","awe"]},{"term":"Harmony of faith and reason","def":"Reason can establish some truths about God, and faith goes beyond reason without contradicting it.","more":"This view, classically set out by Thomas Aquinas, holds that faith and reason are two sources of truth that cannot really conflict, since both come from God. Some truths, such as that God exists, can be shown by philosophical argument; Aquinas called them “preambles of faith”. Others, such as the Trinity, exceed what reason can prove and are accepted on God’s authority in revelation, though reason can show they are not absurd and that trusting the revelation is reasonable. Averroes, in his Decisive Treatise, argued that philosophy and scripture agree when properly read. Also called Faith seeking understanding. Held by Thomas Aquinas, Averroes (Ibn Rushd), Anselm of Canterbury.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Evidentialism","Classical theism"],"thinkers":["aquinas","averroes","anselm"],"position":"harmony_of_faith_and_reason","tags":["faith and reason","natural theology","aquinas","reason and revelation","compatibility of faith and reason","faith seeking understanding"]},{"term":"Divine impassibility","def":"God cannot be acted on or made to suffer by creatures; God’s love is perfect and unchanging, not an emotion that rises and falls.","more":"Divine impassibility is the traditional view, found in the Church Fathers, Anselm, Aquinas, Maimonides and many Islamic theologians, that God does not undergo passions: states that happen to one because of something else, such as grief or anger. God is perfect, so nothing can add to or take away from God’s happiness. This does not make God cold. Anselm, in the Proslogion, asked how God can be compassionate without feeling compassion, and answered that God is compassionate in terms of what we experience, since God frees us from misery, but not in terms of anything God undergoes. Held by Anselm of Canterbury, Thomas Aquinas, Thomas Weinandy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Classical theism"],"thinkers":["anselm","aquinas"],"position":"divine_impassibility","tags":["impassibility","immutable god","unchanging god","classical theism","god cannot suffer"]},{"term":"Divine passibility","def":"God is genuinely affected by creatures: God shares in their joys and suffers with them.","more":"Divine passibility holds that God can be moved by what happens in the world: grieved by evil, delighted by goodness, suffering with those who suffer. Many modern theologians have taken this view. Abraham Joshua Heschel, in The Prophets (1962), described the God of the Hebrew prophets as a God of “pathos”, deeply involved in human affairs. Jürgen Moltmann’s The Crucified God (1972) argued that God suffers in the cross of Christ. Held by Jürgen Moltmann, Alfred North Whitehead, Abraham Joshua Heschel.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Religion","related":["Open theism","Process theism","Panentheism"],"thinkers":["whitehead"],"position":"divine_passibility","tags":["passibility","suffering god","open theism","process theology","god suffers"]},{"term":"Aesthetic realism","def":"Beauty is a real feature of things, there whether or not anyone enjoys it.","more":"Aesthetic realism says that when you call a sunset or a sonata beautiful, you describe the thing itself, the way you do when you call it red or loud. Your statement is true or false, and people who disagree cannot both be right. Beauty depends on other features (colours, shapes, sounds and how they fit together), but it is not just a fact about your feelings. Realists differ on what beauty is: older thinkers such as Plato saw it as a reflection of a perfect Form (an ideal pattern beyond the physical world), while modern realists such as Nick Zangwill treat it as a real quality that rides on ordinary physical ones. Held by Plato, Plotinus, Nick Zangwill, Eddy Zemach.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Ideal-critic theory"],"thinkers":["plato","plotinus","zangwill"],"position":"aesthetic_realism","tags":["objectivity of beauty","beauty is objective","aesthetic objectivism","realism about beauty","objective taste"]},{"term":"Ideal-critic theory","def":"Something is beautiful if the best possible judges would delight in it.","more":"The ideal-critic theory, from David Hume’s essay “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757), says that beauty is not a feature of objects on their own, but nor is it whatever anyone happens to like. Beauty is a response-dependent property: something is beautiful if it would please ideal critics. Hume listed what such critics need: a delicate sense that notices fine detail, long practice, the habit of comparing many works, freedom from prejudice, and good sense. Where such judges agree, their verdict sets the standard, so ordinary people can be wrong. Also called Humean theory of taste, Response-dependence theory of beauty. Held by David Hume, Jerrold Levinson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic realism","Kantian theory of taste"],"thinkers":["hume","levinson"],"position":"ideal_critic_theory","tags":["ideal observer","true judges","hume","standard of taste","ideal critic","qualified judges","humean theory of taste","response-dependence theory of beauty"]},{"term":"Kantian theory of taste","def":"Beauty rests on a feeling, yet when you judge something beautiful you rightly expect everyone to agree.","more":"Immanuel Kant, in the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), argued that a judgement of beauty is based on a feeling of pleasure, not on a concept or a rule. You cannot prove a rose is beautiful by listing its features. Yet the pleasure is special. It is disinterested (it does not depend on wanting to own, eat or use the thing) and it comes from the free play of imagination and understanding as you take in its form. Also called Kantian universality. Held by Immanuel Kant, Hannah Ginsborg.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Ideal-critic theory","Formalism"],"thinkers":["kant"],"position":"kantian_theory_of_taste","tags":["kant","judgment of taste","universal validity","critique of judgment","disinterested pleasure","subjective universality","kantian universality"]},{"term":"Aesthetic relativism","def":"There are real standards of beauty, but only inside a culture or tradition, never one above them all.","more":"Aesthetic relativism says that judgements of beauty and artistic merit can be correct or incorrect, but only relative to a framework: a culture, a tradition or a community’s practices. Inside classical Indian music, Japanese tea ceremony or Western opera, there are real experts and real mistakes. But there is no neutral standpoint from which to rank the traditions themselves. This differs from subjectivism, which denies any standard at all, and from realism and Kantian views, which claim standards that hold for everyone. Held by Barbara Herrnstein Smith.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Sociological critique of taste","Aesthetic subjectivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"aesthetic_relativism","tags":["cultural relativism","relativism about beauty","taste relativism","cultural standards of beauty"]},{"term":"Aesthetic subjectivism","def":"Beauty is in the eye of the beholder: no one’s taste is better than anyone else’s.","more":"Aesthetic subjectivism says that calling something beautiful reports, or simply reflects, your own pleasure in it. If you love a song and I find it dull, neither of us is wrong, because there is no fact about the song’s beauty beyond how it strikes each listener. George Santayana, in The Sense of Beauty (1896), described beauty as pleasure that we treat as though it were a quality of the thing. The view differs from the ideal-critic theory, which makes some tastes better; from relativism, which allows real standards inside traditions; and from non-cognitivism, which says beauty talk expresses feeling rather than reporting it. Held by George Santayana.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic relativism","Aesthetic non-cognitivism"],"thinkers":["santayana"],"position":"aesthetic_subjectivism","tags":["beauty is in the eye of the beholder","subjectivism","taste is subjective","matter of taste","personal taste"]},{"term":"Aesthetic non-cognitivism","def":"Saying “that is beautiful” does not state a fact; it expresses delight, like saying “Wow!”","more":"Non-cognitivism says that aesthetic words such as “beautiful” and “ugly” do not describe anything, not even the speaker’s feelings. Instead they express an attitude, the way a cheer or a sigh does. “Cognitive” here means fact-stating, so non-cognitivism denies that aesthetic judgements are true or false at all. A. Also called Aesthetic expressivism, Aesthetic emotivism. Held by A. J. Ayer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic subjectivism"],"thinkers":["ayer"],"position":"aesthetic_non_cognitivism","tags":["aesthetic expressivism","emotivism","non-cognitivism","beauty as feeling","aesthetic judgment","aesthetic emotivism"]},{"term":"Scientific cognitivism","def":"To appreciate nature properly, you must appreciate it as what it is, using what science tells you.","more":"Scientific cognitivism, developed by Allen Carlson, says that appreciating nature is different from appreciating art, but just as much a matter of knowledge. With art, knowing that a painting is a Cubist portrait shapes what you should notice. With nature, the relevant knowledge comes from natural science: geology, ecology, biology. A whale looks clumsy if you think of it as a fish, graceful if you know it is a mammal adapted to ocean life. Also called Natural environmental model. Held by Allen Carlson, Glenn Parsons.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Positive aesthetics","Aesthetic contextualism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"scientific_cognitivism","tags":["environmental aesthetics","nature appreciation","allen carlson","science and nature","natural environment model","natural environmental model"]},{"term":"Positive aesthetics","def":"All wild nature, untouched by humans, is beautiful.","more":"Positive aesthetics is the claim that the natural world, so far as humans have not altered it, has mostly or wholly positive aesthetic value. Unlike art, which can be clumsy or ugly, wild nature is never aesthetically bad, though some parts may be more striking than others. A mudflat, a dead tree or a spider eating its prey can all be appreciated as beautiful when properly understood. Allen Carlson defended it in “Nature and Positive Aesthetics” (1984), tying it to scientific cognitivism: science finds order and intelligibility in nature, and order is something we find beautiful. Held by Allen Carlson, Holmes Rolston III.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Scientific cognitivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"positive_aesthetics","tags":["beauty of nature","wilderness","nature is beautiful","environmental aesthetics","allen carlson"]},{"term":"Arousal model","def":"You can appreciate nature properly just by being moved by it, without any science.","more":"The arousal model, proposed by Noël Carroll in “On Being Moved by Nature” (1993), says that one fitting way to appreciate nature is simply to be emotionally moved by it: awed by a thundering waterfall, calmed by a still lake. These responses are not mere private feelings; they are appropriate to what you notice (the waterfall really is huge and powerful). So they can be correct or incorrect, but they need no scientific knowledge. Carroll did not reject scientific appreciation; he argued it is one legitimate mode among others. Held by Noël Carroll.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Engagement model"],"thinkers":["carroll"],"position":"arousal_model","tags":["noel carroll","emotional response to nature","being moved by nature","environmental aesthetics"]},{"term":"Landscape model","def":"Nature is best appreciated the way you appreciate a painting: as a scene, framed and viewed for its form and colour.","more":"The landscape model treats nature as scenery. You appreciate a valley by standing at a viewpoint and taking it in as a picture, attending to its colours, composition and lines. The model grew out of the eighteenth-century taste for the picturesque, promoted by writers such as William Gilpin, who taught travellers to look for scenes that would make good paintings (some even carried tinted mirrors to frame the view). The name “landscape model” comes from Allen Carlson, who criticised it. Also called Picturesque model, Scenery model. Held by William Gilpin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Formalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"landscape_model","tags":["scenery","picturesque","scenic beauty","environmental aesthetics","nature as painting","picturesque model","scenery model"]},{"term":"Engagement model","def":"Nature is appreciated by being immersed in it with all your senses, not by looking at it from outside.","more":"The engagement model, defended by Arnold Berleant, says that appreciating nature means taking part in it. You do not stand back and view a forest; you walk through it, smell it, hear it, feel the ground shift underfoot. Berleant argued that the traditional idea of aesthetic appreciation as calm, distant contemplation suits paintings in galleries but not environments, which surround us. The distinction between the appreciator and the thing appreciated starts to break down. Held by Arnold Berleant.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Arousal model"],"thinkers":[],"position":"engagement_model","tags":["immersion","arnold berleant","multisensory","environmental aesthetics","participatory appreciation"]},{"term":"Cluster account of art","def":"No single feature makes something art, but having enough of the usual features does.","more":"The cluster account, developed by Berys Gaut, says there is a list of features that count towards something being art, such as having beauty or other aesthetic qualities, expressing emotion, being intellectually challenging, being complex and coherent, showing skill, being original, being made with an intention to be art, and belonging to an established art form. No single feature is necessary: a work can be art without being beautiful. But if something has enough of these features, it is art. Denis Dutton offered a similar list of features found in art across cultures. Held by Berys Gaut, Denis Dutton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Anti-essentialism about art"],"thinkers":["gaut","dutton"],"position":"cluster_account_of_art","tags":["berys gaut","definition of art","what is art","criteria of art","cluster concept"]},{"term":"Anti-essentialism about art","def":"Art cannot be defined: no list of features will ever fit all art, because art keeps changing.","more":"Anti-essentialism, put forward by Morris Weitz in “The Role of Theory in Aesthetics” (1956), says that art has no essence: no set of features shared by all and only artworks. Drawing on Ludwig Wittgenstein’s idea of family resemblance (games share no single feature but overlap like the faces of a family), Weitz argued that artworks are linked by overlapping similarities. More importantly, “art” is an open concept. Artists constantly do new things, and each time we must decide whether to extend the concept to cover them. Held by Morris Weitz, William Kennick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Cluster account of art"],"thinkers":["weitz"],"position":"anti_essentialism_about_art","tags":["morris weitz","open concept","indefinability of art","family resemblance","definition of art"]},{"term":"Mimetic theory of art","def":"Art is imitation: a work of art represents something, such as a scene, a person or an action.","more":"The mimetic theory, the oldest Western theory of art, says that what makes something art is that it imitates or represents. “Mimesis” is the Greek word for imitation. Plato in the Republic described painting and poetry as imitations of appearances, and distrusted them for that reason. Aristotle in the Poetics treated imitation as natural to humans and a source of learning and pleasure, and analysed tragedy as the imitation of an action. Also called Imitation theory, Representational theory of art. Held by Plato, Aristotle.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Cognitivism about art"],"thinkers":["plato","aristotle"],"position":"mimetic_theory_of_art","tags":["mimesis","imitation","representation","representational art","plato","aristotle","imitation theory","representational theory of art"]},{"term":"Aesthetic definition of art","def":"Something is art if it is made to be experienced for its beauty or form, rather than for some practical use.","more":"Aesthetic definitions say that art is defined by its purpose: to give aesthetic experience, or to have aesthetic qualities such as beauty, gracefulness or unity. Monroe Beardsley defined an artwork roughly as something made with the intention of giving it the capacity to afford an experience with marked aesthetic character. Nick Zangwill defended a related “aesthetic creation” theory: art is something made because the maker intends it to have aesthetic properties that depend on its other features. Clive Bell’s formalist theory, that art is what has “significant form,” is an early relative. Also called Aesthetic functionalism. Held by Monroe Beardsley, Nick Zangwill, Clive Bell.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic empiricism","Formalism"],"thinkers":["beardsley","zangwill","bell"],"position":"aesthetic_definition_of_art","tags":["aesthetic function","definition of art","beauty","aesthetic experience","monroe beardsley","aesthetic functionalism"]},{"term":"Historical definition of art","def":"Something is art if its maker intends it to be regarded in some way that earlier art has been rightly regarded.","more":"Jerrold Levinson’s historical definition, first set out in “Defining Art Historically” (1979), says that an artwork is something a person seriously intends to be regarded in one of the ways that earlier artworks have correctly been regarded: looked at for its form, read for its meaning, felt for its expression, and so on. Art is defined by its link to the history of art, which is why it keeps changing yet stays continuous. Someone who hangs driftwood on a wall, intending it to be gazed at the way people gaze at sculpture, makes art even without any gallery. Unlike the institutional theory, no artworld needs to confer status; the individual’s intention, tied to history, is enough. Held by Jerrold Levinson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Institutional theory of art","Aesthetic contextualism"],"thinkers":["levinson"],"position":"historical_definition_of_art","tags":["historical definition","jerrold levinson","intentional-historical","art history","definition of art"]},{"term":"Embodied meaning theory","def":"Something is art if it is about something and embodies that meaning, which only art history and theory make possible.","more":"Arthur Danto was struck by Andy Warhol’s Brillo Box (1964), which looked just like supermarket cartons. Since the two could not be told apart by eye, he concluded that what makes something art cannot be visible. In “The Artworld” (1964) he argued that seeing something as art requires an atmosphere of art theory and a knowledge of art history. In The Transfiguration of the Commonplace (1981) he proposed that artworks are embodied meanings: they are about something, and they embody that meaning in their form. Also called Artworld theory (Danto). Held by Arthur Danto.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Institutional theory of art","Aesthetic contextualism","Cognitivism about art"],"thinkers":["danto"],"position":"embodied_meaning_theory","tags":["arthur danto","embodied meanings","aboutness","artworld","definition of art","artworld theory (danto)"]},{"term":"Aesthetic empiricism","def":"A work’s value as art lies in the experience it gives to someone who attends to it closely.","more":"Aesthetic empiricism says that what makes art good is the experience it offers. A great painting rewards looking, a great symphony rewards listening, and a work’s artistic value is the value of the experience it gives to an attentive, informed audience. Monroe Beardsley defended a version called instrumentalism: art is valuable because it can produce aesthetic experience marked by unity, intensity and complexity. Malcolm Budd argued that a work’s artistic value is the intrinsic value of the experience it offers. Also called Aesthetic experience theory, Instrumentalism about artistic value. Held by Monroe Beardsley, Malcolm Budd, Alfred Lessing.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Formalism","Aesthetic definition of art"],"thinkers":["beardsley"],"position":"aesthetic_empiricism","tags":["aesthetic experience","experience of art","beardsley","value of art","close attention","aesthetic experience theory","instrumentalism about artistic value"]},{"term":"Cognitivism about art","def":"Great art is great largely because of what it shows us: it gives real knowledge or understanding of life and the world.","more":"Cognitivism about art says that art can give us knowledge or understanding, and that this is a central part of its value as art. A novel can show what jealousy is like from the inside; a painting can teach you to see light differently; a play can make vivid the cost of pride. Nelson Goodman argued in Languages of Art (1968) that the arts, like the sciences, are ways of understanding and organising the world. Martha Nussbaum argued in Love’s Knowledge (1990) that some moral truths can only be shown through the detail of literature, not through abstract theory. Held by Nelson Goodman, Martha Nussbaum, Catherine Elgin.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Mimetic theory of art","Ethicism","Immoralism"],"thinkers":["goodman","nussbaum"],"position":"cognitivism_about_art","tags":["knowledge from art","art and knowledge","aesthetic cognitivism","nelson goodman","understanding through art"]},{"term":"Achievement view of art","def":"A work’s value includes what its maker achieved: the skill, daring and originality it took to make it.","more":"The achievement view says that an artwork is not just an object to experience; it is also the result of a performance, something someone did. Part of its value lies in that achievement: solving a hard problem, inventing a new style, taking a risk. Denis Dutton argued in “Artistic Crimes” (1979) that this is why forgeries are worse as art: a forger who copies Vermeer’s style in 1937 achieves far less than Vermeer did by inventing it. David Davies, in Art as Performance (2004), went further, holding that the artwork really is the generative performance, not just the object. Also called Art as achievement, Originality view. Held by Denis Dutton, David Davies, Gregory Currie.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic contextualism","Intention requirement"],"thinkers":["dutton"],"position":"achievement_view_of_art","tags":["achievement","skill","originality","denis dutton","artistic achievement","art as achievement","originality view"]},{"term":"Pluralism about artistic value","def":"There is no single thing that makes art great: different works are great for different reasons.","more":"Pluralism about artistic value says that works can be good as art in many different ways: by being beautiful, expressive, insightful, original, formally inventive, morally illuminating or emotionally powerful. None of these is the one true source of artistic value, and none is always required. A Bach fugue and a war novel are great for different reasons. Robert Stecker and Berys Gaut have defended versions of this view. Held by Robert Stecker, Berys Gaut.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Cluster account of art"],"thinkers":["gaut"],"position":"pluralism_about_artistic_value","tags":["value pluralism","value of art","artistic value","multiple values"]},{"term":"Actual intentionalism","def":"A work means what its maker actually intended it to mean, as long as the work can bear that meaning.","more":"Actual intentionalism says that the correct interpretation of a work is fixed by what its creator actually intended. E. D. Hirsch, in Validity in Interpretation (1967), argued that without the author’s intention as a standard, there is no fact about what a text means, only readers’ guesses. Also called Moderate actual intentionalism. Held by E. D. Hirsch, Noël Carroll, Paisley Livingston.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Intention requirement"],"thinkers":["hirsch_ed","carroll"],"position":"actual_intentionalism","tags":["authorial intention","author's intent","intentionalism","meaning of art","interpretation","moderate actual intentionalism"]},{"term":"Hypothetical intentionalism","def":"A work means what an ideal, well-informed reader would best guess its maker intended.","more":"Hypothetical intentionalism, defended by Jerrold Levinson and William Tolhurst, says that a work’s meaning is the intention that an ideal reader would most reasonably attribute to its author. This reader knows the work well and knows its public context: the author’s other works, the period, the genre, the publicly known facts about the author’s life. But the reader does not use private evidence such as a secret diary. So if every informed reader would take a poem to be about lost love, that is its meaning, even if the author’s diary says otherwise. Held by Jerrold Levinson, William Tolhurst.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Actual intentionalism"],"thinkers":["levinson"],"position":"hypothetical_intentionalism","tags":["ideal reader","intentionalism","interpretation","authorial intention","jerrold levinson"]},{"term":"Anti-intentionalism","def":"A work means what its words and forms mean; the maker’s intentions are beside the point.","more":"Anti-intentionalism says that the meaning of a work is fixed by the work itself, read in the light of the public conventions of language and art, not by what the author meant. W. K. Wimsatt and Monroe Beardsley named the error of appealing to intentions the “intentional fallacy” in a 1946 essay. Also called Textualism, New Criticism. Held by W. K. Wimsatt, Monroe Beardsley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Formalism"],"thinkers":["wimsatt","beardsley"],"position":"anti_intentionalism","tags":["intentional fallacy","wimsatt","beardsley","new criticism","author's intent","formalism","textualism"]},{"term":"Reader-response theory","def":"Readers make meaning: a work has no single correct meaning, only the meanings readers bring to it.","more":"Reader-response theory, and related ideas, says that meaning is not fixed in a text or by its author but is produced in the act of reading. Roland Barthes, in “The Death of the Author” (1967), argued that treating the author as the source of meaning closes down a text, and that the reader must be freed from the author’s control. Stanley Fish argued that what readers find in a text depends on their “interpretive communities,” shared ways of reading. On these views, the lost-love reading and the grandmother reading of a poem are both real, and neither is simply the correct one. Held by Roland Barthes, Stanley Fish.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic relativism"],"thinkers":["barthes","fish"],"position":"reader_response_theory","tags":["reader response","reception theory","death of the author","interpretation","reader's meaning"]},{"term":"Moderate moralism","def":"A moral flaw sometimes makes a work worse as art: when it stops the work from achieving what it aims at.","more":"Moderate moralism, defended by Noël Carroll in “Moderate Moralism” (1996), says that some moral defects in some works are also artistic defects. The key case is when a work asks its audience to respond in a way that a morally decent person cannot. If a comedy asks you to laugh at cruelty and decent viewers cannot, the comedy fails as comedy; if a tragedy asks you to pity a character who is plainly vicious, the pity does not come and the tragedy falls flat. The moral flaw causes an artistic failure. Held by Noël Carroll, David Hume.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Ethicism"],"thinkers":["carroll","hume"],"position":"moderate_moralism","tags":["moralism","noel carroll","ethics of art","art and morality","ethical criticism"]},{"term":"Autonomism","def":"Artistic and moral value are separate: a work can be morally ugly and still artistically great.","more":"Autonomism says that art is an autonomous (self-governing) realm with its own standards. Moral evaluation of a work is a different kind of judgement from artistic evaluation, and moral flaws do not make a work worse as art. Oscar Wilde put it boldly in the preface to The Picture of Dorian Gray (1891): “There is no such thing as a moral or an immoral book. Books are well written, or badly written. Also called Aestheticism. Held by Oscar Wilde, Clive Bell, James Anderson, Jeffrey Dean.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Formalism"],"thinkers":["wilde","bell"],"position":"autonomism","tags":["aestheticism","art for art's sake","art and morality","separation of art and ethics"]},{"term":"Ethicism","def":"Any moral flaw in the attitude a work invites always counts against it as art, though other strengths can outweigh it.","more":"Ethicism, defended by Berys Gaut in “The Ethical Criticism of Art” (1998) and Art, Emotion and Ethics (2007), says that the ethical assessment of attitudes a work manifests is a legitimate part of its artistic assessment. If a work invites you to admire cruelty or to find suffering funny, that always counts against it as art, to some degree. If it invites morally fitting responses, that always counts in its favour. But these are only pro tanto effects (they count, but can be outweighed): a work with a moral flaw can still be great overall because of its other strengths. Held by Berys Gaut.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Moderate moralism","Cognitivism about art"],"thinkers":["gaut"],"position":"ethicism","tags":["berys gaut","moralism","ethical criticism","art and morality","ethics of art"]},{"term":"Immoralism","def":"A moral flaw in a work can sometimes make it better as art.","more":"Immoralism, in the philosophy of art, says that a work’s moral defects can sometimes be artistic merits. A novel that gets you to side with a monster, or a film that makes you enjoy violence, may teach you something about yourself and human nature that morally impeccable art cannot. Matthew Kieran argued in “Forbidden Knowledge: The Challenge of Immoralism” (2003) that such works can give real understanding. Daniel Jacobson defended immoral art in “In Praise of Immoral Art” (1997), and A. Held by Matthew Kieran, A. W. Eaton, Daniel Jacobson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Cognitivism about art"],"thinkers":["kieran","eaton"],"position":"immoralism_about_art","tags":["immoral art","matthew kieran","art and morality","transgressive art","ethics of art"]},{"term":"Radical moralism","def":"Art’s purpose is moral, so art that corrupts is bad art, however well made.","more":"Radical moralism says that a work’s moral value is the decisive measure of its value as art. Plato, in the Republic, proposed banning most poetry from his ideal city because it corrupts character by imitating vice and stirring up unruly emotions. Leo Tolstoy, in What Is Art? (1897), held that the best art transmits the highest feelings, those that unite humanity, and condemned much admired art, including some of his own earlier novels, as bad because it spread snobbish or harmful feelings. Also called Moralism. Held by Plato, Leo Tolstoy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Ethicism","Expression theory of art"],"thinkers":["plato","tolstoy"],"position":"radical_moralism","tags":["tolstoy","moralism","art and morality","corrupting art","plato on art"]},{"term":"Aesthetic contextualism","def":"What a work is, and how good it is, depends on its context: who made it, when and why.","more":"Aesthetic contextualism says that an artwork cannot be identified or judged apart from its historical context. Kendall Walton, in “Categories of Art” (1970), argued that a work’s aesthetic qualities depend on the category it belongs to: the same picture can look bold or tame depending on what else existed at the time. Jerrold Levinson, in “What a Musical Work Is” (1980), argued that two composers writing identical scores in different centuries create different works, because the context changes what the notes do. Arthur Danto made a similar point about identical-looking objects. Held by Kendall Walton, Jerrold Levinson, Arthur Danto.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Achievement view of art","Historical definition of art","Embodied meaning theory"],"thinkers":["levinson","danto"],"position":"aesthetic_contextualism","tags":["contextualism","art history","context of art","historical context","provenance"]},{"term":"Tool view of AI art","def":"AI images can be art, and the person who prompts, selects and shapes them is the artist; the AI is a tool, like a camera.","more":"The tool view says that AI image generators are instruments, and that any art they help produce belongs to the humans who use them. The person who chooses the prompt, runs many versions, selects, edits and presents the result is making choices, just as a photographer does with a camera. When photography appeared in the nineteenth century, many denied it could be art because a machine made the image; today photographers are recognised artists. ” (2018) that art is a social activity of people, and that computational systems so far are tools artists use. Also called Collaborative view of AI art. Held by Aaron Hertzmann.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Intention requirement"],"thinkers":[],"position":"tool_view_of_ai_art","tags":["ai art","artificial intelligence art","generative art","prompting","human artist","tool","collaborative view of ai art"]},{"term":"Intention requirement","def":"Art needs a maker who means something by it; an AI means nothing, and a short prompt is too little to make the user its artist.","more":"The intention requirement says that something can only be art if it was made by an agent with intentions: someone who chose, meant and cared about what they made. Many theories of art build this in: expression theories require feeling, the historical definition requires an intention to be regarded as art, and aesthetic definitions require an intention to give aesthetic qualities. AI systems, on this view, have no intentions or feelings, so they cannot be artists. And when a human writes a one-line prompt, they make too few choices for the result to count as their work. Held by Sean Dorrance Kelly, Ted Chiang.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Actual intentionalism","Expression theory of art","Achievement view of art"],"thinkers":[],"position":"intention_requirement_for_art","tags":["ai art","artistic intention","authorship","prompting","machine art"]},{"term":"Aesthetic-object view of AI art","def":"If an AI image looks and works like art, it is art, even if nobody counts as its artist.","more":"The aesthetic-object view says that whether something is art depends on what it is like and how it can be experienced, not on whether an artist stands behind it. A striking AI image can reward attention, carry mood and invite interpretation, so it can be art even if the AI is not an artist and the prompter contributed too little to count as one. This fits aesthetic empiricism, for which value lies in the experience a work offers, and anti-intentionalism, for which meaning lies in the work rather than its maker. Experimental studies by Elzė Sigutė Mikalonytė and Markus Kneer found that ordinary people judged paintings made by AI-driven robots to be art about as readily as human paintings, while being much less willing to call the robots artists. Also called Product view of AI art.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic empiricism","Anti-intentionalism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"aesthetic_object_view_of_ai_art","tags":["ai art","aesthetic properties","artificial intelligence art","generative art","product view of ai art"]},{"term":"AI authorship","def":"AI systems can themselves be artists: they make creative choices no human made.","more":"The AI-authorship view says that an AI system can be the creator, and so the artist, of what it produces. When a model generates an image from a short prompt, it makes countless decisions about composition, colour and style that no human made. If creativity means producing something new, surprising and valuable, AI systems may already qualify. Margaret Boden, a pioneer of research on creativity and computers, argued that computers can produce ideas that are new, surprising and valuable, which on her definition is the mark of creativity, though she left open whether they are ‘really’ creative. Held by Simon Colton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":[],"thinkers":[],"position":"ai_authorship","tags":["ai art","machine creativity","computational creativity","artificial creativity","ai artist"]},{"term":"Defence of popular art","def":"The best popular art, such as a great pop song or film, can be as great as the best high art.","more":"This view says there is no fixed hierarchy between “high” art (opera, symphonies, literary fiction) and popular art (pop music, films, comics). Popular forms have their own excellences: a three-minute song can be perfectly shaped, a film can be as rich as a novel. Richard Shusterman, in Pragmatist Aesthetics (1992), defended rap and other popular art against dismissal by intellectuals, arguing that they can have complexity, depth and real aesthetic value. Noël Carroll, in A Philosophy of Mass Art (1998), argued that mass art (art made for mass distribution and easy access) is a genuine art form whose accessibility is a design feature, not a flaw. Held by Richard Shusterman, Noël Carroll.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Sociological critique of taste"],"thinkers":["shusterman","carroll"],"position":"defence_of_popular_art","tags":["popular culture","pop art","mass art","high art vs low art","pop culture"]},{"term":"Hierarchy of the arts","def":"Popular art can be wonderful, but the greatest works in deep, demanding traditions achieve more.","more":"The hierarchy view says that some kinds of art have a higher ceiling than others. A great pop song can be delightful and perfectly made, but the greatest symphonies, operas and novels do more: they develop ideas at length, sustain complexity, and reward a lifetime of study. This is not snobbery about people but a claim about what different forms can contain. Roger Scruton argued in The Aesthetics of Music (1997) that much modern popular music lacks the melodic and harmonic development of the classical tradition. Held by Roger Scruton, Dwight Macdonald.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Culture industry critique"],"thinkers":["scruton"],"position":"hierarchy_of_the_arts","tags":["high art","low art","high culture","mass art","popular culture"]},{"term":"Culture industry critique","def":"Popular art made to sell to millions becomes standardised: it soothes and distracts rather than challenging people.","more":"The culture industry critique was developed by Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer in Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944, revised 1947). They argued that film, radio and popular music had become an industry, producing standardised products to be consumed, much like cars or soap. Songs follow formulas so that listeners always know what is coming; films repeat the same plots. This sameness, they argued, trains people to accept the world as it is and to seek distraction instead of thought, which serves those in power. Also called Frankfurt School critique. Held by Theodor W. Adorno, Max Horkheimer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Hierarchy of the arts"],"thinkers":["adorno","horkheimer"],"position":"culture_industry_critique","tags":["frankfurt school","adorno","horkheimer","mass culture","culture industry","standardization","frankfurt school critique"]},{"term":"Sociological critique of taste","def":"The ranking of high over popular art is social, not artistic: it tracks class and status, not quality.","more":"The sociological critique, developed most fully by Pierre Bourdieu in Distinction (1979), argues that taste is shaped by social class. Bourdieu’s surveys of French society found that preferences for classical music, abstract art or particular films lined up with education and class background. Taste works as a form of “cultural capital”: knowing and liking the right things signals belonging to the educated classes and marks others as outsiders. On this view, calling a symphony higher than a pop song mostly reproduces social hierarchy. Held by Pierre Bourdieu, Herbert Gans.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic relativism","Defence of popular art"],"thinkers":["bourdieu"],"position":"sociological_critique_of_taste","tags":["bourdieu","distinction","cultural capital","class and taste","snobbery"]},{"term":"Thought theory","def":"Emotions do not need belief: vividly thinking of a monster is enough to genuinely frighten you.","more":"The paradox of fiction asks how we can feel real fear, pity or grief for characters we know do not exist. The thought theory, defended by Peter Lamarque and Noël Carroll, says that emotions do not require belief that their object exists. Vividly imagining or entertaining a thought can cause real emotions. You can feel a shudder imagining falling off a cliff while standing safely on the ground, or feel queasy thinking about something disgusting. Held by Peter Lamarque, Noël Carroll.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Compensation theory"],"thinkers":["carroll"],"position":"thought_theory_of_fictional_emotion","tags":["paradox of fiction","emotions and fiction","fear of fiction","entertained thoughts"]},{"term":"Make-believe theory","def":"We do not truly fear fictional monsters: we are playing a game of make-believe in which we fear them.","more":"Kendall Walton’s make-believe theory, set out in “Fearing Fictions” (1978) and Mimesis as Make-Believe (1990), treats fiction as a prop in a game of make-believe, like a child’s game in which tree stumps count as bears. When you watch a horror film, it is make-believe that a monster is coming for you, and it is make-believe that you are afraid. Walton’s example is Charles, who shrieks at a film’s slime monster. His heart pounds and he grips the chair, which Walton calls “quasi-fear,” but he has no belief he is in danger and no wish to flee. Also called Pretend theory. Held by Kendall Walton.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":[],"thinkers":[],"position":"make_believe_theory","tags":["kendall walton","paradox of fiction","quasi-emotions","pretend","fiction","pretend theory"]},{"term":"Irrationalism","def":"Our emotions for fictional characters are real, but irrational: we feel for people we know do not exist.","more":"” (1975), argued that the paradox of fiction cannot be dissolved. We really do pity Anna Karenina and fear the monster. Yet these emotions normally require believing their object exists and faces real harm, and we know they do not. So our emotional responses to fiction are, in Radford’s word, “incoherent”: real but irrational. Also called Irrationalism about fiction. Held by Colin Radford.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":[],"thinkers":[],"position":"irrationalism_about_fictional_emotion","tags":["paradox of fiction","colin radford","irrational emotions","emotions and fiction","irrationalism about fiction"]},{"term":"Conversion theory","def":"The pain of tragedy is turned into pleasure by the beauty of how it is presented.","more":"The paradox of tragedy asks why people enjoy works that make them feel pity, fear and grief, feelings they would avoid in life. David Hume, in “Of Tragedy” (1757), proposed that the beauty and skill of the presentation convert the painful emotions into pleasure. The eloquence of the language, the art of the plot and the power of the performance give a strong pleasure that takes over the sad feelings and transforms them, so that the whole experience becomes enjoyable and even more intense because of the sorrow. If the same events were presented without art, the pain would remain pain. Held by David Hume.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":[],"thinkers":["hume"],"position":"conversion_theory","tags":["paradox of tragedy","hume","tragedy","pleasure in tragedy","painful art"]},{"term":"Compensation theory","def":"Tragedy stays painful, but the pain is outweighed by other rewards, such as insight, admiration or a sense of our shared humanity.","more":"Compensation theories say that we do feel real pain in watching tragedy, but that pain is outweighed by compensating benefits. Susan Feagin, in “The Pleasures of Tragedy” (1983), argued that we feel a pleasant “meta-response”: we are glad to find that we are the kind of people who feel pity and sorrow for others’ suffering. Noël Carroll, in The Philosophy of Horror (1990), gave a similar answer to the related paradox of horror: the disgust and fear monsters cause are the price we pay for the pleasure of curiosity, of finding out about something impossible and fascinating. Other compensations proposed include learning about life and admiring the artistry. Held by Susan Feagin, Noël Carroll.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Thought theory"],"thinkers":["carroll"],"position":"compensation_theory","tags":["paradox of tragedy","tragedy","pleasure in tragedy","painful art"]},{"term":"Catharsis theory","def":"Tragedy rewards us by releasing or clarifying pity and fear, leaving us cleansed or wiser.","more":"In the Poetics, Aristotle said that tragedy, through pity and fear, accomplishes the catharsis of such emotions. He never fully explained what catharsis means, and readings are contested. On the “purgation” reading, tragedy purges excess emotion, like a medical cleansing, leaving us calmer. On the “purification” reading, it refines our emotions so that we feel them in the right way and towards the right things. Held by Aristotle.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Cognitivism about art","Mimetic theory of art"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"position":"catharsis_theory","tags":["catharsis","aristotle","purgation","tragedy","paradox of tragedy","pity and fear"]},{"term":"Classical theory of beauty","def":"Beauty is harmony: order, proportion and unity in variety, many parts fitting into one whole.","more":"The classical theory is the oldest Western account of what beautiful things have in common. Beauty is a real feature of an object, and it consists in the right relation of its parts: order, proportion, symmetry, and a unity that holds variety together. Aristotle said a beautiful thing needs order and a fitting size, so that it can be taken in as one whole. The Renaissance architect Leon Battista Alberti described beauty as a harmony of parts such that nothing could be added, taken away or changed without making it worse. Held by Aristotle, Thomas Aquinas, Leon Battista Alberti.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic realism","Platonism about beauty","Functional beauty"],"thinkers":["aristotle","aquinas"],"position":"classical_theory_of_beauty","tags":["harmony","proportion","symmetry","unity in variety","classical beauty"]},{"term":"Platonism about beauty","def":"Beautiful things share in, or reflect, a perfect Beauty that lies beyond the world we see.","more":"Platonism about beauty holds that the many beautiful things we meet, faces, songs, laws, proofs, are beautiful because they share in one perfect Beauty itself, which never changes and is not a physical thing. In Plato’s Symposium, the priestess Diotima describes a ladder: we begin by loving one beautiful body, rise to beauty in all bodies, then in souls, laws and kinds of knowledge, and at last glimpse Beauty itself. Plotinus developed the idea: physical beauty is form shining through matter, and the soul delights in it because it recognises something akin to itself. On this view beauty is closely tied to truth and goodness, and loving it can make us better. Held by Plato, Plotinus, Iris Murdoch.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic realism","Classical theory of beauty","Radical moralism"],"thinkers":["plato","plotinus"],"position":"platonism_about_beauty","tags":["platonic beauty","form of beauty","plato","symposium","beauty itself"]},{"term":"Functional beauty","def":"Much beauty is looking fit for a job: a thing well made for what it is or does.","more":"Functional beauty is the view that a great deal of beauty, in tools, buildings, bodies, animals and even landscapes, lies in a thing looking well suited to its function. In Xenophon’s Memorabilia, Socrates says even a dung basket is beautiful if it is well made for its work, while a golden shield is ugly if it is badly made for its own. Kant called this ‘dependent’ beauty: beauty judged under a concept of what the thing is meant to be. Glenn Parsons and Allen Carlson revived the idea in Functional Beauty (2008), arguing that knowing what something is for changes how it looks to us, so that a cheetah’s lean frame looks beautiful once we see it as built for speed. Held by Glenn Parsons, Allen Carlson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic realism","Classical theory of beauty","Scientific cognitivism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"functional_beauty","tags":["function and beauty","fitness","form follows function","design","usefulness"]},{"term":"Non-reductive aesthetic realism","def":"Beauty and grace are real qualities, but no rule about shape, order or use captures them; you learn to see them.","more":"Non-reductive aesthetic realism holds that aesthetic qualities, beauty, grace, elegance, garishness, are real features of objects, but cannot be reduced to or deduced from their plain physical features. Frank Sibley argued in “Aesthetic Concepts” (1959) that no list of non-aesthetic features (these colours, those lines) is ever enough to guarantee that a thing is graceful or delicate; seeing that takes taste, a kind of perceptual sensitivity that can be trained. Nick Zangwill adds that aesthetic properties depend on physical ones (two things alike in every physical respect are alike in beauty) and that judgements about them can be true or false. So beauty is like colour seen by a trained eye: real, perceived, but not reachable by rule. Held by Frank Sibley, Nick Zangwill.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic realism","Secondary-quality view of beauty"],"thinkers":["sibley","zangwill"],"position":"non_reductive_aesthetic_realism","tags":["frank sibley","aesthetic concepts","aesthetic realism","non-reductionism","aesthetic properties"]},{"term":"Wabi-sabi aesthetics","def":"Beauty lives in what is imperfect, worn, incomplete and passing.","more":"Wabi-sabi is a Japanese aesthetic ideal, shaped by Zen Buddhism and the tea ceremony. ‘Wabi’ suggests rustic simplicity and quiet poverty; ‘sabi’ suggests the beauty of age, wear and weathering. On this view the deepest beauty is found not in perfect symmetry but in the uneven glaze of a hand-made tea bowl, moss on a stone, a crack mended with visible gold, or cherry blossoms that fall within a week. The tea master Sen no Rikyū, in the sixteenth century, made plain, irregular utensils central to the tea ceremony. Held by Okakura Kakuzō, Sen no Rikyū, Tanizaki Jun’ichirō.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic realism","Aesthetic relativism"],"thinkers":["okakura"],"position":"wabi_sabi_aesthetics","tags":["wabi sabi","japanese aesthetics","imperfection","impermanence","transience","mono no aware"]},{"term":"Hume’s standard of taste","def":"The standard of beauty is the joint verdict of true judges: people of delicate, practised, unprejudiced taste.","more":"In “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757), David Hume began from the saying that beauty is no quality in things themselves but exists in the mind that contemplates them. Yet he noted that anyone who ranked the minor poet John Ogilby with Milton would be thought absurd. To reconcile these, Hume proposed a standard: the joint verdict of ‘true judges’, who have delicacy of taste, practice, experience in comparing many works, freedom from prejudice, and good sense. Such judges are rare, but their agreement, together with the test of time, fixes which works are truly great. Held by David Hume, Jerrold Levinson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Ideal-critic theory","Sensibility relativism","Aesthetic empiricism"],"thinkers":["hume","levinson"],"position":"humean_standard_of_taste","tags":["hume","standard of taste","true judges","ideal critic","delicacy of taste"]},{"term":"Internal sense theory","def":"Beauty is the pleasure a shared inner sense gives us when we perceive uniformity amid variety.","more":"Francis Hutcheson, in An Inquiry into the Original of Our Ideas of Beauty and Virtue (1725), argued that humans have an ‘internal sense’ of beauty, as natural as sight or hearing. Just as sweetness is the idea sugar produces in us, beauty is the pleasant idea produced in us by objects that combine uniformity with variety: a regular figure, a theorem uniting many cases, a well-ordered landscape. Because the sense is part of human nature, people broadly agree about beauty, and differences come from distorting associations or lack of attention. Beauty is therefore a response in us, but a lawful and shared one, triggered by a definite feature of objects. Held by Francis Hutcheson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Ideal-critic theory","Classical theory of beauty","Moral sense theory"],"thinkers":[],"position":"internal_sense_theory","tags":["francis hutcheson","sense of beauty","inner sense","uniformity amid variety","moral sense"]},{"term":"Secondary-quality view of beauty","def":"Beauty is like colour: a real feature of things, but one defined by the responses it merits in us.","more":"The secondary-quality view compares beauty with colour. Redness is a real feature of a tomato, yet what it is to be red cannot be explained without mentioning how red things look to us. Likewise, a thing is beautiful if it merits a certain kind of admiring response from suitably sensitive people. John McDowell, in “Aesthetic Value, Objectivity, and the Fabric of the World” (1983), argued that aesthetic value is not a mere projection of feeling, nor something that would exist with no possible viewers: it is a feature we are responsive to, and our responses can be correct or mistaken. Held by John McDowell, Philip Pettit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Ideal-critic theory","Non-reductive aesthetic realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"secondary_quality_view_of_beauty","tags":["response-dependence","secondary qualities","dispositional theory of beauty","colour analogy"]},{"term":"Kantian formalism","def":"Pure beauty lies in form alone, judged without any concept of what the thing is for.","more":"Kantian formalism takes from Kant’s Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790) the idea that the purest judgements of beauty concern only form: the arrangement of lines, shapes, colours or tones as they play on our imagination. Kant called such cases ‘free beauty’: flowers, many birds, foliage patterns on wallpaper, music without words. Here we need no concept of what the thing is meant to be. Beauty that depends on such a concept, as in a building or a human figure, Kant called ‘dependent’ beauty, and he treated it as less pure. Held by Immanuel Kant, Nick Zangwill.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Kantian theory of taste","Formalism"],"thinkers":["kant","zangwill"],"position":"kantian_formalism","tags":["formalism","kant","free beauty","pure beauty","form"]},{"term":"Kant’s theory of aesthetic ideas","def":"Beauty, especially in art, expresses ideas that set thought going but that no concept can capture.","more":"Kant’s theory of aesthetic ideas, from §49 of the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), qualifies the formalist reading of his aesthetics. An aesthetic idea is an image or representation that ‘occasions much thinking’ without any definite concept being adequate to it: Kant’s example is Jupiter’s eagle with lightning in its claws, which conveys the might of the king of heaven more richly than any definition. Genius is the talent for producing such ideas, and Kant goes so far as to say that beauty in general may be called the expression of aesthetic ideas. On this reading, content and meaning matter to beauty, though in a way no rule or concept can spell out, so it still rests on a feeling that claims everyone’s agreement. Held by Immanuel Kant.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Kantian theory of taste","Cognitivism about art"],"thinkers":["kant"],"position":"aesthetic_ideas_view","tags":["aesthetic ideas","kant","genius","critique of judgment","inexpressible"]},{"term":"Cultural aesthetic relativism","def":"Standards of beauty are real but belong to cultures; none stands above them all.","more":"Cultural aesthetic relativism holds that each culture or artistic tradition has its own standards of beauty and excellence, and that judgements can be correct or mistaken relative to those standards. A Japanese tea master and a Renaissance architect may both judge well, by different standards, and there is no neutral standpoint from which to say which standard is the right one. The view draws on the wider cultural relativism of twentieth-century anthropology, which, from Franz Boas and his students onward, urged that each culture be understood by its own standards, and on the history of taste, which shows standards changing over time and place. Unlike subjectivism, it keeps the idea that taste can be better or worse: but only within a tradition.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic relativism","Sociological critique of taste"],"thinkers":[],"position":"cultural_aesthetic_relativism","tags":["cultural relativism","relativism about beauty","culture-relative beauty","taste relativism"]},{"term":"Sensibility relativism","def":"Even ideal critics can differ blamelessly, because they have different but equally good sensibilities.","more":"Sensibility relativism starts from a remark in Hume’s “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757). Hume admits that some differences in taste are ‘blameless’: a young man may prefer amorous poetry and an older man philosophical reflection, and neither is wrong. Alan H. Goldman, in Aesthetic Value (1995), argued that such differences go all the way up: even fully informed, unprejudiced critics with excellent taste can disagree, because they differ in temperament and sensibility. Held by Alan H. Goldman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic relativism","Hume’s standard of taste"],"thinkers":[],"position":"sensibility_relativism","tags":["faultless disagreement","relativism","ideal critics","taste relativism"]},{"term":"Speaker subjectivism about beauty","def":"“It’s beautiful” reports that the speaker likes it in a certain way, and is true if they do.","more":"Speaker subjectivism holds that calling something beautiful states a fact about yourself: that it pleases you, perhaps in a particular, contemplative way. George Santayana, in The Sense of Beauty (1896), described beauty as ‘pleasure regarded as the quality of a thing’: we feel delight and project it onto the object. On this view, when two people seem to disagree about a song, each speaks truly about their own response, and the apparent disagreement is like one person saying ‘I’m cold’ and another ‘I’m warm’. The view is simple and explains why taste varies, but it struggles to make sense of real arguments about art. Held by George Santayana.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic subjectivism","Relativism about taste predicates"],"thinkers":["santayana"],"position":"speaker_subjectivism_about_beauty","tags":["subjectivism","speaker relativism","personal taste","beauty claims","reports of liking"]},{"term":"Relativism about taste predicates","def":"“It’s beautiful” is true or false only relative to a judge’s taste, so disagreements about it can be real yet faultless.","more":"This view comes from recent philosophy of language. It notices that taste disagreements are odd: when you say a song is beautiful and I say it is not, we seem to contradict each other, yet neither of us seems to have made a mistake. Philosophers call this ‘faultless disagreement’. Max Kölbel, Peter Lasersohn and John MacFarlane proposed that sentences like ‘this is beautiful’ or ‘this is tasty’ have truth values only relative to a standard of taste, often the taste of whoever is assessing them. Held by Max Kölbel, Peter Lasersohn, John MacFarlane.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic subjectivism","Aesthetic relativism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"relativism_about_taste_predicates","tags":["faultless disagreement","predicates of personal taste","relativism","semantic relativism","taste"]},{"term":"Aesthetic emotivism","def":"Aesthetic words express feelings, like a cry of delight, and state no facts at all.","more":"Aesthetic emotivism is the aesthetic half of the theory A. J. Ayer set out in Language, Truth and Logic (1936). Ayer argued that words like ‘beautiful’ and ‘hideous’ are used, like moral words, to express feelings and to provoke a response, not to describe anything. Held by A. J. Ayer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic non-cognitivism","Emotivism"],"thinkers":["ayer"],"position":"aesthetic_emotivism","tags":["emotivism","non-cognitivism","expressivism","aesthetic language"]},{"term":"Aesthetic quasi-realism","def":"Aesthetic verdicts express attitudes, yet we can still rightly call them true, better or worse.","more":"Aesthetic quasi-realism applies to taste the programme Simon Blackburn developed for ethics. It starts as non-cognitivism: calling something beautiful expresses an attitude of admiration rather than describing a property. But it then argues that we are entitled to all the realist-sounding talk we use, saying a verdict is true, that someone’s taste is better, or that a critic made a mistake. Such talk expresses higher-order attitudes about which responses are worth having: attentive, informed, sensitive ones. Held by Simon Blackburn.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic non-cognitivism","Quasi-realism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"aesthetic_quasi_realism","tags":["quasi-realism","simon blackburn","expressivism","aesthetic judgment"]},{"term":"Moral and social view of art","def":"Great art is great mainly for what it does for people: shaping character, binding communities, moving them to act well.","more":"The moral and social view holds that art’s highest value lies in its effects on people and communities. Confucian thinkers treated music and ritual as means of shaping character and harmonising society; Xunzi’s “Discourse on Music” argues that music, rightly made, orders the emotions and unites people. Plato’s Republic judged poetry by its effect on the soul and the city. Leo Tolstoy’s What Is Art? Held by Leo Tolstoy, Confucius, Xunzi.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Radical moralism","Tolstoy’s infection theory"],"thinkers":["tolstoy","confucius","xunzi"],"position":"moral_function_of_art","tags":["art and society","moral function of art","social function of art","tolstoy","confucius on music"]},{"term":"Significant form theory","def":"Art is whatever has significant form: lines, colours or sounds arranged so as to stir a special aesthetic emotion.","more":"The significant form theory was set out by the English critic Clive Bell in Art (1914). Bell asked what Persian bowls, Chinese carpets, Giotto’s frescoes and Cézanne’s paintings have in common, and answered: significant form, meaning combinations of lines and colours that stir in sensitive viewers a distinctive ‘aesthetic emotion’. What a work represents, its story or subject, is irrelevant to its being art; a picture’s value as art lies wholly in its form. Bell and his friend Roger Fry used the theory to defend Post-Impressionism. Held by Clive Bell, Roger Fry.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic definition of art","Formalism"],"thinkers":["bell"],"position":"significant_form_theory","tags":["clive bell","formalism","aesthetic emotion","significant form","form"]},{"term":"Aesthetic creation theory","def":"Something is art if it was made with the insight-guided intention to give it aesthetic properties.","more":"Nick Zangwill’s aesthetic creation theory, set out in Aesthetic Creation (2007), refines the aesthetic definition of art. Something is a work of art, he argues, if its maker had an insight that certain aesthetic properties (gracefulness, tension, elegance) would come about through certain physical features, and intentionally produced those features so that the work would have those properties. Art need not be beautiful or aimed at an audience’s experience; it must be made for its aesthetic properties. Conceptual works that lack any aesthetic aim, Zangwill argues, are art only in a derivative sense, by depending on works that have one. Held by Nick Zangwill.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Aesthetic definition of art","Kantian formalism"],"thinkers":["zangwill"],"position":"aesthetic_creation_theory","tags":["nick zangwill","aesthetic properties","definition of art","aesthetic intention"]},{"term":"Tolstoy’s infection theory","def":"Art is the passing on of a feeling the artist has had, so that others are ‘infected’ by it.","more":"In What Is Art? (1897), Leo Tolstoy rejected definitions of art by beauty, which he thought served the idle tastes of the rich. Art, he argued, is a human activity in which one person, by external signs such as movements, lines, colours, sounds or words, consciously passes on to others feelings they have lived through, so that others are infected by them and experience them too. The more sincere, individual and clear the feeling, the stronger the infection. Held by Leo Tolstoy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Expression theory of art","Moral and social view of art"],"thinkers":["tolstoy"],"position":"infection_theory_of_art","tags":["tolstoy","expression theory","contagion","infection","feeling","what is art"]},{"term":"Collingwood’s expression theory","def":"Art is the clarifying of a feeling: the artist finds out what they feel by expressing it in a medium.","more":"R. G. Collingwood’s The Principles of Art (1938), building on Benedetto Croce, distinguished art proper from craft and from ‘amusement’ and ‘magic’ art made to arouse feelings for some purpose. In art proper, the artist starts with an unclear emotion, a vague disturbance, and by working in words, paint or sound comes to know what that emotion is. Held by R. G. Collingwood, Benedetto Croce.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Expression theory of art"],"thinkers":["collingwood"],"position":"collingwoodian_expression_theory","tags":["collingwood","expression theory","expression of emotion","principles of art","feeling"]},{"term":"Rasa theory","def":"Art turns emotions into ‘rasa’, a savoured, universalised flavour of feeling that delights even when the emotion is grief.","more":"Rasa theory is the central idea of classical Indian aesthetics. The Nāṭyaśāstra, a treatise on drama attributed to Bharata, explains how a play’s situations, gestures and transient feelings combine to produce rasa (literally ‘juice’ or ‘flavour’) in the audience, as spices combine into a taste. There are eight or nine rasas, including the erotic, the heroic, the comic and the compassionate. Around 1000 CE the Kashmiri philosopher Abhinavagupta, building on Bhaṭṭa Nāyaka, argued that in rasa the emotion is freed from personal self-interest and universalised: we savour sorrow without it being our own sorrow, and that savouring is a kind of joy, akin to the bliss of spiritual insight. Held by Bharata Muni, Abhinavagupta.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Beauty and art","related":["Conversion theory","Catharsis theory"],"thinkers":["bharata","abhinavagupta"],"position":"rasa_theory","tags":["rasa","bharata","abhinavagupta","natyashastra","indian aesthetics","aesthetic emotion"]},{"term":"Supernaturalism about meaning","def":"A life is meaningful because of God, or a life beyond this one.","more":"Supernaturalism says that the meaning of life depends on something beyond the natural world, usually God or an immortal soul. Versions differ on how. Some say a life is meaningful when it fulfils the purpose God created us for, much as a tool has meaning in the job it was made to do. Others stress a loving relationship with God, or say that only a life that continues after death can escape being wiped out in the end. Also called God-centred view of meaning, Theistic view of meaning. Held by Augustine of Hippo, Thomas Aquinas, Leo Tolstoy, William Lane Craig.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Hope beyond death","Leap of faith","Essentialism about human nature"],"thinkers":["augustine","aquinas","tolstoy","craig","swinburne"],"position":"supernaturalism_about_meaning","tags":["religious meaning","god-centered view","meaning of life","afterlife","theistic meaning","god-centred view of meaning","theistic view of meaning"]},{"term":"Extreme supernaturalism","def":"Without God and an afterlife, no life could have any meaning at all.","more":"Extreme supernaturalism is the strong form of the God-centred view. It says God (and usually an afterlife) is necessary for meaning: if there were no God, every human life would be meaningless, however loving, brave or creative it was. The name comes from Thaddeus Metz’s survey of the debate. The core worry is that in a godless universe everything ends: people die, memories fade, the human species and eventually the stars disappear. Held by William Lane Craig, Leo Tolstoy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Supernaturalism about meaning","Hope beyond death"],"thinkers":["craig","tolstoy"],"position":"extreme_supernaturalism","tags":["meaning of life","no god no meaning","william lane craig","afterlife","theistic meaning"]},{"term":"Moderate supernaturalism","def":"A godless life could still have some meaning, but God makes a life far more meaningful.","more":"Moderate supernaturalism agrees that love, work, kindness and creativity can give a life real meaning even if there were no God. But it holds that God, and a life beyond death, would add a great deal more: a deeper purpose, a relationship with the source of all goodness, and a future in which nothing good is finally lost. So God is not needed for any meaning, but for the greatest meaning. This view sits between extreme supernaturalism, which says no God means no meaning at all, and naturalist views such as objectivism and the hybrid view, which say meaning needs nothing beyond the natural world. Held by Richard Swinburne.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Supernaturalism about meaning","Objectivism about meaning"],"thinkers":["swinburne"],"position":"moderate_supernaturalism","tags":["meaning of life","god and meaning","theistic meaning","religious meaning"]},{"term":"Objectivism about meaning","def":"A life is meaningful when it engages with what is truly worthwhile, whatever you feel about it.","more":"Objectivism says that some things are worth doing in themselves, independent of anyone’s feelings or choices, and that a life is meaningful to the extent that it is bound up with them. Common examples are moral goodness (helping others, fighting injustice), discovering truth, and creating beauty. Thaddeus Metz, for example, argues that meaning comes from using your reason in ways oriented toward these fundamental goods. Unlike supernaturalism, objectivism needs nothing beyond the natural world: these goods are found in ordinary human life. Also called Naturalist objectivism. Held by Thaddeus Metz.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Objective list theory","Hybrid theory of meaning"],"thinkers":["metz"],"position":"objectivism_about_meaning","tags":["objective meaning","meaning of life","worthwhile","objective value","naturalist objectivism"]},{"term":"Hybrid theory of meaning","def":"A meaningful life needs both: caring about what you do, and what you do being really worthwhile.","more":"Hybrid theories of meaning combine the insights of subjectivism and objectivism. Subjectivists say meaning comes from what you care about; objectivists say it comes from what is really worthwhile. Hybrid theorists say each side alone gets something wrong. A man who devotes his life to counting blades of grass may love it, yet his life seems to lack something; a woman who does valuable work she feels nothing for seems cut off from the meaning of what she does. Also called Fitting fulfilment (hybrid view). Held by Susan Wolf.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Objectivism about meaning","Subjectivism about meaning"],"thinkers":["wolf"],"position":"hybrid_theory_of_meaning","tags":["susan wolf","meaning of life","fitting fulfilment","subjective and objective","meaningful life","fitting fulfilment (hybrid view)"]},{"term":"Subjectivism about meaning","def":"Your life is meaningful when it is filled with what you care about or choose.","more":"Subjectivism says that meaning is not found in the universe but made by us. A life is meaningful when the person living it cares about, chooses or is committed to what fills it. There is no outside standard that could show those commitments to be the wrong ones. Richard Taylor argued that even Sisyphus, the figure from Greek myth condemned to roll a stone uphill forever, would have a meaningful life if he were made to love rolling stones. Also called Self-made meaning. Held by Richard Taylor, Jean-Paul Sartre, Harry Frankfurt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Existentialism","Desire-satisfaction theory"],"thinkers":["richard_taylor","sartre","frankfurt"],"position":"subjectivism_about_meaning","tags":["subjective meaning","meaning of life","choose your meaning","personal meaning","caring","self-made meaning"]},{"term":"Existential nihilism","def":"No life has any real meaning or point.","more":"Existential nihilism holds that human life has no meaning, purpose or point. There is no God to give us one, the universe has no plan for us, and nothing we invent could supply real meaning, because we would only be pretending. Nihilists differ about what follows. Some, like the philosopher Alex Rosenberg, argue for what he calls “nice nihilism”: since nothing really matters, there is no reason to despair either, and people can simply go on living decently as they are inclined to. Also called Nihilism about meaning. Held by Alex Rosenberg.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Absurdism","Philosophical pessimism"],"thinkers":[],"position":"existential_nihilism","tags":["nihilism","meaninglessness","meaning of life","pointlessness","no meaning","nihilism about meaning"]},{"term":"The question is confused","def":"“What is the meaning of life?” is not a real question, so it has no answer to find or miss.","more":"This view says the question of life’s meaning rests on a mistake. Words, signs and tools have meanings or purposes; a life is not like any of them. So asking for “the” meaning of life is like asking what colour the number seven is. The puzzle is not solved but dissolved: once you see how the question misuses words, the urge to ask it fades. Also called Dissolving the question. Held by Ludwig Wittgenstein.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Subjectivism about meaning"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"position":"meaning_question_confused","tags":["meaning of life","pseudo-question","meaningless question","ayer","wittgenstein","dissolving the question"]},{"term":"Absurdism","def":"Life’s clash with a silent universe should be faced with defiance and lived to the full.","more":"Absurdism is Albert Camus’s response to what he called “the absurd”: the clash between our deep need for meaning and clarity and a universe that offers none. The absurd is not in us or in the world alone, but in their collision. Camus rejected two escapes: denying the clash by leaping into religious faith, and giving up on life. Instead he urged revolt: keep the clash in full view, refuse false hope, and live as intensely and freely as possible. Also called Camus’s revolt. Held by Albert Camus.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Existentialism","Ironic acceptance"],"thinkers":["camus"],"position":"absurdism","tags":["absurd","camus","myth of sisyphus","revolt","rebellion","meaning of life","camus’s revolt"]},{"term":"Ironic acceptance","def":"See the absurdity of taking life so seriously, and meet it with irony rather than despair.","more":"Thomas Nagel argued in his essay “The Absurd” that our lives seem absurd not because the universe is huge or we are brief, but because of a feature of our own minds. We live our lives with great seriousness, yet we can also step back and view them from outside, where all our concerns look arbitrary and open to doubt. We cannot stop doing either. The absurd is this clash between our engagement and our ability to step back. Also called Nagel’s irony. Held by Thomas Nagel.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Absurdism","Subjectivism about meaning"],"thinkers":["nagel"],"position":"ironic_acceptance","tags":["thomas nagel","absurdity","irony","the absurd","detachment","nagel’s irony"]},{"term":"Philosophical pessimism","def":"Life contains more suffering and striving than lasting good, so expect little and be compassionate.","more":"Philosophical pessimism is the view that life, taken as a whole, is worse than people usually think: it contains more striving, frustration and pain than lasting happiness. Arthur Schopenhauer argued that we are driven by endless desire: when a want is unmet we suffer, and when it is met we soon grow bored and want something new. Pessimists think our natural optimism, which may help us survive, hides this from us. David Benatar has argued more recently that our judgments about our own lives are biased upward. Also called Pessimism. Held by Arthur Schopenhauer, David Benatar.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Anti-natalism","Buddhist non-attachment"],"thinkers":["schopenhauer","benatar"],"position":"philosophical_pessimism","tags":["pessimism","schopenhauer","suffering","misery","antinatalism"]},{"term":"Hybrid theory of well-being","def":"Your life goes well when you enjoy or care about things that are really good.","more":"The hybrid theory combines the best of the subjective and objective views. Something benefits you only when two conditions hold together: it must be objectively worthwhile (like friendship, knowledge or achievement) and you must enjoy it, want it or engage with it wholeheartedly. Pleasure in worthless things is not enough, and worthwhile things that leave you cold are not enough either. Derek Parfit suggested a view like this when he wrote that what is best for us may be having the good things on the list and wanting or enjoying them. Also called Enjoying the good. Held by Derek Parfit, Shelly Kagan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Hybrid theory of meaning","Objective list theory"],"thinkers":["parfit","kagan"],"position":"hybrid_theory_of_well_being","tags":["well-being","welfare","happiness","objective list","hybrid","enjoying the good"]},{"term":"Meaning as a separate good","def":"Meaning is its own good, separate from happiness, and can be worth choosing at happiness’s expense.","more":"This view holds that a good life has more than one dimension. Susan Wolf argues that meaning is a third value, alongside happiness (how well your life goes for you) and morality (how well you treat others). A life can be happy but meaningless, as with a contented life of trivial amusement, or meaningful but not very happy, as with a stressful life devoted to a cause. Because these values are distinct, people sometimes rightly trade some happiness for meaning, and we understand why they do. Also called Pluralism about the good life. Held by Susan Wolf, Viktor Frankl.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Hybrid theory of meaning","Objectivism about meaning"],"thinkers":["wolf"],"position":"meaning_as_distinct_value","tags":["meaning vs happiness","meaningful life","value of meaning","susan wolf","pluralism about the good life"]},{"term":"Deprivation account of death","def":"Death is bad for you because it takes away the good life you would otherwise have had.","more":"The deprivation account says death is bad for the one who dies not because it hurts (the dead feel nothing) but because of what it takes away. If you die at twenty, you lose the decades of friendship, work and joy you would otherwise have enjoyed. Death is bad for you in the way that missing a wonderful opportunity is bad for you, even if you never find out. The account explains why an early death is usually worse than a late one: more is lost. Also called Deprivationism. Held by Thomas Nagel, Fred Feldman, Shelly Kagan, Ben Bradley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Asymmetry view","Resistance to death"],"thinkers":["nagel","feldman","kagan"],"position":"deprivation_account","tags":["deprivation","badness of death","loss","thomas nagel","fred feldman","harm of death","deprivationism"]},{"term":"Epicureanism about death","def":"Death is nothing to us: when it is here, we are not.","more":"Epicurus argued that death is nothing to fear, because nothing can be bad for someone who does not exist. While you live, death has not arrived; once it arrives, you no longer exist to be harmed. Since all good and bad, for Epicurus, lie in sensation (pleasure and pain), and death ends sensation, death cannot be bad for the one who dies. The fear of death, he believed, is one of the main sources of anxiety in life, and seeing through it frees a person to enjoy a calm, pleasant existence. Also called The Epicurean view of death. Held by Epicurus, Lucretius, Stephen Rosenbaum.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Hedonism","Lucretian symmetry argument","Stoicism"],"thinkers":["epicurus","lucretius"],"position":"epicureanism_about_death","tags":["epicurus","death is nothing","fear of death","epicurean","badness of death","the epicurean view of death"]},{"term":"Time-relative interest account","def":"How bad death is for you depends on how closely you are tied to the future you lose.","more":"Jeff McMahan’s time-relative interest account refines the deprivation view. It agrees that death is bad because it takes away future goods. But it adds that how bad this loss is for you now depends on how strongly you are connected, psychologically, to that future: through memories that carry forward, plans, hopes and a continuing personality. A young adult with rich plans and deep ties to their future loses a great deal. Also called TRIA, Graded view of death’s badness. Held by Jeff McMahan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Deprivation account of death"],"thinkers":["mcmahan"],"position":"time_relative_interest_account","tags":["jeff mcmahan","badness of death","psychological connectedness","interests","harm of death","tria","graded view of death’s badness"]},{"term":"Asymmetry view","def":"Death is bad but the time before birth was not, because only death takes a future you could have had.","more":"The asymmetry view answers Lucretius’s mirror argument. Lucretius said the time after death is a mirror of the time before birth, so we should feel about death as we do about not having existed earlier. Asymmetry theorists agree the time before birth was not bad, but deny that this shows death is not bad. Thomas Nagel suggested that you could not really have been born much earlier, since a person born from different cells at a different time would not be you; but you could have lived longer. Also called Death and prenatal nonexistence differ. Held by Thomas Nagel, John Martin Fischer, Anthony Brueckner.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Deprivation account of death"],"thinkers":["nagel","fischer"],"position":"asymmetry_view","tags":["lucretius","prenatal nonexistence","symmetry","badness of death","asymmetry","death and prenatal nonexistence differ"]},{"term":"Lucretian symmetry argument","def":"The time after death mirrors the time before birth; since that was not bad, death is not bad either.","more":"The symmetry argument comes from the Roman poet Lucretius, who set out Epicurus’s philosophy in his long poem On the Nature of Things. He invites us to look back at the endless ages before we were born. That time did not trouble us at all. The time after death, he says, is like a mirror that nature holds up to show us what is to come. Also called The mirror argument. Held by Lucretius.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Epicureanism about death"],"thinkers":["lucretius"],"position":"lucretian_symmetry_argument","tags":["lucretius","symmetry","prenatal nonexistence","fear of death","epicureanism","the mirror argument"]},{"term":"Both are losses","def":"Late birth and early death can both deprive you of good life.","more":"This view accepts Lucretius’s point that the time before birth and the time after death are alike, but turns it around. Instead of concluding that death is not bad, it concludes that both can be bad. If you could have been born earlier and enjoyed extra good years, your actual later birth deprived you of them, in the same way that an early death deprives you of later years. We don’t feel distressed about it, but that may reflect a quirk in our attitudes (we care more about the future than the past), not a real difference in loss. Also called Symmetric deprivation view. Held by Fred Feldman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Deprivation account of death"],"thinkers":["feldman"],"position":"symmetric_deprivation_view","tags":["symmetry","prenatal nonexistence","late birth","deprivation","badness of death","symmetric deprivation view"]},{"term":"Immortality is desirable","def":"An endless healthy life would be good, because there would always be more to love and do.","more":"This view holds that living forever, in good health and with the people you love, would be good for you. John Martin Fischer argues against the claim that immortality would be boring: many of the best pleasures are “repeatable”, like music, food, friendship, conversation and seeing beautiful places, and we can return to them again and again without them losing their value. A varied, open-ended life could keep finding new projects too. Transhumanist writers like Nick Bostrom go further and see ageing and death as problems humanity should work to solve. Also called Pro-immortality view. Held by John Martin Fischer, Nick Bostrom.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Resistance to death","Deprivation account of death"],"thinkers":["fischer","bostrom"],"position":"immortality_is_desirable","tags":["immortality","eternal life","living forever","immortal life","life extension","pro-immortality view"]},{"term":"Tedium of immortality","def":"An endless life would eventually become empty, because you would run out of things that grip you.","more":"Bernard Williams took his example from a play by Karel Čapek, in which Elina Makropulos has taken a potion that keeps her alive at the age of forty-two for three hundred years. By then everything has become empty for her, and she refuses to take the potion again. Williams argued that this is not bad luck but what would happen to anyone. What gives us reason to go on living are “categorical desires”: projects and passions that are not conditional on staying alive. Also called Williams’s Makropulos argument. Held by Bernard Williams, Shelly Kagan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Mortality gives value"],"thinkers":["williams","kagan"],"position":"tedium_of_immortality","tags":["immortality","boredom","bernard williams","makropulos case","living forever","williams’s makropulos argument"]},{"term":"Mortality gives value","def":"Without an end, nothing would be urgent or precious; our values depend on our mortality.","more":"This view holds that mortality is not just something to accept but part of what makes life valuable. Martha Nussbaum argued that many human virtues, like courage, generosity and love, make sense only for beings who can be hurt and who can lose things; an immortal, invulnerable life might lack them. Samuel Scheffler argues that because our time is limited, we must choose what to do with it, and this scarcity gives our choices weight. Martin Hägglund argues that we care about anything only because it can be lost. Also called Finitude view. Held by Martha Nussbaum, Samuel Scheffler, Martin Hägglund.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Tedium of immortality","Being-toward-death"],"thinkers":["nussbaum","scheffler"],"position":"mortality_gives_value","tags":["mortality","finitude","value of death","meaning and death","preciousness","finitude view"]},{"term":"Buddhist non-attachment","def":"Fear of death comes from clinging to a self that was never fixed; letting go brings peace.","more":"Buddhism teaches that suffering comes from craving and clinging, and that everything we cling to is impermanent. The self, too, is not a fixed, lasting thing but a changing stream of bodily and mental processes, a teaching called anattā (“not-self”). Fear of death, on this view, is the deepest form of clinging: holding on to a self that was never solid. Meditating on impermanence and on death, a practice traditional in many Buddhist schools, aims to loosen this grip. Also called Non-clinging. Held by The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama), Dōgen.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Stoicism","Philosophical pessimism"],"thinkers":["buddha","dogen"],"position":"buddhist_non_attachment","tags":["non-attachment","buddhism","letting go","no-self","fear of death","clinging","non-clinging"]},{"term":"Being-toward-death","def":"Facing your own death squarely frees you to live your own life instead of everyone else’s.","more":"Being-toward-death is Martin Heidegger’s idea, set out in Being and Time. He argued that most of the time we live in a way he called “the they” (das Man): doing what “one” does, thinking what “one” thinks, and treating death as something that happens to other people, someday. This is comfortable but not truly our own. Death is the one possibility that is completely mine: no one can die my death for me. Also called Heideggerian authenticity. Held by Martin Heidegger.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Existentialism","Mortality gives value","Stoicism"],"thinkers":["heidegger"],"position":"being_toward_death","tags":["heidegger","being toward death","authenticity","mortality","dasein","heideggerian authenticity"]},{"term":"Hope beyond death","def":"Death is not the end, so live now in a way that prepares you for what comes after.","more":"Many religious traditions teach that death is not the end of a person. Christianity, Islam and Judaism in various forms speak of resurrection and judgment, or of the soul’s life with God. Hinduism and Buddhism teach rebirth and an ultimate liberation. On these views, how you live now matters for what follows, and death can be faced with hope rather than only fear or resignation. Also called Religious hope, Belief in an afterlife. Held by Plato, Augustine of Hippo, Thomas Aquinas, Blaise Pascal.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Supernaturalism about meaning","Leap of faith"],"thinkers":["plato","augustine","aquinas","pascal"],"position":"religious_hope_beyond_death","tags":["afterlife","life after death","hope","resurrection","heaven","religious hope","belief in an afterlife"]},{"term":"Resistance to death","def":"Death is an enemy, and we should work to push it back rather than make peace with it.","more":"This view refuses to make peace with death. It holds that death is a great evil, the loss of everything a person is and could be, and that the traditional philosophies of acceptance are ways of coping with something we could not change. Some writers, like Miguel de Unamuno, expressed a passionate refusal to accept annihilation. Today, transhumanists such as Nick Bostrom argue that ageing is a disease-like process that science may slow or stop, and that accepting death as natural has made us too passive. Also called Defiance of death, Anti-ageing view. Held by Nick Bostrom, Miguel de Unamuno.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Immortality is desirable","Deprivation account of death"],"thinkers":["bostrom"],"position":"resistance_to_death","tags":["anti-death","life extension","transhumanism","nick bostrom","immortality","defiance of death","anti-ageing view"]},{"term":"Existence can benefit","def":"If a life goes well overall, being brought into it was good for the one who lives it.","more":"This view holds that coming into existence can be good for the person who comes to exist. If someone’s life goes well overall, with more good than bad, then being created was a benefit to them, even though before they existed there was no one who lacked anything. Derek Parfit argued that causing someone to exist with a life worth living benefits that person: we can compare a good life with never existing, not as two states of the same person, but by asking whether the life is good for the one who has it. This view is the common-sense answer for most people and underlies the idea that parents give their children the gift of life. Also called Coming into existence can be good for you. Held by Derek Parfit, Nils Holtug.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Immortality is desirable"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"position":"existence_can_benefit","tags":["benefit of existence","coming into existence","good to be born","procreation","population ethics","coming into existence can be good for you"]},{"term":"Procreative neutrality","def":"Being born is neither good nor bad for the child, since there was no one beforehand to gain or lose.","more":"Procreative neutrality is the view that creating a person, even one with a happy life, does not in itself make anyone better off, because before conception there is no one who could be benefited. Jan Narveson put it in a well-known slogan: we are in favour of making people happy, but neutral about making happy people. Many add that once a person exists, their interests count fully, and that creating someone who would suffer terribly would be wrong. This leads to an asymmetry many people find intuitive: there is a reason not to create a miserable life, but no strong reason to create a happy one. Also called Non-comparative view of existence. Held by Jan Narveson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Epicureanism about death"],"thinkers":[],"position":"procreative_neutrality","tags":["procreation","non-identity","jan narveson","population ethics","making happy people","non-comparative view of existence"]},{"term":"Essentialism about human nature","def":"Humans have a real nature, and a good life is one that fulfils it.","more":"Essentialism about human nature says that being human involves a real, shared nature, a set of capacities and tendencies that make us the kind of beings we are, and that this nature shows what a good human life is. Aristotle held that humans are rational, social animals, and that we flourish by exercising reason and virtue in community. Thomas Aquinas built on this, holding that God created human nature with built-in ends, such as preserving life, knowing truth and living in society, which ground the natural law. On this view, we discover what we are for; we do not invent it. Also called Aristotelian view of human nature, Natural law view. Held by Aristotle, Thomas Aquinas.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Eudaimonism","Supernaturalism about meaning"],"thinkers":["aristotle","aquinas"],"position":"essentialism_about_human_nature","tags":["human nature","essence","aristotle","natural law","flourishing","eudaimonia","aristotelian view of human nature","natural law view"]},{"term":"Evolved human nature","def":"Humans have a real nature shaped by evolution, which shapes what helps us thrive.","more":"This view holds that there is a human nature, but that it is the product of evolution by natural selection, not of design. Our minds come with built-in tendencies, such as attachment to children, fear of snakes, a sense of fairness, and the capacity for language, because these helped our ancestors survive and reproduce. Edward O. Wilson and Steven Pinker argued that ignoring these tendencies leads to bad theories of society and of the good life. Also called Darwinian view of human nature. Held by Steven Pinker, Edward O. Wilson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Essentialism about human nature"],"thinkers":[],"position":"evolved_human_nature","tags":["evolutionary psychology","human nature","evolution","biology","innate","darwinian view of human nature"]},{"term":"Socially made human nature","def":"What humans are like is mostly made by society and history, and changes when they change.","more":"This view holds that most of what we call “human nature”, such as competitiveness, family forms or ambitions, is shaped by the society, economy and history people live in, rather than fixed in us. Karl Marx wrote, in his sixth thesis on Feuerbach, that the human essence is the ensemble of social relations. On this view, people in a capitalist society might seem naturally greedy, but that reflects the society, not some unchanging nature. Change the conditions and people change too. Also called Historical view of human nature. Held by Karl Marx.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Situated freedom"],"thinkers":["marx"],"position":"social_constructionism_about_human_nature","tags":["social constructionism","blank slate","marx","human nature","nurture","historical view of human nature"]},{"term":"Radical freedom","def":"You are always free to choose, and pretending otherwise (bad faith) is lying to yourself.","more":"Jean-Paul Sartre held that humans are radically free: whatever our circumstances, we always choose how to respond to them, and we are responsible for those choices. Because this freedom is frightening, we often hide from it in what Sartre called “bad faith”: a kind of self-deception in which we pretend we are things, fixed by our role, character or situation. His example is a café waiter who plays the waiter too perfectly, as if he simply were a waiter and not a free person. Saying “I had no choice” when we did is bad faith. Also called Sartrean freedom, Bad faith. Held by Jean-Paul Sartre.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Existentialism","Situated freedom"],"thinkers":["sartre"],"position":"radical_freedom","tags":["sartre","bad faith","existentialism","freedom","choice","condemned to be free","sartrean freedom"]},{"term":"Good-cause view of meaning","def":"A life is meaningful to the extent that it does real good in the world, especially for others.","more":"The good-cause view is a form of objectivism about meaning. It says a life gains meaning by bringing about things that are really valuable: relieving suffering, raising children well, advancing knowledge, making beautiful things that others enjoy. What counts is the good your life actually causes, not how you feel about it, and not even whether you know about it. Aaron Smuts defended a version he called the good cause account. Held by Aaron Smuts, Peter Singer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Objectivism about meaning","Fundamentality theory","Transcendence view of meaning"],"thinkers":["singer"],"position":"good_cause_view","tags":["meaning of life","doing good","altruism","helping others","meaningful life"]},{"term":"Fundamentality theory","def":"A life is meaningful when it uses reason to engage positively with the most basic conditions of human existence.","more":"The fundamentality theory is Thaddeus Metz’s account of meaning, set out in Meaning in Life: An Analytic Study (2013). Metz looks at the lives we find most meaningful, devoted to the good, the true and the beautiful, and asks what they share. His answer: each directs human reason, in thought or in action, toward the fundamental conditions of human life, the features that explain much else about it. A reformer tackles the deep causes of injustice, not a parking dispute; a scientist uncovers basic laws, not the number of hairs on someone’s head; a great novelist explores love, death and freedom. Held by Thaddeus Metz.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Objectivism about meaning","Good-cause view of meaning","Transcendence view of meaning"],"thinkers":["metz"],"position":"fundamentality_theory","tags":["thaddeus metz","meaning of life","fundamental conditions","reason"]},{"term":"Transcendence view of meaning","def":"A life gains meaning by reaching beyond its own limits and connecting with things of value outside itself.","more":"Robert Nozick, in Philosophical Explanations (1981) and The Examined Life (1989), argued that the search for meaning is a search for connection. Anything limited seems to lack meaning when considered only in itself; it gains meaning by being linked to something beyond its limits that has value. A life gains meaning through ties to other people, to a tradition or community, to nature, to the pursuit of truth and, for some, to God. The wider and more valuable the things you connect with, the more meaning your life has. Held by Robert Nozick.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Objectivism about meaning","Good-cause view of meaning","Fundamentality theory"],"thinkers":["nozick"],"position":"transcendence_theory_of_meaning","tags":["self-transcendence","transcendence","meaning of life","robert nozick","beyond the self"]},{"term":"Fitting fulfilment view","def":"Meaning arises only when you actively love something that really deserves that love; neither side alone gives any.","more":"Susan Wolf’s fitting fulfilment view is the best-known hybrid theory of meaning. In her phrase, meaning arises when subjective attraction meets objective attractiveness: when you are gripped by, and actively engaged in, something genuinely worthwhile. Both conditions are required. The Blob, who cares about nothing, lacks meaning; so does someone who passionately pursues something worthless; so does someone who does valuable work while feeling alienated from it. Held by Susan Wolf.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Hybrid theory of meaning","Meaning as a separate good"],"thinkers":["wolf"],"position":"fitting_fulfilment_view","tags":["susan wolf","fitting fulfilment","meaning of life","love","meaningful life"]},{"term":"Desire-based subjectivism","def":"Your life is meaningful when you get, or do, what you deeply want, whatever that is.","more":"Desire-based subjectivism is the simplest form of subjectivism about meaning. It says a life is meaningful to the extent that the person gets what they most deeply want, or is absorbed in doing what they want to do. No activity is meaningful or meaningless in itself. Richard Taylor gave the view its most famous illustration in Good and Evil (1970): if the gods made Sisyphus want to roll stones, his endless labour would have all the meaning it needed, because the meaning of a life comes from within us, not from what we achieve. Held by Richard Taylor.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Subjectivism about meaning","Caring view of meaning","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["richard_taylor"],"position":"desire_based_subjectivism","tags":["subjectivism","desire satisfaction","meaning of life","wants","richard taylor"]},{"term":"Caring view of meaning","def":"What gives a life meaning is what you love or care about, whether or not it is valuable in itself.","more":"Harry Frankfurt argued, in The Importance of What We Care About (1988) and The Reasons of Love (2004), that caring is the root of meaning. To care about something is to make it important to yourself: to invest in it, to be vulnerable to how it fares, and to let it shape your choices. Love is the purest form of caring. Frankfurt’s striking claim is that love does not respond to value it finds; it creates value. Held by Harry Frankfurt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Subjectivism about meaning","Desire-based subjectivism","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["frankfurt"],"position":"caring_view_of_meaning","tags":["harry frankfurt","caring","love","meaning of life","what we care about"]},{"term":"Active nihilism","def":"Since life has no given meaning, the strong response is to create new values and say yes to life without any cosmic backing.","more":"Active nihilism is Friedrich Nietzsche’s name, in notebooks from the 1880s, for one response to the collapse of traditional values, which he summed up in the phrase “God is dead”. Passive nihilism sinks into weariness and despair once the old values lose their hold. Active nihilism clears them away as a sign of strength, making room for new values. Nietzsche hoped for people who could create values of their own and affirm life as a whole, pain included; his tests were the thought of eternal recurrence and the attitude he called amor fati, love of fate. Held by Friedrich Nietzsche.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Existential nihilism","Absurdism","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"position":"active_nihilism","tags":["nietzsche","nihilism","revaluation of values","amor fati","create values"]},{"term":"Priorism about the harm of death","def":"Death harms you before it happens, by defeating interests you have while you are alive.","more":"” by saying: before it occurs. While you are alive you have interests in things that reach into the future: finishing a project, seeing your children grow up, simply going on living. Death defeats those interests, and since the interests are yours now, the harm is to you now, even though the death has not yet happened and you may never know it is coming. George Pitcher, in “The Misfortunes of the Dead” (1984), and Joel Feinberg, in Harm to Others (1984), defended versions of the view, which also explains how the dead can be wronged by broken promises or slander. Held by George Pitcher, Joel Feinberg.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Deprivation account of death","Subsequentism about the harm of death","Eternalism about the harm of death"],"thinkers":["feinberg"],"position":"priorism_about_death","tags":["timing of harm of death","harm of death","epicurean problem","interests"]},{"term":"Subsequentism about the harm of death","def":"Death is bad for you at the times after it, when you would otherwise have been living well.","more":"Subsequentism says death harms you after it happens: at the times when, had you not died, you would have been enjoying good things. Ben Bradley defended this in Well-Being and Death (2009). On his view, how well off you are at a time can be compared with how well off you would have been then. After your death your well-being is at zero, which is lower than the good level you would otherwise have had. Held by Ben Bradley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Deprivation account of death","Priorism about the harm of death","Eternalism about the harm of death"],"thinkers":[],"position":"subsequentism_about_death","tags":["timing of harm of death","harm of death","epicurean problem","after death"]},{"term":"Eternalism about the harm of death","def":"That death is bad for you is true at all times, as a fact about your life as a whole.","more":"Eternalism says the badness of death is not located at one moment. Fred Feldman, in Confrontations with the Reaper (1992), argued that death is bad for you because the whole life you get is worse than the whole life you would have had if you had not died when you did. That comparison between two whole lives is true at every time: before your birth, during your life and after your death, rather as it is always true that one number is larger than another. So death is eternally bad for its victim. Held by Fred Feldman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Deprivation account of death","Priorism about the harm of death","Subsequentism about the harm of death"],"thinkers":["feldman"],"position":"eternalism_about_death","tags":["timing of harm of death","harm of death","atemporal","nagel"]},{"term":"Categorical desire view","def":"Death is bad for you because it frustrates the desires that give you reason to go on living.","more":"In “The Makropulos Case: Reflections on the Tedium of Immortality” (1973), Bernard Williams distinguished two kinds of desire. Conditional desires depend on your being alive: if I am alive, I want to be warm and fed. Categorical desires are not conditional in that way: they give you a reason to stay alive at all, such as a desire to finish a great work or to see your children grown. Death is bad for you, Williams argued, because it frustrates your categorical desires. Held by Bernard Williams.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Time-relative interest account","Tedium of immortality","Narrative view of death"],"thinkers":["williams"],"position":"categorical_desire_view","tags":["bernard williams","categorical desires","harm of death","desires","reasons to live"]},{"term":"Narrative view of death","def":"How bad a death is depends on how it affects the story of a life, not just on how many good moments it removes.","more":"The narrative view holds that a life is not just a sum of good and bad moments; its shape matters too. David Velleman, in “Well-Being and Time” (1991), argued that two lives with the same total of momentary well-being can differ in value: a life that rises from hardship to success is better than one that falls from success into misery. What an event means depends on its place in the story. Applied to death, the view says death is worse when it cuts a story off at a point that leaves earlier efforts wasted or struggles unredeemed, and less bad when it comes after the story has reached a fitting close. Held by David Velleman.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Life and death","related":["Time-relative interest account","Categorical desire view","Deprivation account of death"],"thinkers":[],"position":"narrative_view_of_death","tags":["narrative","life story","shape of a life","harm of death","velleman"]},{"term":"Thales of Miletus","def":"Greek philosopher (624 BCE–546 BCE). Everything comes from water. The first try at explaining nature without myths.","more":"Thales lived in Miletus, a Greek city on the coast of what is now Turkey. Later Greeks, starting with Aristotle, called him the first philosopher because he explained the world by natural causes instead of stories about gods. None of his writings survive.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Milesian school"],"thinkers":["thales"],"tags":["milesian school","water as arche","presocratics","first philosopher"]},{"term":"Heraclitus","def":"Greek philosopher (535 BCE–475 BCE). Everything flows; you cannot step into the same river twice.","more":"Heraclitus came from Ephesus, a Greek city in what is now Turkey. He wrote in short, riddling sayings, and only fragments quoted by later authors survive. He is famous for seeing the world as constant change held together by a hidden order, which he called the logos.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Presocratic"],"thinkers":["heraclitus"],"tags":["heraclitus of ephesus","flux","panta rhei","logos","unity of opposites","river"]},{"term":"Parmenides","def":"Greek philosopher (515 BCE–450 BCE). Reason proves that change is impossible; the senses deceive us.","more":"Parmenides lived in Elea, a Greek colony in southern Italy. In a poem about a journey to a goddess, he argued that what truly exists must be one, unchanging and eternal. He was among the first to trust pure argument over what our eyes tell us, and he shaped Greek metaphysics for centuries.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Eleatic school"],"thinkers":["parmenides"],"tags":["parmenides of elea","eleatic school","being","monism","change is impossible"]},{"term":"Zeno of Elea","def":"Greek philosopher (490 BCE–430 BCE). Clever paradoxes that seem to prove motion is impossible.","more":"Zeno was a student and defender of Parmenides in Elea, in southern Italy. He invented paradoxes, like Achilles never catching a tortoise, to show that common sense about motion and plurality leads to absurd results. Mathematicians and philosophers argued about his puzzles for over two thousand years.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Eleatic school"],"thinkers":["zeno_elea"],"tags":["zeno's paradoxes","achilles and the tortoise","dichotomy paradox","eleatic school"]},{"term":"Pythagoras","def":"Greek philosopher (570 BCE–490 BCE). Numbers are the key to the world, and souls are reborn.","more":"Pythagoras was born on the island of Samos and founded a community in Croton, in southern Italy. He wrote nothing, and later followers credited him with many discoveries, so the real man is hard to separate from legend. His school linked mathematics, music and a strict religious way of life.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pythagoreanism"],"thinkers":["pythagoras"],"tags":["pythagoras of samos","pythagoreanism","pythagoreans","music of the spheres","transmigration of souls","numbers"]},{"term":"Democritus","def":"Greek philosopher (460 BCE–370 BCE). Everything is tiny atoms moving through empty space.","more":"Democritus came from Abdera in northern Greece. Building on his teacher Leucippus, he argued that the world is made of countless tiny, uncuttable particles called atoms. His many books are lost, but his atomism inspired Epicurus, Lucretius and, much later, modern science.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Atomism"],"thinkers":["democritus"],"tags":["atomism","atoms and void","atomist","laughing philosopher","leucippus"]},{"term":"Socrates","def":"Greek philosopher (470 BCE–399 BCE). The unexamined life is not worth living. Question everything.","more":"Socrates was an Athenian who wrote nothing; we know him mainly through his students Plato and Xenophon. He questioned people about justice, courage and the good life, often showing that experts did not know what they claimed. Athens sentenced him to death in 399 BCE for impiety and corrupting the young.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Socratic philosophy"],"thinkers":["socrates"],"tags":["socratic method","elenchus","unexamined life","know thyself","socratic irony","apology"]},{"term":"Plato","def":"Greek philosopher (428 BCE–348 BCE). Reality is a world of perfect Forms; what we see are shadows.","more":"Plato was a student of Socrates and founded the Academy in Athens, one of the first schools of higher learning. He wrote dialogues, usually starring Socrates, on justice, love, knowledge and the soul. His work set the agenda for much of Western philosophy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Platonism"],"thinkers":["plato"],"tags":["platon","theory of forms","allegory of the cave","the republic","platonism","philosopher king"]},{"term":"Aristotle","def":"Greek philosopher (384 BCE–322 BCE). Live well by building good habits; everything has a purpose.","more":"Aristotle studied with Plato for twenty years, tutored Alexander the Great, and founded his own school, the Lyceum, in Athens. He wrote on almost everything: logic, biology, physics, ethics, politics, poetry. For nearly two thousand years he was simply called 'the Philosopher'.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Aristotelianism","Peripatetic school"],"thinkers":["aristotle"],"tags":["aristoteles","virtue ethics","golden mean","eudaimonia","four causes","nicomachean ethics","the philosopher"]},{"term":"Diogenes of Sinope","def":"Greek philosopher (412 BCE–323 BCE). Live by nature, own nothing, and mock empty social customs.","more":"Diogenes was exiled from Sinope and lived in Athens and Corinth, reportedly sleeping in a large clay jar. He was the most famous Cynic, teaching through shocking public acts rather than books. Many stories about him, such as searching for an honest man with a lamp in daylight, come from later anecdotes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Cynicism"],"thinkers":["diogenes"],"tags":["diogenes the cynic","cynicism","cynics","living like a dog"]},{"term":"Epicurus","def":"Greek philosopher (341 BCE–270 BCE). Seek calm pleasure, have friends, and do not fear death.","more":"Epicurus founded a school in Athens called the Garden, which admitted women and slaves. He taught that the good life is a pleasant, peaceful one, free of pain and fear. Only a few letters and sayings of his survive, but his ideas were spread widely by followers like Lucretius.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Epicureanism"],"thinkers":["epicurus"],"tags":["epicureanism","ataraxia","pleasure","hedonism","garden","tetrapharmakos"]},{"term":"Zeno of Citium","def":"Greek philosopher (334 BCE–262 BCE). Virtue is the only true good; live in agreement with nature.","more":"Zeno was a merchant from Citium on Cyprus who came to Athens and studied with the Cynic Crates. Around 300 BCE he began teaching at the Stoa Poikile (Painted Porch), which gave Stoicism its name. His writings are lost, but his school became one of the most influential in the ancient world.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Stoicism"],"thinkers":["zeno_citium"],"tags":["zeno the stoic","stoicism","stoics","founder of stoicism","stoa"]},{"term":"Pyrrho of Elis","def":"Greek philosopher (365 BCE–275 BCE). We cannot know how things really are, so suspend judgment and be calm.","more":"Pyrrho travelled with Alexander the Great's army as far as India, and later taught in his home town of Elis. He wrote nothing; his student Timon reported his views. He became the namesake of Pyrrhonism, the most radical form of ancient skepticism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pyrrhonism","Skepticism"],"thinkers":["pyrrho"],"tags":["pyrrhonism","skepticism","scepticism","suspension of judgment","epoche"]},{"term":"Seneca","def":"Roman philosopher (4 BCE–65). Life is long enough if you know how to use it.","more":"Seneca was a Roman statesman, playwright and Stoic philosopher, and an adviser to the emperor Nero. His letters and essays give practical advice on anger, grief, time and death. Nero later ordered him to kill himself, and he did so in 65 CE.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Stoicism"],"thinkers":["seneca"],"tags":["seneca the younger","lucius annaeus seneca","stoicism","letters to lucilius","on the shortness of life"]},{"term":"Epictetus","def":"Roman philosopher (50–135). Some things are up to us and some are not. Know the difference.","more":"Epictetus was born a slave and later freed; he taught Stoic philosophy in Rome and then in Nicopolis, in Greece. He wrote nothing himself, but his student Arrian recorded his lectures. His blunt, practical teaching later deeply influenced Marcus Aurelius.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Stoicism"],"thinkers":["epictetus"],"tags":["stoicism","dichotomy of control","enchiridion","discourses","stoic"]},{"term":"Marcus Aurelius","def":"Roman philosopher (121–180). A Roman emperor's private notes on staying calm and doing your duty.","more":"Marcus Aurelius was Roman emperor from 161 to 180 CE, and spent much of his reign on military campaigns. In private notebooks, never meant for publication, he wrote Stoic reminders to himself. These became the Meditations, one of the most read works of philosophy ever.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Stoicism"],"thinkers":["marcus_aurelius"],"tags":["meditations","stoicism","stoic emperor","roman emperor"]},{"term":"Lucretius","def":"Roman philosopher (99 BCE–55 BCE). A great poem that explains atoms to free us from fear of death.","more":"Lucretius was a Roman poet about whom almost nothing is known. His long Latin poem On the Nature of Things sets out Epicurus's philosophy: atoms, the void, and why we need not fear the gods or death. Rediscovered in 1417, it influenced Renaissance and early modern thinkers.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Epicureanism"],"thinkers":["lucretius"],"tags":["titus lucretius carus","de rerum natura","on the nature of things","epicureanism","atomism"]},{"term":"Sextus Empiricus","def":"Greek philosopher (160–210). For every argument there is an equal argument against it, so suspend judgment.","more":"Sextus Empiricus was a Greek doctor and skeptic who lived in the Roman Empire around 200 CE; we know almost nothing about his life. His books are our main source for ancient Pyrrhonian skepticism. Their rediscovery in the 1500s shaped Montaigne, Descartes and modern debates about knowledge.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pyrrhonism","Skepticism"],"thinkers":["sextus"],"tags":["pyrrhonism","skepticism","scepticism","outlines of pyrrhonism","suspension of judgment"]},{"term":"Plotinus","def":"Greek philosopher (204–270). All things flow from the One, and the soul longs to return to it.","more":"Plotinus was probably born in Egypt, studied in Alexandria, and taught philosophy in Rome. His student Porphyry collected his essays into the Enneads. He founded Neoplatonism, which shaped Christian, Jewish and Islamic thought for over a thousand years.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Neoplatonism"],"thinkers":["plotinus"],"tags":["neoplatonism","the one","emanation","enneads","neoplatonist"]},{"term":"Augustine of Hippo","def":"Medieval Christian philosopher (354–430). Our hearts are restless until they rest in God.","more":"Augustine was born in North Africa, taught rhetoric in Italy, and converted to Christianity in 386. As bishop of Hippo he wrote works that combined Platonism with Christian faith. His Confessions is often called the first autobiography, and his ideas shaped Western Christianity for centuries.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Christian Platonism"],"thinkers":["augustine"],"tags":["saint augustine","st augustine","confessions","city of god","original sin","free will defense"]},{"term":"Boethius","def":"Medieval Christian philosopher (477–524). Written in prison: fortune is fickle, but true happiness cannot be taken away.","more":"Boethius was a Roman aristocrat and official under the Ostrogothic king Theodoric. He translated Aristotle's logic into Latin, which kept it alive in the medieval West. Accused of treason, he wrote The Consolation of Philosophy in prison before his execution.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Christian Platonism"],"thinkers":["boethius"],"tags":["consolation of philosophy","fortune","divine foreknowledge","providence"]},{"term":"Anselm of Canterbury","def":"Medieval Christian philosopher (1033–1109). God is that than which nothing greater can be conceived.","more":"Anselm was born in Aosta in northern Italy, became a monk and abbot at Bec in Normandy, and later Archbishop of Canterbury. He is famous for the ontological argument, which tries to prove God exists by thinking alone. His motto was 'faith seeking understanding'.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Scholasticism"],"thinkers":["anselm"],"tags":["saint anselm","ontological argument","proslogion","faith seeking understanding"]},{"term":"Avicenna (Ibn Sina)","def":"Islamic philosopher (980–1037). A man floating in the dark would still know he exists.","more":"Avicenna was a Persian doctor and philosopher, born near Bukhara. His Canon of Medicine was a standard medical textbook in Europe and the Islamic world for centuries. His philosophy reworked Aristotle and became the starting point for later Islamic and Christian thinkers.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Islamic Aristotelianism (Falsafa)"],"thinkers":["avicenna"],"tags":["abu ali sina","floating man","necessary existent","essence and existence"]},{"term":"Al-Ghazali","def":"Islamic philosopher (1058–1111). Fire does not burn cotton by itself; God makes it happen.","more":"Al-Ghazali was a Persian theologian who taught in Baghdad, then left his post after a spiritual crisis to follow the Sufi path. In The Incoherence of the Philosophers he attacked Avicenna and other philosophers on points where he thought they contradicted Islam. He remains one of the most influential thinkers in Islamic history.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Ash'arism","Sufism"],"thinkers":["alghazali"],"tags":["ghazali","al-ghazzali","algazel","occasionalism","incoherence of the philosophers","sufism"]},{"term":"Averroes (Ibn Rushd)","def":"Islamic philosopher (1126–1198). Philosophy and religion, rightly understood, cannot contradict each other.","more":"Averroes was a judge and doctor from Córdoba in Muslim Spain. He wrote detailed commentaries on Aristotle, so famous that medieval Europeans called him simply 'the Commentator'. He defended philosophy against al-Ghazali's attacks.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Islamic Aristotelianism (Falsafa)"],"thinkers":["averroes"],"tags":["commentator","averroism","decisive treatise","incoherence of the incoherence"]},{"term":"Maimonides","def":"Jewish philosopher (1138–1204). We can say only what God is not; reason and faith work together.","more":"Maimonides (Moses ben Maimon) was born in Córdoba, fled persecution, and settled in Egypt, where he was a doctor and leader of the Jewish community. He organized Jewish law and wrote The Guide for the Perplexed to show how faith fits with Aristotle's philosophy. He is the most important Jewish philosopher of the Middle Ages.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Jewish Aristotelianism"],"thinkers":["maimonides"],"tags":["moses maimonides","rambam","guide for the perplexed","negative theology","via negativa","jewish philosophy"]},{"term":"Thomas Aquinas","def":"Medieval Christian philosopher (1225–1274). Faith and reason both lead to God; nature has a moral law built in.","more":"Aquinas was an Italian Dominican friar who taught in Paris and Italy. He combined Aristotle's philosophy with Christian theology in a huge, carefully argued system. He remains the official model philosopher of the Catholic Church.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Thomism","Scholasticism"],"thinkers":["aquinas"],"tags":["saint thomas aquinas","thomism","five ways","summa theologica","natural law"]},{"term":"William of Ockham","def":"Medieval Christian philosopher (1287–1347). Do not multiply things beyond necessity. Keep explanations simple.","more":"Ockham was an English Franciscan friar and logician. He was summoned to the pope's court over his views, fled, and spent his last years in Munich. He is famous for 'Ockham's razor' and for denying that universals, like 'redness', exist outside the mind.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Nominalism","Scholasticism"],"thinkers":["ockham"],"tags":["occam","ockham's razor","occam's razor","nominalism","parsimony"]},{"term":"The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama)","def":"Indian philosopher (480 BCE–400 BCE). Suffering comes from craving, and there is a path to end it.","more":"Siddhartha Gautama was a prince in what is now Nepal and northern India who left home to seek an end to suffering. After his awakening he taught for decades; his teachings were passed down orally and written down centuries later. Scholars disagree about his exact dates.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Buddhism"],"thinkers":["buddha"],"tags":["gautama buddha","shakyamuni","buddhism","four noble truths","eightfold path","nirvana"]},{"term":"Nagarjuna","def":"Indian philosopher (150–250). Nothing has a fixed nature of its own; everything is empty.","more":"Nagarjuna was an Indian Buddhist monk, probably active in the 2nd century CE; little is reliably known about his life. He founded the Madhyamaka (Middle Way) school. His sharp arguments made him one of the most important thinkers in Buddhist history.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Madhyamaka","Mahayana Buddhism"],"thinkers":["nagarjuna"],"tags":["madhyamaka","emptiness","sunyata","middle way","two truths"]},{"term":"Adi Shankara","def":"Indian philosopher (700–750). Your true self and ultimate reality are one and the same.","more":"Shankara was a Hindu philosopher and teacher; tradition dates him 788–820 CE, while many scholars prefer about 700–750. He wrote commentaries on the Upanishads and other key texts. He is the most famous teacher of Advaita (non-dual) Vedanta.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Advaita Vedanta"],"thinkers":["shankara"],"tags":["sankara","shankaracharya","advaita vedanta","nondualism","brahman","atman"]},{"term":"Confucius","def":"Chinese philosopher (551 BCE–479 BCE). Become a person of good character through learning, ritual and respect.","more":"Confucius (Kongzi) was a teacher and minor official in the state of Lu during a time of political disorder. He hoped to restore the good order of the early Zhou dynasty. His sayings, collected by his students in the Analects, shaped Chinese culture and government for over two thousand years.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Confucianism"],"thinkers":["confucius"],"tags":["kongzi","kong fuzi","confucianism","analects","ren","li","junzi"]},{"term":"Laozi","def":"Chinese philosopher (600 BCE–500 BCE). Follow the Dao: act without forcing, like water flowing downhill.","more":"Laozi is the traditional author of the Daodejing and is said to have lived in the 6th century BCE, but many scholars think he may be legendary. The Daodejing is a short book of poetic verses, probably put together over time. It became the founding text of Daoism and one of the most translated books in the world.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Daoism"],"thinkers":["laozi"],"tags":["lao tzu","lao-tze","tao te ching","daodejing","taoism","daoism","wu wei"]},{"term":"Zhuangzi","def":"Chinese philosopher (369 BCE–286 BCE). Did I dream I was a butterfly, or is a butterfly dreaming it's me?","more":"Zhuangzi (Zhuang Zhou) was a Chinese thinker of the Warring States period. The book named after him uses funny stories, talking animals and paradoxes to challenge fixed ways of seeing. It is, with the Daodejing, a founding text of Daoism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Daoism"],"thinkers":["zhuangzi"],"tags":["chuang tzu","chuang-tzu","butterfly dream","daoism","taoism","zhuang zhou"]},{"term":"Mozi","def":"Chinese philosopher (470 BCE–391 BCE). Care for everyone equally, and judge actions by their benefit to all.","more":"Mozi (Mo Di) was a Chinese thinker, probably from a craftsman background, who founded the Mohist school. Mohists were known for opposing aggressive war and even helping defend cities under attack. He was the first major critic of Confucianism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Mohism"],"thinkers":["mozi"],"tags":["mo tzu","mohism","mohist","universal love","impartial care","jian ai","consequentialism"]},{"term":"Mencius","def":"Chinese philosopher (372 BCE–289 BCE). Human nature is good; anyone would rush to save a child falling into a well.","more":"Mencius (Mengzi) was the most important Confucian after Confucius himself. He travelled between states advising rulers to govern with kindness. His book argues that people are born with the beginnings of goodness.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Confucianism"],"thinkers":["mencius"],"tags":["mengzi","meng tzu","human nature is good","confucianism","four sprouts","child at the well"]},{"term":"Xunzi","def":"Chinese philosopher (310 BCE–235 BCE). Human nature is bad; goodness comes from effort, education and ritual.","more":"Xunzi (Xun Kuang) was a Confucian scholar of the late Warring States period. Against Mencius, he argued that people are born with selfish desires that must be shaped by learning and ritual. Two of his students, Han Fei and Li Si, became leading Legalists.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Confucianism"],"thinkers":["xunzi"],"tags":["hsun tzu","xun kuang","human nature is bad","confucianism","ritual","li"]},{"term":"Zhu Xi","def":"Chinese philosopher (1130–1200). Study things carefully to grasp the principle in everything.","more":"Zhu Xi was a scholar of the Song dynasty who built Confucianism into a complete philosophical system. He chose the Four Books as the core of Confucian learning and wrote commentaries on them. From 1313 until 1905, his commentaries were the basis of China's imperial exams.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Neo-Confucianism"],"thinkers":["zhuxi"],"tags":["chu hsi","neo-confucianism","li","principle","investigation of things"]},{"term":"Wang Yangming","def":"Chinese philosopher (1472–1529). Knowing and acting are one; goodness is already in your mind.","more":"Wang Yangming was a Ming dynasty official, general and philosopher. After failing to find principle by staring at bamboo as Zhu Xi's method seemed to suggest, he concluded that principle is found in the mind itself. His ideas became very influential in China and Japan.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Neo-Confucianism","School of Mind"],"thinkers":["wangyangming"],"tags":["wang shouren","wang yang-ming","neo-confucianism","unity of knowledge and action","innate knowledge","liangzhi"]},{"term":"Dōgen","def":"Japanese philosopher (1200–1253). Just sit: practice itself is enlightenment.","more":"Dōgen was a Japanese Buddhist monk who studied Zen in China and brought the Sōtō school back to Japan. He founded the Eihei-ji temple. His Shōbōgenzō is one of the deepest and most difficult works of Japanese philosophy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Sōtō Zen","Zen Buddhism"],"thinkers":["dogen"],"tags":["dogen","dogen zenji","soto zen","zazen","shikantaza","shobogenzo","zen"]},{"term":"Niccolò Machiavelli","def":"Modern European philosopher (1469–1527). Politics is about how people actually behave, not how they should.","more":"Machiavelli was a diplomat for the Republic of Florence until the Medici family returned to power and he was tortured and exiled. He then wrote The Prince, a blunt guide to gaining and keeping power, and the Discourses, which praise republics. 'Machiavellian' became a word for ruthless cunning.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Republicanism","Political realism"],"thinkers":["machiavelli"],"tags":["the prince","machiavellianism","realpolitik","political realism","ends justify the means"]},{"term":"Michel de Montaigne","def":"Modern European philosopher (1533–1592). What do I know? Honest, curious essays about being human.","more":"Montaigne was a French nobleman and magistrate who retired to his tower library to write about himself and the world. He invented the essay as a form: a personal 'attempt' to think something through. His humane skepticism influenced Descartes, Shakespeare and many writers since.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Renaissance skepticism"],"thinkers":["montaigne"],"tags":["essays","skepticism","que sais-je","essayist"]},{"term":"Francis Bacon","def":"Modern European philosopher (1561–1626). Knowledge is power. Learn from careful experiments, not old books.","more":"Bacon was an English lawyer and statesman who became Lord Chancellor before being disgraced for taking bribes. He argued that science needed a new method based on observation and experiment. He is often called a father of the scientific method.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Empiricism"],"thinkers":["bacon"],"tags":["empiricism","scientific method","novum organum","knowledge is power","idols"]},{"term":"Thomas Hobbes","def":"Modern European philosopher (1588–1679). Without government, life would be nasty, brutish and short.","more":"Hobbes was an English philosopher who lived through the English Civil War. In Leviathan he argued that people should give power to a strong sovereign to escape the war of all against all. He was also a thoroughgoing materialist who saw humans as complex machines.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Social contract theory","Materialism"],"thinkers":["hobbes"],"tags":["leviathan","social contract","state of nature","nasty brutish and short","absolutism"]},{"term":"René Descartes","def":"Modern European philosopher (1596–1650). I think, therefore I am. Doubt everything until you find certainty.","more":"Descartes was a French philosopher and mathematician who spent most of his working life in the Dutch Republic. He invented coordinate geometry and tried to rebuild knowledge on certain foundations. He is often called the father of modern philosophy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Rationalism","Cartesianism"],"thinkers":["descartes"],"tags":["cartesian","cogito","cogito ergo sum","i think therefore i am","dualism","mind-body dualism","method of doubt"]},{"term":"Baruch Spinoza","def":"Modern European philosopher (1632–1677). God and Nature are one; understanding necessity brings freedom.","more":"Spinoza was a Dutch-Jewish philosopher who was expelled from his synagogue at 23 and earned a living partly by grinding lenses. His Ethics, written like a geometry book, argues that there is only one substance: God, or Nature. He was also an early defender of free thought and secular government.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Rationalism"],"thinkers":["spinoza"],"tags":["benedict de spinoza","pantheism","ethics","deus sive natura","monism","rationalism"]},{"term":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz","def":"Modern European philosopher (1646–1716). This is the best of all possible worlds.","more":"Leibniz was a German polymath who invented calculus independently of Newton and designed a calculating machine. He worked as a diplomat, librarian and adviser to princes. His philosophy describes reality as made of simple, mind-like units called monads.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Rationalism"],"thinkers":["leibniz"],"tags":["monads","monadology","best of all possible worlds","theodicy","rationalism","sufficient reason"]},{"term":"John Locke","def":"Modern European philosopher (1632–1704). The mind starts as a blank slate; government must protect our rights.","more":"Locke was an English philosopher and physician involved in the politics of the Glorious Revolution. His Essay argued that all ideas come from experience, and his Two Treatises defended natural rights and government by consent. He shaped the American founders and liberal politics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Empiricism","Liberalism","Social contract theory"],"thinkers":["locke"],"tags":["tabula rasa","blank slate","empiricism","natural rights","social contract","liberalism"]},{"term":"George Berkeley","def":"Modern European philosopher (1685–1753). To be is to be perceived. There is no matter, only minds and ideas.","more":"Berkeley was an Irish philosopher and Anglican bishop. In his twenties he argued that material objects existing outside minds are an unnecessary idea. He thought his view defended common sense and religion against skepticism and atheism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Empiricism","Idealism"],"thinkers":["berkeley"],"tags":["bishop berkeley","idealism","immaterialism","esse est percipi","to be is to be perceived","empiricism"]},{"term":"David Hume","def":"Modern European philosopher (1711–1776). Reason is the slave of the passions; we never see causes, only habits.","more":"Hume was a Scottish philosopher, historian and essayist, a leading figure of the Scottish Enlightenment. He pushed empiricism to skeptical conclusions about causation, the self and religion. Kant said Hume woke him from his 'dogmatic slumber'.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Empiricism","Scottish Enlightenment"],"thinkers":["hume"],"tags":["empiricism","problem of induction","is-ought","skepticism","causation","bundle theory"]},{"term":"Thomas Reid","def":"Modern European philosopher (1710–1796). Trust common sense: we really do perceive the world and act freely.","more":"Reid was a Scottish minister and professor at Aberdeen and Glasgow. He argued that Hume's skepticism came from a mistaken theory that we perceive only ideas in our heads. He founded the Scottish school of Common Sense philosophy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Common Sense philosophy","Scottish Enlightenment"],"thinkers":["reid"],"tags":["common sense philosophy","scottish common sense","direct realism","common sense realism"]},{"term":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","def":"Modern European philosopher (1712–1778). Humans are born free, but everywhere they are in chains.","more":"Rousseau was born in Geneva and worked mainly in France. He argued that civilization and inequality corrupted naturally good human beings. His Social Contract, built on the idea of the 'general will', inspired the French Revolution, and his Emile changed thinking about education.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Social contract theory","Enlightenment"],"thinkers":["rousseau"],"tags":["social contract","general will","noble savage","emile"]},{"term":"Adam Smith","def":"Modern European philosopher (1723–1790). Sympathy shapes our morals; free exchange makes nations rich.","more":"Smith was a Scottish moral philosopher and close friend of Hume. His Theory of Moral Sentiments explains morality through sympathy and an imagined 'impartial spectator'. His Wealth of Nations founded modern economics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Scottish Enlightenment","Classical economics"],"thinkers":["smith"],"tags":["invisible hand","wealth of nations","theory of moral sentiments","sympathy","capitalism","free market"]},{"term":"Mary Wollstonecraft","def":"Modern European philosopher (1759–1797). Women are rational beings and deserve the same education and rights as men.","more":"Wollstonecraft was an English writer, teacher and activist. In A Vindication of the Rights of Woman she argued that women seemed inferior only because they were denied education. She is a founder of modern feminism; her daughter was Mary Shelley, author of Frankenstein.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Liberalism","Feminism"],"thinkers":["wollstonecraft"],"tags":["vindication of the rights of woman","feminism","women's rights","women's education"]},{"term":"Immanuel Kant","def":"Modern European philosopher (1724–1804). Act only on rules you could will everyone to follow.","more":"Kant spent his whole life in Königsberg, Prussia, where he taught at the university. His Critique of Pure Reason tried to settle the conflict between rationalists and empiricists. His ethics of duty and respect for persons remains central to moral philosophy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["German Idealism","Transcendental idealism"],"thinkers":["kant"],"tags":["kantian","categorical imperative","deontology","critique of pure reason","transcendental idealism"]},{"term":"Jeremy Bentham","def":"Modern European philosopher (1748–1832). The right act is the one that produces the greatest happiness.","more":"Bentham was an English legal reformer and the founder of modern utilitarianism. He wanted laws judged by their effects on human pleasure and pain, and campaigned for reforms of prisons, voting and law. He called natural rights 'nonsense upon stilts'.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["bentham"],"tags":["utilitarianism","greatest happiness","felicific calculus","panopticon","hedonic calculus"]},{"term":"G. W. F. Hegel","def":"Modern European philosopher (1770–1831). History is reason slowly coming to understand itself and become free.","more":"Hegel taught at Jena, Heidelberg and Berlin, and became the most influential German philosopher after Kant. His system tries to show how mind, society and history develop through conflicts that are overcome and preserved at a higher level.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["German Idealism"],"thinkers":["hegel"],"tags":["georg wilhelm friedrich hegel","dialectic","absolute spirit","geist","german idealism","phenomenology of spirit"]},{"term":"Arthur Schopenhauer","def":"Modern European philosopher (1788–1860). Beneath everything lies a blind, restless will, and life is mostly suffering.","more":"Schopenhauer lived much of his life as a private scholar in Frankfurt, largely ignored until his final years. He combined Kant's philosophy with ideas from Indian thought, and his pessimism deeply influenced Nietzsche, Wagner and Freud.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pessimism","Transcendental Idealism"],"thinkers":["schopenhauer"],"tags":["will","pessimism","world as will and representation","philosophical pessimism"]},{"term":"Søren Kierkegaard","def":"Modern European philosopher (1813–1855). Truth that matters must be lived and chosen, not just understood.","more":"Kierkegaard spent his short life in Copenhagen writing many books, often under invented pen names. He attacked both Hegel's grand system and the comfortable Christianity of the Danish church, and is often called the father of existentialism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Existentialism","Christian philosophy"],"thinkers":["kierkegaard"],"tags":["soren kierkegaard","existentialism","leap of faith","anxiety","angst","subjectivity"]},{"term":"John Stuart Mill","def":"Modern European philosopher (1806–1873). Seek the greatest happiness, and let people run their own lives.","more":"Mill was given an intense education by his father, James Mill, a close ally of Jeremy Bentham, and worked for the East India Company before serving briefly in Parliament. He refined utilitarianism, defended free speech and individual liberty, and argued for women's equality.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism","Liberalism","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["mill"],"tags":["j. s. mill","utilitarianism","on liberty","harm principle","liberalism"]},{"term":"Karl Marx","def":"Modern European philosopher (1818–1883). History is driven by class struggle over who controls production.","more":"Marx was born in Germany, worked as a radical journalist, and spent most of his later life in exile in London. With Friedrich Engels he developed a theory of history and a critique of capitalism that shaped revolutionary movements worldwide.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Marxism","Historical materialism"],"thinkers":["marx"],"tags":["marxism","communism","historical materialism","class struggle","das kapital","capital","communist manifesto"]},{"term":"Friedrich Nietzsche","def":"Modern European philosopher (1844–1900). God is dead; now we must create new values worth living for.","more":"Nietzsche became a professor of classical philology at Basel at 24, then left because of ill health and wrote his major works as a wanderer in Switzerland and Italy. He collapsed mentally in 1889 and never recovered.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Existentialism (precursor)","Perspectivism"],"thinkers":["nietzsche"],"tags":["god is dead","ubermensch","overman","will to power","eternal recurrence","perspectivism"]},{"term":"Charles Sanders Peirce","def":"American Pragmatism philosopher (1839–1914). An idea means the practical difference it would make.","more":"Peirce was a scientist, logician and founder of pragmatism who never held a lasting academic post and died in poverty. His work on logic, signs and scientific method was rediscovered long after his death.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pragmatism","Fallibilism"],"thinkers":["peirce"],"tags":["c. s. peirce","pragmatism","pragmaticism","pragmatic maxim","semiotics","abduction"]},{"term":"William James","def":"American Pragmatism philosopher (1842–1910). Judge ideas by what they do in the stream of real life.","more":"James trained in medicine, taught at Harvard, and was a founder of American psychology as well as of pragmatism. He wrote vividly about consciousness, religious experience and free will.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pragmatism","Radical empiricism"],"thinkers":["james"],"tags":["pragmatism","stream of consciousness","will to believe","radical empiricism","varieties of religious experience"]},{"term":"John Dewey","def":"American Pragmatism philosopher (1859–1952). Thinking is a tool for solving problems, and democracy is a way of life.","more":"Dewey taught at Chicago and Columbia and became America's best-known public intellectual of his time. He reshaped educational theory and argued that democracy needs active, inquiring citizens.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pragmatism","Instrumentalism"],"thinkers":["dewey"],"tags":["pragmatism","instrumentalism","democracy and education","progressive education"]},{"term":"Edmund Husserl","def":"Continental philosopher (1859–1938). Go back to the things themselves, exactly as they appear to us.","more":"Husserl, trained first in mathematics, founded phenomenology, the careful description of experience from the first-person point of view. He taught at Göttingen and Freiburg, where Heidegger was his assistant, and was later persecuted under Nazi laws because of his Jewish background.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Phenomenology"],"thinkers":["husserl"],"tags":["phenomenology","to the things themselves","epoche","intentionality","lifeworld"]},{"term":"Gottlob Frege","def":"Analytic philosopher (1848–1925). Invented modern logic and showed how words reach the world.","more":"Frege spent his career teaching mathematics at the University of Jena, little noticed in his lifetime. His new logic and his theory of meaning became the foundation of analytic philosophy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Logicism","Analytic philosophy"],"thinkers":["frege"],"tags":["sense and reference","sinn and bedeutung","predicate logic","logicism","analytic philosophy"]},{"term":"Bertrand Russell","def":"Analytic philosopher (1872–1970). Clear logic can untangle philosophy, and doubt is a virtue.","more":"Russell was a founder of analytic philosophy, a logician, a popular writer and a lifelong activist who was jailed for opposing the First World War. He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1950.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Logicism","Logical atomism","Analytic philosophy"],"thinkers":["russell"],"tags":["logical atomism","theory of descriptions","principia mathematica","analytic philosophy","logicism"]},{"term":"G. E. Moore","def":"Analytic philosopher (1873–1958). Goodness is real but simple, and common sense beats clever arguments.","more":"Moore taught at Cambridge and, with Russell, led the revolt against British idealism. He defended common sense against philosophical skepticism and argued that goodness cannot be defined in natural terms.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Ethical non-naturalism","Common sense philosophy"],"thinkers":["moore"],"tags":["g e moore","naturalistic fallacy","open question argument","common sense","here is a hand"]},{"term":"Ludwig Wittgenstein","def":"Analytic philosopher (1889–1951). Philosophy's problems come from language going on holiday.","more":"Born in Vienna to a very wealthy family, Wittgenstein studied with Russell at Cambridge and later taught there. He produced two very different philosophies: the early Tractatus about logic and the world, and the later Investigations about meaning as use.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Logical atomism","Ordinary language philosophy"],"thinkers":["wittgenstein"],"tags":["tractatus","philosophical investigations","language games","family resemblance","private language"]},{"term":"Martin Heidegger","def":"Continental philosopher (1889–1976). We have forgotten to ask what it means for anything to be.","more":"Heidegger studied under Husserl and taught at Marburg and Freiburg. His Being and Time reshaped continental philosophy, while his membership of the Nazi party and antisemitic notebooks remain a lasting stain on his reputation.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Phenomenology","Existentialism"],"thinkers":["heidegger"],"tags":["being and time","dasein","being","existentialism","phenomenology"]},{"term":"Nishida Kitarō","def":"Japanese philosopher (1870–1945). Before subject and object split, there is pure experience.","more":"Nishida taught at Kyoto Imperial University and founded the Kyoto School, the first major movement to bring Western philosophy into dialogue with Zen Buddhism. He was a lifelong practitioner of Zen meditation.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Kyoto School"],"thinkers":["nishida"],"tags":["nishida kitaro","kitaro nishida","kyoto school","pure experience","basho","nothingness"]},{"term":"W. E. B. Du Bois","def":"Africana philosopher (1868–1963). The problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the color line.","more":"Du Bois was the first African American to earn a PhD from Harvard, a sociologist, historian and co-founder of the NAACP. He spent his life fighting racial injustice and died in Ghana.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Africana philosophy","Pan-Africanism"],"thinkers":["du_bois"],"tags":["w e b du bois","double consciousness","color line","souls of black folk","race"]},{"term":"B. R. Ambedkar","def":"Indian philosopher (1891–1956). Caste must be destroyed, because it denies people equal dignity.","more":"Born into a caste treated as 'untouchable', Ambedkar earned doctorates from Columbia and the London School of Economics. He chaired the committee that drafted India's Constitution and, shortly before his death, led a mass conversion to Buddhism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Anti-caste philosophy","Navayana Buddhism"],"thinkers":["ambedkar"],"tags":["bhimrao ambedkar","babasaheb","annihilation of caste","anti-caste","dalit"]},{"term":"Jean-Paul Sartre","def":"Continental philosopher (1905–1980). We are condemned to be free and fully responsible for ourselves.","more":"Sartre was a Parisian philosopher, novelist and playwright who became the public face of existentialism after the Second World War. He refused the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1964 and later tried to combine existentialism with Marxism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Existentialism"],"thinkers":["sartre"],"tags":["existentialism","bad faith","existence precedes essence","being and nothingness","condemned to be free"]},{"term":"Simone de Beauvoir","def":"Continental philosopher (1908–1986). One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman.","more":"Beauvoir was a philosopher and novelist, Sartre's lifelong partner and an independent thinker in her own right. The Second Sex became a founding text of modern feminism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Existentialism","Feminism"],"thinkers":["beauvoir"],"tags":["second sex","existentialism","feminism","the other"]},{"term":"Albert Camus","def":"Continental philosopher (1913–1960). Life is absurd, yet we should live it fully and rebel.","more":"Camus grew up poor in French Algeria, worked as a journalist in the Resistance, and won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1957. He rejected the 'existentialist' label and broke publicly with Sartre over political violence.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Absurdism"],"thinkers":["camus"],"tags":["absurdism","the absurd","myth of sisyphus","the stranger","the rebel"]},{"term":"Frantz Fanon","def":"Africana philosopher (1925–1961). Colonialism wounds minds as well as bodies, and must be overthrown.","more":"Born in Martinique, Fanon trained as a psychiatrist in France and worked in Algeria, where he joined the independence struggle. He died of leukaemia at 36, the year his most famous book appeared.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Anti-colonialism","Existentialism","Africana philosophy"],"thinkers":["fanon"],"tags":["anticolonialism","decolonization","wretched of the earth","black skin white masks","postcolonialism"]},{"term":"A. J. Ayer","def":"Analytic philosopher (1910–1989). If a claim can't be tested and isn't true by definition, it's meaningless.","more":"Ayer brought the logical positivism of the Vienna Circle to Britain in a short, combative book written in his mid-twenties. He later taught at London and Oxford and became a well-known broadcaster.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Logical positivism","Emotivism"],"thinkers":["ayer"],"tags":["a j ayer","alfred ayer","logical positivism","verificationism","emotivism","language truth and logic"]},{"term":"Karl Popper","def":"Analytic philosopher (1902–1994). Science advances by bold guesses and honest attempts to refute them.","more":"Popper grew up in Vienna, fled the Nazis to New Zealand, and later taught at the London School of Economics. He was a major philosopher of science and a fierce critic of totalitarianism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Critical rationalism","Falsificationism","Liberalism"],"thinkers":["popper"],"tags":["falsificationism","falsifiability","critical rationalism","open society","demarcation problem"]},{"term":"Friedrich Hayek","def":"Austrian School philosopher (1899–1992). No planner can know enough; prices let society use scattered knowledge.","more":"Hayek was a Vienna-born economist who taught at the LSE, Chicago and Freiburg, and shared the 1974 Nobel Prize in Economics. He argued that central planning threatens both prosperity and freedom.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Classical liberalism","Austrian economics"],"thinkers":["hayek"],"tags":["f. a. hayek","austrian school","knowledge problem","road to serfdom","spontaneous order","classical liberalism"]},{"term":"W. V. O. Quine","def":"Analytic philosopher (1908–2000). Philosophy is continuous with science; no belief is immune to revision.","more":"Quine spent nearly his whole career at Harvard and was among the most influential analytic philosophers of the 20th century. He attacked key assumptions of logical positivism while keeping its scientific spirit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Naturalism","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["quine"],"tags":["willard van orman quine","naturalized epistemology","two dogmas of empiricism","holism","indeterminacy of translation"]},{"term":"Hannah Arendt","def":"Continental philosopher (1906–1975). Great evil can come from ordinary people who fail to think.","more":"Arendt studied with Heidegger and Jaspers in Germany, fled Nazi persecution, and became a leading political thinker in New York. Her report on the Eichmann trial made her famous and controversial.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Political theory","Phenomenology"],"thinkers":["arendt"],"tags":["banality of evil","eichmann in jerusalem","totalitarianism","human condition","vita activa"]},{"term":"G. E. M. Anscombe","def":"Analytic philosopher (1919–2001). Modern moral philosophy lost its way; go back to virtue and intention.","more":"Anscombe was Wittgenstein's student, translator and literary executor, and held his old chair at Cambridge. A committed Catholic, she publicly opposed Oxford's honorary degree for Truman over the atomic bombings.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Virtue ethics","Analytic Thomism"],"thinkers":["anscombe"],"tags":["elizabeth anscombe","modern moral philosophy","virtue ethics","intention","double effect"]},{"term":"Iris Murdoch","def":"Analytic philosopher (1919–1999). Being good starts with paying loving, honest attention to others.","more":"Murdoch was an Oxford philosopher and a celebrated novelist who wrote 26 novels. In philosophy she challenged the idea that morality is only about choices, stressing how we see the world.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral realism","Platonism"],"thinkers":["murdoch"],"tags":["attention","sovereignty of good","moral vision","unselfing"]},{"term":"Philippa Foot","def":"Analytic philosopher (1920–2010). Goodness in people is like health in plants: a natural kind of thriving.","more":"Foot taught at Oxford and UCLA and helped revive virtue ethics. She invented the original trolley problem in a 1967 paper on abortion and the doctrine of double effect.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Virtue ethics","Ethical naturalism"],"thinkers":["foot"],"tags":["trolley problem","natural goodness","virtue ethics","neo-aristotelianism"]},{"term":"John Rawls","def":"Analytic philosopher (1921–2002). Design society as if you didn't know where you'd end up in it.","more":"Rawls taught at Harvard for most of his career and revived political philosophy with A Theory of Justice. He was shaped by serving as an infantryman in the Pacific in the Second World War.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Liberalism","Contractualism"],"thinkers":["rawls"],"tags":["veil of ignorance","original position","justice as fairness","difference principle","theory of justice"]},{"term":"Robert Nozick","def":"Analytic philosopher (1938–2002). Individuals have rights, and only a minimal state respects them.","more":"Nozick was a Harvard philosopher whose first book became the classic defence of libertarianism, written partly in reply to his colleague Rawls. He later wrote on knowledge, personal identity and the meaning of life.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Libertarianism"],"thinkers":["nozick"],"tags":["minimal state","anarchy state and utopia","libertarianism","experience machine","entitlement theory"]},{"term":"Judith Jarvis Thomson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1929–2020). Clever thought experiments reveal what we really believe about rights.","more":"Thomson taught at MIT for decades and wrote some of the most famous thought experiments in ethics, including the violinist and the 'fat man' on the footbridge. In a 2008 paper she reversed her earlier view and argued that a bystander may not turn the trolley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic ethics"],"thinkers":["thomson"],"tags":["violinist","trolley problem","defense of abortion","thought experiments"]},{"term":"Bernard Williams","def":"Analytic philosopher (1929–2003). Ethics is messier than any theory, and that's not a flaw.","more":"Williams taught at Cambridge, Oxford and Berkeley and was a sharp critic of both utilitarianism and Kantian ethics. He argued that systematic moral theories distort the real texture of ethical life.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral anti-theory"],"thinkers":["williams"],"tags":["moral luck","integrity","ethics and the limits of philosophy","internal reasons"]},{"term":"Thomas Nagel","def":"Analytic philosopher (1937). Science can't capture what it's like to be you, from the inside.","more":"Nagel has taught mainly at Princeton and New York University. He is best known for asking what it is like to be a bat, and for arguing that the subjective point of view resists scientific explanation.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral realism","Anti-reductionism"],"thinkers":["nagel"],"tags":["what is it like to be a bat","view from nowhere","subjectivity","consciousness","moral luck"]},{"term":"Harry Frankfurt","def":"Analytic philosopher (1929–2023). You're free when you act on desires you truly want to have.","more":"Frankfurt taught at Yale and Princeton and reshaped the free will debate with two short papers. Late in life his essay On Bullshit became an unexpected bestseller.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["frankfurt"],"tags":["frankfurt cases","second-order desires","compatibilism","on bullshit","caring"]},{"term":"Peter Singer","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946). Everyone's suffering counts equally, animals' included.","more":"Singer is an Australian philosopher who taught at Monash and later at Princeton. His work helped launch the modern animal liberation movement and inspired effective altruism; he moved from preference utilitarianism to an objectivist hedonistic utilitarianism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism","Effective altruism"],"thinkers":["singer"],"tags":["animal liberation","effective altruism","speciesism","utilitarianism","drowning child"]},{"term":"Derek Parfit","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942–2017). Personal identity matters less than we think.","more":"Parfit spent his career at All Souls College, Oxford, publishing rarely but with huge impact. His work on personal identity, future generations and the objectivity of reasons shaped contemporary ethics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Reductionism about personal identity","Non-naturalist realism"],"thinkers":["parfit"],"tags":["reasons and persons","personal identity","repugnant conclusion","non-identity problem","on what matters"]},{"term":"J. L. Mackie","def":"Analytic philosopher (1917–1981). Objective values don't exist; morality is something we invent.","more":"Mackie was an Australian philosopher who spent his later career at University College, Oxford. He argued that moral claims aim at objective truth but always miss, and wrote a classic case against theism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Error theory","Atheism"],"thinkers":["mackie"],"tags":["error theory","queerness argument","moral skepticism","inventing right and wrong","problem of evil"]},{"term":"Saul Kripke","def":"Analytic philosopher (1940–2022). Names point straight at things, and some truths are necessary yet discovered.","more":"Kripke published important work in modal logic as a teenager and taught at Princeton and the CUNY Graduate Center. His lectures Naming and Necessity changed how philosophers think about language and necessity.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Modal logic","Direct reference"],"thinkers":["kripke"],"tags":["naming and necessity","rigid designation","causal theory of reference","necessary a posteriori","kripkenstein"]},{"term":"David Lewis","def":"Analytic philosopher (1941–2001). Every way the world could have been is a real world.","more":"Lewis taught at Princeton and spent much time in Australia. He built a systematic metaphysics, famously holding that all possible worlds are as real as ours, and made major contributions to causation, convention and the mind.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Modal realism","Humean supervenience"],"thinkers":["lewis"],"tags":["modal realism","possible worlds","counterfactuals","counterpart theory"]},{"term":"Hilary Putnam","def":"Analytic philosopher (1926–2016). 'Meanings just ain't in the head', and minds are what brains do.","more":"Putnam taught at Princeton, MIT and Harvard and was famous for changing his mind in public. He helped found functionalism in the philosophy of mind and later became one of its leading critics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Functionalism","Semantic externalism","Pragmatism"],"thinkers":["putnam"],"tags":["twin earth","semantic externalism","functionalism","brain in a vat","multiple realizability"]},{"term":"John Searle","def":"Analytic philosopher (1932–2025). Running a program isn't understanding; brains make minds biologically.","more":"Searle taught at the University of California, Berkeley for over 50 years. He developed speech act theory and the Chinese Room argument against strong artificial intelligence.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Speech act theory","Biological naturalism"],"thinkers":["searle"],"tags":["chinese room","speech acts","biological naturalism","intentionality","strong ai"]},{"term":"Richard Rorty","def":"American Pragmatism philosopher (1931–2007). Stop trying to mirror reality; aim for useful conversation instead.","more":"Rorty taught at Princeton, Virginia and Stanford, and moved from analytic philosophy to a pragmatism inspired by Dewey. He argued that philosophy should give up its claim to be the judge of all knowledge.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Neopragmatism"],"thinkers":["rorty"],"tags":["neopragmatism","pragmatism","mirror of nature","anti-representationalism","irony"]},{"term":"Daniel Dennett","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942–2024). Consciousness is the brain's busy work, not an inner theatre.","more":"Dennett taught at Tufts University for decades and wrote widely read books on the mind, free will, evolution and religion. He argued that consciousness and freedom are natural, evolved phenomena.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Physicalism","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["dennett"],"tags":["multiple drafts","heterophenomenology","intentional stance","consciousness explained","illusionism"]},{"term":"Frank Jackson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1943). Created the Mary argument, then changed his mind about it.","more":"Jackson is an Australian philosopher based mainly at the Australian National University. His 1982 'Mary's room' argument became a classic case against physicalism, but he later became a physicalist and argued the argument fails.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Physicalism","Analytic functionalism"],"thinkers":["jackson"],"tags":["knowledge argument","mary's room","qualia","physicalism"]},{"term":"David Chalmers","def":"Analytic philosopher (1966). Why does any of the brain's activity feel like something?","more":"Chalmers is an Australian philosopher at New York University who named 'the hard problem of consciousness'. He defends naturalistic dualism and has written on virtual worlds, arguing they can be fully real.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Naturalistic dualism"],"thinkers":["chalmers"],"tags":["hard problem of consciousness","philosophical zombies","zombies","property dualism","extended mind"]},{"term":"Peter van Inwagen","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942). Free will conflicts with determinism, yet we really do have it.","more":"Van Inwagen taught at Syracuse and Notre Dame and is a leading metaphysician and philosopher of religion. His 'consequence argument' is the standard case that free will and determinism cannot both be true.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Libertarian free will","Christian philosophy"],"thinkers":["vaninwagen"],"tags":["consequence argument","incompatibilism","libertarian free will","mereology"]},{"term":"Galen Strawson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1952). No one can make themselves, so no one is ultimately responsible.","more":"Galen Strawson, son of the philosopher P. F. Strawson, has taught at Oxford, Reading and the University of Texas at Austin. He is known for the 'Basic Argument' against ultimate moral responsibility and for defending panpsychism as the realistic form of physicalism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Hard incompatibilism","Panpsychism"],"thinkers":["gstrawson"],"tags":["basic argument","free will skepticism","ultimate responsibility","panpsychism"]},{"term":"Derk Pereboom","def":"Analytic philosopher (1957). Life without free will can still be good and meaningful.","more":"Pereboom, born in the Netherlands, teaches at Cornell University. He defends hard incompatibilism and argues that giving up desert-based blame would make life better, not worse.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Hard incompatibilism"],"thinkers":["pereboom"],"tags":["hard incompatibilism","free will skepticism","four-case argument","manipulation argument"]},{"term":"Christine Korsgaard","def":"Analytic philosopher (1952). Morality comes from what we must value just to be agents.","more":"Korsgaard is a Kantian philosopher at Harvard who studied under Rawls. She argues that moral obligation is built from the structure of rational agency rather than discovered as an independent fact, and has written on our duties to animals.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Kantian constructivism"],"thinkers":["korsgaard"],"tags":["constructivism","kantian ethics","sources of normativity","self-constitution"]},{"term":"Martha Nussbaum","def":"Analytic philosopher (1947). Justice means giving everyone the real ability to live a full human life.","more":"Nussbaum teaches law and philosophy at the University of Chicago. Drawing on Aristotle and the Stoics, she developed the capabilities approach to justice and writes about emotions, literature and vulnerability.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Capabilities approach","Neo-Aristotelianism"],"thinkers":["nussbaum"],"tags":["capabilities approach","capability approach","fragility of goodness","emotions"]},{"term":"Sharon Street","def":"Analytic philosopher (1973). Evolution shaped our values, so they can't be tracking independent moral facts.","more":"Street is a philosopher at New York University who studied with Christine Korsgaard. Her 'Darwinian Dilemma' challenges moral realism, and she defends a Humean constructivism in which values come from the standpoint of valuing creatures.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Humean constructivism"],"thinkers":["street"],"tags":["darwinian dilemma","evolutionary debunking","constructivism","anti-realism"]},{"term":"Philip Goff","def":"Analytic philosopher (1978). Consciousness may be a basic feature of all matter.","more":"Goff is a British philosopher at Durham University and a leading public defender of panpsychism. He argues that physics tells us what matter does but not what it is, leaving room for consciousness at the bottom.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Panpsychism"],"thinkers":["goff"],"tags":["panpsychism","galileo's error","consciousness","russellian monism"]},{"term":"W. D. Ross","def":"Analytic philosopher (1877–1971). We have several basic duties, and good judgement must weigh them case by case.","more":"A Scottish philosopher who spent his career at Oxford and also translated Aristotle. In The Right and the Good (1930) he argued that there is no single master rule of morality. Instead we have several basic duties, such as keeping promises and not harming others, that we know by intuition.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Ethical intuitionism","Moral pluralism"],"thinkers":["ross"],"tags":["prima facie duties","pluralism","deontology","intuitionism","the right and the good"]},{"term":"T. M. Scanlon","def":"Analytic philosopher (1940). An act is wrong if it breaks rules that no one could reasonably reject.","more":"An American moral philosopher who taught at Princeton and Harvard. In What We Owe to Each Other (1998) he argued that morality is about being able to justify ourselves to each other. He also defends the view that there are real truths about reasons for action.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Contractualism"],"thinkers":["scanlon"],"tags":["contractualism","what we owe to each other","reasonable rejection"]},{"term":"Ayn Rand","def":"Modern European philosopher (1905–1982). Each person should live for their own rational self-interest, and a free society should protect that.","more":"A Russian-born American novelist and philosopher, author of The Fountainhead and Atlas Shrugged. She founded Objectivism, which praises reason, selfishness rightly understood, and laissez-faire capitalism. She worked outside academic philosophy, and most academic philosophers have not taken her up.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Objectivism","Ethical egoism"],"thinkers":["rand"],"tags":["objectivism","ethical egoism","virtue of selfishness","atlas shrugged","laissez-faire capitalism"]},{"term":"Robert Merrihew Adams","def":"Analytic philosopher (1937–2024). Moral obligation comes from the commands of a loving God, and all goodness is a likeness of God.","more":"An American philosopher who taught at UCLA, Yale and Oxford, known for work on ethics, the philosophy of religion and Leibniz. He defended a modified divine command theory: wrongness is being contrary to the commands of a loving God. In Finite and Infinite Goods (1999) he argued that God is the Good itself and other things are good by resembling God.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Divine command theory","Theistic Platonism"],"thinkers":["adams_rm"],"tags":["divine command theory","modified divine command theory","finite and infinite goods","theistic ethics"]},{"term":"F. M. Kamm","def":"Analytic philosopher. The way harm is caused matters: you may not make one person's harm the tool for saving others.","more":"An American moral philosopher, at NYU, Harvard and Rutgers, famous for very careful thought experiments. She builds detailed variations on cases like the trolley problem to find the principles behind our moral judgements. Her Principle of Permissible Harm says harm is allowed when it comes from the good outcome, not when it is the means to it.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Non-consequentialism"],"thinkers":["kamm"],"tags":["frances kamm","principle of permissible harm","trolley problem","deontology","doing and allowing"]},{"term":"Warren Quinn","def":"Analytic philosopher. It is worse to harm someone by using them for your goal than to harm them as a side effect.","more":"An American moral philosopher at UCLA whose essays were collected after his death in Morality and Action (1993). He gave influential defences of the doctrine of double effect and the doing/allowing distinction. He argued these distinctions express respect for each person's authority over their own body and life.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Non-consequentialism","Doctrine of double effect"],"thinkers":["quinn_warren"],"tags":["doctrine of double effect","using someone","harmful agency","deontology"]},{"term":"Gilbert Harman","def":"Analytic philosopher (1938–2021). There is no single true morality; moral rules come from agreements within groups.","more":"An American philosopher at Princeton who worked on ethics, the mind, knowledge and reasoning. He argued that we never need moral facts to explain what we observe, which casts doubt on objective morality. He defended moral relativism: moral judgements only make sense relative to a moral framework or implicit agreement.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral relativism","Functionalism"],"thinkers":["harman"],"tags":["moral relativism","relativism","moral explanations","argument from explanation"]},{"term":"David B. Wong","def":"Analytic philosopher. More than one morality can be correct, but not every morality is.","more":"An American philosopher at Duke who works on ethics and on Chinese philosophy, especially Confucianism and Zhuangzi. He defends pluralistic relativism: there is no single true morality, but human nature and the job morality does rule out many moralities as inadequate.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pluralistic relativism","Comparative philosophy"],"thinkers":["wong"],"tags":["pluralistic relativism","moral relativism","natural moralities","confucian ethics"]},{"term":"Richard Joyce","def":"Analytic philosopher (1966). Moral claims are all false, but we might still find it useful to keep talking as if they were true.","more":"A philosopher born in England and raised in New Zealand, now teaching at Victoria University of Wellington. Following J. L. Mackie, he argues that moral judgements try to state facts but there are no moral facts to state. He also suggests that moral thinking evolved to help humans cooperate, which gives no reason to think it is accurate.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral error theory","Moral fictionalism"],"thinkers":["joyce"],"tags":["error theory","moral fictionalism","myth of morality","evolution of morality"]},{"term":"Simon Blackburn","def":"Analytic philosopher (1944). Moral talk expresses our attitudes, yet we can still speak of moral truth.","more":"An English philosopher who taught at Oxford, North Carolina and Cambridge, and wrote popular books such as Think. He develops a Humean view on which moral claims express attitudes rather than describe facts. His 'quasi-realism' tries to explain why, on that view, we can still talk about moral truth and moral mistakes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Quasi-realism","Expressivism"],"thinkers":["blackburn"],"tags":["quasi-realism","expressivism","projectivism","ruling passions"]},{"term":"Allan Gibbard","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942). Calling something rational or wrong expresses our acceptance of norms, not a fact about the world.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Michigan who also proved a key theorem about voting systems. In Wise Choices, Apt Feelings (1990) he argued that calling something rational means accepting norms that permit it. Morality, on his view, concerns when guilt and resentment are fitting.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Expressivism","Norm-expressivism"],"thinkers":["gibbard"],"tags":["norm-expressivism","expressivism","wise choices apt feelings","metaethics"]},{"term":"H. L. A. Hart","def":"Analytic philosopher (1907–1992). Law is a system of social rules, separate from morality, though morality can criticize it.","more":"An English legal philosopher at Oxford, and the most influential philosopher of law of the 20th century. In The Concept of Law (1961) he explained law as a system of rules accepted by officials. He also argued that the law should not punish private immorality that harms no one, and that punishment needs both good effects and fair limits.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Legal positivism"],"thinkers":["hart"],"tags":["legal positivism","concept of law","rule of recognition","hart-devlin debate"]},{"term":"Angela Y. Davis","def":"Africana philosopher (1944). Prisons do not solve social problems, so we should build a world that no longer needs them.","more":"An American philosopher and activist, a student of Herbert Marcuse, who became world famous when she was jailed and then acquitted in 1972. She writes on how race, gender and class oppression work together. She is a leading voice for abolishing prisons and replacing them with schools, health care and community repair.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Black feminism","Marxism","Prison abolitionism"],"thinkers":["davis_angela"],"tags":["angela davis","prison abolition","abolition","black feminism","are prisons obsolete"]},{"term":"Susan Wolf","def":"Analytic philosopher (1952). A meaningful life comes when you love things that are actually worth loving.","more":"An American moral philosopher at the University of North Carolina. She is known for 'Moral Saints' (1982), which argued that being morally perfect is not an ideal life. She later argued that meaning in life is neither purely about feeling fulfilled nor purely about objective value, but both together.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Meaning in life","Reason view of responsibility"],"thinkers":["wolf"],"tags":["meaning in life","fitting fulfilment","sanity theory","moral saints"]},{"term":"Fred Feldman","def":"Analytic philosopher (1941). Death is bad because it takes away the good life you would otherwise have had.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Massachusetts Amherst who works on ethics, the value of life and death, and happiness. In Confrontations with the Reaper (1992) he defended the deprivation view of death against Epicurus. He also defends a form of hedonism and a form of act utilitarianism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism","Hedonism"],"thinkers":["feldman"],"tags":["deprivation account","badness of death","hedonism","welfare"]},{"term":"Jeff McMahan","def":"Analytic philosopher (1954). Soldiers who fight for an unjust cause are not morally equal to those who fight for a just one.","more":"An American moral philosopher, trained at Oxford under Derek Parfit and later Professor at Oxford. In The Ethics of Killing (2002) he argued that how bad death is depends on how closely you are psychologically linked to your future self. In Killing in War (2009) he challenged the traditional idea that soldiers on both sides fight equally legitimately.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Just war revisionism"],"thinkers":["mcmahan"],"tags":["just war theory","killing in war","moral equality of combatants","ethics of killing","time-relative interests"]},{"term":"Protagoras","def":"Greek philosopher (490 BCE–420 BCE). Man is the measure of all things.","more":"The most famous of the Greek Sophists, travelling teachers who taught public speaking and citizenship for a fee. His books are lost; we know him mostly through Plato's dialogues Protagoras and Theaetetus. He said human beings are the measure of all things and that he could not know whether the gods exist.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Sophists"],"thinkers":["protagoras"],"tags":["protagoras of abdera","relativism","sophists","sophistry","man is the measure of all things"]},{"term":"David Benatar","def":"Analytic philosopher (1966). Coming into existence is always a harm, so it is better never to have been born.","more":"A South African philosopher at the University of Cape Town. In Better Never to Have Been (2006) he argued that being born is always a harm, because the absence of pain is good but the absence of pleasure is not bad. He also argues that death is bad, so we are stuck in a 'human predicament': life is worse than we think, and so is ending it.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Antinatalism","Philosophical pessimism"],"thinkers":["benatar"],"tags":["antinatalism","better never to have been","asymmetry argument","procreation"]},{"term":"Roger Scruton","def":"Analytic philosopher (1944–2020). We inherit our home, customs and institutions, and should conserve them with care.","more":"An English philosopher and writer, known for books on aesthetics, music, architecture and conservative political thought. He argued that institutions and traditions carry wisdom built up over generations. He also defended beauty as a real value that modern culture neglects.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Conservatism","Aesthetics"],"thinkers":["scruton"],"tags":["conservatism","oikophilia","beauty","tradition"]},{"term":"Paul Churchland","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942). Everyday talk of beliefs and desires is a rough old theory that brain science will replace.","more":"A Canadian philosopher who spent most of his career at the University of California, San Diego. He argues that folk psychology, our everyday way of explaining people by their beliefs and desires, is a scientific theory that may turn out to be false. He expects neuroscience to give us a better picture of the mind, built on patterns of activity in networks of neurons.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Eliminative materialism","Neurophilosophy","Scientific realism"],"thinkers":["churchland"],"tags":["eliminative materialism","eliminativism","folk psychology","neurophilosophy"]},{"term":"Richard Swinburne","def":"Analytic philosopher (1934). God's existence and the soul can be argued for with the same probability reasoning scientists use.","more":"An Oxford philosopher of religion. He uses probability theory to argue that the existence of God is the best explanation of the universe and its order. He also defends the view that each of us is a soul joined to a body, and that we have free will in a strong sense.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion","Substance dualism","Natural theology"],"thinkers":["swinburne"],"tags":["existence of god","cumulative case","bayesian argument","natural theology","soul"]},{"term":"Thomas Metzinger","def":"Analytic philosopher (1958). Nobody ever was or had a self; the brain builds a model of one and cannot see that it is a model.","more":"A German philosopher of mind at the University of Mainz who works closely with brain scientists. In Being No One he argues that there are no selves in the world, only brains that build a self-model they mistake for a real self. He has also written about the ethics of consciousness research and artificial intelligence.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of mind","Self-model theory","Naturalism"],"thinkers":["metzinger"],"tags":["no-self","being no one","self-model theory","ego tunnel","phenomenal self-model"]},{"term":"Sydney Shoemaker","def":"Analytic philosopher (1931–2022). You are carried by your psychological life, and a property is whatever powers it gives to things.","more":"An American philosopher who taught at Cornell for decades. He defended a modern version of Locke's idea that personal identity is a matter of psychological continuity, and he helped develop functionalism about the mind. He also argued that properties are best understood as bundles of causal powers.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Functionalism","Psychological continuity theory"],"thinkers":["shoemaker"],"tags":["psychological continuity","personal identity","brain transplant","functionalism"]},{"term":"John Worrall","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946). When scientific theories change, the mathematical structure survives; that structure is what we should believe in.","more":"A British philosopher of science at the London School of Economics. He revived structural realism, a middle position between believing everything science says and believing none of it. He has also written on evidence in medicine and on the history of optics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of science","Structural realism"],"thinkers":["worrall"],"tags":["structural realism","scientific realism","pessimistic induction","best of both worlds"]},{"term":"Bas van Fraassen","def":"Analytic philosopher (1941). Science aims to save the observable phenomena, so we can use a theory without believing what it says about the unseen.","more":"A Dutch-born philosopher who taught at Princeton and San Francisco State. In The Scientific Image he introduced constructive empiricism, the view that a good theory only needs to be right about what can be observed. He also criticized speculative metaphysics and wrote on logic, probability and the philosophy of physics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Constructive empiricism","Empiricism","Philosophy of science"],"thinkers":["van_fraassen"],"tags":["constructive empiricism","anti-realism","empirical adequacy","scientific image"]},{"term":"Kurt Gödel","def":"Analytic philosopher (1906–1978). Mathematical truth outruns any set of rules for proving it, so mathematics is discovered, not made.","more":"An Austrian logician who later worked at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton. His incompleteness theorems of 1931 showed that any consistent formal system rich enough for arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove. He believed mathematical objects exist independently of us, and he also used Einstein's relativity to argue that time is not objectively real.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Mathematical Platonism","Rationalism","Logic"],"thinkers":["godel"],"tags":["godel","goedel","incompleteness theorems","incompleteness","mathematical platonism","logic"]},{"term":"David Hilbert","def":"Analytic philosopher (1862–1943). Secure mathematics by treating its proofs as finite strings of symbols and proving the system can never contradict itself.","more":"A German mathematician at Göttingen, one of the most influential of his age. He put geometry on a strict axiomatic footing and proposed a programme to prove that mathematics is free of contradictions using only simple, finite reasoning. Gödel's incompleteness theorems later showed that this programme could not succeed in its original form.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Formalism","Foundations of mathematics"],"thinkers":["hilbert"],"tags":["formalism","hilbert's program","foundations of mathematics","finitism"]},{"term":"Hartry Field","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946). Physics can be done without numbers, so we need not believe numbers exist.","more":"An American philosopher at New York University who works on mathematics, logic and truth. In Science Without Numbers he tried to show that physics can be stated without mentioning numbers, undercutting the main argument for believing in them. He treats mathematics as a useful fiction and has also worked on paradoxes of truth.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Mathematical fictionalism","Nominalism","Deflationism about truth"],"thinkers":["field"],"tags":["nominalism","science without numbers","mathematical fictionalism","fictionalism"]},{"term":"Stewart Shapiro","def":"Analytic philosopher (1951). Mathematics is the science of structure: a number is just a position in a pattern.","more":"An American philosopher of mathematics and logic at Ohio State University. He developed ante rem structuralism, the view that mathematics studies abstract structures and that numbers are simply places within them. He has also defended second-order logic and written on vagueness and on the idea that there may be many correct logics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Mathematical structuralism","Philosophy of logic"],"thinkers":["shapiro"],"tags":["structuralism","mathematical structuralism","ante rem structuralism","philosophy of mathematics"]},{"term":"Laurence BonJour","def":"Analytic philosopher (1943). Pure reason can see why some claims must be true, and without that we could not justify anything at all.","more":"An American epistemologist at the University of Washington. He first defended a coherence theory of justification and later switched to a form of foundationalism. In In Defense of Pure Reason he argued, against Quine and others, that we have genuine a priori knowledge through rational insight.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Rationalism","Foundationalism","Epistemic internalism"],"thinkers":["bonjour"],"tags":["a priori","rationalism","coherentism","in defense of pure reason","foundationalism"]},{"term":"Rudolf Carnap","def":"Analytic philosopher (1891–1970). Many philosophical disputes vanish once we see them as choices between languages, to be judged by usefulness.","more":"A German philosopher and leading member of the Vienna Circle who moved to the United States in 1935. He tried to rebuild knowledge from experience using modern logic, and argued that metaphysical claims that no experience could test are meaningless. He later held that questions such as whether numbers exist are really practical choices about which framework of language to adopt.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Logical positivism","Logical empiricism","Vienna Circle"],"thinkers":["carnap"],"tags":["logical positivism","vienna circle","linguistic frameworks","logical empiricism","internal and external questions"]},{"term":"Timothy Williamson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1955). Vague words have sharp boundaries we cannot know, and knowledge itself comes first.","more":"A British philosopher, born in Sweden, who was Wykeham Professor of Logic at Oxford. He is best known for epistemicism, the view that vague words like heap have precise cut-off points that we cannot discover. He also argued that knowledge cannot be broken down into simpler parts and is the basic concept of epistemology.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Epistemicism","Knowledge-first epistemology","Necessitism"],"thinkers":["williamson"],"tags":["epistemicism","knowledge first","vagueness","knowledge and its limits","modal logic"]},{"term":"Kit Fine","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946). Necessity flows from essence, and a vague word is one whose meaning is not yet fully settled.","more":"A British philosopher and logician who taught at Edinburgh, Michigan and UCLA before moving to New York University in 1997. His 1975 paper on vagueness became the classic statement of supervaluationism, though he later replaced it with a 'global' approach. He also argued that essence is more basic than necessity and developed new views on grounding, time and the first person.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Supervaluationism","Metaphysics of essence","Logic"],"thinkers":["kit_fine"],"tags":["essence","essentialism","supervaluationism","grounding","vagueness"]},{"term":"Dorothy Edgington","def":"Analytic philosopher (1941). Truth for vague words comes in degrees that behave like probabilities.","more":"A British philosopher of logic who taught at Birkbeck and Oxford. She is best known for her work on conditionals, arguing that if-then statements express conditional degrees of belief rather than simple truths. She extended a similar idea to vagueness, treating closeness to truth as a matter of degree.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Degree theory of vagueness","Philosophy of logic"],"thinkers":["edgington"],"tags":["degrees of truth","vagueness","conditionals","probability"]},{"term":"Theodore Sider","def":"Analytic philosopher (1967). Objects stretch out through time as well as space, and good metaphysics carves the world at its joints.","more":"An American metaphysician who has taught at Rutgers, NYU and Cornell. In Four-Dimensionalism he argued that past, present and future things are equally real and that objects persist by having temporal parts. In Writing the Book of the World he defended the idea that some ways of describing reality fit its real structure better than others.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Four-dimensionalism","Metaphysical realism"],"thinkers":["sider"],"tags":["four-dimensionalism","perdurantism","temporal parts","worm theory","writing the book of the world"]},{"term":"Eli Hirsch","def":"Analytic philosopher (1938). Many debates about what exists are just people using words differently, and ordinary language should settle them.","more":"An American philosopher at Brandeis University who works on identity and the nature of metaphysical debate. He argues that disputes such as whether there are tables or only particles arranged table-wise are often merely verbal. Where a dispute is verbal, he holds that the side matching ordinary language is right.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Quantifier variance","Common-sense ontology"],"thinkers":["hirsch"],"tags":["quantifier variance","verbal disputes","deflationism","metaontology","ontology"]},{"term":"Arthur Prior","def":"Analytic philosopher (1914–1969). Only the present is real, and tense is built into the logic of reality.","more":"A New Zealand philosopher who taught in Christchurch, Manchester and Oxford. He invented tense logic, a way of reasoning formally about past, present and future. He used it to defend the view that only the present exists and that the future is genuinely open.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Tense logic","Presentism"],"thinkers":["prior"],"tags":["presentism","tense logic","temporal logic","a-theory","thank goodness that's over"]},{"term":"J. M. E. McTaggart","def":"Modern European philosopher (1866–1925). Time contains a contradiction, so it cannot be real; reality is a community of eternal souls.","more":"A Cambridge philosopher and leading British idealist. His paper The Unreality of Time argued that time requires events to be past, present and future, which leads to contradiction, so time is unreal. He held that reality consists only of minds related by love, rejected belief in God, and believed in personal immortality.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["British idealism","Hegelianism"],"thinkers":["mctaggart"],"tags":["unreality of time","a-series","b-series","idealism","british idealism"]},{"term":"Michael Tooley","def":"Analytic philosopher (1941). The past and present are real and the future is not yet; causation is a real relation, not just a pattern.","more":"A Canadian-born philosopher who taught at the University of Colorado. He defended a growing block theory of time, in which reality grows as new events come into existence. He is also known for realist accounts of causation and laws, for a controversial defence of abortion and infanticide, and for arguments from evil against God.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Growing block theory","Realism about causation","Atheism"],"thinkers":["tooley"],"tags":["growing block","time","causation","laws of nature"]},{"term":"Stephen Mumford","def":"Analytic philosopher (1965). Causes are powers at work in the world, not mere regular sequences.","more":"An English metaphysician at Durham University. He argues that dispositions and powers, like the fragility of glass or the solubility of salt, are real features of things. With Rani Lill Anjum he developed a theory in which causation is the exercise of such powers. He has also written on the philosophy of sport.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Dispositionalism","Powers metaphysics"],"thinkers":["mumford"],"tags":["dispositionalism","powers","causal powers","dispositions","causation"]},{"term":"James Woodward","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946). X causes Y when wiggling X would wiggle Y.","more":"An American philosopher of science who taught at Caltech and Pittsburgh. In Making Things Happen he developed the interventionist account of causation, which ties causal claims to what would happen if we intervened on something. The theory is widely used in the sciences, especially biology, psychology and the social sciences.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Interventionism","Philosophy of science"],"thinkers":["woodward"],"tags":["interventionism","interventionist theory of causation","manipulability","making things happen"]},{"term":"D. M. Armstrong","def":"Analytic philosopher (1926–2014). The mind is the brain, and the world is made of things having real properties that science discovers.","more":"An Australian philosopher at the University of Sydney and a founder of Australian materialism. He argued that mental states are brain states identified by the behaviour they cause. In metaphysics he defended real universals, discovered by science, and the view that laws of nature are relations between them.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Australian materialism","Realism about universals","Naturalism"],"thinkers":["armstrong"],"tags":["david armstrong","identity theory","universals","laws of nature","truthmakers","physicalism"]},{"term":"C. D. Broad","def":"Analytic philosopher (1887–1971). The past is real and the present is its growing edge; the future is simply nothing yet.","more":"An English philosopher at Cambridge who wrote on almost every area of philosophy. In Scientific Thought he proposed the growing block theory of time. He also developed an influential account of emergence in The Mind and Its Place in Nature and surveyed moral theories in Five Types of Ethical Theory.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Emergentism","Growing block theory","British realism"],"thinkers":["broad"],"tags":["growing block","emergentism","time","mind and its place in nature"]},{"term":"Voltaire","def":"Modern European philosopher (1694–1778). Use reason and ridicule to fight fanaticism, and believe in a Creator without the superstition.","more":"A French writer and philosopher who became the most famous voice of the Enlightenment. He spread the ideas of Locke and Newton in France, mocked religious intolerance, and campaigned for victims of unjust trials. His satire Candide (1759) makes fun of the idea that this is the best of all possible worlds.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Enlightenment","Deism"],"thinkers":["voltaire"],"tags":["francois-marie arouet","candide","enlightenment","ecrasez l'infame","religious tolerance","satire"]},{"term":"Thomas Paine","def":"Modern European philosopher (1737–1809). Every generation has the right to govern itself; no king or ancient charter can bind it.","more":"An English-born writer whose pamphlet Common Sense (1776) helped persuade Americans to declare independence. In Rights of Man (1791) he defended the French Revolution against Edmund Burke. In The Age of Reason he argued for belief in God based on nature and reason rather than on the Bible.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Liberalism","Republicanism","Deism"],"thinkers":["paine"],"tags":["common sense","rights of man","age of reason","deism","american revolution"]},{"term":"Thomas Henry Huxley","def":"Modern European philosopher (1825–1895). Believe only what the evidence supports, and admit it when the evidence runs out.","more":"An English biologist known as \"Darwin's Bulldog\" for defending the theory of evolution in public debates. He invented the word \"agnostic\" for his view that we should not claim to know what the evidence cannot show. He also argued that humans and animals are \"conscious automata\" whose bodies are run by physical processes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Agnosticism","Naturalism"],"thinkers":["huxley"],"tags":["darwin's bulldog","agnosticism","agnostic","evolution"]},{"term":"David Estlund","def":"Analytic philosopher. Democracy earns its authority by being a reasonably reliable way of deciding that no reasonable person can reject.","more":"An American political philosopher at Brown University. In Democratic Authority (2008) he defended \"epistemic proceduralism\": democratic decisions have authority because democracy tends to get things right better than chance, and it does so in a way all reasonable citizens can accept. He has also argued that political philosophy may describe ideals even if people are unlikely to live up to them.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Epistemic democracy","Political liberalism"],"thinkers":["estlund"],"tags":["epistemic proceduralism","democratic authority","epistemic democracy"]},{"term":"Thomas Christiano","def":"Analytic philosopher. Democracy matters because it is the way a society publicly treats its members as equals.","more":"An American political philosopher at the University of Arizona. In The Constitution of Equality (2008) he argued that justice requires \"public equality\": treating people as equals in a way everyone can see. Democracy, he argued, is the main way to achieve this, which gives it real authority, though limited by basic rights.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Democratic theory","Egalitarianism"],"thinkers":["christiano"],"tags":["democracy","public equality","constitution of equality","political equality"]},{"term":"Hélène Landemore","def":"Analytic philosopher (1976). Many ordinary people deciding together are smarter than a few experts.","more":"A French political theorist who teaches at Yale. In Democratic Reason (2013) she argued that democracy is valuable because large, diverse groups make better decisions than small groups of experts, thanks to the variety of their viewpoints. In Open Democracy (2020) she proposed giving more power to randomly selected citizens' assemblies.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Epistemic democracy"],"thinkers":["landemore"],"tags":["epistemic democracy","democratic reason","wisdom of crowds","open democracy"]},{"term":"Jason Brennan","def":"Analytic philosopher (1979). If most voters are uninformed, democracy has no special claim; we should use whatever system governs best.","more":"An American philosopher at Georgetown University. In Against Democracy (2016) he argued that many voters are ignorant or biased, that democracy has no value in itself, and that \"epistocracy\", giving more political power to the better informed, might produce better results. He also argues that people who are poorly informed have a duty not to vote.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Libertarianism","Epistocracy"],"thinkers":["brennan"],"tags":["epistocracy","against democracy","voter ignorance","ethics of voting"]},{"term":"Daniel A. Bell","def":"Analytic philosopher (1964). A government can be legitimate if it picks capable leaders and delivers good results, even without national elections.","more":"A Canadian political theorist who has taught for many years in China. In The China Model (2015) he argued that political meritocracy, choosing leaders by ability and track record, is a serious alternative to electoral democracy at the top level of government. He draws on Confucian ideas about rule by the virtuous and capable.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Confucianism","Communitarianism","Political meritocracy"],"thinkers":["daniel_bell"],"tags":["china model","political meritocracy","confucianism","meritocracy"]},{"term":"Jeremy Waldron","def":"Analytic philosopher (1953). When citizens disagree about rights, they deserve an equal say in settling the matter, and an equal standing in society.","more":"A New Zealand-born legal and political philosopher who has taught at Oxford and New York University. In Law and Disagreement (1999) he argued that elected legislatures, not courts, should settle disagreements about rights, because majority voting respects everyone as an equal. In The Harm in Hate Speech (2012) he defended laws against hate speech as protecting the dignity of vulnerable groups.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Legal philosophy","Democratic theory"],"thinkers":["waldron"],"tags":["law and disagreement","majoritarianism","against judicial review","rights"]},{"term":"Joel Feinberg","def":"Analytic philosopher (1926–2004). The law may stop you from harming or seriously offending others, but not from harming yourself.","more":"An American philosopher of law who taught at the University of Arizona. His four-volume work The Moral Limits of the Criminal Law (1984–1988) asked exactly when the state may make something a crime. He defended a liberal answer: preventing harm to others, and some serious public offense, but not protecting people from their own free choices or enforcing morality for its own sake.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Liberalism","Philosophy of law"],"thinkers":["feinberg"],"tags":["offense principle","harm principle","moral limits of criminal law","liberalism"]},{"term":"Cass Sunstein","def":"Analytic philosopher (1954). Design choices so people are gently steered toward what's good for them, while staying free to choose otherwise.","more":"An American legal scholar at Harvard and former head of the White House regulatory office. With the economist Richard Thaler he wrote Nudge (2008), which argued that since someone must design how choices are presented, governments should design them to help people, for example with good default options. He calls this \"libertarian paternalism\".","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Libertarian paternalism","Behavioral law and economics"],"thinkers":["sunstein"],"tags":["nudge","libertarian paternalism","choice architecture","behavioral economics"]},{"term":"Sarah Conly","def":"Analytic philosopher. Since people predictably harm themselves, the state should sometimes simply stop them.","more":"An American philosopher at Bowdoin College. In Against Autonomy (2013) she argued that because people are often irrational about their own long-term good, laws that force them to act in their own interest, such as banning cigarettes, can be justified. She argues that nudges are not enough when the stakes are high.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Coercive paternalism"],"thinkers":["conly"],"tags":["coercive paternalism","paternalism","against autonomy","behavioral economics"]},{"term":"Edmund Burke","def":"Modern European philosopher (1729–1797). Society is a partnership between the dead, the living and the unborn, so reform it with care, not by tearing it down.","more":"An Irish-born statesman and writer who sat in the British Parliament. In Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790) he warned that the revolutionaries' plan to rebuild society from abstract theory would end in violence, which made him the founder of modern conservatism. Earlier he wrote an influential book on the beautiful and the sublime.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Conservatism"],"thinkers":["burke"],"tags":["conservatism","reflections on the revolution in france","tradition","the sublime"]},{"term":"Michael Oakeshott","def":"Modern European philosopher (1901–1990). Politics should be a modest practice guided by tradition, not a project of rebuilding society from a plan.","more":"An English philosopher who taught at Cambridge and the London School of Economics. In essays like \"Rationalism in Politics\" and \"On Being Conservative\" he criticized the belief that society can be run by abstract plans, defending practical knowledge learned through experience. He saw government as an umpire that keeps fair rules rather than a manager directing people toward one goal.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Conservatism","British idealism"],"thinkers":["oakeshott"],"tags":["conservatism","rationalism in politics","tradition","civil association"]},{"term":"Joseph de Maistre","def":"Modern European philosopher (1753–1821). Societies rest on authority, faith and tradition; reason alone tears them apart.","more":"A Savoyard lawyer and diplomat, writing in French, who became the leading critic of the French Revolution and the Enlightenment. He saw the Revolution as divine punishment and called for a return to monarchy and the authority of the Catholic Church and the Pope. He argued that no written constitution can create a nation; real institutions grow over time under Providence.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Counter-Enlightenment","Traditionalism"],"thinkers":["de_maistre"],"tags":["counter-enlightenment","reactionary","throne and altar","traditionalism"]},{"term":"William Godwin","def":"Modern European philosopher (1756–1836). We should do whatever does the most good for everyone, with no special favours for our own family.","more":"An English political writer whose Enquiry Concerning Political Justice (1793) argued that reason and strict impartiality, not law or government, should guide what we do. He is often called the first modern anarchist. He married Mary Wollstonecraft, and their daughter was Mary Shelley.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism","Anarchism"],"thinkers":["godwin"],"tags":["anarchism","political justice","utilitarianism","philosophical anarchism"]},{"term":"Max Stirner","def":"Modern European philosopher (1806–1856). Morality, God and the state are ghosts; the only thing that counts for me is me.","more":"A German writer in the circle of Young Hegelians in Berlin. In The Ego and Its Own (1844) he attacked religion, morality and even humanism as fixed ideas that rule people from outside. He called himself an egoist who answers to nothing above himself.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Egoism","Young Hegelians"],"thinkers":["stirner"],"tags":["egoism","ego and its own","individualist anarchism","spooks","egoist anarchism"]},{"term":"Nel Noddings","def":"Analytic philosopher (1929–2022). Ethics begins in caring for the particular people in front of us, not in universal rules.","more":"An American philosopher of education who taught at Stanford. Her book Caring (1984) made the relation between the one who cares and the one cared for the center of ethics. She applied this view to schools, arguing that teaching should be built around care.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Care ethics"],"thinkers":["noddings"],"tags":["ethics of care","care ethics","caring","feminist ethics"]},{"term":"Virginia Held","def":"Analytic philosopher (1929–2026). Care is the most basic moral value, and justice grows within it.","more":"An American philosopher at the City University of New York and one of the leading builders of care ethics. Her book The Ethics of Care (2006) argues that care is a practice and a value that ethics has long ignored. She applies it to families, politics and global society.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Care ethics","Feminist philosophy"],"thinkers":["held"],"tags":["ethics of care","care ethics","feminist ethics","caring"]},{"term":"Charles Fried","def":"Analytic philosopher (1935–2024). Some acts are wrong in themselves, though even strict rules may give way before a catastrophe.","more":"A Czech-born American legal philosopher at Harvard who also served as US Solicitor General. In Right and Wrong (1978) he defended firm moral rules against lying and harming the innocent. He allowed that such rules might yield only when the stakes become catastrophic.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Deontology","Philosophy of law"],"thinkers":["fried"],"tags":["deontology","contract as promise","absolutism","right and wrong"]},{"term":"Richard Garner","def":"Analytic philosopher (1936–2020). Since there are no moral facts, we would do better to stop talking as if there were.","more":"An American philosopher at Ohio State University. He agreed with Mackie that moral claims are all false, and went further, arguing that we should give up moral talk altogether. He drew on Daoist and Buddhist ideas about living well without moral rules.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral error theory"],"thinkers":["garner"],"tags":["moral abolitionism","error theory","abolitionism","beyond morality"]},{"term":"Jonas Olson","def":"Analytic philosopher. Moral claims are false, but in everyday life we should go on believing and using them.","more":"A Swedish philosopher at Stockholm University. His Moral Error Theory (2014) defends Mackie's view that there are no moral facts and traces its history back to Hume. He argues that we should keep moral beliefs in daily life and set them aside only when doing philosophy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral error theory"],"thinkers":["olson_jonas"],"tags":["error theory","moral conservationism","moral error theory","metaethics"]},{"term":"Clive Bell","def":"Modern European philosopher (1881–1964). What makes something art is significant form: lines and colours arranged so they move us, whatever they depict.","more":"An English art critic and member of the Bloomsbury Group. His book Art (1914) championed Cézanne and the Post-Impressionists and argued that a painting's subject matter does not matter to its value as art.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Formalism"],"thinkers":["bell"],"tags":["formalism","significant form","aesthetic emotion","aesthetic hypothesis","bloomsbury group"]},{"term":"Leo Tolstoy","def":"Modern European philosopher (1828–1910). Art is the passing on of feelings from one person to others, and good art spreads feelings that bring people together.","more":"The Russian novelist who wrote War and Peace and Anna Karenina. Late in life he wrote What Is Art? (1897), which defines art as infecting others with felt emotion and condemns much elite art, including some of his own, as bad.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Expressivism","Christian anarchism"],"thinkers":["tolstoy"],"tags":["lev tolstoy","what is art","infection theory","war and peace","christian anarchism"]},{"term":"R. G. Collingwood","def":"Modern European philosopher (1889–1943). Real art is the artist working out and expressing an emotion, not a craft for producing effects.","more":"An Oxford philosopher, historian and archaeologist. In The Principles of Art (1938) he separated true art, the expression and clarifying of emotion, from mere craft, amusement and magic.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Expressivism","British idealism"],"thinkers":["collingwood"],"tags":["expression theory","principles of art","idea of history","re-enactment"]},{"term":"Oscar Wilde","def":"Modern European philosopher (1854–1900). Art exists for its own sake, and books are well written or badly written, not moral or immoral.","more":"An Irish playwright, novelist and wit, and the best-known voice of the Aesthetic movement. His essays in Intentions (1891) and the preface to The Picture of Dorian Gray argue that art owes nothing to morality.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Aestheticism"],"thinkers":["wilde"],"tags":["aestheticism","art for art's sake","picture of dorian gray","decadence"]},{"term":"Noël Carroll","def":"Analytic philosopher (1947). A moral flaw in a work of art can be a flaw in the art, but art serves many purposes and no single theory captures them.","more":"An American philosopher of art at the City University of New York who writes on film, mass art and the ethics of art. His paper 'Moderate Moralism' (1996) argued that moral defects can sometimes count as artistic defects.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics"],"thinkers":["carroll"],"tags":["moderate moralism","philosophy of mass art","horror","art and morality"]},{"term":"Robert Kane","def":"Analytic philosopher (1938–2024). We have free will because some hard choices are truly open, and in making them we shape who we become.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Texas at Austin and the leading defender of event-causal libertarianism. In The Significance of Free Will (1996) he argued that indeterminism in the brain during struggling choices makes ultimate responsibility possible.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Libertarian free will"],"thinkers":["kane"],"tags":["libertarian free will","event-causal libertarianism","self-forming actions","ultimate responsibility"]},{"term":"John Martin Fischer","def":"Analytic philosopher (1952). We can be morally responsible even if determinism is true, because responsibility needs control by our own reasons-responsive thinking, not alternative choices.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of California, Riverside. With Mark Ravizza he developed semicompatibilism: whatever determinism does to our ability to do otherwise, it leaves moral responsibility intact.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Compatibilism","Semicompatibilism"],"thinkers":["fischer"],"tags":["semicompatibilism","guidance control","reasons-responsiveness","moral responsibility"]},{"term":"Saul Smilansky","def":"Analytic philosopher (1961). There is no libertarian free will, but society needs people to keep believing in it, so the illusion is useful.","more":"An Israeli philosopher at the University of Haifa. In Free Will and Illusion (2000) he argued that both compatibilism and hard determinism are partly right, and that a widespread illusion of free will plays a positive moral role.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Illusionism (free will)"],"thinkers":["smilansky"],"tags":["illusionism","free will illusion","fundamental dualism","free will"]},{"term":"Blaise Pascal","def":"Modern European philosopher (1623–1662). Reason cannot settle whether God exists, so wager on God: you lose little if wrong and gain everything if right.","more":"A French mathematician, physicist and religious thinker who helped found probability theory. His unfinished defence of Christianity, published after his death as the Pensées, contains his famous wager and sharp reflections on human misery and greatness.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Christian apologetics","Jansenism"],"thinkers":["pascal"],"tags":["pascal's wager","wager","pensees","the heart has its reasons","fideism"]},{"term":"Keith DeRose","def":"Analytic philosopher (1962). Whether you count as knowing something depends on how high the stakes and standards are in the conversation.","more":"An American philosopher at Yale and the leading defender of epistemic contextualism. He argues that skeptics win only by raising the standards for knowing, which leaves everyday knowledge claims true in everyday settings.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Contextualism"],"thinkers":["derose"],"tags":["contextualism","epistemic contextualism","skepticism","knowledge attributions"]},{"term":"William Alston","def":"Analytic philosopher (1921–2009). People can experience God much as they perceive the world, and that experience can justify belief.","more":"An American philosopher of language, knowledge and religion. He helped revive Christian philosophy in the late 20th century, arguing that religious experience can justify belief and that we are poorly placed to judge God's reasons for allowing evil.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion","Reformed epistemology"],"thinkers":["alston"],"tags":["perceiving god","religious experience","reformed epistemology","mystical perception"]},{"term":"Ian Hacking","def":"Analytic philosopher (1936–2023). We should believe in the things scientists can use as tools, even if their grand theories change.","more":"A Canadian philosopher of science and historian of ideas. He shifted attention from theories to experiments, arguing that what scientists can manipulate in the lab is real, and he also wrote influential histories of probability and of how categories of people are made.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of science","Entity realism"],"thinkers":["hacking"],"tags":["entity realism","experimental realism","representing and intervening","social construction"]},{"term":"Wesley Salmon","def":"Analytic philosopher (1925–2001). Causes are physical processes that carry something from one event to another.","more":"An American philosopher of science who worked on explanation, probability and causation. He argued that explaining an event means showing the causal processes behind it, and that a causal process is one that can carry a mark or change along with it.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of science"],"thinkers":["salmon"],"tags":["causal process theory","mark transmission","scientific explanation","causal mechanical model"]},{"term":"Albert Einstein","def":"Modern European philosopher (1879–1955). Past, present and future are equally real; the feeling that time flows is a stubborn illusion.","more":"The German-born physicist whose theories of relativity changed our ideas of space and time. He also wrote about philosophy, science and religion, rejecting free will in the tradition of Spinoza and Schopenhauer.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Physics","Philosophy of science"],"thinkers":["einstein"],"tags":["relativity","theory of relativity","block universe","eternalism","spacetime"]},{"term":"Roy Sorensen","def":"Analytic philosopher (1957). Vague words have sharp boundaries that we can never know.","more":"An American philosopher known for his work on paradoxes, vagueness and thought experiments. He defends epistemicism: there really is a last grain that makes a heap, but it is hidden from us.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Epistemicism"],"thinkers":["sorensen"],"tags":["epistemicism","vagueness","sorites paradox","blindspots"]},{"term":"Mary Leng","def":"Analytic philosopher (1974). Mathematics is a useful story; science can use it without numbers really existing.","more":"A British philosopher of mathematics at the University of York. She defends fictionalism: mathematical statements are true only within the story of mathematics, even though that story is indispensable to science.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Mathematical fictionalism"],"thinkers":["leng"],"tags":["fictionalism","mathematical fictionalism","nominalism","indispensability argument"]},{"term":"R. A. Duff","def":"Analytic philosopher (1945). Punishment is a community's way of telling an offender, in a form that words alone can't carry, that what they did was wrong.","more":"Antony Duff is a British philosopher of criminal law who taught for many years at the University of Stirling. He developed the best-known communicative theory of punishment, which treats a sentence as a kind of moral conversation between the community and the offender.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Communicative theory of punishment","Philosophy of criminal law"],"thinkers":["duff"],"tags":["communicative theory of punishment","punishment","penance","criminal law"]},{"term":"Nils Christie","def":"Modern European philosopher (1928–2015). Societies should cut back pain-delivery to a minimum and let victims and offenders settle conflicts themselves.","more":"Nils Christie was a Norwegian criminologist at the University of Oslo. He argued that the state had stolen conflicts from the people involved in them, and became one of the founding voices of restorative justice and penal abolitionism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Penal abolitionism","Restorative justice"],"thinkers":["christie"],"tags":["abolitionism","limits to pain","penal abolition","restorative justice","conflicts as property"]},{"term":"Michael S. Moore","def":"Analytic philosopher (1943). Criminals should be punished because they deserve it, and how much harm they actually cause affects what they deserve.","more":"Michael Moore is an American legal philosopher who has taught at Penn, Illinois and elsewhere. He is one of the leading modern defenders of retributivism, the view that deserved punishment is good in itself.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Retributivism","Moral realism"],"thinkers":["michael_moore"],"tags":["retributivism","retribution","desert","punishment","criminal law"]},{"term":"Michael Sandel","def":"Analytic philosopher (1953). Justice can't be neutral about the good life; politics should be about the common good of a community.","more":"Michael Sandel is a political philosopher at Harvard, famous for his course and book Justice. His critique of Rawls in the early 1980s helped start the liberal-communitarian debate.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Communitarianism","Civic republicanism"],"thinkers":["sandel"],"tags":["communitarianism","justice","what money can't buy","liberalism and the limits of justice"]},{"term":"Alasdair MacIntyre","def":"Analytic philosopher (1929–2025). Modern moral talk is broken fragments; we need communities and traditions where virtues and a shared good make sense again.","more":"Alasdair MacIntyre was a Scottish-born philosopher who taught mostly in the United States, finally at Notre Dame. His book After Virtue argued that the Enlightenment failed to justify morality and called for a return to Aristotle's ethics of virtue, practised in local communities.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Virtue ethics","Thomism"],"thinkers":["macintyre"],"tags":["after virtue","virtue ethics","communitarianism","tradition","practices"]},{"term":"Robert Paul Wolff","def":"Analytic philosopher (1933–2025). Being morally autonomous means never just obeying, so no state can have rightful authority over you.","more":"Robert Paul Wolff was an American philosopher who taught at Harvard, Columbia and the University of Massachusetts Amherst. Besides work on Kant and Marx, he wrote a short and famous book arguing that state authority and individual autonomy cannot be reconciled.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophical anarchism","Kant scholarship"],"thinkers":["rp_wolff"],"tags":["philosophical anarchism","in defense of anarchism","autonomy","anarchism"]},{"term":"Michael Huemer","def":"Analytic philosopher (1969). Governments do things we'd condemn anyone else for doing, so political authority is an illusion.","more":"Michael Huemer is an American philosopher at the University of Colorado Boulder. He is known for defending ethical intuitionism and for a systematic case that no government has genuine authority.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophical anarchism","Ethical intuitionism"],"thinkers":["huemer"],"tags":["problem of political authority","anarchism","ethical intuitionism","anarcho-capitalism"]},{"term":"Stanley Fish","def":"Analytic philosopher (1938). There's no neutral principle of free speech; where to draw the line is always a political choice.","more":"Stanley Fish is an American literary theorist and legal scholar who taught at Berkeley, Johns Hopkins and Duke. He is known for arguing that meaning depends on interpretive communities, and for provocative essays on free speech and law.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Reader-response criticism","Legal pragmatism"],"thinkers":["fish"],"tags":["free speech","there's no such thing as free speech","interpretive communities","reader response"]},{"term":"Richard Taylor","def":"Analytic philosopher (1919–2003). Life's meaning comes from within us: Sisyphus's life would be meaningful if he wanted to roll his stone.","more":"Richard Taylor was an American philosopher who taught at Brown, Columbia and the University of Rochester. He wrote on fate, free will and ethics, and his essay on the meaning of life is one of the most reprinted in the field.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Subjectivism about meaning","Metaphysics"],"thinkers":["richard_taylor"],"tags":["sisyphus","meaning of life","subjectivism","fatalism"]},{"term":"Alastair Norcross","def":"Analytic philosopher (1962). Morality ranks options from better to worse; there is no sharp line where duty begins.","more":"A British-born philosopher who teaches at the University of Colorado Boulder. He is the best-known defender of scalar utilitarianism, the view that right and wrong come in degrees. He is also known for arguments that eating factory-farmed meat is wrong.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism","Scalar consequentialism"],"thinkers":["norcross"],"tags":["scalar consequentialism","scalar utilitarianism","consequentialism","animal ethics"]},{"term":"Rosalind Hursthouse","def":"Analytic philosopher (1943). The right act is what a virtuous person would characteristically do in your situation.","more":"A philosopher born in Bristol and raised in New Zealand, who taught at the Open University and the University of Auckland. Her book On Virtue Ethics (1999) turned modern virtue ethics into a full rival to Kantian and utilitarian theories. She showed how virtue ethics can guide action in hard cases like abortion.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Virtue ethics","Neo-Aristotelianism"],"thinkers":["hursthouse"],"tags":["virtue ethics","on virtue ethics","neo-aristotelianism","right action","abortion"]},{"term":"Jonathan Dancy","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946). Moral reasons depend on context, so no general moral principle is needed or reliable.","more":"A British philosopher who taught at Keele, Reading and the University of Texas at Austin. He is the leading defender of moral particularism, the view that a feature which counts in favour of an act in one case can count against it in another. He is also known for work on reasons for action.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral particularism","Moral realism"],"thinkers":["dancy"],"tags":["moral particularism","particularism","ethics without principles","holism of reasons"]},{"term":"Samuel Scheffler","def":"Analytic philosopher (1951). Morality should promote the best outcome, but each person may give extra weight to their own life and projects.","more":"Scheffler teaches at New York University. His first book argued for a middle path between consequentialism and common-sense morality. He later wrote on special obligations and on why the future of humanity matters to us.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Consequentialism (hybrid)"],"thinkers":["scheffler"],"tags":["agent-centred prerogative","rejection of consequentialism","consequentialism","death and the afterlife"]},{"term":"Shelly Kagan","def":"Analytic philosopher (1954). Morality demands that we do the most good we can, with no special permissions to put ourselves first.","more":"Kagan teaches at Yale. His book The Limits of Morality argues that ordinary moral thinking cannot justify its limits on how much we must do for others. He is also known for a popular course on death.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["kagan"],"tags":["limits of morality","consequentialism","demandingness","death"]},{"term":"Thomas Hurka","def":"Analytic philosopher (1952). A good life develops our human nature: knowledge, achievement and skill are good whether or not we want them.","more":"Hurka teaches at the University of Toronto. He revived perfectionism, the idea that the good is the development of human nature. He has also written on virtue and on the history of British ethics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Perfectionism","Consequentialism"],"thinkers":["hurka"],"tags":["perfectionism","virtue","well-being","human nature"]},{"term":"Julia Driver","def":"Analytic philosopher (1961). A trait is a virtue because it reliably does good in the world, not because of what goes on in the agent's head.","more":"Driver teaches at the University of Texas at Austin. In Uneasy Virtue she argued that virtues are defined by their good effects. She used odd cases like modesty, which seems to require not knowing your own worth, to challenge the Aristotelian picture.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Virtue consequentialism"],"thinkers":["driver"],"tags":["consequentialist virtue ethics","virtue consequentialism","uneasy virtue","virtue"]},{"term":"Peter Carruthers","def":"Analytic philosopher (1952). Morality is an agreement among rational agents, so animals have no moral rights of their own.","more":"Carruthers, a British philosopher at the University of Maryland, works mostly on the mind. In The Animals Issue he argued that contractualism gives animals only indirect protection. He later doubted that most animals have conscious experiences at all.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Contractualism","Philosophy of mind"],"thinkers":["carruthers"],"tags":["contractualism","animal rights","animals","animals issue"]},{"term":"Mark Rowlands","def":"Analytic philosopher (1962). Properly understood, a fair social contract must protect animals too.","more":"Rowlands is a Welsh philosopher at the University of Miami. He argued that behind Rawls's veil of ignorance you would not know whether you were rational or even human, so a fair contract must include animals. He also wrote a popular memoir about living with a wolf.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Animal ethics","Contractarianism"],"thinkers":["rowlands"],"tags":["animal rights","contractarianism","animals like us","social contract"]},{"term":"Joan Tronto","def":"Analytic philosopher (1952). Care is a public, political matter, not just a private virtue of the home.","more":"Tronto is a political scientist at the University of Minnesota. She argued that care work is central to all human life and should shape democratic politics. Her definition of care is one of the most widely cited.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Care ethics","Feminist political theory"],"thinkers":["tronto"],"tags":["ethics of care","care ethics","caring democracy","moral boundaries","political care"]},{"term":"Michael Slote","def":"Analytic philosopher (1941). Empathy is the root of morality: right acts express caring, empathic motives.","more":"Slote teaches at the University of Miami. He began by criticizing consequentialism, then developed an agent-based virtue ethics. Later he built a care ethics grounded in empathy, drawing on Hume.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Care ethics","Sentimentalism"],"thinkers":["slote"],"tags":["ethics of care","care ethics","sentimentalism","empathy","virtue ethics"]},{"term":"Max Scheler","def":"Continental philosopher (1874–1928). Values are real and come in a fixed order of rank, which we grasp through feeling.","more":"Scheler was a German phenomenologist influenced by Husserl. He attacked Kant's purely formal ethics and argued that we know values through emotional experience. He also wrote on sympathy, resentment and love.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Phenomenology","Value ethics"],"thinkers":["scheler"],"tags":["value ethics","material value ethics","phenomenology","hierarchy of values","ressentiment"]},{"term":"Isaiah Berlin","def":"Analytic philosopher (1909–1997). Human values are many and can truly conflict, and no single formula ranks them.","more":"Berlin was born in Riga and taught at Oxford. A historian of ideas, he is best known for distinguishing negative from positive liberty. He argued that the dream of a final harmony of all values leads to tyranny.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Value pluralism","Liberalism"],"thinkers":["berlin"],"tags":["value pluralism","two concepts of liberty","positive liberty","negative liberty","hedgehog and fox"]},{"term":"Arthur Danto","def":"Analytic philosopher (1924–2013). What makes something art is not how it looks but what it means, and only the history and theory of art let it mean that.","more":"An American philosopher and long-time art critic for The Nation. Seeing Andy Warhol's Brillo Box in 1964 convinced him that art and ordinary things can look exactly alike, so the difference must lie in meaning and context.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Artworld theory"],"thinkers":["danto"],"tags":["artworld","end of art","brillo box","institutional theory","transfiguration of the commonplace"]},{"term":"George Dickie","def":"Analytic philosopher (1926–2020). Something is art when it is made to be presented to an art public within the practice of the art world.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Illinois at Chicago. He developed the institutional theory of art and argued that the idea of a special aesthetic attitude or experience is a myth.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Institutional theory of art"],"thinkers":["dickie"],"tags":["institutional theory of art","artworld","definition of art"]},{"term":"Jerrold Levinson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1948). Something is art if it is meant to be regarded in the ways earlier art was rightly regarded, and a work means what an ideal reader would take its author to mean.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Maryland and a leading figure in the philosophy of art and music. He is known for the historical definition of art and for hypothetical intentionalism about interpretation.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics"],"thinkers":["levinson"],"tags":["historical definition of art","intentional-historical","hypothetical intentionalism","music"]},{"term":"Berys Gaut","def":"Analytic philosopher. A bad moral attitude in a work of art always counts against it as art, and art itself is a cluster of features with no single essence.","more":"A philosopher at the University of St Andrews and a past president of the British Society of Aesthetics. He writes on art and ethics, creativity and film, and defends ethicism and a cluster account of art.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Ethicism"],"thinkers":["gaut"],"tags":["ethicism","cluster account","art and morality","ethical criticism"]},{"term":"Nelson Goodman","def":"Analytic philosopher (1906–1998). Art is a way of understanding the world, so what we know about a picture, even that it is a fake, changes how we should see it.","more":"An American philosopher at Harvard who worked on logic, induction and the theory of symbols. His Languages of Art (1968) treated the arts as symbol systems that help us understand, like the sciences.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Nominalism"],"thinkers":["goodman"],"tags":["languages of art","new riddle of induction","grue","ways of worldmaking","cognitivism about art"]},{"term":"Monroe Beardsley","def":"Analytic philosopher (1915–1985). A work's meaning lies in the work itself, and its value is the aesthetic experience it can give.","more":"An American philosopher at Swarthmore and Temple whose book Aesthetics (1958) shaped the field. With W. K. Wimsatt he named the 'intentional fallacy', and he defined art by its aim of giving aesthetic experience.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","New Criticism"],"thinkers":["beardsley"],"tags":["intentional fallacy","aesthetic experience","aesthetic definition of art","new criticism"]},{"term":"W. K. Wimsatt","def":"Analytic philosopher (1907–1975). A poem means what its words mean, not what its author privately intended.","more":"An American literary critic at Yale and a leading New Critic. With Monroe Beardsley he wrote 'The Intentional Fallacy' (1946) and 'The Affective Fallacy' (1949), which argued that critics should judge the work itself.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["New Criticism"],"thinkers":["wimsatt"],"tags":["intentional fallacy","new criticism","anti-intentionalism","affective fallacy"]},{"term":"Roland Barthes","def":"Continental philosopher (1915–1980). Once a text is written the author no longer controls it; meaning is made by the reader.","more":"A French literary theorist and essayist. His essay 'The Death of the Author' (1967) argued that a text is a web of quotations whose meaning comes together in the reader, not in the author's intention.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Structuralism","Post-structuralism"],"thinkers":["barthes"],"tags":["death of the author","semiotics","mythologies","structuralism","post-structuralism"]},{"term":"Theodor W. Adorno","def":"Continental philosopher (1903–1969). Mass-produced culture is standardised to sell and keeps people passive, while real art holds a truth that resists society.","more":"A German philosopher, sociologist and trained composer of the Frankfurt School. With Max Horkheimer he wrote Dialectic of Enlightenment, whose chapter on 'the culture industry' attacked film, radio and popular music.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Frankfurt School","Critical theory"],"thinkers":["adorno"],"tags":["frankfurt school","culture industry","critical theory","dialectic of enlightenment"]},{"term":"Max Horkheimer","def":"Continental philosopher (1895–1973). Mass culture is an industry that makes everything alike and keeps people content with the way things are.","more":"A German philosopher and sociologist who directed the Institute for Social Research in Frankfurt. With Theodor Adorno he wrote Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/47), including the essay on 'the culture industry'.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Frankfurt School","Critical theory"],"thinkers":["horkheimer"],"tags":["frankfurt school","critical theory","culture industry","dialectic of enlightenment"]},{"term":"Pierre Bourdieu","def":"Continental philosopher (1930–2002). Taste is a mark of social class: what counts as refined taste is what powerful groups use to set themselves apart.","more":"A French sociologist at the Collège de France. In Distinction (1979), based on large surveys of French tastes, he argued that preferences in art, music and food track education and class.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Sociology of culture"],"thinkers":["bourdieu"],"tags":["distinction","cultural capital","habitus","sociology of taste","field"]},{"term":"Abhinavagupta","def":"Indian philosopher (950–1016). The heart of art is rasa: a savoured emotion, freed from your personal stake in it.","more":"A Kashmiri philosopher, mystic and literary theorist. His commentaries on Bharata's Natyashastra and Anandavardhana's Dhvanyaloka made rasa theory the centre of Indian aesthetics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Kashmir Shaivism","Rasa theory"],"thinkers":["abhinavagupta"],"tags":["abhinava","rasa theory","kashmir shaivism","indian aesthetics","rasa","dhvani"]},{"term":"Bharata Muni","def":"Indian philosopher (200 BCE). Drama, music and dance work by producing rasa, a feeling the audience tastes like a flavour.","more":"The traditional author of the Natyashastra, an ancient Sanskrit treatise on drama, dance and music compiled roughly between 200 BCE and 200 CE. Its sixth chapter founded the theory of rasa.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Rasa theory"],"thinkers":["bharata"],"tags":["natyashastra","natya shastra","rasa theory","rasa","indian aesthetics","drama"]},{"term":"Matthew Kieran","def":"Analytic philosopher (1968). A work's moral flaws can sometimes make it better as art, because they let it teach what decent art cannot.","more":"A British philosopher at the University of Leeds who writes on art, morality and knowledge. He defends 'cognitive immoralism', the view that a work's immoral perspective can be an artistic merit.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Immoralism"],"thinkers":["kieran"],"tags":["immoralism","art and morality","ethics of art","revealing art"]},{"term":"A. W. Eaton","def":"Analytic philosopher. Getting us to like a deeply immoral character can be a moral flaw and an artistic achievement at once.","more":"Anne W. Eaton is an American philosopher at the University of Illinois at Chicago who works on aesthetics, ethics and feminist philosophy. Her paper 'Robust Immoralism' (2012) uses 'rough heroes' like Tony Soprano as its main case.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Feminist aesthetics","Immoralism"],"thinkers":["eaton"],"tags":["immoralism","robust immoralism","art and morality","rough heroes","feminist aesthetics"]},{"term":"Eduard Hanslick","def":"Modern European philosopher (1825–1904). Music does not represent feelings; its content and beauty are tonally moving forms.","more":"A Prague-born music critic in Vienna and an opponent of Wagner. His short book On the Musically Beautiful (1854) became the classic statement of formalism about music.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Formalism"],"thinkers":["hanslick"],"tags":["formalism","musical formalism","on the musically beautiful","music","philosophy of music"]},{"term":"Denis Dutton","def":"Analytic philosopher (1944–2010). Art is an achievement, and our love of it comes from evolved human instincts.","more":"An American-born philosopher at the University of Canterbury in New Zealand and founder of the website Arts & Letters Daily. He argued that forgery is wrong because art is a performance, and that art has roots in evolution.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Evolutionary aesthetics"],"thinkers":["dutton"],"tags":["art instinct","evolutionary aesthetics","achievement","forgery"]},{"term":"Morris Weitz","def":"Analytic philosopher (1916–1981). Art cannot be defined, because it is an open concept that artists keep stretching.","more":"An American philosopher at Ohio State and Brandeis. His paper 'The Role of Theory in Aesthetics' (1956) applied Wittgenstein's idea of family resemblance to argue that every definition of art fails.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Anti-essentialism"],"thinkers":["weitz"],"tags":["open concept","anti-essentialism about art","definition of art","family resemblance"]},{"term":"Nick Zangwill","def":"Analytic philosopher. Beauty is a real property of things, and art is what is made in order to have such properties.","more":"A British philosopher who has taught at Glasgow, Durham and Hull and at University College London. He defends aesthetic realism, a moderate formalism, and the 'creative theory' of art.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics","Aesthetic realism"],"thinkers":["zangwill"],"tags":["aesthetic realism","aesthetic creation theory","metaphysics of beauty","aesthetic properties"]},{"term":"Frank Sibley","def":"Analytic philosopher (1923–1996). No list of plain features guarantees that something is graceful or beautiful; seeing it takes taste.","more":"An English philosopher who taught at Cornell and Lancaster. His paper 'Aesthetic Concepts' (1959) argued that aesthetic qualities depend on non-aesthetic ones without following rules.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic aesthetics"],"thinkers":["sibley"],"tags":["aesthetic concepts","aesthetic properties","taste","non-condition-governed"]},{"term":"Yoshida Kenkō","def":"Japanese philosopher (1283–1352). Beauty lies in what is incomplete and passing, not in perfection at its peak.","more":"A Japanese poet and Buddhist monk. His Essays in Idleness (Tsurezuregusa), written around 1330, is a classic of Japanese literature and a key source for the aesthetics of impermanence and imperfection.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Buddhism","Japanese aesthetics"],"thinkers":["kenko"],"tags":["kenko","essays in idleness","tsurezuregusa","impermanence","japanese aesthetics","wabi-sabi"]},{"term":"Okakura Kakuzō","def":"Japanese philosopher (1863–1913). The tea ceremony teaches a worship of the imperfect: beauty in what is simple, uneven and unfinished.","more":"A Japanese scholar of art who helped found the Tokyo School of Fine Arts and later worked at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. His Book of Tea (1906), written in English, introduced Japanese aesthetics to Western readers.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Japanese aesthetics"],"thinkers":["okakura"],"tags":["okakura tenshin","book of tea","tea ceremony","teaism","japanese aesthetics","wabi-sabi"]},{"term":"Arthur Koestler","def":"Modern European philosopher (1905–1983). If you cannot tell a fake from the original, valuing the original more is snobbery, not taste.","more":"A Hungarian-born British novelist and essayist, best known for Darkness at Noon (1940). His essay 'The Anatomy of Snobbery' (1955) used the van Meegeren forgeries to attack how we value originals.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":[],"thinkers":["koestler"],"tags":["forgery","the act of creation","darkness at noon","authenticity","ghost in the machine"]},{"term":"E. D. Hirsch","def":"Analytic philosopher (1928). A text means what its author meant by it, and that meaning can be recovered.","more":"An American literary critic at Yale and the University of Virginia, later known for his work on 'cultural literacy' in schools. His Validity in Interpretation (1967) is the classic defence of the author's meaning against New Criticism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Intentionalism"],"thinkers":["hirsch_ed"],"tags":["intentionalism","validity in interpretation","authorial intention","hermeneutics","cultural literacy"]},{"term":"Richard Shusterman","def":"American Pragmatism philosopher (1949). Popular art like rap can be fine art, and the line between high and low culture is not a line of quality.","more":"An American philosopher, raised partly in Israel, who works in the pragmatist tradition of John Dewey. His Pragmatist Aesthetics (1992) defended popular art, and he later founded 'somaesthetics', the study of bodily experience.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pragmatism","Somaesthetics"],"thinkers":["shusterman"],"tags":["somaesthetics","pragmatist aesthetics","popular art","rap","hip hop"]},{"term":"Sean Dorrance Kelly","def":"Continental philosopher. An AI can be skilled, but creativity belongs to agents in a human community who care about it.","more":"An American philosopher at Harvard who works on phenomenology and Heidegger. He co-wrote All Things Shining (2011) with Hubert Dreyfus and has argued in public essays that machines cannot be artists.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Phenomenology"],"thinkers":["kelly_sd"],"tags":["ai creativity","creativity","artificial intelligence","heidegger"]},{"term":"Ted Chiang","def":"Analytic philosopher (1967). Art comes from making many choices, so a one-line prompt is too few choices to make art.","more":"An American science-fiction writer whose story 'Story of Your Life' became the film Arrival. In essays for The New Yorker he has argued that generative AI cannot make real art or use language with intention.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":[],"thinkers":["chiang"],"tags":["ai art","why ai isn't going to make art","science fiction","choices"]},{"term":"Aaron Hertzmann","def":"Analytic philosopher. Computers are not artists; like the camera, they are new tools that human artists use.","more":"An American computer scientist at Adobe Research who works on computer graphics and art. His essay 'Can Computers Create Art?' (2018) argues that art is made by social agents, so AI systems are artists' tools, not authors.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Computer graphics"],"thinkers":["hertzmann"],"tags":["ai art","computer art","can computers create art","tool view"]},{"term":"Simon Colton","def":"Analytic philosopher. Software can be genuinely creative and may one day be accepted as an artist in its own right.","more":"A British researcher in computational creativity at Queen Mary University of London and Monash University. He built The Painting Fool, a program meant to be taken seriously as an artist.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Computational creativity"],"thinkers":["colton"],"tags":["computational creativity","ai art","the painting fool","machine creativity"]},{"term":"Thaddeus Metz","def":"Analytic philosopher (1968). A life is meaningful when it uses reason to engage with what is most fundamental to human existence, and relationships of harmony matter most.","more":"Metz is an American philosopher who has spent most of his career in South Africa, at the Universities of Johannesburg and Pretoria. With Susan Wolf, he helped make the meaning of life a central topic in analytic philosophy. He has also done much to develop ubuntu, the southern African ethic of community, as a moral theory.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Meaning in life","African philosophy"],"thinkers":["metz"],"tags":["meaning in life","fundamentality theory","ubuntu","african philosophy"]},{"term":"William Lane Craig","def":"Analytic philosopher (1949). Without God and life after death, human life would ultimately have no meaning, value or purpose.","more":"Craig is an American philosopher and Christian apologist, best known for reviving the kalam cosmological argument and for public debates on the existence of God. In 'The Absurdity of Life without God' he argues that atheism leads to the conclusion that life is absurd.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Christian philosophy","Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["craig"],"tags":["kalam cosmological argument","kalam","apologetics","absurdity of life without god","christian apologetics"]},{"term":"Joseph Raz","def":"Analytic philosopher (1939–2022). A good life is one of wholehearted and successful engagement in activities that are truly worth doing.","more":"Raz was an Israeli-born philosopher of law and morality who taught at Oxford and Columbia. A student of H. L. A. Hart, he defended legal positivism and a liberalism built on the value of personal autonomy. He held that well-being depends both on what we care about and on whether it is really valuable.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Legal positivism","Liberal perfectionism"],"thinkers":["raz"],"tags":["morality of freedom","perfectionist liberalism","legal positivism","well-being","authority"]},{"term":"John Finnis","def":"Analytic philosopher (1940). Some things, like knowledge, friendship and life itself, are good for every human being, whatever anyone happens to want.","more":"Finnis is an Australian legal philosopher who taught at Oxford and Notre Dame. His book Natural Law and Natural Rights revived Thomas Aquinas's natural law tradition in modern analytic terms. He argues that there is a short list of basic human goods that practical reason sees as worth pursuing for their own sake.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Natural law theory"],"thinkers":["finnis"],"tags":["new natural law","natural law","basic goods","natural law and natural rights"]},{"term":"Nick Bostrom","def":"Analytic philosopher (1973). Ageing and death are harms we should work to defeat, and technology may one day let us.","more":"Bostrom is a Swedish philosopher who founded the Future of Humanity Institute at Oxford. He is known for work on existential risk, the simulation argument and superintelligence. In 'The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant' he argues that ageing is a monster humanity should stop accepting.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Transhumanism"],"thinkers":["bostrom"],"tags":["transhumanism","simulation argument","existential risk","superintelligence","anti-ageing"]},{"term":"John S. Mbiti","def":"Africana philosopher (1931–2019). In African traditional thought a person belongs to a community, and the dead stay part of it for as long as they are remembered.","more":"Mbiti was a Kenyan philosopher, theologian and Anglican priest who taught in Uganda, Kenya and Switzerland. His book African Religions and Philosophy was one of the first broad studies of traditional African thought. He described how the recently dead live on as the 'living-dead' while their names are remembered.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["African philosophy","African theology"],"thinkers":["mbiti"],"tags":["african religions","african philosophy","i am because we are","communalism","ubuntu"]},{"term":"Jan Narveson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1936). We should be in favour of making people happy, but neutral about making happy people.","more":"Narveson is an American-born philosopher who taught for decades at the University of Waterloo in Canada. His early papers on population ethics argued that bringing someone into existence cannot benefit that person. He later became a leading defender of libertarianism on contractarian grounds.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Contractarianism","Libertarianism"],"thinkers":["narveson"],"tags":["procreative asymmetry","procreative neutrality","population ethics","libertarianism"]},{"term":"Emil Cioran","def":"Continental philosopher (1911–1995). Being born is the real misfortune; everything else follows from it.","more":"Cioran was a Romanian-born essayist who moved to Paris and wrote in French from the late 1940s. His short, aphoristic books explore despair, insomnia, history and the trouble of existing at all. He is one of the best-known modern pessimists.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Pessimism"],"thinkers":["cioran"],"tags":["pessimism","antinatalism","trouble with being born","nihilism","despair"]},{"term":"Noam Chomsky","def":"Analytic philosopher (1928). Since physics gave up on a fixed idea of body, the old mind-body problem can't even be stated clearly.","more":"An American linguist whose theory of an inborn universal grammar transformed the study of language. In philosophy of mind he argues that, after Newton, 'body' and 'physical' have no fixed meaning, and that some problems may lie beyond what human minds can solve.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Rationalism","Linguistics"],"thinkers":["chomsky"],"tags":["linguistics","universal grammar","mind-body problem","nativism","generative grammar"]},{"term":"Patricia Churchland","def":"Analytic philosopher (1943). To understand the mind, study the brain; our everyday talk of beliefs and desires may not survive.","more":"A Canadian-American philosopher who founded neurophilosophy, the use of brain science to answer questions about the mind. With her husband Paul Churchland she argues that folk psychology is a rough theory that neuroscience may replace.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Eliminative materialism","Neurophilosophy"],"thinkers":["pchurchland"],"tags":["neurophilosophy","eliminative materialism","eliminativism","folk psychology","neuroscience"]},{"term":"Keith Frankish","def":"Analytic philosopher (1962). Consciousness seems to have an inner glow of feeling, but that seeming is an illusion the brain creates.","more":"A British philosopher who named and developed illusionism: the view that phenomenal consciousness, as it seems to us from the inside, does not exist, and the real task is to explain why it seems to.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Illusionism"],"thinkers":["frankish"],"tags":["illusionism","consciousness","qualia","phenomenal consciousness"]},{"term":"Colin McGinn","def":"Analytic philosopher (1950). The brain produces consciousness naturally, but our minds may be unable to understand how.","more":"A British philosopher best known for 'new mysterianism': consciousness has a natural explanation, but humans may be cognitively closed to it, as a dog is to algebra.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Mysterianism"],"thinkers":["mcginn"],"tags":["mysterianism","new mysterianism","cognitive closure","mind-body problem","consciousness"]},{"term":"Steven Pinker","def":"Analytic philosopher (1954). The mind is a system of computing organs shaped by evolution, though consciousness itself may stay a puzzle.","more":"A Canadian-American cognitive psychologist who has written widely read books on language, mind and human nature. He defends the computational theory of mind but suspects the problem of subjective experience may be beyond us.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Computational theory of mind","Evolutionary psychology"],"thinkers":["pinker"],"tags":["evolutionary psychology","computational theory of mind","blank slate","how the mind works","better angels"]},{"term":"David Papineau","def":"Analytic philosopher (1947). Conscious states are brain states; zombies only seem possible because of the special way we think about experience.","more":"A British philosopher of mind and science. He argues that physical effects all have physical causes, so conscious states must be physical, and explains the intuition that they aren't by the peculiar 'phenomenal concepts' we use for them.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Physicalism","Type-B physicalism"],"thinkers":["papineau"],"tags":["physicalism","zombies","phenomenal concepts","naturalism","consciousness"]},{"term":"Ned Block","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942). Feeling and information-access are different, and we may not be able to tell whether a robot feels anything.","more":"An American philosopher of mind at New York University. He distinguished phenomenal consciousness (what it feels like) from access consciousness (information available for thought and action), and raised famous objections to functionalism, such as the 'China brain'.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Physicalism"],"thinkers":["block"],"tags":["access consciousness","phenomenal consciousness","china brain","chinese nation","functionalism"]},{"term":"Alan Turing","def":"Analytic philosopher (1912–1954). If a machine can hold a conversation we can't tell apart from a person's, we should say it thinks.","more":"An English mathematician who founded computer science and helped break German codes in the Second World War. His 1950 paper proposed the 'imitation game', now called the Turing test, and answered objections to the idea of thinking machines.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Computationalism"],"thinkers":["turing"],"tags":["turing test","imitation game","computability","artificial intelligence","turing machine"]},{"term":"Christof Koch","def":"Analytic philosopher (1956). Consciousness depends on a system's physical causal structure, so a computer could be smart without feeling anything.","more":"A German-American neuroscientist who worked with Francis Crick on the neural basis of consciousness and later championed integrated information theory. He argues that experience is widespread in animals but cannot be produced by computation alone.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Integrated information theory"],"thinkers":["koch"],"tags":["integrated information theory","iit","neural correlates of consciousness","consciousness"]},{"term":"Anil Seth","def":"Analytic philosopher (1972). Consciousness is rooted in being a living body that predicts and regulates itself; intelligence alone isn't enough.","more":"A British neuroscientist who studies consciousness as the brain's 'controlled hallucination' of the world and the body. He argues that consciousness is tied to life, so AI could become very intelligent without becoming conscious.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Predictive processing"],"thinkers":["seth"],"tags":["predictive processing","beast machine","controlled hallucination","being you","consciousness"]},{"term":"Roger Penrose","def":"Analytic philosopher (1931). Human understanding goes beyond any algorithm, so no computer program could be conscious.","more":"An English mathematical physicist and Nobel laureate. Drawing on Gödel's theorem, he argues that human mathematical insight is not computable, and that consciousness depends on as-yet-unknown physics in the brain.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Anti-computationalism"],"thinkers":["penrose"],"tags":["orch-or","emperor's new mind","godel argument","quantum consciousness","consciousness"]},{"term":"Eric Schwitzgebel","def":"Analytic philosopher (1968). We have no reliable test for whether a machine feels, so we will soon build systems whose minds we can't judge.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of California, Riverside, who writes on belief, introspection and consciousness. He argues that introspection is unreliable and that we will soon face AI systems whose consciousness and moral status are genuinely uncertain.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":[],"thinkers":["schwitzgebel"],"tags":["ai consciousness","machine consciousness","moral status of ai","introspection"]},{"term":"George Santayana","def":"American Pragmatism philosopher (1863–1952). Matter does all the work; consciousness is a powerless light that matter kindles.","more":"A Spanish-born philosopher, poet and novelist who taught at Harvard. He was a materialist about how the world works, yet held that spirit, the light of awareness, is real but causes nothing. He is also known for the line that those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Naturalism","Epiphenomenalism"],"thinkers":["santayana"],"tags":["epiphenomenalism","naturalism","life of reason","those who cannot remember the past"]},{"term":"Nicolas Malebranche","def":"Modern European philosopher (1638–1715). God is the only true cause; mind and body never act on each other, they only give God the occasion to act.","more":"A French priest and philosopher who developed Descartes' ideas. To solve the puzzle of how mind and body interact, he argued that they don't: God produces each bodily movement on the occasion of our willing it, and each sensation on the occasion of a bodily change.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Cartesianism","Occasionalism"],"thinkers":["malebranche"],"tags":["occasionalism","vision in god","cartesianism","search after truth"]},{"term":"Patanjali","def":"Indian philosopher (200 BCE). The true self is a pure witness, quite separate from the restless mind it watches.","more":"The compiler of the Yoga Sutras, the classic text of the Yoga school of Indian philosophy; his dates are uncertain (estimates range from about 200 BCE to 400 CE). Drawing on Samkhya, he teaches that the self (purusha) is pure awareness, and yoga is the stilling of the mind so the self can rest in itself.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Yoga"],"thinkers":["patanjali"],"tags":["yoga sutras","yoga","samkhya","purusha","prakriti"]},{"term":"Marya Schechtman","def":"Analytic philosopher (1960). You are the person your life story makes you: the self is constituted by the narrative you live by.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Illinois Chicago. She argues that questions about who we are, and what we care about in surviving, are answered by the self-narratives that organise our lives, not by memory links alone.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Narrative identity"],"thinkers":["schechtman"],"tags":["narrative self","narrative identity","personal identity","constitution of selves"]},{"term":"Sam Harris","def":"Analytic philosopher (1967). Free will is an illusion, and so is the self that seems to sit behind your thoughts.","more":"An American neuroscientist, author and podcaster. He argues that our choices arise from brain events we neither control nor notice, so free will is an illusion, and that meditation shows the sense of a self behind experience is illusory too.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Free will skepticism"],"thinkers":["harris"],"tags":["free will illusion","no-self","meditation","waking up","moral landscape"]},{"term":"Daniel Wegner","def":"Analytic philosopher (1948–2013). The feeling that you willed an action is the mind's guess afterwards, not its cause.","more":"An American social psychologist at Harvard. In The Illusion of Conscious Will he gathered experiments showing that people feel they caused actions they didn't cause, and don't feel they caused ones they did.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Social psychology"],"thinkers":["wegner"],"tags":["illusion of conscious will","free will illusion","conscious will","social psychology"]},{"term":"Baron d'Holbach","def":"Modern European philosopher (1723–1789). Humans are material beings, and every choice is fixed by causes we don't control.","more":"A German-born French philosopher of the Enlightenment whose Paris salon gathered Diderot and other radicals. His System of Nature (1770) defended atheism, materialism and strict determinism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Materialism","Determinism","Enlightenment"],"thinkers":["holbach"],"tags":["holbach","paul-henri thiry","materialism","determinism","atheism","system of nature"]},{"term":"Neil Levy","def":"Analytic philosopher (1967). Luck goes all the way down, so no one is morally responsible in the deep sense.","more":"An Australian philosopher at Macquarie University and Oxford, working on free will, neuroethics and moral psychology. He argues that luck in who we are and in what happens at the moment of choice rules out moral responsibility.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Free will skepticism"],"thinkers":["levy"],"tags":["luck","hard luck","free will skepticism","moral responsibility skepticism"]},{"term":"Gregg Caruso","def":"Analytic philosopher (1974). Since no one deserves punishment, treat crime like public health: prevent, protect, and restrain only as needed.","more":"An American philosopher at SUNY Corning who defends free will skepticism and its practical upshot: replacing retributive punishment with a public health-quarantine model of criminal justice.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Free will skepticism"],"thinkers":["caruso"],"tags":["free will skepticism","public health quarantine model","punishment","hard incompatibilism"]},{"term":"Bruce Waller","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946–2023). Moral responsibility should be abolished: blame does harm, and looking at causes does good.","more":"An American philosopher at Youngstown State University. He argues that the system of moral responsibility is unjustified and harmful, and that we would act better if we gave it up entirely.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Free will skepticism"],"thinkers":["waller"],"tags":["against moral responsibility","moral responsibility skepticism","free will skepticism"]},{"term":"Mark Balaguer","def":"Analytic philosopher (1964). Whether we have the open choices free will needs is a question for science, and it's still open.","more":"An American philosopher at California State University, Los Angeles, working on free will and the philosophy of mathematics. He argues that whether libertarian free will exists depends on an unanswered empirical question about indeterminacy in the brain.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":[],"thinkers":["balaguer"],"tags":["libertarian free will","free will as an open scientific problem","torn decisions","indeterminism"]},{"term":"Roderick Chisholm","def":"Analytic philosopher (1916–1999). When we act freely, we ourselves cause our actions, as prime movers unmoved.","more":"An American philosopher at Brown University, influential in epistemology and metaphysics. His lecture 'Human Freedom and the Self' revived the theory of agent causation.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Agent causation"],"thinkers":["chisholm"],"tags":["agent causation","libertarian free will","foundationalism","epistemology","human freedom and the self"]},{"term":"Carl Ginet","def":"Analytic philosopher (1932). A free choice is uncaused; you can explain it by your reasons without anything causing it.","more":"An American philosopher at Cornell who developed a non-causal theory of free action and defended the principle that responsibility requires being able to do otherwise.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Non-causal libertarianism"],"thinkers":["ginet"],"tags":["non-causal libertarianism","libertarian free will","on action","free will"]},{"term":"Hugh McCann","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942–2016). A decision isn't caused at all: it is something you do, intentional through and through.","more":"An American philosopher at Texas A&M University who argues that decisions are free, uncaused acts that carry their own intentionality, and who writes on divine agency.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Non-causal libertarianism"],"thinkers":["mccann"],"tags":["non-causal libertarianism","volition","libertarian free will","action theory"]},{"term":"David Widerker","def":"Analytic philosopher (1944). Frankfurt's cases fail: if your choice isn't settled in advance, no device could know what you'll do.","more":"An Israeli philosopher at Bar-Ilan University best known for the dilemma defence of the principle of alternative possibilities against Frankfurt-style cases.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Libertarianism"],"thinkers":["widerker"],"tags":["frankfurt cases","principle of alternative possibilities","libertarian free will","dilemma defense"]},{"term":"Gary Watson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1943). You act freely when you act from what you value, not just from what you happen to want.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Southern California whose papers on free agency and on responsibility are classics of the field.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["watson"],"tags":["valuational theory","free agency","reactive attitudes","compatibilism","real self"]},{"term":"R. Jay Wallace","def":"Analytic philosopher (1957). Holding someone responsible is holding them to expectations we care about, with resentment and gratitude.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of California, Berkeley, who developed P. F. Strawson's idea that responsibility is grounded in our reactive emotions into a full theory.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Compatibilism","Strawsonian responsibility"],"thinkers":["wallace"],"tags":["reactive attitudes","responsibility and the moral sentiments","moral responsibility","blame"]},{"term":"Moritz Schlick","def":"Analytic philosopher (1882–1936). Freedom is the opposite of compulsion, not of causation; blame is for changing what people do.","more":"A German philosopher who founded the Vienna Circle of logical positivists. In ethics he argued that the free will problem is a confusion of laws of nature with laws that compel, and that responsibility is about whom punishment and praise can influence.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Logical positivism","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["schlick"],"tags":["vienna circle","logical positivism","compatibilism","freedom and causation"]},{"term":"L. E. J. Brouwer","def":"Philosophy of mathematics philosopher (1881–1966). Mathematics is a creation of the human mind, and a number exists only once it can be built in thought.","more":"A Dutch mathematician who did major work in topology and founded intuitionism. He held that mathematics is a mental activity, not the discovery of a ready-made realm, and so rejected proofs that only show something cannot fail to exist.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Intuitionism"],"thinkers":["brouwer"],"tags":["intuitionism","mathematical intuitionism","constructivism","fixed point theorem"]},{"term":"Michael Dummett","def":"Analytic philosopher (1925–2011). To understand a statement is to know what would prove it, so truth cannot outrun what we could in principle know.","more":"An Oxford philosopher of language and logic and a leading reader of Frege. He argued that many old metaphysical disputes are really disputes about meaning, and defended intuitionist mathematics. He also campaigned against racism in Britain.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Anti-realism","Intuitionism"],"thinkers":["dummett"],"tags":["anti-realism","semantic anti-realism","verificationism","intuitionism","frege"]},{"term":"Vasubandhu","def":"Indian Buddhist philosopher (350–430). What we take to be an outside world is consciousness appearing as objects, as it does in a dream.","more":"A Buddhist philosopher from Gandhara who first wrote a great summary of Abhidharma thought and later, with his half-brother Asanga, founded the Yogacara ('consciousness only') school. He argued that dreams show experience can seem to be of objects with no objects there.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Yogacara","Abhidharma"],"thinkers":["vasubandhu"],"tags":["yogacara","mind-only","consciousness-only","vijnanavada","buddhist idealism"]},{"term":"Akshapada Gautama","def":"Indian (Nyaya) philosopher (150). The world of objects is real and knowable through reliable means of knowing, such as perception and inference.","more":"The traditional author of the Nyaya-sutra, founding text of the Nyaya school of logic and epistemology; little is known of his life. With his commentator Vatsyayana, he set out the valid means of knowledge and defended realism against Buddhist critics who said only parts, or only consciousness, exist.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Nyaya"],"thinkers":["akshapada"],"tags":["aksapada","nyaya","nyaya sutras","realism","pramana","indian logic"]},{"term":"Alvin Goldman","def":"Analytic philosopher (1938–2024). A belief is justified when it comes from a reliable process, one that usually gets things right.","more":"An American epistemologist at Rutgers and a founder of reliabilism. He tied knowledge to the causes and processes behind belief rather than to reasons we can cite, and brought cognitive science and social questions into epistemology.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Reliabilism","Naturalized epistemology"],"thinkers":["goldman"],"tags":["reliabilism","process reliabilism","causal theory of knowing","social epistemology"]},{"term":"Carneades","def":"Hellenistic philosopher (214 BCE–129 BCE). Nothing can be known for certain, but some impressions are persuasive enough to live by.","more":"Head of Plato's Academy in its skeptical phase and a famous debater. On an embassy to Rome in 155 BCE he argued for justice one day and against it the next. He attacked the Stoics' claim to certain knowledge and offered a scale of 'persuasive' impressions to guide action.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Academic skepticism"],"thinkers":["carneades"],"tags":["carneades of cyrene","academic skepticism","probabilism","skeptical academy","plausible impressions"]},{"term":"Marcus Tullius Cicero","def":"Hellenistic philosopher (106 BCE–43 BCE). Follow what seems most probable, and never claim more certainty than you have.","more":"Roman statesman, orator and philosopher who brought Greek philosophy into Latin. In philosophy he called himself an Academic: he weighed the arguments of Stoics, Epicureans and others and accepted what seemed most probable, without claiming certain knowledge.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Academic skepticism"],"thinkers":["cicero"],"tags":["tully","academic skepticism","roman republic","on duties","de officiis","natural law"]},{"term":"Ned Markosian","def":"Analytic philosopher (1961). Only present things exist, and only some collections of things make up a further object.","more":"An American metaphysician who has written influential defences of presentism and of restricted composition, the common-sense view that some but not all collections of things make up wholes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Presentism","Restricted composition"],"thinkers":["markosian"],"tags":["presentism","special composition question","mereology","time"]},{"term":"Daniel Z. Korman","def":"Analytic philosopher (1979). The objects we ordinarily believe in, like tables and cats, are real, and the strange ones philosophers invent are not.","more":"An American metaphysician who defends conservatism: the view that our everyday beliefs about which objects exist are by and large correct. He answers arguments that would either add bizarre objects to the world or remove ordinary ones.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Conservatism about objects"],"thinkers":["korman"],"tags":["ordinary objects","objects","mereology","debunking arguments"]},{"term":"Crispin Wright","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942). Truth can work differently in different areas of talk, and some of what we know rests on things we are entitled to trust without proof.","more":"A British philosopher at St Andrews, Stirling and NYU. He revived Frege's view that numbers are objects knowable through logic and definitions, developed a pluralist theory of truth, and defends a hinge epistemology on which we are entitled to trust basic assumptions we cannot prove.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Neo-Fregeanism","Truth pluralism","Hinge epistemology"],"thinkers":["crispin_wright"],"tags":["truth pluralism","minimalism","superassertibility","neo-fregeanism","anti-realism"]},{"term":"Michael P. Lynch","def":"Analytic philosopher (1966). Truth is one thing, but it shows up in different ways in different subjects.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Connecticut. He is the main defender of truth pluralism and writes on why truth matters for democracy and in the age of the internet.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Truth pluralism"],"thinkers":["lynch"],"tags":["truth pluralism","alethic pluralism","functionalism about truth","know-it-all society"]},{"term":"Jonathan Schaffer","def":"Analytic philosopher (1970). The whole universe is the one basic object, and its parts depend on it, not the other way round.","more":"An American metaphysician at Rutgers. He revived monism in a new form: there are many things, but the cosmos as a whole is fundamental and everything else depends on it. He also works on grounding and causation.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Priority monism"],"thinkers":["schaffer"],"tags":["priority monism","monism","grounding","contrastivism"]},{"term":"Steven Savitt","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942). Once we are clear about spacetime, presentism and eternalism stop looking like rivals.","more":"A philosopher of physics at the University of British Columbia who writes on time in relativity. He argues that the familiar slogans in the debate over which times are real are ambiguous, and that taking spacetime structure seriously dissolves much of the dispute.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of physics"],"thinkers":["savitt"],"tags":["presentism and eternalism","relativity","philosophy of time","spacetime"]},{"term":"Mauro Dorato","def":"Analytic philosopher (1956). The fight between presentists and eternalists is not about the world at all, once words are made precise.","more":"An Italian philosopher of physics at Roma Tre University. He works on time, relativity and quantum theory, and argues that the presentism versus eternalism debate has no real ontological content.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of physics"],"thinkers":["dorato"],"tags":["presentism and eternalism","philosophy of time","relativity","becoming"]},{"term":"Thomas Kuhn","def":"Analytic philosopher (1922–1996). Science moves through paradigms, and after a revolution scientists in a sense work in a different world.","more":"An American physicist turned historian and philosopher of science. His book The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962) argued that science alternates between normal puzzle-solving within a paradigm and revolutions that replace it, and made 'paradigm shift' an everyday phrase.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Historical philosophy of science"],"thinkers":["kuhn"],"tags":["paradigm shift","paradigm","scientific revolutions","incommensurability","normal science"]},{"term":"Ernst Mach","def":"Modern European philosopher (1838–1916). Science is an economical summary of experience, and there are no hidden causes or atoms behind it.","more":"An Austrian physicist and philosopher; the Mach number in aviation is named after him. He held that science should describe the connections among sensations as economically as possible, doubted the reality of atoms, and inspired the Vienna Circle and the young Einstein.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Positivism","Empiricism"],"thinkers":["mach"],"tags":["positivism","mach's principle","economy of thought","empirio-criticism","phenomenalism"]},{"term":"Pierre Duhem","def":"Modern European philosopher (1861–1916). A physical theory summarises and organises experimental laws; it is not a picture of hidden reality.","more":"A French physicist, historian and philosopher of science. He argued that physical theories are mathematical systems for representing experimental laws, that no experiment can test a single hypothesis in isolation, and that medieval science paved the way for Galileo.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Instrumentalism","Philosophy of physics"],"thinkers":["duhem"],"tags":["duhem-quine thesis","underdetermination","instrumentalism","holism","aim and structure of physical theory"]},{"term":"F. H. Bradley","def":"Modern European philosopher (1846–1924). Reality is a single whole, and the separate things, relations and times we see are only appearance.","more":"An Oxford philosopher and the leading British idealist. In Appearance and Reality (1893) he argued that relations, space and time are self-contradictory, so reality must be one all-inclusive experience, the Absolute. Russell and Moore rose by rebelling against him.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["British idealism","Monism"],"thinkers":["bradley"],"tags":["british idealism","absolute idealism","appearance and reality","monism","regress"]},{"term":"Ernest Sosa","def":"Analytic philosopher (1940). Knowledge is a true belief that is right because of the believer's skill, like an archer's well-aimed hit.","more":"A Cuban-born American epistemologist at Brown and Rutgers. He founded virtue epistemology, which treats knowing as a kind of skilled performance, and introduced the 'safety' condition on knowledge.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Virtue epistemology"],"thinkers":["sosa"],"tags":["virtue epistemology","virtue reliabilism","aptness","animal knowledge","safety"]},{"term":"Linda Zagzebski","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946). Knowing is like acting well: a true belief reached through intellectual virtue and because of it.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Oklahoma. She modelled knowledge on Aristotle's ethics of virtue, argued that any analysis leaving a gap between justification and truth will face Gettier cases, and writes on exemplars and religious epistemology.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Virtue epistemology"],"thinkers":["zagzebski"],"tags":["virtue epistemology","virtues of the mind","responsibilism","divine motivation theory"]},{"term":"Keith Lehrer","def":"Analytic philosopher (1936). Knowledge is justified true belief whose justification no further truth would defeat.","more":"An American epistemologist at the University of Arizona. With Thomas Paxson he proposed the defeasibility answer to Gettier: a belief is knowledge only if no truth you are missing would undermine your reasons. He also defended a coherence theory of justification.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Defeasibility theory","Coherentism"],"thinkers":["lehrer"],"tags":["defeasibility theory","coherentism","truetemp","gettier problem","justified true belief"]},{"term":"Brian Weatherson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1974). A simple, powerful theory can be worth keeping even when an intuition seems to count against it.","more":"An Australian philosopher at the University of Michigan who works on epistemology, decision theory and philosophical method. He argued that the Gettier intuition might be the thing to give up, rather than the justified-true-belief theory of knowledge.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytic epistemology"],"thinkers":["weatherson"],"tags":["gettier problem","reflective equilibrium","theory over intuition","jtb theory"]},{"term":"Ross Cameron","def":"Analytic philosopher (1980). Past and future are real, yet the present is special and time genuinely passes.","more":"A Scottish metaphysician at the University of Virginia. His book The Moving Spotlight (2015) gives the leading modern defence of the view that all times exist but one of them is objectively present, and that presentness moves.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moving spotlight theory"],"thinkers":["cameron_r"],"tags":["moving spotlight","moving spotlight theory","time","philosophy of time"]},{"term":"Phil Dowe","def":"Analytic philosopher (1960). A cause passes something physical, like energy or momentum, along to its effect.","more":"An Australian philosopher of science at the Australian National University. He developed the conserved quantity theory of causation, which says causal processes are those that carry conserved quantities from one place to another.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Process theory of causation"],"thinkers":["dowe"],"tags":["conserved quantity theory","causal process","physical causation","causation"]},{"term":"Alfred North Whitehead","def":"Analytic philosopher (1861–1947). Reality is made of events, not things, and God grows along with the world.","more":"English mathematician who co-wrote Principia Mathematica with Russell, then moved to Harvard and built a metaphysics of process. His God offers each moment its best possibility and takes up everything that happens into itself.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Process philosophy"],"thinkers":["whitehead"],"tags":["process philosophy","process and reality","process theology","actual occasions","principia mathematica"]},{"term":"Charles Hartshorne","def":"Analytic philosopher (1897–2000). God includes the world and is enriched by it, and is powerful but not all-controlling.","more":"American philosopher who developed Whitehead's ideas into process theology. He revived the ontological argument and defended panentheism, the view that the world is within God.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Process theology","Panentheism"],"thinkers":["hartshorne"],"tags":["process theology","panentheism","dipolar theism","ontological argument","process philosophy"]},{"term":"Karl Christian Friedrich Krause","def":"Modern European philosopher (1781–1832). Everything is in God, but God is more than everything.","more":"German idealist philosopher who coined the word panentheism. Largely ignored in Germany, his ideas became very influential in Spain and Latin America.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["German Idealism","Panentheism"],"thinkers":["krause"],"tags":["panentheism","krausism","pantheism","idealism"]},{"term":"Clark Pinnock","def":"Christian theology philosopher (1937–2010). God takes risks: he responds to our free choices instead of knowing them in advance.","more":"Canadian evangelical theologian who became the best-known champion of open theism. He argued that a loving God enters into real give-and-take with creatures.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Open theism"],"thinkers":["pinnock"],"tags":["open theism","openness of god","free will theism","risky god"]},{"term":"William Hasker","def":"Analytic philosopher (1935). If we are truly free, not even God can know what we will choose.","more":"American philosopher of religion who gave open theism its main philosophical defence. He argued that foreknowledge of free choices is impossible, even for God.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Open theism","Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["hasker"],"tags":["open theism","divine foreknowledge","emergent dualism","foreknowledge and freedom"]},{"term":"Ramanuja","def":"Indian philosopher (1017–1137). Souls and the world are real, and they are the body of God, who is their inner self.","more":"South Indian philosopher and teacher of devotion to Vishnu. Against Shankara he argued that the world and souls are real, and that they relate to God as a body relates to its self.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Vishishtadvaita Vedanta"],"thinkers":["ramanuja"],"tags":["ramanujacharya","vishishtadvaita","qualified nondualism","vedanta","bhakti","vaishnavism"]},{"term":"Madhva","def":"Indian philosopher (1238–1317). God, souls and matter are forever distinct, and God alone is independent.","more":"South Indian philosopher who founded the dualist school of Vedanta. He taught that Vishnu is the one independent reality, and souls are eternally different from him and from each other.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Dvaita Vedanta"],"thinkers":["madhva"],"tags":["madhvacharya","dvaita","dualism","dvaita vedanta","vedanta","vaishnavism"]},{"term":"Mahavira","def":"Indian philosopher (599 BCE–527 BCE). No god made the world; each soul can free itself from karma by its own effort.","more":"The twenty-fourth Tirthankara, or ford-maker, of Jainism, an older contemporary of the Buddha. He taught strict non-violence and that every soul can become free and all-knowing.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Jainism"],"thinkers":["mahavira"],"tags":["vardhamana","jainism","tirthankara","ahimsa","jain","mahavir"]},{"term":"Umasvati","def":"Indian philosopher (200). Right faith, right knowledge and right conduct together free the soul from karma.","more":"Jain philosopher, dated anywhere from the 2nd to the 5th century CE, who wrote the first systematic summary of Jain teaching in Sanskrit. Both main Jain sects accept his work.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Jainism"],"thinkers":["umasvati"],"tags":["umaswati","tattvartha sutra","jainism","three jewels","jain","ratnatraya"]},{"term":"Dharmakirti","def":"Indian philosopher (600–660). Only perception and reasoning give knowledge, and they show no creator exists.","more":"Indian Buddhist logician whose theory of knowledge shaped Indian and Tibetan philosophy for centuries. He argued that the Buddha's teaching can be tested by reason, and that a permanent creator God is impossible.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Buddhist epistemology"],"thinkers":["dharmakirti"],"tags":["buddhist logic","pramana","epistemology","dignaga","buddhist epistemology"]},{"term":"Swami Vivekananda","def":"Indian philosopher (1863–1902). All religions are paths to one truth, which is the self's oneness with Brahman.","more":"Indian monk, disciple of Ramakrishna, who brought Vedanta and yoga to the West after his famous speech at the 1893 Parliament of Religions in Chicago.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Advaita Vedanta","Neo-Vedanta"],"thinkers":["vivekananda"],"tags":["vedanta","ramakrishna","religious pluralism","neo-vedanta","hinduism"]},{"term":"Paul Tillich","def":"Christian theology philosopher (1886–1965). God is not a being among others but being-itself, the ground of all that is.","more":"German-American Protestant theologian who fled the Nazis in 1933. He taught that faith is 'ultimate concern' and that all talk of God is symbolic.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Existentialist theology"],"thinkers":["tillich"],"tags":["ground of being","being-itself","ultimate concern","courage to be","systematic theology"]},{"term":"Karl Jaspers","def":"Continental philosopher (1883–1969). Transcendence can never be grasped as an object, only glimpsed in ciphers.","more":"German psychiatrist turned philosopher, a founder of existentialism. He defended a 'philosophical faith' open to transcendence but tied to no church or creed.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Existentialism"],"thinkers":["jaspers"],"tags":["existentialism","limit situations","axial age","transcendence","ciphers"]},{"term":"Friedrich Schleiermacher","def":"Christian theology philosopher (1768–1834). Religion is not proof or morality but a feeling of absolute dependence.","more":"German theologian often called the father of modern Protestant theology. He grounded religion in immediate feeling rather than in proofs or creeds.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Romanticism","Liberal theology"],"thinkers":["schleiermacher"],"tags":["feeling of absolute dependence","liberal theology","hermeneutics","romanticism"]},{"term":"Marilyn McCord Adams","def":"Analytic philosopher (1943–2017). God can make even the worst horrors worth it for the person who suffers them.","more":"American philosopher and Episcopal priest, a leading expert on medieval philosophy. She argued that the problem of evil must be met for every single victim, not just on the whole.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["adams_mm"],"tags":["horrendous evils","problem of evil","theodicy","christ","universalism"]},{"term":"Eleonore Stump","def":"Analytic philosopher (1947). Suffering can be redeemed by the closeness to God it makes possible.","more":"American philosopher and leading interpreter of Aquinas. She approaches the problem of suffering through stories such as Job and Samson, focusing on love and relationship.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Thomism","Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["stump"],"tags":["problem of evil","wandering in darkness","theodicy","aquinas","suffering"]},{"term":"Brian Davies","def":"Analytic philosopher (1951). God is not a moral agent like us, so asking why he did not stop evil is a confusion.","more":"English Dominican friar and philosopher who teaches in New York. Following Aquinas, he argues that God is the cause of all being, not a very good person with duties.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Thomism","Classical theism"],"thinkers":["davies_b"],"tags":["problem of evil","classical theism","thomism","god not a moral agent","reality of god and the problem of evil"]},{"term":"Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari","def":"Islamic philosopher (874–936). Whatever God does is just, because no one stands above God to command him.","more":"Muslim theologian from Basra who broke with the rationalist Mu'tazilites and founded the Ash'arite school, which became the main school of Sunni theology.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Ash'arite kalam"],"thinkers":["ashari"],"tags":["al-ashari","ashari","asharism","ash'arism","kalam","divine command","islamic theology"]},{"term":"Nicholas Wolterstorff","def":"Analytic philosopher (1932). Belief in God can be rational without being based on argument.","more":"American philosopher at Calvin College and Yale who, with Plantinga and Alston, founded Reformed epistemology. He also wrote on justice, art and grief.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Reformed epistemology"],"thinkers":["wolterstorff"],"tags":["reformed epistemology","justice","rationality of belief","plantinga"]},{"term":"Karl Rahner","def":"Christian theology philosopher (1904–1984). Sincere people of other faiths can be saved through Christ without knowing it.","more":"German Jesuit, one of the most influential Catholic theologians of the twentieth century. His idea of 'anonymous Christians' shaped the Church's opening to other religions.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Transcendental Thomism"],"thinkers":["rahner"],"tags":["anonymous christians","inclusivism","catholic theology","supernatural existential"]},{"term":"Antony Flew","def":"Analytic philosopher (1923–2010). After a lifetime of atheism, he concluded the evidence points to a God who does not intervene.","more":"English philosopher famous for decades of arguments for atheism, including 'Theology and Falsification' (1950). In 2004 he announced that he now believed in a deist God.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["flew"],"tags":["theology and falsification","presumption of atheism","there is a god","deism","atheism"]},{"term":"D. Z. Phillips","def":"Analytic philosopher (1934–2006). Religious belief is a way of life, not a theory, and theodicies are a moral mistake.","more":"Welsh philosopher, the leading Wittgensteinian philosopher of religion. He argued that God-talk has its sense within religious practice, and that trying to justify God's allowing evil degrades both God and victims.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Wittgensteinian philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["phillips_dz"],"tags":["wittgensteinian fideism","fideism","religious language","problem of evil","wittgenstein"]},{"term":"J. L. Schellenberg","def":"Analytic philosopher (1959). A perfectly loving God would make sure no one who is open to him fails to believe.","more":"Canadian philosopher who launched the modern hiddenness argument against God in 1993. He also defends a non-traditional 'skeptical religion' of imaginative faith.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["schellenberg"],"tags":["divine hiddenness","hiddenness argument","atheism","skepticism"]},{"term":"Sigmund Freud","def":"Modern European philosopher (1856–1939). God is a wish for a protecting father, projected onto the universe.","more":"Austrian neurologist who founded psychoanalysis. He explained religion as an illusion: a belief held because we wish it were true.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Psychoanalysis"],"thinkers":["freud"],"tags":["psychoanalysis","future of an illusion","wish fulfilment","projection","unconscious"]},{"term":"W. K. Clifford","def":"Modern European philosopher (1845–1879). It is always wrong to believe anything on insufficient evidence.","more":"English mathematician and philosopher who died at 33. His essay on the ethics of belief, with its sinking-ship example, is still the classic statement of evidentialism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Evidentialism"],"thinkers":["clifford"],"tags":["ethics of belief","evidentialism","insufficient evidence","william james"]},{"term":"Philip Kitcher","def":"Analytic philosopher (1947). Religions are learned the same way, so none has a better claim than the rest.","more":"British-American philosopher of science at Columbia University. He argues for a secular humanism that keeps some of the values of religion without its supernatural claims.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of science","Secular humanism"],"thinkers":["kitcher"],"tags":["religious diversity","secular humanism","life after faith","pragmatism","philosophy of science"]},{"term":"Michael Martin","def":"Analytic philosopher (1932–2015). There is no good reason to believe in God, and good reason to disbelieve.","more":"American philosopher at Boston University who wrote the most thorough modern defence of atheism, answering the arguments for God one by one.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["martin_m"],"tags":["atheism","atheism a philosophical justification","arguments against god","naturalism"]},{"term":"Paul Draper","def":"Analytic philosopher (1957). Suffering fits a world without God better than one with God, but the question stays open.","more":"American philosopher at Purdue University, known for an influential evidential argument from pain and pleasure. He calls himself a practising agnostic.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["draper"],"tags":["problem of evil","evidential argument from evil","hypothesis of indifference","atheism"]},{"term":"Anthony Kenny","def":"Analytic philosopher (1931–2026). Neither the case for God nor the case against has been made, so the honest view is agnosticism.","more":"English philosopher, a former Catholic priest, later Master of Balliol College, Oxford. He wrote widely on Aquinas, Wittgenstein and the history of philosophy.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion","History of philosophy"],"thinkers":["kenny"],"tags":["agnosticism","aquinas","wittgenstein","god of the philosophers"]},{"term":"Richard Feldman","def":"Analytic philosopher (1948). When equally informed people disagree, each should suspend judgment.","more":"American epistemologist at the University of Rochester who, with Earl Conee, developed modern evidentialism. He applied it to disagreement, including religious disagreement.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Evidentialism","Epistemology"],"thinkers":["feldman_r"],"tags":["peer disagreement","epistemology of disagreement","suspension of judgment","evidentialism"]},{"term":"Murray Rothbard","def":"Austrian School philosopher (1926–1995). Every person owns themselves, so the state, which lives by taxing and ruling people without consent, is organised theft and should be replaced by markets.","more":"An American economist and political philosopher of the Austrian School. He combined natural-rights ethics with free-market economics and coined the modern form of anarcho-capitalism. Police, courts and defence, he argued, could all be supplied by competing private firms.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Anarcho-capitalism","Natural rights libertarianism"],"thinkers":["rothbard"],"tags":["anarcho-capitalism","libertarianism","austrian economics","self-ownership","non-aggression principle"]},{"term":"Peter Kropotkin","def":"Modern European philosopher (1842–1921). Cooperation, not competition, is what lets living things thrive, so people can run society through free communes that share what they produce.","more":"A Russian prince turned geographer and revolutionary. Studying animals in Siberia convinced him that mutual aid is a major force in evolution. He argued for abolishing the state and private property and replacing them with voluntary federations of communes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Anarcho-communism","Social anarchism"],"thinkers":["kropotkin"],"tags":["anarcho-communism","anarchist communism","mutual aid","anarchism","conquest of bread"]},{"term":"Benjamin Tucker","def":"Modern European philosopher (1854–1939). Each person should be free to do anything that does not invade others, with all public affairs left to individuals and voluntary associations.","more":"An American editor who ran the anarchist journal Liberty from 1881 to 1908. He blended Proudhon's mutualism with the egoism of Max Stirner, whose book he published in English. He thought state-backed monopolies in money, land, tariffs and patents were what kept workers poor.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Individualist anarchism","Mutualism"],"thinkers":["tucker"],"tags":["individualist anarchism","anarchism","mutualism","liberty","equal liberty"]},{"term":"Pierre-Joseph Proudhon","def":"Modern European philosopher (1809–1865). Property that lets owners live off others' work is theft, and government should give way to free contracts among workers' associations.","more":"A French printer and self-taught writer, the first person to call himself an anarchist. He attacked income from rent and interest while defending a worker's possession of what they use. He proposed a people's bank offering free credit and a federation of self-governing communities.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Mutualism","Anarchism"],"thinkers":["proudhon"],"tags":["property is theft","mutualism","anarchism","what is property","father of anarchism"]},{"term":"G. A. Cohen","def":"Analytic philosopher (1941–2009). Inequalities that come from luck rather than choice are unjust, and a just society would be socialist, with equality and community at its heart.","more":"A Canadian-born philosopher at Oxford who grew up in a communist Jewish family in Montreal. He rebuilt Marxism with the tools of analytic philosophy, then took on Nozick and Rawls, arguing that justice demands more equality than either allowed.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Analytical Marxism","Luck egalitarianism"],"thinkers":["ga_cohen"],"tags":["luck egalitarianism","analytical marxism","why not socialism","egalitarianism","socialism"]},{"term":"Robert Filmer","def":"Modern European philosopher (1588–1653). Kings rule as fathers rule their families, by an authority God gave to Adam and his heirs, not by the people's consent.","more":"An English gentleman and royalist writer. His Patriarcha, published after his death, argued that royal power descends from Adam's God-given authority over his family. John Locke wrote his First Treatise of Government to refute him.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Divine right of kings","Patriarchalism"],"thinkers":["filmer"],"tags":["patriarcha","divine right of kings","absolutism","patriarchalism"]},{"term":"Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet","def":"Christian theology philosopher (1627–1704). Royal authority is sacred, fatherly and absolute because it comes from God, though kings answer to God for how they use it.","more":"A French bishop, famous preacher and tutor to the son of Louis XIV. He drew a full theory of absolute monarchy from the Bible. He held that subjects must obey even bad kings, while kings must rule for the good of their people and fear God's judgment.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Divine right of kings","Catholic theology"],"thinkers":["bossuet"],"tags":["divine right of kings","absolutism","absolute monarchy","politics drawn from holy scripture"]},{"term":"A. John Simmons","def":"Analytic philosopher (1950). No state has shown that its citizens owe it obedience, though a state can still be good for what it does.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Virginia. He examined each main theory of political obligation, from consent to fair play to gratitude, and concluded that none works for most citizens. He separates a state being justified from its having a right to rule.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophical anarchism","Lockean liberalism"],"thinkers":["simmons"],"tags":["philosophical anarchism","political obligation","legitimacy","moral principles and political obligations"]},{"term":"Philip Pettit","def":"Analytic philosopher (1945). You are free only when no one has the power to interfere in your life at will, even if they never use it.","more":"An Irish philosopher who has taught in Australia and at Princeton. He revived the old republican idea of freedom as not being under anyone's arbitrary power. He uses it to argue for democratic control of government and for laws that protect people from masters, bosses and partners.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Republicanism","Freedom as non-domination"],"thinkers":["pettit"],"tags":["republicanism","neo-republicanism","non-domination","freedom as non-domination"]},{"term":"Quentin Skinner","def":"Analytic philosopher (1940). Living at the mercy of someone else's will takes away your freedom, even if they never actually interfere.","more":"An English historian of political thought at Cambridge and later Queen Mary, London. He showed how to read old texts in their own context and recovered the 'neo-Roman' idea of liberty from Machiavelli and the English republicans. He holds that both interference and dependence make people unfree.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Cambridge School of intellectual history","Republicanism"],"thinkers":["skinner"],"tags":["republicanism","liberty before liberalism","cambridge school","freedom as non-domination","history of ideas"]},{"term":"Eduard Bernstein","def":"Modern European philosopher (1850–1932). Socialism should come step by step through democracy and reform, not through revolution.","more":"A German social democrat and close friend of Engels. He argued that capitalism was not about to collapse and that workers could win socialism gradually through votes, unions and cooperatives. His 'revisionism' shaped the moderate social democratic parties of Europe.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Social democracy","Revisionism"],"thinkers":["bernstein"],"tags":["revisionism","social democracy","evolutionary socialism","reformism","democratic socialism"]},{"term":"Thomas Pogge","def":"Analytic philosopher (1953). Rich countries do not merely fail to help the global poor; they impose rules that keep them poor, so justice must be global.","more":"A German philosopher who studied under Rawls and teaches at Yale. He argues that the global economic order, shaped by rich countries, causes much world poverty. He has proposed practical reforms such as a global resources dividend and a fund to reward new medicines.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Cosmopolitanism","Global justice"],"thinkers":["pogge"],"tags":["global justice","world poverty and human rights","global poverty","cosmopolitanism","negative duties"]},{"term":"David Miller","def":"Analytic philosopher (1946). Nations are real ethical communities, and social justice inside them rests on need, desert and equality together.","more":"An English political theorist at Oxford. He defends the idea that people have special duties to fellow members of their nation and that national self-government matters. On justice he holds that people deserve rewards for their contributions, alongside meeting needs.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Liberal nationalism","Pluralist theory of justice"],"thinkers":["david_miller"],"tags":["nationalism","liberal nationalism","on nationality","social justice","national responsibility"]},{"term":"Ronald Dworkin","def":"Analytic philosopher (1931–2013). Government must treat everyone with equal concern and respect, so people should bear the costs of their choices but not of their bad luck.","more":"An American legal philosopher who taught at Oxford and New York University. He argued against Hart that law includes moral principles, not just rules. In political philosophy he defended equality of resources, which corrects for brute bad luck but holds people responsible for their own choices.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Liberal egalitarianism","Legal interpretivism"],"thinkers":["dworkin"],"tags":["equality of resources","law's empire","rights as trumps","integrity","equal concern and respect"]},{"term":"Elizabeth Anderson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1959). Equality is about people relating as equals, free from oppression and domination, not about compensating everyone for bad luck.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of Michigan. Her 1999 paper attacked luck egalitarianism and argued that the point of equality is a society of equals. She has also written on racial segregation and on the power employers hold over workers.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Relational egalitarianism","Pragmatism"],"thinkers":["anderson_e"],"tags":["relational egalitarianism","democratic equality","private government","egalitarianism"]},{"term":"David D. Friedman","def":"Analytic philosopher (1945). Everything the state does, including law and defence, could be done better by competing private firms.","more":"An American economist and legal scholar, the son of Milton Friedman. Unlike most libertarians he argues from consequences rather than natural rights. He studied historical legal systems, such as medieval Iceland, that worked with little or no state.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Anarcho-capitalism","Law and economics"],"thinkers":["david_friedman"],"tags":["anarcho-capitalism","machinery of freedom","private law","libertarianism"]},{"term":"Sayyid Qutb","def":"Islamic philosopher (1906–1966). Sovereignty belongs to God alone, so any society governed by human-made law is in a state of ignorance that must be replaced by Islamic order.","more":"An Egyptian writer and leading thinker of the Muslim Brotherhood, executed by the Nasser government in 1966. He argued that modern societies, including Muslim ones, had fallen back into jahiliyya, the ignorance before Islam. His writings deeply shaped later Islamist movements, including violent ones.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Islamism","Islamic revivalism"],"thinkers":["qutb"],"tags":["islamism","hakimiyya","milestones","jahiliyya"]},{"term":"Michael Walzer","def":"Analytic philosopher (1935). Each kind of good has its own fair way of being shared, set by what it means in a society, and no good should buy power over all the others.","more":"An American political theorist at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton and long-time editor of Dissent. He argued that justice is rooted in the shared understandings of particular communities. He is also known for his work on just and unjust wars.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Communitarianism","Complex equality"],"thinkers":["walzer"],"tags":["spheres of justice","complex equality","just and unjust wars","just war theory","communitarianism"]},{"term":"Han Feizi","def":"Chinese philosopher (280 BCE–233 BCE). A state is kept in order by clear laws, sure rewards and punishments, and a ruler who keeps firm control, not by virtue or ancient example.","more":"A prince of the state of Han in the late Warring States period and a student of Xunzi. He brought Legalist ideas together into a theory of rule by law, technique and position. The Qin ruler who unified China admired his writings, but Han Feizi died in a Qin prison.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Legalism"],"thinkers":["hanfeizi"],"tags":["han fei","han fei tzu","legalism","fa","chinese legalism","shi","shu"]},{"term":"Al-Farabi","def":"Islamic philosopher (872–950). The best city is ruled by a wise and virtuous leader who guides its citizens to true happiness.","more":"A philosopher from Central Asia who worked in Baghdad and Damascus, known as the 'Second Teacher' after Aristotle. He joined Plato's and Aristotle's political ideas with Islamic thought. He described the virtuous city and compared it with the many cities that pursue wealth, pleasure or honour instead.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Islamic Neoplatonism","Falsafa"],"thinkers":["alfarabi"],"tags":["farabi","alpharabius","second teacher","virtuous city","philosopher king"]},{"term":"Ibn Khaldun","def":"Islamic philosopher (1332–1406). Dynasties rise on the group loyalty of hardy peoples and decay as luxury softens them, and rule guided by religious law is the best kind.","more":"A North African historian, judge and statesman born in Tunis. His Muqaddimah, an introduction to world history, explains the rise and fall of states through 'asabiyya', the solidarity of a group. Many see him as a founder of sociology and economics.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Islamic political thought","Philosophy of history"],"thinkers":["ibn_khaldun"],"tags":["muqaddimah","asabiyya","group solidarity","philosophy of history"]},{"term":"Kwame Nkrumah","def":"Africana philosopher (1909–1972). Africa must free itself from colonial and neo-colonial control and build a socialism that renews its own egalitarian traditions.","more":"The first prime minister and president of Ghana, which in 1957 became the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence. A leading Pan-Africanist, he called for a united Africa. His philosophy, consciencism, sought to unite African tradition, Islam and Christianity under socialist principles.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["African socialism","Pan-Africanism"],"thinkers":["nkrumah"],"tags":["consciencism","pan-africanism","neo-colonialism","anticolonialism","african socialism"]},{"term":"Julius Nyerere","def":"Africana philosopher (1922–1999). Socialism is an attitude of mind rooted in the African extended family, where everyone works and everyone shares.","more":"Tanzania's first president, a teacher by training, known as Mwalimu (teacher). He called his socialism ujamaa, Swahili for familyhood. His 1967 Arusha Declaration put major industries under public control, though his programme of moving people into collective villages largely failed.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["African socialism","Ujamaa"],"thinkers":["nyerere"],"tags":["ujamaa","african socialism","mwalimu","tanzania","familyhood"]},{"term":"Paula Casal","def":"Analytic philosopher (1965). Everyone having enough comes first, but justice also cares how goods are shared above that line.","more":"A Spanish philosopher at Pompeu Fabra University in Barcelona. Her influential article on sufficiency argued that the view is right that everyone needs enough, but wrong that nothing else matters. She has also written on animal ethics and on gender and justice.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Sufficientarianism","Luck egalitarianism"],"thinkers":["casal"],"tags":["sufficientarianism","sufficiency","why sufficiency is not enough","prioritarianism"]},{"term":"Richard Arneson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1945). Justice should improve people's lives, giving priority to the worse off, while holding them responsible for what they freely choose.","more":"An American philosopher at the University of California, San Diego. He first defended equal opportunity for welfare, a leading form of luck egalitarianism. He later moved to a prioritarian view that still makes room for responsibility.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Luck egalitarianism","Prioritarianism"],"thinkers":["arneson"],"tags":["luck egalitarianism","prioritarianism","equality of opportunity for welfare","responsibility-sensitive egalitarianism"]},{"term":"Henry George","def":"Modern European philosopher (1839–1897). What people make belongs to them, but land belongs to everyone, so its rental value should be taxed for the common good.","more":"An American journalist and economist whose book Progress and Poverty sold millions of copies. He asked why poverty deepens as wealth grows, and blamed private capture of land rent. He proposed a single tax on land values in place of other taxes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Georgism","Political economy"],"thinkers":["henry_george"],"tags":["georgism","land value tax","single tax","progress and poverty","geolibertarianism"]},{"term":"Hillel Steiner","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942). You own yourself, but natural resources belong to everyone equally, so those who hold more owe the rest their share.","more":"A Canadian-born philosopher at the University of Manchester. He is a leading left-libertarian, accepting full self-ownership while holding that the earth is owned in common. He has also shaped debates on the nature of rights and of freedom.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Left-libertarianism"],"thinkers":["steiner"],"tags":["left-libertarianism","self-ownership","essay on rights","equal shares of natural resources"]},{"term":"Henry Sidgwick","def":"Modern European philosopher (1838–1900). Reason seems to back both my own good and the good of all, and I can't make them agree.","more":"A Cambridge philosopher whose The Methods of Ethics (1874) is often called the most careful book on ethics ever written. He weighed egoism, common-sense morality and utilitarianism against each other, and ended unable to decide between doing what is best for oneself and what is best for everyone, which he called the dualism of practical reason.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["sidgwick"],"tags":["methods of ethics","utilitarianism","dualism of practical reason","egoism"]},{"term":"R. M. Hare","def":"Analytic philosopher (1919–2002). Think like an archangel when you must; follow good rules when you can't.","more":"An Oxford moral philosopher. He argued that moral words work like commands that must apply to everyone alike, and that this leads to utilitarianism. In Moral Thinking (1981) he split moral reasoning into two levels: everyday intuitive rules, and careful critical thinking for hard cases.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism","Prescriptivism"],"thinkers":["hare"],"tags":["prescriptivism","universal prescriptivism","two-level utilitarianism","archangel","preference utilitarianism"]},{"term":"J. J. C. Smart","def":"Analytic philosopher (1920–2012). If breaking a rule does more good, sticking to it is mere rule worship.","more":"A British-born philosopher who spent his career in Australia. He defended act utilitarianism in its plainest form, accusing rule utilitarians of 'rule worship', and helped launch the theory that mental states are brain processes.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Utilitarianism"],"thinkers":["smart"],"tags":["act utilitarianism","identity theory","rule worship","utilitarianism: for and against"]},{"term":"Brad Hooker","def":"Analytic philosopher. Judge acts by the rules whose general acceptance would do the most good.","more":"A contemporary moral philosopher at the University of Reading, and the leading defender of rule consequentialism. He argues that it fits our considered moral beliefs better than act consequentialism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Rule consequentialism"],"thinkers":["hooker"],"tags":["rule consequentialism","rule utilitarianism","ideal code real world","rule-consequentialism"]},{"term":"Benjamin Constant","def":"Modern European philosopher (1767–1830). Freedom for moderns means a private life the state leaves alone.","more":"A Swiss-born French writer and politician who helped found modern liberalism. In 1797 he criticized the idea of an absolute duty to tell the truth, which drew Kant's famous reply about the murderer at the door.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Liberalism"],"thinkers":["constant"],"tags":["liberty of the ancients and moderns","ancient and modern liberty","liberalism","negative liberty"]},{"term":"James Rachels","def":"Analytic philosopher (1941–2003). If killing and letting die lead to the same death, why treat them differently?","more":"An American moral philosopher whose clear writing brought applied ethics to a wide audience. His 1975 Smith and Jones example is one of the most discussed cases about killing and letting die.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Applied ethics"],"thinkers":["rachels"],"tags":["active and passive euthanasia","killing and letting die","euthanasia","moral relativism"]},{"term":"Peter Unger","def":"Analytic philosopher (1942). Our intuitions about saving lives are distorted by how close the victim is.","more":"An American philosopher known for radical arguments in ethics and the theory of knowledge. His book Living High and Letting Die pushed Singer's drowning-child argument further.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Applied ethics"],"thinkers":["unger"],"tags":["living high and letting die","famine relief","drowning child","skepticism","demandingness"]},{"term":"Alvin Plantinga","def":"Analytic philosopher (1932). God could allow evil if a world with free creatures is worth it.","more":"An American philosopher who led the revival of philosophy of religion within analytic philosophy. His free will defense changed how philosophers discuss the logical problem of evil.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["plantinga"],"tags":["free will defense","reformed epistemology","modal ontological argument","evolutionary argument against naturalism","warrant"]},{"term":"John Hick","def":"Analytic philosopher (1922–2012). Suffering may be the price of a world where souls can grow.","more":"A British philosopher of religion. He argued that a world with real challenges, including suffering, is needed for people to develop into morally mature persons.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["hick"],"tags":["soul-making theodicy","irenaean theodicy","religious pluralism","eschatological verification","problem of evil"]},{"term":"William Rowe","def":"Analytic philosopher (1931–2015). A fawn dying slowly in a forest fire is evidence against God.","more":"An American philosopher of religion who developed the evidential argument from evil. He called himself a 'friendly atheist': he thought believers could still be rational.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Philosophy of religion"],"thinkers":["rowe"],"tags":["evidential problem of evil","evidential argument from evil","bambi","fawn","problem of evil"]},{"term":"Eric Olson","def":"Analytic philosopher. You are a human animal, so you persist as long as that animal does.","more":"A contemporary philosopher, based at the University of Sheffield, best known for defending animalism about personal identity.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Animalism"],"thinkers":["olson"],"tags":["animalism","human animal","personal identity"]},{"term":"David Enoch","def":"Analytic philosopher. Moral facts are as real as mathematical ones.","more":"A contemporary philosopher, long at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem and since 2023 Professor of the Philosophy of Law at Oxford, and a leading defender of robust moral realism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral realism"],"thinkers":["enoch"],"tags":["robust realism","moral realism","taking morality seriously","non-naturalism"]},{"term":"Joshua Greene","def":"Analytic philosopher. Our gut reactions to the trolley cases may not track what matters.","more":"An American psychologist and philosopher at Harvard. His brain-imaging and survey studies of trolley cases launched modern moral psychology, and he defends a pragmatic form of utilitarianism.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Moral psychology"],"thinkers":["greene"],"tags":["dual-process theory","trolley problem","moral psychology","deontology vs consequentialism","moral tribes"]},{"term":"P. F. Strawson","def":"Analytic philosopher (1919–2006). Blame lives in our resentment and gratitude, not in metaphysics.","more":"An Oxford philosopher who shaped postwar analytic philosophy. In 'On Referring' (1950) he challenged Russell's theory of descriptions, and in Individuals (1959) he revived 'descriptive metaphysics', the study of the basic concepts we actually think with. His lecture 'Freedom and Resentment' (1962) moved the free will debate away from determinism and toward the attitudes, like resentment and gratitude, that holding people responsible consists in. He was the father of Galen Strawson.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Ordinary language philosophy","Compatibilism"],"thinkers":["pfstrawson"],"tags":["freedom and resentment","reactive attitudes","individuals","descriptive metaphysics"]},{"term":"Elisabeth of Bohemia","def":"Modern European philosopher (1618–1680). If the soul has no extension, how can it push the body?","more":"A princess of the Palatinate who grew up in exile in the Dutch Republic. In 1643 she began a correspondence with Descartes, asking how an unextended thinking soul could move a body, since moving something seemed to require contact. The question became the classic objection to mind-body dualism. Their letters also covered the passions and how to live well, and Descartes dedicated his Principles of Philosophy (1644) to her. She later became abbess of the Protestant abbey at Herford.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Early modern philosophy"],"thinkers":["elisabeth"],"tags":["elisabeth of the palatinate","princess elisabeth","elizabeth of bohemia","descartes correspondence","mind-body interaction problem","interaction problem"]},{"term":"Selim Berker","def":"Analytic philosopher. Brain scans describe our judgements; they can't tell us which ones are right.","more":"A moral philosopher at Harvard who works on ethics and epistemology. In 'The Normative Insignificance of Neuroscience' (2009) he argued that brain-imaging studies of moral judgement, like Joshua Greene's trolley experiments, do no moral work on their own: every argument from them to a moral conclusion needs a moral premise the neuroscience does not supply.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Philosophers","related":["Metaethics","Moral epistemology"],"thinkers":["berker"],"tags":["neuroscience and ethics","normative insignificance of neuroscience","joshua greene","moral psychology"]},{"term":"Aristotelianism","def":"Aristotle’s followers study the natural world carefully and explain things by their matter, form, causes and purposes.","more":"Aristotle’s followers study the natural world carefully and explain things by their matter, form, causes and purposes. In ethics they aim at flourishing through virtue. Key idea: Everything has a nature and a purpose; living well means fulfilling yours.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["aristotle","avicenna","averroes","aquinas"],"tags":["peripatetic school","peripatetics","aristotle","lyceum","aristotelian","scholasticism","hylomorphism"]},{"term":"Pyrrhonian Skepticism","def":"Pyrrhonists found that for every argument there is an equally strong counter-argument.","more":"Pyrrhonists found that for every argument there is an equally strong counter-argument. So they suspended judgment, and found that peace of mind followed. Key idea: Suspend judgment and find tranquillity.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["pyrrho","sextus"],"tags":["pyrrhonism","pyrrho","sextus empiricus","ancient skepticism","scepticism","epoche","suspension of judgment"]},{"term":"Neoplatonism","def":"Neoplatonists developed Plato’s ideas into a picture of reality flowing out from a single source, the One.","more":"Neoplatonists developed Plato’s ideas into a picture of reality flowing out from a single source, the One. The soul’s task is to turn back and rise toward it. Key idea: All things come from the One and long to return to it.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["plotinus","augustine","boethius"],"tags":["plotinus","the one","emanation","neoplatonist","proclus","porphyry"]},{"term":"Legalism","def":"Legalist thinkers such as Han Feizi held that order comes from clear laws, strict rewards and punishments, and a strong ruler — not from virtue.","more":"Legalist thinkers such as Han Feizi held that order comes from clear laws, strict rewards and punishments, and a strong ruler — not from virtue. Their ideas shaped the Qin unification of China. Key idea: People respond to incentives; rule by law, not virtue.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":[],"tags":["fajia","chinese legalism","han feizi","shang yang","legalists","fa"]},{"term":"Buddhist Philosophy","def":"The Buddha taught that suffering comes from craving and from clinging to a self that is not really there.","more":"The Buddha taught that suffering comes from craving and from clinging to a self that is not really there. Later thinkers like Nagarjuna’s Madhyamaka school argued that all things are ‘empty’ of fixed nature. Key idea: Let go of craving and of the illusion of a fixed self.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["buddha","nagarjuna"],"tags":["buddhism","dharma","dhamma","four noble truths","no-self","anatta","emptiness","nirvana"]},{"term":"Philosophy in the Islamic World","def":"Thinkers writing in Arabic combined Greek philosophy, especially Aristotle, with questions about God, prophecy and the soul.","more":"Thinkers writing in Arabic combined Greek philosophy, especially Aristotle, with questions about God, prophecy and the soul. Their debates — and their translations — later shaped European thought. Key idea: Can reason and revelation be reconciled?","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["avicenna","alghazali","averroes","maimonides"],"tags":["islamic philosophy","arabic philosophy","falsafa","kalam","avicenna","averroes","al-farabi"]},{"term":"Zen Buddhism","def":"Zen emphasises direct insight through meditation rather than study of texts.","more":"Zen emphasises direct insight through meditation rather than study of texts. Dōgen taught that sitting meditation is not a means to enlightenment but its very expression. Key idea: Wake up here and now, through practice.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["dogen"],"tags":["zen","chan","chan buddhism","zazen","satori","koan","dogen"]},{"term":"British Empiricism","def":"The empiricists argued that all our ideas come from experience; the mind starts as a blank slate.","more":"The empiricists argued that all our ideas come from experience; the mind starts as a blank slate. Followed through, this led Hume to doubt causation, the self and induction. Key idea: Nothing is in the mind that was not first in the senses.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["bacon","locke","berkeley","hume"],"tags":["empiricism","locke","berkeley","hume","tabula rasa","blank slate"]},{"term":"Social Contract Theory","def":"Social contract theorists explain political authority as arising from an agreement among free and equal people.","more":"Social contract theorists explain political authority as arising from an agreement among free and equal people. They differ on how bad life without government would be and what the deal includes. Key idea: Government is legitimate because we (could) agree to it.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["hobbes","locke","rousseau","rawls"],"tags":["social contract","contractarianism","contractualism","state of nature","hobbes","locke","rousseau","consent of the governed"]},{"term":"German Idealism","def":"Starting from Kant’s claim that the mind shapes experience, the German Idealists asked how far reality itself is structured by mind or reason.","more":"Starting from Kant’s claim that the mind shapes experience, the German Idealists asked how far reality itself is structured by mind or reason. Hegel saw history as reason coming to know itself. Key idea: The structure of reality and the structure of mind are deeply linked.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["kant","hegel","schopenhauer"],"tags":["idealism","fichte","schelling","hegel","absolute idealism","transcendental idealism","kant"]},{"term":"Ordinary Language Philosophy","def":"Inspired by the later Wittgenstein and J.","more":"Inspired by the later Wittgenstein and J. L. Austin, these philosophers argued that many puzzles come from misusing everyday words. Look at how language actually works, and some problems dissolve. Key idea: Meaning is use.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["wittgenstein","searle"],"tags":["linguistic philosophy","oxford philosophy","j. l. austin","ryle","ordinary language","speech acts","later wittgenstein"]},{"term":"Africana Philosophy","def":"Africana philosophy examines the thought and experience of Africa and its diaspora, including race, colonialism and liberation.","more":"Africana philosophy examines the thought and experience of Africa and its diaspora, including race, colonialism and liberation. Du Bois explored ‘double consciousness’ and Fanon analysed colonial violence and identity. Key idea: Philosophy must reckon with race, colonialism and liberation.","etym":null,"example":null,"notConfused":null,"field":"Schools and movements","related":[],"thinkers":["du_bois","fanon"],"tags":["african philosophy","black philosophy","african diaspora","ubuntu","du bois","fanon","negritude","pan-africanism"]}];
